<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
affirm the district court's judgment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>BACKGROUND</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Polar
Molecular Corporation (&quot;Polar&quot;) is a Utah corporation that
manufactures and sells petrochemical compounds. The defendants-appellees are
Polar officers and directors (the &quot;Directors&quot;). The plaintiffs are
former managers and sales representatives of Polar. In 1989 and 1990, Polar
entered various contracts with the individual plaintiffs, and the present
dispute arises out of Polar's alleged failure to perform obligations under
these contracts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint alleged the following facts:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Walsh and Miles</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
April 1989, Polar hired Chester Walsh and TV. Miles to be managers. Both
employment contracts provided for $ 70,000 in salary and 25,000 shares of
unregistered Polar stock. Because shares must be registered with the Securi=
ties
and Exchange Commission (&quot;SEC&quot;) before they can be sold
&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; on the open market, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>see</i>
Securities Exchange Act of 1934, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78l</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>, the contracts stated that Polar would register the sha=
res
with the SEC &quot;at the earliest opportunity in the year 1989.&quot;</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Polar
gave the 25,000 shares each to Walsh and Miles as promised, but did not
register them. In October 1989, Walsh asked Mark Nelson, president of Polar,
why the shares had not been registered. Nelson said the shares were &quot;in
registration.&quot; Between October and January, other Polar personnel also
told Walsh and Miles that the shares would be registered. Despite these
assurances, Polar never registered any of the shares.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mello</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
May 1989, Polar hired Joseph Mello to sell its products on commission. As p=
art
of the deal, Polar granted Mello an option to purchase 100,000 shares of
Polar's stock at $ 1.75 per share. Polar never registered any stock to sell
Mello, and Mello never exercised his option to purchase.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mills</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>William
Mills was another salesman for Polar. In January 1990, Mills sued Polar for=
 the
breach of a sales representative agreement. On March 20, Mills and Nelson
discussed settlement over the telephone. Nelson told Mills that
Mills&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; would have to accept unregistered stock as payment
because Polar was short of cash. Nelson assured Mills that the stock would =
be
registered immediately after it was issued. On March 27, Nelson again told
Mills over the phone that Polar would register the shares. A week later, Ne=
lson
and Mills met in Michigan. There, they agreed that Polar would execute a new
sales representative agreement for Mills. Nelson promised that Polar would
perform its part of the new agreement in all respects.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mills
and Polar finally signed a contract settling their dispute on April 13, 1990
(the &quot;Settlement&quot;). Under the Settlement, Mills agreed to dismiss=
 his
claims with prejudice in &nbsp;[*1174]&nbsp; exchange for 30,000 shares of
unregistered Polar stock and an option to purchase an additional 200,000
shares. The Settlement also provided that Polar would register the 30,000
shares in the &quot;then current&quot; registration statement on file with =
the
SEC.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Additionally,
as the parties discussed, the Settlement stated that Polar would execute a =
new
sales representative agreement, granting Mills the right to solicit orders =
for
Polar products in South America. The agreement stated that Polar was not
obligated to accept any of Mills' &nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; orders, but that it wou=
ld
not withhold consent unreasonably.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pursuant
to the Settlement, Polar delivered to Mills 30,000 unregistered shares on M=
ay
1, 1990. Afterward, Nelson told Mills on several occasions that registration
was imminent. At the end of August 1990, however, Polar filed a new
registration statement that did not list Mills' shares. The statement, howe=
ver,
did register shares belonging to Nelson and other managers at Polar.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Also
in August, Mills procured a large order of Polar products from a Paraguayan
buyer. Although Polar initially indicated its approval to both Mills and the
Paraguayan buyer, it ultimately rejected the order on the advice of counsel=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>D. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The Proceedings Below</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
early 1991, the plaintiffs brought this action in the United States District
Court for the Southern District of New York against Polar and the Directors=
. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn2' href=3D"#_ftn2" name=3D"_ftnref2" title=3D""=
><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The complaint (which the plaintiffs amended several times) alleged that Pol=
ar
and the Directors promised to register the plaintiffs' shares but never did=
 so.
The complaint further alleged that the aggregation of broken promises
constituted a pattern of mail and wire fraud in violation of RICO. To bolst=
er
the fraud claims, and in an&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; attempt to plead predicate acts
under RICO, the complaint alleged that Polar and the Directors had breached
promises to register shares to several other persons (in addition to the
plaintiffs herein) from 1989 to 1990. Notably, the complaint did not allege
that any Directors personally made fraudulent statements in these other
instances.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mills
added an allegation that the Directors breached their fiduciary duty by not
accepting the Paraguayan order. Mills also alleged claims for breach of
contract, arising out of Polar's failure to perform both the sales
representative agreement and the Settlement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Polar
and the Directors moved to dismiss the fraud claims for failure to satisfy =
the
pleading requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b) of =
the
Federal Rules of Civil Procedure</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and all the other claims for
failure to state a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court granted the motion and dismissed the entire complaint.
&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; It found that the plaintiffs had failed to plead fraud wi=
th
particularity under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and that they had not adequately alleged facts to raise a strong inference =
of
scienter, or, fraudulent intent. The court dismissed the RICO claims under =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for failure to allege predicate acts. It dismissed Mills' fiduciary breach
claim because he failed to make a demand on the Directors before filing sui=
t.
Finally, the court dismissed Mills' contract claims, saying that, for reaso=
ns
never fully articulated, they should be adjudicated in a Michigan state cou=
rt.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs now appeal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>DISCUSSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CC0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
we review the grant of a motion to dismiss under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> or
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
we accept as true the factual allegations of the complaint, and draw all
inferences in favor of the pleader. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CF00=
00400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BBP0-003B-P09V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>IUE AFL-CIO
Pension Fund v. Herrmann,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BBP0-003B-P09V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 9 F.3d 1049,
1993 U.S. App. LEXIS 30077</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, No. 93-7384, slip op. 215, 218=
-19
(2d Cir. 1993). Dismissal under either rule is proper where the plaintiff
cannot recover on the facts he has alleged. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CF0000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-V0B0-003B-G1RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ryder Energy
Distribution Corp. v. Merrill Lynch Commodities Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-V0B0-003B-G1RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 748 F.2d 774,
779 (2d Cir. 1984)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*1175]&nbsp;
I. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Claims</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
securities fraud claims on the grounds that the plaintiffs: (1) had not pled
fraud with particularity under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(2)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
had not alleged facts that gave rise to a strong inference of fraudulent
intent.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D20000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
proscribes persons from making untrue or misleading statements of material =
fact
in connection with a securities transaction. Employment contracts promising
shares as compensation are generally considered securities transactions wit=
hin </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D40000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3CG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7XM0-003B-632M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dubin v. E.F.
Hutton Group Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7XM0-003B-632M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 695 F. Supp.
138, 146-47 (S.D.N.Y. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D40000400_2"></a>A person who promises to p=
erform
a specific act in the future, while secretly intending not to perform, viol=
ates
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
provided that the promise is given as consideration for the transfer of
securities.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Luce v.
Edelstein,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 802 F.2d 49,=
 55
(2d Cir. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
plaintiff must comply with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which requires that fraud be pled with particularity. Specifically, the
complaint must: (1) specify the statements that the plaintiff contends were
fraudulent, (2) identify the speaker, (3) state where and when the statemen=
ts
were made, and (4) explain why the statements&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; were fraudul=
ent.
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3D50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CS0-003B-515C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cosmas v.
Hassett,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CS0-003B-515C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 886 F.2d 8, =
11
