name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MJ0000400"></a>Two years later, with the pr=
oject
unfinished, the defendants already had spent more than three times that amo=
unt.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MJ0000400_2"></a>The plaintiff Ouaknine,=
 who
had invested in the project, sued under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
alleging that the defendants knowingly lied in the offering about what the
project would cost. To plead intent, Ouaknine alleged that the defendants' =
cost
projection had failed to include more than $ 2,000,000 in necessary expense=
s.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 81</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We held that Ouaknine adequately alleged facts to imply the defendants inte=
nded
to deceive when they issued the offering. &nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
in contrast, Mills alleged no fact probative of Nelson's intent at the time=
 he
made the promises to Mills. Mills alleged that Polar registered shares in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>August</i> 1990 for Nelson and other P=
olar
managers, but not for Mills. This allegation does not bear on Nelson's inte=
nt
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>April</i> 1990, when the Settlem=
ent
was executed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
decline Mills' invitation to infer fraudulent intent from the fact that Pol=
ar
made a number of contracts to register shares and never performed any of th=
em.
A contract may be breached for legitimate business reasons. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See generally</i> E. Allan Farnsworth,=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Contracts</i> &sect; 12.8 at 874-75 (2=
d ed.
1990). Contractual breach, in and of itself, does not bespeak fraud, and
generally does not give rise to tort damages.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we affirm dismissal of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claims for failure to state a claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The RICO Claims</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed the RICO mail and wire fraud claims because the
plaintiffs did not sufficiently plead two predicate acts of fraud. We agree=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MM0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
state a mail and wire fraud claim under RICO, a plaintiff must allege that =
the
defendant made two predicate communications, via interstate commerce, that
constitute a pattern&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; of racketeering activity. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3N20000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.L=
.R.
v. Imrex Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 473 U.S. 479,
495-96, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346, 105 S. Ct. 3275 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Like allegations of securities fraud, allegations of predicate mail and wire
fraud acts should state the contents of the communications, who was involve=
d,
where and when they took place, and explain why they were fraudulent. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3N20000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3MN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-88X0-003B-6529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Official
Publications, Inc. v. Kable News Co., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-88X0-003B-6529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 692 F. Supp.
239, 245 (S.D.N.Y. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>aff'd in part, rev'd in part on other grounds,</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3N30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9JR0-003B-51YC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>884 F.2d 664 =
(2d
Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Our conclusion that Walsh, Miles, and Mello failed to
satisfy </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
demands as a corollary that these allegations cannot serve as predicate act=
s.
The plaintiffs' allegations about Polar's broken promises to third parties =
were
equally vague, and cannot serve as predicate acts either.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3N60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Mills' allegations of fraud also=
 do
not suffice. Like </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7"></a>mail and wire fraud requires a showing =
of
fraudulent intent. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NM0000400"></a=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3N50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9RP0-001B-K49F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Beck v.
Manufacturers Hanover Trust Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9RP0-001B-K49F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 820 F.2d at =
49</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NM0000400_2"></a>A complaint alleging mail =
and
wire fraud must plead facts that give rise to a strong inference that the
defendant possessed&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; fraudulent intent. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9RP0-001B-K49F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9RP0-001B-K49F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 50</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
As Mills failed to allege facts that support an inference of intent for his
10b-5 claim, similarly he has not adequately pled a predicate act of mail a=
nd
wire fraud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NP0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Having failed to plead any predi=
cate
act of mail and wire fraud, the plaintiffs have not stated a RICO action
against the Directors. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YJ50-003B-G4TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moss v. Morgan
Stanley, Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YJ50-003B-G4TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 719 F.2d 5, =
18 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YJ50-003B-G4TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*1177]=
&nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YJ50-003B-G4TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> &amp; n.14 (=
2d
Cir. 1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied,<=
/i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3NR0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>465 U.S. 1025 (1984)</i>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-KPK0-0054-54C3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Somerville v.
Major Exploration, Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-KPK0-0054-54C3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 576 F. Supp.
902, 913-14 (S.D.N.Y. 1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, the district court prope=
rly
dismissed these claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mills' Remaining Claims</i> </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed Mills' claims for breach of contract, stating that
they should have been brought in Michigan state court. It dismissed Mills'
breach of fiduciary duty claim because the claim was shareholder derivative=
 in
nature, and Mills failed to demand cure from Polar's board of directors bef=
ore
filing suit. We find that dismissal was proper on these claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The Contract Claims</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a contract claim against the Directors, Mills alleged &nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; th=
at
Polar breached the Settlement by not registering his 30,000 shares, and that
Polar breached the sales contract by not accepting the Paraguayan order. Wh=
ile
Mills may have stated a claim against Polar for breach, we affirm the dismi=
ssal
as against the Directors because Mills stated no actionable breach claim
against them.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PJ0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
director is not personally liable for his corporation's contractual breaches
unless he assumed personal liability, acted in bad faith or committed a tor=
t in
connection with the performance of the contract. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PM0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-B170-003C-F355-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Murtha v. Yon=
kers
Child Care Ass'n, Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-B170-003C-F355-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 45 N.Y.2d 91=
3,
915, 411 N.Y.S.2d 219, 220, 383 N.E.2d 865 (1978)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
That the director, while acting in his official capacity, took actions that
resulted in the breach, does not render him personally liable. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PM0000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i> (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PK000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRM-VH80-003F-723B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Brooksi=
de
Mills,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRM-VH80-003F-723B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 276 A.D. 357,
367, 94 N.Y.S.2d 509, 518 (1950))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:=
"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mills
does not suggest that Nelson assumed personal liability. However, he argues
that Nelson committed a tort by &quot;committing himself . . . to good faith
performance&quot; of the contracts and then breaching. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9"></a>Every party to a contract commits
himself&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; to good faith performance. That does not transmute
breach of contract into a tort.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Fiduciary Breach Claims</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Mills asserts that the district court erred in dismissing his claim that, by
not accepting the Paraguayan sales order, the Directors breached their
fiduciary duty to shareholders.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mills
argues that the district court should have applied the law of Utah, where P=
olar
is incorporated, and contends that Utah does not require a shareholder suing
for fiduciary breach to seek cure from the directors before suing them.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PP0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We disagree with Mills'
interpretation of Utah law. Mills' fiduciary breach claim, in essence, is t=
hat
the Directors mismanaged Polar by failing to accept the Paraguay an sales
order. In Utah, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10"></a>suits for directori=
al
mismanagement and suits to enforce the corporation's rights with respect to=
 a
third-party contract are deemed derivative.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMRYY0K1MNJ3PN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXP-5PR0-003G-F3WK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Richardson v.
Arizona Fuels Corp.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXP-5PR0-003G-F3WK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 614 P.2d 636,
639 (Utah 1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Like most states, Utah require=
s a
shareholder bringing a derivative action to describe in the complaint the
efforts he made to have the directors or shareholders redress his problem, =
and
state why these efforts were fruitless. &nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See</i> Utah R. Civ. P. 23.1 (Michie 1992). Mills' complaint contai=
ned
no such allegations. Accordingly, even if the district court had applied Ut=
ah
law, it would have dismissed Mills' fiduciary breach claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finding
all the plaintiffs' arguments without merit, we affirm the decision of the
district court.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

