<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>JURISDICTION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Before
we can address the propriety of the preliminary injunction, we must ensure =
that
the case is properly&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; before this Court. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_0"></a>Although the parties did not raise the
issue of appellate jurisdiction in their briefs, we have an independent
obligation to ensure that we have jurisdiction over the appeal. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68Y1-GWP1-JXG3-X3PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stafford v. I=
nt'l
Bus. Machs. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68Y1-GWP1-JXG3-X3PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 78 F.4th 62=
, 68
(2d Cir. 2023)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus, after oral argument, this Court ordered the part=
ies
to brief the issue of this Court's appellate jurisdiction. Having reviewed =
the
parties' submissions, we conclude that this appeal is not moot and that we =
have
jurisdiction over the district court's orders. But before explaining the ba=
sis
for our jurisdiction, we review certain aspects of the procedural history
&nbsp;[*77]&nbsp; that bear directly on the question of our appellate
jurisdiction.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
January 2023, while the appeal was pending in this Court, State Farm moved =
for
leave to file a Second Amended Complaint (&quot;SAC&quot;) in which it added
sixteen new Defendants and further details about the alleged fraud. To cover
the newly-added Defendants, State Farm also moved for a temporary restraini=
ng
order and to modify the scope of the previously entered preliminary injunct=
ion.
Chief Judge Brodie granted the temporary restraining order in February 2023=
 and
determined that the existing preliminary injunction already applied to the =
new
Defendants under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 65(d)(2)(C)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, which&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; makes =
an
injunction enforceable against &quot;persons who are in active concert or
participation with&quot; the already-enjoined Defendants. Chief Judge Brodie
then referred State Farm's motion for leave to file the SAC to Magistrate J=
udge
Peggy Kuo who granted the motion, and State Farm filed the SAC on August 17,
