<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Then,
in September 2023, Chief Judge Brodie denied State Farm's motion to modify =
the
scope of the existing preliminary injunction. Referring back to her prior
decision granting the temporary restraining order, Chief Judge Brodie stated
that because the existing injunction already covered the newly-added
Defendants, there was no need to modify the injunction. She elaborated that
&quot;each of the newly-added entities is closely associated with or contro=
lled
by one or more of the existing Defendants, while each of the newly-added
individuals owns an entity alleged to have provided unnecessary treatments
under the challenged scheme.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:69JW-8751-JP9P-G447-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State Farm Mu=
t.
Auto. Ins. Co. v. Metro Pain Specialists P.C</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:69JW-8751-JP9P-G447-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. (</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:69JW-8751-JP9P-G447-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&quot;State F=
arm
II&quot;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:69JW-8751-JP9P-G447-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>), No. 21-CV-=
5523
(MKB), 2023 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 198357, 2023 WL 7181653, at *4 (E.D.N.Y. Sept.=
 13,
2023)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus, the district court declined to modify the
preliminary injunction, which remains unchanged since the district court
granted it in May 2022.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Those
developments occurred while the appeal was pending in this Court, so the
precise question before us is&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; whether the SAC renders this
appeal moot. We conclude that the appeal is not moot. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_0"></a>To avoid mootness, there must be an
&quot;actual controversy&quot; through which the parties can obtain some re=
lief
from the court, and that controversy must exist &quot;through all stages of=
 the
litigation.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57FY-V131-F04K-F4MF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Already, LLC =
v.
Nike, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57FY-V131-F04K-F4MF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 568 U.S. 85,
90-91, 133 S. Ct. 721, 184 L. Ed. 2d 553 (2013)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). &quot;A case becomes moot . . . when the
issues presented are no longer live or the parties lack a legally cognizable
interest in the outcome.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57FY-V131-F04K-F4MF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57FY-V131-F04K-F4MF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 91</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). In other words, and as relevant here, t=
he
preliminary injunction must still be effective, and there must be something
left for the court to do.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Generally, &quot;[o]nce an amend=
ed
pleading is interposed, the original pleading no longer performs any functi=
on
in the case.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2830-006F-M0B8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Laza v. Reish=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2830-006F-M0B8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 84 F.3d 578,
581 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
Building upon that understanding, a growing number of unpublished decisions
from this Court have concluded that the &quot;filing of an amended complaint
will generally moot a pending appeal when . . . the appeal would require the
Court to analyze factual allegations contained in the superseded
complaint.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Medidata Sols., In=
c. v.
Veeva Sys. Inc., 748 F. App'x 363, 365 (2d Cir. 2018)</i> (summary order); =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65N4-35V1-DY33-B07J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tripathy v.
McClowski</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65N4-35V1-DY33-B07J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 21-3094,
2022 U.S. App. LEXIS 15877, 2022 WL 2069228, at *2 (2d Cir. June 9, 2022)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(summary order) (holding that the appeal of a preliminary injunction was
moot&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; because the amended complaint filed while the appeal=
 was
pending requested new injunctive relief); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Adamou v. Doy=
le</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 674 F. App'x
50, 52 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*78]&n=
bsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (2d Cir. 201=
7)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(summary order) (ruling that the &quot;appeal became moot upon the filing of
the . . . amended complaint because the operative facts changed&quot; and t=
he
Court therefore &quot;lack[ed] jurisdiction to review an order that was bas=
ed
on an old universe of facts&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
these cases suggest, the filing of an amended complaint will generally moot=
 the
appeal of a preliminary injunction, because an amended complaint will commo=
nly
&quot;change[]&quot; the &quot;operative facts,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Adamou</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5MJF-BVV1-F04K-J0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 674 F. App'=
x at
52</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'>, on which the district court based the injunction. As a
result, once a new set of facts is alleged, there is typically no point in
assessing whether the now-superseded &quot;universe of facts,&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i>, supported the injunction.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>But
that will not invariably be the case. Two of our sister courts of appeals, =
in
determining whether such appeals are moot, have analyzed whether the revised
pleadings in fact affect the substantive basis for a district court's order=
. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See Auto Driveaway Franchise Sys., LLC=
 v.
Auto Driveaway Richmond, LLC, 928 F.3d 670, 674-75 (7th Cir. 2019)</i>
(concluding that the appeal was not moot because the &quot;new pleadings di=
d no
more than to add&quot; a party and &quot;had no effect on [the plaintiff's]
basic grievance&quot;); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </=
i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:674R-20S1-JJYN-B07H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Johnson v. 3M=
 Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:674R-20S1-JJYN-B07H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 55 F.4th 13=
04,
1309 (11th Cir. 2022)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (determining that the
court&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; had jurisdiction over the appeal of an immunity ord=
er
because the &quot;key point&quot; was that the amended complaint did not ch=
ange
the allegations on which the immunity defense was based, &quot;leav[ing] the
status of th[e] appeal of the immunity order unaffected&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
agree with that reasoning and conclude that whether a revised pleading rend=
ers
an appeal moot turns on whether that pleading materially changes the
substantive basis for the appeal. Here, the SAC borrows heavily from the FA=
C,
with the primary difference between the two complaints being that the SAC a=
dds
sixteen Defendants and further details about the alleged fraudulent scheme.=
 The
newly-added Defendants do not meaningfully alter the substantive basis of t=
he
appeal because, as the district court found, they are either closely associ=
ated
with or controlled by the existing Defendants, or provided unnecessary
treatments under the scheme. Neither party disputes that finding on appeal.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for the additional allegations included in the SAC, the most significant
difference appears to be that the scheme is now described as revolving arou=
nd
Moshe instead of the Metro Pain Defendants. But even that does not alter St=
ate
Farm's basic grievance&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; that Defendants allegedly operate a
complex scheme to fraudulently obtain No-Fault benefits by, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inter alia</i>, providing unnecessary
treatment and services and kickbacks for patient referrals. Moreover, both =
the
SAC and FAC allege that Moshe played an important role in the scheme, with =
the
SAC merely placing greater emphasis on his role. Any other additional alleg=
ations
in the SAC operate to further strengthen State Farm's claims. Tellingly, the
May 2022 preliminary injunction remains predicated on the FAC, as the distr=
ict
court declined to amend it in any way based on the additional allegations in
the SAC. Thus, although the FAC is no longer the operative complaint govern=
ing
further proceedings in the district court, the issues on appeal remain
unaffected by that change, and the propriety of the injunction does not tur=
n on
any change effected by the SAC.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
preliminary injunction thus remains in place, unmodified, and continues to
constrain Defendants by preventing them from commencing new arbitrations and
state-court litigation and proceeding &nbsp;[*79]&nbsp; with the pending
arbitrations. In other words, Defendants are still aggrieved by the prelimi=
nary
injunction even after the filing of the SAC.&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; Accordingly,=
 we
conclude that this appeal is not moot, and we have jurisdiction over it.</s=
pan><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn8' href=3D"#_ftn8" name=3D"_ftnref8" title=3D""=
><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>MERITS DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Standards of Review</span></b=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We review the grant or denial of=
 a
preliminary injunction for abuse of discretion. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BVC-DGG0-0038-X0PN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sunward Elecs=
.,
Inc. v. McDonald</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BVC-DGG0-0038-X0PN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 362 F.3d 17=
, 24
(2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. &quot;A district court 'abuses' or 'exceeds' the
discretion accorded to it when (1) its decision rests on an error of law . =
. .
or a clearly erroneous factual finding, or (2) its decision&#8212;though not
necessarily the product of a legal error or a clearly erroneous factual
finding&#8212;cannot be located within the range of permissible
decisions.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4372-TP70-0038-X1DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Zervos v. Ver=
izon
New York, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4372-TP70-0038-X1DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 252 F.3d 16=
3,
169 (2d Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (footnote omitted). That said,
&quot;[w]here allegations of error in a preliminary injunction involve
questions of law, our review is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de =
novo</i>.&quot;
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GCK-FCR1-F04K-J04K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Briggs v. Bre=
mby</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GCK-FCR1-F04K-J04K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 792 F.3d 23=
9,
241 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, we review the district
court's decision to grant the preliminary injunction here for abuse of
discretion but review its interpretation of the FAA and AIA <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo. See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6BXN-X113-S8KK-41NF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cedeno v. Sas=
son</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6BXN-X113-S8KK-41NF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 100 F.4th 3=
86,
393 (2d Cir. 2024)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (FAA); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56SJ-JYC1-F04K-J22W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wyly v. Weiss=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56SJ-JYC1-F04K-J22W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 697 F.3d 13=
1,
137 (2d Cir. 2012)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (AIA).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Analysis</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
first consider Defendants' appeal, which challenges (1) the propriety of the
preliminary injunction issued by the district court and (2) the district
court's conclusion that the arbitrations can be enjoined, notwithstanding t=
he
FAA. Then, we consider State Farm's cross-appeal, which
challenges&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; the district court's conclusion that the pendi=
ng
state-court proceedings cannot be enjoined under the AIA.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. Preliminary Injunction</span>=
</i></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First,
Defendants challenge the preliminary injunction insofar as it stays the pen=
ding
arbitrations and enjoins them from commencing any new arbitrations or
state-court proceedings. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_0"></a>A prelimi=
nary
injunction &quot;'is an extraordinary and drastic remedy, one that should n=
ot
be granted unless the movant, by a clear showing, carries the burden of
persuasion.'&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-41X0-0038-X1VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore v. Cons=
ol.
Edison Co. of New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-41X0-0038-X1VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 409 F.3d 50=
6,
510 (2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-HY70-003B-R1DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mazurek v.
Armstrong</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-HY70-003B-R1DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 520 U.S. 96=
8,
972, 117 S. Ct. 1865, 138 L. Ed. 2d 162 (1997)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
To obtain a preliminary injunction, a party must show &quot;(1) irreparable
harm; (2) either a likelihood of success on the merits or both serious
questions on the merits and a balance of hardships decidedly favoring the
moving party; and (3) that a preliminary injunction is in the public
interest.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>North American Socc=
er
League, LLC v. United States Soccer Fed'n, 883 F.3d 32, 37 (2d Cir. 2018)</=
i>.
Because under the &quot;serious questions&quot; standard a party would have=
 to
demonstrate both serious questions on the merits and a balance of hardships
decidedly favoring the moving party, the &quot;overall burden is no
lighter&nbsp;[*80]&nbsp; than the one [the party] bears under the 'likeliho=
od
of success' standard.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7Y03-KMW0-YB0V-D01F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Citigroup Glo=
b.
Mkts., Inc. v. VCG Special Opportunities Master Fund Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7Y03-KMW0-YB0V-D01F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 598 F.3d 30=
, 35
(2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
argue that the district court abused its discretion in granting the prelimi=
nary
injunction. We disagree.&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. Irreparable Harm</span></i></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The irreparable harm requirement=
 is
&quot;'the single most important prerequisite for the issuance of a prelimi=
nary
injunction.'&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VTN-K8G0-TXFX-4274-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Faiveley Tran=
sp.
Malmo AB v. Wabtec Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VTN-K8G0-TXFX-4274-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 559 F.3d 11=
0,
118 (2d Cir. 2009)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WY3-10T0-0038-X560-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rodriguez v.
DeBuono</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WY3-10T0-0038-X560-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 175 F.3d 22=
7,
234 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. This requirement must therefor=
e be
satisfied before the other requirements for an injunction can be considered=
. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kamerling v.
Massanari</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 295 F.3d 20=
6,
214 (2d Cir. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. To make this showing, the movi=
ng
party &quot;'must demonstrate that absent a preliminary injunction [it] will
suffer an injury that is neither remote nor speculative, but actual and
imminent, and one that cannot be remedied if a court waits until the end of
trial to resolve the harm.'&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VTN-K8G0-TXFX-4274-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Faiveley</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VTN-K8G0-TXFX-4274-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 559 F.3d at=
 118</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N6R-C7K0-0038-X1NW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Grand River
Enter. Six Nations, Ltd. v. Pryor</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N6R-C7K0-0038-X1NW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 481 F.3d 60=
, 66
(2d Cir. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus, irreparable harm exists &quot;where, but for the
grant of equitable relief, there is a substantial chance that upon final
resolution of the action the parties cannot be returned to the positions th=
ey
previously occupied.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WBP-35S0-0038-X0HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Brenntag Int'l
Chems., Inc. v. Bank of India</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WBP-35S0-0038-X0HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 175 F.3d 24=
5,
249 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. But &quot;[w]here there is an
adequate remedy at law, such as an award of money damages, injunctions are
unavailable except in extraordinary circumstances.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-41X0-0038-X1VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-41X0-0038-X1VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 409 F.3d at=
 510</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Therefore, the moving party must show that &quot;there is a continuing harm
which cannot be adequately redressed by final relief on the merits and for
which money damages cannot provide adequate compensation.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kamerling</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 295 F.3d at=
 214</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
this case, the&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; district court determined that State Farm
satisfied the irreparable harm requirement because &quot;the global and
intertwined nature of the fraud is effectively obscured in the fragmented
state-court proceedings and arbitrations due to an incomplete factual recor=
d,
in addition to the risk of inconsistent judgments, frustration of declarato=
ry
judgment relief, and possible preclusive effect.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65GY-NT51-JKB3-X3RN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State Farm I<=
/span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65GY-NT51-JKB3-X3RN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2022 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 91241, 2022 WL 1606523, at *19</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotati=
on
marks and alterations omitted). The district court also noted that the part=
ies
are currently in the midst of thousands of pending arbitrations, and Defend=
ants
are likely to initiate new arbitrations and lawsuits to recover unpaid clai=
ms.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
discern no error, much less an abuse of discretion, in the district court's
conclusion that State Farm satisfied the irreparable harm requirement. To
begin, State Farm plausibly alleges that the arbitrations and state-court
proceedings often involve single claims for a single date of service, so th=
at
these fragmented proceedings end up obscuring what State Farm contends is an
elaborate and complex fraudulent scheme. Although a state court or arbitrat=
or
reviewing an individual claim may conclude that the claim involves necessary
medical treatment&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; under the No-Fault Act, State Farm
sufficiently alleges that the massive fraudulent scheme here becomes appare=
nt
only when the claims are analyzed altogether. That is, to prove up allegati=
ons
of a predetermined treatment protocol, a reviewing court or arbitrator must
consider whether Defendants have repeatedly provided the same treatment or
services to several patients regardless of their actual medical needs, whic=
h is
exceedingly difficult to establish &nbsp;[*81]&nbsp; in a proceeding on a
single claim. Put another way, Defendants' alleged fraudulent scheme is not
readily apparent when viewed on an individual claim-by-claim basis, and the
arbitrations and state-court proceedings therefore help to insulate the all=
eged
fraud from detection.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>That
risk of harm is amplified by the potential preclusive effect of the state-c=
ourt
proceedings and arbitrations. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_0"></a>It is
settled law that state-court judgments can have a preclusive effect in fede=
ral
court. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6BJN-8CH3-RXKH-52SG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Whitfield v. =
City
of New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6BJN-8CH3-RXKH-52SG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 96 F.4th 50=
4,
522 (2d Cir. 2024)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting that federal courts are
required &quot;to give to a state-court judgment the same preclusive effect=
 as
would be given that judgment under the law of the State in which the judgme=
nt
was rendered&quot; (internal quotation marks omitted)). It is likewise
settled&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; &quot;that res judicata and collateral estoppel a=
pply
to issues resolved by arbitration where there has been a final determinatio=
n on
the merits, notwithstanding a lack of confirmation of the award.&quot; </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HGD0-00B1-D1P8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Jacobson v.
Fireman's Fund Ins. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HGD0-00B1-D1P8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 111 F.3d 26=
1,
267-68 (2d Cir. 1997)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed);
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D280-003B-54P1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Benjamin v.
Traffic Exec. Ass'n E. Railroads</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D280-003B-54P1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 869 F.2d 10=
7,
110 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;[T]he findings of
arbitration boards can serve as the basis for collateral estoppel in a fede=
ral
court proceeding.&quot;). In fact, the No-Fault Act specifically provides t=
hat
&quot;[a]n award by an arbitrator shall be binding.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Ins. Law
&sect; 5106(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Because the arbitrations and state-court proceedings h=
ere
could determine that certain claims were reimbursable based on findings of
medical necessity for individual patients, those decisions could have a
preclusive effect in this action, thereby preventing complete relief to Sta=
te
Farm if it is so entitled. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i>=
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D280-003B-54P1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Benjamin</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D280-003B-54P1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 869 F.2d at=
 114</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting a sister circuit's decision holding that &quot;arbitration findings=
 may
be given collateral estoppel effect over issues in a RICO claim&quot;). Of
course, whether such findings will in fact be preclusive will depend on the
precise circumstances of the proceedings. For example, courts determining t=
he
preclusive effect of arbitrations consider whether the procedures employed
adequately protected the rights of the parties&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; and whethe=
r an
arbitrator's decision and rationale can be determined with &quot;clarity and
certainty.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TF-4910-0038-X15T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Postlewaite v.
McGraw-Hill</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TF-4910-0038-X15T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 333 F.3d 42,
48-49 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks and
emphasis omitted). But it is premature at this point to attempt to ascertain
the definitive preclusive effect of such proceedings because our task at th=
is
juncture is solely to determine whether there is a sufficient showing of a =
risk
of irreparable harm absent an injunction. We believe there is.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Those
harms also threaten to continue absent an injunction. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_0"></a>To establish irreparable harm, the mo=
ving
party must show &quot;a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>continuing<=
/i>
harm which cannot be adequately redressed by final relief on the merits.&qu=
ot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kamerling</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:467J-2RN0-0038-X36R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 295 F.3d at=
 214</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted and emphasis added). Without the prelimin=
ary
injunction currently in place, Defendants would continue bringing new actio=
ns
to recover the remaining unpaid claims for No-Fault benefits, which total
around $9 million as of December 2021. State Farm contends that Defendants =
use
those very proceedings &quot;to monetize their fraud against the State Farm
Companies,&quot; such that they are &quot;essentially financing their
fraudulent practices with proceeds paid by&quot; State Farm. Appellees' Br.
37-38. Thus, we conclude that State Farm has sufficiently&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp;
demonstrated a risk of irreparable harm that threatens to continue absent an
injunction.</span></p>

