<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>4. Public Interest</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Fourth, we consider whether the
preliminary injunction is in the public interest, which concerns the
&quot;public consequences in employing the extraordinary remedy of
injunction.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Yang, 960 F.3d at
135-36</i> (internal quotation marks omitted). Preventing fraud, especially=
 the
kind of elaborate and complex RICO scheme alleged here, is plainly in the
public interest. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4R-W3H1-F04K-J00J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allstate Ins.=
 Co.
v. Mun</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4R-W3H1-F04K-J00J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 751 F.3d 94,
100 (2d Cir. 2014)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (stating that &quot;the prevent=
ion
of insurance fraud for the protection of consumers in New York&quot; is an
&quot;important public policy interest&quot; (internal quotation marks
omitted)); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:549N-XBG1-F04J-602F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Perl v. Meher=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:549N-XBG1-F04J-602F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 18 N.Y.3d 2=
08,
214, 960 N.E.2d 424, 936 N.Y.S.2d 655 (2011)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;No-fault abuse still abounds today. In 2010, no-fault accounted for =
53%
of&nbsp;[**44]&nbsp; all fraud reports received by the Insurance
Department.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>New
York's No-Fault regime is aimed at ensuring prompt compensation for victims=
 of
automobile accidents without regard for fault. Insurers are integral compon=
ents
of that expedited regime as they are uniquely positioned to combat the
depletion of public resources caused by fraudulent claims for No-Fault
benefits. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16"></a>To that end, New York req=
uires
most insurers to implement anti-fraud measures by preparing plans &quot;for=
 the
detection, investigation and prevention of fraudulent insurance
activities&quot; in the state. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11N1-6RDJ-851C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Ins. Law
&sect; 409(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Among other things, the fraud prevention plans must
provide for the &quot;coordination with other units of an insurer for the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>investigation and initiation of civil
actions</i> based upon information received by or through the[ir] special
investigation unit.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> <=
/span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11N1-6RDJ-851C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 409(c)=
(4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added). That public interest in detecting and preventing insurance
fraud is thwarted, however, when insurers like State Farm find themselves
facing thousands of arbitrations and state-court proceedings for the
reimbursement &nbsp;[*86]&nbsp; of individual claims for No-Fault benefits =
into
which &quot;[c]omplex fraud and RICO claims . . . cannot be shoehorned.&quo=
t; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4R-W3H1-F04K-J00J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mun</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4R-W3H1-F04K-J00J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 751 F.3d at=
 99</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, we conclude that&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; the district court did not err in
granting a preliminary injunction to preserve State Farm's ability to prove=
 its
allegations of a complex fraudulent scheme involving insurance benefits, wh=
ich
is clearly in the public interest. The district court therefore did not abu=
se
its discretion in determining that a preliminary injunction was warranted h=
ere.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>5. Security Bond</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the MRI and ASC Defendants argue that the district court erred in declining=
 to
require State Farm to post a bond.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn10'
href=3D"#_ftn10" name=3D"_ftnref10" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We disagree. </span><a
