on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 65(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> provides that a &quot;court may
issue a preliminary injunction . . . only if the movant gives security in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>an amount that the court considers pro=
per</i>
to pay the costs and damages sustained by any party found to have been
wrongfully enjoined or restrained.&quot; (Emphasis added). Courts have the
discretion, however, to require no security at all depending on the specific
circumstances. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4C71-Y9R0-0038-X3TX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
