></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Having
determined that the district court's grant of the preliminary injunction me=
ets
the standards generally applicable to such provisional relief, we turn to
Defendants' remaining contention, that the FAA prohibits injunctions barring
them from instituting or proceeding with arbitrations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19"></a>That is a question&nbsp;[**47]&nbsp; o=
f law
that we review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo. See </i></s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GCK-FCR1-F04K-J04K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Briggs</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GCK-FCR1-F04K-J04K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 792 F.3d at=
 241</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Originally enacted in 1925, the FAA aimed to &quot;reverse the longstanding
judicial hostility to arbitration agreements that had . . . been adopted by
American courts, and to place arbitration agreements upon the same footing =
as
other contracts.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gilmer v.
Interstate/Johnson Lane Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 500 U.S. 20,
24, 111 S. Ct. 1647, 114 L. Ed. 2d 26 (1991)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20"></a>Accordingly, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6572-SW63-GXF6-82NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 2 of =
the
FAA</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> &nbsp;[*87]&nbsp; provides that agreements to arbitrate
controversies arising out of a contract &quot;shall be valid, irrevocable, =
and
enforceable, save upon such grounds as exist at law or in equity for the
revocation of any contract.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6572-SW63-GXF6-82NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9 U.S.C. &sec=
t; 2</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T292-D6RV-H4BN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 3 of =
the
FAA</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> then &quot;provides for stays of proceedings in federal
district courts when an issue in the proceeding is referable to
arbitration,&quot; and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T292-D6RV-H4BP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 4</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides &quot;for orders compelling arbitration when one party has failed,
neglected, or refused to comply with an arbitration agreement.&quot; </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gilmer</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 500 U.S. at=
 25</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T292-D6RV-H4BN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 3-4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Those provisions evince a
&quot;policy [designed] to make arbitration agreements as enforceable as ot=
her
contracts, but not more so.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65HF-YSS1-F361-M04T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morgan v.
Sundance, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65HF-YSS1-F361-M04T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 596 U.S. 41=
1,
418, 142 S. Ct. 1708, 212 L. Ed. 2d 753 (2022)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>New York's No-Fault Act specifies
that &quot;[e]very insurer shall provide a claimant with the option of
submitting any dispute involving the insurer's liability to pay first party
benefits, or additional first party benefits, the amount thereof or any oth=
er
matter which&nbsp;[**48]&nbsp; may arise pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this section to arbitration pursuant to simplified procedures.&quot; </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Ins. Law
&sect; 5106(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The No-Fault Act's implementing regulations further
require that insurers include a &quot;[m]andatory personal injury protection
endorsement&quot; in their liability policies providing that &quot;[i]n the
event any person making a claim for first-party benefits and the [insurer] =
do
not agree regarding any matter relating to the claim, such person shall have
the option of submitting such disagreement to arbitration.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67K1-VMD1-JJK6-S51X-00009-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Comp. Co=
des
R. &amp; Regs. tit. 11, &sect; 65-1.1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The automobile
insurance policies State Farm issued to Defendants' patients complied with =
the
&quot;statutory requirement of providing [State Farm's] insureds with the
