eply
Br. at 1.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc22"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We apply &quot;a two-part test to
determine the arbitrability of claims,&quot; considering &quot;(1) whether =
the
parties have entered into a valid agreement to arbitrate, and, if so, (2)
whether the dispute at issue comes within the scope of the arbitration
agreement.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:83JN-96J1-652R-0203-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Am. Exp=
ress
Fin. Advisors Sec. Litig. (&quot;American Express&quot;)</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:83JN-96J1-652R-0203-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 672 F.3d 11=
3,
128 (2d Cir. 2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The parties here dispute only =
the
first part of this test, disagreeing about whether the arbitration agreement
was &quot;privately negotiated&quot; and therefore valid and enforceable. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23"></a>The Supreme Court has&nbsp;[**49]&nbsp;
acknowledged that &quot;the purpose behind [the FAA's] passage was to ensure
judicial enforcement of privately made agreements to arbitrate,&quot; and t=
he
FAA therefore provides for the enforcement of &quot;privately negotiated
arbitration agreements.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C5Y0-0039-N194-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dean Witter
Reynolds, Inc. v. Byrd</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C5Y0-0039-N194-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 470 U.S. 21=
3,
219, 105 S. Ct. 1238, 84 L. Ed. 2d 158 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
That axiom recognizes that &quot;an obligation to arbitrate can be based on=
ly
on consent,&quot; because like any other contract, a party cannot be requir=
ed
to arbitrate a dispute that it has not agreed to arbitrate. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TGF-J800-TX4N-G1J6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sokol Holding=
s,
Inc. v. BMB Munai, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TGF-J800-TX4N-G1J6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 542 F.3d 35=
4,
358 (2d Cir. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, it is &quot;essentially =
only
by making a commitment to arbitrate that one gives up the right of access t=
o a
court of law in favor of arbitration,&quot; which is most frequently done
&quot;by entering into an agreement to arbitrate or by entering into a
relationship which is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>governed</i> =
by an
agreement to arbitrate.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</=
i>
(emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
the district court agreed with State Farm that because the arbitration
provision is mandated by New York law, it is not &quot;privately
negotiated&quot; and is therefore unenforceable under the FAA. Defendants
respond that the arbitration provision is &quot;privately negotiated&quot;
because &nbsp;[*88]&nbsp; State Farm chose to do business in New York and
therefore agreed to be bound by its laws, including the No-Fault Act.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
parties' and district court's&nbsp;[**50]&nbsp; focus on the &quot;privately
negotiated&quot; requirement, however, is misguided. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24"></a>Because &quot;[p]ersons are generally
entitled to have their dispute settled by the ruling of a court of law,&quo=
t;
the &quot;privately negotiated&quot; requirement is aimed at ensuring that
persons are subjected to arbitration only if they have <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>consented</i> to arbitrate. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>See
id.</i> (&quot;It is black letter law that an obligation to arbitrate can be
based only on consent.&quot;). In other words, this requirement is intended=
 to
protect parties who may have been coerced or defrauded into forgoing their
opportunity to bring claims in court, not to render arbitration agreements
invalid simply because they were mandated by state insurance law. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:83JN-96J1-652R-0203-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>American Expr=
ess</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:83JN-96J1-652R-0203-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 672 F.3d at=
 128</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that a company had &quot;consented to arbitrate with its
customers&quot; &quot;by virtue of its membership in&quot; a self-regulatory
organization).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>That
understanding of the &quot;privately negotiated&quot; requirement finds fur=
ther
support in other cases concluding that even adhesion contracts containing
arbitration provisions that were offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis are=
 not
necessarily invalid. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., </i=
></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gilmer</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KT70-003B-R1P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 500 U.S. at=
 33</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(holding that an arbitration agreement was enforceable even when an employer
required arbitration because there was&nbsp;[**51]&nbsp; no indication that=
 the
employee was &quot;coerced or defrauded into agreeing to the arbitration
clause&quot;); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XTK-VXG0-YB0V-D03Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ragone v. Atl.
Video Manhattan Ctr.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XTK-VXG0-YB0V-D03Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 595 F.3d 11=
5,
122 (2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (rejecting the argument that an
arbitration agreement was signed under &quot;procedurally unconscionable&qu=
ot;
conditions because it was offered on a take-it-or-leave-it basis). We have =
even
enforced arbitration agreements against non-signatories in certain situatio=
ns. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N27-MPB0-TVRV-1320-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ross v. Am.
Express Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N27-MPB0-TVRV-1320-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 478 F.3d 96=
, 99
(2d Cir. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (noting that this Court has &quot;recognized a number of
common law principles of contract law that may allow non-signatories to enf=
orce
an arbitration agreement&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc25"></a>Th=
us,
the crux of the &quot;privately negotiated&quot; requirement is to ensure t=
hat
parties consent to arbitration and are not coerced or defrauded into agreei=
ng
to arbitrate.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>There
was no such coercion here. It is undisputed that State Farm and the insureds
knowingly agreed to enter into insurance policies that included a provision=
 to
arbitrate certain disputes regarding No-Fault benefits. There is no allegat=
ion
that the arbitration provision was extracted by fraud, coercion, duress, or=
 in
some other unconscionable way that would render the provision invalid.
Moreover, State Farm <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>chose</i> to do
business in New York, and that choice requires it to comply with New York l=
aw
(including&nbsp;[**52]&nbsp; the No-Fault Act). Thus, State Farm and the
insureds both &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>agreed in advan=
ce</i>
to submit such grievances to arbitration,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7J90-0039-N4TD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AT&amp;T
Technologies, Inc. v. Communications Workers of America</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7J90-0039-N4TD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 475 U.S. 64=
3,
648-49, 106 S. Ct. 1415, 89 L. Ed. 2d 648 (1986)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added), and their relationship is &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>governed</i> by an agreement to arbitrate,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TGF-J800-TX4N-G1J6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sokol</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TGF-J800-TX4N-G1J6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 542 F.3d at=
 358</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>State
Farm would have us hold that an agreement to arbitrate is not &quot;private=
ly
negotiated&quot; solely because governing law mandates such arbitration. In=
 its
view, and in the view of the district court, an arbitration agreement lacks
consent where applicable law requires parties to arbitrate because the
provision was not &quot;bargained for.&quot; We decline to adopt an
interpretation of the &quot;privately negotiated&quot; requirement that wou=
ld
have such sweeping consequences. Not only would such an interpretation call
into question the validity of all sorts of arbitration agreements that are
required by federal or state law, but those &nbsp;[*89]&nbsp; consequences =
may
even extend to other types of contractual terms that are mandated by law. T=
hat
cannot be what Congress intended in enacting the FAA. Thus, given that both
State Farm and the insureds consented to policies that include arbitration
provisions, we conclude that the provisions here are &quot;privately
negotiated&quot; under the FAA.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>But
although we depart&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp; from the district court's conclusion on
this specific issue, we nonetheless agree with the district court that it w=
as
permitted to enjoin the arbitrations, notwithstanding the FAA, because of t=
he
unique circumstances in this case. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc26"></a>=
Although
the FAA provides for the enforcement of arbitration agreements, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6572-SW63-GXF6-82NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 2-4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, there are exceptions to that
general rule. Developed by the Supreme Court, the &quot;effective
vindication&quot; doctrine is a judge-made exception to the FAA that
invalidates, &quot;on public policy grounds, arbitration agreements that
operate as a prospective waiver of a party's right to pursue statutory
remedies.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Am. Express C=
o.
v. Italian Colors Rest.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 570 U.S. 22=
8,
235, 133 S. Ct. 2304, 186 L. Ed. 2d 417 (2013)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks, alterations, and emphasis omitted).</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn11' href=3D"#_ftn11" name=3D"_ftnref11" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The &quot;effective vindication&quot; exception to the FAA originated in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mitsubishi Motors Corp. v. Soler
Chrysler-Plymouth, Inc.</i>, where the Supreme Court noted that although
statutory claims can be the subject of arbitration agreements, &quot;not . =
. .
all controversies implicating statutory rights are suitable for
arbitration.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9WR0-0039-N476-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>473 U.S. 614,
627, 105 S. Ct. 3346, 87 L. Ed. 2d 444 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court further explained that &quot;[b]y agreeing to arbitrate a statuto=
ry
claim, a party does not forgo the substantive rights afforded by the statut=
e;
it only submits to their resolution in an arbitral,&nbsp;[**54]&nbsp; rather
than a judicial, forum.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9WR0-0039-N476-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9WR0-0039-N476-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 628</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65PC-D1W1-JXG3-X3N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Viking River
Cruises, Inc. v. Moriana</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65PC-D1W1-JXG3-X3N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 596 U.S. 63=
9,
653, 142 S. Ct. 1906, 213 L. Ed. 2d 179 (2022)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;An arbitration agreement thus does not alter or abridge substantive
rights; it merely changes how those rights will be processed.&quot;).
Therefore, &quot;so long as the prospective litigant <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>effectively may vindicate</i> its statutory cause of action in the
arbitral forum, the statute will continue to serve both its remedial and
deterrent function.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9WR0-0039-N476-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitsubishi Mo=
tors</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9WR0-0039-N476-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. at=
 637</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc27"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The &quot;effective
vindication&quot; exception serves as a way to &quot;reconcile[] the . . . =
FAA
. . . with all the rest of federal law&quot; and &quot;prevent arbitration
clauses from choking off a plaintiff's ability to enforce congressionally
created rights.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Italian Color=
s</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 570 U.S. at=
 240</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Kagan, J., dissenting). The exception therefore furthers the purposes of t=
he
FAA, which &quot;reflects a federal policy favoring&quot; the enforcement of
valid contracts for arbitration &#8212; &quot;that is, arbitration as a
streamlined 'method of resolving disputes,' not as a foolproof way of killi=
ng
off valid claims.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58PH-0TS1-F04K-F00W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 243-44</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BCM0-003B-423N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rodriguez de
Quijas v. Shearson/American Express, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BCM0-003B-423N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 490 U. S. 4=
77,
481, 109 S. Ct. 1917, 104 L. Ed. 2d 526 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The exception accordingly stems &nbsp;[*90]&nbsp; from the principle that o=
ther
federal statutes are on equal footing with the FAA.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
the Supreme Court has so far declined to apply the &quot;effective
vindication&quot; exception in any case before it, it has
repeatedly&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp; reaffirmed the existence of the exception. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., 14 </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VYX-9940-TXFX-12TC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
