style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morgan</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:65HF-YSS1-F361-M04T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 596 U.S. at=
 418</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc30"></a>Ultimately, however, arbitration is an=
 issue
of contract, and the &quot;effective vindication&quot; exception intends to
balance &nbsp;[*92]&nbsp; the liberal federal policy favoring the enforceme=
nt
of valid arbitration agreements with the recognition that other federal
statutes are on equal footing with the FAA. It does so by permitting
interference with arbitration in limited circumstances where, as here,
arbitration would deny State Farm the effective vindication of its federal
statutory claims under RICO. We therefore conclude that the &quot;effective
vindication&quot; exception&nbsp;[**60]&nbsp; to the FAA applies here, allo=
wing
the district court to stay the pending arbitrations and enjoin any new
arbitrations. We emphasize that this case presents unusual circumstances th=
at
drive our conclusion that the &quot;effective vindication&quot; exception is
applicable; namely, the thousands of allegedly baseless arbitrations that h=
elp
to further the massive and complex RICO scheme alleged here. Thus, as to
Defendants' appeal, we conclude that the district court did not abuse its
discretion in granting a preliminary injunction barring the arbitrations.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C. Anti-Injunction Act</span></i=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
we turn to State Farm's cross-appeal. State Farm contends that the district
court erred in declining to include in the preliminary injunction a provisi=
on
enjoining Defendants from proceeding with already-pending state-court
litigation, on the ground that the AIA bars federal courts from enjoining s=
uch
state-court proceedings. State Farm argues that various exceptions to the A=
IA's
general prohibition on such injunctions apply to the unusual facts of this
case. For the reasons set forth below, we agree that one of those exceptions
applies here.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn13' href=3D"#_ftn13"
name=3D"_ftnref13" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc31"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Almost as old as the Constitutio=
n,
the AIA today provides that &quot;[a] court&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp; of the United
States may not grant an injunction to stay proceedings in a State court exc=
ept
as expressly authorized by Act of Congress, or where necessary in aid of its
jurisdiction, or to protect or effectuate its judgments.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
2283</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. The AIA reflects our &quot;dual system of federal and
state courts,&quot; and its &quot;core message is one of respect for state
courts.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:533S-JFW1-F04K-F4C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Smith v. Bayer
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:533S-JFW1-F04K-F4C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 564 U.S. 29=
9,
306, 131 S. Ct. 2368, 180 L. Ed. 2d 341 (2011)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). Application of the AIA is limited only =
by
the &quot;three specifically defined exceptions&quot; in the statute that a=
re
&quot;narrow,&quot; so &quot;[a]ny doubts as to the propriety of a federal
injunction against state court proceedings should be resolved in favor of
permitting the state courts to proceed.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Id.</i> (internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
this case, the district court declined to enjoin the pending state-court
proceedings, concluding that no exceptions to the AIA apply here. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc32"></a>Application of the AIA is a question o=
f law
that we review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo. See </i></s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56SJ-JYC1-F04K-J22W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wyly</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56SJ-JYC1-F04K-J22W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 697 F.3d at=
 137</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In its cross-appeal, State Farm contends that the district court erred beca=
use
the first and second exceptions to the AIA, which respectively allow
injunctions of state-court proceedings &quot;as expressly authorized by Act=
 of
Congress&quot; or &quot;where necessary in aid of its
jurisdiction,&quot;&nbsp;[**62]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
2283</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, are applicable. We agree with State Farm that the
preliminary injunction here falls within the &quot;expressly-authorized&quo=
t;
exception, and we therefore have no occasion to decide whether the second
exception also applies.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc33"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As mentioned, the first exceptio=
n to
the AIA allows federal courts to enjoin state-court proceedings when
&quot;expressly authorized by Act of Congress.&quot; &nbsp;[*93]&nbsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Although that language would s=
eem to
require Congress to have (1) expressly authorized (2) injunctions of
state-court proceedings in the relevant federal statute, the Supreme Court =
held
in the seminal case of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mitchum v. F=
oster</i>
that &quot;no prescribed formula is required&quot; for the exception to app=
ly,
and that the federal statute &quot;need not expressly refer to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0VY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 2283</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
nor &quot;expressly authorize an injunction of a state court proceeding in
order to qualify as an exception.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>407 U.S. 225,
237, 92 S. Ct. 2151, 32 L. Ed. 2d 705 (1972)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc34"></a=
>Instead,
for the exception to apply, &quot;Congress must have created a specific and
uniquely federal right or remedy, enforceable in a federal court of equity,
that could be frustrated if the federal court were not empowered to enjoin a
state court proceeding.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id. M=
itchum</i>
accordingly created a two-part test providing that the
&quot;expressly-authorized&quot;&nbsp;[**63]&nbsp; exception to the AIA app=
lies
if Congress &quot;[1] clearly creat[ed] a federal right or remedy enforceab=
le
in a federal court of equity, [2] [that] could be given its intended scope =
only
by the stay of a state court proceeding.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 238</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mitchum</i>, the Supreme Court held th=
at an
injunction of state-court proceedings under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> fell within the scope of that exception even when the
statute merely authorized suits in equity without any mention of state-court
proceedings. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 243</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In arriving at that conclusion, the Court relied on </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
legislative history which provided that &quot;[t]he very purpose of </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>[Section] 198=
3</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
was to interpose the federal courts between the States and the people, as
guardians of the people's federal rights&#8212;to protect the people from
unconstitutional action under color of state law, whether that action be
executive, legislative, or judicial.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 242</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). Congress was concerned that &quot;state
instrumentalities could not protect&quot; the federally created rights,
highlighting that both state officers and courts might &quot;be antipatheti=
c to
the vindication of those rights.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Id.</i>
More specifically, proponents of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
had &quot;noted that state courts were being used to harass and injure
individuals,&nbsp;[**64]&nbsp; either because the state courts were powerle=
ss
to stop deprivations or were in league with those who were bent upon abroga=
tion
of federally protected rights.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 240</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Based on that legislative history, the Supreme Court concluded that injunct=
ions
of state-court proceedings under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
fell within the &quot;expressly-authorized&quot; exception to the AIA. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 243</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Since
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mitchum</i>, the Supreme Court has
addressed that issue only in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo Co. v.
Lektro-Vend Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 433 U.S. 62=
3,
97 S. Ct. 2881, 53 L. Ed. 2d 1009 (1977)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the Court split three ways on whether <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>Section
16 of the Clayton Act</i> fell within the &quot;expressly-authorized&quot;
exception. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc35"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Section
16 of the Clayton Act</i> authorizes federal courts to issue injunctions
against the &quot;threatened loss or damage by a violation of the antitrust
laws.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>15 U.S.C. &sect; 26</i>.
Justice Rehnquist authored the lead plurality opinion in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and was joined by Justices Stewart and Powell in concluding that even thoug=
h <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Section 16</i> satisfied the first par=
t of
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mitchum</i> test as a uniquely
federal right or remedy, the statute failed the second part of the test bec=
ause
the Clayton Act could be given its intended scope without staying the
state-court proceedings there. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
