1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, which evinced concern that &quot;state courts were
powerless to stop deprivations or were in league with those who were
&nbsp;[*97]&nbsp; bent upon abrogation&nbsp;[**72]&nbsp; of federally prote=
cted
rights,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitchum</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 407 U.S. at=
 240</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and accordingly authorized equitable relief. Therefore, the similarities
between RICO's purpose and function and those of the Clayton Act and </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> bolster our conclusion that RICO is a sufficiently spec=
ific
and unique federal remedy under the first part of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitchum</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
test.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn16' href=3D"#_ftn16" name=3D"_ft=
nref16"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>13</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Turning
to the second part of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitchum</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
test, we conclude that RICO could be given its intended scope only by a sta=
y of
the hundreds of pending state-court proceedings here. As discussed in detail
above, a majority of the Supreme Court in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
believed that, where multiple meritless proceedings are a part of a scheme =
to
violate the relevant federal law in order to give that law its intended sco=
pe,
an injunction of state-court proceedings could fall within the
&quot;expressly-authorized&quot; exception. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>See</i>
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>433 U.S. at
643-45</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (Blackmun, J., concurring); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 645-66</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Stevens, J., dissenting). Per Justice Blackmun's controlling opinion, the
narrow path carved by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies only in rare circumstances where the state-court proceedings are
&quot;themselves part of a pattern of baseless, repetitive claims that are
being used&quot; to further a violation of&nbsp;[**73]&nbsp; the relevant
federal law. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 644</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Blackmun, J., concurring) (internal quotation marks omitted). The instant
action presents exactly the kind of injunction that a majority of the </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Court had contemplated could fall within the &quot;expressly-authorized&quo=
t;
exception.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
State Farm contends that &quot;Defendants are using a pattern of baseless s=
tate
no-fault proceedings to monetize and perpetuate their violations of RICO&qu=
ot;
in order &quot;to harass and injure&quot; State Farm. Appellees' Br. 64. St=
ate
Farm elaborates that &quot;the state-court litigation is a component of the
overall fraud scheme alleged in the federal action; it is Defendants' means=
 of
collecting on their fraudulent claims for &nbsp;[*98]&nbsp; reimbursement a=
nd
provides a financial lifeline for their scheme.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Id.</i> Thus, State Farm alleges that the hundreds of frivolous
state-court proceedings <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>themselves<=
/i>
help to further the RICO violation because Defendants use the fraudulently
obtained No-Fault benefits from those proceedings to continue financing the=
ir
scheme, thereby constituting a &quot;pattern of baseless, repetitive
claims.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 433 U.S. at=
 644</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Blackmun, J., concurring) (internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
agree with State Farm that the unusual circumstances here fall squarely
within&nbsp;[**74]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-99X0-003B-S1CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vendo</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The state-court proceedings further the alleged RICO violation in two key w=
ays.
First, the proceedings help finance the purported scheme because whenever S=
tate
Farm denies claims for No-Fault benefits, Defendants typically commence
allegedly baseless litigation in state court (or arbitration actions) to
fraudulently obtain the benefits. Second, because the state-court proceedin=
gs
often concern individual claims, the piecemeal nature of those proceedings
obscures the overall complex scheme, which becomes apparent only when one z=
ooms
out to view the alleged predetermined treatment protocols and
&quot;pay-to-play&quot; arrangements relevant to several claims. In other
words, State Farm plausibly argues that the state courts are unlikely to ev=
en
recognize the alleged massive RICO scheme because those proceedings often
concern individual claims. That renders the &quot;state courts . . . powerl=
ess
to stop&quot; the alleged RICO violations which the state-court proceedings
help monetize and sustain. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitchum</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 407 U.S. at=
 240</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
thus conclude that state litigation of the kind alleged here &#8212; involv=
ing
hundreds of baseless state-court proceedings that are part of a massive
fraudulent scheme &#8212; may itself further a RICO violation,
which&nbsp;[**75]&nbsp; in this case consists of alleged repeated violation=
s of
the federal mail fraud statute, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn17' href=3D"#_ftn=
17"
name=3D"_ftnref17" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> If the pending
state-court proceedings are allowed to continue, State Farm faces the
irreparable harm of never having a forum to demonstrate the existence of
Defendants' alleged RICO scheme. Thus, the only way to give RICO its intend=
ed
scope in the specific circumstances here is by staying the pending state-co=
urt
proceedings.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Given
that both parts of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D6J0-003B-S2JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mitchum</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
test are therefore satisfied, we conclude that enjoining the pending
state-court proceedings here falls within the &quot;expressly-authorized&qu=
ot;
exception to the AIA. We again note that our decision today is
&nbsp;[*99]&nbsp; narrow and limited to the unusual circumstances alleged h=
ere,
which involve a massive scheme including hundreds of purportedly meritless
state-court proceedings that help further a RICO violation, such that RICO =
can
only be given its intended scope if those proceedings are enjoined.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn18' href=3D"#_ftn18" name=3D"_ftnref18" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<a name=3D"Bookmark_5WD24TVSBD000000T500001"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
weight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
have considered all of the parties' remaining arguments and conclude that t=
hey
are without merit. For the foregoing reasons, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
weight:
normal'>REVERSE</b> the district court's orders declining to enjoin the pen=
ding
state-court proceedings,&nbsp;[**76]&nbsp; <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight=
:normal'>AFFIRM</b>
its orders in all other respects, and <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:norm=
al'>REMAND</b>
the matter for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

