k'>Prior
History:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;Plaintiff-Appellant David Monk challenges the dist=
rict
court's dismissal of his </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
securities fraud case for failure to state a claim. Monk alleges that
materially misleading proxy materials issued by Shanda Games Limited
(&quot;Shanda&quot;) as part of a freeze-out merger caused him to accept the
merger price instead of exercising his appraisal rights. The United States
District Court for the Southern District of New York (Carter, J.) dismissed
Monk's claims on the ground that he failed properly to allege loss causatio=
n.
We hold that the district court erred in dismissing Monk's claims. We agree=
 with
the district court that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4S-6801-F04K-J00K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>City of Ponti=
ac
Policemen's and Firemen's Retirement System v. UBS AG, 752 F.3d 173 (2d Cir.
2014)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>, created a limited exception (for foreign securities
purchased on foreign exchanges) to the general rule, set forth in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YSN-SP50-YB0V-90M7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morrison v. N=
at'l
Australia Bank Ltd., 561 U.S. 247 (2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, that
&quot;transactions in securities listed on a domestic exchange&quot; fall
within the scope of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
as this case does. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5C4S-6801-F04K-J00K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>City of Ponti=
ac,
752 F.3d at 179-181</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YSN-SP50-YB0V-90M7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morrison, 561
U.S. at 267</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>). We further conclude that Monk has adequately alleged
material misstatements; that federal law governs the adverse interest excep=
tion
to imputation for scienter in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;
cases; and that the scienter of the conflicted directors&#8212;who had the
motive and opportunity to deflate the merger price for their own financial
gain&#8212;can be imputed to the&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; company. We also agree wi=
th
the district court that Monk may invoke the rebuttable presumption of
fraud-on-the-market to satisfy transaction causation because he relied on t=
he
market price to decide not to exercise his appraisal rights. We disagree wi=
th
the district court's conclusion with respect to certain misrepresentations =
and
on loss causation and hold that Monk adequately pleaded loss causation. We
therefore AFFIRM in part and VACATE in part the judgment of the district co=
urt.</span></p>

