<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
Amortization&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; &amp; Depreciation</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
conclude, with the district court, that Monk's next allegation of a material
misrepresentation <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>is</i> well-plead=
ed.
Monk contends that it was materially misleading to describe the process use=
d to
create the March 2015 Projections as &quot;reasonable&quot; and reflecting =
the
&quot;best available estimates&quot; when the method used to calculate
amortization and depreciation in preparing the estimates violated basic and
&nbsp;[*45]&nbsp; accepted accounting principles that a reasonable investor
would assume were followed. App'x 769, &para; 187; App'x 772-73, &para; 201;
App'x 775, &para; 215. For the following reasons, we agree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
the start, Monk <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>has</i> adequately
alleged that basic accounting principles were violated in the preparation of
the March 2015 Projections. Prior to the Freeze-Out Merger, Shanda had
estimated depreciation and amortization based on the estimated useful life =
of
the assets. But as the Complaint alleges, for the March 2015 Projections,
Shanda used a new method, one which &quot;assumed that depreciation and
amortization would grow at a rate that was a function of revenue growth&quo=
t;
in violation &quot;of basic accounting principles.&quot; App'x 761, &para; =
169.
This method resulted in the impossible outcome of the book value of Shanda's
capital assets&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; becoming negative in 2018. App'x 761-62,
&para; 170.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST62D6MX30040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To be sure, Shanda was permitted=
 to
choose any accepted accounting method while characterizing its process for
preparing the Projections as reasonable. But it was limited to <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>accepted</i> methods. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Cf. </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST62D6MX30030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690Y-7JH1-F5DR-23KK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New Eng. Carp=
.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690Y-7JH1-F5DR-23KK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 80 F.4th at=
 170</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;[A] plaintiff will be unable to establish that [a statement] is false
merely by showing that other <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>reason=
able</i>
alternative views exist. Where those alternatives exist, the speaker making=
 the
statement (expressing an opinion) can choose among them . . . .&quot; (emph=
asis
added)). This is because a reasonable investor assured by Shanda that the
Projections were reasonably prepared would infer that basic accounting
principles were followed. As Judge Kaplan has aptly explained:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
