ded
to Merrill Lynch and to the Buyer Group.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>D. Statements Concerning Fairnes=
s</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Monk
next argues that the statements in both the Initial Proxy and the Final Pro=
xy
that describe the Freeze-Out Merger as fair were misleading opinion stateme=
nts
because the Defendants disbelieved the statements and the statements did not
align with the information in their possession. For the following reasons, =
we
agree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
the start, we <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>disagree</i> with Mon=
k that
the testimony of Shanda's expert witness at the Appraisal Action provides a
basis on which we may infer that Shanda did not believe the fairness statem=
ents
when it issued the Proxies. In the Proxies, Shanda opined that the merger p=
rice
of $7.10 per ADS was fair. During the Appraisal Action in the Cayman Island=
s,
Shanda's valuation expert &quot;found that as of November 18, 2015, when the
Transaction closed, the fair value of Shanda's equity was $9.56 per ADS . .=
 .
.&quot; App'x 758, &para; 158. Monk&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; argues that
&quot;Shanda's admission by way of the expert report it proffered . . .
indicates its belief that $7.10 was unfair.&quot; Appellant's Br. at 55. But
this is incorrect.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST928T4JC0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST928T4JC0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Monk directs us to </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST928T4JC0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3Y74-31K0-0038-Y13K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kreppel v.
Guttman Breast Diagnostic Institute</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3Y74-31K0-0038-Y13K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 95-cv-1=
0830
(SWK) (MHD), 1999 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 19602, 1999 WL 1243891 (S.D.N.Y. Dec. 21,
1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>, which held that an expert's testimony may be admitted =
as
an adoptive admission under the Federal Rules of Evidence. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92D6MY00020000400"></a>Monk extrapolates from </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST928T4JC0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3Y74-31K0-0038-Y13K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kreppel</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
holding about admissions under the Federal Rules of Evidence to suggest that
&quot;when a party proffers an expert report it may be treated as the parti=
es'
opinion.&quot; Appellant's Br. at 55. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92D6MY00020000400_2"></a>But </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92D6MY00020000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92D6MY00010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3Y74-31K0-0038-Y13K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kreppel</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
itself explains that one of the reasons that an expert's opinion can be an
adoptive admission is because the party &quot;was obviously fully aware of =
[the
expert's] opinion and analysis by no later than the date on which its couns=
el
received the report, and that [the party] chose nonetheless to proffer&quot;
the witness. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92D6MY00030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3Y74-31K0-0038-Y13K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1999 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 19602, [WL] at *1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Here, even if we attributed the
expert's views to Shanda, it would simply show that at the time of the
Appraisal Action, eighteen months after the Proxies were published, Shanda
believed that the fair value &nbsp;[*49]&nbsp; of Shanda's equity was $9.56=
 per
ADS. It does not tell us what Shanda believed when the Proxies were publish=
ed. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_0"></a>And for an opinion statement to be
actionable&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp; on the theory that a defendant did not believe=
 it,
the defendant must not have believed the statement <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>when it was made</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Setting
the expert opinion aside, however, we conclude that Monk <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>has</i> adequately pleaded that the fairness opinions in the Initial
Proxy did not align with the information in Shanda's possession when it iss=
ued
that Proxy and that Shanda did not believe that the transaction was fair. M=
onk
has alleged that the Defendants understood that Merrill Lynch's assessment =
of
the deal price was based, in part, on the revenue estimates for MIIM. But we
have already concluded that Monk has adequately alleged that Shanda did not
believe the MIIM estimates to be correct. The same allegations supporting t=
hat
conclusion lead, here, to the determination that Monk has adequately alleged
that the $15 million revenue estimate relied on by Merrill Lynch did not al=
ign
with the information that Shanda had about MIIM's likely success. And by
adopting a fairness opinion that was based on such estimates, Shanda misled=
 the
shareholders.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn28' href=3D"#_ftn28"
name=3D"_ftnref28" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SW003RS0V2005NF"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Relying on a district court opin=
ion,
Shanda argues, to the contrary, that &quot;as a matter of law, Monk cannot =
show
that a statement is misleading based on the&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; unreasonablen=
ess
of underlying projections.&quot; Appellee's Br. at 44 (citing <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0020000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Gray v. Wesco Aircraft Holdings, Inc., 454 F. Supp. 3d 366, 387
(S.D.N.Y. 2020)</i>). We disagree. As an initial matter, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62R0040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Gray</i><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62V0040000400"></a>'s reason=
ing is
based on the PSLRA safe harbor, which is not applicable here. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62V0010000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Gray, 454 F. Supp. 3d at 387, 390</i>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_0"></a>But more importantly, a projection i=
s not
a magic wand that immunizes all statements that relate to that projection. =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62V0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5KTB-BRT1-F04K-J4FB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Vivendi,
S.A. Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5KTB-BRT1-F04K-J4FB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 838 F.3d 22=
3,
246 (2d Cir. 2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92HM62V0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6147-4FJ1-JC0G-63W0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Shreiber</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6147-4FJ1-JC0G-63W0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 832 F. App'=
x at
57</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'> (analyzing statements about future revenue using the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Omnicare</i> framework). We analyze the
fairness statement like any other opinion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
also conclude that Monk has adequately alleged that by the time of the Final
Proxy the Defendants did not believe that the Merger was fair and that
describing the Merger as fair did not align with the information in Shanda's
possession. At that point, MIIM had launched and the game's initial success=
 was
such that the Defendants &quot;could not possibly have believed the Buyout =
was
fair.&quot; Appellant's Br. at 57. Shanda was able to track in-game purchas=
es,
the source of revenue for MIIM, in real time and management reviewed the da=
ta
at least weekly. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92SF8T20030000400"></a>It is
reasonable to infer that Defendants therefore would have known about MIIM's
success because MIIM generated over $90 million each month between its laun=
ch
in August and the issuance of the Final Proxy&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; in October.
Monk thus plausibly alleges that Shanda knew that the fairness opinion was
based on assumptions that had proven incorrect, yet Shanda continued to sta=
te
that the Merger was fair. Accordingly, Monk has adequately pleaded that the=
se
statements are actionable. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i>=
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92SF8T20020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Time Wa=
rner
Inc. Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 9 F.3d 259,=
 266
(2d </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*50]&n=
bsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> Cir. 1993)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(finding opinion statements nonactionable when &quot;the complaint contains=
 no
allegations to support the inference that the defendants either did not have
these favorable opinions on future prospects when they made the statements =
or
that the favorable opinions were without basis in fact&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Shanda
argues that Monk has not adequately alleged that the Special Committee knew
about MIIM's initial revenue figures or that such knowledge would establish
that the Special Committee did not believe the Merger was fair. But this
argument misses the mark. The Special Committee, comprised entirely of Board
members, would have been privy to the same internal monitoring of MIIM's
success as the rest of the Board. According to the Complaint, the Special
Committee and the Board each unanimously determined that the Freeze-Out Mer=
ger
was fair after knowledge of MIIM's immediate success was shared across the
company. And the Final Proxy&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp; continued to reaffirm those
determinations by stating that &quot;'the Company believes' that the
[Freeze-Out Merger] is 'fair to, and in the best interests of' the Unaffili=
ated
Holders.'&quot; App'x 751, &para; 137. The Complaint adequately alleges that
all Board members, including the Special Committee, knew of the problems wi=
th
the March 2015 Projections and thus did not believe the fairness assessment=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Shanda argues that because information about MIIM's immediate success was
available to shareholders before the deadline to dissent and the Proxies
declared that the Projections had not been updated, shareholders could not =
have
been misled. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92SF8T20050000400"></a>We again dis=
agree.
Monk does not claim that the Proxies misled the shareholders into believing
that the MIIM estimates came true. He alleges that the Proxies misled the
shareholders as to Shanda's belief in the March 2015 Projections and the
fairness opinions based on them. The public's knowledge of MIIM's success d=
id
not inform the shareholders of the extent of the inaccuracies in the March =
2015
Projections or that Shanda did not, in fact, believe in their accuracy, or =
in
the Proxies' fairness opinions. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf.=
 </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29ST92SF8T20040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VRC-KX60-0038-X4GH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Koppel v. 4987
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VRC-KX60-0038-X4GH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 167 F.3d 12=
5,
132-33 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that, even though adve=
rse
information&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp; was disclosed in a Solicitation of Consent, t=
he
Solicitation of Consent was misleading because it implied that a recommenda=
tion
considered the adverse information when it did not). For these reasons, Mon=
k's
allegations with regard to statements made about the fairness of the Merger
adequately support a claim for securities fraud. We therefore vacate and re=
mand
as to the district court's determination that these statements were not act=
ionable.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>E. Omissions of MIIM Information=
</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Monk argues that the Individual Defendants are liable for omissions because
Cayman law required them to disclose the success of MIIM, and that Shanda i=
s in
turn liable for those omissions because the Individual Defendants were Shan=
da's
agents. We disagree. Assuming <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>argue=
ndo</i>
that we would impose a duty to disclose based on a relationship established=
 by
Cayman law, we agree with the district court that Cayman law establishes no
such duty here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STB2SF8T80020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Monk's sole citation for the
existence of a disclosure duty is a case from the New York Appellate Divisi=
on
purporting to apply Cayman law. This New York case in turn relies on <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Sharp v. Blank</i>, [2015] EWHC (Ch) 3=
220, a
case from the High Court of Justice of England and&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; Wales.=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STB2SF8T80010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5RX8-TS21-F5DR-2554-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Davis v. Scot=
tish
Re Grp. Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5RX8-TS21-F5DR-2554-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 159 A.D.3d =
528,
74 N.Y.S.3d 10 (N.Y. App. </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5RX8-TS21-F5DR-2554-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*51]&n=
bsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5RX8-TS21-F5DR-2554-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> Div. 1st Dep=
t.
2018)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn29' href=3D"#_ftn=
29"
name=3D"_ftnref29" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_0"></a>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>Sharp</i>,
the High Court held that &quot;although a director of a company can owe
fiduciary duties to the company's shareholders, he does not do so by the me=
re
fact of being a director, but only where there is on the facts of the
particular case a 'special relationship' between the director and the
shareholders.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Sharp v. Blank<=
/i>
[2015] EWHC (Ch) 3220 [12].</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
be sure, the High Court determined that a non-fiduciary relationship between
directors and shareholders <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>does</i>=
 give
rise to a &quot;sufficient information duty, which is expressly accepted to
include a duty not to mislead or conceal information, and a duty to give ad=
vice
and information in clear and readily comprehensible terms.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 15. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STB2SF8T80040000400"></a>But this non-fiduciary
relationship does not necessarily give rise to a duty for a director &quot;=
to
disclose material facts before entering into a transaction with his
principal.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 23(1). =
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_0"></a>We think this statement makes clear =
that
the duty of sufficient information, despite its name, is not a duty to disc=
lose
for the purposes of omission liability under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn30' href=3D"#_ftn30" name=3D"_ftnref30" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> We
thus agree with the district court that Cayman law does not impose a duty to
disclose. Accordingly,&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; because Monk has not adequately
pleaded a disclosure duty, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i>=
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STB2SF8T80030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Time Wa=
rner
Inc. Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 9 F.3d at 2=
67</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the district court did not err in concluding that the failure to disclose t=
he
success of MIIM was not actionable.</span></p>

