by
doing so. The failure to vote thus does not establish that a person in Monk=
's
position made no decision&#8212;and thus did not rely on material
misrepresentations. We therefore conclude that at this stage, Monk may rely=
 on the
fraud-on-the-market presumption. Like all </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
defendants, Shanda and the Individual Defendants may rebut the presumption,=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2N1RWS0030000400"></a><a
