<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>V. Loss Causation</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc32_0"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T00=
10000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs invoking </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
private cause of action must prove not only transaction causation but also =
loss
causation. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2N1RWS0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lentell</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 396 F.3d at=
 172</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Loss causation is &quot;the causal link between the alleged misconduct and =
the
economic harm ultimately suffered by the plaintiff.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49FX-VY30-0038-X15P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Emergent Cap.
Inv. Mgmt., LLC v. Stonepath Grp. Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49FX-VY30-0038-X15P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 343 F.3d 18=
9,
197 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T0050000400_2"></a>It requires that &quot;the
subject of the fraudulent statement or omission was the cause of the actual
loss suffered.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2HM65T0040000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43J7-K8Y0-0038-X1KN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Castellano v.
Young &amp; Rubicam, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43J7-K8Y0-0038-X1KN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 257 F.3d 17=
1,
186 (2d Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks and
citation omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2SF7MB0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As we have previously
explained,&nbsp;[**54]&nbsp; &quot;[t]he transaction effected by a proxy
involves not only the merger of the corporate entities . . . but also the
forfeiture of shareholder's appraisal rights,&quot; so &quot;[t]he injury
sustained by a minority shareholder powerless to affect the outcome of the
merger vote is . . . the loss of his appraisal right.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2SF7MB0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0490-008H-V0T3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wilson</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0490-008H-V0T3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 979 F.2d at=
 931</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In such a situation &quot;loss causation may be established when a proxy
statement prompts a shareholder to accept an unfair exchange ratio for his
shares rather than recoup a greater value through a state appraisal.&quot; =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Monk
has alleged that he suffered an economic loss when he accepted the tender p=
rice
due to the misleading statements in the Proxies instead of receiving a high=
er
value in an appraisal action. App'x 792, &para; 277 (&quot;Plaintiff and Cl=
ass
members suffered economic loss when they sold their Shanda Securities for l=
ess
than those securities were worth. . . . Had the holders of Shanda Securities
not been induced to sell at deflated prices they could have secured the fair
value of their shares through appraisal.&quot;). Under our precedent, such
allegations adequately plead loss causation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Shanda
argues, to the contrary, that Monk cannot allege loss causation because the
tender price&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp; was not affected by the fraud and Monk does =
not
allege that it was. But that misunderstands Monk's theory &nbsp;[*58]&nbsp;=
 of
loss causation. He does not claim that the price he received through the
Freeze-Out Merger would have been higher if not for the misstatements. He
alleges that he would have exercised his appraisal rights and received grea=
ter
value for his shares through an appraisal action.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Shanda
next argues that Monk has failed adequately to plead loss causation because=
 he
has not adequately pleaded that he would have sought appraisal. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK2SF7MB0040000400"></a>But this argument conflates
transaction and loss causation. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc33_0"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STK28T4NM0030000400"></a>Whether the Proxies induced =
the
shareholders to forfeit their appraisal rights is a question of transaction
