<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiff is a minority shareholder who was forced to tender his shares in a
freeze-out merger that (by definition) he was powerless to prevent. The
majority opinion concludes that he can claim reliance on an open market pri=
ce
that was constrained by the merger price--as a basis for challenging his
passive acquiescence to the forced tendering. To that extent I respectfully
dissent. The majority opinion is an unsound extension of a distinguishable =
case
on a close question; but the matter is nonetheless important because, in the
context of a freeze-out merger, it allows a federal securities claim to be
established by pure speculation.</span></p>

