<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005S5"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STR2SF7NF0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I cannot join my colleagues in
sanctioning this theory of causation in this case. Unless the votes of mino=
rity
shareholders (like Monk) are needed for approval of a proposed merger, they
cannot as a matter of law establish a causal link between pre-merger proxy
statements and the approval of the impending merger. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STP2D6N320050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KRF0-003B-R07J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Virginia
Bankshares, Inc. v. Sandberg</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KRF0-003B-R07J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 501 U.S. 10=
83,
1102, 111 S. Ct. 2749, 115 L. Ed. 2d 929 (1991)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The fraud-on-the-market theory presumes reliance when an investment decisio=
n is
made to buy or sell a security at a market price affected&nbsp;[**64]&nbsp;=
 by
fraud; it allows courts to presume a reason (fraud) for an investor's decis=
ion
to enter a transaction, which is evidenced by the transaction itself. But h=
ere,
there was no transaction and thus no investment decision, let alone a devel=
oped
market in which it hypothetically could have been made. We have the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>absence</i> of an investment decision:
Monk's passive holding of his stock while he failed &nbsp;[*62]&nbsp; to
preserve and perfect his appraisal rights. In vindicating Monk's speculative
theory, the majority opinion expands the implied right of action under </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 10(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
the point where reliance in any form is no longer required. Monk's theory of
causation fails as a matter of law.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn40'
href=3D"#_ftn40" name=3D"_ftnref40" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a></p>

