:contentItem:3S65-KRF0-003B-R07J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 501 U.S. at
1105</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>). To avoid that, <u>Grace</u> requires an &nbsp;[*67]&n=
bsp;
affirmative vote in favor of merger before a <u>Wilson</u> claim can be
pursued.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PK"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2N1PNY0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Crucially, Monk does not claim to
have been induced to vote in favor of the merger. Instead, he invokes a fra=
ud
on the market to presume he relied on the proxy statements by taking no act=
ion
whatsoever. Neither this Court nor the Supreme Court has ever expressly
endorsed a lost-state-remedy action based on the fraud-on-the-market
presumption.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn44' href=3D"#_ftn44"
name=3D"_ftnref44" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It is a mistake for the majorit=
y to
do so now. <u>Wilson</u> is a close case that expands a judicially implied
right of action by discounting the element of reliance. The majority opinio=
n is
an expansion of that expansion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PJ"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2D6N3X0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PH"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2D6N3X0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To construct causation, the majo=
rity
opinion burdens the fraud-on-the-market theory with more weight than it can
bear. The doctrine is that &quot;the market price of shares traded on
well-developed markets reflects all publicly available information, and, he=
nce,
any material misrepresentations&quot;; and that the typical &quot;investor =
who
buys or sells stock at the price set by the market does so in reliance on t=
he
integrity of that price.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2D6N3X0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FKK0-003B-409H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Basic</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FKK0-003B-409H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 485 U.S. at
246-47 (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus &quot;reliance on any public material
misrepresentations . . . may be presumed for purposes of a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
action,&quot; <u>id.</u>, if the investor shows &quot;(1) that the alleged
misrepresentations were publicly known, (2) that they were material, (3) th=
at
the stock traded in an efficient market, and (4) that the plaintiff traded =
the
stock between the time the misrepresentations were made and when the truth =
was
revealed.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2D6N3X0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CH0-84P1-F04K-F007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Halliburton C=
o.
v. Erica P. John Fund, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CH0-84P1-F04K-F007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 573 U.S. 25=
8,
268, 134 S. Ct. 2398, 189 L. Ed. 2d 339 (2014)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PG"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The fraud-on-the-market theory is
not fit for purpose in this case. An overt step--generally a stock transact=
ion
in a developed market at the market price--is a premise because hypothetical
traders have no </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 10(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
right of action. The investor's actual purchase or sale of a security at a
price set by&nbsp;[**78]&nbsp; a developed market is needed to connect the
premise (that the market price of a security reflects all public material
misstatements) to the causal inference (that the reason for an investor's
trading decision was reliance on public material misstatements). <u>See </u=
></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2D6N3X0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CH0-84P1-F04K-F007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CH0-84P1-F04K-F007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 274</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;[T]o indirectly rely on a misstatement in the sense relevant for the=
 <u>Basic</u>
presumption, [the investor] need only trade stock based on the belief that =
the
market price will incorporate public information within a reasonable
period.&quot;). There can be no reliance on the market price of a security
without an affirmative investment decision because the theory explains the
investment decision; it does not invent it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
rebuttable presumption that an investment decision relies on a price set by=
 a
developed market is unavailable in this case because <b style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-weight:
normal'>(1)</b> Monk's actual transaction did not occur at a price set by a
market; <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>(2)</b> Monk's hypothetical
action would not have occurred in a developed market; <b style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-weight:
normal'>(3)</b> Monk's holding of his stock was not an actionable investment
decision; and <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>(4)</b> the presumpt=
ion
having thus been drained of any substance, it is not subject to rebuttal.
Consider these reasons one by one.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PF"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0030000400"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-we=
ight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>(1)</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Monk did not &quot;buy[] or sell[]&nbsp;[**79]&nbsp; st=
ock <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>at the price set by the market</i>.&qu=
ot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FKK0-003B-409H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Basic</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FKK0-003B-409H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 485 U.S. at=
 247</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added). <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PC"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0050000400"></a>The only actual stock transa=
ction
was &nbsp;[*68]&nbsp; the cancellation of his shares in a freeze-out merger
that he was powerless to prevent, effected at a privately negotiated price.=
 A
minority shareholder's decision &quot;to hold and then exchange their shares
for a privately negotiated above-market price [is] not of th[e] nature&quot=
; of
transactions supporting the fraud-on-the-market presumption. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STT2HM67R0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:67V1-4YV1-JJYN-B472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Altimeo Asset
Mgmt. v. Qihoo 360 Tech. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:67V1-4YV1-JJYN-B472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 663 F. Supp=
. 3d
334, 366 n.18 (S.D.N.Y. 2023)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Engelmayer, J.). A &quot;passi=
ve[]
claim[],&quot; unsupported by &quot;concrete allegations,&quot; that
&quot;every tenderer shareholder individually consulted and relied upon the
market price in evaluating of the merits of the Merger, the adequacy of the
exchange price, and the wisdom of pursuing the appraisal process&quot; is so
&quot;conclusory&quot; as to be &quot;implausible.&quot; <u>Id.</u></span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(2)</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Even if Monk had decided whether to exercise his appraisal rights, he could=
 not
have done so in a &quot;developed&quot; market. The majority opinion presum=
es
that &quot;most investors who acquire or divest themselves of stock look at=
 the
market price to determine that stock's value.&quot; The majority opinion
further explains that this presumption is appropriate only for &quot;shares
that trade in a developed market.&quot; The majority then&nbsp;[**80]&nbsp;
whizzes past this caveat by adding: &quot;as here.&quot; But &quot;here&quo=
t;
there was no developed market from which to learn of such a price. The mark=
et
price &quot;here&quot; in the lead-up to the merger (when Monk needed to ha=
ve
acted to preserve his appraisal rights) was determined by the merger price
rather than market efficiencies; the buyer group's $7.10 merger price
constrained the market. And the merger price was privately negotiated before
any alleged misrepresentations were publicly disseminated. Once the freeze-=
out
merger incepted, there was no developed market price for Shanda shares--any
more than a tax levy reflects a price set by a developed market. It is a
command datum.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005PB"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2SF7P60020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P9"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2SF7P60040000400"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-we=
ight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>(3)</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Monk's supposed transaction is purely hypothetical. It =
is
not sufficient to say that Monk's failure to act occurred <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>while</i> the fraud affected the market price (even assuming a mark=
et
price in a developed market). None of the cases that Monk cites involved
freeze-out mergers, and all involved actions that the plaintiffs affirmativ=
ely
took.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn45' href=3D"#_ftn45" name=3D"_ft=
nref45"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Monk's descriptions of the fraud-on-the-market theory
acknowledge that an active decision to transact is necessary--a requirement
that sinks his argument. <u>See</u> Appellant's Reply Br. 17 (&quot;<u>Basi=
c&nbsp;[**81]&nbsp;</u>
presumed investors rely on prices when purchasing or selling
securities.&quot;), 18 (&quot;[A]ll investors are presumed to reply on the
market price when trading in relevant securities.&quot;). Monk was no more =
than
a bystander.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P8"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2HM67W0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P7"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2HM67W0030000400"></a><u><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Wilson</span></u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and <u>Grace</u>, the only precedential decisions of this Court addressing
lost-state-remedy actions in the freeze-out merger context, were decided ye=
ars
after the Supreme Court blessed the fraud-on-the-market presumption of reli=
ance
in <u>Basic</u>; yet neither decision mentions the presumption. In <u>Wilso=
n</u>,
minority shareholders alleged that fraudulent proxies induced them to vote =
in
favor of the proposed merger and that &quot;their deceptively procured vote=
[s]
in favor of the merger deprived them of their state appraisal rights.&quot;=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2SF7P60050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0490-008H-V0T3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>979 F.2d at 9=
31</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The holding explicitly referenced the shareholders' affirmative acts that t=
ook
the claim out of the &nbsp;[*69]&nbsp; realm of speculation:
&quot;[T]ransaction causation may be shown when a proxy statement, because =
of
material misrepresentations, causes a shareholder to forfeit his appraisal
rights <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>by voting in favor of the pr=
oposed
corporate merger</i>.&quot; Id. By the same token, Grace rejected a claim b=
ased
on the&nbsp;[**82]&nbsp; forfeiture of appraisal rights because (in part) t=
he
minority shareholders &quot;did not vote in favor of the merger and did not
show that they relied on the proxy materials in any way that caused them to
forfeit their rights to appraisal.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2HM67W0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4135-YW60-0038-X4WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>228 F.3d at 4=
9-50</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Monk's claim, likewise fueled by inaction, should meet the same end.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Precedent
defeats the idea (on which the majority opinion rests) that it makes no
difference whether Monk actively relinquished his appraisal rights by votin=
g to
approve the merger or whether he sat idly by. The majority concedes this,
noting that the investors who we can presume have relied on the market price
are those &quot;who acquire or divest themselves of stock&quot;--but Monk t=
ook
neither step. The freeze-out merger, not any action by Monk, is what divest=
ed
him of his stock. To invoke a presumption for why he acted as he did, Monk =
must
first show that he acted at all.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P6"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2HM67W0050000400"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-we=
ight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>(4)</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'> The majority opinion says that &quot;[l]ike all </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 10(b)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
defendants, Shanda and the Individual Defendants may rebut the
presumption.&quot; It is the nature of the presumption to be rebuttable in
order to limit litigation on speculative and hypothetical claims. As the
Supreme Court explained in <u>Basic</u>, &quot;[a]ny showing that severs the
link&nbsp;[**83]&nbsp; between the alleged misrepresentation and either the
price received (or paid) by the plaintiff, or his decision to trade at a fa=
ir
market price, will be sufficient to rebut the presumption of reliance.&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2HM67W0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FKK0-003B-409H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Basic, 485 U.=
S.
at 248</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Here, the presumption is defeated at the outset. There=
 is
no link to sever between the proxies and the merger transaction, which was
negotiated and announced before the proxies ever were published. Nor can Mo=
nk
claim a link between the proxies and his inaction: the presumption accounts=
 for
choices made by actual actors in developed markets, not hypothetical actors=
 in
markets that are constrained. The presumption cannot explain a decision that
was never made, in a market that did not exist.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>* *
*</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P5"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2N1PP50020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GTF069SX003RS0V2005P4"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2N1PP50040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Since, as I have demonstrated, no
presumption of fraud on the market is available in the freeze-out merger
context, it follows that transaction causation in a lost-state-remedy action
for securities fraud must be pleaded with particularity and with nonspecula=
tive
allegations of direct reliance on the claimed fraud--including an allegation
that the plaintiff did more than merely hold the same security the plaintiff
owned before the merger announcement. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2N1PP50010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4135-YW60-0038-X4WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Grace</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4135-YW60-0038-X4WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 228 F.3d at
48-50</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. That the fraudulent proxies caused&nbsp;[**84]&nbsp; M=
onk
to forgo appraisal cannot be inferred merely from his failure to pursue it.=
 <u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6F29STV2N1PP50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Blue Chip Sta=
mps</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 421 U.S. at
737-38</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Monk can sustain his burden only by plausibly alleging
that he read and directly relied on the proxy statements in voting to appro=
ve
the merger. He does not.</span></p>

