name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P2HM6BM0030000400"></a>Moreover, to determine wheth=
er a
civil RICO claim is timely, courts in the Second Circuit apply &quot;a
discovery accrual rule, under which the limitations period begins to run wh=
en
the plaintiff discovers or should have discovered &nbsp;[**61]&nbsp;the RICO
injury.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. (internal quo=
tation
marks omitted). But the Second Circuit has also recognized a &quot;separate
accrual rule&quot; whereby &quot;a new claim accrues, triggering a new
four-year limitations period, each time plaintiff discovers, or should have
discovered, a new injury caused by the predicate RICO violations.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P28T4S80050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXN-WVV0-001T-D184-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bingham v. Zo=
lt</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXN-WVV0-001T-D184-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 66 F.3d 553,
559 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P2HM6BM0030000400_2"></a>As a result, &quot;[a]s lo=
ng as
separate and independent injuries continue to flow from the underlying RICO
violations&#8212;regardless of when those violations occurred&#8212;[a]
plaintiff may wait indefinitely to sue, but may then win compensation only =
for
injuries discovered or discoverable within the four-year 'window' before su=
it
was filed . . . .&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P2HM6BM0020000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXN-WVV0-001T-D184-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RXN-WVV0-001T-D184-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 560</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
filed their initial Complaint in January 2012. (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>See</i> Dkt. No. 1.) Defendants are therefore correct that &quot;[a=
]ny
RICO claims that arose [four years] prior to&quot; filing the Complaint wou=
ld
be time barred. (Mem. 23.) And Plaintiff Greene does allege that Defendants
fraudulently sued her over four years prior to the filing of the Complaint.=
 (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> Compl. &para;&para; 95, 99-100
(alleging that Defendants obtained a default judgment against her &quot;[a]=
bout
[four-and-a-half years]&quot; before February &nbsp;[**62]&nbsp;2012).) <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P2HM6BM0050000400"></a>However, Defendants are inco=
rrect
when they argue that &quot;all of Plaintiff Greene's claims arose . . . when
she learned that she had been sued by [NLS].&quot; (Mem. 23.) Even if
Defendants sued Greene more than four years before she brought her claim, i=
t is
the RICO injury that triggers the accrual, not the RICO <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>violation. See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01P2HM6BM0040000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y060-001B-K0GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Trust=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y060-001B-K0GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 859 F.2d at
1105</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24_1"></a>(&quot;[E]ach=
 time
plaintiff discovers or should have discovered an injury caused by defendant=
's
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
new cause of action arises as to that injury, regardless of when the actual
violation occurred.&quot;). In this context, the Court has already held that
legal expenses can constitute a RICO injury, and Greene alleges that she
incurred such expenses at some point after February 2012. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01R2HM6BN0020000400"></a>(<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>See</i> Compl. &para;&para; 99-101 (alleging that Defendants obtain=
ed a
default judgment against Greene, that they subsequently froze her mother's =
bank
account in February 2012, &nbsp;[*159]&nbsp; and that Greene thereafter
&quot;had to retain a lawyer and incur legal expenses in New York to vacate=
 the
default judgment&quot;).) Under the separate accrual rule, therefore, her
claims for those damages are not time-barred. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5C0W01R2HM6BN0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y060-001B-K0GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Trust=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y060-001B-K0GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 859 F.2d at
1105</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (holding, &quot;[u]nder [the] rule &nbsp;[**63]&nbsp;of
separate accrual,&quot; and in the context of the plaintiff's claim for
&quot;past legal fees and other expenses,&quot; that the plaintiff &quot;may
recover any of these expenses which it discovered or should have
discovered&quot; within four years of bringing the action). The Court there=
fore
denies Defendants' Motion To Dismiss Greene's RICO claims.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn53' href=3D"#_ftn53" name=3D"_ftnref53" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>III. Conclusion</span></u></p>

