k'>B.
Elements of a RICO Cause of Action</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
the Court cannot conclude at this juncture that plaintiff's claims are
time-barred, the Court turns to the sufficiency of plaintiff's pleadings as=
 it
relates to the elements of a RICO claim. As stated <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>supra</i>, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_2"></a>a RICO claim
contains three elements: (1) a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>; (2) injury to plaintiff's business or property; and (3)
causation of the injury by the violation. The Court groups the moving
defendants' arguments by element. For the reasons discussed below, the amen=
ded
complaint has adequately alleged the first and third elements, but has fail=
ed
to allege the second element (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e</=
i>.,
RICO injury). Accordingly, the Court dismisses the amended complaint, witho=
ut
prejudice to plaintiff either filing a second amended complaint or commenci=
ng a
new action when its RICO injury becomes clear and definite.</span></p>

