:contentItem:52DB-Y0B1-652J-D00K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DeSilva v. N.
Shore-Long Island Jewish Health Sys., Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52DB-Y0B1-652J-D00K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 770 F. Sup=
p.
2d 497, 521 (E.D.N.Y. 2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (where plaintiffs claimed that
defendant-hospital's fraud had prevented them from bringing timely claims f=
or
&nbsp;[**58]&nbsp;unpaid wages, RICO claim was unripe because plaintiffs had
not yet attempted to sue for unpaid wages, and thus &quot;the injury alleged
was contingent upon uncertain litigation-related events&quot;). Moreover, t=
he
rationale for the ripeness requirement&#8212;that a RICO injury is speculat=
