/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*235]&nbsp;
A. &nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The Concept In
General</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Any
discussion of proximate cause should be approached with some trepidation
because, as a scholarly treatise teaches, no topic is subject to more
disagreement or such confusion. Proximate cause is an elusive concept, one
&quot;always to be determined on the facts of each case upon mixed
considerations of logic, common sense, justice, policy and precedent.&quot;=
 W.
Page Keeton et al., <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Prosser &amp; K=
eeton
on The Law of Torts</i>, &sect; 42, at 279 (5th ed. 1984) (quoting 1 Street=
, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Foundations of Legal Liability</i>, 110
(1906)). In everyday terms, the concept might be explained as follows: Beca=
use
the consequences of an act go endlessly forward in time and its causes stre=
tch
back to the dawn of human history, proximate cause is used essentially as a
legal tool for limiting a wrongdoer's liability only to those harms that ha=
ve a
reasonable connection to his actions. The law has wisely determined that it=
 is
futile to trace the consequences of a wrongdoer's actions to their ultimate
end, if end there is.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
notion of limiting liability is well-accepted; it is its application to a g=
iven
case that is more troublesome. The dictionary definition of the word
&quot;proximate&quot; provides&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; little assistance in this
regard, limited as it is to such broad synonyms as &quot;very near,
immediately, adjoining, close.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Webster's
Third New International Dictionary</i> 1828 (1981).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Direct Injury as a Requirement of Prox=
imate
Cause</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4S90000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Over the passage of time, howeve=
r,
courts have somewhat clarified the definition of proximate cause by identif=
ying
several traditional common law principles limiting liability whose applicat=
ion,
in aggregate, formulates the proximate cause analysis. As noted in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, &quot;'proximate cause' [is
used] to label generically the judicial tools used to limit a person's
responsibility for the consequences of that person's own acts.&quot; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4S80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
68</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
