mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 272 n.20=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, it identified three policy factors to guide courts in their
application of the general principle that plaintiffs with indirect injuries
lack standing to sue under RICO. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Se=
e </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 269-70</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
First, the more indirect an injury is, the more difficult it becomes to
determine the amount of plaintiff's damages attributable &nbsp;[*237]&nbsp;=
 to
the wrongdoing as opposed to other, independent factors. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WS0000400"></a>Second, recognizing claims b=
y the
indirectly injured would require courts to adopt complicated rules apportio=
ning
damages among plaintiffs removed at different levels of injury from the
violative acts, in order to avoid the risk of multiple recoveries. Third,
struggling with the first two problems is unnecessary where there are direc=
tly
injured parties who can remedy the harm without these attendant problems. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at
269-70</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Examples of Indirect Injury</i></span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to illustrate the difference between direct and indirect injuries, we
review several cases on the subject. First, we examine&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; ca=
ses
where the injuries were indirect.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, plaintiff Securities Inves=
tor
Protection Corp. (SIPC) insured brokers, such that when a broker went bankr=
upt,
SIPC was obligated by statute to reimburse the broker's customers for certa=
in
claims not satisfied by the broker's liquidated assets. SIPC alleged that
Robert Holmes had conspired in a stock manipulation scheme that led to the
bankruptcy of two brokers, disabling them from meeting their obligations an=
d,
as a result, triggering SIPC's statutory duty to reimburse the customers.
Asserting a subrogation claim that allowed SIPC to stand in the shoes of the
brokers' customers, SIPC sued Holmes under RICO for amounts reimbursed to t=
he
customers because of the bankruptcies resulting from Holmes' alleged scheme=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Supreme Court found the
plaintiffs' injuries to be indirect, because their losses were purely
contingent on the insolvency of third parties, the brokers, that had result=
ed
from defendants' actions. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4X80000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 271</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Hence, the Court ruled that the suit by SIPC against Holmes could not stand,
because the harm to the customers was too remote, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, there was no proximate cause. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4X80000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See id</i>.; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i>
&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>also </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4WW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9XB0-003B-P30M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Manson v.
Stacescu</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9XB0-003B-P30M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 11 F.3d 112=
7,
1130-31 (2d Cir. 1993)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (creditors of bankrupt corporat=
ion
lacked RICO standing to sue defendants who had caused bankruptcy).</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4XB0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly, courts have held that
plaintiffs who are obligated to pay the medical expenses of another may not
recover against the tortfeasor who caused the damage, because their injuries
are indirect since they derive wholly from the injuries sustained by the th=
ird
party. This concept is not new. An 1883 treatise cited a case where</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
