<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4Y40000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4YC0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4Y30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>GICC Capital
Corp. v. Technology Finance Group, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 30 F.3d 289,
290 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, plaintiff accepted a promissory
note from defendants as part of an unrelated settlement. During the negotia=
tion
of the terms and issuance of the note the defendants did not
disclose&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; that, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>at=
 the
same time</i> they were agreeing to cause the corporation to issue the note,
they were stripping it of assets, rendering it unable to meet that
obligation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4YB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 292-93</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4YF0000400"></a>The defendants' failure to
disclose the stripped-down status of the corporation induced the plaintiff =
to
accept the note in settlement. We stated that because of the timing and
magnitude of the note, the plaintiffs had RICO standing.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND4YD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 293</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In other words, if the plaintiffs had accepted a promissory note from a
corporation that was at that time able to repay the note, and later the
defendants stripped the corporation, the injury would have been indirect
because it would have derived wholly from injuries to the corporation. But,
when the promissory note was issued, the corporation had already been strip=
ped
of enough assets to undermine the possibility of repayment. Thus, because of
the defendants' misrepresentations, the plaintiff in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>GICC</i> (much like the plaintiffs in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Cerebelli</i>) accepted an over-valued asset, the note, in exchange=
 for
the settlement of their litigation claims. The injury was direct because the
plaintiff was immediately injured in the process&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; of
negotiating the settlement contract with the defendants, rather than as a
derivative result of later injuries to a third party namely, the corporation
that issued the note.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
these cases demonstrate, the critical question posed by the direct injury t=
est
is whether the damages a plaintiff &nbsp;[*239]&nbsp; sustains are derivati=
ve
of an injury to a third party. If so, then the injury is indirect; if not, =
it
is direct. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn69' href=3D"#_ftn69"
name=3D"_ftnref69" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; C. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Applying
the Direct Injury Test to Instant Case</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
Parties' Contentions</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
defendants' principal point on appeal is that the plaintiffs' damages are
entirely derivative of the harm suffered by individual smokers as a result =
of
defendants' acts. Hence, defendants believe that plaintiffs' alleged injuri=
es
are indirect, since they flow from the misfortunes visited upon third perso=
ns,
and that plaintiffs therefore stand at too remote a distance to recover.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
response, plaintiffs declare that their complaint alleges a direct injury
brought about by defendants' misconduct towards the Funds themselves.
Plaintiffs allege that defendants misrepresented the health risks of smoking
and the addictiveness of nicotine, causing the Funds to fail to implement
smoking cessation programs, and deliberately shifting the costs of health c=
are
for tobacco related illnesses from the tobacco companies to the Funds. In t=
urn,
this led to what the district court termed &quot;harm to [plaintiffs']
infrastructure, financial stability, [and] ability to project costs.&quot; =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SM3-S3W0-0038-Y33K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Laborers Loca=
l 17</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SM3-S3W0-0038-Y33K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 7 F. Supp. =
2d
at 295.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> Plaintiffs insist that these injuries were direct, since
the &quot;activities of&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; the Funds themselves were affecte=
d by
defendants' misconduct.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ultimately,
however, whether plaintiffs' injuries are labeled as &quot;infrastructure
harm&quot; or &quot;harm to financial stability,&quot; their damages are
entirely derivative of the harm suffered by plan participants as a result of
using tobacco products. Without injury to the individual smokers, the Funds
would not have incurred any increased costs in the form of the payment of
benefits, nor would they have experienced the difficulties of cost predicti=
on
and control that constituted the crux of their infrastructure harms. Being
purely contingent on harm to third parties, these injuries are indirect.
Consequently, because defendants' alleged misconduct did not proximately ca=
use
the injuries alleged, plaintiffs lack standing to bring RICO claims against
defendants.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
Policy Considerations</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Further, this conclusion is
consistent with the three policy factors addressed by <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Holmes</i>, which buttress the principle that plaintiffs with indir=
ect
injuries lack standing to sue under RICO. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at
269-70</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. &quot;First, the less direct an injury is, the more
difficult it becomes to ascertain the amount of a plaintiff's damages
attributable&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; to the violation, as distinct from other,
independent, factors.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 269</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AGC</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. at
542-43</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>). For instance, in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Holmes</i>,
it was difficult to tell whether plaintiff's inability to collect from cert=
ain
brokers had resulted from the defendants' conspiracy to manipulate the brok=
ers
or, instead, from the brokers' poor business practices.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
73</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The present case is similar because the damage claims here are incredibly
speculative. It will be virtually impossible for plaintiffs to prove with a=
ny
certainty: (1) the effect any smoking &nbsp;[*240]&nbsp; cessation programs=
 or
incentives would have had on the number of smokers among the plan
beneficiaries; (2) the countereffect that the tobacco companies' direct fra=
ud
would have had on the smokers, despite the best efforts of the Funds; and (=
3)
other reasons why individual smokers would continue smoking, even after hav=
ing
been informed of the dangers of smoking and having been offered smoking
cessation programs. On a fundamental level, these difficulties of proving
damages stem from the agency of the individual smokers in deciding whether,=
 and
how frequently, to smoke. In this light, the&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; direct injury
test can be seen as wisely limiting standing to sue to those situations whe=
re
the chain of causation leading to damages is not complicated by the interve=
ning
agency of third parties (here, the smokers) from whom the plaintiffs' injur=
ies
derive.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>These
concerns become particularly pointed in a case, like the present one, where=
 the
injuries are alleged to derive not simply from defendants' affirmative
misconduct but also from plaintiffs' fraudulently induced inaction. That is=
, it
is often easier to ascertain the damages that flow from actual, affirmative
conduct, than to speculate what damages arose from a party's failure to act=
. In
the latter situation, as in the case at hand, it becomes difficult to
distinguish among the multitude of factors that might have affected the
damages. Here, for example, plaintiffs' alleged damages might have derived =
from
inefficiencies in the Funds' own management, as well as from non-smoking
related health problems suffered by the smokers, and it would be the sheere=
st sort
of speculation to determine how these damages might have been lessened had =
the
Funds adopted the measures defendants allegedly induced them not to adopt.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complexity&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; of these calculations makes the ultimate quest=
ion
of damages suffered by the Funds virtually impossible to determine. Indeed,
this case seems to present precisely the type of large, complicated damages
claims that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Associated General Contractors</i> sou=
ght to
avoid. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>e.g.</i>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AGC</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. at=
 545</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;Massive and complex damages litigation not only burdens the courts, =
but
also undermines the effectiveness of treble-damages suits.&quot;). Moreover,
for us to rule otherwise could lead to a potential explosion in the scope of
tort liability, which, while perhaps well-intentioned, is a subject best le=
ft
to the legislature.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND50V0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The second policy factor address=
ed
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> focuses on the possib=
ility
that &quot;recognizing claims of the indirectly injured would force courts =
to
adopt complicated rules apportioning damages among plaintiffs removed at
different levels of injury from the violative acts, to obviate the risk of =
multiple
recoveries.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND50T0000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
69</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AGC</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. at
543-44</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>). Under New York law, the smokers are prohibited from
recovering medical costs paid to them by insurers. New York law dictates th=
at
&quot;where the&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; plaintiff seeks to recover the cost of
medical care&quot; and the court finds that such costs were reimbursed by an
insurance company, the court &quot;shall reduce the amount of award by such=
 a
finding.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-84SF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. C.P.L.R.
&sect; 4545(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (McKinney 1992). As a result, the district court believ=
ed
the suit presented no risk of multiple recoveries. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SM3-S3W0-0038-Y33K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Laborers Loca=
l 17</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SM3-S3W0-0038-Y33K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 7 F. Supp. =
2d
at 295.</span></i></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Admittedly,
the smokers in this case do not present a risk of double recovery under RIC=
O.
At the same time, however, other remote payors like the employers or health
insurers with whom the Funds may contract might bring suit claiming that th=
ey
ultimately bore the costs. Here, the Funds argue that ERISA prevents the
employers from obtaining such a recovery. But the provision cited by plaint=
iffs
merely prevents the assets of the Fund from inuring to the benefit of an
employer. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67F7-75G3-CGX8-02NJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1103(c)(1) (1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Although this might prevent
recovery by an employer against the Fund, it in no way prevents a suit by t=
he
employer against the defendants &nbsp;[*241]&nbsp; as tortfeasors. Thus, a
possibility of duplicative recoveries exists.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5170000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In this context, defendants asse=
rt
that New&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp; York State has already sued some of the tobacco
companies, seeking recovery of costs it has expended for the medical care of
its employees, some of whom are participants in certain of the public-sector
union Funds. But, the Funds respond, such duplicative recovery is prevented=
 by
the single satisfaction rule. The single satisfaction rule is often used to
prevent a single plaintiff from recovering its damages several times over f=
rom
multiple defendants. But, even were we to assume that the single satisfacti=
on
rule would prohibit duplicative recoveries by multiple plaintiffs against a
single defendant, it would not cure the ultimate problem set forth in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, that is, that courts would=
 be
forced to &quot;adopt complicated rules apportioning damages among&quot; the
Funds, the employers, and perhaps even the unions, each of which is
&quot;removed at [a] different level[] of injury.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5160000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 269</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the third policy factor discussed by <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Holmes</i>,
the Supreme Court concluded that the need to grapple with the problems of
calculating and apportioning damages was unjustified where &quot;directly
injured victims can generally be counted on to vindicate the law as
private&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; attorneys general, without any of the problems
attendant upon suits by plaintiffs injured more remotely.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. (citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AGC</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. at
541-42</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>). The Funds correctly note that these RICO causes of ac=
tion
could not be asserted by the smokers or by the Funds in a subrogation action
because the RICO statute requires an injury to &quot;business or
property,&quot; whereas the smokers' injuries are personal in nature. Hence,
the Funds conclude there are no more directly injured &quot;private attorne=
ys
general&quot; who could vindicate the law for these alleged RICO violations.
The district court also found that because individual smokers would not be =
able
to bring a RICO action, the harms alleged in plaintiffs' complaint would go
unremedied were the Funds' action not allowed to continue.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51R0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Yet, to the contrary, our holding
that plaintiffs lack standing under RICO need not bring about the result
plaintiffs fear. The Funds may still bring a subrogation action to recover =
the
medical costs paid out for the individual smokers, and the smokers themselv=
es
have sufficient independent incentive to pursue their own causes of action =
for
such additional types of injuries as pain and suffering.
Although&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; these will not be RICO claims, they will remedy =
the
harm done by defendants' alleged misconduct. Moreover, these actions will
promote &quot;the general interest in deterring injurious conduct,&quot; wh=
ich <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> noted as the objective of t=
his
policy factor.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
69</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Especially where such other actions are available, the lack of a RICO damag=
es
remedy for even direct personal injuries (such as those suffered by the
individual smokers) actually weighs heavily <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>against</i>
RICO standing for parties (such as the Funds) alleging harms that are purely
contingent on personal injuries suffered by others.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, the policy considerations highlighted in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Holmes</i> support our conclusion that the Funds have failed to
establish proximate causation and that, accordingly, they lack standing to =
sue
the defendants under RICO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III
Is a Defendant's Specific Intent to Harm a Plaintiff An Exception to the
Direct-Injury Rule?</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs aver that even if the=
ir
claims fail the direct injury test, they should still have standing to sue
because an exception to this rule exists where the defendants specifically
intend to harm plaintiffs. The complaint asserts that the tobacco companies
intended&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp; to injure the Funds. The Funds urge that <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>AGC</i> -- a case that discusses the
principles of proximate cause in the antitrust context and upon which <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> heavily relied -- quotes a
scholarly treatise to this effect, &quot;'where &nbsp;[*242]&nbsp; the
plaintiff sustains injury from the defendant's conduct to a third person, i=
t is
too remote . . . unless the wrongful act is willful for that purpose.'&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>459 U.S. at 5=
32
n.25</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (quoting 1 J.G. Sutherland, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>A Treatise on the Law of Damages</i> 55-56 (1883)).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51W0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51Y0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5230000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Yet, contrary to plaintiffs'
argument, this view was specifically rejected in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>AGC</i>. The Court there stated that &quot;although buttressed by an
allegation of intent to harm the [plaintiffs]&quot; the complaint was
&quot;insufficient as a matter of law&quot; for several reasons, including =
the
fact that the plaintiffs alleged merely indirect injuries and were therefore
barred from recovery as lacking proximate cause. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>459 U.S. at 5=
45</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In addition, the Court observed that the availability of a remedy in that c=
ase
was &quot;'not a question of the specific intent of the conspirators.'&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND51X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 537</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5200000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FV0-003B-S4KH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Blue Shield v.
McCready</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FV0-003B-S4KH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 457 U.S. 46=
5,
479, 73 L. Ed. 2d 149, 102 S. Ct. 2540 (1982))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;
The Ninth Circuit has also interpreted <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>AGC</i>
to mean that &quot;the existence of specific intent does not answer the
question of whether the injury is specifically direct.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5220000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3J20-003B-P2KS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pillsbury,
Madison &amp; Sutro v. Lerner</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3J20-003B-P2KS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 31 F.3d 924,
929 (9th Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Moreover, that court stated th=
at
&quot;nothing in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> suggest=
s that
allegations of specific intent to cause RICO harm override the necessity to
evaluate the directness of injury.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Id</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073N"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5250000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In addition, the cases plaintiffs
cite to support their argument are not persuasive, either because their
discussion of intent is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>dicta</i> o=
r they
involve a direct injury. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5240000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRJ-CDK0-003C-T28H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Chelsea Moving
&amp; Trucking Co. v. Ross Towboat Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRJ-CDK0-003C-T28H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 280 Mass. 2=
82,
286, 182 N.E. 477, 479 (1932)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting in <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>dicta</i> that malice was not alleged in that case); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND52J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-W380-003B-T32F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Crab Orchard
Improvement Co. v. Chesapeake &amp; Ohio Ry. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-W380-003B-T32F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 115 F.2d 27=
7,
282-83 (4th Cir. 1940)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (suggesting in <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>dicta</i> that an employer may maintain a suit against a tortfeasor=
 who
injured an employee, if the tortfeasor intended to harm the employer in his
contractual obligations); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND52M00004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>GICC</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4K90-003B-P209-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 30 F.3d at
292-93</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (examining intent, but ultimately involving a
direct&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; injury, as discussed above); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND52P0000400"></a><a
