:contentItem:3S4X-9XB0-003B-P30M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Manson</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9XB0-003B-P30M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 11 F.3d at =
1130</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that the defendants in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bank=
ers
Trust</i> had acted for the &quot;direct purpose&quot; of harming the
plaintiffs; however, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bankers Trust<=
/i>
involved a direct injury, as discussed above).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
such, these decisions regarding specific intent do not support the proposit=
ion
that an indirect injury is actionable where the injury was specifically
intended by the defendant. In other words, an allegation of specific intent
does not overcome the requirement that there must be a direct injury to
maintain this action. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn70' href=3D"#_f=
tn70"
name=3D"_ftnref70" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>IV
Common Law Fraud and Special Duty Claims</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND53B0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55C0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our inquiry into RICO standing b=
egan
with the Supreme Court's holding in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>Holmes</i>
that the statute requires that at least the proximate causation
principles&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; found generally in the common law, including t=
he
direct injury requirement discussed above, be met. These principles also ap=
ply
in general terms to the fraud and special duty causes of action asserted by
plaintiffs under New York common law. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>See</i>,
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g.</i>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5390000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-BCP0-003C-F1S2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ferguson v. G=
reen
Island Contracting Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-BCP0-003C-F1S2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 36 N.Y.2d 7=
42,
743, 368 N.Y.S.2d 163, 163, 328 N.E.2d 792 (1975)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>per curiam</i>) (employer may not reco=
ver
damages it sustains when key employee is injured by third-party tortfeasor)=
; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND53C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-0YX0-003C-C21M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rockaway Blvd.
Wrecking &amp; Lumber Corp. v. Raylite Elec. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-0YX0-003C-C21M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 A.D.2d 9,
12, 269 N.Y.S.2d 926, 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (1st Dep't 1966) (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>per curiam</i>) (&quot;'where a third =
person
suffers damage by reason of a contractual obligation to the injured party, =
such
damage is too remote and indirect to become the subject of a direct action =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ex delicto</i>, in the absence of
subrogation.'&quot; (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND53T00=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-6DS0-003G-404P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Forcum-James =
Co.
v. Duke Transp. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-6DS0-003G-404P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, </span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-6DS0-003G-404P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*243]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-6DS0-003G-404P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 231 La. 953,
962, 93 So. 2d 228, 230-31 (1957)))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>see also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND53W0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-B630-003C-F4YX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pulka v. Edel=
man</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-B630-003C-F4YX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 40 N.Y.2d 7=
81,
785, 390 N.Y.S.2d 393, 396, 358 N.E.2d 1019 (1976)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(New York law requires proximate cause in special duty cases).
&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; Although New York law appears to permit limited exceptio=
ns
to the general requirement that the direct injury test be met, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND53Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-0YX0-003C-C21M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rockaway</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-0YX0-003C-C21M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 A.D.2d a=
t 12</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
plaintiffs have brought no cases to our attention that would permit such a
departure from the general rule in the present case. Hence, analogous
principles to those that doomed plaintiffs' RICO causes of action also bar
plaintiffs' common law fraud and special duty actions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>V
Remaining Points</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Trust Principles</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
assert that the direct injury test is not applicable in this case, because
certain of the Funds are trusts governed by ERISA and the Taft-Hartley Act =
and
other Funds are non-ERISA not-for-profit public employee trusts. First,
plaintiffs contend that the direct injury test is only a rule of state
insurance law, and state insurance law is inapplicable to ERISA funds. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70RF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1144(b)(2)(B)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (plan not to be subjected to &quot;any law . . . purpor=
ting
to regulate . . . insurance contracts&quot;). However, as demonstrated by <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>AGC</i>, and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kinsman</i>, t=
he
proximate cause direct injury requirement is a common law rule of general
application, not one simply limited&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp; to insurance cases.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
plaintiffs aver that traditional trust principles apply and displace the di=
rect
injury requirement. However, nothing in ERISA supports this argument. Even
assuming that trust principles govern, case law suggests that when a Fund
brings an action at law for tort against a third person it should be treated
like any other plaintiff. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55F0000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See Restatement (Second) of Trusts &se=
ct;
280 cmt. a</i> (1959) (&quot;If a third person commits a tort with respect =
to
the trust property, the trustee can maintain such actions at law as he could
maintain by reason of his ownership of the property as if he held it free of
trust.&quot;); IV A.W. Scott, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The L=
aw of
Trusts</i> &sect; 280.1 (1967) (remedies for torts against trust property a=
re
the same as if the property were held free of trust). Thus, it follows, that
both ERISA and non-ERISA funds, like any plaintiff, would be subject to the
requirements of proximate cause and its direct injury inquiry. Moreover, the
specific civil remedies set forth in ERISA for redressing violations of that
statute or of the provisions of an ERISA plan are simply inapplicable here,
since no such violation is at issue in this case.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6JH3-K443-SBKB-44R7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1132</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>;&nbsp;[**44]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>see
also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55D0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RJ6-FCX0-003B-R0MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mertens v. He=
witt
Assocs.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RJ6-FCX0-003B-R0MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 508 U.S. 24=
8,
253-54, 124 L. Ed. 2d 161, 113 S. Ct. 2063 (1993)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Therefore, that the funds are governed by ERISA or general trust principles
does not, insofar as plaintiffs' complaint is concerned, change the law of
proximate cause.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>State Entity Cases</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
also point to numerous cases that have arisen where States have brought a
direct action against the tobacco companies to recover medical costs they
expended on smokers, and district courts have upheld their right to sue. Bu=
t,
many of these cases are distinguishable because they involved state statuto=
ry
provisions that the courts construed as allowing the state to sue directly.=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See State ex rel. Norton v. R.J. Reyno=
lds
Tobacco Co.</i>, 1997 CV-003432 (Colo. Dist. Ct. Oct. 2, 1998); <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>State ex rel. Kelley v. Philip Morris,=
 Inc.</i>,
No. 96-84281-CZ (Mich. Cir. Ct. May 28, 1997); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>State ex rel. Humphrey v. Philip Morris, Inc.</i>, No. C1-94-8565
(Minn. Dist. Ct. Feb. 19, 1998); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>St=
ate v.
Philip Morris, Inc.</i>, No. S 744-97 CnC (Vt. Super. Ct. Mar. 25, 1998); <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>State v. American Tobacco Co.</i>, No.
96-2-15056-8 SEA, 1996 WL 931316 (Wash. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08F5GFSN3H003RRTD80073S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55H0000400"></a>Super. Nov. 19, 1996); <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>State</i> &nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>ex rel. Bronster v. Brown &amp;
Williamson Tobacco Corp.</i>, No. 97-0441-01 (Haw. Cir. Ct. Sept. 18, 1998)=
. In
addition, in some cases the unique quasi-sovereign rights of a state to
&nbsp;[*244]&nbsp; sue to protect the health and welfare of their citizens =
was
found to sustain standing. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i>=
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55G0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SV2-X670-0038-Y0F6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Texas v. Amer=
ican
Tobacco Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SV2-X670-0038-Y0F6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 14 F. Supp.=
 2d
956, 962-63 (E.D. Tex. 1997)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Time=
s",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Hence, in general, state cases=
 are
often distinguishable on the issue of proximate causation given the state's
unique role relative to protection of its citizens, statutes governing Medi=
caid
recoupment, and certain state statutes that permit states to maintain actio=
ns
on behalf of their citizenry. However, this is not the scenario before us, =
and
we have no reason to pass on it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55Y0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As both parties acknowledge, we =
have
discretion to consider all aspects of the district court's March 25 order, =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND55X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-4BW0-003B-R18P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Yamaha Motor
Corp. v. Calhoun</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-4BW0-003B-R18P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 516 U.S. 19=
9,
205, 133 L. Ed. 2d 578, 116 S. Ct. 619 (1996)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
but also have discretion to decline to adjudicate any aspect of the order,
including the questions actually certified, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>see
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F8XMV10K1MND5600000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YK80-006F-M2K1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Koehler v. Ba=
nk
of Bermuda Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YK80-006F-M2K1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 101 F.3d 86=
3,
864 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp; We answer the
first question certified to us in the affirmative, that is, we hold that the
economic injuries alleged in plaintiffs' complaint are purely derivative of=
 the
physical injuries suffered by plan participants and therefore too remote as=
 a
matter of law for them to have standing to sue defendants. In light of that
disposition, we need not reach or determine the second certified question, =
that
is, whether the federal Cigarette Labeling and Advertising Act preempts
plaintiffs' state common law claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal5 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we hereby reverse the district court's March 25, 1998 order with respect to
causes of action I and II (the RICO claims), V (the fraud claim), and VI (t=
he
special duty claim). The case is remanded to the district court with direct=
ions
to dismiss plaintiffs' complaint.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </=
span></span></p>

