989,000.00 for the building and that the building was worth it.&quot; (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. P 31.)</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint also alleged that the&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; acts of Yekimov and
Rachkauskas &quot;constituted at least two acts of fraud in connection with=
 the
purchase and sale of securities&quot; (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>id</i>.
P 35), and that &quot;Rachkauskas and Yekimov committed other acts of fraud=
 and
theft during the time period covered by RICO&quot; (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>id</i>. P 36), including &quot;setting up bogus real estate deals in
New Jersey with plaintiff Nicholas Blinov&quot; (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>id</i>. P 37).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, in opposition to early motions by defendants for summary judgment,
several of the plaintiffs submitted affidavits stating that prior to purcha=
sing
their shares they had not been informed that there was a flip sale. Defenda=
nts'
early summary judgment motions were denied, and discovery followed.</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
1992, defendants again moved for summary judgment, contending that all part=
ies
to the transaction had known (a) that the transaction was a flip sale, (b) =
that
Bankin purchased the building and assigned his interest in it to Yekimov and
Rachkauskas, and (c) that the $ 219,000 difference between the $ 770,000 pr=
ice
shown in the September 10 contract and the $ 989,000 paid by Riccobono was =
paid
to Bankin in December 1985 as an assignment fee. Khani argued in addition t=
hat,
because he was merely the attorney&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; for Yekimov and
Rachkauskas, and not a principal, the complaint failed to state a claim aga=
inst
him under the securities laws or RICO. Defendants also moved for the imposi=
tion
of sanctions pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
11</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
opposition to the summary judgment motions, plaintiffs' attorney sought
&quot;to eliminate any confusion&quot; as to the nature of plaintiffs' clai=
m of
fraud, stating that their central contention was not so much that they were
unaware of the flip nature of the transaction but rather &quot;that there n=
ever
was any 'flip' deal in this matter.&quot; (Affidavit of Joseph H. Neiman da=
ted
October 16, 1992, P 2.) He stated that plaintiffs contended</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>that
Vladimir Bankin never received the $ 219,000.00 profit that he has claimed =
to
receive and that this scheme was designed to convince all of the plaintiffs
that defendants &nbsp;[*516]&nbsp; Yekimov and Rachkauskas paid their pro r=
ata
share of $ 989,000.00</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id</i>.), leading plaintiffs to believe
&quot;that Rachkauskas and Yekimov were paying the same pro rata price [in
Riccobono] as everyone else&quot; (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Id</i>.
P 4). In support of their contention that Yekimov and Rachkauskas had bought
the building directly from Equities and that the supposed September 20
flip/assignment&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; was a sham, plaintiffs submitted, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inter alia</i>, a September 11, 1985 c=
heck
for $ 15,000 from Rachkauskas to the law firm representing Equities; the
&quot;memo&quot; line on this check read: &quot;downpayment for 219 E. 29 S=
t.
NY, NY&quot;. Plaintiffs also submitted the pertinent page of the municipal
plat records listed in the Standard Manhattan Residential Directory for
1988-1989 (&quot;plat directory&quot;), which listed Riccobono as the owner=
 of
the building and as having purchased it for $ 770,000. In addition, Blinov,=
 one
of the Group B plaintiffs, submitted an affidavit stating that in the sprin=
g of
1991, Bankin telephoned and</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>told
me . . . that he made no money and in no way was profiting from that deal.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. .
. .</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>5.
He told me even though he stated at the deposition that he made a profit on
this deal, in reality he made no profit and that he was just used as a dumm=
y.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
