k'>II.
DISCUSSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDVMMS0K1MNJ3T50000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_5"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
motion for summary judgment may not be granted unless the court determines =
that
there is no genuine issue of material fact to be tried and that the moving
party is therefore entitled to judgment as a matter of law. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
56(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. When a defendant moving for summary judgment has point=
ed
to the absence of evidence to support an essential element on which the
plaintiff has the burden of&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; proof, the plaintiff, in orde=
r to
avoid summary judgment, must show the presence of a genuine issue by
&nbsp;[*517]&nbsp; coming forward with evidence that would be sufficient, if
all reasonable inferences were drawn in his favor, to establish the existen=
ce
of that element at trial. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i><=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDVMMS0K1MNJ3T40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6HC0-0039-N37R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Celotex Corp.=
 v.
Catrett</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6HC0-0039-N37R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 477 U.S. 31=
7,
322-23, 91 L. Ed. 2d 265, 106 S. Ct. 2548 (1986)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
56(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal6 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_5"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In considering a motion for summ=
ary
judgment, the district court may rely on &quot;any material that would be
admissible or usable at trial.&quot; 10A C. Wright &amp; A. Miller, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Federal Practice and Procedure: Civil<=
/i>
&sect; 2721 at 40 (2d ed. 1983). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDVMMS0K1MNJ3T700004=
00"></a>In
considering the record before it, however, the court must resolve all
ambiguities and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of the nonmoving pa=
rty,
and it may not properly grant summary judgment where the issue turns on the
credibility of witnesses. Any assessments of credibility and all choices
between available inferences are matters to be left for a jury, not matters=
 to
be decided by the court on summary judgment. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>See</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g.</i>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
56(e)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>, 1963 Advisory Committee Note; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDVMMS0K1MNJ3T60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SR0-003B-S1RK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Agosto v. INS=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SR0-003B-S1RK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 436 U.S. 74=
