<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff-appellant
Walter Friedl appeals from the January 13, 1999 order of the United States
District Court for the Southern District of New York (John S. Martin, Jr., =
<u>District
Judge</u>), granting defendants' motion to dismiss Friedl's action under </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> and denying Friedl's motion for leave to amend the
complaint. We conclude that the district court erred by considering materia=
ls
outside the pleadings in ruling on defendants' motion&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; to
dismiss and that Friedl has stated a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, we vacate the judgment of the district court and remand for
further proceedings. On remand, we direct the district court to allow plain=
tiff
to amend his complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>BACKGROUND</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
following facts are taken largely from plaintiff's complaint. Walter Friedl=
 was
convicted in 1982 for criminal possession of a forged instrument, but was n=
ot
incarcerated until 1993, eleven years later, due to the misplacement of his
conviction and commitment records by state and county officials. Ultimately=
, he
was placed in the Queensboro Correctional Facility, which is operated by the
New York State Department of Correctional Services (&quot;DOCS&quot;). On
December 15, 1993, plaintiff was granted work release pursuant to
&nbsp;[*82]&nbsp; New York State Correction Law, which allows inmates to
&quot;leave the premises of an institution . . . for the purpose of on-the-=
job
training or employment.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>N.Y.
Correct. Law &sect; 851(3)</i> (McKinney 1999). Over time, Friedl was allow=
ed
to spend most nights at home, residing in prison only two nights each week.=
 </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
May 24, 1994, plaintiff applied to defendant New York City Human Resources
Administration (&quot;HRA&quot;) for public&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; assistance, fo=
od
stamps, and Medicaid, disclosing his work release status and the fact that =
he
was residing at home five nights a week. His application was denied on June=
 3,
1994, because he was &quot;presently incarcerated and in [a] work release
program&quot; and thus allegedly &quot;not eligible&quot; for these benefit=
s.
Plaintiff requested and was granted a fair hearing with the New York State
Department of Social Services (&quot;DSS&quot;) to appeal the HRA's
determination. On August 8, 1994, an administrative law judge (&quot;ALJ&qu=
ot;)
ruled in favor of Friedl and instructed the HRA to &quot;continue to process
the Appellant's application and afford the applicant the opportunity to sub=
mit
any other documents necessary to establish eligibility.&quot; The ALJ wrote=
:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
dispositive issue . . . is not whether the Appellant is under supervision of
the State of New York Department of Correctional Services, but whether the
Appellant's income and resources are sufficient or insufficient . . . . The
Appellant's credible testimony establishes that he is not incarcerated, nor
residing in a public institution, that he lacks income and resources suffic=
ient
to meet his needs and that his needs are not being met. The denial
of&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; Appellant's application . . . is therefore improper.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Notwithstanding
the ALJ's ruling, on August 19, 1994, HRA once again denied Friedl's
application because he was &quot;incarcerated and in a work release
program&quot; and thus &quot;not eligible&quot; for benefits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to the complaint, after the fair hearing decision was rendered, city defend=
ant
&quot;Ms. Blackheath&quot; (later identified as Ms. Blackett), an HRA
supervisor, contacted Friedl's parole officer, Pedro Torres, to inform him =
of
plaintiff's application for benefits. Torres then charged plaintiff with
&quot;Inmate Misbehavior&quot; under DOCS's rules, alleging that Friedl had
lied and attempted to commit welfare fraud. As a result, on August 25, 1994,
Friedl appeared before the Queensboro Temporary Release Committee, which
recommended his removal from the work release program, and the Queensboro
superintendent, in turn, approved the recommendation. According to plaintif=
f,
both the Committee and the superintendent refused to consider Friedl's
favorable ALJ ruling and &quot;the absence of any restriction on applying f=
or
public assistance in the written rules and regulations governing his Work
Release&quot; in making their determination that he had
engaged&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; in misconduct. Friedl appealed his removal from wo=
rk
release, but before receiving the decision, he was approved for a second wo=
rk
release on or about November 15, 1994, approximately eighty days after his
first work release was revoked. When the decision was ultimately issued, it
concluded that the appeal was &quot;Moot&quot; because Friedl had been
&quot;reapproved for work release.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
July 24, 1997, Friedl filed the instant action under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, naming as defendants both: (1) the City of New York and
&quot;Ms. Blackheath&quot; (the &quot;city defendants&quot;); and (2) the
members of the Queensboro Temporary Release Committee and the Queensboro
superintendent (the &quot;state defendants&quot;). He claimed that the state
defendants had violated his rights by &quot;revoking his Work Release and
reincarcerating him for welfare fraud when they knew from the administrative
law judge's decision . . . that he had not committed welfare fraud and was =
merely
exercising his federal rights to apply for benefits.&quot; He alleged that
these actions deprived him of liberty and property interests without due
process of law, amounted to unlawful retaliation for his exercise of various
constitutional and statutory&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; rights, &nbsp;[*83]&nbsp; and
infringed his </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
right to petition the government.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>While
discovery was proceeding, all defendants moved to dismiss the complaint
pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'>. Plaintiff then sought to amend his complaint to plead
additional facts, identify various defendants individually rather than by
occupational title, and add Officer Torres as a defendant. In reply papers,
city defendants submitted, <u>inter alia</u>, excerpts from the deposition
testimony of Blackett and Officer Torres. Plaintiff apparently objected to
consideration of materials outside the pleadings.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND06Y0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On January 13, 1999, in an opini=
on
that repeatedly indicated that Friedl's application for benefits violated a
condition of his work release, the district court dismissed the complaint in
its entirety and denied plaintiff's motion to amend. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND06X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Friedl v. Cit=
y of
New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1999 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 123</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0700000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>No. 97 CIV. 5=
453
(JSM), 1999 WL 11551</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (S.D.N.Y. Jan. 13, 1999). The c=
ourt
found that, although Friedl &quot;likely had a liberty interest in . . .
continued participation in the work release program,&quot; he &quot;failed =
to
allege how this liberty interest was deprived without due process of law&qu=
ot;
since he received notice, a hearing, &nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; and review of the
Committee's recommendation to revoke his release. <u>Id</u>. at *1. The
district court also found no unlawful retaliation in the revocation of his =
work
release since he had no constitutionally protected right under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
apply for public benefits. <u>See id</u>. at *2. Finally, the court found no
violation of plaintiff's </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
right to petition the government because Friedl had failed to establish that
the speech in his petition was a matter of public concern. <u>See id</u>. T=
his
appeal followed. Friedl later settled with the city defendants for $ 20,000=
 and
withdrew the appeal as against them. Accordingly, we will confine our
discussion to Friedl's claims against the state defendants.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>DISCUSSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, we consider whether the district court erred in, first, dismissing
plaintiff's complaint for failure to state a claim, and second, denying
plaintiff's motion to amend his complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I. <u>Dismissal
of the Complaint</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0970000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0990000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_6"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review <u>de novo</u> a district court's decision to dismiss a complaint for
failure to state a claim, taking all factual allegations in the complaint as
true and construing all reasonable inferences in favor of the plaintiff.
&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND07200=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VSG-9630-0038-X061-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lee v. Bankers
Trust Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VSG-9630-0038-X061-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 166 F.3d 54=
0,
543 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Dismissal is &quot;appropriate
only if 'it appears beyond doubt that the plaintiff can prove no set of fac=
ts
in support of his claim which would entitle him to relief.'&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0980000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X3C-B0V0-0038-X337-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Harris v. Cit=
y of
New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X3C-B0V0-0038-X337-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 186 F.3d 24=
3,
250 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND09B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-J5D0-003B-S1MW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Conley v. Gib=
son</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-J5D0-003B-S1MW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 355 U.S. 41,
45-46, 2 L. Ed. 2d 80, 78 S. Ct. 99 (1957))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We conclude that the district court here erred in dismissing plaintiff's
complaint against the state defendants because: (1) the court considered
materials outside the pleadings in ruling on the motion to dismiss; and (2)=
 the
complaint states a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. Materials Outside the Pleadin=
gs</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0BW0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0BY0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_6"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;When
matters outside the pleadings are presented in response to a 12(b)(6)
motion,&quot; a district court must either &quot;exclude the additional
material and decide the motion on the complaint alone&quot; or &quot;convert
the motion to one for summary judgment under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
56</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'> and afford all parties the opportunity to present
supporting material.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0BV0000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0X40-001B-K3G0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fonte v. Boar=
d of
Managers of Continental Towers Condominium</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0X40-001B-K3G0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 848 F.2d 24=
, 25
(2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0CC0000400"></a>This conversion requirement =
is
strictly enforced whenever there is a &quot;legitimate possibility&quot; th=
at
the district court relied on material outside the complaint in ruling on the
motion.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0BX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WMS-64C0-0038-X4WX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Amaker v. Wei=
ner</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WMS-64C0-0038-X4WX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 179 F.3d 48=
, 50
(2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0CC0000400_2"></a>Th=
us, a
district court errs when it &quot;considers affidavits and exhibits submitt=
ed
by&quot; defendants, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0C00000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H610-008H-V1WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kopec v. Coug=
hlin</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H610-008H-V1WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 922 F.2d 15=
2,
155 (2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, or relies on factual allegatio=
ns
contained in legal briefs or memoranda, <u>see </u>&nbsp;[*84]&nbsp; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0CD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0X40-001B-K3G0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fonte</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0X40-001B-K3G0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 848 F.2d at=
 25</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
in ruling on a 12(b)(6) motion to dismiss. Vacatur is required even where t=
he
court's ruling simply &quot;makes a connection not established by the compl=
aint
alone&quot; or contains an &quot;unexplained reference&quot; that &quot;rai=
ses
the possibility that it improperly relied on matters outside the pleading in
granting the defendant's </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
