/p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_6"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. Friedl articulates two claims under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
First, he alleges that he was denied procedural due process when prison
authorities withdrew him from the work release program. Second, he contends
that his withdrawal from the program amounts to a substantive due process
violation because his work release was revoked in retaliation for his
application for, and appeal of the denial of, public benefits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0GD0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In our view, plaintiff has state=
d a
claim for violation of his procedural due process rights. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0GJ0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc4_6"></a>&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp;
Prisoners on work release have a liberty interest in continued participatio=
n in
such programs. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0CW00004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW80-0038-X2C3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kim v. Hursto=
n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW80-0038-X2C3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 182 F.3d 11=
3,
117 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0GF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YRD0-0039-M2VC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tracy v. Sala=
mack</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YRD0-0039-M2VC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 572 F.2d 39=
3,
395-96 (2d Cir. 1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (<u>per curiam</u>). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0GJ0000400_2"></a>The Due Process Clause pro=
tects
inmates against &quot;atypical and significant&quot; deprivation of liberty=
, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0GH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S0D-H4X0-003B-R24N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sandin v. Con=
ner</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S0D-H4X0-003B-R24N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 515 U.S. 47=
2,
484, 132 L. Ed. 2d 418, 115 S. Ct. 2293 (1995)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
including withdrawal of work release. Thus, prisoners are entitled to
procedural due process before they are subjected to such a deprivation of
liberty.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J70000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Friedl's complaint successfully
alleged facts demonstrating that the procedures used to remove him were
insufficient to satisfy minimum due process requirements. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J90000400"></a>In the parole revocation con=
text,
the Supreme Court has held that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_5"></a>due
process requires: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J90000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J70000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) written notice of the claimed
violations of parole; (b) disclosure to the parolee of evidence against him;
(c) opportunity to be heard in person and to &nbsp;[*85]&nbsp; present . . .
evidence; (d) the right to confront and cross-examine adverse witnesses . .=
 .;
(e) a &quot;neutral and detached&quot; hearing body such as a traditional
parole board . . .; and&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; (f) a written statement by the
factfinders as to the evidence relied on and reasons for revoking parole.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J90000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J70000400_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64XPBB7T1N000RGP78005YB"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0KS0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D510-003B-S236-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morrissey v.
Brewer</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D510-003B-S236-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 408 U.S. 47=
1,
489, 33 L. Ed. 2d 484, 92 S. Ct. 2593 (1972)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0KS0000400_2"></a>The Court has extended such
protections to revocation of probation, <u>see </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0J80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CVD0-003B-S2SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gagnon v.
Scarpelli</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CVD0-003B-S2SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 411 U.S. 77=
8,
782, 36 L. Ed. 2d 656, 93 S. Ct. 1756 (1973)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
&quot;good time credits,&quot; <u>see </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0JB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CBK0-003B-S1Y2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wolff v.
McDonnell</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CBK0-003B-S1Y2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 418 U.S. 53=
9,
558, 41 L. Ed. 2d 935, 94 S. Ct. 2963 (1974)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and pre-parole release, <u>see </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0KN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-J150-003B-R2DC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Young v. Harp=
er</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-J150-003B-R2DC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 520 U.S. 14=
3,
152-53, 137 L. Ed. 2d 270, 117 S. Ct. 1148 (1997)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and similar requirements would logically apply to revocation of work releas=
e. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0KS0000400_3"></a>In addition, the Court has
