0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>does
not guarantee inmates the wherewithal to transform themselves into litigati=
ng
engines capable of filing everything from shareholder derivative actions to
slip-and-fall claims. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I2CXS4CCTKY000YJP8D002FF"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VK0000400"></a>The tools it requires to be
provided are those that the inmates need in order to attack their sentences,
directly or collaterally, and in order to challenge the conditions of their
confinement. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I64XPBB8T3C000RGP78005YG"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VN0000400"></a>Impairment of any other liti=
gating
capacity is simply&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; one of the incidental (and perfectly
constitutional) consequences of conviction and incarceration.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VN0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VK0000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0TG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S04-RP80-003B-R23X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lewis v. Case=
y</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S04-RP80-003B-R23X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 518 U.S. 34=
3,
355, 135 L. Ed. 2d 606, 116 S. Ct. 2174 (1996)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Sitting <u>in banc</u>, a sharply divided Sixth Circuit recently interprete=
d <u>Lewis</u>
to mean that &quot;a prisoner's right to access the courts extends to direct
appeals, habeas corpus applications, and civil rights claims only.&quot; </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VYT-J460-0038-X3J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thaddeus-X v.
Blatter</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VYT-J460-0038-X3J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 175 F.3d 37=
8,
391 (6th Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (<u>in banc</u>). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>However,
these pronouncements do not alter our conclusion that Friedl's adjudication=
 was
constitutionally protected. Nothing in <u>Lewis</u> forecloses an
administrative challenge by a prisoner to the conditions of his confinement,
and we decline to follow the restrictive interpretation placed on <u>Lewis<=
/u>
by the Sixth Circuit in <u>Thaddeus-X</u>, since not every &quot;challenge =
to
the conditions of confinement&quot; takes the form of a civil rights action=
. In
particular, Friedl's administrative adjudication, though it involved no civ=
il
rights claim, plainly implicated &nbsp;[*87]&nbsp; the conditions of his
confinement: he wanted to discover whether, as a result of his status as a
participant in the work release&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; program, he was entitled =
to
public assistance benefits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VR0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0Y80000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, we reject the
contention, accepted by the district court, that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_4"></a>where the plaintiff alleges retaliat=
ion
for protected speech in the form of a petition to the government, he must
&quot;establish that the speech contained in his petition to the government=
 was
a matter of public concern.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0VP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Friedl</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1999 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 123</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, at *6, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0WG000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1999 WL 11551=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
at *2 (citing </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0WJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GVF0-003B-P50N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>White Plains
Towing Corp. v. Patterson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GVF0-003B-P50N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 991 F.2d 10=
49,
1058 (2d Cir. 1993))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0YB0000400"></a>The &quot;public concern&quo=
t;
requirement, developed in the context of public employee speech, has no pla=
ce
in the context of prisoner petitions for redress of grievances, which typic=
ally
address matters of personal concern. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0WM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VYT-J460-0038-X3J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thaddeus-X</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VYT-J460-0038-X3J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 175 F.3d at=
 392</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0YB0000400_2"></a><u>Cf</u>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0Y90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1PG0-006F-M4R0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Graham v.
Henderson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1PG0-006F-M4R0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 89 F.3d 75,=
 77
(2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (allowing a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim to go forward where a prisoner claimed retaliation based on his petit=
ions
regarding the elimination of showers in the prison workshop). In sum, we
conclude that Friedl's conduct in pursuing public assistance benefits was b=
oth
statutorily and constitutionally protected.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
Friedl explicitly and sufficiently alleges that&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; prison
officials revoked his work release in retaliation for this protected conduc=
t.
According to the complaint, there is a clear causal chain: Friedl appealed =
the
denial of benefits; the ALJ reversed this denial; an HRA worker informed
Friedl's parole officer of his application for benefits; the officer charged
Friedl with inmate misbehavior; Friedl was brought before the Temporary Rel=
ease
Committee; the Committee recommended revocation of his work release;
ultimately, within ten days of the date the parole officer was informed of
Friedl's application for public benefits, his work release was withdrawn. T=
hese
allegations of retaliation are not conclusory; they are sufficiently specif=
ic
to state a claim. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
may well be that the state defendants can defend this action for retaliatio=
n by
successfully establishing a right under the terms of the work release progr=
am
to remove Friedl from the program when he applied for public benefits. The
parties strongly dispute whether work release was explicitly conditioned on=
 not
applying for benefits. This issue is more appropriately resolved after
discovery, either on a motion for summary judgment or at trial.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.
<u>Denial of Motion to Amend</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;
On August 14, 1998, plaintiff moved to amend his complaint based on informa=
tion
acquired during depositions and other discovery. First, he wanted to drop t=
he
New York City Department of Human Services as a defendant, identify some cu=
rrent
defendants by name (specifically, Blackett, the members of the Temporary
Release Committee, and the Deputy Superintendent of the prison), and add
Officer Torres as a defendant. Second, he sought to plead additional facts,
including alleged actions by and communications between Blackett and Officer
Torres aimed at undermining Friedl's application for public benefits and
punishing him for applying. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0YD0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc13_3"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
responsive pleadings have been filed, subsequent amendments are possible on=
ly
with leave of court, but such leave &quot;shall be freely given when justic=
e so
requires.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
15(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1080000400"></a>In
general, district courts should not deny leave unless there is a substantial
reason to do so, such as excessive delay, prejudice to the opposing party, =
or
futility. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND0YC0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJ3-7D40-0038-X1H1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Jones v. N.Y.
Div. of Military and Naval Affairs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJ3-7D40-0038-X1H1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 166 F.3d 45=
, 50
(2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (futility); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TW9-R490-0038-X4FX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>MacDraw, Inc.=
 v.
CIT Group Equip. Fin., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TW9-R490-0038-X4FX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 157 F.3d 95=
6,
962 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; (<u>per curia=
m</u>)
(delay or prejudice). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1080000400_2"></a>We
review a district court's denial of a motion to amend for abuse of discreti=
on;
but where the &quot;court based its denial . . . on a conclusion of law [,]=
 . .
. that conclusion of law is subject to review <u>de novo</u>.&quot; </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1070000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S29-WXT0-0038-X4Y6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sheldon v. PHH
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S29-WXT0-0038-X4Y6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 135 F.3d 84=
8,
851-52 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1120000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*88]&nbsp; The district c=
ourt
here denied plaintiff's motion to amend his complaint &quot;because plainti=
ff
has failed to state a cause of action for any of his claims.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND1090000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Friedl</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1999 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 123</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, at *6, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F7DNF50K1MND113000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VJX-7JR0-0038-Y450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1999 WL 11551=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
at *3. However, as discussed above, plaintiff's complaint does state a claim
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1983</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>; thus, the district court's disposition was based on an
error of law. Moreover, there has been no showing of either undue delay, gi=
ven
that the amendment was proposed only after discovery revealed additional
relevant facts, or prejudice to the defendants, given the minimal extent of=
 the
proposed changes. Accordingly, on remand, we instruct the district court to
grant plaintiff leave to amend his complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we conclude that the district court erred in dismiss=
ing
plaintiff's complaint against the state&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; defendants becaus=
e:
(1) the court considered materials outside the pleadings in ruling on the
motion to dismiss; and (2) the complaint states a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1983<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We also find that the district court should have permitted Friedl to amend =
his
complaint. Accordingly, we vacate the judgment of the district court and re=
mand
for further proceedings consistent with these determinations. The costs of =
this
appeal shall be taxed against the appellees.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

