mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In 1996, plaintiffs-appellants f=
iled
this securities fraud class action, alleging violations of sections 10(b) a=
nd
20(a) of the Securities Exchange Act (&quot;the 1934 Act&quot;) and </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
promulgated thereunder. In two opinions issued in&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; 1998, the
district court dismissed both the original complaint and the plaintiffs'
amended complaint pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7025-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78u-4(b)(3)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> for failure to plead with sufficient particularity facts
supporting a strong inference that the defendants had acted fraudulently. <=
u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND0GM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak v. Kasa=
ks</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 997 F. Supp.
425 (S.D.N.Y. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;<u>Novak I</u>&quot;)
(dismissing original complaint); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND0GP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak v. Kasa=
ks</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 F. Supp.=
 2d
658 (S.D.N.Y. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;<u>Novak II</u>&quot;)
(dismissing amended complaint). On appeal, the appellants contend that the
district judge erred in granting the defendants' motions to dismiss.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
light of Second Circuit precedent and the provisions of the Private Securit=
ies
Litigation Reform Act (&quot;PSLRA&quot;), we hold that the district court
erred in: (1) concluding that the plaintiffs had failed to plead sufficient
facts to support a strong inference of fraudulent intent; and (2) imposing =
an
exceedingly onerous burden on the plaintiffs with respect to their obligati=
on
to plead facts with particularity. We see no persuasive alternative grounds=
 for
upholding the district court's dismissal of the complaint. Accordingly,
&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; we vacate the judgment of the district court and remand f=
or
further proceedings consistent with these determinations. In addition, we
instruct the district court to allow the plaintiffs to replead to the extent
they wish to do so in light of this opinion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>BACKGROUND</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
April 25, 1996, plaintiffs Carol Novak and Robert Nieman brought this actio=
n on
behalf of all purchasers of the common stock of the AnnTaylor Stores
Corporation between February 3, 1994, and May 4, 1995 (the &quot;Class
Period&quot;). In their complaint, the plaintiffs named two groups of
defendants: (1) <u>the AnnTaylor defendants</u>, both the corporation itsel=
f --
which, through its wholly-owned subsidiary, defendant AnnTaylor, Inc., is a
specialty retailer of women's clothing, shoes, and accessories -- and sever=
al
officers at the highest level of management; and (2) <u>the Merrill Lynch
defendants</u>, a group of entities and individuals that collectively held a
dominant share of AnnTaylor stock and sold a significant fraction of their
holdings during the Class Period.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint -- in both its original and amended forms -- essentially alleges
that, during the Class Period, the defendants &nbsp;[*304]&nbsp; made, or
controlled others who made, &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; materially false and misleadi=
ng
statements and omissions concerning the financial performance of AnnTaylor
(&quot;the Company&quot;), primarily by failing properly to account for
millions of dollars of inventory. According to the plaintiffs, the defendan=
ts
knowingly and intentionally issued financial statements that overstated
AnnTaylor's financial condition by accounting for inventory that they knew =
to
be obsolete and nearly worthless at inflated values and by deliberately fai=
ling
to adhere to the Company's publicly stated markdown policy. The following f=
acts
are taken largely from the plaintiffs' complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs' specific allegations focus on AnnTaylor's so-called &quot;Box a=
nd
Hold&quot; practice, whereby a substantial and growing quantity of out-of-d=
ate
inventory was stored in several warehouses during the Class Period without
being marked down. Internal Company documents (&quot;Weekly Reports&quot;) =
--
distributed at regular Monday morning merchandise meetings in which the
AnnTaylor defendants participated -- distinguished between regular inventory
and &quot;Box and Hold&quot; inventory. According to the complaint, these r=
eports
demonstrated that: (1) much of the &quot;Box and Hold&quot; inventory was
several years&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; old and thus unlikely to be sold at full pri=
ce,
if at all; and (2) the levels of such inventory grew significantly during t=
he
Class Period, from about 10% to about 34% of total inventory. However,
AnnTaylor's public financial statements did not distinguish between types of
inventory, nor did AnnTaylor write off any of the &quot;Box and Hold&quot;
inventory during the Class Period, allegedly in violation of Generally Acce=
pted
Accounting Principles (&quot;GAAP&quot;) that required markdowns under these
circumstances. Instead, the defendants made or caused to be made a series of
positive statements to the public about the status of AnnTaylor's inventori=
es,
describing them at various points during the Class Period as &quot;under
control,&quot; &quot;in good shape,&quot; and at &quot;reasonable&quot; or
&quot;expected&quot; levels; stating that &quot;no major or unusual markdow=
ns
were anticipated&quot;; and attributing rising levels of inventory to growt=
h,
expansion, and planned future sales.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs contend that this course of conduct amounts to securities fraud.=
 Had
AnnTaylor taken appropriate write-downs, they argue, the Company's earnings
would have been substantially lower than reported. Thus, the AnnTaylor
defendants' alleged deception painted&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; too rosy a picture of
the Company's current performance and future prospects and kept the company=
's
stock price at an artificially high level during the Class Period. Accordin=
g to
the amended complaint, during this time,</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
