<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>However,
these general standards offer little insight into precisely what actions and
behaviors constitute recklessness sufficient for &sect; 10(b) liability. It=
 is
the actual facts of our securities fraud cases that provide the most concre=
te
guidance as to the types of allegations required to meet the pre-PSLRA plea=
ding
standard in this circuit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to these cases, securities fraud claims typically have sufficed to state a
claim based on recklessness when they have specifically alleged defendants'
knowledge of facts or access to information contradicting their public
statements. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND16B0000400"></a>Under such
circumstances, defendants knew or, more importantly, should have known that
they were misrepresenting material facts related to the corporation.
&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; Thus, for example, the pleading standard was met where t=
he
plaintiffs alleged that the defendants made or authorized statements that s=
ales
to China would be &quot;an important new source of revenue&quot; when they =
knew
or should have known that Chinese import restrictions in place at the time
would severely limit such sales. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND16D00004=
00"></a><u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1690000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CS0-003B-515C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cosmas v. Has=
sett</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CS0-003B-515C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 886 F.2d 8,=
 12
(2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND16D0000400_2"></a>Si=
milarly,
the pleading standard was met where the plaintiffs alleged that the defenda=
nts
released to the investing public several highly positive predictions about =
the
marketing prospects of a computer system to record hotel guests' long-dista=
nce
telephone calls when they knew or should have known several facts about the
system and its consumers that revealed &quot;grave uncertainties and proble=
ms
concerning future sales of&quot; the system.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND16C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JH70-0039-P4N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Goldman</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JH70-0039-P4N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 754 F.2d at
1063, 1070</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1710000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under certain circumstances, we =
have
found allegations of recklessness to be sufficient where plaintiffs alleged
facts demonstrating that defendants failed to review or check information t=
hat
they had a duty to monitor, or ignored obvious signs of fraud. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1730000400"></a>Thus, the pleading standard =
was
met where the plaintiff alleged that&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; the &nbsp;[*309]&nbs=
p;
defendant, his broker, consistently reassured the plaintiff that the invest=
ment
advisor responsible for the plaintiff's portfolio &quot;knew what he was
doing&quot; but never actually investigated the advisor's decisions to
determine &quot;whether there was a basis for the [defendant's]
assertions.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1700000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YX60-0039-M41N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rolf</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YX60-0039-M41N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 570 F.2d at
47-48</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. Similarly, the pleading standard was met where the
defendant allegedly included false statements in SEC filings despite &quot;=
the
obviously evasive and suspicious statements made to him&quot; by the corpor=
ate
officials upon whom he was relying for this information and despite outside
counsel's recommendation that these statements not be included. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1730000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1720000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S66-5N70-0038-X00Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>SEC v. McNult=
y</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S66-5N70-0038-X00Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 137 F.3d 73=
2,
741 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18D0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18G0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>At the same time, however, we ha=
ve
identified <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_3"></a>several important
limitations on the scope of liability for securities fraud based on reckless
conduct. First, we have refused to allow plaintiffs to proceed with allegat=
ions
of &quot;fraud by hindsight.&quot; <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1740000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3W61-2SM0-0038-X31R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stevelman</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3W61-2SM0-0038-X31R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 174 F.3d at=
 85</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18J0000400"></a>Corporate officials need not=
 be
clairvoyant; they are only responsible for revealing those material facts
reasonably available to them. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YH00-0039-M1CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Denny v. Barb=
er</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YH00-0039-M1CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 576 F.2d 46=
5,
470 (2d Cir. 1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18J0000400_2"></a>Thus, allegations that
defendants should have anticipated future events and made certain disclosur=
es
earlier than they actually did do not suffice to make out a claim of securi=
ties
fraud. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND18H0000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Acito</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 47 F.3d at =
53</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1950000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, as long as the public
statements are consistent with reasonably available data, corporate officia=
ls
need not present an overly gloomy or cautious picture of current performance
and future prospects. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND19V0000400"></a><u>=
See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1940000400"></a><a
