<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Our
own review of the text and legislative history leads us to a middle ground.=
 We
conclude that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_3"></a>the PSLRA effective=
ly
raised the nationwide pleading standard to that previously existing in this
circuit and no higher (with the exception of the &quot;with particularity&q=
uot;
requirement). At the same time, however, we believe that Congress's failure=
 to
include language about motive and opportunity suggests that we need not be
wedded to these concepts in articulating the prevailing standard. We are le=
d to
these conclusions by the considerations that follow.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to gauge the implications of paragraph (b)(2), we apply familiar cano=
ns
of statutory construction. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1G90000400"></=
a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc18_3"></a>We look first to the text of the sta=
tute.
If that language is plain and its meaning sufficiently clear, we need look =
no
further. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1GC0000400"></a><u>See </u></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1FR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJK0-003B-R4X5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Connecticut N=
at'l
Bank v. Germain</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJK0-003B-R4X5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. 24=
9,
254, 117 L. Ed. 2d 391, 112 S. Ct. 1146 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1GC0000400_2"></a>Only if the text of the st=
atute
is not unambiguous do we turn for guidance to legislative history and the
purposes of the statute. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1GB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B0C0-0039-N4CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dowling v. Un=
ited
States</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B0C0-0039-N4CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 20=
7,
218, 87 L. Ed. 2d 152, 105 S. Ct. 3127 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;
Applying these principles, we conclude that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclsc=
c19_3"></a>the
enactment of paragraph (b)(2) did not change the basic pleading standard for
scienter in this circuit. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1GF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In this case, our interpretive t=
ask
begins and ends with the text of the statute. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HR0000400"></a>In drafting paragraph (b)(2),
Congress specifically incorporated this circuit's &quot;strong inference&qu=
ot;
language to define the pleading standard for securities fraud cases. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HT0000400"></a><u>Compare</u> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7025-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78u-4(b)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (requiring plaintiffs to &quot;state with particularity
facts giving rise to a strong inference that the defendant acted with the
required state of mind&quot;), <u>with </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1GD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Acito</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 47 F.3d at =
52</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;Plaintiffs must allege facts that give rise to a strong inference of
fraudulent intent.&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HT0000400_2">=
</a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HR0000400_2"></a>We agree with the Third Ci=
rcuit
that this &quot;use of the Second Circuit's language compels the conclusion
that the Reform Act establishes a pleading standard approximately equal in
stringency to that of the Second Circuit.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Advanta
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 180 F.3d at=
 534</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <u>Cf.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9170-003B-P37M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Johnson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9170-003B-P37M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 14 F.3d 766,
770 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (finding that Congress's use of
&quot;substantially identical language&quot; to that of an earlier
statute&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; &quot;bespeaks an intention to import&quot; judic=
ial
interpretations of that language into the new statute).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Given
the absence of ambiguity in the statutory text, no resort to legislative
history or the purposes of the PSLRA is required. In any event, there is
nothing in these sources that would alter our conclusion. As far as the gen=
eral
purposes of the PSLRA are concerned, Congress plainly sought to impose a
stricter nationwide pleading standard and did so. But this purpose does not
require raising the standard above that of this circuit, particularly in li=
ght
of the explicit Congressional recognition that our pre-PSLRA standard was t=
he
most stringent in the nation. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1J70000400"=
></a><u>See</u>
H.R. Conf. Rep. No. 104-369, at 41. &quot;In many jurisdictions, adoption o=
f a
'strong inference' standard will substantially heighten the barriers to
pleading scienter, a result Congress expressly intended. Moreover, even <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20_3"></a>in jurisdictions already employing t=
he
Second Circuit standard, the additional requirement that plaintiffs
&nbsp;[*311]&nbsp; state facts 'with particularity' represents a heightenin=
g of
the standard.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1HV0000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Advanta
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 180 F.3d at=
 534</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1J90000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Meanwhile, in our view, as is so
often the case with legislative history&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; generally, the
legislative history of the PSLRA contains &quot;conflicting expressions of
legislative intent&quot; with respect to the pleading requirement. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1J80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WRH-VW90-0038-X2C5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 533</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
For example, while the Conference Committee rejected language from the Sena=
te
bill that would have adopted the Second Circuit rule wholesale, including
language about motive and opportunity and recklessness, <u>see</u> H.R. Con=
f.
Rep. No. 104-369, at 41 &amp; 48 n.23, the Senate Committee reporting the b=
ill
stated that it was proposing not &quot;a new and untested pleading standard
that would generate additional litigation,&quot; but rather &quot;a uniform
standard modeled upon the pleading standard of the Second Circuit.&quot; S.
Rep. No. 104-98, at 15 (1995), <u>reprinted in</u> 1995 U.S.C.C.A.N. 679, 6=
94
(noting that courts interpreting the proposed &quot;strong inference&quot;
pleading standard might find Second Circuit case law &quot;instructive&quot=
;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
all is said and done, we believe that the enactment of paragraph (b)(2) did=
 not
change the basic pleading standard for scienter in this circuit (except by =
the
addition of the words &quot;with particularity&quot;). Accordingly, we hold
that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21_3"></a>the PSLRA adopted our &quot;=
strong
inference&quot; standard: In order to plead&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; scienter,
plaintiffs must &quot;state with particularity facts giving rise to a strong
inference that the defendant acted with the required state of mind,&quot; as
required by the language of the Act itself. Although litigants and lower co=
urts
need and should not employ or rely on magic words such as &quot;motive and
opportunity,&quot; we believe that our prior case law may be helpful in
providing guidance as to how the &quot;strong inference&quot; standard may =
be
met. Therefore, in applying this standard, district courts should look to t=
he
cases and factors discussed in Section I.B.1 above to determine whether
plaintiffs have pleaded facts giving rise to the requisite &quot;strong
inference.&quot; These cases suggest, in brief, that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc22_3"></a>the inference may arise where the
complaint sufficiently alleges that the defendants: (1) benefitted in a
concrete and personal way from the purported fraud, <u>see supra</u> at <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>[pages 14-15]</b>; (2) engaged in
deliberately illegal behavior, <u>see supra</u> at <b style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-weight:
normal'>[page 15]</b>; (3) knew facts or had access to information suggesti=
ng
that their public statements were not accurate, <u>see supra</u> at <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>[pages 16-17]</b>; or (4) failed to c=
heck
information they had a duty to monitor, <u>see supra</u>&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; =
at <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>[pages 17-18]</b>. We now turn to the
complaint in this case to determine whether the plaintiffs have met their
burden to plead scienter.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3.
Strong Inference of Fraudulent Intent on the Part of the AnnTaylor Defendan=
ts</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court concluded that the plaintiffs had failed to plead facts givi=
ng
rise to a strong inference of the defendants' fraudulent intent, as require=
d to
state a claim under &sect; 10(b). We disagree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to the complaint, the AnnTaylor defendants knew at all relevant times that =
the
Company had serious inventory problems that they sought to disguise by adop=
ting
the &quot;Box and Hold&quot; scheme. By refusing to mark down inventory they
knew to be &quot;worthless,&quot; &quot;obsolete,&quot; and
&quot;unsalable,&quot; the defendants acted &quot;intentionally and
deliberately&quot; to artificially inflate AnnTaylor's reported financial
results. They discussed the need to mark down inventory but refused to do so
because that would damage the Company's financial prospects. Further, in
approving the inventory management practices of &quot;Box and Hold,&quot; t=
he
defendants knowingly sanctioned procedures that violated the Company's own
markdown policy, as stated in the Company's public filings. In doing so,
they&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; caused those filings to be materially misleading in =
that
the disclosed policy no longer reflected actual practice. Lastly, despite
knowledge of the true reasons for rising inventory levels, the defendants m=
ade
repeated statements to the investment community either offering false
&nbsp;[*312]&nbsp; reassurances that inventory was under control or giving
false explanations for its growth. In short, the Complaint alleges that the
defendants engaged in conscious misstatements with the intent to deceive. T=
here
is no doubt that this pleading satisfies the standard for scienter under <u=
>Hochfelder</u>,
and the requirement of the PSLRA that plaintiffs state facts with particula=
rity
that give rise to a strong inference of the required state of mind.</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JC0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In the end, we believe that the
district court applied the correct standard but erroneously found that this
standard was not met on these pleadings. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JT0000400"></a>According to the district co=
urt,
the scienter requirement can be satisfied by pleading either &quot;conscious
recklessness&quot; -- i.e., a state of mind &quot;approximating actual inte=
nt,
and not merely a heightened form of negligence&quot; -- or &quot;actual
intent.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak I</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 997 F. Supp=
. at
430</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>. This was an accurate statement&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; of the
law. However, the district court believed that the facts pleaded by the
plaintiffs supported nothing more than an inference that the managers of
AnnTaylor disagreed over matters of business judgment, such as the valuatio=
n of
inventory and the timing of markdowns. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JT0000400_2"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak II</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 F. Supp.=
 2d
at 660</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. This was incorrect as a matter of law. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23_3"></a>When managers deliberately make mate=
rially
false statements concerning inventory with the intent to deceive the invest=
ment
community, they have engaged in conduct actionable under the securities law=
s.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C. Particularity of the Facts
Pleaded</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court also found the facts pleaded by the plaintiffs insufficiently
particularized, in large part because they did not reveal the identity of t=
he
personal sources of their critical factual allegations. We disagree with the
district court's reasoning and accordingly vacate and remand for further
proceedings consistent with the discussion that follows.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
discussed above, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24_3"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
has long required plaintiffs in securities fraud cases to state &quot;the
circumstances constituting fraud . . . with particularity.&quot; The PSLRA
imposed an additional requirement: whenever plaintiffs&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp;
allege, on information and belief, that defendants made material misstateme=
nts
or omissions, the complaint must &quot;state with particularity all facts on
which that belief is formed.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7025-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78u-4(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. This requirement plainly applies in this case. In nume=
rous
places in their complaint, the plaintiffs allege, based on information and
belief, that the AnnTaylor defendants made materially misleading statements=
 or
omissions. Most importantly, they allege that the defendants made false
statements concerning the value of inventory because &quot;Box and Hold&quo=
t;
merchandise was &quot;unsalable,&quot; &quot;obsolete,&quot; and &quot;near=
ly
worthless,&quot; and its &quot;actual value was nearly zero.&quot; In order=
 to
survive at this stage, the complaint must state with particularity sufficie=
nt
facts to support the belief that the &quot;Box and Hold&quot; inventory was=
 of
limited value, and accordingly that the defendants' positive public stateme=
nts
concerning inventory growth were false and misleading. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JW0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KC0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court concluded that
the plaintiffs had failed to meet these particularity requirements, in
substantial part because they failed to reveal their confidential sources f=
or
some of the facts on which their&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; belief in the essential
worthlessness of the &quot;Box and Hold&quot; inventory was based. <u>See <=
/u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak I</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S77-66W0-0038-Y3ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 997 F. Supp=
. at
431-32</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1JX0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak II</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 F. Supp.=
 2d
at 660-61</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The lower court found the plaintiffs' allegations in t=
his
respect at worst &quot;conclusory, unsupported and inflammatory,&quot; and =
at
best &quot;based upon reports by . . . anonymous 'former employees'&quot; w=
ho
should have been identified by name.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Novak II</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V4R-WKS0-0038-Y0F2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 F. Supp.=
 2d
at 661</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. The district court's reasoning and conclusions were fl=
awed
in several respects.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
one thing, the complaint provides specific facts concerning the Company's
significant write-off of inventory directly following the Class Period, whi=
ch
tends to support the plaintiffs' contention that inventory &nbsp;[*313]&nbs=
p;
was seriously overvalued at the time the purportedly misleading statements =
were
made. Specifically, the plaintiffs allege that: (1) in AnnTaylor's May 1995
reporting of its first quarter fiscal 1995 results, the Company &quot;admit=
ted
to analysts that its inventories were too high&quot; and that &quot;invento=
ry
liquidation&quot; would follow; (2) in AnnTaylor's July 29, 1995 10-Q filed
with the SEC, it &quot;admitted that the decrease in the Company's
gross&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp; profit percentage was attributable to 'increased co=
st
of goods sold as a percentage of net sales, <u>primarily resulting from
markdowns</u>'&quot;; and (3) a January 22, 1996 Weekly Report showed that =
even
six months after the Class Period, substantial amounts of &quot;Box and
Hold&quot; inventory still dated from 1993 and 1994, which supports the
inference that inventory during the Class Period was similarly dated. Thus,=
 the
complaint identifies with particularity several documentary sources that
support the plaintiffs' belief that serious inventory problems existed duri=
ng
the Class Period itself.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
recognize that the complaint does not state with particularity every fact u=
pon
which this belief was based, since it is apparent that there were also pers=
onal
sources who were not specifically identified. However, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc25_2"></a>plaintiffs who rely on confidential
sources are not always required to name those sources, even when they make
allegations on information and belief concerning false or misleading
statements, as here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2D323JBGCN000V598K0038V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KF0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KW0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>First, there is nothing in the
caselaw of this circuit that requires plaintiffs to reveal confidential sou=
rces
at the pleading stage. The defendants rely heavily on </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3T70-0039-M2FK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Segan v. Drey=
fus
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3T70-0039-M2FK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 513 F.2d 695
(2d Cir. 1975)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>,&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; in which we held that a plaintiff's
complaint was insufficiently specific and rejected the argument that further
disclosure would, among other things, identify a confidential informant. <u=
>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3T70-0039-M2FK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3T70-0039-M2FK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 696</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KY0000400"></a>But in <u>Dreyfus</u>, we he=
ld
only that the plaintiff had to plead additional facts, not that the plainti=
ff
was required to reveal the name of the informant. <u>See id.</u> (&quot;A s=
uit
charging fraud may not be based on <u>facts</u> so secret that the defendan=
ts
cannot be told what they are.&quot;) (emphasis added). Some district courts=
 in
this circuit have on occasion stated that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires plaintiffs in securities fraud cases to allege the &quot;sources t=
hat
support the alleged specific facts,&quot; <u>e.g.</u>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1KX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CXJ0-0054-4347-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Blanchard v. =
Katz</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CXJ0-0054-4347-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 705 F. Supp.
1011, 1012 (S.D.N.Y. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1M00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-T7P0-0054-518X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Crystal v. Fo=
y</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-T7P0-0054-518X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 562 F. Supp.
422, 425 (S.D.N.Y. 1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, but in no case have they dismi=
ssed
a complaint for failure to identify confidential sources.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2D323JBM6T000V598K0038W"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1MF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, while paragraph (b)(1) m=
ay
compel revelation of confidential sources under certain circumstances, such
circumstances are not necessarily present in this case. The defendants poin=
t to
district court decisions outside this circuit that&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; hold or
imply that the PSLRA generally requires plaintiffs to include the names of
their confidential sources. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1MD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S45-ND50-00B1-F4JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Silicon
Graphics Inc. Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S45-ND50-00B1-F4JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 970 F. Supp.
746, 763 (N.D. Cal. 1997)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1MG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WMG-D190-0038-Y081-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Aetna I=
nc.
Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WMG-D190-0038-Y081-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1999 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 8038</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1MX0000400"></a>No. =
CIV.
A. MDL 1219, 1999 WL 354527, at *4 (E.D. Pa. May 26, 1999). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1N10000400"></a>However, this rule is based =
on a
misreading of the legislative history of the PSLRA. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1N10000400_2"></a>Specifically, the court in=
 <u>Silicon
Graphics</u> relied primarily on the hyperbolic statements of legislators
attempting (unsuccessfully) to amend the proposed Act to lighten plaintiffs'
pleading burden. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1N0000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S45-ND50-00B1-F4JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Silicon Graph=
ics</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S45-ND50-00B1-F4JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 970 F. Supp=
. at
763-64</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. In fact, the applicable provision of the law as ultima=
tely
enacted requires plaintiffs to plead only facts and makes no mention of the
sources of these facts. <u>See</u> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7025-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78u-4(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>More
fundamentally, our reading of the PSLRA rejects any notion that confidential
sources must be named as a general matter. In our view, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc26_2"></a>notwithstanding the use of the word
&quot;all,&quot; paragraph (b)(1) does not require that plaintiffs plead wi=
th
particularity every single fact upon which their beliefs
concerning&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp; false or misleading statements are based. Rath=
er,
plaintiffs need only plead with particularity <u>sufficient</u> facts to
support &nbsp;[*314]&nbsp; those beliefs. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn72' href=3D"#_ftn72" name=3D"_ftnref72" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Accordingly, where plaintiffs rely on confidential personal sources but als=
o on
other facts, they need not name their sources as long as the latter facts
provide an adequate basis for believing that the defendants' statements were
false. Moreover, even if personal sources must be identified, there is no
requirement that they be named, provided they are described in the complaint
with sufficient particularity to support the probability that a person in t=
he
position occupied by the source would possess the information alleged. In b=
oth
of these situations, the plaintiffs will have pleaded enough facts to suppo=
rt
their belief, even though some arguably relevant facts have been left out.
Accordingly, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc27_2"></a>a complaint can meet=
 the
new pleading requirement imposed by paragraph (b)(1) by providing documenta=
ry
evidence and/or a sufficient general description of the personal sources of=
 the
plaintiffs' beliefs.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NF0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc28_2"></a>Thus, we find no requirement in exis=
ting
law that, in the ordinary course, complaints in securities fraud cases must
name confidential sources, and we see no reason to impose such a requirement
under the circumstances of this case. &quot;The primary purpose of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
to afford defendant fair notice of the plaintiff's claim and the factual gr=
ound
upon which it is based.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1N20=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5150-003B-53WT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ross v. Bolto=
n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5150-003B-53WT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 904 F.2d 81=
9,
823 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. This purpose, which also under=
lies
paragraph (b)(1), can be served without requiring plaintiffs to name their
confidential sources as long as they supply sufficient specific facts to
support their allegations. Imposing a general requirement of disclosure of
confidential sources serves no legitimate pleading purpose while it could d=
eter
informants from providing critical information to investigators in meritori=
ous
cases or invite retaliation against them. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
express no view as to whether the plaintiffs' allegations in this case were
sufficiently particularized. Instead, we remand to the district court with
instructions to: (1) allow the plaintiffs to replead in light of our discus=
sion
above; and (2) reconsider the particularity of the plaintiffs'
&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; pleadings in light of the proper standards.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.
<u>Alternative Grounds for Dismissal</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
appellees argue that we still may affirm the district court's dismissal
because: (1) the appellants have not adequately alleged that statements mad=
e by
securities analysts can be attributed to the AnnTaylor defendants; and (2) =
the
alleged false and misleading statements made during the Class Period are not
actionable in any event. We reject both arguments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NH0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc29_2"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
the law of this circuit, plaintiffs may state a claim against corporate
officials for false and misleading information disseminated through analyst=
s'
reports by alleging that the officials either: (1) &quot;intentionally fost=
ered
a mistaken belief concerning a material fact&quot; that was incorporated in=
to
reports; or (2) adopted or placed their &quot;imprimatur&quot; on the repor=
ts. <u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-71W0-0039-W420-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Elkind v. Lig=
gett
&amp; Myers, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-71W0-0039-W420-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 635 F.2d 15=
6,
163-64 (2d Cir. 1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NK0000400"></a>This was plainly the case he=
re.
The plaintiffs alleged that the AnnTaylor defendants both made misstatements
that were later incorporated into analysts' reports <u>and</u> subsequently=
 (at
least implicitly) adopted the contents of those reports. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NK0000400_2"></a>These allegations were
sufficiently detailed&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; to meet the pleading threshold beca=
use
generally the circumstances of the statements -- including dates and
participants -- were particularized. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Acito</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H190-001T-D1Y4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 47 F.3d at =
51</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*315]&nbsp; Moreover, the
types of statements the defendants are alleged to have made or adopted are
actionable. While statements containing simple economic projections,
expressions of optimism, and other puffery are insufficient, <u>see, e.g.</=
u>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-F550-008H-V355-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Friedman v.
Mohasco Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-F550-008H-V355-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 929 F.2d 77=
, 79
(2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, defendants may be liable for misrepresentations of
existing facts, <u>see </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F84PCC0K1MND1NP0000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-JGR0-006F-P4YC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Prudent=
ial
Sec. Inc. Ltd. Partnerships Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-JGR0-006F-P4YC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 930 F. Supp.
68, 74-75 (S.D.N.Y. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Here, the complaint alleges th=
at
the defendants did more than just offer rosy predictions; the defendants st=
ated
that the inventory situation was &quot;in good shape&quot; or &quot;under
control&quot; while they allegedly knew that the contrary was true. Assumin=
g,
as we must at this stage, the accuracy of the plaintiffs' allegations about
AnnTaylor's &quot;Box and Hold&quot; practices, these statements were plain=
ly
false and misleading.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we hold, first, that: (a) in order to plead scienter=
 in
securities fraud&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; cases, plaintiffs must &quot;state with
particularity facts giving rise to a strong inference that the defendant ac=
ted
with the required state of mind&quot;; and (b) the plaintiffs here pleaded
sufficient facts to establish a strong inference of fraudulent intent on the
part of the AnnTaylor defendants. Second, we hold that the district court
erred, under the circumstances of this case, by requiring the plaintiffs to
reveal the names of their confidential sources in order to meet the
particularity requirements of the PSLRA. On remand, we instruct the district
court to: (a) permit the plaintiffs to replead; and (b) evaluate their
pleadings anew in light of our interpretation of these requirements. Finall=
y,
we see no alternative grounds for affirming the district court's dismissal =
of
the plaintiffs' complaint. Accordingly, the judgment of the district court =
is
vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings consistent with these
rulings.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

