<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*199]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Barrington D. Parker</span>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>AlixPartners
LLP and McKinsey &amp; Co., Inc. are major competitors in a niche bankruptcy
advising&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; market involving estates with assets in excess of=
 one
billion dollars. Jay Alix, as assignee of AlixPartners, sued McKinsey &amp;
Co., Inc., three of its subsidiaries (together, &quot;McKinsey&quot;), and
several current or former McKinsey employees under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), 18 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1961 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>et seq.</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and state law. The amended complaint alleges that McKinsey secured lucrative
consulting assignments in this market by knowingly and repeatedly filing
disclosure statements in the Bankruptcy Court containing incomplete,
&nbsp;[*200]&nbsp; misleading, or false representations concerning conflict=
s of
interest. Alix alleges that this pattern of misrepresentations to the
Bankruptcy Court resulted in injury to AlixPartners through the loss of
engagements it otherwise would have secured and of substantial revenues tho=
se assignments
would have generated, as well as through the loss of the opportunity to com=
pete
for them in an unrigged market.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_8"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>RICO affords a private right of
action to &quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by reason =
of a
[RICO] violation.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Alix alleges that AlixPartners was directly harmed by
McKinsey's conduct because, had McKinsey truthfully and timely disclosed its
conflicts to the Bankruptcy Court, McKinsey would have been
disqualified&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; from obtaining at least some of the assignmen=
ts
it secured. In turn, Alix alleges that AlixPartners, because of its major
presence in this niche market, would have been retained in at least some of=
 the
cases.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Alix
also alleges a &quot;pay-to-play&quot; scheme under which McKinsey arranged
meetings between its clients and bankruptcy attorneys in exchange for exclu=
sive
bankruptcy assignment referrals from those attorneys. Consistent with this
scheme, Alix alleges that McKinsey offered to introduce AlixPartners to its
clients if Alix would &quot;drop[] the issues he had raised concerning
McKinsey's acknowledged pay-to-play scheme and its illegal disclosure
declarations.&quot; The District Court for the Southern District of New Yor=
k (Jesse
M. Furman, District Judge) dismissed Alix's RICO claims under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The court concluded that Alix's
allegations were insufficient to establish the required causal connection
between McKinsey's purported RICO violations and AlixPartners's injury. This
appeal followed. The dispositive issue is whether the amended complaint
adequately alleges proximate causation under RICO. We hold that it does and,
consequently, we vacate and remand for further proceedings.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Alix
is the&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; founder and a minority equity holder of AlixPartner=
s,
one of a handful of consulting firms operating in a high-end corporate
bankruptcy advising market. McKinsey Recovery &amp; Transformation Services
U.S., LLC (&quot;McKinsey RTS&quot;), a subsidiary of McKinsey &amp; Co., I=
nc.,
along with FIT Consulting, and Alvarez &amp; Marsal are among the other maj=
or
competitors in this market. The amended complaint alleges that AlixPartners,
FIT Consulting, and Alvarez &amp; Marsal were retained in 75% of the bankru=
ptcy
cases since 2010 involving assets over $ 1 billion in which McKinsey did not
serve as the advisor. Of those cases, AlixPartners obtained about 24% of the
contracts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_8"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In order to secure engagements,
bankruptcy advisors must demonstrate that they &quot;do not hold or represe=
nt
an interest adverse to the estate&quot; and are &quot;disinterested
persons&quot; within the meaning of the Bankruptcy Code. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S8T-0CK2-8T6X-73HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>11 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
327(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also id.</=
i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6GGG-2SM3-RRRC-N3PF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 101(14=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn73' href=3D"#_ftn73" name=3D"_ftnref73" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
When these requirements are satisfied, a bankruptcy professional may be
retained &quot;with the court's approval.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Id.</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S8T-0CK2-8T6X-73HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 327(a)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In addition, bankruptcy courts require that an application &nbsp;[*201]&nbs=
p;
for retention be &quot;accompanied by a verified statement of the person to=
 be
employed setting forth the person's connections with the
debtor,&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; creditor, any other party in interest, their
respective attorneys and accountants, the United States trustee, or any per=
son
employed in the office of the United States trustee.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6D9G-37F3-RSRX-S464-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Bankr=
. P.
2014(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The statements requiring these detailed disclosures are
submitted under penalties of perjury and are subject to the bankruptcy fraud
statute. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0P5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1746</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 152(2)-(3)</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GB2HM61Y0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GB2HM61Y0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Alix alleges that McKinsey viola=
ted
these disclosure requirements by submitting to the Bankruptcy Court false
statements in thirteen bankruptcy proceedings in which it was appointed
advisor.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn74' href=3D"#_ftn74" name=3D"=
_ftnref74"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Specifically, Alix alleges that, despite &quot;the size=
 and
complexity of McKinsey's business and business relationships&quot; as one of
the world's largest consulting firms, it &quot;disclosed <b style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-weight:
normal'>no</b> connections by name in its initial declarations for these ei=
ght
cases&quot; and disclosed only a few in supplemental declarations filed in =
only
two of the cases. Given AlixPartners's significant market share, Alix argue=
s,
if McKinsey had filed compliant disclosure statements, it would have been
disqualified from certain assignments and AlixPartners would have secured at
least some of the assignments from which McKinsey would have been disqualif=
ied.
In other words, Alix contends that AlixPartners's&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; injury w=
as a
foreseeable and direct consequence of McKinsey's failure to follow the law =
and
a fraud on the Bankruptcy Court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
amended complaint includes additional allegations concerning a pay-to-play
scheme orchestrated by McKinsey. On September 3, 2014, Alix alleges that he=
 met
with two former or current McKinsey partners, defendants Dominic Barton and
Robert Sternfels. During that meeting, Alix allegedly told Barton and Stern=
fels
that he had become aware of McKinsey's practice of agreeing to host meetings
between its clients and bankruptcy attorneys in exchange for exclusive
referrals of bankruptcy assignments from those attorneys, which, Alix warned
them, was illegal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
following month, Barton allegedly admitted the existence of, and McKinsey R=
TS's
participation in, the scheme and also admitted that his outside counsel
confirmed its illegality. Barton, acting on behalf of McKinsey, then agreed=
 to
remove the senior leadership of McKinsey RTS for this illegal conduct withi=
n 30
days of Barton's re-election as Global Managing Partner of McKinsey &amp; C=
o.
in January 2015, and to remove McKinsey&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; from the bankruptcy
consulting business by March 2015. In exchange, McKinsey allegedly asked Al=
ix
to &quot;refrain from acting at that time.&quot; In other words, Alix alleg=
ed
that McKinsey asked him not to pursue legal action targeting the pay-to-play
scheme or &nbsp;[*202]&nbsp; McKinsey's allegedly fraudulent disclosure sta=
tements.
But Barton, according to Alix, did not keep his end of the bargain. When Al=
ix
confronted him in October 2015 regarding McKinsey's continued misconduct,
Barton allegedly offered to introduce AlixPartners to Fortescue&#8212;a lar=
ge
Australian iron ore mining company&#8212;and Volvo Europe for possible
consulting assignments. Alix alleges that he declined these offers because =
he
construed them as &quot;blatant attempted pay-offs and bribes&quot; intende=
d to
silence him.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Alix
argues that McKinsey's pay-to-play scheme was aimed at eliminating the
competitive process by which debtors and their trustees select a bankruptcy
advisor. Additionally, Alix alleges that AlixPartners &quot;was never even
asked to pitch&quot; in three cases where it typically would have competed =
for
a contract and attributes this to McKinsey's alleged scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>McKinsey
moved to dismiss the complaint under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and the district court granted the motion,&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; while neverthel=
ess
noting that Alix's allegations were &quot;indeed concerning.&quot; Still, t=
he
district court, in a careful opinion navigating a body of case law that,
charitably speaking, is less than pellucid, found the allegations insuffici=
ent
to satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement. The court concluded that
&quot;independent intervening decisions&quot; of the trustees and the
bankruptcy court rendered the causal connection between the alleged miscond=
uct
and injury &quot;too remote, contingent, and indirect to sustain a RICO
claim.&quot; As to the pay-to-play allegations, the court concluded that th=
ey
too failed to meet the pleading standards and suffered from the same defect=
s as
the allegations concerning fraudulent disclosures because they did not
sufficiently narrow the gap between the alleged fraud and the alleged resul=
ting
injury. This appeal followed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GJ2HM62R0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc3_8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> a district court=
's
dismissal of a complaint pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
construing the complaint liberally, accepting all factual allegations in the
complaint as true, and drawing all reasonable inferences in the plaintiff's
favor. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GG2SF7VW0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:456H-9WR0-0038-X4H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Chambers v. T=
ime
Warner, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:456H-9WR0-0038-X4H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 282 F.3d 14=
7,
152 (2d Cir. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
