Conservation v. Fed. Energy Regul. Comm'n, 991 F.3d 439, 445 (2d Cir. 2021)=
</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
amended complaint initially contained several state-law claims in addition =
to
the federal RICO claims at issue on this appeal. After the district court
dismissed the federal claims under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it directed the parties to brief whether an independent basis for federal
jurisdiction over the remaining state law claims existed. Alix responded by
filing a notice to dismiss without prejudice, under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-22N1-6N19-F026-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 41</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, his state law claims, which he
later attempted to retract. In response, the district court ruled that Alix=
's
voluntary </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-22N1-6N19-F026-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 41</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
dismissal of state law claims was effective and could not be withdrawn. The
court also denied Alix's motion for entry of judgment on his &nbsp;[*203]&n=
bsp;
federal RICO claims and his alternative request to revive the state law cla=
ims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GK2HM62Y0040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc4_8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ordinarily,
immediate appeal is unavailable to a plaintiff, such as Alix, who seeks rev=
iew
of an adverse decision on some of his claims by voluntarily dismissing the
others without prejudice. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GM2HM63400100004=
00"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GK2HM62Y0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H76-M2M0-0038-X044-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rabbi Jacob
Joseph Sch. v. Province of Mendoza</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H76-M2M0-0038-X044-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 425 F.3d 20=
7,
210 (2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. That is because our jurisdicti=
on
is limited to appeals from final decisions of the district court, which are
orders that end the litigation on the merits and leave
nothing&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; for the court to do but execute the judgment. </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GM2HM6340010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GM2HM6340050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GK2HM62Y0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DM2-C6G0-TVRV-12RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hallock v. Bo=
nner</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DM2-C6G0-TVRV-12RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 387 F.3d 14=
7,
152 (2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GM2HM6340020000400"></a><a
