<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GN28T3VW0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Alix made a similar effort to cu=
re
the jurisdictional defect in this case. When filing this appeal, he disclai=
med
his state law claims by filing an addendum to Form C, which&nbsp;[**12]&nbs=
p;
states that he would &quot;not pursue his appeal of the district court's Ju=
ly
6, 2020 ruling&quot; and that he &quot;hereby discontinues with prejudice t=
he
State Law Claims.&quot; Doc. 10-4. This statement was sufficient to cure any
defect in appellate jurisdiction and to permit us to review the district
court's order dismissing Alix's RICO claims. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GN28T3VW0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5F8N-V9R1-F04K-J00Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Jewish People=
 for
the Betterment of Westhampton Beach</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5F8N-V9R1-F04K-J00Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 778 F.3d at=
 394</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I40FW3M8CGY000058KM00004"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GN28T3VW0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GP2HM63H0020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6_7"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
establish a RICO claim, a plaintiff must prove: (1) a violation of the RICO
statute, (2) an injury to business or property, and (3) that the injury was
caused by the RICO violation. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GN28T3VW0040=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58P9-74D1-F04K-J00C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cruz v.
FXDirectDealer, LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58P9-74D1-F04K-J00C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 720 F.3d 11=
5,
120 (2d Cir. 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. This appeal implicates the causation element, pursuant=
 to
which a plaintiff must plausibly allege that the RICO violations were (1)
&quot;the proximate cause of his injury, meaning there was a direct
relationship between the plaintiff's injury and the defendant's injurious
conduct&quot;; and that they were (2) &quot;the but-for (or transactional)
cause of his injury, meaning that but for the RICO violation, he would not =
have
been injured.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GP2HM63H0010000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>UFCW Loc. 177=
6 v.
Eli Lilly &amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 620 F.3d 12=
1,
132 (2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The dispositive issue here is
whether Alix plausibly alleges proximate cause.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court concluded that Alix failed to allege proximate cause for thr=
ee
reasons. First, the alleged harm to AlixPartners, it concluded, was directly
caused&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; &nbsp;[*204]&nbsp; by the decisions of the various
debtors' trustees not to hire AlixPartners rather than by McKinsey's
misconduct. Second and relatedly, the court concluded that the existence of
several intervening factors rendered the relationship between the alleged f=
raud
and injury too indirect and remote. Lastly, the court believed that there w=
as
&quot;at least one 'better situated' party,&quot; such as the U.S. Trustee,
&quot;who can seek appropriate remedies for the most direct consequences of
McKinsey's alleged misconduct.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
disagree with the district court's analysis and conclusions as to the thirt=
een
engagements. In general, we conclude that its analysis conflated proof of
causation and proof of damages and that it did not draw all reasonable
inferences in Alix's favor. More specifically (and more importantly) we bel=
ieve
the district court gave insufficient consideration to the fact that McKinse=
y's
alleged misconduct targeted the federal judiciary. As a consequence, this c=
ase
requires us to focus on the responsibilities that Article III courts must
shoulder to ensure the integrity of the Bankruptcy Court and its processes.=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_7"></a>Litigants in all of our courts are en=
titled
to expect that the rules will be followed, the required&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp;
disclosures will be made, and that the court's decisions will be based on a
record that contains all the information applicable law and regulations
require. If McKinsey's conduct has corrupted the process of engaging bankru=
ptcy
advisors, as Alix plausibly alleges, then the unsuccessful participants in =
that
process are directly harmed. The fact that this case invokes our supervisory
responsibilities makes our resolution of it <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>sui
generis</i> and of little, if any, application to &quot;ordinary&quot; RICO
cases where these responsibilities are not front and center. But in light of
these special considerations, we hold that Alix has plausibly alleged proxi=
mate
cause with respect to all thirteen engagements.</span></p>

