<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed Alix's RICO claims predicated on the pay-to-play
scheme because it found that the allegations failed to meet the pleading and
proximate cause standards. It concluded that the pay-to-play allegations we=
re
&quot;devoid of any supporting specifics&quot; and inadequate to meet the
pleading requirements, and that even if they did, that they failed to show a
sufficiently direct link between the allegedly unlawful conduct and injury.=
 We
disagree. We hold that Alix adequately pleaded bankruptcy fraud under </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 9(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and that the allegations show a
sufficiently direct link between the alleged fraud and injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV28T3WV0050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc13_5"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Alix
alleges that McKinsey's pay-to-play scheme violated <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>18 U.S.C. &sect; 152(6)</i>, which requires a showing that the
defendant acted &quot;fraudulently&quot; in &quot;giv[ing], offer[ing],
receiv[ing], or attempt[ing] to obtain any money or property, renumeration,
compensation, reward,&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; advantage, or promise thereof for
acting or forbearing to &nbsp;[*209]&nbsp; act in any [bankruptcy cases].&q=
uot;
In alleging that McKinsey violated this statute, Alix was required to plead
with sufficient particularity the circumstances constituting the fraud. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
9(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. Crucially, however, &quot;allegations may be based on
information and belief when facts are particularly within the opposing part=
y's
knowledge,&quot; provided that they &quot;adduce specific facts supporting a
strong inference of fraud.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV28T3WV0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5S70-003B-51RS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wexner v. Fir=
st
Manhattan Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5S70-003B-51RS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 902 F.2d 16=
9,
172 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court found Alix's pay-to-play allegations insufficient because th=
ey
did not &quot;specify any single act of 'paying' or 'playing'&quot; or conn=
ect
instances of corruption to a particular bankruptcy case. However, the amend=
ed
complaint does identify several engagements that Alix believes had been
influenced by the pay-to-play scheme. For example, it specifically alleges =
the
influence of the pay-to-play scheme in the Alpha Natural Resources, NII
Holdings, and Edison Mission Energy bankruptcies because AlixPartners was
&quot;never even asked to pitch for the work&quot; despite its strong
relationship with the debtors or extensive expertise in the relevant
industries.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, the details that the
district court found lacking are rarely&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; within the knowle=
dge
of a victim of fraud and are more appropriately left for discovery. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480040000400"></a>At this stage, Alix's pay-to=
-play
allegations need only suggest &quot;a strong inference of fraud.&quot; </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5S70-003B-51RS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wexner</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5S70-003B-51RS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 902 F.2d at=
 172</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The allegations in the complaint about specific cases, when combined with t=
he
unusually detailed allegations (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see=
</i>
pp. 8-10) regarding Alix's meetings with Barton, one of which allegedly led=
 to
Barton admitting McKinsey's role and participation in an illegal scheme and
supposed agreement to take steps to end that scheme, easily raise a strong
inference of fraud. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480040000400_2"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley Fin.
(Jersey) No. 3 Ltd. v. Wells Fargo Sec., LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d 16=
0,
171 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;In determining the adequ=
acy
of [a plaintiff's] fraud pleading[] . . . [a court] view[s] the alleged fac=
ts
in their totality, not in isolation.&quot;). Accordingly, we conclude that
Alix's detailed pay-to-play allegations comfortably meet the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
standard.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Even
if they are adequately pleaded, McKinsey contends that the pay-to-play
allegations suffer from the same problem of insufficient causal connection =
as
the allegations concerning fraudulent disclosure statements. The pay-to-play
allegations, McKinsey argues, &quot;narrow&quot; the gap between the alleged
predicate acts and injury but fall short of &quot;eliminat[ing]&quot; that =
gap
because of the independent&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; decisions of debtors and the
bankruptcy court. Specifically, McKinsey contends that Alix's RICO claim
predicated on the pay-to-play scheme necessarily fails when the allegations=
 do
not show that the debtors would have hired AlixPartners in the absence of t=
he
scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
disagree. At the motion to dismiss stage, Alix need only plausibly allege t=
hat
the pay-to-play scheme proximately caused AlixPartners' harm. We believe th=
at
the pay-to-play allegations are sufficiently robust to plausibly allege that
the causal connection has been met. Whether Alix can substantiate his
allegations is a question for summary judgment or trial, but at this junctu=
re
we find that the allegations are sufficient to allege proximate cause.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GW28T3WW0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The injury alleged due to the
fraudulent disclosure statements is, as we have discussed, the loss of
assignments as a bankruptcy consultant.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:f=
tn78'
href=3D"#_ftn78" name=3D"_ftnref78" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Pay-to-play is different
&nbsp;[*210]&nbsp; because the purported injury is the lost opportunity to
compete in an unrigged &quot;beauty contest.&quot; Where this occurs,
competitors who do not pay are <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ipso=
 facto</i>
harmed. In this sense, the allegations concerning the pay-to-play scheme are
like those in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I64KM5GV2HM6480050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
where the Court recognized the existence of an injury
resulting&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; from the rigged lottery system. Here, Alix like=
wise
plausibly alleges a direct causal chain between AlixPartners's loss (the
opportunity to participate in an unrigged contest) and McKinsey's pay-to-pl=
ay
scheme that was intended to buy off the competition. For each pay-to-play
engagement, Alix specifically alleges that AlixPartners &quot;was never even
asked to pitch for the work&quot; in cases in which it ordinarily would have
competed for an assignment absent the scheme. Furthermore, it follows from
Alix's pay-to-play allegations, which we must accept as true at this point,
that McKinsey eviscerated what had historically been an even playing field =
in
the bankruptcy advising marketplace. Suffice it to say that it is
implausible&#8212;indeed inconceivable&#8212;that any Bankruptcy Court would
have approved McKinsey's retention if Alix's allegations were substantiated.
There is accordingly a plausibly alleged direct causal link between McKinse=
y's
purported marketplace manipulation and the harm to Alix of being excluded f=
rom
a market that had been rigged.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
view of Alix's allegations that competitors had been bought off, we, in the
absence of discovery and on an undeveloped record, are not in a position
to&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; identify intervening causes that could have severed th=
is
causal chain. And given this Court's responsibility to oversee the integrit=
y of
the bankruptcy process, we see no other victims with the appropriate incent=
ive
to remedy the harm caused by McKinsey's scheme as alleged by Alix. Accordin=
gly,
we hold that the pay-to-play allegations plausibly allege RICO proximate
causation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>VACATE a=
nd
REMAND</b> for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

