:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d at=
 79</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
What is lacking from all of Shields's allegations are particularized facts =
to
support the inference that the defendants acted recklessly or with fraudule=
nt
intent. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ3810000400"></a>Shields's pleading techni=
que is
to couple a factual statement with a conclusory allegation of fraudulent
intent. For example, she claims that defendants &quot;knew or were reckless=
 in
not knowing&quot; that the loan loss reserve was inadequate; that defendants
&quot;intentionally concealed&quot; the vulnerability to real estate losses
inherent in shared appreciation loans; that defendants &quot;knew but
concealed&quot; the poor condition of Citytrust's loan portfolio; and that
defendants &quot;knew or should have known&quot; that Citytrust's loan prob=
lems
were growing worse and that the loan loss reserve would have to be increase=
d a
second time. However, she does not allege facts that would give rise to a
strong inference that defendants knew or recklessly disregarded the fact th=
at
the loan loss reserve was inadequate, or that continuing erosion of the real
estate market would render the loan portfolio precarious, or that the outlo=
ok
was poor. Shields's frequent conclusory allegations--that Defendants &quot;=
knew
but concealed&quot; some things, or &quot;knew or were reckless in not
knowing&quot; other things--do not satisfy the requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We have held in the context of securities fraud claims that such allegations
are &quot;so broad&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; and conclusory as to be meaningless.&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ3800000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2XY0-003B-G2JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Decker v.
Massey-Ferguson, Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2XY0-003B-G2JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 681 F.2d 11=
1,
119-20 (2d Cir. 1982)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Conscious Misconduct or Recklessness.<=
/i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nowhere
in her Complaint does Shields adduce the kind of circumstantial evidence th=
at
would indicate conscious fraudulent behavior or recklessness. Shields recor=
ds
statements by defendants predicting a prosperous future and holds them up
against the backdrop of what actually transpired. In April 1989, for exampl=
e,
Taylor announced that Citytrust was expecting that year to be its twelfth
consecutive year of record earnings. Then, two months later, Citytrust
announced that it would instead report a loss for 1989 as a result of an
increase in its loan loss reserve. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ388000=
0400"></a>Similarly,
Shields claims that Citytrust filed a document in August 1989 stating that =
it
believed its loan loss reserve was adequate. Yet the following October it
turned out that the reserve was inadequate and that Citytrust would make a
significant addition to it. This technique is sufficient to allege that the
defendants were wrong; but misguided optimism is not a cause of action, and
does not support an inference of fraud. We have rejected&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; =
the
legitimacy of &quot;alleging fraud by hindsight.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ3870000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YH00-0039-M1CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Denny v. Barb=
er</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YH00-0039-M1CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 576 F.2d 46=
5,
470 (2d Cir. 1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
facts alleged in the Second Amended Complaint show a dimming of the company=
's
prospects as subsequent reviews were conducted of the company's loan portfo=
lio,
and resulting cascades of bad news and dropping stock prices. Shields does =
not
allege that the company's disclosures were incompatible with what the most
current reserve reports showed at the time the disclosures were made. The
closest she comes is to allege that &quot;defendants knew or were reckless =
in
not knowing . . . that Citytrust's reserves for loan losses were completely
inadequate in light of the risks which the loan portfolio entailed, and that
such reserves would have to be substantially increased&quot; (Second Amended
Complaint at P 27); and that &quot;defendant Taylor . . . knew or should ha=
ve
known that Citytrust's loan problems had worsened materially and were worse=
ning
and that it was highly likely, if not certain, that Citytrust would soon ag=
ain
greatly increase its loan loss reserves&quot; (Second Amended Complaint at P
34). These allegations do not say, however, that the company's disclosures
were&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; inconsistent with current data. The pleading strongly
suggests that the defendants should have been more alert and more skeptical,
but nothing alleged indicates that management was promoting a fraud. People=
 in
charge of an enterprise are not required to take a gloomy, fearful or defea=
tist
view of the future; subject to what current data indicates, they can be
expected to be confident &nbsp;[*1130]&nbsp; about their stewardship and the
prospects of the business that they manage. The district court did not err =
in
concluding that the allegations of the Second Amended Complaint are
insufficient to support an inference of fraud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Motive and Opportunity.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_8"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Scienter can also be pleaded by
alleging facts that demonstrate both motive and opportunity to commit fraud.
Motive would entail concrete benefits that could be realized by one or more=
 of
the false statements and wrongful nondisclosures alleged. Opportunity would
entail the means and likely prospect of achieving concrete benefits by the
means alleged. Shields argues that the following allegation supports the
inference that Taylor and Engelman had the motive and opportunity to commit
fraud:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
purpose and effect of said scheme was (i) to inflate &nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; the
price of the common stock of the Company and to conceal the adverse facts
concerning the Company's business, liabilities, revenues, earnings, assets,
forecasts and future prospects; and (ii) to maintain artificially high mark=
et
prices for the common stock of Citytrust and to induce plaintiff and the ot=
her
members of the Class to purchase Citytrust common stock . . . at artificial=
ly
inflated prices so that individual defendants could protect their executive
positions and the compensation and prestige they enjoy thereby.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38B0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc9_8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
allege a motive sufficient to support the inference that optimistic but
erroneous statements were fraudulently made, a plaintiff must do more than
merely charge that executives aim to prolong the benefits of the positions =
they
hold. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ3890000400"></a>Ferber v. Travelers Corp., 7=
85 F.
Supp. 1101, 1107 (D. Conn. 1991)</i> (Nevas, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>J</i>.) (&quot;Incentive compensation can hardly be the basis on wh=
ich
an allegation of fraud is predicated. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38S0000400"></a>On a practical level, were t=
he
opposite true, the executives of virtually every corporation in the United
States could be subject to fraud allegations.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38S0000400_2"></a>In looking for a sufficient
allegation of motive, we assume that&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; the defendant is act=
ing
in his or her informed economic self-interest. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-6W70-0054-41JK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Atlantic Gyps=
um
Co. v. Lloyds International Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-6W70-0054-41JK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 753 F. Supp.
505, 514 (S.D.N.Y. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Mukasey, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>J</i>.) (&quot;Plaintiffs' view of the facts defies economic reason,
and therefore does not yield a reasonable inference of fraudulent
intent.&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38V0000400"></a>It is har=
d to
see what benefits accrue from a short respite from an inevitable day of
reckoning. There is no claim here that false statements were made in an eff=
ort
to sell off shares held by management, or to delay a criminal prosecution. =
For
related reasons, the Complaint fails to allege a sufficient opportunity to
derive a benefit from the alleged misstatements and nondisclosures: the
ordinary course of bank business would lead to the review of the loan
portfolios, as it did. If motive could be pleaded by alleging the defendant=
's
desire for continued employment, and opportunity by alleging the defendant's
authority to speak for the company, the required showing of motive and
opportunity would be no realistic check on aspersions of fraud, and mere mi=
sguided
optimism would become actionable under the securities laws. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_8"></a>While some fraud may go&nbsp;[**21]&=
nbsp;
unpunished as a result of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
heightened pleading standard, we recently acknowledged that we cannot elimi=
nate
all opportunities for &quot;unremedied fraud&quot; without creating
opportunities for &quot;undeserved settlements.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FDMNS60K1MNJ38T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Time Wa=
rner</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B5R0-003B-P4JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 9 F.3d at
263-64</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
