<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*378]&nbsp;
LEVAL, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff-appellant
Commercial Cleaning Services, L.L.C. (Commercial) appeals from the dismissa=
l of
its suit. Commercial brought this putative class-action suit
for&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; damages against a business competitor, defendant-appel=
lee
Colin Service Systems, Inc. (Colin), under the Racketeer Influenced and Cor=
rupt
Organizations statute (RICO), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964 (c) (2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The complaint alleges that Col=
in
engaged in a pattern of racketeering activity by hiring undocumented aliens=
 for
profit in violation of Section 274 of the Immigration and Nationality Act
(INA), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324 (a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, a RICO predicate offense. According to the complaint,
Colin's illegal hiring practices enabled it to lower its variable costs and
thereby underbid competing firms, which consequently lost contracts and
customers to Colin. Colin moved to dismiss the complaint pursuant to </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be
granted. The district court granted Colin's motion and dismissed the compla=
int
without leave to amend, granting judgment in Colin's favor, on the grounds =
that
(i) Commercial had no standing to sue because it did not allege a direct in=
jury
proximately caused by Colin's illegal hiring, and (ii) Commercial failed to
provide a sufficiently detailed RICO case statement as required by the
Connecticut&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; district court's Standing Order in Civil RICO
Cases (Standing Order). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
agree with Commercial's contentions that its allegations satisfy the proxim=
ate
cause requirement for civil RICO cases and that the deficiencies in its RICO
case statement filed pursuant to the district court's Standing Order did not
justify the grant of judgment in defendant's favor. We therefore vacate the
judgment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. The Complaint</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND0NX0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_10"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the purposes of reviewing the grant of Colin's motion to dismiss, we take as
true the factual allegations of Commercial's complaint, as supplemented by =
the
RICO case statement submitted pursuant to the district court's Standing Ord=
er. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND0NW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-47K0-008H-V4DN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McLaughlin v.
Anderson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-47K0-008H-V4DN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 962 F.2d 18=
7,
189 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
The Parties</span></i></b></p>

