Difficulty of Determining Damages Attributable to the RICO Violation</span>=
</i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court found plaintiff's claim deficient on the first <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> factor, because a fact find=
er
would be required to determine whether Commercial's lost business to Colin =
was
the result of the illegal immigrant hiring&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; scheme as oppo=
sed
to independent business reasons, such as the comparative quality of the
companies' services, their comparative business reputations, the fluctuatio=
ns
in demand for their services, or other reasons customers might have for
selecting one cleaning company over another. The district court concluded t=
hat,
even if a fact finder could make such a determination, the calculation of
damages attributable to the illegal immigrant hiring scheme would be
&quot;daunting, if not impossible.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
difficulty of proof identified in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>H=
olmes</i>,
however, was quite different from the circumstances of this case. Here, the
plaintiffs bid against the defendant as direct competitors. The complaint
asserts that Pratt &amp; Whitney chose Colin because Colin submitted
&quot;significantly lower&quot; bids in a &quot;highly competitive&quot;
price-sensitive market. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1530000400"></a>A=
ccording
to the complaint, Colin was able to underbid its competitors because its sc=
heme
to hire illegal immigrant workers permitted it to pay well below the prevai=
ling
wage for legal workers. Although we do not deny that there may be disputes =
as
to whether the plaintiff class lost business because of defendant's violati=
on
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1324(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> or
for other reasons, the&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; plaintiff class was no less direct=
ly
injured than the insolvent broker-dealers in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Holmes</i>, whose trustees, the Court indicated, would be proper
plaintiffs. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1400000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 273</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
If plaintiffs can substantiate their claims, the plaintiffs may well show t=
hat
they lost contracts directly because of the cost savings defendant realized
through its scheme to employ illegal workers.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1550000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This theory fits our suggestion =
in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1540000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sperber</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 849 F.2d at=
 65</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
where we affirmed the dismissal on proximate causation grounds of a civil R=
ICO
complaint by investors whose share values declined in the wake of the
defendant's guilty plea to insider trading. Although we found the causation
chain offered by plaintiffs too remote, we distinguished a circumstance whe=
re a
plaintiff was a direct competitor against a defendant. <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>See id.</i> We stated that the RICO statute would grant standing if
plaintiff were a &quot;head-to-head bidder against [defendant] who lost bec=
ause
of [defendant's] illegally-enhanced reputation or economic power.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn80' href=3D"#_ftn80" name=3D"_ftnref80" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;
Where, as here, &nbsp;[*383]&nbsp; the parties have bid against each other,=
 the
difference between the lowest and second lowest bid </span><a style=3D'mso-=
footnote-id:
ftn81' href=3D"#_ftn81" name=3D"_ftnref81" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
readily discoverable. &nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; If Commercial can prove that but f=
or
Colin's lower wage costs attributable to its illegal hiring scheme, Commerc=
ial
would have won the contract and would have earned a profit on it, it will h=
ave
shown a proximately caused injury, compensable under RICO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Colin
objects that any reduced labor costs were due to its alleged underpayment of
workers and failure to pay other employment-related costs of doing business,
not its participation in the illegal immigrant hiring scheme. In other word=
s,
Colin claims that Commercial complains of an injury caused by the low wages
paid to Colin's workers--and not by their immigration status. Of course, pa=
ying
workers less than the prevailing wage and failing to withhold payroll taxes=
 are
not RICO predicate acts. Nonetheless, the purpose of the alleged violation =
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324 (a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, the hiring of illegal alien workers, was to take advan=
tage
of their diminished bargaining position, so as to employ a cheaper labor fo=
rce
and compete unfairly on the basis of lower costs. By illegally hiring
undocumented alien labor, Colin was able to hire cheaper labor and compete
unfairly. The violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1324(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
alleged by the complaint was a proximate cause of Colin's ability to underb=
id
the plaintiffs&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; and take business from them.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
Difficulty of Apportioning Damages Among Injured Parties</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1760000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Holmes</i> Court warned that if courts did not limit recovery to
injuries directly related to the RICO violation, they would be forced to de=
vise
complicated rules apportioning damages among plaintiffs at different degree=
s of
separation from the violative acts alleged. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>See
</i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1750000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 273</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court noted the difficulty of apportioning damages between the
broker-dealers and customers who suffered losses when the broker-dealers be=
came
insolvent. Colin contends that its business competitors are not the only
aggrieved parties who could recover under Commercial's theory and that the
difficulty of apportioning damages among potential plaintiffs will be sever=
e.
Colin's response misses the point. The point made in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Holmes</i> was that, if damages are paid both to first tier
plaintiffs--those directly injured by defendant's alleged acts--and to seco=
nd
tier plaintiffs--those injured by the injury to the first tier plaintiffs--=
then
the payment of damages to the first tier plaintiffs would cure the harm to =
the
second tier plaintiffs, and the payment of damages to&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; the
latter category would involve double compensation. Colin's answer is no ans=
wer
to this point. If a defendant's illegal acts caused direct injury to more t=
han
one category of plaintiffs, the defendant may&nbsp;[*384]&nbsp; well be
obligated to compensate different plaintiffs for different injuries. It does
not follow that any plaintiff will have been twice benefitted, which was the
concern in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND17T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Unlike the situation in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, Commercial and its fellow =
class
members are not alleging an injury that was derivative of injury to others.
Commercial does not seek to recover based on &quot;the misfortunes visited =
upon
a third person by the defendant's acts.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND17S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 268</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also Laborers</i>, 191 F.3d at 238=
-39
(&quot;The critical question posed by the direct injury test is whether the
damages a plaintiff sustains are derivative of an injury to a third party. =
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1890000400"></a>If so, then the injury is
indirect; if not, it is direct.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1890000400_2"></a>It claims to have lost pro=
fits
directly as the result of Colin's underbidding, which it achieved through i=
ts
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1324(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18P0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND17X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Terminate Con=
trol
Corp. v. Horowitz</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 28 F.3d 133=
5,
1343 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that the&nbsp;[**21]&n=
bsp;
value of business opportunities lost due to defendant's RICO violations is
compensable); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7W60-003B-P3HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mid Altantic
Telecom, Inc. v. Long Distance Servs., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7W60-003B-P3HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 18 F.3d 260,
264 (4th Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting that plaintiff was not
seeking to vindicate claims of customers who accepted defendant's fraudulen=
t,
ostensibly lower rates, but rather alleged &quot;distinct and independent
injuries: lost customers and lost revenues&quot;); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>see also </i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18D0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D0C0-001T-D091-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Israel Travel
Advisory Serv., Inc. v. Israel Identity Tours, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D0C0-001T-D091-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 61 F.3d 125=
0,
1257 (7th Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18P0000400_2"></a>We have stated a plaintiff=
 has
standing where the plaintiff is the direct target of the RICO violation. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams v. Y=
oung
&amp; Rubicam Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d 234,
238 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>American Expr=
ess</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 39 F.3d at =
400</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(targets of RICO violations were competitive rivals not shareholders harmed=
 by
decrease in stock value upon exposure of scheme); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>see also </i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND18T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7W60-003B-P3HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mid Atlantic
Telecom</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7W60-003B-P3HG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 18 F.3d at =
263</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(plaintiff has standing if it can show that it was a &quot;direct target&qu=
ot;
of defendant's RICO violations). As discussed above, the theory of Commerci=
al's
claim is that Colin undertook the illegal immigrant hiring scheme
in&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; order to undercut its business rivals, thus qualifying
them as direct targets of the RICO violation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Colin
raises the specter of a proliferation of civil RICO suits that would be
permitted under Commercial's theory. It argues that a finding in Commercial=
's
favor would mean that a dance club that failed to pay license fees on
recordings it played, thereby decreasing its overhead costs and thereby
allowing it to decrease its admission charge, would be liable not only to t=
he
copyright holder but to all the infringer's business competitors. We do not
find this hypothetical problematic. First, the hypothetical competitors wou=
ld
still be required to overcome the hurdle of showing that their loss of busi=
ness
was proximately caused by the infringer's decrease in admission fees. But m=
ore
importantly, once again, the concern of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>Holmes</i>
was that a violator might be obligated to pay double compensation if requir=
ed
to compensate those directly injured and those injured by the injury to tho=
se
directly injured. It was not that a violator might be obligated to compensa=
te
two or more different classes of plaintiffs, each of which suffered a diffe=
rent
concrete injury, proximately caused by the violation. &nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; In
Colin's hypothetical, the competitors and the copyright owners would have
suffered entirely separate injuries. Although there may well be other reaso=
ns
such plaintiffs would lack standing, they would not be barred from bringing=
 a
RICO action because of a concern for multiple recoveries. Compensating both
would not overcompensate any plaintiff.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3.
Ability of Other Parties to Vindicate Aims of the Statute</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1980000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In relation to the third <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> policy factor, the Supreme =
Court
has observed&nbsp;[*385]&nbsp; that &quot;<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9=
_9"></a>the
existence of an identifiable class of persons whose self-interest would
normally motivate them to vindicate the public interest in [RICO] enforceme=
nt
diminishes the justification for allowing a more remote party . . . to perf=
orm
the office of a private attorney general.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1970000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated Ge=
n.
Contractors of California, Inc. v. California State Council of Carpenters</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. 51=
9,
542, 74 L. Ed. 2d 723, 103 S. Ct. 897 (1983)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cited in </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1990000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 270</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Colin argues that this factor weighs against Commercial's standing, because
other parties, such as state and federal authorities charged with collecting
unpaid taxes and workers' compensation fees, &nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; may sue to
vindicate the statute. Moreover, the INS, which enforces </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1324(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
has already obtained Colin's agreement to pay $ 1 million for violations of=
 the
immigration laws.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Once
again, Colin misses the point. If the existence of a public authority that
could prosecute a claim against putative RICO defendants meant that the
plaintiff is too remote under <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holme=
s</i>,
then no private cause of action could ever be maintained, for every RICO
predicate offense, as well as the RICO enterprise itself, is separately
prosecutable by the government. In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Holmes</i>,
those directly injured could be expected to sue, and their recovery would
redound to the benefit of the plaintiffs suing for indirect injury. Here, in
contrast, suits by governmental authorities to recover lost taxes and fees
would do nothing to alleviate the plaintiffs' loss of profits. There is no
class of potential plaintiffs who have been more directly injured by the
alleged RICO conspiracy than the defendant's business competitors, who have=
 a
greater incentive to ensure that a RICO violation does not go undetected or
unremedied, and whose recovery would indirectly cure the loss suffered by t=
hese
plaintiffs.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Violation</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;<b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'> of the Standing Order</b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court's alternative ground for dismissing the complaint was that
Commercial had &quot;grievously violated&quot; the District of Connecticut's
Standing Order in Civil RICO Cases. The Standing Order requires that a
plaintiff in a civil RICO case submit a RICO Case Statement within 20 days =
of
filing the complaint. The case statement must provide &quot;in detail&quot;
information including, among other things, the names of the individuals,
partnerships, or other legal entities constituting the RICO enterprise, the=
 dates
of the predicate acts with a description of the facts surrounding the predi=
cate
acts, and the identity of the alleged wrongdoers and victims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND19S0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court gave little
explanation of this ground for dismissal. It is not clear whether the court
understood the dismissal as justified by plaintiff's failure to furnish
information relating to the claim required by a rule of law (as is the case
when a court grants summary judgment because the plaintiff fails to show
evidence capable of proving the elements of the claim, or grants a motion u=
nder
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> because the facts pleaded would not constitute a violat=
ion
of law), or as a sanction&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; imposed because of plaintiff's
failure to obey a court order (as might be appropriate if the plaintiff ref=
used
to appear for his deposition). <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See,=
 e.g.</i>,
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND19R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Valentine v.
Museum of Modern Art</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 29 F.3d 47 =
(2d
Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (dismissing action with prejudice for plaintiff's refus=
al
to comply with order to appear for deposition). Although either theory can
justify grant of judgment to the defendant in appropriate circumstances, the
circumstances presented here could not justify the entry of judgment on eit=
her
theory.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
consider first the theory of insufficient information. For at least two
reasons, dismissal for insufficient information was not justified. First, t=
he
Standing&nbsp;[*386]&nbsp; Order calls for information far in excess of the
essential elements of a RICO claim. On a motion for summary judgment, or for
judgment as a matter of law at the time of trial, a defendant would not be
entitled to judgment because the plaintiff's evidence failed to include all=
 the
&quot;individuals, partnerships, corporations, associations, or other legal
entities [that constitute] the RICO enterprise,&quot; or the identities of =
all
&quot;wrongdoers&quot; and &quot;victims.&quot; To the extent the Standing
Order called for presentation&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; of information going beyond
what a plaintiff needs to present to establish a legally sufficient case,
plaintiff's inability to produce it could not justify the grant of judgment=
 to
defendant.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
standing order of this nature may appropriately require a plaintiff to set
forth the information it possesses in helpfully categorized form, as an aid=
e to
the court and to the accused defendant. But it may not make the prosecution=
 of
the action dependent on the plaintiff's ability to furnish more information
than is required, as a matter of law, to prove the essential elements of the
claim. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND19V0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, the district court gave =
the
plaintiff no opportunity to conduct discovery so as to fill the deficiencie=
s in
the information it provided. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_9"></a>Alth=
ough </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
11(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'> seeks to ensure, by imposing responsibility on attorney=
s,
that claims are &quot;warranted&quot; and &quot;likely to have evidentiary
support after a reasonable opportunity for further investigation or
discovery,&quot; it makes clear by the latter quoted phrase that a plaintif=
f is
not required to know at the time of pleading all facts necessary to establi=
sh
the claim. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1B80000400"></a><i style=3D'ms=
o-bidi-font-style:
normal'>See </i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND19T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YBN0-006F-M1CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>O'Brien v.
Alexander</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YBN0-006F-M1CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 101 F.3d 14=
79,
1489 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (a sanction&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; u=
nder
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 11(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
may not be imposed for failure to make reasonable inquiry &quot;unless a
particular allegation is utterly lacking in support&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND1B80000400_2"></a>Similarly, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
56(f)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'> provides, as interpreted by court opinions, that when a
party facing an adversary's motion for summary judgment reasonably advises =
the
court that it needs discovery to be able to present facts needed to defend =
the
motion, the court should defer decision of the motion until the party has h=
ad
the opportunity to take discovery and rebut the motion. <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>See </i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F54HVJ0K1MND19W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FV50-001T-D3T7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Meloff v. New
York Life Ins. Co.</span></i></a><a
