on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
against certain insureds, claimants and body shops who had associated toget=
her
to submit false claims to the insurer. The Court held that the evidence vie=
wed
in the light most favorable to the insurer permitted the jury to conclude t=
hat
&quot;by acting with purpose to cause Aetna to make payments on false claim=
s,
appellants were participating in the 'operation' of Aetna.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1620000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YX10-003B-P0N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YX10-003B-P0N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1560</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1650000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND16K0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>P&amp;B Autobody</span></i><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> reached its conclusion by
extrapolating from <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reves'</i> recog=
nition
that an enterprise might be operated or controlled by bribery to find that
the&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; defendant exerted a degree of control over Aetna that
gave rise to a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1640000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YX10-003B-P0N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>43 F.3d at 15=
60</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
However, other courts that have considered the bribery context examined in =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reves</i> have noted that the effect of
bribery &quot;is qualitatively different from mere influence or assistance
because the outsider paying the bribes buys actual control over the actions=
 of
a person within the enterprise. Giving a bribe can be tantamount to gaining=
 a
management position within the enterprise, because the insider taking the
bribes acts under the direction of the outsider giving them in conducting t=
he
affairs of the RICO enterprise.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND16J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-R140-006F-P4JJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dept. of Econ.
Dev. v. Arthur Andersen &amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-R140-006F-P4JJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 924 F. Supp.
449, 466-67 (S.D.N.Y. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. If a defendant bribes a company
official to engage in certain conduct, the defendant can rightly be seen as
&quot;participating&quot; in the operation and management of the company
through the conduct of the bribed official. It is considerably less clear
whether that characterization properly applies when an outside has merely
defrauded a company.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MNRYF000D07DC0002V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND16N0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MKRV1000D07DC0002C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MKWN5000D07DC0002D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND18W0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, the Court is concerned
that the First Circuit's construction of the operation and management test
might&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp; well read that test out of the act. For under
Allstate's reading of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>P&amp;B Autob=
ody</i>,
</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn94' href=3D"#_ftn94" name=3D"_ftnref9=
4" title=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
any time a company is defrauded by the conduct of a defendant, one could say
that the defendant &quot;controlled&quot; the company's operations, since
absent the fraud, the company would not have done what it did or acted in t=
he
manner in which it did. Such a free-wheeling interpretation of the operation
and management test would appear to be inconsistent with </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND16M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S70-NFC0-003B-R50T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reves</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and as Allstate's counsel essentially conceded at oral argument, with the m=
ore
rigorous approach to the operation and management requirement that district
courts in the Second Circuit have adopted. Transcript at 101; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND16P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43N7-3YY0-0038-Y1RM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Redtail Leasi=
ng,
Inc. v. Bellezza</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43N7-3YY0-0038-Y1RM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2001 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 10814, 2001 WL 863556, at *4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;the 'operation and
management' test . . . is a very difficult test to satisfy&quot;) (citation
omitted); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND18X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-79T0-00B1-F1RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vickers Stock
Research Corp. v. Quotron Sys., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-79T0-00B1-F1RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1997 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 10837, No. 96 Civ. 2269, 1997 WL 420265, at *4 (S.D.N.Y. July 25, 199=
7)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;[A] defendant &nbsp;[*276]&nbsp; will not be found to participate in=
 the
management or operation of the enterprise simply because he enjoys substant=
ial
persuasive power to induce the alleged enterprise to take certain
actions.&quot;) (citation&nbsp;[**47]&nbsp; and quotation marks omitted).</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1C60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court's considerable skeptic=
ism
about Allstate's theory aside, the Court is fully cognizant that it conside=
rs
this issue on a motion to dismiss, before discovery and before all of the f=
acts
regarding Defendant's conduct and its effect on Allstate are fully known. In
these circumstances, the Court is unwilling at this preliminary stage of
the&nbsp;[**48]&nbsp; proceeding to decide definitively that Allstate will =
be
unable to adduce any evidence to buttress its allegations that Seigel P.C. =
did
in fact &quot;participate in [Allstate's] affairs, directly or indirectly,
through a pattern of racketeering activity.&quot; Amended Compl. P421; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see, e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND19X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BSF-P0G0-0038-Y4YY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States
Fire Ins. Co., </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BSF-P0G0-0038-Y4YY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2004 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 2455, 2004 WL 324477, at *15</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (denying motion to dismiss); </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1C70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48RR-BV90-0038-Y0WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RD Mgmt. Corp=
. v.
Samuels</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48RR-BV90-0038-Y0WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2003 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 9013, 2003 WL 21254076, *8 (S.D.N.Y. 2003)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(denying motion to dismiss); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cf. </=
i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1C90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TBC-07K0-0038-X03D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Allen</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TBC-07K0-0038-X03D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 155 F.3d 35=
, 42
(2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (reversing summary judgment on the issue of participati=
on
in the operation and management of an enterprise's affairs). Therefore, the
Court will not dismiss Count II against Seigel P.C.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III.</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MNM49000D07DC0002T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1CY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants' motions to dismiss
Allstate's common law fraud and CUTPA claims do not require substantial
discussion. Defendants' motion to dismiss the common law fraud claims is
founded on its proximate cause arguments, which this Court has already reje=
cted.
Since the Second Circuit has observed that &quot;in practice, our cases have
held RICO plaintiffs to a more stringent showing of proximate cause than wo=
uld
be required at common law,&nbsp;[**49]&nbsp; &quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1CX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48DP-W9V0-0038-X15Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Desiano, </sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48DP-W9V0-0038-X15Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>326 F.3d at 3=
48</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation and citation omitted), it necessarily follows that at l=
east
at this stage of the case, Allstate has pleaded sufficient facts to withsta=
nd a
motion to dismiss the common law claims as a matter of law. Even if, as
Defendants have argued, Connecticut has adopted the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>&quot;Holmes</i> factors in determining standing for state statutory
and common law claims,&quot; for the reasons stated previously, the Court
concludes that at this stage Allstate has satisfied </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1D00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes'</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
pleading requirements, and, therefore, Connecticut's as well.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DT0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MNF1F000D07DC0002S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DW0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As for Allstate's CUTPA claims,
relying on </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1D20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42SR-8NV0-0038-Y32D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Connecticut P=
ipe
Trades Health Fund v. Philip Morris, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42SR-8NV0-0038-Y32D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 153 F. Supp=
. 2d
101, 113 n.13 (D. Conn. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, Defendants argue that Allstate=
 has
not alleged any &quot;connection or nexus - business, consumer, competitor,
commercial or otherwise - between&quot; Allstate and Seigel with respect to=
 the
insured tortfeasor claims. However, the court's concern in <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Connecticut Pipe</i> was the apparent absence of <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>any</i> allegation of a commercial or trade relationship between the
plaintiffs and the defendants. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I75075MK92W000D07DC00028"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DY0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42SR-8NV0-0038-Y32D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> As
Allstate was the recipient of Defendants' fraudulent&nbsp;[**50]&nbsp; invo=
ices
whose payment obligations and litigation decisions depended upon Defendants'
invoices and medical reports, and as Seigel well understood that Allstate (=
and
other insurers) would be the ultimate payer of his bills in both the insured
patient and insured tortfeasor situations, the Court is not prepared at this
stage to conclude that there was no &quot;connection or nexus&quot; between
Allstate and the Defendants sufficient to satisfy CUTPA. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DY0000400_2"></a>Furthermore, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Connecticut Pipe</i> makes it clear th=
at <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20_4"></a>directly injured parties have standi=
ng
under CUTPA. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F0WVM10K1MND1DX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42SR-8NV0-0038-Y32D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42SR-8NV0-0038-Y32D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 113</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;By limiting standing under CUTPA to those who were directly injured =
by a
defendant's unfair trade practices, the intended beneficiaries of CUTPA are
afforded full relief.&quot;). &nbsp;[*277]&nbsp; Since, as discussed above,=
 the
Court is persuaded at this stage that Allstate was directly injured by
Defendants' fraudulent acts, the Court will not dismiss Allstate's CUTPA cl=
aim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV.</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
