nt-style:
normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4NM0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The creative pleading in the ins=
tant
cases serves as a reminder why the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt
Organizations Act's (&quot;RICO&quot;) treble damages provisions are not
available to remedy every possible injury that can, with some ingenuity, be=
 attributed
to a defendant's injurious conduct. Plaintiffs appeal from a judgment of the
United States District Court for the Eastern District of New York (Block, J=
.)
dismissing two companion actions, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>L=
erner
v. Fleet Bank, N.A.</i>, No. 98 CV 7778 (&quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Lerner</i>&quot;), and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayr=
off v.
Fleet Bank, N.A.</i>, No. 98 CV 7779 (&quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Bayroff</i>&quot;),
for lack of subject matter jurisdiction. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4NK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4360-XMF0-0038-Y1JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner v. Fle=
et
Bank, N.A., 146 F. Supp. 2d 224, 226 (E.D.N.Y. 2001)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Plaintiffs, investors who were defrauded of millions of dollars held in esc=
row
accounts by an unscrupulous attorney, allege that the banks in which those
funds were deposited are liable for their losses. Plaintiffs principally al=
lege
that the defendant banks fraudulently concealed the attorney's criminal
behavior from state disciplinary authorities and thereby prevented the atto=
rney
from being disbarred. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4NP0000400"></a>Had=
 the
attorney been disbarred, plaintiffs claim, &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; they would have
refused to make further investments with him and their losses would have be=
en
averted. The district court concluded that plaintiffs lacked standing to pu=
rsue
their civil RICO claims under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> and declined to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over
plaintiffs' state-law claims. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <=
/i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4NN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4360-XMF0-0038-Y1JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id. at 232</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
hold that lack of RICO standing does not divest the district court of
jurisdiction over the action, because RICO standing, unlike other standing
doctrines, is sufficiently intertwined with the merits&nbsp;[*117]&nbsp; of=
 the
RICO claim that such a rule would turn the underlying merits questions into
jurisdictional issues. Thus, we affirm the district court's dismissal of
plaintiffs' civil RICO claims because the alleged pattern of racketeering
activity was not the proximate cause of plaintiffs' injuries; however, we d=
o so
not under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, for lack of jurisdiction, as the district court did, b=
ut
rather under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, for failure to state a claim. Dismissal of the state-l=
aw
claims in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner </i>was improper,
however, because federal jurisdiction in that action was also premised on
diversity. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1332</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(a)(1)</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Finally, we find that the district court may, in its discretion, exercise
supplemental jurisdiction over the state-law claims in <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>Bayroff </i>because RICO standing is not a jurisdictional prerequis=
ite
the absence of which would divest the district court of the original
jurisdiction required to support supplemental jurisdiction. We remand the
state-law claims in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff </i>for=
 the
district court to determine whether exercising supplemental jurisdiction ov=
er
these claims is appropriate.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4PG0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_12"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
reviewing the district court's dismissal under either </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> or </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>12(b)(1)</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
we accept the following factual allegations contained in plaintiffs' compla=
ints
as true and draw all reasonable inferences in favor of plaintiffs. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4NR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-J5D0-003B-S1MW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Conley v. Gib=
son,
355 U.S. 41, 45-46, 2 L. Ed. 2d 80, 78 S. Ct. 99 (1957)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>);</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4PH0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3XJT-DJM0-0038-X0B3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McGinty v. St=
ate
of New York, 193 F.3d 64, 68 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>). Plaintiffs in these companion actions are victims of a
fraudulent investment scheme engineered by then-attorney David Schick. Schi=
ck
convinced investors that he had devised a no-risk scheme for generating a h=
igh
return&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; on their investments. Schick would bid on distressed
mortgage pools at auctions by the Resolution Trust Company, the Federal Dep=
osit
Insurance Company (&quot;FDIC&quot;), and other banking institutions. Upon
being awarded the bid, he would immediately try to re-sell the mortgage poo=
l to
another buyer for a quick profit. The acceptance of his bid was subject to a
ninety-day due diligence period, so Schick assured his investors that if he=
 was
unable to find a buyer within the ninety-day time period, he would be able =
to
rescind his original purchase without incurring any penalty. Schick's plan =
was
apparently foolproof--except, he explained to the investors, in order to ma=
ke
this scheme work, Schick had to prove to the FDIC that he could complete the
purchase. He would therefore be required to deposit substantial sums of cas=
h as
evidence of his good faith. This is where Schick's potential investors came=
 in.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4PM0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To convince wary investors that
their money would be secure, Schick agreed to deposit the entrusted funds in
escrow accounts covered by restrictive provisions. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4PK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4360-XMF0-0038-Y1JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner v. Fle=
et
Bank, N.A., 146 F. Supp. 2d 224, 225-27 (E.D.N.Y. 2001)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
He also entered into escrow agreements with&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; the investors =
that
stated: &quot;Escrow Agent are attorneys [<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>sic</i>]
admitted to practice in the State of New York and shall act as fiduciary in
accordance with the relevant provisions of the Judiciary Law and all other
ethical or legal standards for attorneys admitted to practice in the State =
of
New York and expressly agrees that the only person who shall be entitled to=
, or
have any right or interest in the Escrow Deposit shall be the Depositor.&qu=
ot;
Armed with these guarantees, and relying on the fact that Schick was an
attorney in good standing with the New York bar, the investors turned their
money over to Schick for deposit in the defendant banks. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4R20000400"></a>Ultimately, however, these e=
scrow
agreements provided little protection against Schick's unscrupulous conduct.
Before the investors&nbsp;[*118]&nbsp; discovered his fraud, Schick had rai=
ded
the accounts repeatedly and managed to steal approximately $ 82 million. </=
span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn96' href=3D"#_ftn96" name=3D"_ftnref96" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4R40000400"></a>Some of these defrauded
investors pursued a federal RICO action against Fleet Bank, alleging that F=
leet
Bank had aided Schick in stealing their money by approving withdrawals that
violated restrictive provisions on the accounts; failing to inform state ba=
nking
authorities or investors of the fraud; misleading investors regarding their
accounts; approving overdrafts on these accounts; and submitting to investo=
rs a
fraudulent report overstating the balances of the main escrow accounts. </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4TS0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4R30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TCN-KVF0-0038-Y42M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Schmidt v. Fl=
eet
Bank, 16 F. Supp. 2d 340, 344-45 (S.D.N.Y. 1998)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The district court dismissed these claims both for failure to plead an
enterprise adequately and for failure to plead that Fleet had directed the
affairs of an enterprise. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4TS00004=
00_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4R50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TCN-KVF0-0038-Y42M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 346-51=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
