<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. RICO Standing</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W00000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc5_11"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
RICO statute grants standing to &quot;any person injured in his business or
property by reason of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> .
. . .&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W20000400"></a>To
demonstrate standing, a plaintiff must plead, at a minimum, &quot;(1) the
defendant's violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(2)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> an
injury to the plaintiff's business or property, and (3) causation of the in=
jury
by the defendant's violation.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4VY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:44J1-MRD0-0038-X29K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Commercial
Cleaning Servs., L.L.C. v. Colin Serv. Sys., Inc., 271 F.3d 374, 380 (2d Ci=
r.
2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; This third requirement is satisfied =
if
the defendant's injurious conduct is both the factual and the proximate cau=
se
of the injury alleged. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W20000400_=
2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes, 503 U=
.S.
at 268</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W40000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We have previously implied that
there might be an additional standing component--that the plaintiff must fa=
ll
within the zone of interests RICO seeks to protect. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>See </i>Laborers Local </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X6T-17R0-0038-X509-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>17 Health &am=
p;
Benefit Fund v. Philip Morris, Inc., 191 F.3d 229, 236 (2d Cir. 1999)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_11"></a>&quot;The zone of interests test . .=
 . is
an inquiry 'into whether . . . the plaintiff is within the class of persons
sought to be benefited by the provision at issue.'&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Id. </i>(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W5000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes, 503 U=
.S.
at 287</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (Scalia, J., concurring)).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>While
the zone-of-interests test may indeed be relevant to determining statutory
standing, it is unclear precisely how this test would apply under the compl=
ex
structure of the RICO statute. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W80000400=
"></a>A
RICO violation encompasses not only a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
but also violations of the underlying statutes that constitute the array of
predicate acts. In addition, in this case, plaintiff's RICO violation also
encompasses the alleged corruption of New York's regulatory system for
disciplining&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; lawyers. The district court relied upon its
determination that plaintiffs did not fall within the zone of interests of =
the
reporting regulations for this system in dismissing plaintiffs' RICO claim =
for
lack of standing. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W70000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4360-XMF0-0038-Y1JB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner, 146 F.
Supp. 2d at 231</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Whether a plaintiff must fall
within the zone of interests of both RICO and the underlying statutes and
regulations in order to pursue a RICO action is a question we have not yet
answered.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WB0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WD0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WG0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Both the Supreme Court in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> and this Circuit in </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4W90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powers v. Bri=
tish
Vita, P.L.C., 57 F.3d 176, 188 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
have been squarely confronted with the issue, and both courts have found it
wiser to resolve those cases on grounds of proximate causality. In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, the Supreme Court granted
certiorari to consider whether &quot;a party which was neither a purchaser =
nor
a seller of securities, and for that reason lacked standing to sue under
Section 10(b) of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
thereunder, is free of that limitation on standing when presenting essentia=
lly
the same claims under the [RICO] Act.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes, 503 U=
.S.
at 265 n.7</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The Court had previously held that
standing&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; to bring a private cause of action under &sect;
10(b) was confined to buyers and sellers of the security. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WR0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Blue Chip Sta=
mps
v. Manor Drug Stores, 421 U.S. 723, 44 L. Ed. 2d 539, 95 S. Ct. 1917 (1975)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WR0000400_2"></a>The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Holmes</i> majority declined to resolve the standing issue that had
split the circuits, however, as it found that plaintiffs had not suffered a
direct&nbsp;[*121]&nbsp; injury and thus could not satisfy the causation
element of RICO standing. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WT00004=
00"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes, 503 U=
.S.
at 275-76</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;A review of the conflicting cases shows that all
could have been resolved on proximate-causation grounds, and that none invo=
lved
litigants like those in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Blue Chip S=
tamps
v. Manor Drug Stores</i>, persons who had decided to forgo securities
transactions in reliance on misrepresentations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WT0000400_2"></a>Thus, we think it inopport=
une to
resolve the issue today.&quot; (internal citation omitted)). </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn98' href=3D"#_ftn98" name=3D"_ftnref98" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WW0000400"></a>Four concurring Justices =
wrote
separately to express their views on the question on which certiorari had b=
een
granted. Justice O'Connor, joined by Justices White and Stevens, reasoned t=
hat
standing under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
focuses only on the &quot;injury&quot; of any person, not on the legal righ=
t of
such plaintiff to sue for violations of the predicate acts. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 280</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(O'Connor, J., concurring). Justice O'Connor therefore concluded that RICO =
does
not incorporate the standing requirements of the predicate acts alleged. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WY0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4X80000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Justice Scalia also concurred in=
 the
result reached by the majority, but rejected the positions taken by Justice
O'Connor and the majority regarding the RICO standing requirement. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4X10000400"></a>Justice Scalia instead argued
that, for a plaintiff to have standing to pursue a RICO action, he or she m=
ust
satisfy the traditional elements of statutory standing of the predicate
statutes, thereby demonstrating the proper nexus between the injuries alleg=
ed
and the predicate acts. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4WX0000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 288-89=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Scalia, J., concurring). These requirements include proximate causation </=
span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn99' href=3D"#_ftn99" name=3D"_ftnref99" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and the zone-of-interests test. Justice Scalia argued that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the RICO statute did&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; not extend the zone-of-interests
protected by the underlying statutes, allowing plaintiffs to recover under =
RICO
for a class of harms that was not encompassed within the underlying statute=
. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4X10000400_2"></a><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:ftn100'
href=3D"#_ftn100" name=3D"_ftnref100" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4X00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 288</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4XB0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Powers</i>, this Court was similarly confronted with the question
whether a plaintiff must satisfy the standing requirements of &sect; 10(b) =
in
order to bring a RICO claim&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; predicated on violations of t=
hat
provision. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4XD0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4X90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powers, 57 F.=
3d
at 188</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. After surveying the conflicting positions taken on this
standing issue in the concurring opinions in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Holmes</i>, we decided to follow the Supreme Court's lead and,
refraining from entering the fray, resolved the standing issue on the groun=
ds
of proximate causation. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4XD0000400_2"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:normal'>cf.
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4XC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams v. Y=
oung
&amp; Rubicam, Inc., 79 F.3d 234, 238 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn101' href=3D"#_ftn101" name=3D"_ftnref101" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;
Although we are troubled by the issues potentially raised by the
application&nbsp;[*122]&nbsp; of the zone-of-interests test to determine
standing in the RICO context, we need not delve deeply into the implication=
s in
the present case. We believe the better approach is that adopted by the Sup=
reme
Court in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> and this Court =
in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Powers</i>: <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_11"></a>if the standing issue may be resolve=
d on
proximate cause grounds, the question whether the plaintiff must also satis=
fy
the standing requirements of the underlying statutes whose violations
constitute predicate acts (the question that was addressed by the concurren=
ces
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>) or the underlying
regulatory system allegedly corrupted (the question that was addressed by t=
he
district court) need not be reached. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn=
102'
href=3D"#_ftn102" name=3D"_ftnref102" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> As plaintiffs cannot show that
defendants' alleged violations of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
proximately caused their injuries, they lack standing to pursue their RICO
claims. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn103' href=3D"#_ftn103"
name=3D"_ftnref103" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4YJ0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4YW0000400"></a>Plaintiffs have not demonstr=
ated
that their injury was proximately caused &quot;by a&nbsp;[*123]&nbsp; patte=
rn
of racketeering activity violating </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> or
by individual RICO predicate acts.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4YH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht v. Comm=
erce
Clearing House, Inc., 897 F.2d 21, 23 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4YK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.R=
.L.
v. Imrex Co., 473 U.S. 479, 497, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346, 105 S. Ct. 3275 (1985)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;The compensable injury necessarily is the harm caused by predicate a=
cts
sufficiently related to constitute a pattern, for the essence of the violat=
ion
is the commission of those acts in connection with the conduct of an
enterprise. . . . Any recoverable damages occurring by reason of a violatio=
n of
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
will flow from the commission of the predicate acts.&quot;); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4YN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FN0-003B-P0RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Nationw=
ide
Bank v. Gelt Funding Corp., 27 F.3d 763, 769 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><=
span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;RICO provides a civil remedy only to those persons injured 'by reason
of' the defendant's predicate acts.&quot;). First, plaintiffs principally
contend that their injuries were caused by defendants' violations of state
reporting requirements. At worst, this offense qualifies as a breach of
defendants' agreements with the Lawyer's Fund; it certainly does
not&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; qualify as a RICO predicate act under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Any injury caused by the failure to abide by the reporting agreements cannot
form the basis for RICO standing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
to the extent that plaintiffs' complaint can be read to allege that defenda=
nts
engaged in a pattern of racketeering activity that led to the concealment of
Schick's improper conduct from the Lawyer's Fund, </span><a style=3D'mso-fo=
otnote-id:
ftn104' href=3D"#_ftn104" name=3D"_ftnref104" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the connection between the RICO violation and the injury alleged is too
attenuated to satisfy the proximate cause requirement. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5010000400"></a>The RICO statute requires th=
at the
pattern of racketeering activity or the individual predicate acts impose a
direct injury on plaintiffs. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn105'
href=3D"#_ftn105" name=3D"_ftnref105" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>See Laborers Local 17</i>, 191 F.3d at 240. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_11"></a>&quot;Central to the notion of proxi=
mate
cause [under RICO] is the idea that a person is not liable to all those who=
 may
have been injured by his conduct, but only to those with respect to whom his
acts were 'a substantial factor in the sequence of responsible causation,' =
and
whose injury was 'reasonably foreseeable or anticipated as a natural
consequence.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5000000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FN0-003B-P0RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Nationw=
ide
Bank, 27 F.3d at 769</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5070000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht, 897 F.=
2d
21 at 23-24)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i>L=
aborers
Local 17, 191 F.3d at 235-36. The racketeering activities alleged are not a
substantial factor in the chain of causation that led to plaintiffs' losses.
Nor were those losses a reasonably foreseeable consequence&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp=
; of
that conduct. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND50J0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;The mere recitation of the
chain of causation alleged by the plaintiffs is perhaps the best explanatio=
n of
why they do not have standing in this case.&quot; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND50H0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Newton v. Tyson Foods, Inc., 207 F.3d 444, 447 (8th Cir. 2000)</i>.=
 In
order to demonstrate some link between the RICO violations alleged and the =
loss
of their investments, plaintiffs must show that, if the defendant banks had=
 not
committed the predicate acts of mail and wire fraud, (1) unspecified third
parties would have become aware that there were insufficient funds in the
escrow accounts to cover Schick's checks; (2) the Lawyer's Fund, in some
fashion, would&nbsp;[*124]&nbsp; then have become aware of&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;
these bounced checks; (3) the DDC or DGC, upon receiving the report of
dishonored checks from the Lawyer's Fund, would have recommended to the
Appellate Division that Schick be suspended or disbarred; (4) the Appellate
Division would have investigated Schick and adopted this recommendation alm=
ost
&quot;immediately&quot; after Schick bounced these checks; and (5) plaintif=
fs
would have been made aware of Schick's removal from the practice of law in =
time
to halt further investments. Simply put, plaintiffs' chain of causation mak=
es
clear that they were not directly injured by defendants' alleged racketeeri=
ng
activity; we cannot say that such a circuitous route, relying on the
independent action of various third parties, imposes a direct injury on
plaintiffs. Each of the assumptions upon which plaintiffs' theory rests is
inherently speculative. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i>
(&quot;The alleged injuries . . . are far distant along the chain of causat=
ion
from [defendants'] alleged wrongs and are too attenuated and removed from t=
hose
wrongs to provide a basis for standing under RICO.&quot;). For example, it
would be impossible to determine if and when the Appellate Division would h=
ave
disciplined Schick. Thus, there is no rational way to allocate the damages =
that
flow from the failure of the Appellate Division to act. <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>Cf. </i>Laborers Local 17, 191 F.3d at 239-40 (holding that a health
plan's suit against tobacco companies for their actions against plan
participants was not sufficiently direct&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; to support RICO
standing because the damages claims would be incredibly speculative and
dependent on unpredictable intervening events such as the agency of individ=
ual
smokers).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND50W0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, we have repeatedly
emphasized that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_11"></a>the reasonably
foreseeable victims of a RICO violation are typically limited to the target=
s,
competitors and intended victims of the racketeering enterprise. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND50V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Am. Exp=
ress
Co. S'holders Litig., 39 F.3d 395, 400 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(holding that injury to American Express shareholders was not the foreseeab=
le
result of a scheme to discredit an American Express competitor); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND50X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht, 897 F.=
2d
at 24</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'> (holding that the loss of employment for failure to
cooperate in RICO scheme was not proximately caused by racketeering activity
because the employee was not &quot;the target of the racketeering
enterprise&quot;); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5150000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sperber v.
Boesky, 849 F.2d 60, 64 (2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that
plaintiffs were not injured by defendant's racketeering activities with reg=
ard
to companies whose stock the plaintiffs did not own, because any artificial
increase in the price of stocks plaintiffs owned was too remote from
defendant's&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; illegal activities to satisfy the proximate
causation requirement.).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
in the instant case are too far removed from the actual predicate acts
committed to recover for any injuries that they suffered. The fundamental f=
law
in plaintiffs' theory is plaintiffs' allegation that defendants proceeded
independently of Schick, without the purpose or design of aiding in his fra=
ud
against plaintiffs. Defendants' goal was to &quot;perpetrate a fraud on the
attorney disciplinary system.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>Lerner
</i>Compl. at P 122. That alleged fraud, however, is not tied to plaintiffs'
injuries from Schick's fraud. Accepting plaintiffs' allegation would mean t=
hat
the Lawyer's Fund, not plaintiffs, was the target of defendants' alleged
racketeering enterprise. Plaintiffs' harm is therefore an unintended and
unanticipated byproduct of the banks' alleged racketeering activities. </sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Diversity Jurisdiction</span=
></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court declined to exercise jurisdiction over the state-law claims
contained in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i> complai=
nt,
although diversity jurisdiction exists in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Lerner</i> action because plaintiffs' citizenship is diverse from
defendants' and the amount in controversy is more than $ 75,000.
&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1332(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. On appeal, defendants contend that this dismissal was =
not
in error because the plaintiffs improperly and&nbsp;[*125]&nbsp; deliberate=
ly
manufactured jurisdiction by dividing the diverse plaintiffs and the
non-diverse plaintiffs into two separate actions. Plaintiffs'
&quot;manipulation&quot; is not, however, of the type that defeats diversity
jurisdiction.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
know of no rule of law that requires plaintiffs to align with all other
similarly injured plaintiffs before they may bring suit. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn106' href=3D"#_ftn106" name=3D"_ftnref106" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Nor are we aware of any principle that prevents an injured party from choos=
ing
among potential co-plaintiffs based on diversity concerns. Had the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner </i>plaintiffs brought their su=
it
independently of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i>
plaintiffs, defendants could not argue that we lacked federal jurisdiction =
over
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i> action because those
plaintiffs refused to allow New York citizens to join their suit. Defendant=
s'
argument therefore amounts to a claim that the filing of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff </i>complaint in tandem with t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner </i>complaint somehow divests t=
he
federal courts of jurisdiction. This clearly is not the case. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5180000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51H0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_11"></a>In determining whether the parties =
have
improperly manufactured federal jurisdiction, the essential inquiry is whet=
her
&quot;there is a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>bona fide</i>
controversy between . . . citizens of different states.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5170000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D3X0-003B-P4WV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Md. Cas. Co. =
v.
W.R. Grace &amp; Co., 23 F.3d 617, 623 (2d Cir. 1993)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51V0000400"></a>Selecting parties to a lawsu=
it
based upon citizenship does not implicate this concern in the same manner as
the misalignment of parties, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </=
i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51G0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BN80-003B-H3HB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>City of Dawso=
n v.
Columbia Ave. Sav. Fund, Safe Deposit, Title &amp; Trust Co., 197 U.S. 178,=
 49
L. Ed. 713, 25 S. Ct. 420 (1905)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, the failure to name an
indispensable party, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XM50-003B-G3PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Envirotech Co=
rp.
v. Bethlehem Steel Corp., 729 F.2d 70, 76 (2d Cir. 1984)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
or the collusive assignment of claims, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>see
</i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0H3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1359</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;A district court shall not have jurisdiction of a
civil action in which any party, by assignment or otherwise, has been
improperly or collusively made or joined to invoke the jurisdiction of such
court.&quot;). These practices are prohibited because the parties, through
manipulation, have funneled what are essentially local disputes between
non-diverse parties into the federal courts. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51V0000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See, e.g.</i>&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; , </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND51M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DGB0-001T-D3PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Airlines
Reporting Corp. v. S &amp; N Travel, Inc., 58 F.3d 857, 861-62 (2d Cir. 199=
5)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i> plaintiffs are genui=
nely
diverse from, and have a genuine dispute with, these defendants. Their choi=
ce
to band together as diverse plaintiffs injured by defendants is, therefore,=
 not
an improper attempt to manufacture federal jurisdiction. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III. Supplemental Jurisdiction</=
span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
argue that the district court should have exercised supplemental jurisdicti=
on
over the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law clai=
ms
because they are so closely related to the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Lerner
</i>claims that trying the two sets of claims together is judicially
economical.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A district court may exercise
supplemental jurisdiction over state-law claims &quot;in any civil action of
which the district courts have original jurisdiction,&quot; as long as the
state-law claims &quot;are so related to claims in the action within such
original jurisdiction that they form part of the same case or controversy u=
nder
Article III of the United States Constitution.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1367(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i>
plaintiffs' state-law claims clearly fall within the same case or controver=
sy
as their RICO claim; thus, the second&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; portion of the test=
 is
easily met in this case. The more difficult question is whether the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> plaintiffs' lack of RICO
standing implies a jurisdictional bar to their RICO claims. If so,
&nbsp;[*126]&nbsp; the district court did not have &quot;original
jurisdiction&quot; over the action because the RICO claim was the only fede=
ral
claim and, for the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i>
plaintiffs, the only basis for subject matter jurisdiction. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1331</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_10"></a>(&quot;The
district courts shall have original jurisdiction of all civil actions arisi=
ng
under the Constitution, laws, or treaties of the United States.&quot;).</sp=
an></p>

