<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND52M0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND52P0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc13_8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Standing
generally has two aspects: constitutional standing, a mandate of the &quot;=
case
or controversy&quot; requirement in Article III, and prudential considerati=
ons
of standing, which involve &quot;judicially self-imposed limits on the exer=
cise
of federal jurisdiction . . . .&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND52K0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3B30-003B-S2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allen v. Wrig=
ht,
468 U.S. 737, 751, 82 L. Ed. 2d 556, 104 S. Ct. 3315 (1984)</span></i></a><=
span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Prudential considerations include &quot;the general prohibition on a litiga=
nt's
raising another person's legal rights, the rule barring adjudication of
generalized grievances more appropriately addressed in the representative
branches, and the requirement that a plaintiff's complaint fall within the =
zone
of interests protected by the law invoked.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id</i>. Finally, a related aspect of standing, statutory standing, =
is
broadly described as a part of the prudential considerations regarding the
proper limits of jurisdiction. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn107'
href=3D"#_ftn107" name=3D"_ftnref107" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>See id.</i>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND52N0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:463W-R010-0038-X1NS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Law Offices of
Curtis V. Trinko, L.L.P. v. Bell Atl. Corp., 305 F.3d 89, 97 (2d Cir. 2002)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp;
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5310000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5380000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc14_6"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is clear that constitutional standing is a jurisdictional prerequisite to s=
uit.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5300000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Steel Co. v.
Citizens for a Better Env't, 523 U.S. 83, 101, 140 L. Ed. 2d </span></i></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*127]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 210, 118 S. =
Ct.
1003 (1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;While some . . . cases must be acknowledged to h=
ave
diluted the absolute purity of the rule that Article III jurisdiction is al=
ways
an antecedent question, none of them even approaches approval of a doctrine=
 of
hypothetical jurisdiction that enables a court to resolve contested questio=
ns
of law when its jurisdiction is in doubt.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND53B0000400"></a>Issues relating&nbsp;[**35]&=
nbsp;
to the merits of an action, almost by definition, are not jurisdictional. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5320000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id. at 89</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND53B0000400_2"></a>Although prudential
considerations of standing are also generally treated as jurisdictional in
nature, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see, e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5390000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-003B-P3K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thompson v.
County of Franklin, 15 F.3d 245, 248 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the applicability of this rule to RICO's statutory proximate cause requirem=
ent
is less clear. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn108' href=3D"#_ftn108"
name=3D"_ftnref108" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55V0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55X0000400"></a>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Steel Co.</i>, the Supreme Court noted that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_5"></a>courts may determine whether a cause=
 of
action exists under a given statute, an issue of statutory construction that
goes to the merits of the action, before addressing the zone-of-interests
prudential dimension of standing, an issue the Court labeled as statutory
standing. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 97</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also Nat'l</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CFM0-003B-S4N6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>R.R. </span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CFM0-003B-S4N6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Passenger Cor=
p.
v. Nat'l Ass'n of R.R. Passengers, 414 U.S. 453, 465 n.13, 38 L. Ed. 2d 646=
, 94
S. Ct. 690 (1974)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (deciding whether a statutory c=
ause
of action existed before determining whether the plaintiff met the statutory
standing requirements). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5650000400"></a>A=
s the
Supreme Court explained: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5650000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55X0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55V0000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The merits inquiry and the statu=
tory
standing inquiry often 'overlap.' The question whether <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>this</i> plaintiff has a cause of action under the statute, and the
question whether <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> plaintiff =
has a
cause of action under the statute are closely connected - indeed, depending
upon the asserted basis for lack of statutory standing, they are sometimes
identical, so that it would be exceedingly artificial to draw a distinction
between the two. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-left:20.0pt;text-align:justify;line-hei=
ght:
15.0pt'><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5650000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55X0000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55V0000400_3"></a><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal13><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND55W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Steel Co., 523
U.S. at 97 n.2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (internal citation omitted). &nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; Thus, at
least in some circumstances, lack of statutory standing does not divest a
district court of jurisdiction to determine the underlying merits of the
action. In declining to require statutory standing as a prerequisite to
jurisdiction when the merits of whether a cause of action exists are so
intertwined with the statutory standing question, the Supreme Court reasoned
that if &quot;all cause-of-action questions may be regarded as jurisdiction=
al
questions, and thus capable of being decided where there is no genuine case=
 or
controversy, it is hard to see what is left of that limitation in Article
III.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5640000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 93</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5670000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0060000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In determining whether RICO's
proximate cause requirement should be treated as jurisdictional, we take a =
cue
from the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Steel Co.</i> Court's emph=
asis
on the problem of having merits issues become jurisdictional&nbsp;[*128]&nb=
sp;
if merits issues are so intertwined with the standing issue that any
distinction becomes &quot;exceedingly artificial.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND5660000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S53-89K0-004C-1007-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 97 n.2=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Our decision in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Thompson</i> is not=
 to
the contrary. In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Thompson</i>, we h=
eld
that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_5"></a>standing, whether in its
constitutional or prudential form, was a jurisdictional limitation and as s=
uch
could not be waived. &nbsp;[**38]&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0000000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-003B-P3K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thompson, 15 =
F.3d
at 248</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. The question in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Thompson</i>
was whether an individual plaintiff could maintain an action under the
Nonintercourse Act, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-WBG2-D6RV-H2N1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>25 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
177</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>, which regulates the ownership of Indian tribal land, or
whether the only proper plaintiff was the Indian tribe itself. Thus, in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Thompson</i>, the issue of statutory
standing did not implicate the merits of the action, which related to the
proper boundaries of the St. Regis reservation; it related solely to the
