name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0070000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore, 189 F.=
3d
at 169 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;Because loss causation is an element of standing=
 to
sue under the RICO statute, the appropriate ground of dismissal for failure=
 to
plead loss causation would seem to be the lack of subject matter
jurisdiction.&quot;); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cf. </i></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0090000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8ND0-003B-533C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Seward v. Dev=
ine,
888 F.2d 957, 964 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The exercise of pendent
jurisdiction . . . should abide&quot; the district court's&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;
finding with respect to RICO standing.). Still, we recognized in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Moore</i> that we have treated dismiss=
als
for lack of RICO standing as dismissals for failure to state a claim pursua=
nt
to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, rather than as dismissals for lack of subject-matter
jurisdiction pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00K0000400_2"=
></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00X0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore, 189 F.=
3d
at 169 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>see, e.g.</i>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DFN0-003B-51WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Burdick v. Am.
Express Co., 865 F.2d 527, 528 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(per curiam). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00X0000400_2"></a>As we not=
ed in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Moore</i>, this distinction rarely has
practical consequences, because <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_5"></a>t=
he
standards for dismissal under 12(b)(6) and 12(b)(1) are substantively
identical. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore, 189 F.=
3d
at 169 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>But see </i></=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND00Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-003B-P3K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
