6)
motion, in which the defendant has the burden of proof).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0120000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In this case, however, the
difference between a jurisdictional bar to bringing a claim and a failure to
state a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'> is critical, because it determines whether the district
court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; claims. To our knowledge, o=
nly
one Circuit has directly addressed this issue in an analogous situation,
holding that statutory standing under the antitrust laws is not a prerequis=
ite
to federal subject matter jurisdiction. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0110000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:45TT-MR40-0038-X2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Lorazep=
am
&amp; Clorazepate Antitrust Litig., 351 U.S. App. D.C. 223, 289 F.3d 98, 10=
7-08
(D.C. Cir. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Unlike constitutional
standing, this court's jurisdiction does not turn on antitrust
standing.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01G0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01J0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01M0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Lorazepam</i>, the D.C. Circuit relied upon the Supreme Court's
differentiation between antitrust standing and constitutional standing in <=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated
General Contractors of Cal. v. Cal. State Council of Carpenters, 459 U.S. 5=
19,
74 L. Ed. 2d 723, 103 S. Ct. 897 (1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Associated General Contractors</i>, the
Supreme Court stated that determining &quot;whether [plaintiff] may recover=
 for
the injury it allegedly suffered by reason of the defendants' coercion agai=
nst
certain third parties . . . requires us to evaluate the plaintiff's harm, t=
he
alleged wrongdoing by the defendants, and the relationship between them.&qu=
ot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01H0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 535</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;The
label &quot;antitrust standing&quot; has traditionally been applied to some=
 of
the elements of this inquiry. &nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; As&nbsp;[*129]&nbsp;
commentators have observed, the focus of the doctrine of 'antitrust standin=
g'
is somewhat different from that of standing as a constitutional doctrine.&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01K0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 535 n.=
31</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01P0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>While the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Lorazepam</i> court does not make its reasoning explicit, it is
consistent with the guideline articulated in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Steel Co.</i> that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc18_5"></a>if sta=
tutory
standing is essentially indistinguishable from the merits of the action, its
absence is not a jurisdictional bar to suit, but a failure to state a claim=
. As
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Associated General Contractors</i>
emphasizes, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_5"></a>the proximate causati=
on
requirement in antitrust standing requires a court to evaluate the merits of
the action. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated Ge=
n.
Contractors, 459 U.S. at 535</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Time=
s",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>see also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01R0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-F2C0-003B-S3BN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ass'n of Data
Processing Serv. Orgs., Inc. v. Camp, 397 U.S. 150, 153, 25 L. Ed. 2d 184, =
90
S. Ct. 827 (1970)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (distinguishing question whether
plaintiff asserted a legal interest that was harmed by agency action from t=
he
question of standing; &quot;[the] legal interest test goes to the merits. T=
he
question of standing is different. It concerns, apart from the 'case' or
'controversy' test, the question whether the interest sought to be protecte=
d by
the&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; complainant is arguably within the zone of interests =
to
be protected or regulated by the statute or constitutional guarantee in
question.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01V0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01X0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20_5"></a>the requirement that a plaintiff
demonstrate proximate causation to have standing under the RICO statute goe=
s to
the merits of the action. &quot;Where the plaintiff alleges each element of=
 a
[RICO] violation, the compensable injury necessarily is the harm caused by
[the] predicate acts . . . .&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, 473 U=
.S.
at 497</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. In evaluating whether a plaintiff has standing under R=
ICO,
the court must, as in an antitrust case, &quot;evaluate the plaintiff's har=
m,
the alleged wrongdoing by the defendants, and the relationship between
them.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated Ge=
n.
Contractors, 459 U.S. at 535</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Time=
s",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, the issue of proximate
causation in the RICO standing context is sufficiently intertwined with the
merits of the RICO claim that any potential distinction between statutory
standing as a jurisdictional issue and the merits issue is untenable. It wo=
uld
change the merits issues into limitations on jurisdiction.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0200000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>By implicitly addressing whether
lack of statutory standing under RICO is a jurisdictional bar to suit, other
courts reinforce&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp; our determination that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21_5"></a>RICO standing is not a jurisdictional
issue. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g.</i>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND01Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40Y8-1PT0-0038-X15D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Maio v. Aetna,
Inc., 221 F.3d 472, 481-82 n.7 (3d Cir. 2000)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that the Third Circuit's practice was to treat RICO and antitrust
standing as non-jurisdictional, but declining to decide whether this practi=
ce
was proper); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0210000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-G450-00B1-D4H2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DeMauro v.
DeMauro, 115 F.3d 94, 96 (1st Cir. 1997)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (stating that whe=
ther
a plaintiff has pled &quot;a claim of 'injury' to her 'business or property=
' as
required for a civil RICO damages action . . . is sometimes described as a
'standing' issue&quot; and noting that &quot;there is plainly a case or con=
troversy
under Article III; but the statutory precondition of injury to business or
property must also be met&quot;);<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'> c=
f. </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0230000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-58N0-008H-V4HN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Whalen v. Car=
ter,
954 F.2d 1087, 1097 (5th Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Because we
have concluded that [plaintiff] might have the requisite standing to assert=
 his
RICO claims, we note that there is at least some possibility [plaintiff] co=
uld
assert his state law claims under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1367(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> . . . . Neither [plaintiff] nor the defendants have bri=
efed
the issue whether the district court can exercise supplemental
jurisdiction&nbsp;[**44]&nbsp; here.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc22_5"></a>despite describing the proxi=
mate
causation requirement as &quot;RICO standing,&quot; such standing is not
jurisdictional in nature under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, but is rather an element of the&nbsp;[*130]&nbsp; meri=
ts
addressed under a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> motion for failure to state a claim. RICO standing is
sufficiently intertwined with the merits of the action, such that its
determination requires an evaluation of the merits of the action and makes =
any
potential distinction between the merits and RICO standing exceedingly
artificial.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff </i>plaintiffs' lack of sta=
tutory
standing does not divest the district court of original jurisdiction over t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff </i>action. The district court=
 had
original jurisdiction to support the exercise of supplemental jurisdiction =
over
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff </i>plaintiffs' state-l=
aw
claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0260000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Having determined that the distr=
ict
court may exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law claims, we now turn to the prudential aspects=
 of
whether the district court should in fact do so. The factors that a district
court should consider in making this decision are unchanged by our analysis
above. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23_4"></a>In most circumstances, a
district court should&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; decline supplemental jurisdiction if
all federal claims have been dismissed at the pleading stage. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG450K1MND0250000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-G5P0-003B-S3PK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United Mine
Workers v. Gibbs, 383 U.S. 715, 726, 16 L. Ed. 2d 218, 86 S. Ct. 1130 (1966=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Because the district court must adjudicate identical issues in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i>, however, judicial economy =
might
best be served by exercising supplemental jurisdiction over the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law claims. Because =
it
dismissed both the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> actions, the district cour=
t did
not consider whether retaining supplemental jurisdiction over the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law claims might be
appropriate given that it retains jurisdiction over the <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>Lerner</i> state-law claims. As this decision is within the district
court's discretion, we remand the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>B=
ayroff</i>
action for its determination.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the reasons stated, we affirm the district court's dismissal of plaintiffs'
RICO claims for lack of standing, but we do so under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for failure to state a claim rather than under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(1)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> as
the district court did. Because the district court has diversity jurisdicti=
on
over the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i> plaintiffs' sta=
te-law
claims, we vacate the dismissal of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Lerner</i>&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp;
complaint and remand for adjudication of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Lerner </i>state-law claims. We also vacate the dismissal of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bayroff</i> state-law claims and reman=
d to
the district court for determination whether the exercise of supplemental
jurisdiction is appropriate in this case.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&=
nbsp;
</span></span></p>

