Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-68</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, to bring his claims against an=
y of
the defendants, and (2) declining to exercise its supplemental jurisdiction
over Baisch's state law claims. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. BACKGROUND </span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. The Racketeering Scheme</span=
></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Baisch
is in the business of providing construction services, including extending
financing to other construction firms. The Rubinos are father and son
contractors who operated Raycon Construction Company. Gallina is a sharehol=
der,
broker, and vice-president of McKinnon-Doxsee Insurance Agency, which provi=
des
or brokers insurance. Gallina and Rubino, Jr. were friends who were in regu=
lar
contact. Because we are reviewing the district court's grant of summary
judgment against Baisch, the following facts are presented in a light most
favorable to Baisch. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to Baisch, the defendants defrauded him, as well as Nassau County and unnam=
ed
insurance companies, by falsifying construction documents, payroll forms, a=
nd
other documents. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn109' href=3D"#_ftn10=
9"
name=3D"_ftnref109" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Starting in 1990, Nassau County
hired Raycon for various construction projects. One of the terms of their
contract was that the workers on the projects could&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; not be
independent contractors, but had to be employed by Raycon. The contract also
required Raycon to post performance bonds and to provide the County
periodically with proof of workers' compensation, disability, and general
public liability insurance. Raycon had to provide the County with estimates=
 of
the number and type of workers needed for each project, along with the numb=
er
of hours to be worked and the hourly rate per worker. Baisch claims that the
defendants engaged in a pattern of racketeering activity by causing Raycon =
to
submit to Nassau County fraudulent documents, including inflated estimates =
and
falsified claim vouchers. Baisch alleges that by 1990 Gallina and
McKinnon-Doxsee, who were providing insurance brokerage services to Raycon,
knew that the Rubinos were engaged in racketeering activity. Gallina and
McKinnon-Doxsee helped the Rubinos and Raycon obtain the insurance policies=
 and
performance bonds to enable them to continue their racketeering activities,
provided them with false certificates of insurance, and fraudulently obtain=
ed
replacement policies when Raycon's policies were cancelled for nonpayment.
Gallina and McKinnon-Doxsee also prepared false information&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp=
; to
be used by the workers' compensation and disability insurance carriers in t=
heir
payroll audits of Raycon in order to reduce Raycon's premiums. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
April 1994, Baisch alleges that Gallina asked him if he would provide a
commercial loan to Raycon and the Rubinos, whom &nbsp;[*370]&nbsp; Gallina
represented as reliable and creditworthy. Baisch claims that he relied on
Gallina's representations when he made his first loan to the Rubinos that y=
ear
and thereafter continued lending them money until July 1996. The Rubinos
allegedly used those funds to support their fraudulent activity. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
February 1997, after Baisch had checked Raycon's credit, Baisch and Rubino,=
 Jr.
entered into a factoring agreement, under which Baisch would loan money to
Raycon approximately equal to the amount of Raycon's claim
vouchers&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; submitted to Nassau County. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Then
Baisch would collect on each loan when Nassau County paid Raycon for its
claimed work. Baisch alleges that Gallina urged him to enter the factoring
agreement and reiterated earlier misrepresentations of Raycon's and the
Rubinos' financial reliability. Gallina conceded that he arranged for the 1=
994
loan from Baisch to Raycon so that he could &quot;procure the necessary bond
for Raycon, because Raycon lacked the funds needed for collateral.&quot; </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Baisch
argues that the Rubinos used the 1997 factoring agreement &quot;to continue=
 the
racketeering activities.&quot; Under the factoring agreement, the Rubinos
submitted forty-four vouchers to Baisch between February 1997 and May 1999,
upon which Baisch advanced to Raycon more than $ 850,000. Baisch alleges th=
at
the vouchers were fraudulent and that some of the vouchers were never even
submitted to Nassau County, leading Baisch to provide loans that, under the
terms of the factoring agreement, could not be repaid. The Rubinos failed to
repay ten loans from Baisch and only partially repaid four more, leaving $
306,000 unpaid. In August 1999, at a meeting with Baisch and Baisch's attor=
ney,
Rubino, Jr. admitted to Baisch that&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; he had submitted false=
 invoices
to him in order to obtain the needed loans. Rubino, Jr. later that month si=
gned
a confession of judgment in favor of Baisch in the amount of $ 357,440.21. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Supporting
his allegation that Gallina and McKinnon-Doxsee knew of Rubino's fraudulent
activities, and hence were racketeering co-conspirators, Baisch offers
statements by Gallina from a videotape surreptitiously recorded in March 20=
00,
about a year after the Rubinos began defaulting on his loans and several mo=
nths
after the authorities started investigating Raycon's fraud schemes. Gallina
does not deny that he made these statements. Rather, Gallina argues that his
later depositions, in which he denied knowledge of the fraud, are more cred=
ible
than the recordings. He also does not argue that the videotape should be
inadmissible in this civil proceeding. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the videotape, Gallina states: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>You
know the situation over there and the time sheets that [Rubino] was submitt=
ing
with phony names on it. He was getting them signed off when he didn't have =
any
people there . . . [Rubino has been engaged in these practices for] ten . .=
 .
years at least that I know of . . . It's been going on forever.
&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; He was paying off everybody that he could. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. .
. . </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>See,
I thought it was a matter of okay, he gets these phony time sheets. Guys si=
gn
off on it. He pays these guys a few bucks for signing off on it. But it must
have gone a lot deeper than that for the FBI to come in. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
further evidence of Gallina's knowledge, Baisch offers McKinnon-Doxsee's
records of Raycon's ten cancelled insurance policies from 1994 to 1997 and
internal McKinnon-Doxsee memos referring to Rubino's &quot;terrible [paymen=
t]
history back to 1996.&quot; Baisch also refers to Gallina's recorded statem=
ents
on the videotape stating that he provided insurance
certificates&nbsp;[*371]&nbsp; to Nassau County when he knew that Raycon did
not have insurance, and to Gallina's awareness that Raycon's insurers
repeatedly cancelled Raycon's insurance. Nevertheless, Gallina continued to
seek insurance for Raycon and falsely stated on Raycon's insurance applicat=
ions
in 1994 and 1998 that Raycon's insurance had not been cancelled. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
June 2001, Rubino, Jr. filed a bankruptcy petition in the United States
Bankruptcy Court for the Eastern District of New York, and in October 2001,=
 a
discharge was granted. Also in October 2001, &nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; Rubino, Jr. =
was
arrested and charged with defrauding Nassau County. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. The District Court Decision</=
span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I55BYKWGS1K000PRYKV001MK"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08H0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Baisch commenced this action in
August 2000. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08K0000400"></a>His amended
complaint alleges violations of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> and (d)</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
RICO, as well as violations of state common law. Gallina and McKinnon- Doxs=
ee
moved for summary judgment. The motion was heard by a magistrate judge sitt=
ing
as the district court upon the consent of parties, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0BK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
636(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>, and the district court determined that Baisch lacked
statutory standing to bring his claims against the defendants under RICO. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08G0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Baisch v. Gallina</i>, Civ. 00-5019, at 8-11 (E.D.N.Y. August 8, 20=
02).
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08K0000400_2"></a>The district court appl=
ied
the &quot;zone-of-interests&quot; test to determine whether &quot;apart from
the directness of the injury, the plaintiff is within the class of persons
sought to be benefitted by the provision at issue.&quot; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08J0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id</i>. at 9 (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08M0=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes v. Sec.
Investor Prot. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. 25=
8,
287-88, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532, 112 S. Ct. 1311 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Scalia, J., concurring)). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08X0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0900000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court concluded that
&quot;the factoring agreement and the relationship between Baisch, [the
Rubinos], and Gallina was too distinct&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; from the overall Co=
unty
scheme to be part of it for the purpose of RICO liability&quot; and that
&quot;there is no evidence to suggest that the intent of <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>the enterprise</i> - as opposed to the factoring agreement - was to
defraud Baisch.&quot; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08W0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. at 10. Relying on its reading =
of our
decision in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND08Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams v.</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Young &amp;
Rubicam Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d 234=
 (2d
Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, the district court concluded that Baisch lacked standi=
ng
on two independent grounds. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B80000400"><=
/a>First,
in order for a plaintiff to have standing in RICO's zone of interests, he m=
ust
be &quot;the target of the racketeering enterprise.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Id</i>. (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B5000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d at =
238</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B80000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0C20000400"></a>The district court concluded=
 that
Nassau County, not Baisch, was the target of the racketeering enterprise.
Second, the plaintiff's injury must &quot;flow from the harms that the
predicate acts . . . were intended to cause and the laws against them were =
intended
to prevent.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. (quoting =
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d at =
238</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0C20000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0C40000400"></a>The district court found that
&quot;Baisch's injury was the result of [the Rubinos'] failure to repay him,
not the result of the alleged RICO enterprise or its predicate acts.&quot; =
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0B90000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id</i>. at 10-11. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0C40000400_2"><=
/a>The
district court characterized&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; Baisch's claims as a
&quot;creative effort[] to stretch&quot; common law fraud into a RICO claim=
. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0C30000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id</i>. at 11. The district court declined to exercise its suppleme=
ntal
jurisdiction over Baisch's state law claims, and also found an independent
ground for dismissing the claims against Rubino, Jr. because the bankruptcy
court for the Eastern District of New York had discharged Rubino, Jr.'s deb=
ts. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. at 11. Baisch has appealed fro=
m the
district court's grant of summary judgment to the defendants. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. DISCUSSION </span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. Standard of Review</span></b>=
<span
