lscc4_13"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
recently clarified&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; our approach to RICO standing in </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0CX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner v. Fle=
et
Bank, N.A.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 318 F.3d 11=
3,
120-24 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DR0000400"></a>We need not repeat <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lerner</i>'s thorough analysis, but we=
 will
review its main points. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DT0000400"></a>To
demonstrate standing, a plaintiff must plead, at a minimum, &quot;(1) the
defendant's violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>[&sect;] 1962=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
(2) an injury to the plaintiff's business or property, and (3) causation of=
 the
injury by the defendant's violation. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DT0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DR0000400_2"></a>This third requirement is
satisfied if the defendant's injurious conduct is both the factual and the
proximate cause of the injury alleged.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 120</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks and citations omitted). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FK0000400"></a>The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>Lerner</i> court then turned to the question of whether to apply
separately the &quot;statutory standing&quot; or zone-of-interests test,
&quot;which seeks to determine whether, apart from the directness of the
injury, the plaintiff is within the class of persons sought to be benefitte=
d by
the provision at issue.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0DS0=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 287</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Scalia, J., concurring). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FK0000400_2"><=
/a>In
1992, the Supreme Court majority could have addressed this issue in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, but thought it was
&quot;inopportune to resolve the issue&quot; at that time, and instead reso=
lved
the standing&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; question solely on proximate causation groun=
ds. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FN0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 276</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FN0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0H80000400"></a>In his concurrence, Justice =
Scalia
argued that a plaintiff has standing under RICO only if he or she fulfills =
the
traditional elements of statutory standing of the predicate statutes on whi=
ch
the RICO claim is based. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0HB000040=
0"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at
288-89</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (Scalia, J., concurring). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0HB0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0H80000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0J60000400"></a>Two of those requirements are
proximate causation and the zone- of-interests test. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Id</i>. Justice O'Connor, joined by Justices White and Stevens,
rejected Justice Scalia's approach, concluding that RICO does not incorpora=
te
the standing requirements of the predicate acts. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0FP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 280</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(O'Connor, J., concurring). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0J60000400_2"=
></a>We
followed <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> by resolving the
standing issue on proximate cause grounds in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0H90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powers v. Bri=
tish
Vita, P.L.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 57 F.3d 176,
188 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and later we noted that &quot;=
it
is unclear precisely how this test would apply under the complex
&nbsp;[*373]&nbsp; structure of the RICO statute.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0HC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 318 F.3d at=
 120</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0J60000400_3"></a>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Lerner</i>, we concluded that &quot;if the standing issue may be
resolved on proximate cause grounds, the question whether the plaintiff must
also satisfy the standing requirements&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; of the underlying
statutes whose violations constitute the predicate acts . . . need not be
reached.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND0J50000400"></a><a
