<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND13X0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND15K0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Nothing in the above discussion =
is
in any way inconsistent with our holding in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Abrahams</i>,
which incorporated the zone-of- interests test into the aforementioned
proximate cause analysis by requiring that a RICO plaintiff &quot;must show
both that he is within the class the statute sought to protect and that the
harm done was the one that the statute meant to prevent.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND13W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d at =
237</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_11"></a>The Supreme Court has interpreted R=
ICO
expansively, &quot;primarily [because] of the breadth of the predicate
offenses, &nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; in particular the inclusion of wire, mail, and
securities fraud, and the failure of Congress and the courts to develop a
meaningful concept of 'pattern.'&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND15J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J. Inc.</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 492 U.S. at=
 236</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). And, RICO was designed, in part, to pro=
tect
investors and lenders from losing money to elaborate mail fraud schemes. Gi=
ven
all this, it is plain that Baisch's allegations are sufficient to establish
standing. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND15N0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In holding that the plaintiff in=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Abrahams</i> was not a &quot;target&qu=
ot; of
the enterprise and thus did not have standing, we explained that his
&quot;injuries did not flow from the harms that the predicate acts . . . we=
re
intended to cause and the laws against them were intended to prevent.&quot;=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND15M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Abrahams</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-37G0-006F-M529-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 79 F.3d at =
238</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
And that was true enough on the facts in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Abrahams</i>.
In the instant case, however, the district court, in relying on this langua=
ge
to conclude that Baisch lacked standing, read &quot;intended harm&quot; too
restrictively. In the facts of this case, we look at the intent to create <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>a risk of harm</i>. <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Abrahams</i> cited William Prosser, et al., <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Prosser and Keeton on the Law of Torts</i>, &sect; 36, at 224-25, b=
ut
nowhere do those cited pages&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; support a requirement that t=
he
defendant intend the specific harm, only that the statute must be intended =
to
protect a particular class of persons and to prevent a particular class of
risks. Nor can we find any decision in any other circuit read &quot;intended
harm&quot; so narrowly. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn110' href=3D"=
#_ftn110"
name=3D"_ftnref110" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[*376]&nbsp; Moreover, in several paradigmatic racketeering enterpris=
es,
the racketeer does not necessarily intend to harm the specific victim. He or
she may, instead, intend no more than to create a substantial risk of injur=
y to
the victim. In a Ponzi scheme, for example, the racketeer targets investors
through fraud, but he does not consciously intend for those investors to lo=
se
their money; he simply wants to perpetuate the Ponzi scheme for as long as
possible. Similarly, in a case where a racketeer collects loans under false
pretenses, he may intend to repay the loan so that no one suffers an injury,
but he has fraudulently created a high risk of default, and the plaintiff m=
ay
be injured as a result of that risk. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_11"=
></a>Where
a racketeering enterprise intends no specific harms to any particular
individual, but causes harm by the creation of substantial risk of harm, the
victim injured by that enterprise's harm may have RICO standing under <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Abrahams</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>E. Baisch's Standing Against Gal=
lina</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Gallina
also argues that he is not a proper defendant for Baisch's RICO claim becau=
se
1) he committed no predicate criminal acts; 2) he did not operate or manage=
 the
enterprise; and 3) even if Baisch&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; has presented an issue =
of
Gallina's knowledge of fraud, such knowledge is insufficient to establish R=
ICO
conspiracy. We addressed Gallina's first argument <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>supra</i>, and found that Baisch has presented a genuine question a=
s to
Gallina's committing mail fraud and causing Baisch's injury. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND17N0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Baisch has also adequately alleg=
ed
that Gallina participated in the operation or management of the enterprise.=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND19B0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc13_9"></a>To
be liable under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which prohibits participation in a racketeering
enterprise, &quot;one must have some part in directing [the enterprise's]
affairs. . . . RICO liability is not limited to those with primary
responsibility for the enterprise's affairs.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND17M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S70-NFC0-003B-R50T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reves v. Ernst
&amp; Young</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S70-NFC0-003B-R50T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 507 U.S. 17=
0,
179, 122 L. Ed. 2d 525, 113 S. Ct. 1163 (1993)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND19B0000400_2"></a>One is liable under RICO i=
f he
or she has &quot;discretionary authority in carrying out the instructions of
the [conspiracy's] principals,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND1990000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WFF-KH90-00B1-D05K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Diaz, 176 F.3d 52, 93 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, or &quot;played =
some
part in directing the affairs&quot; of the RICO enterprise, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND19C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42PH-01G0-0038-X2BT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>De Falco v.
Bernas</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42PH-01G0-0038-X2BT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 244 F.3d 28=
6,
311 (2d Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>see also</i> </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F3M5JX0K1MND19F0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FXR0-00B1-D35F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Miller</span></i></a><a
