 </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, Eternity challenges the dismissal of its claims. For the reasons set
forth below, we affirm the dismissal of the fraudulent and negligent
misrepresentation claims but reverse the dismissal of the contract claim and
remand for further proceedings.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
behalf of its investors, Eternity trades in global bonds, equities and
currencies, including emerging-market debt. During the relevant period,
Eternity's investment portfolio included short-term Argentine sovereign and
corporate bonds. In emerging markets such as Argentina, a significant credit
risk is &quot;country risk,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i=
.e.</i>,
&quot;the risk that economic, social, and political conditions and events i=
n a
foreign country will adversely affect an institution's financial
interests,&quot; including &quot;the possibility of nationalization or
expropriation of assets, government repudiation of external indebtedness, .=
 . .
and currency depreciation or devaluation.&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn111' href=3D"#_ftn111" name=3D"_ftnref111" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Credit risk can be managed, however. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn=
112'
href=3D"#_ftn112" name=3D"_ftnref112" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Banks, investment funds and oth=
er
institutions increasingly use financial contracts known as &quot;credit
derivatives&quot; to mitigate credit risk. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn113' href=3D"#_ftn113" name=3D"_ftnref113" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
October 2001, in&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; light of Argentina's rapidly deteriorating
political and economic prospects, Eternity purchased CDSs to hedge the cred=
it
risk on its in-country investments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp;
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>I</b> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>By
way of introduction, we briefly review the terminology, documentation, and
structure of Eternity's credit default swaps. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Terminology</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
credit default swap is the most common form of credit derivative, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, &quot;[a] &nbsp;[*172]&nbsp;
contract which transfers credit risk from a protection buyer to a credit
protection seller.&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn114'
href=3D"#_ftn114" name=3D"_ftnref114" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; Protection buy=
ers
(here, Eternity) can use credit derivatives to manage particular market
exposures and return-on-investment </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn11=
5'
href=3D"#_ftn115" name=3D"_ftnref115" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; and protection sellers (here,
Morgan) generally use credit derivatives to earn income and diversify their=
 own
investment portfolios. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn116' href=3D"#=
_ftn116"
name=3D"_ftnref116" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Simply put, a credit default sw=
ap
is a bilateral financial contract in which &quot;[a] protection buyer makes=
[]
periodic payments to . . . the protection seller, in return for a contingent
payment if a predefined credit event occurs in the reference credit,&quot; =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, the obligation on which the
contract is written. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn117' href=3D"#_f=
tn117"
name=3D"_ftnref117" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Often,
the reference asset that the protection buyer delivers to the protection se=
ller
following a credit event is the instrument that is being hedged. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn118' href=3D"#_ftn118" name=3D"_ftnref118" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
But in emerging markets, an investor may calculate that a particular credit
risk &quot;is reasonably correlated with the performance of [the sovereign]
itself,&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn119' href=3D"#_ftn119"
name=3D"_ftnref119" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; so that (as he=
re)
the investor may seek to isolate and hedge country risk with credit default
swaps written on some portion of the sovereign's outstanding debt. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn120' href=3D"#_ftn120" name=3D"_ftnref120" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
many contexts a &quot;default&quot; is a simple failure to pay; in a credit
default swap, it references a stipulated bundle of &quot;credit events&quot;
(such as bankruptcy, debt moratoria, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn=
121'
href=3D"#_ftn121" name=3D"_ftnref121" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and debt
restructurings) that will trigger the protection seller's obligation to
&quot;settle&quot; the contract via the swap mechanism agreed to between the
parties. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn122' href=3D"#_ftn122"
name=3D"_ftnref122" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The entire bundle=
 is
typically made subject to a materiality threshold. </span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn123' href=3D"#_ftn123" name=3D"_ftnref123" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;
The occurrence of a credit event triggers the &quot;swap,&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, the protection seller's obli=
gation
to pay on the contract according to the settlement mechanism. &quot;The
contingent payment can be &nbsp;[*173]&nbsp; based on cash settlement . . .=
 or
physical delivery of the reference asset, in exchange for a cash payment eq=
ual
to the initial notional [<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, =
face]
amount [of the CDS contract].&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn1=
24'
href=3D"#_ftn124" name=3D"_ftnref124" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> A CDS buyer holdi=
ng a
sufficient amount of the reference credit can simply tender it to the CDS
seller for payment; but ownership of the reference credit prior to default =
is
unnecessary. If a credit event occurs with respect to the obligation(s) nam=
ed in
a CDS, and notice thereof&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; has been given </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn125' href=3D"#_ftn125" name=3D"_ftnref125" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(and the CDS buyer has otherwise performed), the CDS seller must settle.
Liquidity in a secondary market increases the usefulness of a CDS as a hedg=
ing
tool, though the limited depth of that market &quot;can make it difficult to
offset . . . positions prior to contract maturity.&quot; </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn126' href=3D"#_ftn126" name=3D"_ftnref126" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Documentation</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3730000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The principal issue dividing the
parties is whether the CDS contracts at issue are ambiguous in any material
respect. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_14"></a>&quot;An ambiguity exists
where the terms of a contract could suggest 'more than one meaning when vie=
wed
objectively by a reasonably intelligent person who has examined the context=
 of
the entire integrated agreement and who is cognizant of the customs, practi=
ces,
usages and terminology as generally understood in the particular trade or
business.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3150000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
