if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>If</span></i><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> an Obligation
Exchange has occurred, the determination as to whether one of the events
described under Section 4.7(a)(i) to (v) has occurred will be based on a <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>comparison</i> of the terms of the
Obligation immediately before such Obligation Exchange and the terms of the
resulting Obligation immediately following such Obligation Exchange. </span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Id.</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&sect; 4.7(c) (emphases added). Thus <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>if</i>
the voluntary debt exchange&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; is an &quot;Obligation
Exchange,&quot; then it is a restructuring <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>if</i>
the terms of the Secured Loans--as compared with the terms of the exchanged
sovereign bonds--indicate that &quot;one of the events [that constitutes a
restructuring under &sect; 4.7] has occurred.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Id.</i> Morgan concedes that such a comparison would show that the
voluntary debt exchange was a restructuring credit event: The secured loans
undeniably provide a lower return over a longer maturity than the original
bonds, two features that qualify as restructuring occurrences under &sect; =
4.7.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>id.</i> &sect; 4.7(a)(i), (iii). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Morgan
argues, however, that because &sect; 4.9 of the 1999 Definitions states tha=
t an
&quot;Obligation Exchange&quot; is a &quot;mandatory transfer&quot; of one =
set
of obligations for another, and because participation in the government's d=
ebt
exchange program was &quot;voluntary,&quot; a comparison of the two instrum=
ents
is irrelevant. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3JN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The term &quot;mandatory
transfer&quot; as it appears in &sect; 4.9 of the ISDA definitions is not
self-reading, and is therefore ambiguous if it could suggest more than one
meaning when viewed objectively, in the context of each CDS agreement and t=
he &quot;customs,
practices, [and] usages . . . as generally understood&quot; &nbsp;[**33]&nb=
sp;
in the credit derivatives trade. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3JM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49N3-WNY0-0038-X34Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>World Trade</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49N3-WNY0-0038-X34Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49N3-WNY0-0038-X34Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ctr. Props.</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49N3-WNY0-0038-X34Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 345 F.3d at=
 184</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Morgan makes the intuitively appealing argument that a &quot;mandatory
transfer&quot; cannot be an exchange offered on &quot;voluntary terms.&quot;
The district court was persuaded by this argument, citing Black's Law
Dictionary: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3JN0000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Argentina's [voluntary debt]
exchange program cannot qualify as &quot;mandatory.&quot; Eternity made a
choice. Along with &quot;a substantial portion&quot; of holders of those
Argentine obligations, Eternity chose to exchange the obligations for lower=
-interest
secured loans. (Second Am. Compl. PP38-39, 61.). . . . <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KM0000400"></a>Despite Eternity's attempt to
argue that &quot;mandatory&quot; &nbsp;[*181]&nbsp; should be read to encom=
pass
situations that are &quot;economically coercive,&quot; the plain meaning of=
 the
term &quot;mandatory&quot; does not permit such a finding. The term
&quot;mandatory&quot; is defined by Black's Law Dictionary (7th Ed. 1999) as
&quot;of, relating to, or constituting a command&quot; or &quot;required.&q=
uot;
The choice made by Eternity may have been unpalatable, but that does not ma=
ke
it mandatory. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KM0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3JP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eternity II</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2003 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 12351, 2003 WL 21305355, at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. A dictionary
definition, however, does not take into account what &quot;mandatory
transfer&quot; means&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; in the context of a particular indus=
try.
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Eternity
makes the less obvious but plausible argument that to credit-risk protection
buyers such as itself, a &quot;mandatory transfer&quot; includes any obliga=
tion
exchange achieved by &quot;economic coercion,&quot; regardless of its
classification as &quot;voluntary&quot; or &quot;mandatory&quot; by the
initiating party. According to Black's, &quot;economic coercion&quot; is
&quot;conduct that constitutes improper use of economic power to compel ano=
ther
to submit to the wishes of one who wields it.&quot; Black's Law Dictionary =
252
(7th ed. 1999). Assuming that the government's debt exchange was economical=
ly
coercive, &quot;voluntary&quot; participation by the &quot;coerced&quot;
bondholders appears to resemble &quot;mandatory&quot; action. At the same t=
ime,
from Argentina's perspective, the exchange may have been voluntary in fact,=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, the government may have had =
the
intention to honor its debt obligations to nonparticipants without delay or
reduction. But Argentina's characterization--which is self-serving--does not
control, particularly when one considers Eternity's allegation that Morgan =
was
an architect of the debt exchange. A proper interpretation of the CDS contr=
acts
must be drawn from the contract language and, &nbsp;[**35]&nbsp; where
necessary, from other indicia of the parties' intent. Section 4.9 is silent=
 as
to whose perspective dictates whether an obligation exchange has occurred (=
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g.</i>, the issuer or the holder, or=
 the
investment press or community), and the parties' competing interpretations =
are
plausible. We cannot resolve that ambiguity at this stage, as the district
court took no submissions on the customs and usages of the credit derivativ=
es
industry, and the parties point us to no definitive source that resolves the
difficulty. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
the CDS contracts are silent on the precise meaning of &quot;mandatory
transfer,&quot; the ISDA has not ignored the issue. In its complaint, Etern=
ity
refers to a draft &quot;User's Guide&quot; to the 1999 Definitions, publica=
tion
of which appears to have been tabled indefinitely. </span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn134' href=3D"#_ftn134" name=3D"_ftnref134" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>24</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The draft guide &nbsp;[*182]&nbsp; explained that the &quot;reference to
'mandatory transfer' in [the] definition of ['Obligation Exchange'] . . .
should be read as clarifying rather than restricting the Restructuring
definition and should not be read to mean that the optional exchange of
Obligations for other assets cannot constitute a Restructuring.&quot; The
User's Guide to the 1999 Definitions was never formally promulgated,
&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; but it does indicate that, (at least) as of the time Ete=
rnity
purchased the credit default swaps, the meaning of &quot;mandatory
transfer&quot; was perhaps more open-ended than Morgan contends. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KP0000400"></a>Finally, in drawing its
conclusion that the voluntary debt exchange was not an obligation exchange,=
 the
district court appears to have relied on its observation that Eternity &quo=
t;<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>made a choice</i>&quot; to participate=
 in
the voluntary debt exchange. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KN00=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eternity II</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2003 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 12351, 2003 WL 21305335, at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (emphasis added).=
 Any
such reliance was misplaced. Whether Eternity actually owned Argentina's
sovereign bonds (and whether it chose to participate in the government's de=
bt
swap if it did) is irrelevant to whether the exchange was a restructuring
credit event under the disputed CDS contracts. The swaps were triggered if =
the
government defaulted on its own debt obligations, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, upon the occurrence of a &quot;credit event&quot; with
respect to those obligations <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>as a c=
lass</i>.
The CDS contracts did not require or contemplate that the credit protection
buyer (here, Eternity) would hold the reference bonds except as may become
necessary to exercise the put. Thus, if the Argentine government's voluntary
debt exchange was a restructuring within the meaning of the 1999 Definition=
s,
the CDS contracts were triggered; any election Eternity might have made with
respect to its own bond holdings does not matter. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp;
We go on to consider Eternity's arguments that the government's debt exchan=
ge
was a restructuring even if it was <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
not</i>
an obligation exchange. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(ii)
The effect of the voluntary debt exchange on Argentina's original debt
obligations </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
the 1999 Definitions, a restructuring credit event occurs when any one of f=
ive
enumerated events &quot;occurs, is agreed [to] . . . or is announced&quot; =
with
respect to an &quot;obligation&quot; upon which a credit default swap is
written. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>1999 Definitions</i>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>, &sect; 4.7(a). These includ=
e: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(i)a
reduction in the rate of payable interest or principal on the obligation, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i> &sect; 4.7(a)(i)-(ii); </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(ii)
a postponement of payment on interest or principal, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>id.</i> &sect; 4.7(a)(iii); and </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(iii)
a subordination of the obligation that did not exist prior to the occurrenc=
e of
the restructuring credit event, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.=
</i>
&sect; 4.7(a)(iv). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Eternity
contends that each of these three events &quot;occurred&quot; with respect =
to
certain classes of Argentine sovereign bonds as a consequence of the
government's voluntary debt exchange. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KS0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court held that
&quot;under the terms of the 'voluntary debt exchange,' the obligations
submitted&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp; into the Trust . . . themselves remained
unchanged.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3KR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eternity</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>II</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48TB-X2B0-0038-Y1HX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2003 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 12351, 2003 WL 21305355, at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. That observation=
 may
be sound as far as it goes. But &sect; 4.7 provides that a restructuring
results if any event defined in the section &quot;occurs&quot; with respect=
 to
&quot;one or more obligations.&quot; Thus, the proper inquiry is whether th=
e debt
exchange caused a restructuring to occur with respect to <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>any</i> of the Argentine sovereign bonds. </span><a style=3D'mso-fo=
otnote-id:
ftn135' href=3D"#_ftn135" name=3D"_ftnref135" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>25</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
For the &nbsp;[*183]&nbsp; purpose of this analysis, it is useful to consid=
er
separately the impact of the debt exchange on obligations that were exchang=
ed
pursuant to the government's &quot;voluntary&quot; offer (participating
obligations), and on those retained by sovereign-bond holders who elected to
forgo the government's program (nonparticipating obligations). To negative =
the
possibility that there was a restructuring credit event, it must be clear t=
hat
none of the &quot;events&quot; described in &sect;&sect; 4.7(a)(i)-(v) occu=
rred
with respect either to the participating obligations or to the nonparticipa=
ting
obligations. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp;
(a)Participating Obligations </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Participating
obligations were ultimately deposited with the Central Bank of the Republic=
 of
Argentina pursuant to Article 23 of Presidential Decree 1387, issued Novemb=
er
1, 2001. Six weeks later, Presidential Decree 1646 approved both a Secured =
Loan
Agreement to govern the terms of the loans that would replace the bonds
tendered in the exchange, and a Trust Agreement that governed the status of
obligations so tendered. Eternity contends that a restructuring credit event
occurred with respect to tendered obligations because their terms were exte=
nded
and/or payment was suspended for the duration of their life-in-trust. </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Maturity Extension</span></i><sp=
an
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
On November 28, 2001 the Ministry of Economy promulgated Resolution 767 whi=
ch, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inter</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>alia</i>, defined the sovereign bonds eligible to participate in the
voluntary exchange and, at Annex II, delineated the &quot;exchange
mechanism&quot; for those securities, including the provision that
&quot;eligible Securities whose total or partial original maturity is prior=
 to
December 31, 2010 shall receive secured loans that will extend the average =
life
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>of</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>the Eligible Security</i> by 3 years.&quot; (emphasis added). Morgan
does not&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; directly respond to Eternity's contention that t=
his
wording extends the maturity of certain debt obligations within the class
referenced in the disputed CDS contracts. This provision creates a question=
 of
fact as to whether the three-year extension constitutes a restructuring cre=
dit
event under &sect; 4.7(iii) of the 1999 Definitions. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Suspension of Payment</span></i>=
<span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Under the terms of the voluntary debt exchange (primarily laid down in Decr=
ee
1646), participating obligations were placed in trust. The Trust Agreement
named Caja de Valores as trustee and provided that the government of Argent=
ina
would &quot;acquire Participation Certificates in the . . . Trust, the
underlying assets of which are the Argentine government debt securities&quo=
t;
that participated in the exchange. By virtue of its participation, the Repu=
blic
of Argentina was the &quot;Original Beneficiary&quot; of the trust. Decree =
1646
further provided that &quot;for purposes of determining the outstanding amo=
unt
of Argentine government debt or of determining the national debt ceiling, .=
 . .
[tendered obligations] shall not be included in such calculations for so lo=
ng
as the Argentine Federal Government is the Beneficiary of any payments made=
