if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3SS0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court observed in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eternity I</i> that the Fund's complai=
nt
lacks &quot;any allegation of a special relationship of trust between the
parties. It appears to have been a typical arm's length business
transaction.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3SR0000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:473W-MBN0-0038-Y23Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eternity I</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:473W-MBN0-0038-Y23Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2002 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 20706, 2002 WL 31426310, at *7</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotati=
on
marks omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T10000400"></a>Judge McK=
enna
concluded nevertheless that Eternity's allegations with respect to the othe=
r <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kimmell</i> prongs-Morgan's expertise =
in the
credit derivatives markets and its knowledge of Eternity's intended use of =
the
misrepresented information--brought the case within <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Suez</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Investors</i>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T10000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i> We disagree=
. In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Suez Investors</i>, this Court found t=
hat </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T10000400_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>plaintiff's complaint implied a
relationship between the parties that extended beyond the typical arms leng=
th
business&nbsp;[**57]&nbsp; transaction [because] defendants initiated conta=
ct
with plaintiffs, induced them to forbear from performing their own due
diligence, and repeatedly vouched for the veracity of the allegedly decepti=
ve
information. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T10000400_4"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:430W-YKX0-0038-X2GN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>250 F.3d at 1=
03</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Eternity makes no similar allegations, and the complaint suggests
&nbsp;[*189]&nbsp; that the Fund was itself sophisticated about credit defa=
ult
swaps. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to its complaint, &quot;Eternity turned to Morgan,&quot; 2d Am. Compl. P16,=
 to
facilitate its investment strategy in Argentina, which included
&quot;sophisticated hedging techniques,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>id.</i> P7. Eternity's Alberto Franco was &quot;an experienced trad=
er
in securities and derivatives,&quot; who had worked at Bankers Trust, where=
 he
&quot;developed strategies for minimizing the risk of investing in the
securities of Latin American corporations . . . [including] offsetting the =
risk
of such securities with transactions in credit derivatives.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> PP8-9. These descriptions, tak=
en
directly from Eternity's second amended complaint, indicate that the Fund w=
as
sophisticated about the credit derivatives transactions it was contemplatin=
g,
was not dissuaded from due diligence, and had the capacity to conduct it.
Moreover, &nbsp;[**58]&nbsp; the misrepresentation alleged was made in a si=
ngle
phone call in February 2001, eight months before Eternity signed the disput=
ed
CDS contracts, in the context of an express disclaimer of any <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>commitment</i> to unwind the CDS
transactions. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn140' href=3D"#_ftn140"
name=3D"_ftnref140" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>30</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Eternity
does allege that Morgan held itself out&nbsp;[**59]&nbsp; as an expert in
credit derivatives, and that Morgan was made aware of the possibility that
Eternity might want to liquidate through a secondary market. But these
allegations must be viewed against the backdrop of the specific transactions
Eternity and Morgan undertook. Eternity's complaint acknowledges that </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>although
the credit swap market is large, there is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>no</i>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>substantial liquidity</i> or after-=
market
trading for <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> <i style=3D'mso=
-bidi-font-style:
normal'>particular swap</i>. The terms of the swaps are often unique and th=
eir
value is often dependent upon the credit of one or both parties thereto. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T30000400"></a>For example, <i style=3D'mso=
-bidi-font-style:
normal'>swaps are generally transferable</i>, but opportunities for third p=
arty
transfers can be scarce due to the unique nature of each credit swap. </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T30000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>2d Am. Compl. P20 (emphases adde=
d). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_12"></a>The New York Court of Appeals has
observed that a relationship sufficiently special to justify reliance (and a
subsequent action for negligent misrepresentation) may arise when a person
&quot;wholly without knowledge seek[s] assurances from one with exclusive
knowledge.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F252FJ0K1MND3T20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-7810-003V-B2XM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heard</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-7810-003V-B2XM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 82 N.Y.2d a=
t 75</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Reliance is less plausible when the defendant is not &quot;imparting exclus=
ive
information,&quot; and the plaintiff&nbsp;[**60]&nbsp; exhibits familiarity
with the &quot;hazards&quot; inherent to a particular transaction. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:normal'>id</i>.
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
Eternity concedes, it was aware (i) that there is a secondary market for cr=
edit
default swaps, and (ii) that the market for any particular swap is chancy.
Absent any allegations suggesting that Morgan misrepresented the
transferability of the specific credit default swaps at issue here--as oppo=
sed
to statements about the general transferability of such instruments--the
district court's dismissal was correct. Eternity simply failed to allege any
facts suggesting that its purported reliance on Morgan's representations ab=
out
the liquidity &nbsp;[*190]&nbsp; of CDS contracts written on Argentine debt=
 was
justified. The &quot;'casual' statements and contacts&quot; cited by the Fu=
nd
are insufficient to preserve its negligent misrepresentation claim from 12(=
b)(6)
dismissal. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal15 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we affirm the judgment insofar as it dismissed
Eternity's claims premised on fraud and negligent misrepresentation. We rev=
erse
the judgment insofar as it dismissed Eternity's claim for breach of contrac=
t,
and remand for further proceedings consistent with this opinion, on the gro=
und
that certain material&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp; terms of the contracts cannot be fo=
und
unambiguous on the basis of the pleadings alone.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

