<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, appellants have abandoned their securities fraud claim and argue on=
ly
that the district court erred in dismissing their RICO and common law claim=
s.
In the alternative, appellants contend that the district court erred in
dismissing the complaint without granting them leave to replead. Because we
agree with the district court that appellants' allegations failed to satisfy
RICO's proximate cause requirement, and because the district court did not
abuse its discretion in denying leave to replead, we affirm the district
court's dismissal of the complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>BACKGROUND</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ52Y0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Appellants' complaint alleges a =
tale
of international intrigue in which top-level officers of one of this countr=
y's
preeminent corporations conspired with shady overseas operatives, greedy
journalists, and corrupt foreign politicians in a scheme to defame a rival =
by
falsely linking him to organized crime, Columbian drug trafficking, and the
Iran-Contra affair. In reviewing a dismissal under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, we of course assume these allegations to be true. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ52X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8N20-008H-V0YR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allen v.
WestPoint-Pepperell, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8N20-008H-V0YR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 945 F.2d 40=
, 44
(2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
1983, American Express purchased Trade Development Bank (&quot;TDB&quot;), =
an
international bank headquartered in Geneva, Switzerland and founded by Edmo=
nd
J. Safra, an international banker and financier. As part of the acquisition,
Safra agreed to a five-year employment contract with American Express that =
made
Safra an American Express Vice Chairman and a member of the company's Board=
 of
Directors. Safra obligated himself also not to compete with American Expres=
s in
banking, insurance, and brokerage services until March 1, 1988.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Safra,
however, soon became disillusioned with James D. Robinson, III, American
Express's Chairman of the Board and CEO. Frustrated by Robinson's &quot;gen=
eral
disregard of his input and counsel,&quot; Safra in 1984 negotiated a severa=
nce
agreement with American Express. As part of this agreement, Safra returned =
to
Geneva, reaffirmed his prior non-compete agreement, &quot;and agreed to
additional non-competition covenants also to expire on March 1, 1988.&quot;=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
leaving American Express, Safra began plans to open a new Swiss bank -- Saf=
ra
Republic Holdings S.A. -- when the non-compete agreements expired. (Safra h=
ad
earlier founded Republic National Bank, an institution with a
&quot;worldwide&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; reputation for quality and excellence.&quo=
t;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to the complaint:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
prospects of a new Safra-controlled bank based in Switzerland alarmed Ameri=
can
Express executives, particularly Robinson and [Robert F.] Smith [then chair=
man
of American Express Bank]. In their view, a competing Swiss bank, especially
one operated and controlled by Safra, could cause injury to the already
floundering American Express Bank. Fearing their inability to compete
legitimately with Safra and Republic, Robinson, [Harry L.] Freeman [then
Executive Vice President for Corporate Affairs and Communications
&nbsp;[*397]&nbsp; of American Express, and Robinson's &quot;right-hand
man&quot;], and Smith sought to protect themselves from further public
embarrassment through a calculated plan of disinformation and deception.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Robinson
and Smith initiated a campaign to sabotage Safra's application for a Swiss
banking license. In furtherance of this campaign, Robinson and Smith direct=
ed
American Express employees to investigate, among other things, whether Safra
had been misappropriating American Express employees who had previously wor=
ked
for Safra at TDB and whether he had violated United States tax laws. Americ=
an
Express also formally &nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; opposed Safra's application for the
bank license on the grounds that Safra had violated his non-compete agreeme=
nt.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Meanwhile,
again according to the complaint, &quot;an illegal and criminal scheme was
being devised by the highest ranking senior executives of American Express =
to
defame and discredit Safra and Republic National Bank through the public
dissemination of false, scandalous and malicious rumor and innuendo.&quot;
After reading an erroneous report in the New York Times that a Safra jet had
been used to carry U.S. officials to Iran to negotiate the release of Ameri=
can
hostages, Freeman, who &quot;perceived Safra as being bent on 'destroying
TDB,'&quot; began to probe for evidence of Safra's involvement in the
Iran-Contra affair.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Joining
Robinson, Smith, and Freeman in this scheme was Susan Cantor, an American
Express employee and a former producer of television documentaries at ABC N=
ews.
Cantor quickly established contact with a Swiss journalist probing an Ameri=
can
lawyer allegedly involved with the Contras who also had ties to Safra. Cant=
or
also attempted, unsuccessfully, to interest the House Select Committee
investigating Iran-Contra in probing Safra's alleged involvement in the aff=
air.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp;
further unsuccessful efforts to generate any evidence of wrongdoing by Safra
and to plant anti-Safra articles in European periodicals, Cantor was put in
contact with Antonio Giuseppe Greco, an experienced international investiga=
tor
who had previously worked for American Express. After surreptitious meetings
with Cantor in Nice and Barcelona, Greco agreed to assist in the investigat=
ion
of Safra and Republic National Bank for a fee of $ 750 per day, plus expens=
es.
Among Greco's goals was to provide law-enforcement authorities with enough
supposed evidence of Safra's wrongdoing to generate a formal investigation =
of
Safra, the existence of which Greco would then leak to journalists in order=
 to
&quot;discredit Safra in the eyes of his depositors and the public
generally.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
January 26, 1988, Safra's application for a Swiss banking license was grant=
ed.
Shortly thereafter, Greco met Cantor in a New York coffee shop. At the meet=
ing,
Cantor offered Greco $ 500,000 on top of his current salary and expenses to
facilitate the publication of news stories or articles with negative portra=
yals
of Safra and/or Republic National Bank. Cantor also authorized Greco to off=
er
journalists $ 10,000 for each &nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; anti-Safra article publishe=
d.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Greco's
efforts quickly met with success. In February 1988, Greco supplied Jacques
Bertrand of the French newspaper <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>La
Depeche du Midi</i> with the fruits of his investigation of Safra. On March=
 11,
1988, an article bearing Bertrand's by-line and sub-titled &quot;Gangsters =
in
White Collars&quot; appeared in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>La
Depeche du Midi</i>. According to the complaint, &quot;the article purporte=
d to
link Safra to an organization of 'persons in white collars who call on
professional killers for the elimination of nuisances . . . .'&quot;</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Greco
thereafter contacted a Peruvian government official, Victor Tirado, with wh=
om
he was socially acquainted. Through Tirado, Greco managed to plant articles=
 in
both <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hoy</i>, a Peruvian newspaper,=
 and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Uno Mas Uno</i>, a Mexico City newspap=
er,
linking Safra and Republic National Bank to organized crime, money launderi=
ng,
and drug trafficking. Greco paid Tirado $ 18,000 for his efforts in getting=
 the
stories published. Greco also paid another Peruvian government official $
30,000 to bribe other Peruvian journalists to publish anti-Safra articles.
Greco regularly sent Cantor the anti-Safra articles he had managed to get
published, and Cantor in &nbsp;[*398]&nbsp; turn passed&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; the
articles on to Freeman and Robinson. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn=
141'
href=3D"#_ftn141" name=3D"_ftnref141" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
scheme then began to unravel. Greco had caused a French reporter to publish=
 a
series of articles in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Minute</i>, a
French newspaper, during the summer and fall of 1988 accusing Safra and
Republic of participating in various criminal ventures, including
money-laundering for Columbian drug lords. In October 1988, Safra filed the
first of several criminal libel suits against <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Minute</i> in the French courts. According to the complaint, all of
these suits were &quot;successful.&quot; In the course of the litigation, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Minute</i> disclosed many of the docum=
ents
Greco had supplied <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Minute</i>'s rep=
orter.
One of the documents turned over was a copy of a fax bearing&nbsp;[**10]&nb=
sp;
American Express's fax telltale and dated February 25, 1988.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Safra
subsequently confronted Robinson with the evidence of American Express's ro=
le
in planting the series of defamatory articles. After initially denying Amer=
ican
Express's involvement, Robinson capitulated. In July 1989 he issued a public
apology to Safra and caused American Express to pay $ 8 million to charitie=
s of
Safra's choosing. In a press release and a personal letter written to Safra,
Robinson acknowledged the &quot;untrue and defamatory&quot; nature of the
articles published.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Subsequent
to this settlement, American Express's Board of Directors formed a committe=
e of
board members to investigate the wrongdoing. Although the committee hired
prominent attorneys to head its inquiry, the complaint alleges that its
investigation was inadequate. The committee interviewed only Freeman, Robin=
son,
Smith, and Cantor, and conducted no investigation into the bribing of forei=
gn
journalists and officials despite Freeman's suggestion that such bribes had
been paid. In addition, the only interview of Greco conducted by the attorn=
eys
was perfunctory.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Over
the course of the scheme's existence, Greco's aggregate fees (including a $
300,000 &nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; bonus authorized by Freeman and Robinson) totall=
ed
approximately $ 640,000. In addition, Greco was compensated for between $ 1=
.5
and $ 2 million in expenses.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Based
on the foregoing events, the complaint charged that Freeman, Smith, and Can=
tor
(the &quot;RICO defendants&quot;) &quot;have conspired to conduct and have
conducted, directly or indirectly, the affairs of American Express and its
divisions and subsidiaries through a pattern of racketeering activity,
consisting of repeated acts of mail and wire fraud violative of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1343</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and repeated acts of bribery&quot; in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(d)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The complaint's RICO count further alleged that the victims of the defamati=
on
scheme were Safra, Republic National Bank, and American Express. Finally, t=
he
complaint alleged that &quot;as a proximate result of the defendants' illeg=
al
activities violative of RICO, 18 U.S.C. &sect;&sect; 1961-1968, [American
Express] has been injured in its business and property in that it has suffe=
red
the loss of substantial amounts in sales and profits, has been exposed to
potential liability, has incurred&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; damage to its business
reputation and goodwill, and has expended, and will continue to expend, lar=
ge
sums of costs, expenses and legal fees in connection with defendants' unlaw=
ful
acts.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed plaintiffs-appellants' RICO claim for each of two
independently sufficient reasons. First, the district court concluded that
American Express's alleged injuries were not proximately caused by the RICO
defendants' alleged RICO violations. Second, the court held that the compla=
int
failed to adequately allege a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot;
under RICO. Because the district court also dismissed plaintiffs-appellants'
Section 14(a) claim, it concluded that it would be appropriate to decline to
exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the state law claims and therefore
dismissed the complaint in its entirety without leave to replead. This appe=
al
followed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>DISCUSSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Appellants
confine their appeal to the dismissal of their RICO claim. Appellees assert
&nbsp;[*399]&nbsp; that the judgment of the district court should be affirm=
ed
for the reasons relied on by the district court, as well as on five other
grounds. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn142' href=3D"#_ftn142"
name=3D"_ftnref142" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> However, we need consider only
appellees' contention that the injury to American&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; Express=
 was
not proximately caused by the alleged acts of the RICO defendants.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ54B0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_15"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>RICO
grants standing to sue to &quot;any person injured in his business or prope=
rty <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>by reason of</i> a violation of </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this chapter.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c) (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (emphasis added). This language limits standing to
plaintiffs whose injuries were both factually and proximately caused by the
alleged RICO violation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ5490000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes v.
Securities Investor Protection Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 117 L. Ed. =
2d
532, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 1316-18 (1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>;&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp=
; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ54C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht v. Comm=
erce
Clearing House, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d 21=
, 23
(2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;By itself, factual causation (e.g., 'cause-in-fa=
ct'
or 'but for' causation) is not sufficient.&quot;); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ55B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sperber v. Bo=
esky</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 849 F.2d 60=
, 63
(2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (RICO &quot;liability should not extend as far as factu=
al
causation&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ55F0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>Holmes</span></i><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> described <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_15"></a>the common law proximate cause requi=
rement
as often taking the shape of a &quot;direct relation between the injury
asserted and the injurious conduct alleged.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ55D0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 112 S. Ct. =
at
1318</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. The requirement of such a &quot;direct relation&quot;
therefore generally precludes recovery by &quot;a plaintiff who complains of
harm flowing merely from the misfortunes visited upon a third person by the
defendant's acts.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53YR0K1MNJ0090000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53YR0K1MNJ0170000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Holmes essentially endorsed a
definition of proximate cause that we had earlier adopted. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53SX0K1MNJ55G0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d 21,
23-24</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FD53YR0K1MNJ00B0000400"><=
/a><a
