</p>

<p class=3DNormal17><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*634]&nbsp;
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>ROTH</b>, <u>Circuit Judge</u>:</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
interlocutory appeal involves claims brought against GlaxoSmithKline LLC (G=
SK)
by third-party payors (TPPs), based on GSK's alleged misrepresentation and
concealment of the significant safety risks associated with use of Avandia,
Avandamet, and Avandaryl (collectively, Avandia), Type II diabetes drugs. G=
SK
argues that the District Court erred in finding that the TPPs adequately
alleged the elements of standing&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; under the Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO).</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn145' href=3D"#_ftn145" name=3D"_ftnref145" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> We
agree with the District Court's analysis, finding standing, and therefore we
will affirm.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I</span></b><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A.</span></b><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn146' href=3D"#_ftn146" name=3D"_ftnref146" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs,
Allied Services Division Welfare Fund, UFCW Local 1776 and Participating
Employers Health and Welfare Fund, and United Benefit Fund, are TPPs. They =
are
union health and welfare funds and are suing GSK on behalf of themselves and
other similarly situated TPPs. TPPs typically provide medical coverage,
including prescription drug coverage, to their members and members' depende=
nts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Whether
a TPP will cover the cost of a member's prescription, in whole or in part,
depends on whether that drug is listed in the TPP's &quot;formulary.&quot;
Pharmacy Benefit Managers (PBMs) prepare TPPs' formularies of drugs approved
for use by the TPPs' members. The formularies are prepared by analyzing
research regarding a drug's cost effectiveness, safety and efficacy.
&nbsp;[*635]&nbsp; When a PBM determines that&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; a drug offers
advantages over a competing drug, it will give that drug preferred status on
the formulary. A TPP will typically cover more of the cost of a particular =
drug
when that drug has a higher preference status on the formulary. The greater
coverage of cost by the TPP allows the member to pay a lower co-payment when
prescribed that drug.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Type
II diabetes is the most common form of diabetes and results from the body's
failure to produce enough insulin or its inability to properly use insulin.
Type II diabetes was first treated with oral medications, primarily metform=
in
and sulfonylureas, or with injected insulin. In the 1990s, pharmaceutical
companies began to develop a new form of Type II diabetes treatment known as
thiazolidinediones (TZDs). On May 25, 1999, the Food and Drug Administration
(FDA) approved Avandia, a TZD, for sale in the United States. GSK marketed
Avandia as a more effective and safer alternative to the cheaper, existing =
Type
II oral medications. In turn, TPPs included Avandia in their formularies and
covered Avandia prescriptions at a favorable rate.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Soon
after the FDA approved Avandia, concerns regarding its heart-related side
effects began to surface. For example,&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; in 2001, the FDA
requested that GSK add a warning to the prescription label regarding the
increased risk of fluid retention resulting from Avandia use. Shortly
thereafter, GSK's sales representatives denied the existence of this risk. =
As a
result, the FDA instructed GSK to stop minimizing the risk of heart attacks=
 and
heart-related diseases in its marketing. In 2006, the FDA required GSK to
update the warning to include new data about the potential increased occurr=
ence
of heart attack and heart-related chest pain in some Avandia patients.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
May 2007, Steven E. Nissen and Kathy Wolski published a paper in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The New England Journal of Medicine</i=
>,
documenting the results of forty-two clinical trials of Avandia. The Nissen
study concluded that, compared with the use of competing diabetes drugs,
Avandia use was associated with a significant increase in the risk of
myocardial infarction and a borderline-significant increase in the risk of
death from heart-related diseases. According to the TPPs, GSK responded to =
the
Nissen study with a marketing campaign designed to sway doctors and consumer
confidence. This campaign included publishing full-page advertisements in m=
ore
than a dozen newspapers&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; and the release of promotional
materials to prescribing physicians. Specifically, GSK challenged the Nissen
study's methodology and conclusions and described the results of its own
favorable study.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
May 23, 2007, the FDA recommended that GSK add a &quot;black box&quot; warn=
ing
to Avandia's label to warn of the risk of congestive heart failure in
connection with the use of Avandia. On August 14, 2007, GSK added the warni=
ng,
which stated that TZDs &quot;cause or exacerbate congestive heart failure in
some patients. . . . Avandia is not recommended in patients with symptomatic
heart failure.&quot; Three months later, the FDA added a second black box
warning, describing the Nissen study's results as showing &quot;Avandia to =
be associated
with an increased risk of myocardial ischemic events such as angina or
myocardial infarction.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
February 2010, the U.S. Senate Finance Committee released a report on Avand=
ia.
The Committee concluded that the &quot;totality of the evidence suggests th=
at
GSK was aware of the possible cardiac risks associated with Avandia years
before such evidence became public&quot; and that GSK failed to notify the =
FDA
and the &nbsp;[*636]&nbsp; public of these risks despite its duty to do so.=
 The
report also noted that&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; GSK attempted to minimize or
misrepresent those risks in order to contradict the Nissen study and to
intimidate independent physicians.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ultimately,
on September 23, 2010, the FDA restricted access to Avandia in response to
increasing evidence of its cardiovascular risks. Specifically, the FDA limi=
ted
access to existing users and to new patients whose blood sugar could not be
controlled with other medications and who had decided with their doctor not=
 to
take Actos, a competing TZD drug. Doctors were required to advise existing
Avandia users of Avandia's cardiovascular risks before continuing to prescr=
ibe
it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Since
its release, Avandia has been used on a regular basis by at least one milli=
on
individuals in the United States and has generated billions of dollars in
revenue for GSK. A one-month supply of Avandia has sold for $90 to $220, wi=
th
the TPP covering between $135 and $140 per prescription and the patient pay=
ing
the balance. This was a dramatic increase in the cost of Type II diabetes
treatment. Previously, the most prevalent oral drug therapy, metformin, cost
approximately $45 to $55 for a one-month supply, with the TPP covering $40 =
to
$50 of that amount. Although plaintiffs identify&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; Actos as
another alternative to Avandia, they do not provide the price which TPPs
typically covered for Actos prescriptions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B</span></b><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
bring this class action on behalf of themselves and other similarly situated
TPPs that covered the cost of Avandia after May 25, 1999. They assert that
GSK's failure to disclose Avandia's significant heart-related risks violated
RICO based on predicate acts of mail fraud,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn147' href=3D"#_ftn147" name=3D"_ftnref147" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
wire fraud,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn148' href=3D"#_ftn148"
name=3D"_ftnref148" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> tampering with witnesses,</span=
><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn149' href=3D"#_ftn149" name=3D"_ftnref149" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and use of interstate facilities to conduct unlawful activity.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn150' href=3D"#_ftn150" name=3D"_ftnref150" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
They also assert claims for unjust enrichment and violations of the
Pennsylvania Unfair Trade Practices and Consumer Protection Law</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn151' href=3D"#_ftn151" name=3D"_ftnref151" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and other states' consumer protection laws.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
allege that GSK deliberately concealed the significant safety risks associa=
ted
with the use of Avandia and continued to promote Avandia as a safer treatme=
nt
for diabetes despite the known risks of heart attack and disease. Specifica=
lly,
plaintiffs allege that GSK selectively manipulated data and scientific
literature, made false and misleading statements in its 2007 advertising
campaign, and intimidated physicians to publish false and misleading
articles&#8212;all in&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; order to increase Avandia sales.
According to plaintiffs, TPPs and PBMs included Avandia in their formularies
and covered Avandia at favorable rates in reliance on these misrepresentati=
ons
by GSK. Plaintiffs allege that Avandia was worth less than the favorable ra=
tes
at which they covered it (their &quot;excess price&quot; theory). Similarly,
they allege that physicians relied on GSK's misrepresentations in deciding =
to
prescribe Avandia and would have prescribed Avandia to fewer patients had G=
SK
not concealed Avandia's risks (their &quot;quantity effect&quot; theory).
Plaintiffs seek compensatory, punitive, and statutory damages for the finan=
cial
harm they suffered as a result of GSK's conduct, and they seek injunctive
relief to prevent &nbsp;[*637]&nbsp; GSK from continuing its allegedly unla=
wful
activities.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
November 3, 2010, GSK moved to dismiss, in part, because plaintiffs failed =
to
adequately allege standing under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)
of RICO</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The District Court rejected GSK's arguments, holding t=
hat
plaintiffs plausibly alleged that they had suffered a concrete economic inj=
ury
based on the substantial savings they would have experienced had they cover=
ed
cheaper alternatives to Avandia. This was true regardless of whether any
beneficiary who had ingested Avandia&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; became ill.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
District Court also rejected GSK's argument that plaintiffs failed to
adequately allege proximate causation. According to the District Court, it =
is
sufficient that plaintiffs alleged that doctors relied upon GSK's
misrepresentations in prescribing Avandia and that the TPPs themselves reli=
ed
upon those misrepresentations in making formulary decisions. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2D6NW40040000400"></a>The District Court noted, ho=
wever,
that plaintiffs may have difficulty in proving causation at the next litiga=
tion
stage because they did not restrict access to Avandia after the Nissen study
publicized Avandia's heart-related risks. The District Court also rejected
GSK's argument that prescribing doctors' independent actions broke the chai=
n of
causation. The District Court relied on <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>In
re Neurontin Marketing and Sales Practices Litigation</i>,</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn152' href=3D"#_ftn152" name=3D"_ftnref152" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> in
which the First Circuit Court of Appeals held that, where a TPP is a primary
and intended victim and the injury is foreseeable, the doctor's independent
actions do not break the causal chain.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ft=
n153'
href=3D"#_ftn153" name=3D"_ftnref153" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 19, 2014, the District Court certified its decision for interlocut=
ory
appeal under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0FV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1292(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The certified questions are the following:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2HM6P10010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1) Did the Court err in its
application of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maio v. AETNA, Inc.<=
/i></span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn154' href=3D"#_ftn154" name=3D"_ftnref154" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2)
Did the TPPs sufficiently plead that Defendant's alleged misrepresentation
about Avandia's safety caused their injuries, when the TPPs continued to
include Avandia on their formularies and cover the cost of Avandia for their
members after the alleged cardiovascular risks of Avandia were well-publici=
zed,
and</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3)
Does the independent judgment of doctors and decision-making of the physici=
ans
who wrote the prescriptions for Avandia render the causal chain too attenua=
ted
to state a claim?</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn155' href=3D"#_ftn15=
5"
name=3D"_ftnref155" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
granted permission to appeal on April 15, 2014.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II.</span></b><a style=3D'mso-fo=
otnote-id:
ftn156' href=3D"#_ftn156" name=3D"_ftnref156" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2HM6P10030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2HM6P10050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2D6NW50020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2D6NW50040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_16"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
exercise plenary review over a district court's denial of a motion to dismi=
ss
for failure to state a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn157' href=3D"#_ftn157" name=3D"_ftnref157" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;A motion to dismiss pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[*638]&nbsp; may be granted only if, accepting all well pleaded
allegations in the complaint as true, and viewing them in the light most
favorable to plaintiff, plaintiff is not entitled to relief.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn158' href=3D"#_ftn158" name=3D"_ftnref158" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The facts alleged in the complaint must state a &quot;plausible claim for
relief.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn159' href=3D"#_ftn159"
name=3D"_ftnref159" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &quot;The issue is
not whether a plaintiff will ultimately prevail but whether the claimant is
entitled to offer evidence to support the claims.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn160' href=3D"#_ftn160" name=3D"_ftnref160" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_16"></a>We also exercise plenary review over=
 a
district court's legal determination that plaintiffs have standing to pursu=
e a
civil RICO action.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn161' href=3D"#_ftn1=
61"
name=3D"_ftnref161" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
issue on appeal is whether plaintiffs have adequately pled standing to purs=
ue a
civil action under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
RICO. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_16"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Any person injured in his busine=
ss
or property by reason of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this chapter may sue therefor in any appropriate United States district cou=
rt
and shall recover threefold the damages he sustains and the cost of the sui=
t,
including a reasonable attorney's fee . . ..</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn162' href=3D"#_ftn162" name=3D"_ftnref162" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2HM6P20030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G2HM6P20050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc5_14"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
language of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires a RICO plaintiff to&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; show that the plaintiff suff=
ered
an injury to business or property and that the plaintiff's injury was cause=
d by
the defendant's violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn163' href=3D"#_ft=
n163"
name=3D"_ftnref163" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
&quot;limitation of RICO standing to persons 'injured in [their] business or
property' has a 'restrictive significance, which helps to assure that RICO =
is
not expanded to provide a federal cause of action and treble damages to eve=
ry
tort plaintiff.'&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn164' href=3D"#_=
ftn164"
name=3D"_ftnref164" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A</span></b><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G28T4JF0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We must first determine whether
plaintiffs adequately alleged injury to business or property within the mea=
ning
of RICO. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_14"></a>&quot;'[A] showing of in=
jury
requires proof of a concrete&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; financial loss, and not mere
injury to a valuable intangible property interest.'&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn165' href=3D"#_ftn165" name=3D"_ftnref165" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This requirement &quot;can be satisfied by allegations and proof of actual
monetary loss, i.e., an out-of-pocket loss.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn166' href=3D"#_ftn166" name=3D"_ftnref166" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4RWG700001B4800001"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R300010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R300030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R300050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NW60020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NW60040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>GSK claims that the TPPs fail to
assert a concrete injury, citing our decision in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45G28T4JF0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40Y8-1PT0-0038-X15D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Maio</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40Y8-1PT0-0038-X15D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. </span></i>=
</a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
that case, we considered whether health insurance beneficiaries could maint=
ain
a RICO claim for economic injury against their insurer, Aetna, based on all=
eged
misrepresentations regarding the services included in their HMO plans.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn167' href=3D"#_ftn167" name=3D"_ftnref167" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>23</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The insured parties claimed that the insurer's &nbsp;[*639]&nbsp; failure to
disclose restrictive internal policies caused them injury by causing them to
&quot;pa[y] too much in premiums for an 'inferior' health care product.&quo=
t;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn168' href=3D"#_ftn168" name=3D"_ftnref168" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>24</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
They alleged that the internal policies were designed to improve profitabil=
ity
at the expense of quality of care, whereas the insurer's marketing campaign
represented that the purchased policy focused on quality of care.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn169' href=3D"#_ftn169" name=3D"_ftnref169" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>25</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The insured parties also claimed that the internal policies &quot;restrict[=
ed]
the physicians' ability to provide the high quality health care . . .
promised.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn170' href=3D"#_ftn170"
name=3D"_ftnref170" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>26</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R320010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R320030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R320050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6P50020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We rejected the plaintiffs' clai=
ms,
finding that the insured parties suffered no cognizable&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;
injury. We construed the insured parties' property interests as the intangi=
ble
&quot;contractual right to receive benefits in the form of covered medical
services,&quot; and found that the insured parties had suffered no injury
absent allegations that they had received &quot;inadequate, inferior delayed
care, personal injuries resulting therefrom, or [the] denial of benefits due
under the insurance arrangement.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn171'
href=3D"#_ftn171" name=3D"_ftnref171" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>27</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Because the insur=
ed
parties specifically disclaimed any contractual shortcoming on the part of =
the
insurer, they &quot;simply c[ould not] establish as a factual matter that t=
hey
received anything less than what they bargained for.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn172' href=3D"#_ftn172" name=3D"_ftnref172" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>28</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Instead, the alleged economic harm was &quot;contingent upon the impact of
events in the future&quot; &#8212; namely, inadequate care produced by the
insurer's internal policies.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn173'
href=3D"#_ftn173" name=3D"_ftnref173" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>29</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We concluded that
plaintiffs could not establish that they had suffered a tangible economic h=
arm
because their theory of injury was premised solely on the possibility that =
they
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>might</i> receive inadequate health=
care
in the future.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn174' href=3D"#_ftn174"
name=3D"_ftnref174" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>30</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>GSK
argues that here too, the TPPs' injury is predicated on the possibility that
future events might occur &#8212; namely, that the drugs purchased by the T=
PPs
will prove to&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; be unsafe or ineffective. However, because =
the
TPPs do not allege that they received unsafe or ineffective prescriptions, =
GSK
argues that they have received exactly what they bargained for and that they
have not suffered a concrete injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4SBFG00001B4800002"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6P50040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JK0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4SH8S00001B4800003"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JK0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF88X0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The TPPs respond that their inju=
ry
is one which has long been considered concrete: overpayment due to illegal =
or
deceptive marketing practices. They cite our decision in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6P50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F03-7JF0-0038-X19X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Warfarin
Sodium Antitrust Litigation</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn175' href=3D"#_ftn175" name=3D"_ftnref175" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>31</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> in
which TPPs alleged that DuPont violated antitrust law by disseminating false
and misleading information about a cheaper generic drug, causing the TPPs to
cover the cost of duPont's more expensive brand name drug.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn176' href=3D"#_ftn176" name=3D"_ftnref176" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>32</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> We
held that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_14"></a>&quot;TPPs, like indivi=
dual
consumers, suffer[] direct economic harm when, as a result of [a pharmaceut=
ical
company's] alleged misrepresentations, they pa[y] supracompetitive prices f=
or
&nbsp;[*640]&nbsp; [brand drugs] instead of purchasing lower-priced generic
[drugs].&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn177' href=3D"#_ftn177"
name=3D"_ftnref177" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>33</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> According to the
TPPs, if allegedly anticompetitive behavior that leads to overpayment
establishes a concrete injury, then so should allegedly fraudulent behavior
that leads to overpayment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4SNJR00001B4800004"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NW80010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We agree with the TPPs that <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Warfarin</i> offers the closest analog=
y to
the facts of this case and that GSK's reliance on </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF88X0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40Y8-1PT0-0038-X15D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Maio</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
distinguishable in one crucial respect: unlike the injury suffered by
plaintiffs in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maio</i>, the injury
suffered by the TPPs here is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>not</i>
contingent on future events. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NW80030000400">=
</a>The
TPPs' damages do not depend on the effectiveness of the Avandia that they
purchased, but rather on the inflationary effect that GSK's allegedly
fraudulent behavior had on the price of Avandia. By contrast, the damages
suffered by the plaintiffs in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maio<=
/i>
were entirely dependent on the quality of the health care they received. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NW80030000400_2"></a>Because the plaintiffs in =
that
case did not allege that they had received inadequate care, their &quot;the=
ory
of present economic loss require[d] a significant degree of factual
speculation,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn178' href=3D"#_ftn1=
78"
name=3D"_ftnref178" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>34</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and was thus
insufficient to establish standing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
further illustrate the point, suppose that the defendants in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Warfarin</i> had&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; ass=
erted
that the TPPS had failed to establish standing because they had not alleged
that the drugs they had purchased were ineffective. That argument would have
been rejected by the court: the injury suffered by the TPPs in that case did
not depend on the drug's ineffectiveness but rather on the defendant's
anticompetitive behavior. That same logic would apply here. The injury suff=
ered
by the TPPs in this case does not depend on Avandia's ineffectiveness, but
rather on GSK's fraudulent behavior. As such, the TPPs' theory of economic =
loss
does not require factual speculation. If we accept the plausible allegation=
s in
the complaint as true, the fraudulent behavior alleged in their complaint h=
as
already occurred, and its effect on the price of Avandia is not contingent =
on
future events.</span></p>

