Corporation (SIPC) had failed to satisfy the proximate cause requirement.</=
span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn200' href=3D"#_ftn200" name=3D"_ftnref200" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>56</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The SIPC, as a subrogee, alleged that the defendant engaged in a stock
manipulation scheme, which caused two broker-dealers to become insolvent an=
d,
in turn, required that the SIPC reimburse the broker-dealers' customers'
losses.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn201' href=3D"#_ftn201"
name=3D"_ftnref201" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>57</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Supreme Court
held that, even if plaintiffs stood in the shoes of the customers, &quot;the
link is too&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; remote between the stock manipulation alleged=
 and
the customers' harm, being purely contingent on the harm suffered by the br=
oker-dealers.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn202' href=3D"#_ftn202" name=3D"_ftnref202" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>58</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4T8NR00001B4800008"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6P80050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JN0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JN0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R360010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R360030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R360050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8900020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Since <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Holmes</i>, the Court has found <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_=
10"></a>proximate
cause lacking in RICO cases when the conduct directly causing the harm was
distinct from the actions that gave rise to the fraud. In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6P80040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza v. Ideal
Steel Supply Corp.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn203' href=3D"#_ftn203" name=3D"_ftnref203" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>59</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
plaintiff alleged that a competing &nbsp;[*643]&nbsp; business caused it ha=
rm
by defrauding the State tax authority and using the proceeds to offer lower
prices to attract more customers.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn204'
href=3D"#_ftn204" name=3D"_ftnref204" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>60</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Court held th=
at
the cause of plaintiff's harm was &quot;a set of actions (offering lower
prices) entirely distinct from the alleged RICO violation (defrauding the
State.).&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn205' href=3D"#_ftn205"
name=3D"_ftnref205" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>61</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> A plurality of the
justices reached a similar decision in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Hemi
Group, LLC v. City of New York</i>,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn20=
6'
href=3D"#_ftn206" name=3D"_ftnref206" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>62</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> where New York Ci=
ty
alleged that out-of-state cigarette sellers failed to file Jenkins Act repo=
rts
with the State, and asserted injury in the form of lost taxes from City
residents.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn207' href=3D"#_ftn207"
name=3D"_ftnref207" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>63</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The plurality
concluded that causation was even more attenuated than in <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Anza</i> because &quot;the City's theory of liability rest[ed] not =
just
on separate <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>actions</i>, but separa=
te
actions carried out by separate <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>par=
ties</i>.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn208' href=3D"#_ftn208" name=3D"_ftnref208" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>64</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;Put simply,&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; Hemi's obligation was to file the Jenki=
ns
Act reports with the State, not the City, and the City's harm was directly
caused by the customers, not Hemi.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn209'
href=3D"#_ftn209" name=3D"_ftnref209" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>65</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4TDW500001B4800009"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8900040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PB0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PB0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PB0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R370020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In contrast, however, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_8"></a>if there is a sufficiently direct
relationship between the defendant's wrongful conduct and the plaintiffs'
injury, the Court has held that a RICO plaintiff who did not directly rely =
on a
defendant's misrepresentation can still establish proximate causation.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn210' href=3D"#_ftn210" name=3D"_ftnref210" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>66</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8900030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge v. Pho=
enix
Bond &amp; Indemnity Co.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, bidders at a county tax lien
auction alleged that they were directly harmed by other bidders' fraudulent
scheme to win more bids at the auction.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:f=
tn211'
href=3D"#_ftn211" name=3D"_ftnref211" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>67</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The defendants ar=
gued
that the plaintiffs could not establish proximate causation because even th=
ough
the county may have relied on defendants' misrepresentations, plaintiffs did
not.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn212' href=3D"#_ftn212" name=3D"_f=
tnref212"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>68</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Rejecting this argument, the Court held that the
&quot;alleged injury&#8212;the loss of valuable liens&#8212;[was] the direct
result of petitioners' fraud [because] . . . . [i]t was a foreseeable and
natural consequence of petitioners' scheme to obtain more liens for themsel=
ves
that other bidders would obtain fewer liens.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-fo=
otnote-id:
ftn213' href=3D"#_ftn213" name=3D"_ftnref213" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>69</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4TJXT00001B480000B"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4TR0H00001B480000C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4V1G100001B480000D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4V5SM00001B480000F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R370040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Keeping in mind that at the
motion-to-dismiss stage&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; we must accept all plausible
allegations in the complaint as true, we view the case before us as more ak=
in
to </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R370030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
than to </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R370050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span></i><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NWD0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
or </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NWD0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hemi</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes, Anza</i>, and =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hemi</i> was concerned that the conduct
causing plaintiffs' injuries was different than the conduct allegedly
constituting a RICO violation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn214'
href=3D"#_ftn214" name=3D"_ftnref214" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>70</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Each of those cas=
es
featured &nbsp;[*644]&nbsp; plaintiffs alleging harm that was derivative of
harm suffered by a more immediate victim of the RICO activity. Here, GSK
focuses on the presence of intermediaries&#8212;physicians and
patients&#8212;in the causal chain. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JS00300=
00400"></a>But
GSK does not argue that a doctor's decision to prescribe Avandia or a patie=
nt's
decision to take Avandia caused plaintiffs' injuries. The conduct that
allegedly caused plaintiffs' injuries is the same conduct forming the basis=
 of
the RICO scheme alleged in the complaint &#8212; the misrepresentation of t=
he
heart-related risks of taking Avandia that caused TPPs and PBMs to place
Avandia in the formulary. The injury alleged by the TPPs is an economic inj=
ury
independent of any physical injury suffered by Avandia users.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn215' href=3D"#_ftn215" name=3D"_ftnref215" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>71</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
And, as far as we can tell, prescribing physicians did not suffer RICO inju=
ry
from GSK's marketing of Avandia.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nor
should&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; there be difficulty in distinguishing between the
amount of damages attributable to a defendant's violation and to other,
independent factors. The amount of damages is either the difference between
what Avandia coverage cost and the cost of coverage of cheaper, safer drugs
and/or the overvaluation of Avandia caused by GSK's misrepresentations. This
issue of damages, rather than demonstrating a lack of proximate causation,
raises an issue of proof regarding the overall number of prescriptions (und=
er
the &quot;quantity effect&quot; theory) or amount of price inflation (under=
 the
&quot;excess price&quot; theory) attributable to GSK's actions. This is a
question of damages and, more specifically, a question for another day.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>GSK,
however, claims that plaintiffs' theory of causation&#8212;that TPPs relied=
 on
GSK's misrepresentations when including Avandia on formularies&#8212;fails =
as a
matter of law. According to GSK, plaintiffs cannot establish causation beca=
use
they continued to cover Avandia prescriptions after its safety risks were
publicly exposed in May 2007. But this argument is based on two faulty
assumptions. GSK first asks us to assume, in the absence of contrary
allegations, that plaintiffs did not change their&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; coverag=
e of
Avandia in 2007.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn216' href=3D"#_ftn216"
name=3D"_ftnref216" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>72</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> At this stage,
however, we do not know that this is true.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, GSK's argument assumes that plaintiffs knew the full scope of GSK=
's
alleged fraud based on the Nissen study. Other TPPs, however, may have chos=
en
to remove Avandia from their formularies in May 2007 simply out of an abund=
ance
of caution, not due to knowledge of Avandia's full scope of risks. In fact,=
 GSK
responded to the Nissen study with a marketing campaign, which plaintiffs
allege was specifically designed to minimize the report's effects on the
medical community. Furthermore, the FDA merely added black box warnings to
Avandia in 2007 and did not restrict Avandia usage until September 2010, ov=
er
three years after the Nissen study's release. Viewing these facts in the li=
ght
most favorable to plaintiffs, we cannot conclude at this stage that Avandia=
's
cardiovascular risks were fully known in May 2007.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>GSK
further argues that plaintiffs' claim, that doctors relied on GSK's
misrepresentations &nbsp;[*645]&nbsp; when&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; prescribing
Avandia, fails because there are no allegations that alternative prescripti=
ons
would have been cheaper. As a preliminary matter, plaintiffs' injury is not
entirely contingent on the existence of cheaper alternative drugs. Although
these allegations are central to plaintiffs' &quot;quantity effect&quot;
theory, they are less important to an &quot;excess price&quot; theory. Under
that theory, plaintiffs may be able to show that Avandia cost too much
regardless of whether cheaper drugs existed on the market.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
any event, plaintiffs identify metformin as a cheaper alternative drug, whi=
ch
they allege was the most prevalent oral drug therapy for Type II diabetes p=
rior
to Avandia and cost substantially less than Avandia. Despite GSK's contenti=
on,
it was not necessary for plaintiffs to have included a price comparison bet=
ween
Avandia and Actos, another Type II diabetes drug. Although metformin may be=
long
to an older class of drugs, it is not entirely clear when -- or even if --
Actos was a more popular alternative to Avandia than metformin. Again, GSK
seeks a dismissal as a matter of law when there is a factual dispute between
plaintiffs and GSK on the existence of alternative therapies. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_6"></a>It is sufficient&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; t=
hat a
plaintiff identify in the pleadings a specific alternative drug that doctors
would have prescribed and that would have cost less.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4V9V400001B480000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JS0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PC0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PC0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Finally, GSK argues that the
presence of intermediaries, doctors and patients, destroys proximate causat=
ion
because they were the ones who ultimately decided whether to rely on GSK's
misrepresentations. But</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H28T4JS0040000400"=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
precludes that argument. The plaintiffs in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Bridge</i>
were the &quot;primary and intended victims of the scheme to defraud&quot; =
and
their injury was a &quot;foreseeable and natural consequence of [the]
scheme,&quot; regardless of whether they relied on the misrepresentations.<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn217' href=3D"#_ftn217" name=3D"_ftnref217" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>73</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The same is true here. Plaintiffs allege that drug manufacturers are well a=
ware
that TPPs cover the cost of their drugs and describe the alleged RICO schem=
e as
consisting of &quot;deliberately misrepresenting the safety of Avandia so t=
hat
Plaintiff and members of the Class paid for this drug.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn218' href=3D"#_ftn218" name=3D"_ftnref218" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>74</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This fraudulent scheme could have been successful only if plaintiffs paid f=
or
Avandia, and this is the very injury that plaintiffs seek recovery for. We
conclude therefore that plaintiffs' alleged injury is sufficiently direct to
satisfy the RICO proximate cause requirement at this stage.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn219' href=3D"#_ftn219" name=3D"_ftnref219" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>75</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I78P3D4VG2Y00001B480000H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8910010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8910030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2SF8910050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R380020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Nor does this decision conflict =
with
our holding in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2HM6PC0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3W4G-X4J0-0038-X2HF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Steamfitters
Local Union No. 420 Welfare Fund v. Philip Morris, Inc</span></i></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></i><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn220' href=3D"#_ftn220" name=3D"_ftnref220" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>76</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
There, we held that proximate causation was lacking where TPPs sued cigaret=
te
manufacturers based on alleged misrepresentations and sought damages for the
money spent treating beneficiaries' smoking-related health conditions.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn221' href=3D"#_ftn221" name=3D"_ftnref221" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>77</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Analogizing to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, we concl=
uded
that the smokers, like the broker-dealers there, were the &quot;third party
linking the plaintiffs and defendants.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn222' href=3D"#_ftn222" name=3D"_ftnref222" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>78</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
both cases, plaintiffs only &quot;suffered a loss because of the harm that =
the
defendants brought upon th[at] third party.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn223' href=3D"#_ftn223" name=3D"_ftnref223" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>79</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
That is not what happened here. Although GSK identifies &nbsp;[*646]&nbsp;
third parties, doctors and patients, within the causal chain, plaintiffs did
not suffer economic harm because those third parties were injured.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2N1R380040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5HDS45H2D6NWF0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To sum up, this case does not
present any of the three fundamental causation concerns expressed in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>. At least for the purposes =
of
this motion to dismiss, the injury is sufficiently direct. There is no risk=
 of
duplicative recovery here. And, no one is better suited&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; to
sue GSK for its alleged fraud.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn224'
href=3D"#_ftn224" name=3D"_ftnref224" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>80</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> At this stage in =
the
litigation, plaintiffs &quot;need only put forth allegations that raise a
reasonable expectation that discovery will reveal evidence&quot; of proxima=
te
causation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn225' href=3D"#_ftn225"
name=3D"_ftnref225" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>81</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> They have done th=
at
here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV</span></b><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
have plausibly alleged the elements of RICO standing, and GSK has not offer=
ed a
valid justification for limiting the claims at this stage of the litigation.
While many of these issues will resurface in the future, we will not opine =
on
the likelihood of plaintiffs' success down the road. We simply hold that it
would be premature to dismiss plaintiffs' well-pled RICO allegations at this
juncture. Accordingly, we will affirm the judgment of the District Court.</=
span></p>

