Shenker (&quot;Shenker&quot;), James Bell (&quot;Bell&quot;), Jack Friedman
(&quot;Friedman&quot;), Stephen Berman (&quot;Berman&quot;), Joel Bennett
(&quot;Bennett&quot;), and Brian Farrell (&quot;Farrell&quot;). Plaintiff's
original Complaint asserted federal claims arising under the Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(d)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and the Robinson-Patman Act, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-74XJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
13(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. The Complaint also alleged causes of action under New =
York
State law for commercial bribery, fraudulent inducement, unjust enrichment,
breach of fiduciary duty, inducement of breach of fiduciary duty, tortious
interference with contractual relations, and conspiracy to engage in each of
the above acts. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
will be discussed more fully below, on March 31, 2005, Plaintiff filed an
Amended Complaint. The Amended Complaint contains more detailed factual
allegations of wrongdoing by Defendants and also adds a Sherman Act cause of
action. Plaintiff seeks compensatory, &nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; statutory, and puni=
tive
damages, a declaration that the licensing agreements entered into or extend=
ed
as a result of the alleged bribery are void, an accounting of all revenues =
and
profits obtained by Defendants under the licenses, &nbsp;[*488]&nbsp;
disgorgement and/or restitution for any improper revenues and/or profits
obtained under the agreements, disgorgement and/or restitution of any amoun=
ts
allegedly paid to Defendants as bribes, and attorney's fees and costs. </sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>All
Defendants have moved to dismiss the RICO causes of action on the ground th=
at
they fail to adequately plead a RICO enterprise. Certain Defendants have al=
so
moved to dismiss the Robinson-Patman and Sherman Act causes of action again=
st
them. Furthermore, Defendants Shenker and SSAI have moved to dismiss the RI=
CO
causes of action on res judicata and abstention grounds. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn226' href=3D"#_ftn226" name=3D"_ftnref226" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
For the reasons discussed in detail herein, the Motions to Dismiss the RICO
causes of action are denied and the Motions to Dismiss the Robinson-Patman =
and
Sherman Act causes of action are granted. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp;<u>I. Background</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>A. Parties</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
action involves a large number of parties with various roles in the unlawful
scheme alleged by Plaintiff. For clarity, the Court provides a summary of t=
he
Parties and their respective roles in the alleged scheme. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff
WWE is principally engaged in the development, promotion, and marketing of
television and pay-per-view programming and live arena events related to
professional wrestling. (Am. Compl. P9) As part of its business, WWE also
creates characters whose names and likenesses may be licensed to third part=
ies.
(Am. Compl. P9) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Jakks principally sells action figures and toys. (Am. Compl. P10) Defendant
Jack Friedman is the Chief Executive Officer and Chairman of Jakks. (Am. Co=
mpl.
P13) He co-founded Jakks with Defendant Berman. (Am. Compl. P13) Prior to
founding Jakks, Friedman was the CEO of Defendant THQ. (Am. Compl. P14) </s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Jakks
and Friedman also own Jakks H.K. and Road Champs, both Hong Kong Corporatio=
ns.
(Am. Compl. PP11-12) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>During
the times relevant to the Amended Complaint, Defendant Berman was the Execu=
tive
Vice President of Jakks. (Am. Compl. P15) Berman also served at times as
Jakks's President, Secretary, and&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; Chief Operating Officer.=
 He
currently serves on Jakks's Board of Directors. (Am. Compl. P15) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Bennett was Jakks's Chief Financial Officer during the times relevant to the
Complaint. (Am. Compl. P16) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
THQ, Inc. markets and sells video games. (Am. Compl. P17) Defendant Farrell=
 is
the President, Chief Executive Officer, and a member of the Board of Direct=
ors
of THQ. (Am. Compl. P18) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>THQ
and Jakks formed THQ/Jakks Pacific LLC as a joint venture on June 10, 1998.
(Am. Compl. P19) THQ/Jakks was formed in order to be the official licensee =
for
WWE's video game license. (Am. Compl. P19) Defendant Berman was authorized =
to
act on behalf of the joint venture. (Am. Compl. P19) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
SSAI served as WWE's licensing agent from approximately April 1995 through =
June
13, 2000. (Am. &nbsp;[*489]&nbsp; Compl. P20) Defendant Shenker is the sole
owner and President of SSAI. (Am. Compl. P21) He is also the sole owner and
alleged alter ego of a Hong Kong corporation known as Stanfull Industrial, =
Ltd.
(Am. Compl. P21) Shenker allegedly used Stanfull for a variety of criminal =
and
fraudulent enterprises. (Am. Compl. P21) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Bell Licensing is a limited liability company and was allegedly
formed&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; to launder bribes paid to Bell while he was an
executive at WWE. (Am. Compl. P22) Defendant Bell is a former WWE executive=
 and
is the President and sole owner of Bell Licensing. (Am. Compl. P23) On Febr=
uary
10, 2005, Bell pled guilty in the United States District Court for the Dist=
rict
of Connecticut to one count of mail fraud in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1342</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> in connection with his receipt of bribes relating to WW=
E's
licensing program. (Am. Compl. P24) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>B. Facts</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
purposes of this Motion, the Court accepts as true the allegations in the
Amended Complaint, which are: In March 1995, WWE hired Bell to negotiate and
procure licenses for its intellectual property. (Am. Compl. P29) From Octob=
er
1996 to his termination on March 24, 2000, Bell served as WWE's Senior Vice
President of Licensing and Merchandising. (Am. Compl. P29) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
Bell's urging, WWE entered into a nonexclusive agency agreement with SSAI in
April 1995. (Am. Compl. P32) SSAI was to procure and negotiate licensing
contracts on behalf of WWE. (Am. Compl. P32) The agreement between SSAI and=
 WWE
provided that SSAI would receive a commission of eleven percent of royaltie=
s on
licenses negotiated&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; by SSAI. (Am. Compl. P77) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Shortly
after SSAI and WWE entered into the agency agreement in 1995, Friedman
approached Bell and Shenker at the annual New York Toy Fair. (Am. Compl. P3=
5)
Friedman inquired about obtaining a license on behalf of Jakks to make WWE
toys. (Am. Compl. P35) On October 24, 1995, WWE and Jakks entered into a
domestic toy license which was scheduled to terminate on December 31, 1997,
with a one-year right to renew. (Am. Compl. P35) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
around the same time, Shenker and SSAI entered into undisclosed agreements =
with
Jakks to represent Jakks's interests at the same time as Shenker and SSAI w=
ere
serving as WWE's agents. (Am. Compl. PP40-46) At first, Shenker's work for
Jakks was on matters unrelated to WWE. But at the New York Toy Fair in 1996,
Shenker and Jakks discussed hiring Shenker as Jakks's agent on WWE matters =
as
well. (Am. Compl. P49) Jakks's outside legal counsel, however, advised that
such an arrangement would present a conflict of interest for Shenker unless
Shenker disclosed the arrangement to WWE and obtained WWE's full consent. (=
Am.
Compl. P50) Nevertheless, without informing WWE, Shenker and Jakks entered =
into
an arrangement whereby Shenker would&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; secretly serve as Jak=
ks's
agent in negotiations with WWE. (Am. Compl. PP53-56) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 12, 1996, Jakks paid SSAI $ 2,500 in connection with Shenker's wor=
k as
its agent. (Am. Compl. P48) Shortly thereafter, on April 22, 1996, the dome=
stic
toy license between WWE and Jakks was amended to provide additional rights =
to
Jakks. (Am. Compl. P56) According to WWE, in compensation for his services,
Jakks and Shenker agreed that Shenker would accept bribes from Jakks, and s=
plit
them with Bell if necessary, to obtain various toy and video game licenses =
from
WWE. The monies used to pay the bribes would be laundered through foreign s=
hell
corporations. (Am. Compl. P62) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*490]&nbsp;
Pursuant to SSAI's and Shenker's recommendation, on January 21, 1997, WWE
amended the domestic toy license with Jakks a second time, further expanding
Jakks's domestic licensing rights. (Am. Compl. PP64-67) Subsequently, on
February 10, 1997, again on the recommendation of SSAI and Shenker, WWE ent=
ered
into an international toy licensing agreement with Jakks. (Am. Compl. P71)
Shenker's dual role in these negotiations was never disclosed to WWE. On Ma=
rch
17 1997, WWE hired SSAI as its exclusive outside licensing agent. (Am.
&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; Compl. P72) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff
also alleges that Defendants conspired on behalf of Jakks to obtain the vid=
eo
game license from WWE, which had been previously licensed to video game-mak=
er
Acclaim. (Am. Compl. PP100-03) WWE alleges that Shenker wanted Jakks to have
the video game license because SSAI and Shenker would not receive royalties
from the Acclaim license, which had been negotiated before SSAI was hired. =
(Am.
Compl. PP83, 102) In order to procure the video game license, on January 14,
1998, Jakks paid a bribe to Shenker in the form of payment for a false invo=
ice,
(Am. Compl. PP85-86, 95) which Jakks laundered through offshore entities,
including Shenker's alter ego, Stanfull. (Am. Compl. PP86-87, 94-99) Shenker
then paid Bell a portion of the monies received from Jakks in order to secu=
re
his assistance. (Am. Compl. P97) In turn, Bell agreed to prohibit Acclaim f=
rom
submitting a proposal to renew its video game license with WWE and to inste=
ad
have the license awarded to Jakks. (Am. Compl. P104) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>WWE
contends that Bell failed to forward superior offers and proposals from Jak=
ks's
competitors to WWE's management in order to protect the scheme to award the
license to Jakks. (Am. Compl. &nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; P104) Specifically, accord=
ing
to the Amended Complaint, Bell tried to convince WWE management not to renew
the video game license with Acclaim, tried to convince Acclaim not to bid, =
and
submitted a deal memo recommending granting the license to Jakks without
seeking any other bidders. (Am. Compl. PP107-08) Upon Bell's recommendation,
WWE informed Acclaim in April 1998 that it would not be renewing its licens=
e.
(Am. Compl. P115) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the meantime, one of WWE's licensees for action figures, Playmates, had beg=
un
complaining in January 1997 that Jakks had been granted conflicting domestic
rights by WWE. (Am. Compl. P79) At the direction of Shenker and Jakks, and =
in
order to eliminate competition against Jakks, Bell offered to absolve Playm=
ates
of the obligation of its guarantee to WWE if Playmates would give up its ri=
ghts
to produce action figures. (Am. Compl. PP80-83) WWE alleges that this agree=
ment
cost the company its guarantee and royalties above the guarantee. (Am. Comp=
l.
P83) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Jakks
was also facing competition in bidding on the video game license from THQ, =
and
a new bidder, Activision. (Am. Compl. PP127-29) On March 30, 1998, Bell
recommended that WWE accept Jakks's offer for the&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; video g=
ame
license. (Am. Compl. P110) Subsequently, without solicitation from Bell or
Shenker, Activision and THQ emerged as potential bidders in early April 199=
8.
(Am. Compl. PP127-29) In response, Bell and Shenker attempted to convince T=
HQ
and Activision not to bid, going so far as to refuse to provide them with
prospective terms for a bid. (Am. Compl. P129) Nevertheless, THQ and Activi=
sion
submitted informal proposals which were superior to Jakks's previously
submitted proposal. (Am. Compl. P131-32) In order to protect the bribery
scheme, Shenker and/or Bell concealed the informal proposals from WWE, but
advised Jakks of the terms of THQ's and Activision's proposals. (Am. Compl.
P134-35, 145) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*491]&nbsp;
Notwithstanding their efforts to conceal the bribery scheme, Jakks became
concerned that WWE would learn of the superior offers from THQ and Activisi=
on.
(Am. Compl. P133) In order to save the scheme, Jakks approached THQ with an
offer to act as joint venture partners in securing the video game license. =
With
THQ as a partner, Jakks could make a more competitive offer to WWE, while
continuing to make a generous profit on the license. THQ would be required =
to
pay both Jakks and WWE for the license, &nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; while also fundi=
ng
and managing the licensed operations. (Am. Compl. PP137, 178-79, 182-85) </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to WWE, THQ accepted the offer because it was in desperate financial straits
after the cancellation of one of its most lucrative licenses. (Am. Compl.
PP119-25) The Amended Complaint further alleges that THQ knew Jakks had nev=
er
been in the video game business, knew that it was improper for Jakks to dis=
cuss
with THQ the terms by which THQ could gain access to WWE's intellectual
property, and knew that what Jakks was telling THQ was different from what =
Bell
and Shenker had shared earlier. (Am. Compl. PP139-43, 164) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
early May 1998, Bell submitted a deal memo recommending granting the video =
game
license to THQ and Jakks as a joint venture. (Am. Compl. P152) Shortly
thereafter, Activision sent a more formalized version of its earlier inform=
al
proposal, which was also superior to the THQ/Jakks proposal. (Am. Compl. P1=
53)
However, Bell and Shenker did not forward the Activision proposal to WWE
management. (Am. Compl. P153) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Jakks
and THQ agreed to form a joint venture on June 10, 1998. (Am. Compl. P154) =
On
that same day, WWE and THQ/Jakks executed a licensing agreement. (Am. Compl.
&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; P154) The agreement was for ten years with a five-year r=
ight
to renew -- an allegedly long amount of time for such an agreement. (Am. Co=
mpl.
PP148, 157) The terms of the domestic and international toy licenses, which
Jakks already had with WWE, were extended to coincide with the term of the
video game license. (Am. Compl. P165) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>WWE
alleges that the royalties it received under the June 10, 1998 deal were
below-market because Shenker and Bell had foreclosed competitive bidding and
because some of the money that should have been paid to WWE was instead pai=
d by
THQ to Jakks. (Am. Compl. PP150, 160-63) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
June 10, 1998 deal allegedly resulted in profits for Bell and Shenker, as w=
ell
as for Jakks and THQ. In addition to payments from Jakks, Bell and Shenker
received a portion of the revenue stream pursuant to SSAI's agency agreement
with WWE. Shenker split the commission with Bell under their unlawful plan.
(Am. Compl. PP151, 166-70) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
March 24, 2000, WWE terminated Bell's employment for reasons unrelated to t=
he
bribery scheme (which, at the time, WWE did not know about). (Am. Compl. P1=
87)
On June 13, 2000, WWE terminated its contract with SSAI based on a change of
business direction. &nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; (Am. Compl. P189) In October 2000, S=
SAI
brought a breach of contract claim against WWE in Connecticut state court
seeking to be paid commissions on licenses SSAI had allegedly procured for =
WWE
(Am. Compl. P190) WWE cross-claimed against SSAI, sent interrogatories to S=
SAI,
Shenker, Bell, Jakks, and THQ, and deposed Shenker, Friedman, Berman, and
Bennett during the course of discovery in the Connecticut state court actio=
n.
(Am. Compl. PP191-239) WWE alleges that, during the Connecticut litigation,
Defendants destroyed evidence of the bribery, provided perjured testimony, =
made
false statements to auditors, and concealed documents. &nbsp;[*492]&nbsp; (=
Am.
Compl. PP191-239) Eventually, WWE discovered the purportedly unlawful schem=
e,
despite Defendants' alleged attempts to conceal it. (Am. Compl. PP210-12) <=
