<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>II. Discussion</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>A. Standard of Review</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15K0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15N0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_17"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pursuant
to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, a party may seek to dismiss a
complaint for &quot;failure to state a claim upon which relief can be
granted.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15R0000400"></a>In
examining a motion to dismiss, the Court must &quot;accept as true the fact=
ual
allegations made in the complaint and draw all inferences in favor of the
plaintiffs.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15J0000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SYY-7D00-0038-X3F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Grandon v.
Merrill</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SYY-7D00-0038-X3F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SYY-7D00-0038-X3F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lynch &amp; C=
o.,
Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SYY-7D00-0038-X3F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 147 F.3d 184,
188 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. &quot;The court's function on =
a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion is not to weigh the evidence that might be presented at a trial but
merely to determine whether the complaint itself is legally sufficient.&quo=
t; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JH70-0039-P4N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Goldman v.
Belden,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JH70-0039-P4N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 754 F.2d 105=
9,
1067 (2d Cir. 1985)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15R0000400_2"></a>Dismissal is appropriate
&quot;only if there are no legal grounds upon which relief may be
granted.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G2C-3M20-0038-X4WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Virgilio v. C=
ity
of New York,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G2C-3M20-0038-X4WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 407 F.3d 105,
111 (2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>B. RICO Enterprise Pleading Requirement</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants seek dismissal of the
RICO claims in Counts I and II on several&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; grounds. The on=
ly
one fully briefed to this point is Defendants' claim that Plaintiff failed =
to
properly plead a RICO enterprise distinct from the alleged racketeering act=
s. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn229' href=3D"#_ftn229" name=3D"_ftnref229" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
particular, Defendants &nbsp;[*494]&nbsp; contend that while Plaintiff may
allege an enterprise that is an association-in-fact, a properly pled RICO
enterprise must be comprised of more than just the predicate acts of
racketeering. Rather, according to Defendants, a RICO enterprise must be an
&quot;ongoing enterprise&quot; whose members &quot;function as a continuing
unit.&quot; (Jakks Mem. 27-28 (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Turkette,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 452 U.S. 576,
583, 101 S. Ct. 2524, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246 (1981)))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
This requires Plaintiff to allege the existence of an ongoing organization =
that
has an identifiable hierarchy and organization, and involves conduct that
demonstrates that the enterprise's members work together toward a common
purpose. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp;
Plaintiff, on the other hand, argues that an enterprise can merely be the s=
um
of the alleged predicate acts, and that no separate, ascertainable structur=
e is
required. (WWE Mem. in Opp. to Defs.' Mot. to Dismiss 19, 22 (&quot;WWE Res=
p.&quot;))
In its Amended Complaint, Plaintiff contends that there are sufficient
allegations to satisfy this minimal burden. Thus, resolution of the dispute=
 on
the point of law is dispositive, for Plaintiff admits that its Complaint do=
es
not allege a RICO enterprise that has an ascertainable structure beyond the
purported racketeering acts. (Hr'g Tr. 143-44, Jan. 11, 2006 (&quot;Tr.&quo=
t;))
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15W0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_17"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>RICO
defines an &quot;enterprise&quot; to include any &quot;individual, partners=
hip,
corporation, association, or other legal entity, and any union or group of
individuals associated in fact although not a legal entity.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. According to the Supreme Court, a RICO enterprise is
&quot;a group of persons associated together for a common purpose of engagi=
ng
in a course of conduct,&quot; which must be proved &quot;by evidence of an
ongoing organization, formal or informal, and by evidence of the various
associates functioning as a continuing unit.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Turkette,</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 452 U.S. at =
583.</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;
Moreover, a RICO enterprise &quot;is an entity separate and apart from the
pattern of activity in which it engages.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id.</i> Thus, &quot;the existence of an enterprise at all times rem=
ains
a separate element which must be proved.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15Y0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ1630000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;Defendants interpret <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>[Turkette],</i> not without reason, to=
 hold
that the evidence necessary to establish an enterprise must be distinct from
that which a plaintiff must adduce to show a pattern of racketeering
activity.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ15X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-6G20-00B1-F564-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hansel 'N Gre=
tel
Brand, Inc. v. Savitsky,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-6G20-00B1-F564-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 1997 U.S. Di=
st.
LEXIS 13324, No. 94 Civ. 4027, 1997 WL 543088, at *2 (S.D.N.Y. Sept. 3, 199=
7)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>abrogated on other grounds by </i></sp=
