style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 105 U.S. 77,
84-85, 26 L. Ed. 1111 (1882)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Time=
s",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting that courts &quot;should
disregard the punctuation, or repunctuate, if need be, to render the true
meaning of the statute&quot;). More recently, the Supreme Court unanimously
observed that while &quot;the meaning of a statute will typically heed the
commands of its punctuation,&quot; a &quot;purported plain meaning analysis
based only on punctuation is necessarily incomplete and runs the risk of
distorting a statute's true meaning.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RJ6-FD00-003B-R0MS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States
Nat'l Bank of Oregon v. Indep. Ins. Agents of Am., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RJ6-FD00-003B-R0MS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 508 U.S. 439,
454, 113 S. Ct. 2173, 124 L. Ed. 2d 402 (1993)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I05BC9CYTT20000P4G700018"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GF0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GK0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The pertinent part of the statute
proscribes the payment or receipt of payment &quot;of anything of value as a
commission, brokerage, or other compensation, or any allowance or discount =
in
lieu thereof, except for services rendered in connection with the sale or
purchase of goods, wares, or merchandise.&quot; &nbsp;[**62]&nbsp; In <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Freeman,</i> the Seventh Circuit sugge=
sted
that a comma should have been inserted between &quot;rendered&quot; and
&quot;in connection.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GD0000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-TNK0-0039-X1MB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>505 F.2d at
529-30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GH0000400"></a> </sp=
an><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn240' href=3D"#_ftn240" name=3D"_ftnref240" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> By
doing this, the punctuation &nbsp;[*507]&nbsp; would better reflect the
statute's meaning, as well as reflect Congress's intent in enacting the RPA=
. As
noted above, the primary practice that Congress was seeking to eliminate in=
 the
RPA was the use of dummy brokers, i.e., fictitious entities that were recei=
ving
payments for no services rendered. Thus, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-74XJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 2(c)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
should be viewed accordingly, as barring only those payments made where the=
re
is no consideration given for the fee, i.e., no brokerage services rendered=
. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GH0000400_2"></a>Viewed as such, the
&quot;services rendered&quot; exception relates to the brokerage fees, or t=
heir
equivalents, and is not further limited by reference to &quot;the sale or
purchase of goods, wares, or merchandise.&quot; Indeed, if the statute were=
 to
be read as suggested by Plaintiff, by leaving out the comma, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-74XJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 2(c)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;would apparently prohibit the payment of a commission in connection w=
ith
the purchase or sale of intangibles even if 'services' had been rendered.&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-TNK0-0039-X1MB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Freeman,</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-TNK0-0039-X1MB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 505 F.2d at =
530
n.5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>. Plainly, this is not what Congress enacted,
&nbsp;[**63]&nbsp; or intended to enact. Therefore, Plaintiff's concern abo=
ut
grammatical anarchy is unfounded. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I05BC9CYX9J0000P4G700019"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Ultimately, this issue ends wher=
e it
started, with Judge Sprizzo's admonition to a plaintiff who appeared to ign=
ore
the &quot;abundance of authority establishing that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_15"></a>the Robinson-Patman Act applies only=
 to
goods and commodities and does not apply to services.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-98X0-001T-73KH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Empire State
Pharm.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-98X0-001T-73KH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>,</span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-98X0-001T-73KH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 778 F. Supp.=
 at
1258</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. Accordingly, because the alleged bribes here related o=
nly
to licenses, and not to any goods, Defendants' Motion to Dismiss Count VI is
granted. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>D. Duplicative Litigation</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
next question is whether Plaintiff can maintain&nbsp;[**64]&nbsp; an action
against the Shenker Defendants in federal court. The Shenker Defendants arg=
ue
that Plaintiff should be estopped from pursuing a civil RICO action against
them in federal court because it is duplicative of litigation in Connecticut
state court arising out of the same events. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
October 2000, SSAI brought a breach of contract claim against WWE in
Connecticut state court seeking license commissions it was allegedly due. (=
Am.
Compl. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GR0000400"></a>P190) WWE filed
counterclaims against SSAI and discovery ensued. On October 16, 2003, the
Connecticut Superior Court entered an Order dismissing SSAI's claims with
prejudice and granting a default judgment in favor of WWE on its countercla=
ims.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stanley Shenk=
er
and Assocs., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 844 A.2d at =
974,
977</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;Shenker's recantations and his postrecantations
deposition testimony provide uncontroverted and irrefutable evidence of con=
duct
so violative of the judicial process that dismissal with prejudice of the
present case is the only appropriate sanction&#8230;. Based on &#8230; the
record of egregious discovery abuse in the present case, the court hereby
enters a default judgment against [SSAI] and in favor of [WWE] on each of
[WWE's] counterclaims. &nbsp;[**65]&nbsp; &quot;) The Shenker Defendants ar=
gue
that the Connecticut Order constitutes a sufficiently final judgment such t=
hat
Plaintiff should be estopped from pursuing its current RICO claim against t=
hem
in federal court. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Shenker Defendants assert three different grounds for their estoppel claim:=
 (1)
res judicata; (2) the prior pending action doctrine; and (3) the &quot;one
satisfaction&quot; rule. The Court will address each one in turn. </span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
Res Judicata</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2GT0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6_15"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;'Where
there is a final state court judgment, a federal court looks to that state's
