if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bank of
Louisiana,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S6D-D410-003B-R50F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 522 U.S. 470,
476, 118 S. Ct. 921, 139 L. Ed. 2d 912 (1998)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that res judicata prohibits relitigation of issues that either
&quot;were or could have been raised&quot; (quotations omitted)). </span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2HK0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8_15"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to determine if res judicata applies, the Court must compare &quot;the
complaint in this action with the pleadings and the judgment in the earlier
action.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2HJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-YT60-003D-8190-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Joe's Pizza,
Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-YT60-003D-8190-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 675 A.2d at =
447</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Here, the Court has the Amended Complaint, &nbsp;[**67]&nbsp; the October 1=
6,
2003 Order from the Connecticut court, WWE's answer and counterclaim in
Connecticut, and the stipulated pre-judgment remedy in the Connecticut acti=
on.
Therefore, the Court has a sufficient basis for determining the application=
 of
res judicata. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WN0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc9_15"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
the Defendants chose to raise the affirmative defense of res judicata in a =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion, Defendants must accept the more stringent standard of review for a
motion to dismiss. The defense must be supported by facts appearing on the =
face
of the complaint and the motion may be granted only where &quot;it appears
beyond doubt that the plaintiff can prove no set of facts in support of his
claim that would entitle him to relief.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2WN0-008H-V1HJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Citibank, N.A=
. v.
K-H Corp.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2WN0-008H-V1HJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 968 F.2d 148=
9,
1494 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*509]&nbsp;
<u>a. Identity of Parties</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WR0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Here, the first requirement for =
res
judicata is met -- the parties in the state and federal actions are the sam=
e or
in privity. Although Shenker is not presently a party in his personal capac=
ity
in the Connecticut action, he is in privity with SSAI because he is the
President and sole shareholder of SSAI. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-YT60-003D-8190-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Joe's Pizza,
Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX3-YT60-003D-8190-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 675 A.2d at =
445</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(adopting <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 59 of the Restatem=
ent
(Second) of Judgments</i>&nbsp;[**68]&nbsp; in holding that a judgment agai=
nst
the shareholders of a closely held corporation is binding upon the
corporation). SSAI and WWE are parties in both this action and the Connecti=
cut
action. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b.
Identity of Claims</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WT0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As to the second requirement, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_15"></a>Connecticut takes a transactional
approach in determining whether claims are the same. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:488Y-3SR0-0038-X1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>AmBase,</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:488Y-3SR0-0038-X1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 326 F.3d at =
73</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;'The determination, therefore, whether the doctrine shall be invoked=
 is
now based on the underlying transaction and not on substantive legal theori=
es
or types of relief which are sought.'&quot; (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-9130-003C-K4J4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Maldonado v.
Flynn,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-9130-003C-K4J4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 417 A.2d 378,
381-82 (Del. Ch. 1980)))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Transaction refers to a
&quot;'common nucleus of operative facts.'&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i>(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2WX0000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-8FF0-003C-K1C1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Schnell v. Po=
rta
Sys. Corp.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-8FF0-003C-K1C1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1994 Del. Ch.
LEXIS 47, No. Civ. 12,948, 1994 WL 148276, at *4 (De. Ch. Apr. 12, 1994)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X10000400"></a>&quot;'If the pleadings fram=
ing
the issues in the first action would have permitted the raising of the issue
sought to be raised in the second action, and if the facts were known, or c=
ould
have been known to the plaintiff in the second action at the time of the fi=
rst
action,' then the claims in the second action are precluded<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X10000400_2"></a>. &nbsp;[**69]&nbsp; &quot=
; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>(quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-8440-003C-K4P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ezzes v.
Ackerman,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRT-8440-003C-K4P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 43 Del. Ch. =
420,
234 A.2d 444, 445-46 (De. 1967))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
comparison of the pleadings and the Order in the Connecticut case reveals t=
hat
this action arises out of a common nucleus of operative facts as the
Connecticut action. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X30000400"></a>WWE's
counterclaims in the Connecticut action consist of allegations that Shenker=
 and
Bell received kickbacks from WWE's licensees, including Jakks, in exchange =
for
licenses. (Freeman Decl. Exs. C, E; Gruenglas Decl. Exs. C, E) These same f=
acts
-- that potential licensees, including Jakks, made improper payments to
Shenker, SSAI and/or Bell in order to obtain favorable licensing agreements=
 --
form the core of WWE's RICO allegations in this action. <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8B90-003B-4227-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tafflin v.</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8B90-003B-4227-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8B90-003B-4227-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Levitt,</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8B90-003B-4227-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 493 U.S. 455,
458, 110 S. Ct. 792, 107 L. Ed. 2d 887 (1990)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(holding that state and federal courts have concurrent jurisdiction over ci=
vil
RICO claims). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X50000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Despite Shenker's perjury and
Shenker's and Bell's destruction of evidence, WWE knew about Stanfull and
Jakks's role in the bribery scheme during the pendency of the Connecticut
action and could have added a RICO claim in that action. In the October 16,=
 2003
Order dismissing Shenker's complaint&nbsp;[**70]&nbsp; and entering a defau=
lt
judgment in favor of WWE, the court noted that Shenker had perjured himself
about the existence of Stanfull and about improper payments made by license=
es,
including Jakks. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stanley Shenk=
er
and Assocs., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 844 A.2d at =
970</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
WWE, therefore, knew before the entry of the Order about Stanfull and the
payments by Jakks to Shenker. Indeed, WWE had obtained copies of invoices
revealing the payment scheme between Bell and Shenker as early as September=
 26,
2002. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X70000400"></a>(Freeman Decl. Ex. =
B)
Moreover, in February 2003, WWE alleged a RICO violation against Bell in an
action in Connecticut court. (Gruenglas Decl. Ex. C) WWE filed its motion f=
or
sanctions in the Shenker action, upon which the October 16, 2003 Order was =
based,
on May 23, 2003. (WWE Resp. 7) Thus, even if WWE did not know the full exte=
nt
of the facts supporting a RICO claim, WWE knew enough to plead a RICO viola=
tion
in Connecticut state &nbsp;[*510]&nbsp; court. </span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn242' href=3D"#_ftn242" name=3D"_ftnref242" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S6D-D410-003B-R50F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rivet,</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S6D-D410-003B-R50F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 522 U.S. at =
474</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(res judicata bars relitigation of &quot;issues that were or could have been
raised&quot; in previous action). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**71]&nbsp;
<u>c. Final Judgment on the Merits</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X90000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The third requirement is that the
judgment be both on the merits and final. However, this requirement cannot =
be
met here. Connecticut Justice Roberts explicitly stated in the 2003 Order t=
hat
the court's &quot;findings do not constitute findings regarding the merits =
of
the underlying case.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2X80000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stanley Shenk=
er
and Assocs., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:49XS-XWW0-0039-44H5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 844 A.2d at =
966
n.1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>. The court's basis for dismissing the action and enteri=
ng a
default judgment in favor of WWE was Shenker's &quot;own admissions of
[discovery] misconduct,&quot; not the underlying merits. <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2XC0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2XF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, the October 16, 2003
Order is not a final judgment. In Connecticut, a default judgment ordinarily
&quot;admits the material facts constituting a cause of action.&quot; </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2XB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RX4-16J0-003D-82K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kloter v.</sp=
an></i></a><a
