<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>d.
Full and Fair Opportunity to Litigate</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
to the fourth and final element, WWE did not have an opportunity to fully a=
nd
fairly litigate its RICO claim in the Connecticut action. Even though WWE h=
ad
sufficient facts to plead a RICO violation against the Shenker Defendants in
the Connecticut action, because of Shenker's persistent and pervasive abuse=
 of
the discovery process, WWE never had an opportunity to actually litigate its
counterclaims. The October 16, 2003 Order dismissed SSAI's suit against WWE=
 and
entered a default judgment in favor of WWE as a sanction for Shenker's perj=
ury
and abuse of the discovery process. The Connecticut court did not reach any
conclusions regarding &nbsp;[*511]&nbsp; WWE's allegations against SSAI or
SSAI's defenses to those allegations. As such, WWE's counterclaims were not
litigated in the Connecticut action. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Shenker Defendants cannot meet the third and fourth requirements for res
judicata according to Connecticut law. Therefore, the Shenker Defendants'
Motion to preclude Plaintiff's RICO claim on the ground of res judicata is
denied without prejudice. If circumstances change such that the requirements
for res judicata are met, the Shenker Defendants&nbsp;[**74]&nbsp; may info=
rm
the Court at that time, and, if appropriate, seek leave to refile the Motio=
n. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
Prior Pending Action Doctrine</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
