<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>c.
Avoidance of Piecemeal Litigation</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
next factor invokes the obvious desire to avoid piecemeal litigation. This
factor also weighs against abstention. WWE did not bring a RICO cause of ac=
tion
against the Shenker Defendants in Connecticut state court. For the reasons
discussed above, WWE is not precluded under res judicata from now bringing a
RICO claim against the Shenker Defendants in federal court. However, if the
Court were to abstain as to the Shenker Defendants on the RICO claim, WWE w=
ould
still be required to litigate its RICO action against the remaining Defenda=
nts
now, and then return to federal court after the completion of the state cou=
rt
action, and potentially litigate the same RICO claim against the Shenker
Defendants. Thus, if anything, abstention here would be more likely to resu=
lt
in piecemeal litigation. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ2YH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, because of the
procedural posture of the case, all claims against all parties cannot be
resolved in one forum. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></sp=
an><a
