mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the Sherman Antitrust Act. However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_7"><=
/a>that
provision does not provide for a private right of action. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ3990000400"></a>Instead, a plaintiff alleging
injury from an antitrust violation may seek damages only under <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 4</i> of the Clayton Act, which
authorizes private lawsuits by &quot;any person who shall be injured in his
business or property by reason of anything forbidden in the antitrust
laws.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>15 U.S.C. &sect; 15(a)<=
/i>.
&quot;It is a well-established principle that, while the United States is
authorized to sue anyone violating the federal antitrust laws, a private
plaintiff must demonstrate 'standing.'&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ3350000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H8P-D5Y0-0038-X0H8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Daniel v.</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H8P-D5Y0-0038-X0H8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H8P-D5Y0-0038-X0H8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Am. Bd. of
Emergency Med.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H8P-D5Y0-0038-X0H8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 428 F.3d 408,
436 (2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ3990000400_2"></a>&quot;This standing requir=
ement
originates in the Supreme Court's recognition that, although <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 4</i> of the Clayton Act appear=
s to
confer a broad private right of action&nbsp;[**96]&nbsp; for antitrust dama=
ges,
'Congress did not intend the antitrust laws to provide a remedy in damages =
for
all injuries that might conceivably be traced to an antitrust violation.'&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JT82MC0K1MNJ3370000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H8P-D5Y0-0038-X0H8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
