style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>350-a</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and New York's common law of unfair competition. ICC alleges&nbsp;[**3]&nbs=
p;
that UpCodes falsely asserted that its codes are always up to date, that its
codes integrate all amendments enacted by local jurisdictions, and that it =
is
the sole provider of such integrated amendments. The United States District
Court for the Southern District of New York (Marrero, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>J.</i>) <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>sua sponte</i> and
without notice converted the parties' pre-motion letters into a motion to
dismiss and a response, and then granted that motion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, we conclude that the district court erred by failing to provide ICC
with notice and an opportunity to fully defend the sufficiency of its
complaint. We reach the merits because the pleadings are before us, the par=
ties
have fully briefed all issues raised in the appeal, and we review a grant o=
f a
motion to dismiss <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i>. We
conclude that ICC adequately pled falsity as to UpCodes's assurances of
accuracy, statements regarding integration of all amendments, and assertion=
s of
unique services. We also conclude that ICC sufficiently alleged the materia=
lity
of the challenged statements. We affirm the district court's decision only =
to
the extent that it dismissed claims premised on UpCodes's promises that its
customers would gain a &quot;complete understanding&quot;&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; =
of
relevant code, but we do so on different grounds from those relied upon by =
the
district court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Factual Background</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG728T4N80020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Unless otherwise noted, &quot;[a=
]ll
facts are taken from plaintiff['s] complaint, and because plaintiff[] appea=
l[s]
from an order granting a motion to dismiss, we accept all factual allegatio=
ns
in the complaint as true.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG728T4N80010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5JRX-CDC1-F04K-J002-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Salazar v. Ki=
ng</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5JRX-CDC1-F04K-J002-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 822 F.3d 61=
, 68
n.5 (2d Cir. 2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>ICC
is a nonprofit organization that develops model building codes, fire safety
codes, plumbing codes, and other similar materials. It publishes updated
versions of these codes&#8212;known as &quot;International Codes&quot; or
&quot;I-Codes&quot;&#8212;every three years. Many state and local governmen=
ts
adopt these model codes into their statutes and regulations, establishing t=
hem
as binding law within their jurisdictions. When a local government adopts an
I-Code, it often does not publish the entirety of the code; rather, it codi=
fies
the code by reference and then publishes its own amendments. ICC also publi=
shes
&quot;Custom Codes&quot; that integrate these amendments into the I-Codes, =
to
reflect the governing law in those jurisdictions. Although the public can v=
iew
I-Codes free of charge, ICC covers the cost of developing codes by selling
physical and electronic copies of the I-Codes and&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; Custom C=
odes
and by offering enhanced services that allow customers to access code
commentaries and use tools such as highlighting, bookmarking, and annotatio=
n.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2016, defendants Garrett Reynolds and Scott Reynolds founded UpCodes, a
for-profit company that directly competes with ICC. UpCodes's business model
allegedly relies on &quot;selling and giving away unauthorized copies of the
I-Codes and the Custom Codes.&quot; App'x 27, &para; 28. UpCodes allegedly
charges their subscribers a premium to access versions of ICC's publications
with integrated amendments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
relevant to ICC's claims, UpCodes made three categories of statements on its
website and Twitter account about its products: (1) representations relatin=
g to
the accuracy of the codes available on UpCodes's website, including claims =
that
its codes were &quot;always up to date,&quot; App'x 33-34, &para; 50, and t=
hat
the website &quot;provides a complete understanding of relevant material,&q=
uot;
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i> at 34-35, &para; 52; (2)
statements &nbsp;[*52]&nbsp; relating to UpCodes's publication of codes with
integrated amendments, including claims such as, &quot;UpCodes hosts the
adopted codes as enacted by the state or local jurisdiction,&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i> at 36, &para; 54; and (3) stat=
ements
relating to UpCodes being&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; the sole source of codes with
integrated amendments, including claims that UpCodes is the &quot;only place
where all the codes are kept up-to-date with all the amendments integrated
natively into the code,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</=
i> at
34, &para; 51.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>ICC
alleges that these statements were false and misleading. First, ICC claims =
that
UpCodes's assertions of accuracy were false because its codes contained
significant errors. These alleged errors fall into four categories: (1)
scanning errors; (2) posting non-law, i.e., publishing I-Code material as l=
aw
when it had not been adopted; (3) omitting law, i.e., failing to post I-Code
material that had been adopted as law; and (4) failing to integrate various
state and local amendments. Second, ICC claims that UpCodes's statements th=
at
it hosts codes <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>as enacted</i> by
jurisdictions were false because of the errors relating to amendment
integration. Finally, ICC alleges that UpCodes falsely advertised that it is
the sole provider of integrated amendments when ICC also offers Custom Codes
with integrated amendments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Procedural History</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG728T4N80040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On August 17, 2017, ICC filed a
separate copyright infringement action against UpCodes in the United States
District Court for the Southern District&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; of New York, clai=
ming
that UpCodes infringed ICC's copyrights by posting the I-Codes and integrat=
ed
codes that incorporated the I-Codes by reference. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG728T4N80030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:600N-YVF1-F2F4-G4KT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Int'l Code
Council, Inc. v. UpCodes, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:600N-YVF1-F2F4-G4KT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 17 CIV.
6261, 2020 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 92324, 2020 WL 2750636, at *4 (S.D.N.Y. May 27,
2020)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. After discovery was completed in that action, ICC moved
for summary judgment, and UpCodes filed a cross motion for partial summary
judgment. The district court denied both motions in May 2020.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
June 5, 2020, shortly after the denial of summary judgment in the copyright
action, ICC filed this suit against UpCodes and its founders for false
advertising in violation of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:61XJ-43W3-GXJ9-33DV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lanham Act</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7177-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1125(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York Gene=
ral
Business Law &sect;&sect; 349</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HBB-32Y3-S0T7-R4P7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>350-a</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and for unfair competition under New York common law. On June 15, 2020, the
district court consolidated the false advertising action with the copyright
action for pre-trial purposes only.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Prior
to UpCodes's filing of a motion to dismiss and pursuant to Judge Marrero's
published individual practices, the parties exchanged pre-motion
letters&#8212;which were limited to three pages by those
practices&#8212;outlining their arguments for or against dismissal. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG72N1RBX0010000400"></a>Instead of scheduling the
post-letter conference called for by those same individual practices, howev=
er,
on March 1, 2021, the district court <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>sua
sponte</i> and without notice converted the parties' pre-motion
letters&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; into a motion to dismiss and opposition. Based on
those three-page letters alone and without providing the parties an opportu=
nity
to file a motion and response, much less to be heard with full briefing, the
court dismissed ICC's complaint in its entirety. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG728T4N80050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Int'l Code
Council, Inc. v. UpCodes, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 17 CIV.
6261, 2021 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 66802, 2021 WL 1236106 (S.D.N.Y. Mar. 1, 2021)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG72N1RBX0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG72N1RBX0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>According to the district court,
although ICC had adequately pled that UpCodes's representations of accuracy=
 and
completeness were false, those statements were &quot;nonactionable
puffery&quot; because they were &quot;exaggerated&quot; claims &quot;upon w=
hich
no reasonable buyer would be justified in relying.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG72N1RBX0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 66802, [WL] at *6-8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0020000400"></a>The court further conclud=
ed
that &nbsp;[*53]&nbsp; ICC failed to plead falsity as to UpCodes's
representations regarding integrated amendments, determining that those cla=
ims
&quot;are neither literally nor impliedly false&quot; because ICC conceded =
that
UpCodes's website does include &quot;some but not all&quot; integrated
amendments. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG72N1RBX0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 66802, [WL] at *6</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Finally, the court determined =
that
ICC had abandoned its claim that UpCodes made false statements about being =
the
exclusive provider of codes with integrated local amendments. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0020000400_2"></a>In their pre-motion letter=
, the
defendants had argued that ICC did not adequately allege falsity because on=
e of
the statements touting UpCodes&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; as &quot;the only source of
integrated amendments&quot; was limited to &quot;jurisdictions [that] do not
provide integrated code books.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 66802, [WL] at *8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Based on ICC's pre-motion lett=
er,
the court determined that &quot;Plaintiff d[id] not respond to this
argument,&quot; and the court &quot;consider[ed] it conceded.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The court therefore dismissed al=
l of
ICC's false advertising claims under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:61XJ-43W3-GXJ9-33DV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lanham Act</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and New York statutory law and dismissed ICC's New York common law claims f=
or
unfair competition claims because they &quot;mirror the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:61XJ-43W3-GXJ9-33DV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lanham Act</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claims.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:62CN-FF21-F4NT-X0PT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 66802, [WL] at *9</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, ICC argues that the district court erred by relying solely on
pre-motion letters and dismissing its complaint without allowing the partie=
s to
brief the issues in the ordinary course. ICC also argues that the district
court erred by holding that UpCodes's promises of accuracy were nonactionab=
le
puffery and by concluding that it failed to adequately plead the falsity of=
 the
other categories of statements. ICC further asserts that it adequately pled=
 the
materiality of challenged statements. For reasons given below, we agree with
ICC on nearly all counts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Standard of Review</span></b>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82D6N590010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82D6N590030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG828T4NW0020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_18"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review the dismissal of a complaint for failure to state a claim
upon&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; which relief can be granted <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>de novo</i>. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG82SF86T0050000400"></a>Dane v. UnitedHealthcare Ins=
. Co.,
974 F.3d 183, 188 (2d Cir. 2020)</i>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCG828T4NW0040000400"></a>&quot;To survive a motion to
dismiss, a complaint must contain sufficient factual matter, accepted as tr=
ue,
to 'state a claim to relief that is plausible on its face.'&quot; </span><a
