if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2021 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 66802, 2021 WL 1236106, at *8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting App'x 32=
-33,
&para; 47). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCGK28T4SB0020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCGK2D6N800050000400_2"></a>Although &quot;under certa=
in
circumstances, the presence of a disclaimer or similar clarifying language =
may
defeat&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; a claim of deception,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCGK2D6N800040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58BX-H251-F04K-J05F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fink</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:58BX-H251-F04K-J05F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 714 F.3d at=
 742</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the sufficiency of UpCodes's disclaimer depends upon its effect on consumer=
s,
which raises factual questions that are not well suited for resolution on a
motion to dismiss. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g.</i>, <=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCGK28T4SB0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RV6-DWM0-003B-60WR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Consumers Uni=
on
of U.S., Inc. v. New Regina Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RV6-DWM0-003B-60WR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 664 F. Supp.
753, 772 (S.D.N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The adequacy of a discla=
imer
. . . presents an issue of fact.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I22T9681FM100002PD60001R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I662SCGK28T4SB0040000400"></a>UpCodes notes that, in at le=
ast
one case, the Ninth Circuit relied on a disclaimer &nbsp;[*63]&nbsp; at the
motion-to-dismiss stage to find that consumers would not be deceived by a
challenged statement. That case, however, presented unusual circumstances: =
the
disclaimer appeared in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>same sen=
tence</i>
as the allegedly deceptive words and used language that was so clear &quot;=
that
no reasonable addressee could believe&quot; the plaintiffs' allegations of
