<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*768]&nbsp;
PER CURIAM: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
J. Virgil Waggoner and J.V.W. Investment Ltd. (the &quot;Waggoner
Parties&quot;), two of several defendants below, appeal from the dismissal =
of a
declaratory judgment claim brought by plaintiff Correspondent Services
Corporation (&quot;CSC&quot;). Having found no basis&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; for t=
he
exercise of its subject matter jurisdiction over the declaratory judgment
claim, the district court declined to exercise supplemental jurisdiction ov=
er
appellants' state law cross-claims against appellee Suisse Security Bank &a=
mp;
Trust, Ltd. (&quot;SSBT&quot;) and therefore vacated its previously-entered
attachment of SSBT's assets. We affirm the dismissal for lack of subject ma=
tter
jurisdiction. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Background</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PRRH2R5NM001091B7000PN"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ33X0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As we noted in our July 16, 2003
opinion in this case, &quot;while the facts of this case illustrate one of =
the
myriad ways in which complicated investment transactions can turn sour, the
question that confronts us is much less complicated: whether the district c=
ourt
had jurisdiction over this action . . . .&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ33W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4934-2WF0-0038-X1HD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Correspondent
Servs. Corp. v. First Equities Corp. of Florida</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4934-2WF0-0038-X1HD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 338 F.3d 11=
9,
121 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. There, we affirmed the district
court's dismissal of CSC's interpleader action but remanded for the district
court to consider whether it could exercise subject matter jurisdiction over
CSC's declaratory judgment claim in its amended complaint. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
recounted the specifics of the underlying investment scheme in our 2003 opi=
nion
and here provide only those additional&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; facts relevant to t=
he
disposition of the present appeal. Those facts are contained in the followi=
ng
paragraphs of CSC's Amended Complaint: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>P20.
In response to CSC's announced intention to deliver the CD to FECF, Kelleher
wrote CSC on July 21, 1999 demanding that the CD not be transferred and that
CSC remain as custodian of either the CD itself &quot;or it's [sic] cash
equivalent of USD 10.6 million.&quot; Indeed, Kelleher even referred to the=
 CD
as &quot;the valuable CD worth 10,600,000.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>P22.
Kelleher wrote CSC yet again on July 23, 1999, once more referring to the C=
D as
&quot;our asset of USD 10,600,000&quot; and threatening CSC with litigation=
 by
suggesting that CSC must have notified SIPC and its insurer, the Travelers
Casualty and Surety Company of America, &quot;of the probability of a claim=
 if
you transfer our assets as you note . . . .&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>P23.
CSC was confronted with repeated claims by Kelleher that the CD had a value=
 of
$ 10,600,000, that CSC was obligated to retain either the CD or the cash
equivalent in the amount of $ 10,600,000, and that CSC risked being subject=
 to
a claim by Kelleher in that amount if it transferred the CD to anyone other
than him or those designated by him. &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>P37.
There is an actual controversy pending as to the ownership of the CD which =
CSC
has deposited into Court. Among other things, CSC has been threatened with
claims by Kelleher in excess of ten million dollars ($ 10,000,000)
&nbsp;[*769]&nbsp; in the event it transferred the CD to anyone but Kellehe=
r or
his designees.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>P38.
CSC seeks a declaration pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:60C5-33V3-CH1B-T09F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
2201</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> that CSC has no liability to Kelleher and that CSC, hav=
ing
acted in accordance with the terms of the CD and/or having deposited the CD
with the Clerk of the Court, has no further liability to any party in
connection with the CD or its role as custodian of the CD, that it is
discharged from liability, and awarding CSC its costs and attorneys' fees. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Standard of Review</span></b><sp=
an
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ3400000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ34S0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_19"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal from a dismissal for lack of subject matter jurisdiction, we review =
the
district court's legal conclusions de novo, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ33Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DGN-2WG0-0038-X323-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>J.S. ex rel. =
N.S.
v. Attica Cent. Sch.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DGN-2WG0-0038-X323-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 386 F.3d 10=
7,
112 (2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and its factual findings for c=
lear
error. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ3410000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:45W9-DYX0-0038-X34T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Luckett v. Bu=
re</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:45W9-DYX0-0038-X34T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 290 F.3d 49=
3,
496 (2d Cir. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Discussion</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
are asked in this appeal to consider whether CSC's declaratory
judgment&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; claim failed to satisfy the
&quot;amount-in-controversy&quot; requirement such that the district court
properly dismissed the claim for lack of subject matter jurisdiction under =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1332</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. The parties do not dispute that when CSC brought this
action, the CD had no value. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
the district court correctly noted,<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_19"></=
a> the
Declaratory Judgment Act does not by itself confer subject matter jurisdict=
ion
on the federal courts. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ34V0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:60C5-33V3-CH1B-T09F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
2201(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_19"></a>(&quot;In a c=
ase of
actual controversy <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>within its
jurisdiction</i> . . . any court of the United States . . . may declare the
rights and other legal relations of any interested party seeking such
declaration, whether or not further relief is or could be sought.&quot;
(emphasis added)). Rather, there must be an independent basis of jurisdicti=
on
before a district court may issue a declaratory judgment. <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ34T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15P0-006F-M3HW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Niagara Mohawk
Power Corp. v. Tonawanda Band of Seneca Indians</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15P0-006F-M3HW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 94 F.3d 747,
752 (2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The asserted basis for
jurisdiction in this case is </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1332</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, requiring that the parties be diverse and that the amo=
unt
in controversy exceed $ 75,000. Diversity of&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; citizenship is
not disputed here; the jurisdictional dispute relates only to the amount in
controversy. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ34X0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ3770000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc4_19"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;In
actions seeking declaratory or injunctive relief, it is well established th=
at
the amount in controversy is measured by the value of the object of the
litigation.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ34W0000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CY0-003B-S1K0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hunt v.
Washington State Apple Adver. Comm'n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9CY0-003B-S1K0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 432 U.S. 33=
3,
347, 97 S. Ct. 2434, 53 L. Ed. 2d 383 (1977)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We have observed that &quot;the amount in controversy is calculated from the
plaintiff's standpoint; the value of the suit's intended benefit or the val=
ue
of the right being protected or the injury being averted constitutes the am=
ount
in controversy when damages are not requested.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JKPVNT0K1MNJ3760000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6750-0039-X00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kheel v. Port=
 of
New York Auth.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6750-0039-X00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 457 F.2d 46=
, 49
(2d Cir. 1972)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (internal quotations omitted). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Based
on its review of the relevant allegations in the complaint, the district co=
urt
concluded that the object of the litigation in this case was the worthless =
CD
and therefore determined that the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1332</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
jurisdictional amount had not been satisfied. In support of its decision, t=
he
district court cited paragraph 37 of the complaint, which specified that
&quot;there is an actual controversy pending <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>as to the ownership of the CD</i>&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; which CSC has
deposited into Court.&quot; The district court therefore held: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
the CD has been identified by the plaintiff in this action as the object of=
 the
&quot;actual controversy,&quot; its value provides the appropriate basis for
the measure of the amount in controversy . . . . It follows that, since the=
 CD
&quot;had no value when CSC brought suit,&quot; the amount in controversy w=
ith
regard to Count Three falls short of the requisite $ 75,000.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*770]&nbsp;
In reaching this conclusion, the district court considered the Waggoner
Parties' argument that CSC brought the declaratory judgment claim in order =
to
avoid liability to Kelleher in the approximate amount of $ 10 million and t=
hat
this is the appropriate measure of the jurisdictional amount under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1332</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The district court emphasized, however, that &quot;while [the Waggoner Part=
ies
are] correct that the amended complaint reflects a concern with potential
liability, the allegations contained in [the declaratory judgment count] am=
ply
demonstrate that this attention to liability is derived from the 'controver=
sy
pending as to the ownership of the CD.'&quot; Focusing its </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1332</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
analysis on the CD as the object of the litigation, the district court
declined&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; to exercise jurisdiction. We agree with and adopt=
 the
above reasoning of the district court, and therefore affirm its holding that
diversity jurisdiction was lacking over CSC's declaratory judgment claim. <=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal20 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Conclusion</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

