<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Chapter 96 of Title 18 of the United
States Code, entitled Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations (RICO),
was added to Title 18 by the Organized Crime Control Act of 1970.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Title </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1962 (c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, which is part of RICO, makes it
unlawful &quot;for any person employed by or associated with any enterprise
engaged in, or the activities of which affect, interstate or foreign commer=
ce,
to conduct or participate, directly or indirectly, in the conduct of such
enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity or collecti=
on
of unlawful debt.&quot; The term &quot;enterprise&quot; is defined in </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1961 (4)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> as including &quot;any individu=
al,
partnership, corporation, association, &nbsp;[****2]&nbsp; or other legal
entity, and any union or group of individuals associated in fact although n=
ot a
legal entity.&quot; An indictment charged respondent and others with, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inter alia</i>, a conspiracy to violat=
e </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962 (=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The indictment described the enter=
prise
in question as a group of individuals associated in fact for the purpose of
engaging in certain specified criminal activities.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Respondent was convicted in Federal
District Court, but the Court of Appeals reversed on the ground that RICO w=
as
intended solely to protect legitimate business enterprises from infiltratio=
n by
racketeers and does not make it criminal to participate in an association w=
hich
performs only illegal acts and has not infiltrated or attempted to infiltra=
te a
legitimate enterprise.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_11"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><s=
pan
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Held</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>:
The term &quot;enterprise&quot; as used in RICO encompasses both legitimate=
 and
illegitimate enterprises.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Pp.
580-593.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_12"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) Neither the language nor
structure of RICO limits its application to legitimate enterprises.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On its face, the definition of
&quot;enterprise&quot; in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961 (=
4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
appears to include both legitimate and illegitimate enterprises within its
scope.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The section describes =
two
separate categories of associations that come within&nbsp;[****3]&nbsp; the
purview of an &quot;enterprise&quot; -- the first encompassing organizations
such as corporations, partnerships, and other &quot;legal entities,&quot; a=
nd
the second covering &quot;any union or group of individuals associated in f=
act
although not a legal entity.&quot; The second category is not a more
generalized description of the first, and hence the rule of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ejusdem generis</i> cannot be properly
applied to hold that the second category should be limited by the specific
examples enumerated in the first.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Pp. 580-582.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_13"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(b) With respect to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962 (=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
an &quot;enterprise&quot; is not a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&q=
uot;
but is an entity separate and apart from the pattern of activity in which it
engages.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In order to secure a
conviction, the Government must prove both the existence of an
&quot;enterprise&quot; and the connected &quot;pattern of racketeering
activity.&quot; Pp. 582-583.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_14"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(c) Applying RICO to illegitimat=
e as
well as legitimate enterprises does not render any portion of the statute
superfluous nor does it create any structural incongruities within the
statute's framework.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On the c=
ontrary,
insulating the wholly criminal enterprise from prosecution under RICO is the
more incongruous position.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Pp.
583-587.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_15"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(d) Nothing in&nbsp;[****4]&nbsp;
RICO's legislative history requires a conclusion that the statute is limite=
d in
its application to legitimate enterprises.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>In view of the purposes of the Organized Crime Control Act of 1970 to
eradicate organized crime in the United States, it cannot be said that Cong=
ress
nevertheless confined the reach of the law to only narrow aspects of organi=
zed
crime, and, in particular, under RICO, to only the infiltration of legitima=
te business.
Pp. 588-593.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><a
