<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ4SH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_1"></a>[=
1A]Chapter
96 of Title 18 of the United States Code, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-1968 (1976 ed. and Supp. III)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
entitled &nbsp;[*578]&nbsp; Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations
(RICO), was added to Title 18 by Title IX of the Organized Crime Control Ac=
t of
1970, Pub. L. 91-452, 84 Stat. 941. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ4SK00=
00400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The question in this case is whethe=
r the
term &quot;enterprise&quot; as used in RICO encompasses both legitimate and
illegitimate enterprises or is limited in application to the former.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court of Appeals for the First
Circuit held that Congress did not intend to include within the definition =
of
&quot;enterprise&quot; those organizations which are exclusively criminal.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ4SG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>632 F.2d 896
(1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ4SK0000400_2"></a>Th=
is
position is contrary to that adopted by every other Circuit that has addres=
sed
the issue. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn262' href=3D"#_ftn262"
name=3D"_ftnref262" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We granted certiorari to resolve
this conflict.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ4SJ0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>449 U.S. 1123 (1981)</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****6]&nbsp;
I</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[***252]&nbsp;
Count Nine of a nine-count indictment charged respondent and 12 others with
conspiracy to conduct and participate in the affairs of an enterprise </spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn263' href=3D"#_ftn263" name=3D"_ftnref263" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
engaged in interstate commerce &nbsp;[*579]&nbsp; through a pattern of
racketeering activities, in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1962 (d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn264' href=3D"#_ftn264" name=3D"_ftnref264" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The indictment described the enterprise as &quot;a group of individuals
associated in fact for the purpose of illegally trafficking in narcotics and
other dangerous drugs, committing arsons, utilizing the United States mails=
 to
defraud insurance companies, bribing and attempting to bribe local police
officers, and corruptly influencing and attempting to corruptly influence
&nbsp;[**2527]&nbsp; the outcome of state court proceedings . . . .&quot; T=
he
other eight counts of the indictment charged the commission of various subs=
tantive
criminal acts by those engaged in and associated with the criminal enterpri=
se,
including possession with intent to distribute and distribution of controll=
ed
substances, and several counts of insurance fraud by arson and other means.=
<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The common thread to all counts was
respondent's alleged leadership of this criminal organization through which=
 he
orchestrated&nbsp;[****7]&nbsp; and participated in the commission of the
various crimes delineated in the RICO count or charged in the eight precedi=
ng
counts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****8]&nbsp;
After a 6-week jury trial, in which the evidence focused upon both the
professional nature of this organization and the execution of a number of
distinct criminal acts, respondent was convicted on all nine counts.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>He was sentenced to a term of 20 y=
ears
on the substantive counts, as well as a 2-year special parole term on the d=
rug
count.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On the RICO conspiracy=
 count
he was sentenced to a 20-year concurrent term and fined $ 20,000.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, respondent argued that RICO was intended &nbsp;[*580]&nbsp; solely =
to
protect legitimate business enterprises from infiltration by racketeers and
that RICO does not make criminal the participation in an association which
performs only illegal acts and which has not infiltrated or attempted to
infiltrate a legitimate enterprise.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The Court of Appeals agreed.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>We reverse.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ5200000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_2"></a>[=
1B] <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_1"></a>[2] <a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN3_1"></a>[3] <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN4_1"></a>[4]In <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_20">=
</a>determining
the scope of a statute, we look first to its language. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ52S0000400"></a>&nbsp;[****9]&nbsp; If the
statutory language is unambiguous, in the absence of &quot;a clearly expres=
sed
legislative intent to the contrary, that language must ordinarily be regard=
ed
as conclusive.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ51Y0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76J0-003B-S17P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Consumer Prod=
uct
Safety Comm'n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76J0-003B-S17P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76J0-003B-S17P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>GTE Sylvania,=
 Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76J0-003B-S17P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 447 U.S. 1=
02,
108 (1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Of course, there is no errorless test for identifying =
or
&nbsp;[***253]&nbsp; recognizing &quot;plain&quot; or &quot;unambiguous&quo=
t;
language. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ52S0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Also, authoritative administrative
constructions should be given the deference to which they are entitled, abs=
urd
results are to be avoided and internal inconsistencies in the statute must =
be
dealt with.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ52R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SN0-003B-S1RG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Trans Alaska
Pipeline Rate Cases</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SN0-003B-S1RG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 436 U.S. 63=
1,
643 (1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ52T0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GNB0-003B-S1D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Commissioner<=
/span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GNB0-003B-S1D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GNB0-003B-S1D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Brown</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GNB0-003B-S1D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 380 U.S. 56=
3,
571 (1965)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We nevertheless begin with the language of the statute=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962 =
(c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_20"></a>makes it unlawful &quot;for any pers=
on
employed by or associated with any enterprise engaged in, or the activities=
 of
which affect, interstate or foreign commerce, to conduct or participate,
directly&nbsp;[****10]&nbsp; or indirectly, in the conduct of such enterpri=
se's
affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity or collection of unlawful
debt.&quot; The term &quot;enterprise&quot; is defined as including &quot;a=
ny
individual, partnership, corporation, association, or other legal entity, a=
nd
any union or group of individuals associated in fact although not a legal
entity.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961 (=
4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>There is no restriction upon the
associations embraced by the definition: an enterprise includes any union or
group of individuals associated in fact.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span>On its face, the definition appears to include both legitimate and
illegitimate enterprises within its scope; it no more excludes
&nbsp;[*581]&nbsp; criminal enterprises than it does legitimate ones.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Had Congress not intended to reach
criminal associations, it could easily have narrowed the sweep of the
definition by inserting a single word, &quot;legitimate.&quot; But it did
nothing to indicate that an enterprise consisting of a group of individuals=
 was
not covered by RICO if the purpose of the enterprise was exclusively crimin=
al. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN5_1"></a>[=
5]The
Court of Appeals, however, clearly departed from and limited the statutory
language. &nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; It gave several reasons for doing so, none of
which is adequate.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>First, it =
relied
in part on <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_20"></a>the rule of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ejusdem generis</i>, an aid to statuto=
ry
construction problems suggesting that where general words follow a specific
enumeration of persons or things, the general words should be limited to
persons or things similar to those specifically enumerated.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See 2A C. Sands, Sutherland on Sta=
tutory
Construction &sect; 47.17 &nbsp;[**2528]&nbsp; (4th ed. 1973).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court of Appeals ruled that be=
cause
each of the specific enterprises enumerated in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961 (=
4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
a &quot;legitimate&quot; one, the final catchall phrase -- &quot;any union =
or
group of individuals associated in fact&quot; -- should also be limited to
legitimate enterprises. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ53F0000400"></a><=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>There are at least two flaws in this
reasoning.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The rule of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ejusdem generis</i> is no more than an=
 aid
to construction and comes into play only when there is some uncertainty as =
to
the meaning of a particular clause in a statute.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ52W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7740-003B-S1H3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
