<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1961 =
(4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
describes two categories of associations that come &nbsp;[***254]&nbsp; wit=
hin
the purview of the &quot;enterprise&quot; definition.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The first encompasses organization=
s such
as corporations and partnerships, and other &quot;legal entities.&quot; The
second covers &nbsp;[*582]&nbsp; &quot;any union or group of individuals
associated in fact although not a legal entity.&quot; The Court of Appeals
assumed that the second category was merely a more general description of t=
he
first.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Having made that assum=
ption,
the court concluded that the more generalized description in the second
category should be limited by the specific examples enumerated in the
first.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But that assumption is
untenable.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Each category desc=
ribes
a separate type of enterprise to be covered by the statute -- those that are
recognized as legal entities and those that are not.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The latter is not a more general
description of the former.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The
second&nbsp;[****13]&nbsp; category itself not containing any specific
enumeration that is followed by a general description, <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>ejusdem generis</i> has no bearing on the meaning to be attributed =
to
that part of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961 (=
4)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn265' href=3D"#_ftn265" name=3D"_ftnref265" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN6_1"></a>[=
6A] <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN7_1"></a>[7]A second reason offered by the Court of A=
ppeals
in support of its judgment&nbsp;[****14]&nbsp; was that giving the definiti=
on
of &quot;enterprise&quot; its ordinary meaning would create several internal
inconsistencies in the Act.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>W=
ith
respect to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962 (=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it was said:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ54C0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;If 'a pattern of racketeer=
ing'
can itself be an 'enterprise' for purposes of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962 =
(c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
then the two phrases 'employed by or associated with any enterprise' and 't=
he
conduct of such enterprise's affairs through [a pattern of racketeering
activity]' add nothing to the meaning of the section.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The words of the statute are coher=
ent
and logical only if they are read as applying to legitimate enterprises.&qu=
ot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ54B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>632 F.2d, at =
899</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*583]&nbsp;
This conclusion is based on a faulty premise.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That a wholly criminal enterprise =
comes
within the ambit of the statute does not mean that a &quot;pattern of
racketeering activity&quot; is an &quot;enterprise.&quot; In order to secur=
e a
conviction under RICO, the Government must prove both the existence of an
&quot;enterprise&quot; and the connected &quot;pattern of racketeering
activity.&quot; The enterprise is an entity, for present purposes a group of
persons associated together for a common purpose of engaging in a course of
conduct. <span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The pattern of
racketeering activity is, on the other hand, &nbsp;[****15]&nbsp; a series =
of
criminal acts as defined by the statute.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1961 (1) (1976 ed., Supp. III)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The former is proved by evidence o=
f an
ongoing organization, formal or informal, and by evidence that the various
associates function as a continuing unit.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&=
nbsp;
</span>The latter is proved by &nbsp;[**2529]&nbsp; evidence of the requisi=
te
number of acts of racketeering committed by the participants in the
enterprise.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>While the proof u=
sed to
establish these separate elements may in particular cases coalesce, proof of
one does not necessarily establish the &nbsp;[***255]&nbsp; other.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The &quot;enterprise&quot; is not =
the
&quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot;; it is an entity separate and
apart from the pattern of activity in which it engages.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The existence of an enterprise at =
all
times remains a separate element which must be proved by the Government. </=
span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn266' href=3D"#_ftn266" name=3D"_ftnref266" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN6_2"></a>[=
6B]</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****16]&nbsp;
Apart from </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962 (=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
proscription against participating in an enterprise through a pattern of
racketeering activities, RICO also proscribes the investment of income deri=
ved
from racketeering activity in an enterprise engaged in or which
&nbsp;[*584]&nbsp; affects interstate commerce as well as the acquisition o=
f an
interest in or control of any such enterprise through a pattern of racketee=
ring
activity. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1962 (a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(b)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn267' href=3D"#_ftn267" name=3D"_ftnref267" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The Court of Appeals concluded that these provisions of RICO should be
interpreted so as to apply only to legitimate enterprises.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If these two sections are so limit=
ed,
the Court of Appeals held that the proscription in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962 (=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
at issue here, must be similarly limited.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&=
nbsp;
</span>Again, we do not accept the premise from which the Court of Appeals
derived its conclusion.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It is
obvious that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect; =
1962
(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(b)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
address the infiltration by organized crime of legitimate businesses, but we
cannot agree that these sections were not also aimed at preventing racketee=
rs
from investing or reinvesting in wholly illegal enterprises and from acquir=
ing
through a pattern of racketeering activity wholly illegitimate enterprises =
such
as an illegal&nbsp;[****17]&nbsp; gambling business or a loan-sharking
&nbsp;[*585]&nbsp; operation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span=
>There
is no inconsistency or anomaly in recognizing that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies to both legitimate and illegitimate enterprises.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Certainly the language of the stat=
ute
does not warrant the Court of Appeals' conclusion to the contrary.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****18]&nbsp;
Similarly, the Court of Appeals noted that various civil remedies
&nbsp;[***256]&nbsp; were provided by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn268' href=3D"#_ftn268" name=3D"_ftnref268" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****19]&nbsp;
including divestiture, dissolution, &nbsp;[**2530]&nbsp; reorganization,
restrictions on future activities by violators of RICO, and treble
damages.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>These remedies it th=
ought
would have utility only with respect to legitimate enterprises.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As a general proposition, however,=
 the
civil remedies could be useful in eradicating organized crime from the soci=
al
fabric, whether the enterprise be ostensibly legitimate or admittedly
criminal.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The aim is to dives=
t the
association of the fruits of its ill-gotten gains.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:normal'>infra</i>,
at 591-593.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Even if one or mo=
re of
the civil remedies might be inapplicable to a particular illegitimate
enterprise, this fact would not serve to limit the enterprise concept.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Congress has provided civil remedi=
es for
use when the circumstances so warrant.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>It is untenable to argue that their existence limits the scope of the
criminal provisions. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn269' href=3D"#_f=
tn269"
name=3D"_ftnref269" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*586]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN8_1"></a>=
[8]Finally,
it is urged that the interpretation of RICO to include both legitimate and
illegitimate enterprises will substantially alter the balance between feder=
al
and state enforcement of criminal law.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>This is particularly true, so the argument goes, since included with=
in
the definition of racketeering activity are a significant number of acts ma=
de
criminal under state law. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1961 (1) (1976 ed., Supp. III)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But even assuming that the more
inclusive definition of enterprise will have the effect suggested, </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn270' href=3D"#_ftn270" name=3D"_ftnref270" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the language of the statute and its legislative history
indicate&nbsp;[****20]&nbsp; that Congress was well aware that it was enter=
ing
a new domain of federal involvement through the enactment of this measure.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, the very purpose of the
Organized Crime Control Act of 1970 was to enable the Federal Government to
address a large and seemingly neglected problem.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The view was that existing law, st=
ate
and federal, was not adequate &nbsp;[***257]&nbsp; to address the problem,
which was of national dimensions.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>That Congress included within the definition of racketeering activit=
ies
a number of state crimes strongly indicates that RICO criminalized conduct =
that
was also criminal under state law, at least when the requisite elements of a
RICO offense are present.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As =
the
hearings and legislative debates reveal, Congress was well aware of the fear
that RICO would &quot;[move] large substantive areas formerly totally within
the police power of &nbsp;[*587]&nbsp; the State into the Federal realm.&qu=
ot;
116 Cong. Rec. 35217 (1970) (remarks of Rep. Eckhardt).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See also <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>id</i>., at 35205 (remarks of Rep. Mikva); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>id</i>., at 35213 (comments of the American Civil Liberties Union);
Hearings on Organized Crime Control before Subcommittee No. 5 of the House
Committee on the Judiciary, 91st Cong., 2d Sess. &nbsp;[****21]&nbsp; , 329,
370 (1970) (statement of Sheldon H. Eisen on behalf of the Association of t=
he
Bar of the City of New York). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ54F0000400"=
></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>In the face of these objections, Co=
ngress
nonetheless proceeded to enact the measure, knowing that it would alter
somewhat the role of the Federal Government in the war against organized cr=
ime
and that the alteration would entail prosecutions involving acts of
racketeering &nbsp;[**2531]&nbsp; that are also crimes under state law. The=
re
is no argument that Congress acted beyond its power in so doing.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That being the case, the courts are
without authority to restrict the application of the statute.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HRM0K1MNJ54D0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9010-003B-S2W7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9010-003B-S2W7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9010-003B-S2W7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Culbert</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9010-003B-S2W7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 435 U.S. 37=
1,
379-380 (1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****22]&nbsp;
Contrary to the judgment below, neither the language nor structure of RICO
limits its application to legitimate &quot;enterprises.&quot; Applying it a=
lso
to criminal organizations does not render any portion of the statute superf=
luous
nor does it create any structural incongruities within the framework of the
Act.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The result is neither ab=
surd
nor surprising.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On the contra=
ry,
insulating the wholly criminal enterprise from prosecution under RICO is the
more incongruous position. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN9_1"></a>[=
9A]Section
904 (a) of RICO, 84 Stat. 947, directs that &quot;[the] provisions of this
Title shall be liberally construed to effectuate its remedial purposes.&quo=
t;
With or without this admonition, we could not agree with the Court of Appea=
ls
that illegitimate enterprises should be excluded from coverage.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We are also quite sure that nothin=
g in
the legislative history of RICO requires a contrary conclusion. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn271' href=3D"#_ftn271" name=3D"_ftnref271" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN9_2"></a>[=
9B]</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****23]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[*588]&nbsp; III</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
statement of findings that prefaces &nbsp;[***258]&nbsp; the Organized Crime
Control Act of 1970 reveals the pervasiveness of the problem that Congress =
was
addressing by this enactment:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;The
Congress finds that (1) organized crime in the United States is a highly
sophisticated, diversified, and widespread activity that annually drains
billions of dollars from America's economy by unlawful conduct and the ille=
gal
use of force, fraud, and corruption; (2) organized crime derives a major
portion of its power through money obtained from such illegal endeavors as
syndicated gambling, loan sharking, the theft and fencing of property, the
importation and distribution of narcotics and other dangerous drugs, and ot=
her
forms of social exploitation; (3) this money and power are increasingly use=
d to
infiltrate and corrupt legitimate business and labor unions and to subvert =
and
corrupt our democratic processes; (4) organized crime activities in the Uni=
ted
States weaken the stability of the Nation's economic system, harm innocent
investors and competing organizations, interfere with free competition,
seriously burden interstate and foreign commerce, threaten the domestic
security, &nbsp;[****24]&nbsp; and undermine the general welfare of the Nat=
ion
and its citizens; and (5) organized crime continues &nbsp;[*589]&nbsp; to g=
row
because of defects in the evidence-gathering process of the law inhibiting =
the
development of the legally admissible evidence necessary to bring criminal =
and
other sanctions or remedies to bear on the unlawful activities of those eng=
aged
in organized crime and because the sanctions and remedies available to the
Government are unnecessarily limited in scope and impact.&quot; 84 Stat.
922-923.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ0090000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**2532]&nbsp; In light of=
 the
above findings, it was the declared purpose of Congress &quot;to seek the
eradication of organized crime in the United States by strengthening the le=
gal
tools in the evidence-gathering process, by establishing new penal
prohibitions, and by providing enhanced sanctions and new remedies to deal =
with
the unlawful activities of those engaged in organized crime.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ0080000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., at 923</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn272' href=3D"#_ftn272" name=3D"_ftnref272" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The various Titles of the Act provide the tools through which this goal is =
to
be accomplished.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Only three of
those Titles create substantive offenses, Title VIII, which is directed at
illegal gambling operations, Title IX, at issue here, and Title XI, which
addresses the importation, &nbsp;[****25]&nbsp; distribution, and storage of
explosive materials.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The other
Titles provide various procedural and remedial devices to aid in the
prosecution and incarceration of persons involved in organized crime.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00R0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Considering this statement of the
Act's broad purposes, the construction of RICO suggested by respondent and =
the
court below is unacceptable.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
Whole
areas of organized criminal activity would be placed beyond the substantive
reach of the enactment. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00T0000400"></a><=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>For example, associations of persons
engaged solely in &nbsp;[***259]&nbsp; &quot;loan sharking, the theft and
fencing of property, &nbsp;[****26]&nbsp; &nbsp;[*590]&nbsp; the importation
and distribution of narcotics and other dangerous drugs,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-98G0-0039-W1F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., at 922-923=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
would be immune from prosecution under RICO so long as the association did =
not
deviate from the criminal path.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an>Yet
these are among the very crimes that Congress specifically found to be typi=
cal
of the crimes committed by persons involved in organized crime, see </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1961 (1) (1976 ed., Supp. III)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and as a major
source of revenue and power for such organizations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00T0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>See Hearings on S. 30 et al. before=
 the
Subcommittee on Criminal Laws and Procedures of the Senate Committee on the
Judiciary, 91st Cong., 1st Sess., 1-2 (1969). </span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn273' href=3D"#_ftn273" name=3D"_ftnref273" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Along these same lines, Senator McClellan, the principal sponsor of the bil=
l,
gave two examples of types of problems RICO was designed to address.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Neither is consistent with the vie=
w that
substantive offenses under RICO would be limited to legitimate enterprises:
&quot;Organized criminals, too, have flooded the market with cheap
reproductions of hit records and affixed counterfeit popular labels.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>They are heavily engaged in the il=
licit
prescription drug industry.&quot; 116 Cong. Rec. 592 (1970).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In view of the purposes and
goals&nbsp;[****27]&nbsp; of the Act, as well as the language of the statut=
e,
we are unpersuaded that Congress nevertheless confined the reach of the law=
 to
only narrow aspects of organized crime, and, in particular, under RICO, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>only</i> the infiltration of legitimate
business.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****28]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[*591]&nbsp; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ01C0000400"></a>This i=
s not
to gainsay that the legislative history forcefully supports the view that t=
he
major purpose of Title IX is to address the infiltration of legitimate busi=
ness
by organized crime. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00W0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ01C0000400_2"></a>The point is made time and=
 again
during the debates and in the hearings before the House and Senate. </span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn274' href=3D"#_ftn274" name=3D"_ftnref274" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[**2533]&nbsp; But none of these statements requires the negative
inference that Title IX did not reach the activities of enterprises organiz=
ed
and existing for criminal purposes.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ00V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Naftalin</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 441 U.S. 76=
8,
774-775 (1979)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4R64HVD0K1MNJ0180000400"><=
/a><a
