<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*707]&nbsp;&nbsp;[***740]&nbsp;&nbsp;[**1446]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span>JUSTICE <b style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-weight:
normal'>BLACKMUN</b> delivered the opinion of the Court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
August 1983, petitioner Wayne T. Schmuck, a used-car distributor, was indic=
ted
in the United States District Court for the Western District of Wisconsin o=
n 12
counts of mail fraud, in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[****7]&nbsp; and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1342</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>App. 3.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
alleged fraud was a common and straightforward one.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Schmuck purchased used cars, rolle=
d back
their odometers, and then sold the automobiles to Wisconsin retail dealers =
for
prices artificially inflated because of the low-mileage readings.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>These unwitting car dealers, relyi=
ng on
the altered odometer figures, then resold the cars to customers, who in turn
paid prices reflecting Schmuck's fraud.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>To complete the resale of each automobile, the dealer
&nbsp;[***741]&nbsp; who purchased it from Schmuck would submit a title-app=
lication
form to the Wisconsin Department of Transportation on behalf of his retail
customer. The receipt of a Wisconsin title was a prerequisite for completing
the resale; without it, the dealer could not transfer title to the customer=
 and
the customer could not obtain Wisconsin tags.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The submission of the title-applic=
ation
form supplied the mailing element of each of the alleged mail frauds.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Before
trial, Schmuck moved to dismiss the indictment on the ground that the maili=
ngs
at issue -- the submissions of the title-application forms by the automobile
dealers -- were not in furtherance of the fraudulent scheme and, thus, did =
not
&nbsp;[*708]&nbsp; satisfy&nbsp;[****8]&nbsp; the mailing element of the cr=
ime
of mail fraud. Schmuck also moved under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Criminal Procedure 31(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn279' href=3D"#_ftn279" name=3D"_ftnref279" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for a jury instruction on the then misdemeanor offense of tampering with an
odometer, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71Y5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1984</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71YG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1990c(a) (1982
ed.)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn280' href=3D"#_f=
tn280"
name=3D"_ftnref280" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The District Court denied both
motions. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn281' href=3D"#_ftn281"
name=3D"_ftnref281" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> After trial, the jury returned
guilty verdicts on all 12 counts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****9]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ2XV0000400"></a>A divided panel of the Un=
ited
States Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit reversed and remanded the c=
ase
for a new trial.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ2XT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CT90-0039-P2T6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>776 F. 2d 1368
(1985)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ2XX0000400"></a>Alth=
ough
the panel rejected Schmuck's claim that he was entitled to a judgment of
acquittal because the mailings were not made in furtherance of his scheme, =
it
ruled that under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the District Court should have instructed the jury on the lesser offense of
odometer tampering. The panel applied the so-called &quot;inherent
relationship&quot; test for determining what constitutes a lesser included
offense for the purpose of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ2XX0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>See, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:normal'>e.
g., </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ2XW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>v.</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> Whitaker</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 144 U. S. A=
pp.
D. C. 344, 349, 447 F. 2d 314, 319 (1971)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Under that test,=
 one
offense &nbsp;[**1447]&nbsp; is included in another when the facts as alleg=
ed
in the indictment and proved at trial support the inference that
&nbsp;[*709]&nbsp; the defendant committed the less serious offense, and an
&quot;inherent relationship&quot; exists between the two offenses. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ30Y0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>This relationship arises when the t=
wo
offenses relate to the protection of the same interests and the proof of the
greater offense can generally be expected to require proof of the lesser
offense.&nbsp;[****10]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.<=
/i>
Applying this test, the court concluded that both the mail fraud and odomet=
er
tampering statutes protect against fraud, and that the proof of mail fraud
generally entails proving the underlying &nbsp;[***742]&nbsp; fraudulent
conduct. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn282' href=3D"#_ftn282"
name=3D"_ftnref282" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The panel then held that Schmuck
was entitled to the lesser offense instruction because a rational jury could
have found him guilty of odometer tampering, yet acquitted him of mail frau=
d on
the ground that the mailings were too tangential to the fraudulent scheme to
satisfy the requirements of mail fraud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3110000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3130000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court of Appeals vacated the
panel decision and ordered the case to be reheard en banc.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3100000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8XR0-0039-P2S5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>784 F. 2d 846
(1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. On rehearing, by a divided vote, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3120000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FY0-001B-K16S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>840 F. 2d 384
(1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>, the en banc court rejected the &quot;inherent
relationship&quot; &nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; test for defining lesser included
offenses, and adopted instead the &quot;elements test&quot; whereby one off=
ense
is necessarily included within another only when the elements of the lesser
offense form a subset of the elements of the offense charged. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FY0-001B-K16S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FY0-001B-K16S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 387</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court of Appeals found that the
elements test &quot;is grounded in the terms and history of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
comports with the constitutional requirement of notice to defendant of the
potential for conviction of an offense not separately charged, permits a
greater degree of certainty in the application of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and harmonizes the concept of 'necessarily included' under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
with that of a lesser included offense where the issue is double
jeopardy.&quot; </span><a
