of odometer tampering because he could have been convicted &nbsp;[*710]&nbs=
p;
of mail fraud without a showing that he actually altered the odometers, but
could not have been convicted of odometer tampering absent such a showing.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Since the elements of odometer tam=
pering
are not a subset of the elements of mail fraud, odometer tampering did not
qualify&nbsp;[****12]&nbsp; as a lesser included offense of mail fraud and,
accordingly, the District Court was not required under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
instruct the jury on the odometer-tampering offense.</span></p>

