if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>v.</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CFN0-003B-S4ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> Maze</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CFN0-003B-S4ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 414 U.S. 395
(1974)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>, argues that mail fraud can be predicated only on a mai=
ling
that affirmatively assists the perpetrator in carrying out his fraudulent
scheme. The mailing element of the offense, he contends, cannot be satisfie=
d by
a mailing, such as those at issue here, that is routine and innocent in and=
 of
itself, and that, far from furthering the execution of the fraud, occurs af=
ter
the fraud has come to fruition, is merely tangentially related to the fraud,
and is counterproductive in that it creates a &quot;paper trail&quot; from
which the fraud may be discovered.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Brief for Petitioner 20-24.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>We disagree both with this characterization of the mailings in the
present case and with this description of the applicable law. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_2"></a>[=
2B]We
begin by considering the scope&nbsp;[****15]&nbsp; of Schmuck's fraudulent
scheme. Schmuck was charged with devising and executing a scheme to defraud
Wisconsin retail automobile customers who based their decisions to purchase
certain automobiles at least in part on the low-mileage readings provided by
the tampered odometers. This was a fairly large-scale operation.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Evidence at trial indicated that S=
chmuck
had employed a man known only as &quot;Fred&quot; to turn back the odometer=
s on
about 150 different cars.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Tr.=
 102-103.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Schmuck then marketed these cars t=
o a
number of dealers, several of whom he dealt with on a consistent basis over=
 a
period of about 15 years.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JDV0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JDV0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 33-34, 5=
3</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, of the 12 automobiles that=
 are
the subject of the counts of the indictment, 5 were sold to &quot;P and A
Sales,&quot; and 4 to &quot;Southside Auto.&quot; App. 6-7.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Thus, Schmuck's was not a
&quot;one-shot&quot; operation in which he sold a single car to an isolated
dealer. His was an ongoing fraudulent venture.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A rational jury could have conclud=
ed
that the success of Schmuck's venture depended &nbsp;[*712]&nbsp; upon his
continued harmonious relations with, and good reputation among, retail deal=
ers,
which in turn required the smooth flow of cars from the dealers to their
Wisconsin&nbsp;[****16]&nbsp; customers.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
these circumstances, we believe that a rational jury could have found that =
the
title-registration mailings were part of the execution of the fraudulent
scheme, a scheme which did not reach fruition until the retail dealers reso=
ld
the cars and effected transfers of title. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3GF0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Schmuck's scheme would have come to=
 an
abrupt halt if the dealers either had lost faith in Schmuck or had not been
able to resell the cars obtained from him.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>These resales and Schmuck's relationships with the retail
&nbsp;[***744]&nbsp; dealers naturally depended on the successful passage of
title among the various parties.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Thus, although the registration-form mailings may not have contribut=
ed
directly to the duping of either the retail dealers or the customers, they =
were
necessary to the passage of title, which in turn was essential to the
perpetuation of Schmuck's scheme. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3HY0000=
400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>As noted earlier, a mailing that is
&quot;incident to an essential part of the scheme,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3GD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JDV0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pereira</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JDV0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 347 U.S., a=
t 8</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
satisfies the mailing element of the mail fraud offense. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3HY0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The mailings here fit this
description.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e. g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3GG0000400"></a><a
