<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Once
the full flavor of Schmuck's scheme is appreciated, the critical distinctio=
ns
between this case and the three cases in which this Court has delimited the
reach of the mail fraud statute -- <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Kann,
Parr</i>, and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maze</i> -- are readi=
ly
apparent.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The defendants in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kann</i> were corporate officers and
directors &nbsp;[*713]&nbsp; accused of setting up a dummy corporation thro=
ugh
which to divert profits into their own pockets.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As part of this fraudulent scheme,=
 the
defendants caused the corporation to issue two checks payable to them.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The defendants cashed these checks=
 at
local banks, which then mailed the checks to the drawee banks for collectio=
n. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2M56RGWK0S000DP5RP002RS"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3KX0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>This Court held&nbsp;[****18]&nbsp;=
 that
the mailing of the cashed checks to the drawee banks could not supply the
mailing element of the mail fraud charges.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>The defendants' fraudulent scheme had reached fruition. &quot;It was
immaterial to them, or to any consummation of the scheme, how the bank which
paid or credited the check would collect from the drawee bank.&quot; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3KB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2RT0-003B-703J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>323 U.S., at =
94</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3M00000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Parr</i>, several defendants were charged, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>inter alia</i>, with having fraudulently obtained gasoline and a
variety of other products and services through the unauthorized use of a cr=
edit
card issued to the school district which employed them.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The mailing element of the mail fr=
aud
charges in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Parr</i> was purportedly
satisfied when the oil company which issued the credit card mailed invoices=
 to
the school district for payment, and when the district mailed payment in the
form of a check.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Relying on <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kann</i>, this Court held that these
mailings were not in execution of the scheme as required by the statute bec=
ause
it was immaterial to the defendants how the oil company went about collecti=
ng
its payment.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3KY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HPT0-003B-S0CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>363 U.S., at =
393</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn285' href=3D"#_ftn285" name=3D"_ftnref285" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****19]&nbsp;&nbsp;[*714]&nbsp;&nbsp;[***745]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3MR0000400"></a>Later, in <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Maze</i>, the defendant allegedly stole his roommate's credit card,
headed south on a winter jaunt, and obtained food and lodging at motels alo=
ng
the route by placing the charges on the stolen card.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The mailing element of the mail fr=
aud
charge was supplied by the fact that the defendant knew that each motel
proprietor would mail an invoice to the bank that had issued the credit car=
d,
which in turn would mail a bill to the card owner for payment.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court found that these mailings
could not support mail fraud charges because the defendant's scheme had rea=
ched
fruition when he checked out of each motel.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The success of his scheme in no way
depended on the mailings; they merely determined which of his victims would
ultimately bear the loss.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3MP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CFN0-003B-S4ND-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>414 U.S., at =
402</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
title-registration mailings at issue here served a function different from =
the
mailings in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kann, Parr</i>, and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maze</i>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The intrabank mailings in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kann</i> and the credit card invoice
mailings in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Parr</i> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Maze</i> involved little more than
post-fraud accounting among the potential victims of the various schemes, a=
nd
the long-term success of the fraud did not turn on which&nbsp;[****20]&nbsp=
; of
the potential victims bore the ultimate loss.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&nbsp;[**1450]&nbsp; Here, in cont=
rast,
a jury rationally could have found that Schmuck by no means was indifferent=
 to
the fact of who bore the loss.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </spa=
n>The
mailing of the title-registration forms was an essential step in the succes=
sful
passage of title to the retail purchasers. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3NW0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Moreover, a failure of this passage=
 of
title would have jeopardized Schmuck's relationship of trust and goodwill w=
ith
the retail dealers upon whose unwitting cooperation his scheme depended.
Schmuck's reliance on our prior cases limiting the reach of the mail fraud
statute is simply misplaced. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN3_2"></a>[=
3B]To
the extent that Schmuck would draw from these previous cases a general rule
that routine mailings that are innocent &nbsp;[*715]&nbsp; in themselves ca=
nnot
supply the mailing element of the mail fraud offense, he misapprehends this
Court's precedents. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3NY0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Parr</i>
the Court specifically acknowledged that &quot;innocent&quot; mailings -- o=
nes
that contain no false information -- may supply the mailing element.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3MS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HPT0-003B-S0CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>363 U.S., at =
390</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3NY0000400_2"></a>In other cases, the Court =
has
found the elements of mail fraud to be&nbsp;[****21]&nbsp; satisfied where =
the
mailings have been routine.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>S=
ee, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e. g.,</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3NX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GBS0-003B-429M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Carpenter </s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GBS0-003B-429M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>v.</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GBS0-003B-429M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> United State=
s</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GBS0-003B-429M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 484 U.S. 19=
, 28
(1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (mailing newspapers).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
also reject Schmuck's contention that mailings that someday may contribute =
to
the uncovering of a fraudulent scheme cannot supply the mailing element of =
the
mail fraud offense.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The relev=
ant
question at all times is whether the mailing is part of the execution of the
scheme as conceived by the perpetrator at the time, regardless of whether t=
he
mailing later, through hindsight, may prove to have been &nbsp;[***746]&nbs=
p;
counterproductive and return to haunt the perpetrator of the fraud.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The mail fraud statute includes no
guarantee that the use of the mails for the purpose of executing a fraudule=
nt
scheme will be risk free.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Tho=
se who
use the mails to defraud proceed at their peril. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_3"></a>[=
2C]For
these reasons, we agree with the Court of Appeals that the mailings in this
case satisfy the mailing element of the mail fraud offenses.</span></p>

