Criminal Procedure 31(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> provides in relevant part: <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_22"></a>&quot;The defendant may be found gui=
lty of
an offense necessarily included in the offense charged.&quot; As noted abov=
e,
the Courts of Appeals have adopted different tests to determine when, under
this Rule, a defendant is entitled to a lesser included offense instruction.
The Seventh Circuit's original panel opinion applied the &quot;inherent
relationship&quot; approach formulated in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3P00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C7X0-0039-X1CN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
