<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3P10000400_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The en banc Seventh Circuit reje=
cted
this&nbsp;[****23]&nbsp; approach in favor of the &quot;traditional,&quot; =
or
&quot;elements&quot; test.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_22"></a>Under this test, one offense is not
&quot;necessarily included&quot; in another unless the elements of the less=
er
offense are a subset of the elements of the charged offense.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Where the lesser offense requires =
an
element not required for the greater offense, no instruction is to be given
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN4_2"></a>[=
4B] <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN5_1_0"></a>[5A]We now adopt the elements approach to =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2M56RGX84G000DP5RP002VF"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3RD0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>As the Court of Appeals noted, this
approach is grounded in the language and history of the Rule and provides f=
or
greater certainty in its application.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>It, moreover, is consistent with past decisions of this Court which,
though not specifically endorsing a particular test, employed the elements
approach in cases involving &nbsp;[**1451]&nbsp; lesser included offense
instructions. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn286' href=3D"#_ftn286"
name=3D"_ftnref286" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN5_2"></a>[=
5B]</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****24]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN4_3"></a>=
[4C]First,
the wording of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
although not conclusive, supports the application of the elements
approach.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Rule speaks in =
terms
of an offense that is &quot;necessarily included in the offense charged.&qu=
ot;
This language suggests that the comparison to be drawn is between <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>offenses</i>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Since offenses are statutorily def=
ined,
that comparison is appropriately conducted by reference to the statutory
elements of the offenses in question, and not, as the inherent relationship
approach &nbsp;[*717]&nbsp; would mandate, &nbsp;[***747]&nbsp; by referenc=
e to
conduct proved at trial regardless of the statutory definitions.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Furthermore, the language of </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
speaks of the necessary <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inclusion</=
i> of
the lesser offense in the greater.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>While the elements test is true to this requirement, the inherent
relationship approach dispenses with the required relationship of necessary
inclusion: the inherent relationship approach permits a lesser included off=
ense
instruction even if the proof of one offense does not invariably require pr=
oof
of the other as long as the two offenses serve the same legislative goals.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, the inherent&nbsp;[****25]&nbsp; relationship approach, in practi=
ce,
would require that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> be
applied in a manner inconsistent with its language.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Rule provides that a defendant
&quot;may be found guilty&quot; of a lesser included offense, without
distinguishing between a request for jury instructions made by the Governme=
nt
and one made by the defendant.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </spa=
n>In
other words, the language of the Rule suggests that a lesser included offen=
se
instruction is available in equal measure to the defense and to the prosecu=
tion.
</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn287' href=3D"#_ftn287" name=3D"_ftnre=
f287"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>9</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Yet, under the inherent relationship approach, such
mutuality is impossible.</span></p>

