<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****32]&nbsp;
Third, the elements test is far more certain and predictable in its applica=
tion
than the inherent relationship approach.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span>Because the elements approach involves a textual comparison of crimi=
nal
statutes and does not depend on inferences that may be drawn from evidence
introduced at trial, the elements approach permits both sides to know in
advance what jury instructions will be available and to plan their trial
strategies accordingly. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VH0000400"></a><=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The objective elements approach,
moreover, promotes judicial economy by providing a clearer rule
&nbsp;[*721]&nbsp; of decision and by permitting appellate courts to decide
whether jury instructions were wrongly refused without reviewing the entire
evidentiary record for nuances of inference. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN4_7"></a>[=
4G]<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN8_1_0"></a>[8]The inherent relationship approach, in
contrast, is rife with the potential for confusion. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VS0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Finding an inherent relationship be=
tween
offenses requires a determination that the offenses protect the same intere=
sts
and that &quot;in general&quot; proof of the lesser &quot;necessarily&quot;
involves proof of the greater.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </spa=
n>In
the present&nbsp;[****33]&nbsp; case, the Court of Appeals appropriately no=
ted:
&quot;These new layers of analysis add to the uncertainty of the propriety =
of
an instruction in a particular case: not only are there more issues to be
resolved, but correct resolution involves questions of degree and judgment,
with the attendant probability that the trial and appellate courts may
differ.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FY0-001B-K16S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>840 F. 2d, at
389-390</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VS0000400_2"></a>Th=
is
uncertainty was illustrated here.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The three judges of the original appellate panel split in their
application of the inherent relationship test to the offenses of mail fraud=
 and
odometer tampering. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VR0000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CT90-0039-P2T6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>776 F. 2d, at
1373-1375</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (opinion concurring in part and dissenting in part).<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In the context of rules of criminal
procedure, where certainty and predictability are desired, we prefer the
clearer standard for applying </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2731-FG36-13WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 31(c)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN9_1_0"></a=
>[9A]Turning
to the facts of this &nbsp;[***750]&nbsp; case, we agree with the Court of
Appeals that the elements of the offense of odometer tampering are not a su=
bset
of the elements of the crime of mail fraud. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJW6R50K1MNJ3VT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FY0-001B-K16S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>840 F. 2d, at=
 386</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&nbsp;[****34]&nbsp;<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_19"></a>There are two elements in mail fraud=
: (1)
having devised or intending to devise a scheme to defraud (or to perform
specified fraudulent acts), and (2) use of the mail for the purpose of
executing, or attempting to execute, the scheme (or specified fraudulent
acts).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_19"></a>The offense of odometer tampering in=
cludes
the element of knowingly and willfully causing an odometer to be altered. T=
his
element is not a subset of any element of mail fraud. Knowingly and willful=
ly
tampering with an odometer is not identical to devising &nbsp;[*722]&nbsp; =
or
intending to devise a fraudulent scheme. Compare </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71Y5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1984</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71YG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1990c(a)</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>V</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_4"></a>[=
2D] <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN9_2_0"></a>[9B]We conclude that Schmuck's conviction =
was
consistent with the statutory definition of mail fraud and that he was not
entitled to a lesser included offense instruction on odometer tampering. The
judgment of the Court of Appeals, accordingly, is affirmed. </span></p>

