<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
July 24, 1981, SIPC sought a decree from the United States District Court f=
or
the Southern District of Florida to protect the customers of First State
Securities Corporation (FSSC), a broker-dealer and SIPC member. Three days
later, it petitioned the United States District Court for the Central Distr=
ict
of California, seeking to protect the customers of Joseph Sebag, Inc. (Seba=
g),
also a broker-dealer and SIPC member. Each court issued the requested decree
and appointed a trustee, who proceeded to liquidate the broker-dealer.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1315]&nbsp;
Two years later, SIPC and the two trustees brought this suit in the United
States District Court for the Central District of California, accusing some=
 75
defendants&nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; of conspiracy in a fraudulent scheme leading=
 to the
demise of FSSC and Sebag. Insofar as they are relevant here, the allegations
were that, from 1964 &nbsp;[***541]&nbsp; through July 1981, the defendants
manipulated stock of six companies by making unduly optimistic statements a=
bout
their prospects and by continually selling small numbers of shares to create
the appearance of a liquid market; that the broker-dealers bought substanti=
al
amounts of the stock with their own funds; that the market's perception of =
the
fraud in July 1981 sent the stocks plummeting; &nbsp;[*263]&nbsp; and that =
this
decline caused the broker-dealers' financial difficulties resulting in their
eventual liquidation and SIPC's advance of nearly $ 13 million to cover the=
ir
customers' claims. The complaint described Holmes' participation in the sch=
eme
by alleging that he made false statements about the prospects of one of the=
 six
companies, Aero Systems, Inc., of which he was an officer, director, and ma=
jor
shareholder; and that over an extended period he sold small amounts of stoc=
k in
one of the other six companies, the Bunnington Corporation, to simulate a
liquid market. The conspirators were said to have violated &sect; 10(b)
of&nbsp;[****12]&nbsp; the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-700R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect; 78j(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>17 CFR &sect;
240.10b-5 (1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and the mail and wire fraud
statutes, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1343 (1988 ed=
.,
Supp. II)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Finally, the complaint concluded that their acts amoun=
ted
to a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; within the meaning of the
RICO statute, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1961(1)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(5) (1988 ed.=
 and
Supp. II)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, so as to entitle the plaintiffs to recover treble dama=
ges,
</span><a
