k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_3_0"></a=
>[2C]SIPC
also claims a statutory entitlement to pursue Holmes for funds advanced to =
the
trustees for administering the liquidation proceedings. See Tr. of Oral Arg.
30. Its theory here apparently is not one of subrogation, to which the stat=
ute
makes no reference in connection with SIPC's obligation &nbsp;[*275]&nbsp; =
to
make such advances. See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-703K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect; 78fff-3(b)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn312' href=3D"#_ftn312" name=3D"_ftnref312" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
SIPC relies instead, see Brief for Respondent 37, and n. 180, on this SIPA
provision:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;SIPC
participation -- SIPC shall be deemed to be a party in interest as to all
matters arising in a liquidation proceeding, with the right to be heard on =
all
such matters, and shall be deemed to have intervened with respect to all su=
ch
matters with the same force and effect as if a petition for such purpose had
been allowed by the court.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-703F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect; 78eee(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****35]&nbsp;
The language is inapposite to the issue here, however. On its face, it simp=
ly
qualifies SIPC as a proper &nbsp;[***549]&nbsp; party in interest in any
&quot;matter arising in a liquidation proceeding&quot; as to which it
&quot;shall be deemed to have intervened.&quot; By extending a right to be
heard in a &quot;matter&quot; pending between other parties, however, the
statute says nothing about the conditions necessary for SIPC's recovery as a
plaintiff. How the provision could be read, either alone or with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
