<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H50000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Arguably, even if &sect; 10(b)'s
reference to fraud &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in connect=
ion
with</i>&quot; the sale of a security is insufficient to limit the plaintiff
class to purchasers and sellers, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
reference to fraud &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in</i>&quo=
t; the
sale of a security performs just such a narrowing function. But we have
previously had occasion to express reservations on the&nbsp;[****51]&nbsp;
validity of that distinction. In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Naftalin</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 441 U.S. 76=
8,
60 L. Ed. 2d 624, 99 S. Ct. 2077 (1979)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, we reinstated the
conviction of a professional investor who engaged in fraudulent &quot;short
selling&quot; by placing orders with brokers to sell shares of stock which =
he
falsely represented that he owned. This Court agreed with the District Court
that Naftalin was guilty of fraud &quot;in&quot; the &quot;offer&quot; or
&quot;sale&quot; of securities in violation of &sect; 17(a)(1) of the
Securities Act of 1933, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-7543-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U. S. C.
&sect; 77q(a)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, even though the fraud was
perpetrated on the brokers, not their purchasing clients. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H70000400"></a>The Court noted:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H70000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H90000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;[Naftalin] contends that t=
he
requirement that the fraud be 'in' the offer or sale connotes a narrower ra=
nge
of activities than does the phrase 'in connection with,' which is found in
&sect; 10(b) . . . . First, we are not necessarily persuaded that 'in' is
narrower than 'in connection with.' Both Congress, see H. R. Rep. No. 85, 7=
3d
Cong., 1st Sess., 6 (1933), and this Court, see </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Superintenden=
t of
Insurance</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Life
&amp; Cas. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 404 U.S. 6,=
 10,
30 L. Ed. 2d 128, 92 S. Ct. 165 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*285]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DCV0-003B-S0VK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (1971)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
have on&nbsp;[****52]&nbsp; occasion used the terms interchangeably. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H90000400_2"></a>But even if 'in' were mean=
t to
connote a narrower group of transactions &nbsp;[***555]&nbsp; than 'in
connection with,' there is nothing to indicate that 'in' is narrower in the
sense insisted upon by Naftalin.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0H80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8610-003B-S1M8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>441 U.S. at 7=
73,
n. 4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>So
also in today's case. To the extent that there is a meaningful difference
between Congress' choice of &quot;in&quot; as opposed to &quot;in connection
with,&quot; I do not view it as limiting the class of RICO plaintiffs to th=
ose
who were parties to a sale. Rather, consistent with today's decision, I vie=
w it
as confining the class of defendants to those proximately responsible for t=
he
plaintiff's injury and excluding those only tangentially &quot;connected
with&quot; it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HC0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HM0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Blue Chip Stamps</i>, we adopted the purchaser/seller standing
limitation in &sect; 10(b) cases as a prudential means of avoiding the prob=
lems
of proof when no security was traded and the nuisance potential of vexatious
litigation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>421 U.S. at
738-739</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. In that case, however, we were confronted with limiting
access to a private cause of action that was judicially implied.
&nbsp;[**1327]&nbsp; We expressly acknowledged that &quot;if Congress had
legislated the elements&nbsp;[****53]&nbsp; of a private cause of action for
damages, the duty of the Judicial Branch would be to administer the law whi=
ch
Congress enacted; the Judiciary may not circumscribe a right which Congress=
 has
conferred because of any disagreement it might have with Congress about the=
 wisdom
of creating so expansive a liability.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BRX0-003B-S28N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 748</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HP0000400"></a>To be sure, the problems of
expansive standing identified in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bl=
ue
Chip Stamps</i> are exacerbated in RICO. In addition to the threat of treble
damages, a defendant faces the stigma of being labeled a &quot;racketeer.&q=
uot;
Nonetheless, Congress <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>has</i> legis=
lated
the elements of a private cause of action under RICO. Specifically, Congress
has authorized &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> person
injured in his business or property by reason of&quot; a RICO violation to
bring suit under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Despite the very real specter of vexatious litigation based on speculative
damages, it is within Congress' power to create a private right of action
&nbsp;[*286]&nbsp; for plaintiffs who have neither bought nor sold securiti=
es.
For the reasons stated above, I think Congress has done so. &quot;That being
the case, the courts are without authority to restrict the application of t=
he
statute.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Turkette</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. 57=
6,
587, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246, 101 S. Ct. 2524 (1981)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&nbsp;[****54]&nbsp;
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, we granted certiorari to resolve a split among the Circuits as to whet=
her
a nonpurchaser or nonseller of securities could assert RICO claims predicat=
ed
on violations of &sect; 10(b) and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
See cases cited n. 1, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>. I
recognize that, like the case below, some of those decisions might have been
more appropriately cast in terms of proximate causation. That we have now m=
ore
clearly articulated the causation element of a civil RICO action does not
change the fact that the governing precedent in several Circuits is in
disagreement as to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Blue Chip Stamps=
'</i>
applicability in the RICO context. Because that issue was decided below and
fully addressed here, we should resolve it today. I would sustain the Court=
 of
Appeals' determination that RICO plaintiffs alleging predicate acts of frau=
d in
the sale of securities need not be actual purchasers or sellers of the
securities at issue. Accordingly, I join all of the Court's opinion except =
Part
IV.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[***556]&nbsp;
JUSTICE SCALIA, concurring in the judgment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I
agree with JUSTICE O'CONNOR that in deciding this case we ought to reach,
rather than avoid, the question on which we granted certiorari. I also agree
with her on the answer&nbsp;[****55]&nbsp; to that question: that the
purchaser-seller rule does not apply in civil RICO cases alleging as predic=
ate
acts violations of Securities and Exchange Commission </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 10b-5</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:603K-8JY1-DYB7-W40R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>17 CFR &sect;
240.10b-5 (1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. My reasons for that conclusion,
however, are somewhat different from hers.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The ultimate question here is
statutory standing: whether the so-called <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>nexus</i>
(mandatory legalese for &quot;connection&quot;) between the harm of which t=
his
plaintiff complains and the defendant's so-called predicate acts is of the =
sort
that will support &nbsp;[*287]&nbsp; an action under civil RICO. See </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0HR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S. P.=
 R.
L.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Imrex Co.</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
497, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346, 105 S. Ct. 3275 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
One of the usual elements of statutory standing is proximate causality. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0J10000400"></a>It is required in RICO not s=
o much
because RICO has language similar to that of the Clayton Act, which in turn=
 has
language similar to that of the Sherman Act, which, by the time the Clayton=
 Act
had been passed, had been interpreted to include a proximate-cause requirem=
ent;
but rather, I think, because it has always been the practice of common-law
courts (and probably of all courts, under all legal systems) to require as a
condition of recovery, &nbsp;[****56]&nbsp; unless the legislature specific=
ally
prescribes otherwise, that the injury have been proximately caused by the
offending conduct. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0J10000400_2"></a>Life=
 is
too short to pursue every human act to its most remote consequences; &quot;=
for
want of a nail, a kingdom was lost&quot; is a commentary on fate, not the
statement of a major cause of action against a blacksmith. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0J00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated
General Contractors of Cal., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Carpenters</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. 51=
9,
536, 74 L. Ed. 2d 723, 103 S. Ct. 897</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[**1328=
]&nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (1983)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0J30000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Yet another element of statutory
standing is compliance with what I shall call the &quot;zone-of-interests&q=
uot;
test, which seeks to determine whether, apart from the directness of the
injury, the plaintiff is within the class of persons sought to be benefited=
 by
the provision at issue. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn318' href=3D"=
#_ftn318"
name=3D"_ftnref318" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>*</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Judicial inference of a
zone-of-interests requirement, like judicial inference of a proximate-cause
requirement, is a background practice against which Congress legislates. Se=
e </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0J20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3DB0-003B-S3CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Block</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3DB0-003B-S3CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3DB0-003B-S3CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Community
Nutrition Institute</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3DB0-003B-S3CJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 467 U.S. 34=
0,
345-348, 81 L. Ed. 2d 270, 104 S. Ct. 2450 (1984)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Sometimes considerable limitations upon the zone of interests are set forth
explicitly in the statute itself -- but rarely, &nbsp;[****57]&nbsp; if eve=
r,
are those limitations so complete that they are &nbsp;[*288]&nbsp; deemed to
preclude the judicial inference of others. If, for example, a securities fr=
aud
statute specifically conferred a cause of action upon &quot;all purchasers,
sellers, or owners of stock injured by securities fraud,&quot; I doubt whet=
her
a stockholder who suffered a heart attack upon reading a false earnings rep=
ort
could recover his medical expenses. &nbsp;[***557]&nbsp; So also here. The
phrase &quot;any person injured in his business or property by reason of&qu=
ot;
the unlawful activities makes clear that the zone of interests does not ext=
end <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>beyond</i> those injured in that respe=
ct --
but does not necessarily mean that it includes <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>all</i> those injured in that respect. Just as the phrase does not
exclude normal judicial inference of proximate cause, so also it does not
exclude normal judicial inference of zone of interests.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****58]&nbsp;
It seems to me obvious that the proximate-cause test and the zone-of-intere=
sts
test that will be applied to the various causes of action created by </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 1964</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> are not uniform, but vary according to the nature of the
criminal offenses upon which those causes of action are based. The degree of
proximate causality required to recover damages caused by predicate acts of
sports bribery, for example, see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5032-D6RV-H523-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect; 224</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, will be quite different from the degree required for
damages caused by predicate acts of transporting stolen property, see </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 2314-2315</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. And so also with the applicable
zone-of-interests test: It will vary with the underlying violation. (Where =
the
predicate acts consist of different criminal offenses, presumably the plain=
tiff
would have to be within the degree of proximate causality and within the zo=
ne
of interests as to all of them.)</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
also seems to me obvious that unless some reason for making a distinction
exists, the background zone-of-interests test applied to one cause of action
for harm caused by violation of a particular criminal provision should be t=
he
same as the&nbsp;[****59]&nbsp; test applied to another cause of action for
harm caused by violation of the same provision. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I7TG719BJ69000YV2W3000BK"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0JD0000400"></a>It is principally in this re=
spect
that I differ from JUSTICE O'CONNOR's analysis, &nbsp;[*289]&nbsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ante</i>, at 280 (opinion concurring i=
n part
and concurring in judgment). If, for example, one statute gives persons inj=
ured
by a particular criminal violation a cause of action for damages, and anoth=
er
statute gives them a cause of action for equitable relief, the persons comi=
ng
within the zone of interests of those two statutes would be identical. Hence
the relevance to this case of our decision in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFVT6Y0K1MNJ0JC0000400"></a><a
