<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_1_2"></a=
>[1A]This
case requires us once again to interpret the provisions of the Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations &nbsp;[****5]&nbsp; (RICO) chapter of =
the
Organized Crime Control Act of 1970, Pub. L. 91-452, Title IX, 84 Stat. 941=
, as
amended, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-1968 (1988 ed. and Supp. II)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_24"></a>makes it unlawful &quot;for any pers=
on
employed by or associated with any enterprise engaged in, or the activities=
 of
which affect, interstate or foreign commerce, to conduct or participate,
directly or indirectly, in the conduct of such enterprise's affairs through=
 a
pattern of racketeering activity . . . .&quot; The question presented is
whether one must participate in the operation or management of the enterpri=
se
itself to be subject to liability under this provision.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Farmer's Cooperative of Arkansas and Oklahoma, Inc. (Co-Op), began operatin=
g in
western Arkansas and eastern Oklahoma in 1946. To raise money for operating
expenses, the Co-Op sold promissory notes payable to the holder on demand. =
Each
year, Co-Op members were elected to serve on its board. The board met month=
ly
but delegated actual management of the Co-Op to a general manager. In 1952,=
 the
board appointed Jack White as general manager.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
January 1980, White began taking loans from the Co-Op to finance the
&nbsp;[****6]&nbsp; construction of a gasohol plant by his company,
&nbsp;[*173]&nbsp; White &nbsp;[**1167]&nbsp; Flame Fuels, Inc. By the end =
of
1980, White's debts to the Co-Op totaled approximately $ 4 million. In
September of that year, White and Gene Kuykendall, who served as the accoun=
tant
for both the Co-Op and White Flame, were indicted for federal tax fraud. At=
 a
board meeting on November 12, 1980, White proposed that the Co-Op purchase
White Flame. The board agreed. One month later, however, the Co-Op
&nbsp;[***533]&nbsp; filed a declaratory action against White and White Fla=
me
in Arkansas state court alleging that White actually had sold White Flame to
the Co-Op in February 1980. The complaint was drafted by White's attorneys =
and
led to a consent decree relieving White of his debts and providing that the
Co-Op had owned White Flame since February 15, 1980.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YG0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>White and Kuykendall were convic=
ted
of tax fraud in January 1981. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3TF0-003B-G17T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3TF0-003B-G17T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3TF0-003B-G17T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>White</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3TF0-003B-G17T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 671 F.2d 11=
26
(CA8 1982)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (affirming their convictions). Harry Erwin, the managing
partner of Russell Brown and Company, an Arkansas accounting firm, testified
for White, and shortly thereafter the Co-Op retained Russell Brown to perfo=
rm
its 1981 financial audit. Joe Drozal, &nbsp;[****7]&nbsp; a partner in the
Brown firm, was put in charge of the audit and Joe Cabaniss was selected to
assist him. On January 2, 1982, Russell Brown and Company merged with Arthur
Young and Company, which later became respondent Ernst &amp; Young. </span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn321' href=3D"#_ftn321" name=3D"_ftnref321" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN2_1_2"></a=
>[2A]One
of Drozal's first tasks in the audit was to determine White Flame's fixed-a=
sset
value. After consulting with White and reviewing White Flame's books (which
Kuykendall had prepared), Drozal concluded that the plant's value at the en=
d of
1980 was $ 4,393,242.66, the figure Kuykendall had employed. Using this fig=
ure
as a base, Drozal factored in the 1981 construction costs and capitalized
expenses and concluded that White Flame's 1981 fixed-asset value was
approximately &nbsp;[*174]&nbsp; $ 4.5 million. Drozal then had to determine
how that value should be treated for accounting purposes. If the Co-Op had
owned White Flame from the beginning &nbsp;[****8]&nbsp; of construction in
1979, White Flame's value for accounting purposes would be its fixed-asset
value of $ 4.5 million. If, however, the Co-Op had purchased White Flame fr=
om
White, White Flame would have to be given its fair market value at the time=
 of
purchase, which was somewhere between $ 444,000 and $ 1.5 million. If White
Flame were valued at less than $ 1.5 million, the Co-Op was insolvent. Droz=
al
concluded that the Co-Op had owned White Flame from the start and that the
plant should be valued at $ 4.5 million on its books.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
April 22, 1982, Arthur Young presented its 1981 audit report to the Co-Op's
board. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YJ0000400"></a>In that audit's No=
te 9,
Arthur Young expressed doubt whether the investment in White Flame could ev=
er
be recovered. Note 9 also observed that White Flame was sustaining operating
losses averaging $ 100,000 per month. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Arthur Young
&amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reves</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 937 F.2d 13=
10,
1318 (CA8 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Arthur Young did not tell the
board of its conclusion that the Co-Op always had owned White Flame or that
without that conclusion the Co-Op was insolvent.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
May 27, the Co-Op held its 1982 annual meeting. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YM0000400"></a>At that meeting, the Co-Op,
through Harry C. Erwin, a partner &nbsp;[****9]&nbsp; in Arthur Young,
distributed to the members condensed financial statements. These included W=
hite
Flame's $ 4.5 million asset value among its total assets but omitted the in=
formation
contained in the audit's Note 9. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>937 F.2d at
1318-1319</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Cabaniss was also &nbsp;[***534]&nbsp; present. Erwin =
saw
the condensed financial statement for the first time when he arrived at the
meeting. In a 5-minute presentation, he told his audience that the statemen=
ts
were condensed and that copies of the full audit were available at the Co-O=
p's
office. In response to questions, Erwin explained that the Co-Op owned White
Flame and that the plant had incurred approximately &nbsp;[*175]&nbsp; $ 1.2
million &nbsp;[**1168]&nbsp; in losses but he revealed no other information
relevant to the Co-Op's true financial health.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YP0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Co-Op hired Arthur Young als=
o to
perform its 1982 audit. The 1982 report, presented to the board on March 7,
1983, was similar to the 1981 report and restated (this time in its Note 8)
Arthur Young's doubt whether the investment in White Flame was recoverable.=
 See
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BTW0-008H-V3GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>937 F.2d at 1=
320</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The gasohol plant again was valued at approximately $ 4.5 million and was
responsible for the Co-Op's showing &nbsp;[****10]&nbsp; a positive net wor=
th.
The condensed financial statement distributed at the annual meeting on March
24, 1983, omitted the information in Note 8. This time, Arthur Young review=
ed
the condensed statement in advance but did not act to remove its name from =
the
statement. Cabaniss, in a 3-minute presentation at the meeting, gave the
financial report. He informed the members that the full audit was available=
 at
the Co-Op's office but did not tell them about Note 8 or that the Co-Op was=
 in
financial difficulty if White Flame were written down to its fair market va=
lue.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
February 1984, the Co-Op experienced a slight run on its demand notes. On
February 23, when it was unable to secure further financing, the Co-Op filed
for bankruptcy. As a result, the demand notes were frozen in the bankruptcy
estate and were no longer redeemable at will by the noteholders.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 14, 1985, the trustee in bankruptcy filed suit against 40 individu=
als
and entities, including Arthur Young, on behalf of the Co-Op and certain
noteholders. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YS0000400"></a>The District=
 Court
certified a class of noteholders, petitioners here, consisting of persons w=
ho
had purchased demand notes between February 15, 1980, and February
&nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; 23, 1984. Petitioners settled with all defendants exce=
pt
Arthur Young. The District Court determined before trial that the demand no=
tes
were securities under both federal and state law. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9GY0-0039-R0X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Robertson</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9GY0-0039-R0X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9GY0-0039-R0X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>White</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9GY0-0039-R0X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 635 F. Supp.
851, 865 (WD Ark. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. &nbsp;[*176]&nbsp; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ3YV0000400"></a>The court then granted summa=
ry
&nbsp;[**1169]&nbsp; judgment in favor of Arthur Young on the RICO claim. S=
ee <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Robertson</i> v. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>White</i>, Nos. 85-2044, 85-2096, 85-2155, and 85-2259 (WD Ark., Oc=
t.
15, 1986), App. 198-200. The District Court applied the test established by=
