:contentItem:3S4X-7BD0-0039-N4HV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sorenson</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7BD0-0039-N4HV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7BD0-0039-N4HV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Secretary of
Treasury</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7BD0-0039-N4HV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 475 U.S. 85=
1,
860, 89 L. Ed. 2d 855, 106 S. Ct. 1600 (1986)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.As
a verb, &quot;conduct&quot; means &nbsp;[***536]&nbsp; to lead, run, manage=
, or
direct. Webster's Third New International Dictionary 474 (1976). Petitioners
urge us to read &quot;conduct&quot; as &quot;carry on,&quot; Brief for
Petitioners &nbsp;[****15]&nbsp; 23, so that almost &nbsp;[*178]&nbsp; any
involvement in the affairs of an enterprise would satisfy the &quot;conduct=
 or
participate&quot; requirement. But context is important, and in the context=
 of
the phrase &quot;to conduct . . . [an] enterprise's affairs,&quot; the word
indicates some degree of direction. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn3=
22'
href=3D"#_ftn322" name=3D"_ftnref322" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****16]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_3_1"></=
a>[1C]The
dissent agrees that, when &quot;conduct&quot; is used as a verb, &quot;it is
plausible to find in it a suggestion of control.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>Post</i>, at 187. The dissent prefers to focus on &quot;conduct&quo=
t;
as a noun, as in the phrase &quot;participate, directly or indirectly, in t=
he
conduct of [an] enterprise's affairs.&quot; But unless one reads
&quot;conduct&quot; to include an element of direction when used as a noun =
in
this phrase, the word becomes superfluous. Congress could easily have writt=
en
&quot;participate, directly or indirectly, in [an] enterprise's affairs,&qu=
ot;
but it chose to repeat the word &quot;conduct.&quot; We conclude, therefore,
that as both a noun and a verb in this subsection &quot;conduct&quot; requi=
res
an element of direction.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ41M0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**1170]&nbsp; The more
difficult question is what to make of the word &quot;participate.&quot; This
Court previously has characterized this word as a &quot;ter[m] . . . of
breadth.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ41K0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-40C0-003B-S1ST-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Russello</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-40C0-003B-S1ST-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 464 U.S. at
21-22</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. Petitioners argue that Congress used
&quot;participate&quot; as a synonym for &quot;aid and abet.&quot; Brief for
Petitioners 26. That would be a term of breadth indeed, for &quot;aid and
abet&quot; &quot;comprehends all assistance rendered by words, acts,
encouragement, support, or presence.&quot; Black's Law Dictionary 68 (6th e=
d.
1990). But &nbsp;[****17]&nbsp; within the context of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
&quot;participate&quot; appears to have a narrower meaning. We may mark
&nbsp;[*179]&nbsp; the limits of what the term might mean by looking again =
at
what Congress did <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>not</i> say. On t=
he one
hand, &quot;to participate . . . in the conduct of . . . affairs&quot; must=
 be
broader than &quot;to conduct affairs&quot; or the &quot;participate&quot;
phrase would be superfluous. On the other hand, as we already have noted,
&quot;to participate . . . in the conduct of . . . affairs&quot; must be
narrower than &quot;to participate in affairs&quot; or Congress' repetition=
 of
the word &quot;conduct&quot; would serve no purpose. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ41P0000400"></a>It seems that Congress chose=
 a
middle ground, consistent with a common understanding of the word
&quot;participate&quot; -- &quot;to take part in.&quot; Webster's Third New
International Dictionary 1646 (1976). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_4_0"></a=
>[1D]<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN6_1_2"></a>[6]Once we understand the word
&quot;conduct&quot; to require some degree of direction and the word
&quot;participate&quot; to require some part in that direction, the meaning=
 of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
comes into focus. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_24"></a>In order to
&quot;participate, directly or indirectly, in the conduct of such enterpris=
e's
affairs,&quot; one must have some part in &nbsp;[***537]&nbsp; directing th=
ose
affairs. Of course, the word &quot;participate&quot; makes clear that RICO
liability is not limited to those with primary responsibility
&nbsp;[****18]&nbsp; for the enterprise's affairs, just as the phrase
&quot;directly or indirectly&quot; makes clear that RICO liability is not
limited to those with a formal position in the enterprise, </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn323' href=3D"#_ftn323" name=3D"_ftnref323" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
but <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>some</i> part in directing the
enterprise's affairs is required. The &quot;operation or management&quot; t=
est
expresses this requirement in a formulation that is easy to apply.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>IV</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
