<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****21]&nbsp;
On June 3, Assistant Attorney General Will Wilson presented the views of the
Department of Justice on a number of bills relating to organized crime,
including S. 1623 and S. 1861, to the Subcommittee on Criminal Laws and
Procedures of the Senate Committee on the Judiciary. Wilson criticized S. 1=
623
on the ground that &quot;it is too narrow in that it merely prohibits the
investment of prohibited funds in a business, but fails to prohibit the con=
trol
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>or operation</i> of such a business=
 by
means of prohibited racketeering activities.&quot; Measures Related to
Organized Crime: Hearings before the Subcommittee on Criminal Laws and
Procedures of the Senate Committee on the Judiciary, 91st Cong., 1st Sess.,=
 387
(1969) (emphasis added). He praised S. 1861 because the &quot;criminal
provisions of the bill contained in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
are broad enough to cover most of the methods by which ownership, control <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>and operation</i> of business concerns=
 are
acquired.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid</i>. (emphasis
added). See Blakey, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>, at 2=
58, n.
59.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
alterations not relevant here, S. 1861 became Title IX of S. 30. The House =
and
Senate Reports that accompanied S. 30 described the three-part structure of=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;(1)
making unlawful &nbsp;[****22]&nbsp; the receipt or use of income from
'racketeering activity' or its proceeds by a principal in commission of the
activity to acquire an interest in or establish an enterprise engaged in
interstate commerce; (2) prohibiting the acquisition of any enterprise enga=
ged
in interstate commerce through a 'pattern' of 'racketeering &nbsp;[*182]&nb=
sp;
activity;' and (3) proscribing the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
operation
of any enterprise</i> engaged in interstate commerce through a 'pattern' of
'racketeering activity.'&quot; H. R. Rep. No. 91-1549, p. 35 (1970); S. Rep.
No. 91-617, p. 34 (1969) (emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
their comments on the floor, Members of Congress consistently referred to
subsection (c) as prohibiting the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>o=
peration</i>
of an enterprise through a pattern of racketeering activity and to subsecti=
ons
(a) and (b) as prohibiting the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>acqu=
isition</i>
of an enterprise. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn326' href=3D"#_ftn3=
26"
name=3D"_ftnref326" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Representative Cellar, who was
chairman of &nbsp;[**1172]&nbsp; the House Judiciary Committee that voted R=
ICO
out in 1970, described </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> as
proscribing the &quot;conduct of the affairs of a business by a person acti=
ng
in a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>managerial</i> capacity, throu=
gh
racketeering activity.&quot; 116 Cong. Rec. 35196 (1970) (emphasis added).<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ42S0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[****23]&nbsp; Of course, =
the
fact that Members of Congress understood </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
