style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Dissent
by:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-fam=
ily:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;SOUTER </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:17.0pt;page-brea=
k-after:
avoid'><a name=3D"Dissent_1"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><=
span
style=3D'font-size:14.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Dissent</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'line-height:3.0pt;mso-line-height-rule:exactly=
'><span style=3D'mso-ignore:vglayout;position:
 relative;z-index:251721728;left:-2px;top:0px;width:686px;height:4px'><img
 width=3D686 height=3D4 src=3D"data:image/gif;base64,R0lGODlhrgIEAHcAMSH+Gl=
NvZnR3YXJlOiBNaWNyb3NvZnQgT2ZmaWNlACH5BAEAAAAALAIAAACrAgQAggAAAACb2wCZ2QCd2=
wCe2wECAwECAwECAwN3CLrc/jDKSau9OOvNu/9gKI5kaZ5oqq5sNrxwLM90bd94ru987//AoHBI=
LBqPyKRyyWw6n9BoDyCtWq/YrHbL7Xq/4LB4SKCaB+c0eq1us9/uOHwur9Pv9jx+r+/z/36BgIO=
ChYSHhomIi4qNjI+OkZCTkpWUcAkAOw=3D=3D" v:shapes=3D"_x0000_s1113"></span><br
style=3D'mso-ignore:vglayout' clear=3DALL>
</p>

<p class=3DNormal25><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>JUSTICE
SOUTER, with whom JUSTICE WHITE joins, dissenting.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the word &quot;conduct,&quot; the Court today finds a clear congressional
mandate to limit RICO liability under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> to participants in the &quot;operation or management&qu=
ot;
&nbsp;[*187]&nbsp; of a RICO enterprise. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Ante</i>,
at 177-179. What strikes the Court as clear, however, looks at the very lea=
st
hazy to me, and I accordingly find the statute's &quot;liberal
construction&quot; provision not irrelevant, but dispositive. But even if I
were to assume, with the majority, that the word &quot;conduct&quot; clearly
imports some degree of direction or control into </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, I
would have to say that the majority misapplies its own &quot;operation or
management&quot; test to the facts presented here. I therefore respectfully
dissent.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
word &quot;conduct&quot; occurs twice in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
first as a verb, then as a noun.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;It
shall be unlawful for &nbsp;[****31]&nbsp; any person employed by or associ=
ated
with any enterprise engaged in, or the activities of which affect, intersta=
te
or foreign commerce, to conduct or participate, directly or indirectly, in =
the
conduct of such enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering acti=
vity
or collection of unlawful debt.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
the Court is surely correct that the cognates should receive consistent
&nbsp;[***542]&nbsp; readings, see <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
ante</i>,
at 177, and correct again that &quot;context is important&quot; in coming to
understand the sense of the terms intended by Congress, <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>ante</i>, at 178, the majority goes astray in quoting only the verb
form of &quot;conduct&quot; in its statement of the context for divining a
meaning that must fit the noun usage as well. Thus, the majority reaches its
pivotal conclusion that &quot;in the context of the phrase 'to conduct . . .
[an] enterprise's affairs,' the word indicates some degree of direction.&qu=
ot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid</i>. (footnote omitted). To be su=
re, if
the statutory setting is so abbreviated as to limit consideration to the wo=
rd
as a verb, it is plausible to find in it a suggestion of control, as in the
phrase &quot;to conduct an orchestra.&quot; (Even so, the suggestion
&nbsp;[****32]&nbsp; is less than emphatic, since even when &quot;conduct&q=
uot;
is used as a verb, &quot;the notion of direction or leadership is often
obscured or lost; e. g. an investigation is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>conducted</i>
by all those who take &nbsp;[*188]&nbsp; part in it.&quot; 3 Oxford English
Dictionary 691 (2d ed. 1989) (emphasis in original).)</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
any event, the context is not so limited, and several features of the full
subsection at issue support a more inclusive construction of
&quot;conduct.&quot; The term, when used as a noun, is defined by the
majority's chosen dictionary as, for example, &quot;carrying forward&quot; =
or
&quot;carrying out,&quot; Webster's Third New International Dictionary 473
(1976), phrases without any implication of direction or control. The sugges=
tion
of control is diminished further by the fact that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
covers not just those &quot;employed by&quot; an enterprise, but those mere=
ly
&quot;associated with&quot; it, as well. And associates (like employees) are
prohibited not merely from conducting the affairs of an enterprise through a
pattern of racketeering, not merely from participating directly in such
unlawful conduct, but even from indirect participation in the conduct of an
enterprise's affairs in such a manner. The very breadth of this prohibition
renders the &nbsp;[****33]&nbsp; majority's &nbsp;[**1175]&nbsp; reading of
&quot;conduct&quot; rather awkward, for it is hard to imagine how the
&quot;operation or management&quot; test would leave the statute with the
capacity to reach the indirect participation of someone merely associated w=
ith
an enterprise. I think, then, that this contextual examination shows
&quot;conduct&quot; to have a long arm, unlimited by any requirement to pro=
ve
that the activity includes an element of direction. But at the very least, =
the
full context is enough to defeat the majority's conviction that the more
restrictive interpretation of the word &quot;conduct&quot; is clearly the o=
ne
intended. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn330' href=3D"#_ftn330"
name=3D"_ftnref330" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*189]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[****34]&nbsp; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ43P0000400"></a>What,
then, if we call it a tie &nbsp;[***543]&nbsp; on the contextual analysis? =
The
answer is that Congress has given courts faced with uncertain meaning a cle=
ar
tiebreaker in RICO's &quot;liberal construction&quot; clause, which directs
that the &quot;provisions of this title shall be liberally construed to
effectuate its remedial purposes.&quot; Pub. L. 91-452, &sect; 904(a), 84 S=
tat.
947, note following </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. We have relied before on this &quot;express
admonition&quot; to read RICO provisions broadly, see </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ43N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S. P.=
 R.
L.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Imrex Co.</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
497-498, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346, 105 S. Ct. 3275 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and in this instance, the &quot;liberal construction&quot; clause plays its
intended part, directing us to recognize the more inclusive definition of t=
he
word &quot;conduct,&quot; free of any restricting element of direction or
control. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn331' href=3D"#_ftn331"
name=3D"_ftnref331" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Because the Court of Appeals
employed a narrower reading, I would reverse.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****35]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY37W0K1MNJ47H0000400"></a>Even if I were to adopt t=
he
majority's view of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
however, I still could not join the judgment, which seems to me unsupportab=
le
under the very &quot;operation or management&quot; test the Court announces=
. If
Arthur Young had confined itself in this case to the role traditionally
performed by an outside auditor, I could agree with the majority that Arthur
Young took no part in the management or operation of the Farmer's Cooperati=
ve
of Arkansas and Oklahoma, Inc. (Co-op). But the record on summary judgment,
viewed &nbsp;[*190]&nbsp; most favorably to Reves, </span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn332' href=3D"#_ftn332" name=3D"_ftnref332" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
shows that Arthur Young created the very financial statements it was hired,=
 and
purported, to audit. Most importantly, Reves adduced evidence that Arthur Y=
oung
took on management responsibilities by deciding, in the first instance, what
value to assign to the Co-op's most important fixed asset, the White Flame
gasohol plant, and Arthur Young itself conceded below that the alleged acti=
vity
went beyond traditional auditing. Because I find, then, that even under the
majority's &quot;operation or management&quot; test the Court of Appeals
erroneously affirmed the summary judgment for Arthur Young, I would (again)
reverse.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****36]&nbsp;
For our purposes, the line between managing and auditing is fairly clear. In
describing the &quot;respective responsibilities of management and
auditor,&quot; Arthur Young points &nbsp;[**1176]&nbsp; to the Code of
Professional Conduct developed by the American Institute of Certified Public
Accountants (AICPA). Brief for Respondent 31. This auditors' code points up
management's ultimate responsibility for the content of financial statement=
s:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;The
financial statements are management's responsibility. The auditor's
responsibility is to express an opinion on the financial statements. Manage=
ment
is responsible for adopting sound accounting policies and for establishing =
and
maintaining an internal control structure that will, among other things,
record, process, summarize, and report financial data &nbsp;[***544]&nbsp; =
that
is consistent with management's assertions embodied in the financial
statements. . . . The independent auditor may make &nbsp;[*191]&nbsp; sugge=
stions
about the form or content of the financial statements or draft them, in who=
le
or in part, based on information from management's accounting system.&quot;=
 1
CCH AICPA Professional Standards, SAS No. 1, &sect; 110.02 (1982).</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
short, management chooses the assertions to appear in financial
&nbsp;[****37]&nbsp; statements; the auditor &quot;simply expresses an opin=
ion
on the client's financial statements.&quot; Brief for Respondent 30. These
standards leave no doubt that an accountant can in no sense independently a=
udit
financial records when he has selected their substance himself. See </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-mat=
erials&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:3T1N-Y280-000Y-40XV-00000-00&amp;context=3D=
"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Thomas =
P.
Reynolds Securities, Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-mat=
erials&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:3T1N-Y280-000Y-40XV-00000-00&amp;context=3D=
"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, Exchange Act
