span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the McCarran-Ferguson Act, which exempts from federal regulation &quot;the
business of insurance,&quot; except &quot;to the extent that such business =
is
not regulated by State Law.&quot; Although it held the conduct involved to =
be
&quot;the business of insurance,&quot; the Court of Appeals ruled that the
foreign reinsurers did not fall within </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-74X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 2(b)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
protection because their activities could not be &quot;regulated by State
Law,&quot; and that the domestic insurers had forfeited their </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-74X4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 2(b)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
exemption when they conspired with the nonexempt foreign reinsurers.
Furthermore, held the court, most of the conduct in question fell within &s=
ect;
3(b), which provides that nothing in the McCarran-Ferguson Act &quot;shall
render the . . . Sherman Act inapplicable to any . . . act of boycott . . .
.&quot; Finally, the court rejected the District Court's conclusion that the
principle of international comity barred it from exercising Sherman Act
jurisdiction over the three claims brought solely against the London reinsu=
rers.</span></p>

