<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN17_1"></a>=
[17]In
addition to its use in the antitrust field, the concept of &quot;boycott&qu=
ot;
frequently appears in labor law, and in this context as well there is a cle=
ar
distinction between boycotts and concerted agreements seeking terms. The
ordinary strike &nbsp;[*805]&nbsp; seeking better contract terms is a
&quot;refusal to deal&quot; -- <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.=
</i>,
union members refuse to sell their labor until the employer capitulates to
their contract demands. But no one would call this a boycott, because the
conditions of the &quot;refusal to deal&quot; relate directly to the terms =
of
the refused transaction (the employment contract). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1PG0000400"></a>A refusal to work changes fr=
om
strike to boycott only when it seeks to obtain action from the employer
unrelated to the employment contract. This distinction is well illustrated =
by
the &nbsp;[**2914]&nbsp; famous boycott of Pullman cars by Eugene Debs' Ame=
rican
Railway Union in 1894. The incident began when workers at the Pullman Palace
Car Company called a strike, but the &quot;boycott&quot; occurred only when
other members of the American Railway Union, not Pullman employees, support=
ed
the strikers by refusing to work on any train drawing&nbsp;[****61]&nbsp; a
Pullman car. See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1PF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-F1V0-003B-H2N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Debs</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-F1V0-003B-H2N7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 158 U.S. 56=
4,
566-567, 39 L. Ed. 1092, 15 S. Ct. 900 (1895)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(statement of the case); H. Laidler, Boycotts and the Labor Struggle 100-108
(1968). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1R30000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1RR0000400"></a>The refusal to handle Pullma=
n cars
had nothing to do with Pullman cars themselves (working on Pullman cars was=
 no
more difficult or dangerous than working on other cars); rather, it was in
furtherance of the collateral objective of obtaining better employment terms
for the Pullman workers. In other labor cases as well, the term
&quot;boycott&quot; invariably holds the meaning that we ascribe to it: Its
goal is to alter, not the terms of the refused transaction, but the terms of
workers' employment. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn362' href=3D"#_f=
tn362"
name=3D"_ftnref362" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[****62]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1SW0000400"></a>&nbsp;[*806]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[***645]&nbsp; The one case in which we have found an activity to
constitute a &quot;boycott&quot; within the meaning of the McCarran-Ferguson
Act is </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1SV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8PV0-003B-S1BR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>St. Paul Fire
&amp; Marine Ins. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8PV0-003B-S1BR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8PV0-003B-S1BR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Barry</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8PV0-003B-S1BR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 438 U.S. 53=
1,
57 L. Ed. 2d 932, 98 S. Ct. 2923 (1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. There the plaint=
iffs
were licensed physicians and their patients, and the defendant (St. Paul) w=
as a
malpractice insurer that had refused to renew the physicians' policies on an
&quot;occurrence&quot; basis, but insisted upon a &quot;claims made&quot; b=
asis.
The allegation was that, at the instance of St. Paul, the three other
malpractice insurers in the State had collectively refused to write insuran=
ce
for St. Paul's customers, thus forcing them to accept St. Paul's renewal te=
rms.
Unsurprisingly, we held the allegation sufficient to state a cause of actio=
n.
The insisted-upon condition of the boycott (not being a former St. Paul
policyholder) was &quot;artificial&quot;: it bore no relationship (or an
&quot;artificial&quot; relationship) to the proposed contracts of insurance
that the physicians wished to conclude with St. Paul's competitors. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN18_1"></a>=
[18A]Under
the standard described, &nbsp;[****63]&nbsp; it is obviously not a
&quot;boycott&quot; for the reinsurers to &quot;refuse to reinsure coverages
written on the ISO CGL forms until the desired changes were made,&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ante</i>, at 788, because the terms of=
 the
primary coverages are central elements of the reinsurance contract -- they =
are <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>what</i> is reinsured. See App. 16-17 =
(Cal.
Complaint PP26-27). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TC0000400"></a>The
&quot;primary policies are . . . the basis of the losses that are shared in=
 the
reinsurance agreements.&quot; 1 B. Webb, H. Anderson, J. Cookman, &amp; P.
Kensicki, Principles of Reinsurance 87 (1990); see also </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-G450-003B-7498-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-G450-003B-7498-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 55</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
Gurley, Regulation of Reinsurance in the United States, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:3RK3-JY50-00C2-M4NK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>19 Forum 72, =
73
(1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TC0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TH0000400"></a>Indeed, reinsurance is so cl=
osely
tied to the terms of the primary insurance contract that one of the two
categories of reinsurance (assumption reinsurance) substitutes the reinsurer
for the primary or &quot;ceding&quot; insurer and places the reinsurer into
contractual privity with the primary insurer's policyholders. See <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i>, at 73-74; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y90-003B-419B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Colonial Amer=
ican
Life Ins. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y90-003B-419B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y90-003B-419B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Commissioner<=
/span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y90-003B-419B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 491 U.S. 24=
4,
247, 105 L. Ed. 2d 199, 109 S. Ct. 2408 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
B. Ostrager &amp; T. Newman, Handbook on Insurance Coverage &nbsp;[*807]&nb=
sp;
Disputes&nbsp;[****64]&nbsp; chs. 15-16 (5th ed. 1992). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TH0000400_2"></a>And in the other category =
of
reinsurance (indemnity reinsurance), either the terms of the underlying
insurance policy are incorporated &nbsp;[**2915]&nbsp; by reference (if the
reinsurance is written under a facultative agreement), see J. Butler &amp; =
R.
Merkin, Reinsurance Law B.1.1-04 (1992); R. Carter, Reinsurance 235 (1979),=
 or
(if the reinsurance is conducted on a treaty basis) the reinsurer will requ=
ire
full disclosure of the terms of the underlying insurance policies and usual=
ly
require that the primary insurer not vary those terms without prior approva=
l,
see </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ1TG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JGP0-003B-S128-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JGP0-003B-S128-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 256, 297=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>JUSTICE
SOUTER simply disregards this integral relationship between the terms of the
primary insurance &nbsp;[***646]&nbsp; form and the contract of reinsurance=
. He
describes the reinsurers as &quot;individuals and entities who were not mem=
bers
of ISO, and who would not ordinarily be parties to an agreement setting the
terms of primary insurance, not being in the business of selling it.&quot; =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i>, at 788. While this factual
assumption is crucial to JUSTICE SOUTER's reasoning (because otherwise he w=
ould
not be able to distinguish permissible agreements among primary insurers), =
he
offers&nbsp;[****65]&nbsp; no support for the statement. But even if it hap=
pens
to be true, he does not explain why it <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>must</i>
be true -- that is, why the law must exclude reinsurers from full membership
and participation. The realities of the industry may make explanation
difficult:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;Reinsurers
also benefit from the services by ISO and other rating or service
organizations. The underlying rates and policy forms are the basis for many
reinsurance contracts. Reinsurers may also subscribe to various services. F=
or
example, a facultative reinsurer may subscribe to the rating service, so th=
at
they have the rating manuals available, or purchase optional services, such=
 as
a sprinkler report for a specific property location.&quot; 2 R. Reinarz, J.
Schloss, G. Patrik, &amp; P. Kensicki, Reinsurance Practices 18 (1990).</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*808]&nbsp;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN18_2"></a=
>[18B]JUSTICE
SOUTER also describes reinsurers as being &quot;outside the primary insuran=
ce
industry.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i>, at 789. T=
hat is
technically true (to the extent the two symbiotic industries can be separat=
ed)
but quite irrelevant. What matters is that the scope and predictability of =
the
risks assumed in a reinsurance&nbsp;[****66]&nbsp; contract depend entirely=
 upon
the terms of the primary policies that are reinsured. The terms of the prim=
ary
policies are the &quot;subject-matter insured&quot; by reinsurance, Carter,=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>, at 4, so that to insist upon
certain primary-insurance terms as a condition of writing reinsurance is in=
 no
way &quot;artificial&quot;; and hence for a number of reinsurers to insist =
upon
such terms jointly is in no way a &quot;boycott.&quot; </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn363' href=3D"#_ftn363" name=3D"_ftnref363" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span></p>

