<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ2620000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Petitioners in No. 91-1128, vari=
ous
British corporations and other British subjects, argue that certain of the
claims against them constitute an inappropriate extraterritorial applicatio=
n of
the Sherman &nbsp;[***649]&nbsp; Act. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ft=
n372'
href=3D"#_ftn372" name=3D"_ftnref372" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It is important to distinguish =
two
distinct questions raised by this petition: whether the District Court had
jurisdiction, and whether the Sherman Act reaches the extraterritorial cond=
uct
alleged here. On the first question, I believe that the District Court had
subject-matter jurisdiction over the Sherman Act claims against all the
defendants (personal jurisdiction is not contested). Respondents asserted
nonfrivolous claims under the Sherman Act, and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1331</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> vests district courts with subject-matter jurisdiction =
over
cases &quot;arising under&quot; federal statutes. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ2640000400"></a>As precedents such as </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFY3Y90K1MNJ2610000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JFW0-003B-S0B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lauritzen</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JFW0-003B-S0B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JFW0-003B-S0B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Larsen</span>=
</i></a><a
