 359</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
It is not realistic, and also not helpful, to pretend that the only really
relevant issue in this litigation is not before us. In any event, if one
erroneously chooses, as the Court does, to make adjudicative jurisdiction (=
or,
more precisely, abstention) the vehicle for taking account of the needs of
prescriptive comity, the Court still gets it wrong. It concludes that no
&quot;true&nbsp;[****108]&nbsp; conflict &quot; counseling nonapplication of
United States law (or rather, as it thinks, United States judicial jurisdic=
