k'>&nbsp;[*971]&nbsp;
TASHIMA, Circuit Judge:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
case involves an Idaho county's attempt to recover damages under the Racket=
eer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-1968</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, for additional monies it claim=
s to
have expended on public health care and law enforcement services for
undocumented immigrants. Plaintiff-appellant Canyon County
&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp;commenced this action against four companies and one
individual under RICO's civil enforcement provision, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, alleging that defendants engaged in an illegal scheme =
of
hiring and/or harboring undocumented immigrant workers within the County, a=
nd
that their actions forced the County to pay &quot;millions of dollars for
health care services and criminal justice services for the illegal
immigrants.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court concluded that the County did not have statutory standing un=
der </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
because the County did not meet the threshold requirement that a civil
plaintiff be &quot;injured in his business or property&quot; by reason of t=
he
alleged RICO violation. Consequently, the court dismissed the County's
complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*972]&nbsp;
We have jurisdiction pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0FT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1291</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, and we affirm the district court. We agree with the
district court that the County has failed to allege that it was injured in =
its
business or property. We also conclude that, with respect to almost all of =
the
defendants' alleged RICO violations, the County cannot show that its claimed
injuries were proximately caused by defendants' conduct. For both of these
reasons, the County lacks statutory standing to pursue its federal
&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp;RICO claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Civil Enforcement Under RICO<=
/span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_26"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>RICO focuses on &quot;racketeeri=
ng
activity,&quot; which the statute defines as a number of specific criminal =
acts
under federal and state laws. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</=
i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. As relevant to this case, acts which are indictable un=
der </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 274 of=
 the
Immigration and Nationality Act</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"T=
imes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;INA&quot;) are included =
in
the definition of racketeering activity. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)(F)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>INA &sect; 27=
4</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(codified as amended at </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>) criminalizes the bringing in, transportation, harborin=
g,
and employment of undocumented aliens.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_26"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Substantive violations of RICO a=
re
defined in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. Under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it is illegal for any person &quot;to conduct or participate, directly or
indirectly, in the conduct of [an] enterprise's affairs through a pattern of
racketeering activity,&quot; where that enterprise affects interstate comme=
rce.
It is also illegal for any person to conspire to do so. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. A &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; require=
s at
least two predicate acts of racketeering activity, as defined in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, within a period of ten years. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(5)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn376' href=3D"#_f=
tn376"
name=3D"_ftnref376" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I8375D1MPGG00002S6T0029R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I8375D1MS2B00002S6T0029S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XNFB0K1MNJ55B0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc4_26"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
RICO's civil enforcement mechanism, &quot;[a]ny person injured in his busin=
ess
or property by reason of a violation of [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>] may sue therefor in any appropriate United States dist=
rict
court and shall recover threefold the damages he sustains and the cost of t=
he
suit, including a reasonable attorney's fee . . . .&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. To have standing under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
civil RICO plaintiff must show: (1) that his alleged harm qualifies as inju=
ry
to his business or property; and (2) that his harm was &quot;by reason of&q=
uot;
the RICO violation, which requires the plaintiff to establish proximate
causation. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XNFB0K1MNJ5590000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes v. Sec.
Investor Prot. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. 25=
8,
268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XNFB0K1MNJ55C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.R=
.L.
v. Imrex Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
496, 105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Canyon County's Complaint</s=
pan></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County's first amended complaint (&quot;complaint&quot;) names as defendants
Syngenta Seeds, Inc. (&quot;Syngenta&quot;), Sorrento Lactalis, Inc.
(&quot;Sorrento&quot;), Swift Beef Company (&quot;Swift&quot;), Harris Moran
Seed Company (&quot;Harris&quot;), and Albert Pacheco. Because we are revie=
wing
the dismissal of the complaint, we assume that the factual allegations of t=
he
complaint, summarized below, are true.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to the complaint, each of the four defendant companies
&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;knowingly employed and/or harbored large numbers of illegal
immigrants within Canyon County, in an &quot;Illegal Immigrant Hiring
Scheme.&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn377' href=3D"#_ftn377"
name=3D"_ftnref377" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The companies' actions have dam=
aged
the County because the County &quot;has paid millions of dollars for health
care services and criminal justice services for the illegal immigrants who =
have
been employed by the &nbsp;[*973]&nbsp; defendants in violation of federal
law.&quot; The individual defendant, Pacheco, has engaged in a policy of
&quot;Wilful Blindness and Harboring&quot; of illegal immigrants, in his ro=
le
as director of a local social service agency, which has resulted in similar=
ly
increased costs for the County. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
Syngenta and Harris are both growers and processors of agricultural
commodities. The County claims that both companies have deliberately hired
hundreds of workers who the companies knew were not authorized to work in t=
he
United States. Working with a farm labor contractor, Ag Services, the compa=
nies
agreed to employ undocumented immigrants supplied by Ag Services. The
contractor acts as a &quot;front&quot; for Syngenta and
&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp;Harris: in addition to supplying workers, the contractor
channels the workers' wages to them, completes fraudulent I-9 employment
eligibility forms for the workers, and supplies the workers with false
documents. The companies have thus allegedly violated both </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324(a)(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn378' href=3D"#_f=
tn378"
name=3D"_ftnref378" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> which criminalizes knowing hiri=
ng
of more than ten unauthorized aliens during a single year, and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324(a)(1)(A)(iii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn379' href=3D"#_ftn379" name=3D"_ftnref379" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
which criminalizes harboring of unauthorized aliens.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County further alleges that Syngenta and Harris have each formed an
&quot;association-in-fact enterprise&quot; with the farm labor contractor, =
and
that the companies' sustained custom of hiring and/or harboring undocumented
workers amounts to a pattern of racketeering activity. As a consequence, the
companies have allegedly violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, by participating in the conduct of an enterprise's aff=
airs
through a pattern of racketeering activity.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint contains similar allegations against Sorrento, a cheese processor,
and Swift, a meat packer, the only difference being that Sorrento and Swift
have allegedly formed &quot;association-in-fact&quot; enterprises with a
different labor contractor, Labor Ready.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County's claim against defendant Pacheco is distinct, as it is not based on=
 the
hiring of undocumented immigrants. Instead, the County alleges that Pacheco=
, in
his position as Executive Director of the Idaho Migrant Council, has direct=
ed
his staff to assist immigrant workers in fraudulently applying for public
benefits, despite Pacheco's knowledge that the workers lacked legal status
&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp;in the United States and were ineligible for such benefits=
. In
directing his staff to take these actions, Pacheco has allegedly committed a
pattern of racketeering activity, by knowingly harboring undocumented
immigrants in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-T0S2-D6RV-H49T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1324(a)(1)(A)(iii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, based on his association
with the Migrant Council, which is asserted to be a RICO enterprise, Pacheco
has allegedly violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1962(d)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn380' href=3D"#_ftn380" name=3D"_ftnref380" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III. Dismissal by the District C=
ourt</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
filed motions to dismiss the County's complaint, challenging both the
&nbsp;[*974]&nbsp; County's standing under RICO and the adequacy of the
County's allegations of substantive RICO violations. The district court gra=
nted
defendants' motions and dismissed the complaint in its entirety. It held th=
at
the County lacked statutory standing to bring its federal RICO claims becau=
se
it had not been injured in its business or property by the alleged conduct
constituting the RICO violations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00M0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00P0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In evaluating whether the County=
 had
alleged injuries of a type cognizable under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the district court first discussed the tort &nbsp;[**9]&nbsp;doctrine known=
 as
the &quot;municipal cost recovery rule,&quot; drawing on our decision in </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XNFB0K1MNJ55F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>City of Flags=
taff
v. Atchison, Topeka &amp; Santa Fe Ry.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 719 F.2d 322
(9th Cir. 1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00S0000400"></a>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>City of Flagstaff</i>, we held that, under Arizona law, a municipal=
ity
could not recover the cost of public services for fire or safety protection
from a negligent tortfeasor. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00N00=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 323</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00S0000400_2"></a>We did, however, recognize=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_20"></a>several exceptions to the &quot;muni=
cipal
cost recovery rule,&quot; including exceptions allowing municipal recovery
&quot;where it is authorized by statute or regulation&quot; and &quot;where=
 the
acts of a private party create a public nuisance which the government seeks=
 to
abate.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ00R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 324</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Noting
that the County appeared &quot;to concede the existence of the municipal co=
st
recovery rule,&quot; the district court rejected the County's argument that=
 its
claims fit within either the exception for suits to abate a public nuisance=
, or
the exception for suits authorized by statute. First, the district court st=
ated
that the County was not acting in its governmental capacity to abate a
nuisance, but suing for treble damages under civil RICO. It then considered=
 the
County's argument that the RICO statute itself authorized its recovery. The
court rejected &nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;this contention, citing what it characteri=
zed
as &quot;extensive persuasive authority&quot; from other circuits to the ef=
fect
that a municipality may not recover under RICO for alleged injuries to its
governmental functions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
relying on the municipal cost recovery rule and on case law from other
circuits, the district court concluded that the County's claim for the cost=
s of
municipal services did not qualify as an injury to business or property wit=
hin
the meaning of RICO. On this basis, the court dismissed the federal RICO cl=
aims
against all defendants and entered judgment against the County. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn381' href=3D"#_ftn381" name=3D"_ftnref381" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The County timely appealed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>STANDARD OF REVIEW</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0280000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ02B0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ05Y0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8_19"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
reviewing dismissal of the County's complaint under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn382' href=3D"#_ftn382" name=3D"_ftnref382" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;we accept as &nbsp;[*975]&nbsp; true the factual allegations in the
amended complaint.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ027000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza v. Ideal
Steel Supply Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S. 45=
1,
126 S. Ct. 1991, 1994, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720 (2006)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ03N0000400"></a>We will uphold the dismissal
&quot;only if it is clear that no relief could be granted under any set of
facts that could be proved consistent &nbsp;[**11]&nbsp;with the
allegations.&quot; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn383' href=3D"#_ftn=
383"
name=3D"_ftnref383" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0290000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46NW-J9X0-0038-X31T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mendoza v. Zi=
rkle
Fruit Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46NW-J9X0-0038-X31T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 301 F.3d 11=
63,
1167 (9th Cir. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citation and internal quotation
marks omitted). We may, however, affirm the dismissal on any ground support=
ed
by the record. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ03N0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ02C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4993-8350-0038-X48R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McKesson HBOC,
Inc. v. N.Y. State Common Ret. Fund, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4993-8350-0038-X48R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 339 F.3d 10=
87,
1090 (9th Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX3K98000RGTW00045Y"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ06T0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_16"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A civil RICO &quot;plaintiff only
has standing if, and can only recover to the extent that, he has been injur=
ed
in his business or property by the conduct constituting the violation.&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ06S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. at=
 496</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Below, we examine whether the allegations in the Count=
y's
complaint meet the requirements for standing under RICO's civil suit provis=
ion,
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We discuss first whether the County has alleged the
requisite type of injury, then whether the defendants' alleged RICO violati=
ons
could have proximately caused the County's injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Canyon County Has Failed to
Allege </span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp;<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:norma=
l'>that
It Has Been Injured in Its Business or Property</b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ07D0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ07G0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_14"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To determine whether a plaintiff=
 has
sufficiently alleged that he has been &quot;injured in his business or
property,&quot; we must examine carefully the nature of the asserted harm. =
Our
circuit requires that a plaintiff asserting injury to property allege
&quot;concrete financial loss.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ06V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3CC0-008H-V4D8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Oscar v. Univ.
Students Coop. Ass'n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3CC0-008H-V4D8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 965 F.2d 78=
3,
785 (9th Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (en banc). Financial loss alone,
however, is insufficient. &quot;Without a harm to a specific business or
property interest -- a categorical inquiry typically determined by referenc=
e to
state law -- there is no injury to business or property within the meaning =
of
RICO.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ07F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4GWK-XNW0-0038-X25B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Diaz v. Gates=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4GWK-XNW0-0038-X25B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 420 F.3d 89=
7,
900 (9th Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (en banc), <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>cert. denied</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>546 U.S. =
1131,
126 S. Ct. 1069, 163 L. Ed. 2d 928 (2006)</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
this case, the County bases its RICO claims on the fact that &quot;it has p=
aid
millions of dollars for health care services and criminal justice services =
for
the illegal immigrants who have been employed [and/or harbored] by the
defendants in violation of federal law.&quot; The County argues that it has
sufficiently alleged that it has been injured in its property because it cl=
aims
to have been forced to spend money. &nbsp;[*976]&nbsp; According to the Cou=
nty,
&quot;[a]ny &nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;involuntary expenditure of money is a loss of=
 'property.'&quot;
However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_12"></a>a government entity can=
not
rely on expenditures alone to establish civil RICO standing, and there is no
indication that the County holds a property interest in the law enforcement=
 or
health care services that it provides to the public.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A.
Government Expenditures Alone Are Insufficient to Qualify as Injury to Prop=
erty</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ07J0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In the ordinary context of a
commercial transaction, a consumer who has been overcharged can claim an in=
jury
to her property, based on a wrongful deprivation of her money. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0850000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ07H0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reiter v.
Sonotone Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 442 U.S. 33=
0,
342, 99 S. Ct. 2326, 60 L. Ed. 2d 931 (1979)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(interpreting provision of the Clayton Act that grants standing to &quot;any
person . . . injured in his business or property&quot; by an antitrust
violation). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0870000400"></a>As the Supreme
Court reasoned in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reiter</i>, &quot=
;[i]n
its dictionary definitions and in common usage 'property' comprehends anyth=
ing
of material value owned or possessed. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0870000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0850000400_2"></a>Money, of course, is a for=
m of
property.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0840000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 338</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citation omitted). Thus, the Court concluded that &quot;where petitioner
alleges a wrongful deprivation of her money because the price of the hearing
aid she bought was artificially inflated . . ., she has alleged an injury in
&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp;her 'property' . . . .&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0860000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 342</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ08N0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly,<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_8"></a> government entities that have been
overcharged in commercial transactions and thus deprived of their money can
claim injury to their property. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See=
 </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0880000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B6Y0-003B-H2PN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Chattanooga
Foundry &amp; Pipe Works v. City of Atlanta</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-B6Y0-003B-H2PN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 203 U.S. 39=
0,
396, 27 S. Ct. 65, 51 L. Ed. 241 (1906)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that cit=
y's
claim that it was overcharged for its purchases of water pipe qualified as
allegation that it was &quot;injured in its property . . . by being led to =
pay
more than the worth of the pipe&quot;); <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ08P0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>County of Oakland v. City of Detroit, 866 F.2d 839, 847 (6th Cir. 1=
989)</i>
(outlying counties &quot;sustained an injury in their property when they pa=
id
the allegedly excessive charges&quot; to Detroit for treatment and disposal=
 of
sewage).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ08T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>But <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_7"></a>the law commonly distinguishes betwe=
en the
status of a governmental entity acting to enforce the laws or promote the
general welfare and that of a governmental entity acting as a consumer or o=
ther
type of market participant. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.=
g.</i>,
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ08S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5D90-003B-S4DB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Alfred L. Sna=
pp
&amp; Son, Inc. v. Puerto Rico ex rel. Barez</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5D90-003B-S4DB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 458 U.S. 59=
2,
601-02, 102 S. Ct. 3260, 73 L. Ed. 2d 995 (1982)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(distinguishing between the sovereign, quasi-sovereign, and proprietary
interests of governmental bodies). When a governmental body acts in its
sovereign or quasi-sovereign &nbsp;[**17]&nbsp;capacity, seeking to enforce=
 the
laws or promote the public well-being, it cannot claim to have been
&quot;injured in [its] . . . property&quot; for RICO purposes based solely =
on
the fact that it has spent money in order to act governmentally. All govern=
ment
actions require the expenditure of money in this sense, insofar as the
government acts through public servants who are paid for their services. If
government expenditures alone sufficed as injury to property, any RICO
predicate act that provoked any sort of governmental response would provide=
 the
government entity with standing to sue under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> --
an interpretation of the statute that we think highly improbable. We find it
particularly inappropriate to label a governmental entity &quot;injured in =
its
property&quot; when it spends money on the provision of additional public
services, given that those services are based on legislative mandates and a=
re
intended to further the public interest.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*977]&nbsp; <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>B. The County Does Not Have a Property
Interest in Services It Provides to Enforce the Laws and Promote the Public
Welfare</i></b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
the County cannot satisfy the requirement of injury to a &quot;specific
property interest&quot; based solely on its expenditure of money to provide
&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;public services, we must examine whether the County can c=
laim
a property interest in the services themselves. We conclude that the govern=
ment
does not possess a property interest in the law enforcement or health care
services that it provides to the public; therefore, a governmental entity is
not &quot;injured in its property&quot; when greater demand causes it to pr=
ovide
additional public services of this type.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
base this conclusion on several factors. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX3R4D000RGTW000460"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0970000400"></a>First, the ordinary meaning =
of the
term &quot;property&quot; does not include the interests of local governmen=
ts
in performing functions such as policing, caring for the indigent sick, and
otherwise protecting the well-being of the public. Moreover, following <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Diaz</i>'s instruction to determine wh=
ether
the relevant state's law recognizes the alleged property interest, we see no
indication that Idaho law creates a property-like entitlement in such servi=
ces
on the part of counties. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0990000400"></a>=
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0960000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4GWK-XNW0-0038-X25B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Diaz</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4GWK-XNW0-0038-X25B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 420 F.3d at=
 900</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0990000400_2"></a>The County has made no sho=
wing
that Idaho law grants counties a protectable legal interest in the public
services they provide, and we have found none. The persuasive authority of
other jurisdictions tilts against such an interest. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Cf. id.</i> (plaintiff's &nbsp;[**19]&nbsp;claim for lost wages due=
 to
false imprisonment alleged harm to a property interest, because California =
law
recognizes the torts of intentional interference with contract and interfer=
ence
with prospective business relations); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>see
also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0980000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4HR8-P3W0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Living Design=
s,
Inc. v. E.I. Dupont de Nemours &amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4HR8-P3W0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 431 F.3d 35=
3,
364 (9th Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (plaintiff's claim alleging
diminished litigation settlement due to the defendant's fraud alleged injur=
y to
a property interest, because fraudulent inducement is an actionable tort un=
der
Hawaii law), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied</i>, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ09B0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>547 U.S. 1192, 126 S. Ct. 2861, 165 L. Ed. 2d 895 (2006)</i>.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
the Supreme Court has interpreted the statutory provision that served as a
model for </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
exclude claims for damages to governments' non-proprietary interests. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ09T0000400"></a>The civil enforcement provis=
ion of
the antitrust laws, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 4 of the
Clayton Act</i>, provides a private right of action to &quot;any person who
shall be injured in his business or property by reason of anything forbidde=
n in
the antitrust laws . . . .&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>15
U.S.C. &sect; 15(a)</i>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B40000400"></a>=
Because
Congress based the RICO private right of action on <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>&sect; 4</i>, courts have often, though not invariably, interpreted=
 the
two statutory provisions in a like manner. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>See,
e.g.</i>, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ09S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at
267-68</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ09V0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. at
489, 498-99</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B40000400_2"></a>&n=
bsp;[**20]&nbsp;Interpreting
the Clayton Act, the Court has read the phrase &quot;injured in his busines=
s or
property&quot; to encompass only injury to a state's &quot;commercial
interests,&quot; meaning &quot;the interests of the [state] as a party to a
commercial transaction.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B30=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reiter</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 442 U.S. at
341-42</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. If a similar interpretation of the identical phrase is
applied in the RICO setting, a government's claim for its law enforcement a=
nd
health care expenditures would not qualify as injury to property.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B60000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hawaii v.
Standard Oil Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 405 U.S. 25=
1,
92 S. Ct. 885, 31 L. Ed. 2d 184 (1972)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, the State of Haw=
aii
sued four petroleum suppliers for antitrust violations. Hawaii raised claim=
s in
three capacities: a claim in the state's proprietary capacity for overcharg=
es
it paid directly as a consumer of petroleum products; a claim in its parens
patriae capacity as a representative of the state's citizens; and a class
action claim on behalf of all consumers in the state. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BH0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0B70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 253-56</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BH0000400_2"></a>To support the claims brou=
ght in
the state's parens patriae capacity, Hawaii alleged various harms,
&nbsp;[*978]&nbsp; including loss of its citizens' revenues, increases in t=
axes
as a result of lost revenues, lost opportunities in commerce, and frustrati=
on
of state measures to promote its citizens' welfare. &nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;</spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 255</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court held that the state, acting in a parens patriae capacity to redre=
ss
injury to its general economy, had not been injured in its property and thus
lacked standing to recover damages under <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>&sect;
4</i> of the Clayton Act. The Court stated: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Like
the lower courts that have considered the meaning of the words 'business or
property,' we conclude that they refer to commercial interests or enterpris=
es. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BK0000400"></a>When the State seeks damages=
 for
injuries to its commercial interests, it may sue under <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>&sect; 4</i>. But where, as here, the State seeks damages for other
injuries, it is not properly within the Clayton Act. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BK0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 264</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citations omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX3X78000RGTW000461"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court also relied on <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>15 U.S.C. &sect; 15a</i>, an adjacent
provision permitting the federal government to recover damages whenever
&quot;injured in its business or property&quot; by an antitrust violation. =
That
provision, the Court wrote, did not permit the United States to &quot;recov=
er
for economic injuries to its sovereign interests, as opposed to its proprie=
tary
functions.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D9N0-003B-S4BG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 265</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Similarly, the Court held, &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>[&=
sect;
] 4</i>, which uses identical language, does not authorize recovery for
economic injuries to the sovereign interests of a State.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Later,
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reiter</i>, the Court
&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;clarified that states could claim to have been injured in
their property when alleging harm to their interests as consumers, despite =
the
reference in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hawaii</i> to the stat=
e's
&quot;commercial interests&quot;: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX422D000RGTW000462"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BX0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The phrase 'commercial interests'
was used [in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hawaii</i>] as a gener=
ic
reference to the interests of the State of Hawaii as a party to a commercial
transaction. This is apparent from <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Hawaii</i>'s
explicit reaffirmance of the rule of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Chattanooga
Foundry</i> and statement that, where injury to a state 'occurs in its capa=
city
as a consumer in the marketplace' through a 'payment of money wrongfully
induced,' treble damages are recoverable by a state under the Clayton Act. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BX0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reiter</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 442 U.S. at
341-42</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_7"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As used in the Clayton Act's pri=
vate
right of action, then, the phrase &quot;business or property&quot; excludes
states' interests in their sovereign or quasi-sovereign capacities, but does
include states' interests as ordinary marketplace actors. We believe that t=
his
interpretation of the phrase &quot;business or property&quot; should apply =
in
the context of a civil RICO claim, as well.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CB0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CD0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Second Circuit has already
applied <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hawaii</i> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reiter</i> in this way. In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0BY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Town of West
Hartford v. Operation Rescue</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 915 F.2d 92,
103-04 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, &nbsp;[**23]&nbsp;the court
considered a town's civil RICO claims against abortion protesters who had
demonstrated for two days at a local clinic. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CG0000400"></a>According to the town, the
protesters' activities amounted to extortion against the town under the Hob=
bs
Act and thus were a pattern of racketeering activity. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 95-98</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CG0000400_2"></a>The town alleged that the
protesters had limited the police department's ability to respond to other
public safety needs, and caused extraordinary police overtime wage expenses,
among other harms, all of which amounted to &quot;injury to the governmental
functions and property&quot; of the town. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 95-96</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CX0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The court first concluded that
extortion under the Hobbs Act required the &quot;obtaining of property from
another by wrongful means&quot; and that neither &quot;altered official
conduct&quot; nor overtime police expense qualified as property for this pu=
rpose.
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0D00000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 101-02</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). Therefore, the town had failed properly=
 to
allege any RICO predicate acts and there was &quot;no plausible basis for .=
 . .
assertion&quot; of the RICO claim. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0D00000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0D70000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0CY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 102-03</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0D70000400_2"></a>The court then analyzed wh=
ether
the town had &nbsp;[*979]&nbsp; alleged injury to its &quot;business or
property&quot; resulting from the RICO violation, as required for civil
&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;RICO standing. Relying on <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Hawaii</i>
and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Reiter</i> for the proposition =
that a
governmental entity may &quot;recover for such injury only when it functions
'as a party to a commercial transaction,' &quot; the Second Circuit held th=
at
&quot;[i]njuries of the sort asserted by the Town do not fall within the am=
bit
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0D10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 104</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
the Second Circuit held that the town's claimed injuries stemming from the
burden the protesters placed on the town's police department -- which inclu=
ded
expenditures for increased overtime costs -- did not qualify as injury to t=
he
town's business or property. Rather, these were injuries to the town's
sovereign or quasi-sovereign interests of the sort held non-compensable in =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Hawaii</i>. </span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn384' href=3D"#_ftn384" name=3D"_ftnref384" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX4MMP000RGTW000466"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0GH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The injuries claimed
&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;by the County in this case, increased expenditures for law
enforcement and health care services, are analogous to those that the Second
Circuit ruled non-compensable in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0GG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2270-003B-53GM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Town of West
Hartford</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The County sustained these injuries in its sovereign
and/or quasi-sovereign capacities, and may not claim the costs as damages to
its property for purposes of civil RICO standing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX49PP000RGTW000464"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0HG0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We note finally that the common =
law
doctrine barring government recovery of the costs of public safety services=
 in
tort supports our holding. The rationale underlying the &quot;municipal cost
recovery rule&quot; is twofold: (1) that there is little reason for courts =
to
use tort law to unsettle expectations and disrupt the existing, tax-payer
funded system of providing public safety services; and (2) that it is not t=
he
courts' role to disturb the legislature's decision to fund such services as=
 a
part of its overall fiscal policy choices. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0HJ0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0KW0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0HF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>City of Flags=
taff</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y7W0-003B-G332-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 719 F.2d at
323-24</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0KW0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0HJ0000400_2"></a>A number of <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc18_7"></a>courts have applied this rule to bar
recovery by local governments of public safety expenses. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn385' href=3D"#_ftn385" name=3D"_ftnref385" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*980]&nbsp;
In this instance, we are not dealing with state common law, but with a
statutory cause of action created by Congress. Therefore, we are not concer=
ned
that our court might upset the local legislative body's fiscal policy by
allowing recovery for public safety services. Instead, the question is one =
of
Congress' intent, specifically whether Congress meant to disrupt settled
expectations and alter the legislatively-chosen system of funding local
government services. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0MW0000400"></a>We
recognize that Congress could have used its powers to do so, enabling local
governments to pursue treble damages under RICO for injuries arising from t=
heir
provision of governmental services. But had Congress &nbsp;[**28]&nbsp;inte=
nded
such a result, we believe that Congress would have been more explicit; the =
term
&quot;business or property&quot; does not readily connote a government's
interest in the services it provides in its sovereign or quasi-sovereign
capacity. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn386' href=3D"#_ftn386"
name=3D"_ftnref386" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> As we think it
unlikely that Congress intended this type of recovery under RICO, and becau=
se
Idaho's legislature has assigned the fiscal burden for law enforcement and
health care services differently, it is not our place to disturb these
decisions. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Contrary
to the County's argument, in holding that the costs of their law enforcement
and public health care services are not recoverable damages under civil RIC=
O,
we are not inserting &nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;an additional injury requirement int=
o </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Rather, our holding is based on the statutory language itself. We simply do=
 not
think that the term &quot;business or property&quot; can be interpreted to
encompass the sorts of interests that the County relies on in this case.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
light of the foregoing reasoning, we conclude that the County lacks RICO
standing and cannot bring suit under RICO for the injuries that it asserts =
in
its complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Canyon County Cannot Show Th=
at
the Alleged RICO Violations Proximately Caused Its Injuries</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P30000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Even if the County's claimed
injuries were cognizable under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> as
injuries to property, the County's complaint is flawed in another critical
aspect: most of the defendants' alleged RICO violations do not bear a direct
connection to the County's asserted harms. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0N00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 126 S. Ct. =
at
1998</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_7"></a>When a=
 court
evaluates a RICO claim for proximate causation, the central question it must
ask is whether the alleged violation led directly to the plaintiff's
injuries.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A.
The County's Claims Against the Defendant Companies</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Canyon
County alleges that it &quot;has paid millions of dollars for health care
services and criminal justice services for the illegal immigrants who have
&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp;been employed by the defendants in violation of federal
law.&quot; We conclude that the County cannot, as a matter of law, show an
adequate causal nexus between the four defendant companies' employment of
undocumented workers and the financial harm the County claims to have suffe=
red.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*981]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P50000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20_6"></a>A &quot;showing that the defendant
violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the plaintiff was injured, and the defendant's violation was a 'but for' ca=
use
of plaintiff's injury&quot; is insufficient to meet the requirement in </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
that the plaintiff's injury be &quot;by reason of&quot; the RICO violation.=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P70000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at
265-66</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Rather, a plaintiff must also show that the defendant's
RICO violation proximately caused her injury. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P70000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0P60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 268</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Proximate causation requires &quot;some direct relation between the injury
asserted and the injurious conduct alleged.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R10000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Holmes</i>, the Court applied the proximate cause requirement to
preclude a RICO suit by a plaintiff whose injury was entirely contingent on=
 the
injury of direct victims. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R000004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 271-74</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R30000400"></a>In that case, a number of
conspirators had allegedly engaged in a fraudulent stock manipulation scheme
which led to the insolvency of two securities broker-dealers. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R30000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RP0000400"></a>As a result of the insolvenc=
y, the
broker-dealers &nbsp;[**31]&nbsp;could no longer meet their obligations to
their customers, and the plaintiff, the Securities Investor Protection
Corporation (&quot;SIPC&quot;), was forced to cover the broker-dealers' deb=
ts. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 262-63</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RP0000400_2"></a>SIPC asserted the
broker-dealers' customers' claims against the conspirators, arguing that the
customers had been injured &quot;by reason of&quot; the conspirators'
fraudulent scheme in violation of RICO. The Court disagreed, noting that
&quot;the conspirators have allegedly injured these customers only insofar =
as
the stock manipulation first injured the broker-dealers and left them witho=
ut
the wherewithal to pay customers' claims.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0R40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 271</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Finding &quot;the link . . . too remote between the stock manipulation alle=
ged
and the customers' harm, being purely contingent on the harm suffered by the
broker-dealers,&quot; the Court concluded that proximate causation was lack=
ing.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RS0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Subsequently, in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i>, the Supreme Court clarified =
that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21_6"></a>the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Holmes</i>
proximate cause requirement not only bars RICO suits by derivative victims,=
 or
those whose injuries are &quot;purely contingent on the harm suffered by&qu=
ot;
direct victims, but generally precludes recovery by those whose injuries are
only tenuously related to the RICO violation at &nbsp;[**32]&nbsp;issue. </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>126 S. Ct. at
1996</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. Under <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i>,
courts must scrutinize the causal link between the RICO violation and the
injury, identifying with precision both the nature of the violation and the
cause of the injury to the plaintiff. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0RT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1996-98</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Where the violation is not itself the immediate cause of the plaintiff's
injury, proximate cause may be lacking.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0SF0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0SH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Anza</i>, Ideal Steel Supply Company sued its competitor, alleging =
that
the competitor had violated RICO by conducting its business through a patte=
rn
of defrauding the State of New York of sales tax payments. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VC0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0SD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1994</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
According to Ideal, the defendant was able to undersell Ideal by not chargi=
ng
sales tax on cash purchases, and thus deprived Ideal of sales it otherwise
would have made. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0SG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1994-95, =
1997</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VC0000400_2"></a>The Court concluded, howev=
er,
that the competitor's alleged violations could not have proximately caused
Ideal's injuries, because &quot;[t]he cause of Ideal's asserted harms . . .=
 is
a set of actions (offering lower prices) entirely distinct from the alleged
RICO violation (defrauding the State).&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0SJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1997</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In support of this conclusion, t=
he
Court discussed the rationale for the requirement that the plaintiff's harm
directly result &nbsp;[**33]&nbsp;from the alleged RICO violation. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at
269-270</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>). First, the Court cited &quot;the difficulty that can
arise when a court attempts to ascertain the damages caused by some remote
action.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> The Court
emphasized the attenuated causal chain between the defendant's tax fraud and
the &nbsp;[*982]&nbsp; plaintiff's loss of sales. It noted the difficulty of
determining whether the defendant's lower prices were in fact based on the
defendant's fraudulent failure to pay sales tax, or whether other causes
determined the defendant's pricing. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>Id.</i>
The Court also pointed out that the plaintiff's lost sales could have resul=
ted
from a host of factors other than its competitor's fraud. <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, the Court discussed
&quot;the speculative nature of the proceedings that would follow if Ideal =
were
permitted to maintain its claim.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0VG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1998</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. A
court would have to determine the portion of Ideal's damages resulting from=
 the
RICO violation, by evaluating the relative causal role of the defendant's f=
raud
in lowering the defendant's prices, and the relative causal role of those
lowered prices in diminishing Ideal's sales -- in effect, requiring a compl=
ex
apportionment of fault among various causes. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Id.</i> The &nbsp;[**34]&nbsp;proximate causation requirement &quot=
;is
meant to prevent these types of intricate, uncertain inquiries from overrun=
ning
RICO litigation.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the Court referenced the consideration of whether &quot;the immediate victi=
ms
of an alleged RICO violation can be expected to vindicate the laws by pursu=
ing
their own claims.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> In =
the
case before the Court, the State of New York could be expected to pursue its
own remedies for the fraud practiced upon it by the defendant. Thus, the Co=
urt
found &quot;no need to broaden the universe of actionable harms to permit R=
ICO
suits by parties who have been injured only indirectly.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX4SFV000RGTW000467"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0X10000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0X00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
we must determine whether the County meets the proximate cause requirement =
by
examining &quot;whether the alleged violation led directly to the plaintiff=
's
injuries.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn387' href=3D"#_ftn387" name=3D"_ftnref387" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The basis of the RICO violation, according to the County's complaint, is the
defendant companies' knowing hiring of undocumented immigrants. The alleged
harm is the County's increased expenditures on health care and criminal jus=
tice
services. Here, just as in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i>=
, the
cause of the plaintiff's asserted harms is a set of actions (increased dema=
nd
by people within Canyon County for public health care &nbsp;[**35]&nbsp;and=
 law
enforcement services) entirely distinct from the alleged RICO violation (the
defendants' knowing hiring of undocumented workers). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX4GTJ000RGTW000465"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0YJ0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The three rationales for the
proximate cause requirement described in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Anza</i>
also suggest that the County's harm fails the proximate cause test. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ0YH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1997</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
As to the first consideration, the difficulty of assessing causation, the
asserted causal chain in this instance is quite attenuated, and there
&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp;are numerous other factors that could lead to higher
expenditures by the County. In fact, it is not clear how the companies' hir=
ing
of undocumented immigrants would increase demand for health care and law
enforcement within Canyon County. Further, holding employers liable for the
actions of their employees that are not even recognized as being a basis fo=
r respondent
superior liability would be contrary to centuries of precedent concerning
proximate causation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*983]&nbsp;
The causal chain would also be difficult to ascertain because there are
numerous alternative causes that might be the actual source or sources of t=
he
County's alleged harm. Increased demand for public health care and law
enforcement may result from such varied factors as: demographic changes;
alterations in criminal laws or policy; changes in public health practices;
shifts in economic variables such as wages, insurance coverage, and
unemployment; and improved community education and outreach by government.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ10H0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, the proceedings required=
 to
evaluate the County's injury would be speculative in the extreme, perhaps m=
ore
so than those discussed in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i>=
. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ10G0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1998</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. A
court would be forced to evaluate the extent to which the
&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp;companies' illegal hiring practices had created increased
demand for County services. This would not consist of simply estimating the
number of undocumented immigrants employed by the companies and their avera=
ge
usage of County services. Rather, the court would have to construct the
alternative scenario of what would have occurred had the companies employed
legally authorized workers, and determine how this might have affected the
County's total population, and how these alternative workers might have
differed from the undocumented workers in their consumption of County servi=
ces,
if at all. This would be an &quot;intricate, uncertain&quot; inquiry of the
type that the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i> Court warned
against. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ10K0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11J0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Given the substantial -- and fat=
al
-- shortcomings of the complaint in meeting <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Anza</i>'s
proximate cause requirements, we need not inquire into the question of whet=
her
there are more immediate victims of the defendants' alleged RICO violations=
 who
are likely to sue. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ10J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4RNG-82F0-TXFX-D2PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Newcal Indus.,
Inc. v. IKON Office Solution</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4RNG-82F0-TXFX-D2PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 513 F.3d 10=
38,
2008 WL 185520, at *13 (9th Cir. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn388' href=3D"#_ftn388" name=3D"_ftnref388" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County has attempted to limit <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza<=
/i>'s
reach, characterizing the holding as applying only to &quot;derivatively
injured plaintiffs.&quot; Because the County's injury is not &quot;derivati=
ve
of an injury suffered by any other party,&quot; the County asserts that <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i> is inapplicable.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0MSBYX4X90000RGTW0004RM"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11M0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We are, however, unpersuaded by =
the
County's attempt to distinguish <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anz=
a</i>
as a &quot;derivative or passed-on&quot; injury case. It is true that the C=
ourt
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> dealt with derivative
injury, in the sense that any harm to the plaintiff occurred only to the ex=
tent
that a direct victim's injuries were &quot;passed on,&quot; or flowed throu=
gh
another victim. But there was no such &quot;passed-on&quot; harm in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Anza</i>: the plaintiff alleged that i=
t lost
business due to the defendant's lowered prices, a harm which was distinct f=
rom
and not contingent on the harm suffered by the state due to the defendant's
sales tax fraud. Thus, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24_4"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11M0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11K0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies fully to cases like this one, where the harm to the plaintiff from =
the
defendant's RICO violation &nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;does not flow through any
intervening victims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County's claims against the defendant companies fail for lack of proximate
causation. The asserted link between the companies' hiring practices and
increased demand for County services is far too attenuated.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.
The County's Claim Against Pacheco</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
County's claim against Albert Pacheco, the former Executive Director of the
Idaho Migrant Council, is founded on &nbsp;[*984]&nbsp; two specific factual
allegations: first, that Pacheco directed his staff to assist undocumented
immigrants in filing false applications for the County's indigent medical
assistance fund, which cost the County money; and second, that Pacheco dire=
cted
his staff to assist undocumented immigrants in procuring public housing, and
that those immigrants subsequently burdened the County's public assistance =
and
criminal justice systems. As we noted above, neither of these allegations
supports a claim that the County was injured in its &quot;property,&quot; a=
nd
so the claim against Pacheco was properly dismissed for that reason. The se=
cond
allegation against Pacheco, that he assisted immigrants in securing housing,
suffers from an additional flaw: lack of proximate causation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11P0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Here, as in <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>Anza</i>, the defendant's &nbsp;[**40]&nbsp;alleged RICO violation
(assisting undocumented immigrants in securing housing) is distinct from the
cause of the plaintiff's harm (increased demand placing a burden on Canyon
County's public assistance and criminal justice systems). Further, the
considerations cited in Anza demonstrate that proximate cause is absent. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4S7XT6C0K1MNJ11N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1997-98</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The causal chain between the RICO violation and the plaintiff's harm is dub=
ious
for any number of reasons: for example, the immigrants might have secured
public housing without Pacheco's staff's assistance, and the immigrants mig=
ht
have remained in the County even without being able to occupy public housin=
g.
It is even more attenuated to postulate that having the benefit of public
housing made the immigrants more prone to commit crimes, require health car=
e,
or otherwise increase their use of County services. Given the speculative
nature of the causal links between the alleged harm and Pacheco's actions, a
court attempting to identify the specific portion of the County's financial
loss caused by Pacheco's actions would be sorely pressed to do so. Finally,=
 to
the extent that Pacheco's actions were indeed unlawful, the most direct vic=
tim
is the public &nbsp;[**41]&nbsp;housing authority itself; there is little n=
eed
to allow the County to pursue this RICO claim in order to vindicate the law=
s.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
we conclude that proximate causation is lacking as to this portion of the
County's claim against Pacheco.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
conclude that the County lacks statutory standing under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> to proceed with its federal RICO claims against the four
defendant companies. First, the County has failed to plead that it has been
&quot;injured in [its] business or property&quot; by reason of the defendan=
ts'
alleged RICO violations. Second, the County cannot show that its claimed
injuries were proximately caused &quot;by reason of&quot; the defendant
companies' alleged RICO violations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for the County's claim against the individual defendant, Pacheco, the County
lacks standing to pursue this claim as well: the County has not pled injury=
 to
its business or property, and proximate causation is lacking as to at least=
 a
portion of Pacheco's alleged RICO violations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal27 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a consequence, the judgment of the district court dismissing the County's
federal RICO claims is <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>AFFIRMED.</=
b></span></p>

