mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[***724]&nbsp; The Rackete=
er
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO) prohibits certain conduct
involving a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, and makes a private right of action available to
&quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by reason of a
violation&quot; of RICO's substantive restrictions, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
provided that the alleged violation was the proximate cause of the injury, =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Securities
Investor Protection Corporation,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 503 U.S. 258,
268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Respondent Ideal Steel Supply
Corporation (Ideal) has stores in Queens and the Bronx. &nbsp;[****2]&nbsp;
Petitioner National Steel Supply, Inc. (National), owned by petitioners Jos=
eph
and Vincent Anza, has stores in the same locations and is Ideal's principal
competitor. Ideal filed suit in the District Court, claiming that National
failed to charge New York's sales tax to cash-paying customers, allowing it=
 to
reduce its prices without affecting its profit margin; and that it submitted
fraudulent state &nbsp;[***725]&nbsp; tax returns to conceal the conduct, w=
hich
involved committing mail and wire fraud, both forms of &quot;racketeering
activity&quot; under RICO.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Id=
eal
alleged that the Anzas violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which forbids conducting or participating in the conduct of an enterprise's
affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity. It also claimed that all
the petitioners violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
--which makes it unlawful for a person &quot;to use or invest&quot; income
derived from a pattern of racketeering activity in an enterprise engaged in=
 or
affecting interstate or foreign commerce--when they used funds generated by=
 the
fraudulent tax scheme to open National's Bronx location, causing Ideal to l=
ose
business and market share.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The
District Court granted petitioners' motion to dismiss under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>,&nbsp;[****3]&nbsp; concluding =
that
Ideal had not shown reliance on petitioners' misrepresentations, as require=
d in
RICO mail and wire fraud claims.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Vacating, the Second Circuit held, with regard to the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim, that a complaint alleging a pattern of racketeering activity designe=
d to
give a defendant a competitive advantage adequately pleaded proximate cause
even where the scheme depended on fraudulent communications made to a third
party; and held that Ideal adequately pleaded its </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim by alleging injury resulting from petitioners' use and investment of
racketeering proceeds. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_21"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><s=
pan
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Held:</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_22"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. Ideal cannot maintain its </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Under <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Holmes,</i> proximate cause for </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
purposes requires &quot;some direct relation between the injury asserted and
the injurious conduct alleged.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, =
117 L.
Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The dire=
ct
victim of the alleged RICO violation is the State of New York, not Ideal.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Ideal's claim is too attenuated to
satisfy <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>' requirement of
directness. This result is confirmed by the directness requirement's underl=
ying
premises, one of which is the difficulty that can arise when a court attemp=
ts
to ascertain the damages caused by some remote&nbsp;[****4]&nbsp; action.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Ideal claims lost sales because of
National's decreased prices, but National could have lowered prices for rea=
sons
unrelated to the asserted tax fraud, and Ideal's lost sales could have resu=
lted
from other factors as well.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>T=
he
attenuated connection between Ideal's injury and the Anzas' injurious condu=
ct
thus implicates fundamental concerns expressed in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Holmes.</i> Further illustrating the absence of proximate cause is =
the
speculative nature of the proceedings that would follow if Ideal were permi=
tted
to maintain its claim.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A court
would have to calculate the portion of National's price drop attributable to
the pattern of racketeering activity and then calculate the portion of lost
sales attributable to the relevant part of the price drop, but <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>' proximate causation elemen=
t was
meant to prevent such intricate, uncertain inquiries from overrunning RICO
litigation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A direct causal
connection is especially warranted where the immediate victims can be expec=
ted
to vindicate the laws by pursuing their own claims.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Contrary to the Second Circuit's
rationale, a RICO plaintiff cannot circumvent the proximate-cause requireme=
nt
simply by claiming that the defendant's aim was to&nbsp;[****5]&nbsp; incre=
ase
market share at a competitor's expense.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>Because Ideal has not satisfied that requirement, this Court has no
occasion to address the substantial &nbsp;[***726]&nbsp; question whether a
plaintiff asserting a RICO claim predicated on mail or wire fraud must show
that it relied on the defendant's misrepresentations. </span></p>

