<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Respondent
Ideal Steel Supply Corporation (Ideal) sells steel mill products along with
related supplies and services.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </spa=
n>It
operates two store locations in New York, one in Queens and the other in the
Bronx.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioner National St=
eel
Supply, &nbsp;[*454]&nbsp; Inc. (National), owned by petitioners Joseph and
Vincent Anza, is Ideal's principal competitor. National&nbsp;[****7]&nbsp;
offers a similar array of products and services, and it, too, operates one
store in Queens and one in the Bronx. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ideal
sued petitioners in the United States District Court for the Southern Distr=
ict
of New York.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It claimed petit=
ioners
were engaged in an unlawful racketeering scheme aimed at &quot;gain[ing] sa=
les
and market share at Ideal's expense.&quot; App. 7.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>According to Ideal, National adopt=
ed a
practice of failing to charge the requisite New York sales tax to cash-payi=
ng
customers, even when conducting transactions that were not exempt from sales
tax under state law.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>This pra=
ctice
allowed National to reduce its prices without affecting its profit margin.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners allegedly submitted
fraudulent tax returns to the New York State Department &nbsp;[***727]&nbsp=
; of
Taxation and Finance in an effort to conceal their conduct. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1995]&nbsp;
Ideal's amended complaint contains, as relevant here, two RICO claims.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The claims assert that petitioners=
, by
submitting the fraudulent tax returns, committed various acts of mail fraud=
 (when
they sent the returns by mail) and wire fraud (when they sent them
electronically).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1343 (2000 ed=
.,
Supp. III)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_28"></a>Mail fraud and wire fraud are forms =
of
&quot;racketeering&nbsp;[****8]&nbsp; activity&quot; for purposes of RICO.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)(B)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners' conduct allegedly
constituted a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity,&quot; see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(5=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed.), because the fraudulent returns were submitted on an ongoing and
regular basis. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ideal
asserts in its first cause of action that Joseph and Vincent Anza violated =
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_28"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which makes it unlawful for &quot;any person employed by or associated with=
 any
enterprise engaged in, or the activities of which affect, interstate or for=
eign
commerce, to conduct or participate, directly or indirectly, in the conduct=
 of
such enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity or
collection of unlawful debt.&quot; The complaint states that the Anzas' goa=
l,
which &nbsp;[*455]&nbsp; they achieved, was to give National a competitive
advantage over Ideal. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
second cause of action is asserted against all three petitioners.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It alleges a violation of </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_25"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which makes it unlawful for any person who has received income derived from=
 a
pattern of racketeering activity &quot;to use or invest&quot; that income
&quot;in acquisition of any interest in, or the establishment or operation
of,&quot; an enterprise engaged in or affecting interstate or foreign
commerce.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As described in
the&nbsp;[****9]&nbsp; complaint, petitioners used funds generated by their
fraudulent tax scheme to open National's Bronx location.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The opening of this new facility c=
aused
Ideal to lose &quot;significant business and market share.&quot; App. 18. <=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Petitioners
moved to dismiss Ideal's complaint under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rules=
 of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9(b)</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The District Court granted the </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion, holding that the complaint failed to state a claim upon which relief
could be granted.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The court b=
egan
from the proposition that to assert a RICO claim predicated on mail fraud or
wire fraud, a plaintiff must have relied on the defendant's misrepresentati=
ons.
Ideal not having alleged that it relied on petitioners' false tax returns, =
the
court concluded Ideal could not go forward with its RICO claims. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ0YT0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ0YW0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Ideal appealed, and the Court of
Appeals for the Second Circuit vacated the District Court's judgment.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ0XH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CS5-W8G0-0038-X04X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>373 F.3d 251 (2004)</span></i></a><=
span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Addressing Ideal's </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim, the court held that where a complaint alleges a pattern of racketeer=
ing
activity &quot;that was intended to and did give the defendant a competitive
advantage over the plaintiff, the complaint adequately pleads proximate cau=
se,
and the plaintiff has standing&nbsp;[****10]&nbsp; to pursue a civil RICO
claim.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ0YV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CS5-W8G0-0038-X04X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CS5-W8G0-0038-X04X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CS5-W8G0-0038-X04X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 263</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>This is the case, the court explai=
ned,
&quot;even where the scheme depended on fraudulent communications directed =
to
and relied on by a third party rather than the plaintiff.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ0YY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The court reached the same
conclusion with respect to Ideal's </span><a
