<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Our
analysis begins--and, as will become evident, largely ends--with <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes.</i> &nbsp;[**1996]&nbsp; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ10M0000400"></a>That case arose from a compl=
aint
filed by the Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC), a private
corporation with a duty to reimburse the customers of registered broker-dea=
lers
who became unable to meet their financial obligations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>SIPC claimed that the petitioner, =
Robert
Holmes, conspired with others to manipulate stock prices.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>When the market detected the fraud=
, the
share prices plummeted, and the &quot;decline&nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; caused [t=
wo]
broker-dealers' financial difficulties resulting in their eventual liquidat=
ion
and SIPC's advance of nearly $13 million to cover their customers'
claims.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ10K0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 262, 263, 112 S. Ct. 1=
311,
117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>SIPC sued on several theories, inc=
luding
that Holmes participated in the conduct of an enterprise's affairs through a
pattern of racketeering activity in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and conspired to do so in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(d=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_2_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>[1B] The Court held that SIPC co=
uld
not maintain its RICO claims against Holmes for his alleged role in the sch=
eme.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1170000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The decision relied on a careful
interpretation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which provides a civil cause of action to persons injured &quot;by reason
of&quot; a defendant's RICO violation. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1190000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The Court recognized the phrase &qu=
ot;by
reason of&quot; could be read broadly to require merely that the claimed
violation was a &quot;but for&quot; cause of the plaintiff's injury.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ10N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 265-266,=
 112
S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Ti=
mes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1190000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>It rejected this reading, however, =
noting
the &quot;unlikelihood that Congress meant to allow all factually injured
plaintiffs to recover.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ11800=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
