mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Securities
Investor Protection Corporation</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. 25=
8,
268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
on which the Court principally relies.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>The Court's stringent proximate-causation requirement succeeds in
precluding recovery in cases alleging a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
that, like the present one, have nothing to do with organized crime, the ta=
rget
of the RICO statute.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>However,=
 the
Court's approach&nbsp;[**2000]&nbsp; also eliminates recovery for plaintiffs
whose injuries are precisely those that Congress aimed to remedy through the
authorization of civil RICO suits.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Because this frustration of congressional intent&nbsp;[****23]&nbsp;=
 is
directly contrary to the broad language Congress employed to confer a RICO
cause of action, I respectfully dissent from Part II of the Court's opinion=
. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
language of the civil RICO provision, which broadly permits recovery by
&quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by reason of a
violation&quot; of the Act's substantive restrictions, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed.), plainly covers the lawsuit brought by respondent. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P4P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ18N0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Respondent alleges that it was inju=
red in
its business, and that this injury was the direct result of petitioners'
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn390' href=3D"#_ftn390" name=3D"_ftnref390" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
App. 12-17.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In &nbsp;[*464]&n=
bsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, however, we held that a RI=
CO
plaintiff is required &nbsp;[***733]&nbsp; to show that the RICO violation
&quot;not only was a 'but for' cause of his injury, but was the proximate c=
ause
as well.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ18M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, =
117 L.
Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We emplo=
yed
the term &quot;'proximate cause' to label generically the judicial tools us=
ed
to limit a person's responsibility for the consequences of that person's own
acts.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i> These tools r=
eflect
&quot;'ideas of what justice demands, or of what is administratively possib=
le
and convenient.'&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i> (q=
uoting
W. Keeton, D. Dobbs, R. Keeton, &amp; D. Owen, Prosser and Keeton on Law of
Torts &sect; 41, p 264 (5th ed. &nbsp;[****24]&nbsp; 1984) (hereinafter Pro=
sser
&amp; Keeton)). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SB0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P4R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1980000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Invoking one of the common-law
proximate-cause considerations, we held that a RICO plaintiff must prove
&quot;some direct relation between the injury asserted and the injurious
conduct alleged.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ18P0000400"=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, =
117 L.
Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Today the
Court applies this formulation to conclude that the &quot;attenuated
relationship&quot; between the violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and Ideal's injury &quot;implicates fundamental concerns expressed in
Holmes&quot; and that the absence of proximate causation is equally clear in
both cases. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1S90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ante</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at ____, 16=
4 L.
Ed. 2d, at 729</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But the
Court's determination&nbsp;[****25]&nbsp; relies on a theory of
&quot;directness&quot; distinct from that adopted by <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1980000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Holmes.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P4T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ19B0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Holmes</i>, the Court explained that &quot;a plaintiff who complain=
ed
of harm flowing merely from the misfortunes visited upon a third person by =
the
defendant's acts was generally said to stand at too remote a distance to
recover.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1990000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268-269, 112 S. Ct. 13=
11,
117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The plaintiff in <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Holmes</i> was indirect in precisely this sense.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The defendant was alleged to have
participated in a stock manipulation scheme that disabled two broker-dealers
from meeting their obligations to customers. Accordingly, the plaintiff,
Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC), had to advance nearly $13
million to cover the claims of customers of those broker-dealers. SIPC
attempted to sue based on the claim that it was subrogated to the rights of
those customers of the broker-dealers who did not purchase manipulated
securities. <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P4V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ19D0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>We held that &nbsp;[*465]&nbsp; the
nonpurchasing customers' injury was not proximately caused by the defendant=
's
conduct, because &quot;the conspirators have allegedly injured these custom=
ers
only insofar as the stock manipulation first injured the broker-dealers and
left them without the wherewithal to pay customers' claims.
&nbsp;[****26]&nbsp; &quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ19C000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 271, 112 =
S.
Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn391' href=3D"#_ftn391" name=3D"_ftnref391" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SD0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[**2001]&nbsp; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P4W"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1B10000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SD0000400_2"></a>Here, in contrast, it was =
not <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>New York's injury</i> that caused
respondent's damages; rather, it was petitioners' own conduct--namely, their
underpayment of tax--that permitted them to undercut respondent's prices and
thereby take away its business.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Indeed, the Court's acknowledgment that there is no appreciable
&nbsp;[***734]&nbsp; risk of duplicative recovery here, in contrast to<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'> Holmes,</i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>ante,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at ____, 164=
 L.
Ed. 2d, at 730</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, is effectively a concession that petitioners'
damages&nbsp;[****27]&nbsp; are not indirect, as that term is used in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes.</i> See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1B00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 269, 112 S. Ct. 1311, =
117 L.
Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;[R]ecognizing claims of the indirectly injured w=
ould
force courts to adopt complicated rules apportioning damages among plaintif=
fs
removed at different levels of injury from the violative acts, to obviate t=
he
risk of multiple recoveries&quot;).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The mere fact that New York is a direct victim of petitioners' RICO
violation does not preclude Ideal's claim that it too is a direct victim.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Because the petitioners' tax
underpayment directly caused respondent's injury, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Holmes</i> does not bar respondent's recovery. </span></p>

