mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 268, 269,=
 112
S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Ti=
mes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Cf. Prosser &amp; Keeton &sect;
42, at 273 (noting &quot;two contrasting theories of legal cause,&quot; one
extending liability to, but not beyond, &quot;the scope of the 'foreseeable
risks,'&quot; and the other extending liability to, but not beyond, all
&quot;'directly traceable'&quot; consequences &nbsp;[*470]&nbsp; and those
indirect consequences that are foreseeable). </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn395' href=3D"#_ftn395" name=3D"_ftnref395" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The proximate-cause limitation serves to ensure that &quot;a defendant is n=
ot
answerable for anything beyond the natural, ordinary and reasonable
consequences of his conduct.&quot; Sutherland 57.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&quot;If one's fault happens to co=
ncur
with something extraordinary, and therefore not likely to be foreseen, he w=
ill
not be answerable for such unexpected result.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Ibid.</i> Based on this principle, courts have&nbsp;[****35]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[***737]&nbsp; historically found proximate causation for injuries fr=
om
natural causes, if a wrongful act &quot;rendered it probable that such an
injury will occur,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i>, a=
t 62;
for injuries where the plaintiff's reliance is the immediate cause, such as=
 in
an action for fraud, so long as the reliance was &quot;reasonably induced by
the prior misconduct of the defendant,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>id.</i>, at 62, 63; and for injuries where an innocent third party
intervenes between the tortfeasor and the&nbsp;[**2004]&nbsp; victim, such =
that
the innocent third party is the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>imm=
ediate</i>
cause of the injury, so long as the tortfeasor &quot;contributed so effectu=
ally
to [the injury] as to be regarded as the efficient or at least concurrent a=
nd
