serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, into which federal courts had
implied a proximate-cause limitation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1KJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, =
117 L.
Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Accordin=
gly,
it was fair to interpret the broad language &quot;by&nbsp;[**2008]&nbsp; re=
ason
of&quot; as meaning, in all civil RICO cases, that the violation must be bo=
th
the cause-in-fact and the proximate cause of the plaintiff's injury. </span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P67"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1M10000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Here, by contrast, the civil act=
ion
provision cannot be read to always require that the plaintiff have relied on
the defendant's action.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Relia=
nce is
not a general limitation on civil recovery in tort; it &quot;is a specializ=
ed
condition that happens to have grown up with common law fraud.&quot; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1KM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loiselle, sup=
ra</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 104</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>For most of the predicate acts
underlying RICO violations, it cannot be argued that the common law, if it =
even
recognized such acts as civilly actionable, required proof of reliance.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed., Supp. III).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In oth=
er
words, there is no&nbsp;[****49]&nbsp; language in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed.) that could fairly be read to add a reliance requirement in fraud
cases only.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Nor is there any =
reason
to believe that Congress would have defined &quot;racketeering activity&quo=
t;
to include acts indictable under the mail and wire fraud statutes, if it
intended fraud-related acts to be predicate acts under RICO only
&nbsp;[*478]&nbsp; when those acts would have been actionable under the com=
mon
law. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
reliance cannot be read into </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect; =
1341</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1343</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
nor into RICO itself, it is not an element of a civil RICO claim. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P68"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1M30000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>This is not to say that,
&nbsp;[***742]&nbsp; in the general case, a plaintiff will not have to prove
that someone relied on the predicate act of fraud as part of his case.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If, for example, New York had not
believed petitioners' misrepresentation with respect to their sales, Ideal =
may
well not have been injured by petitioners' scheme, which would have faltere=
d at
the first step.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, petit=
ioners
recognize that &quot;in the ordinary misrepresentation case, the reliance
requirement simply functions as a necessary prerequisite to establishing the
causation required by the language of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
Brief for Petitioners 29.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But=
 the
fact that proof of reliance is often used to prove an element of
the&nbsp;[****50]&nbsp; plaintiff's cause of action, such as the element of
causation, does not transform reliance itself into an element of the cause =
of
action. See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1M20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loiselle, sup=
ra</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 104</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;Reliance is doubtless the most obvious way in which fraud can cause
harm, but it is not the only way&quot;).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span>Because respondent need not allege reliance at all, its complaint, w=
hich
alleges that New York relied on petitioners' misrepresentations, App. 16, is
more than sufficient. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>* *
* </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P69"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1M50000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6B"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1MK0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Congress that enacted RICO m=
ay
never have intended to reach cases like the one before us, and may have
&quot;federalize[d] a great deal of state common law&quot; without any
intention of &quot;produc[ing] these far-reaching results.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1M40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S., at
506, 105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Marshall, J.,
dissenting).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But this Court h=
as
always refused to ignore the language of the statute to limit it to &quot;t=
he
archetypal, intimidating mobster,&quot; and has instead recognized that
&quot;[i]t is not for the judiciary to eliminate the private action in
situations where Congress has provided it simply because plaintiffs are not
taking advantage of it in its more difficult applications.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1MJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., at 499-500,
105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:=
"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&nbsp;[*479]&nbsp; Today, however,=
 the
Court not only eliminates private&nbsp;[****51]&nbsp; RICO actions in some
situations Congress may have inadvertently regulated, but it substantially
limits the ability of civil RICO to reach even those cases that motivated
Congress' enactment of this provision in the first place.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>I respectfully dissent. Justice <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Breyer,</b> concurring in part and
dissenting in part. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
my view, the civil damages remedy in the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt
Organizations Act (RICO), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-1968 (2000 ed. and Supp. III)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
does not cover claims of injury by one&nbsp;[**2009]&nbsp; competitor where=
 the
legitimate pro-competitive activity of another competitor immediately causes
that injury.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>I believe that t=
his is
such a case and would consequently hold that RICO does not authorize the
private action here at issue. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>RICO
essentially seeks to prevent organized criminals from taking over or operat=
ing
legitimate businesses.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Its
language, however, extends its scope well beyond those central purposes.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>RICO begins by listing certain pre=
dicate
acts, called &quot;'racketeering activity,'&quot; which consist of other
crimes, ranging from criminal copyright activities, the facilitation of
gambling, and mail fraud to arson, kidnapping, and murder.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(2000
ed., Supp. III). &nbsp;[****52]&nbsp; It then defines a &quot;'pattern of
racketeering activity'&quot; to include engaging in &quot;at least two&quot;
predicate acts in a 10-year period.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(5=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed.).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And
&nbsp;[***743]&nbsp; it forbids certain business-related activities involvi=
ng
such a &quot;pattern&quot; and an &quot;enterprise.&quot; The forbidden
activities include using funds derived from a &quot;pattern of racketeering
activity&quot; in acquiring, establishing, or operating any enterprise, and
conducting the affairs of any enterprise through such &quot;a pattern.&quot=
; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;
1962(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(c)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*480]&nbsp;
RICO, a federal criminal statute, foresees criminal law enforcement by the
Federal Government.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1963</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed., Supp. III).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It als=
o sets
forth civil remedies.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2000 ed.).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>District courts
&quot;have jurisdiction to prevent and restrain [RICO] violations.&quot; </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And a person &quot;injured in his
business or property by reason of a [RICO] violation&quot; may seek treble
damages and attorney's fees.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
present case is a private RICO treble-damages action.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A steel supply company, Ideal Stee=
l, has
sued a competing steel supply company, National Steel, and its owners, Jose=
ph
and Vincent Anza (to whom I shall refer collectively as
&quot;National&quot;).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Ideal =
says
that National committed mail fraud&nbsp;[****53]&nbsp; by regularly filing
false New York state sales tax returns in order to avoid paying sales tax t=
hat
it owed--activity that amounts to a &quot;pattern of racketeering
activity.&quot; This activity enabled National to charge lower prices witho=
ut
reducing its profit margins.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
Ideal
says National used some of these excess profits to fund the building of a n=
ew
store.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Both the lower prices =
and
the new outlet attracted Ideal customers, thereby injuring Ideal.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Hence, says Ideal, it was injured
&quot;in [its] business . . . by reason of&quot; violations of two RICO
provisions, the provision that forbids conducting an &quot;enterprise's
affairs&quot; through a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; and the
provision that forbids investing funds derived from such a &quot;pattern&qu=
ot;
in an &quot;enterprise.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(a)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1964(c).</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The question before us is whether =
RICO
permits Ideal to bring this private treble-damages claim. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1MN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This Court, in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1MM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Securities
Investor Protection Corporation,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 503 U.S. 258,
268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
held that RICO's private treble-damages provision &quot;demand[ed] . . . so=
me
direct relation between the injury asserted and the injurious conduct
alleged.&quot; The Court then determined that the injury &nbsp;[*481]&nbsp;
alleged by&nbsp;[****54]&nbsp; the plaintiff in that case was too remote fr=
om
the injurious conduct to satisfy this requirement. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I
do not agree with the majority insofar as it believes that <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Holmes</i>' holding in respect to the fact pattern there at issue
virtually dictates the answer to the question here.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In my view, the &quot;causal
connection&quot; between the forbidden conduct and&nbsp;[**2010]&nbsp; plai=
ntiff's
harm is, in certain key ways, more direct here than it was in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes.</i> In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Holmes,</i> the RICO plaintiff was a surrogate for creditors of
broker-dealers that went bankrupt after losing money in stocks that had been
overvalued due to fraudulent statements made by the RICO defendant and
others.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Put in terms of
&quot;proximate cause,&quot; the plaintiff's harm (an ordinary creditor los=
s)
differed in kind from the harm that the &quot;predicate acts&quot; (securit=
ies
fraud) would ordinarily cause (stock-related monetary losses).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The harm was &quot;indirect&quot; =
in the
sense that it was entirely derivative of the more &nbsp;[***744]&nbsp; dire=
ct
harm the defendant's actions had caused the broker-dealers; and, there were
several steps between the violation and the harm
(misrepresentation--broker-dealer losses--broker-dealer business
failure--ordinary creditor loss). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SX0000=
400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Here, however, &nbsp;[****55]&nbsp;=
 the
plaintiff alleges a harm (lost customers) that flows directly from the lower
prices and the opening of a new outlet--actions that were themselves allege=
dly
caused by activity that Congress designed RICO to forbid (conducting a busi=
ness
through a &quot;pattern&quot; of &quot;predicate acts&quot; and investing in
business funds derived from such a &quot;pattern&quot;).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In this sense, the causal links be=
fore
us are more &quot;direct&quot; than those in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Holmes.</i> See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4KJNS0M0K1MNJ1SW0000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>ante,</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at ____ - __=
__,
164 L. Ed. 2d, at 733-734</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Thomas, J., concurring in part=
 and
dissenting in part). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1NF0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1NH0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Nonetheless, I agree with the
majority that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> points the
way.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That case makes clear th=
at
RICO contains important limitations on the scope of private rights of
action.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It specifies that RIC=
O does
not provide a private right of action &quot;simply on showing that the
defendant violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the plaintiff was injured, and the defendant's violation was a 'but for' ca=
use
of [the] plaintiff's injury.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1MP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 265-266, 112 S. Ct. 13=
11,
117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &nbsp;[*482]&nbsp; (footnote
omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P6H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1P10000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Pointing out &quot;the very unlikel=
ihood
that Congress meant to allow <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>all</i>
factually injured plaintiffs to recover,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1NG0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 266, 112=
 S.
Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (emphasis added), <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> concludes that RICO imposes=
 a
requirement of &quot;proximate&nbsp;[****56]&nbsp; cause,&quot; a phrase th=
at
&quot;label[s] generically the judicial tools used to limit a person's
responsibility for the consequences of that person's own acts,&quot; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1NJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 268, 112 =
S.
Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1P10000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>It recognizes that these tools seek=
 to
discern &quot;'what justice demands, or . . . what is administratively poss=
ible
and convenient.'&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i> (q=
uoting
W. Keeton, D. Dobbs, R. Keeton, &amp; D. Owen, Prosser and Keeton on Law of
Torts &sect; 41, p 264 (5th ed. 1984)).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>It also explains that &quot;proximate cause&quot; demands
&quot;directness,&quot; while specifying that &quot;directness&quot; is only
one of &quot;the many shapes this concept took at common law.&quot; </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1P00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>503 U.S., at 268, 269, 112 S. Ct. 1=
311,
117 L. Ed. 2d 532</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And it points to antitrust law, bo=
th as
a source of RICO's treble-damages provisions and as an aid to their
interpretation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
my view, the &quot;antitrust&quot; nature of the treble-damages provision's
source, taken together with both RICO's basic objectives and important
administrative concerns, implies that a cause is &quot;indirect,&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, it is not a &quot;proximate
cause,&quot; if the causal chain from forbidden act to the injury caused a
competitor proceeds through a legitimate business' ordinary competitive
activity.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>To use a physical m=
etaphor,
ordinary competitive&nbsp;[****57]&nbsp; actions undertaken by the defendant
competitor cut the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>direct</i> causa=
l link
between the plaintiff competitor's injuries and the forbidden acts. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
basic objective of antitrust law is to encourage the competitive process.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In particular, that law encourages
businesses to compete by offering lower prices, better products, better met=
hods
of production, and better systems of distribution.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:normal'>e.g.,</i>
1 P. Areeda &amp; H. Hovenkamp, Antitrust Law: An Analysis of Antitrust
Principles and Their Application P 100a, pp 3-4&nbsp;[**2011]&nbsp; (2d ed.
2000).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As I shall explain, th=
ese
principles suggest that RICO does not permit private action based solely up=
on
this &nbsp;[***745]&nbsp; competitive type of harm, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, &nbsp;[*483]&nbsp; harm a plaintiff suffers only because =
the
defendant was able to attract customers through normal competitive methods,
such as lower prices, better products, better methods of production, or bet=
ter
systems of distribution.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In s=
uch
cases, the harm falls outside the limits that RICO's private treble-damages
provision's &quot;proximate-cause&quot; requirement imposes.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In such cases the distance between=
 the
harm and the predicate acts that funded (or otherwise enabled) such ordinary
competitive activity is too distant.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The harm&nbsp;[****58]&nbsp; is not &quot;direct.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
the same time, those principles suggest that other types of competitive
injuries not within their protective ambit could lie within, not outside,
&quot;proximate-cause&quot; limits.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Where, for example, a RICO defendant attracts customers in ways that
involve illegitimate competitive means, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>e.g.,</i>
by threatening violence, a claim may still lie.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Claims involving RICO violations t=
hat
objectively target a particular competitor, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>e.g.,</i>
bribing an official to harass a competitor, could also be actionable. </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Several
considerations lead to this conclusion.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>First, I have found no case (outside the Second Circuit, from which =
this
case arose) in which a court has authorized a private treble-damages suit b=
ased
upon no more than a legitimate business' ordinary procompetitive activity (=
even
where financed by the proceeds of a RICO predicate act). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
