<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fifth,
without this limitation, RICO enforcement and basic antitrust policy could =
well
collide.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Firms losing the
competitive battle might find bases for a RICO attack on their more success=
ful
competitors in claimed misrepresentations or even comparatively minor misde=
eds
by that competitor. <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GN7B0406003RWD7P00P70"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1SY0000400"></a>Firms &nbsp;[*486]&nbsp; tha=
t fear
such treble-damages suits might hesitate to compete vigorously, particularl=
y in
concentrated industries where &nbsp;[***747]&nbsp; harm to a competitor is =
more
easily traced but where the consumer's need for&nbsp;[****63]&nbsp; vigorous
competition is particularly strong.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The ultimate victim of any such tendency to pull ordinary competitive
punches of course would be not the competing business, but the consumer.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Although Congress did not intend i=
ts
RICO treble-damages provision as a simple copy of the antitrust laws' simil=
ar
remedies, see,<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'> e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4K76R6C0K1MNJ1SX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, supra=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 498-499,=
 105
S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, there is no sound reason to
interpret RICO's treble-damages provision as if Congress intended to set it=
 and
its antitrust counterpart at cross-purposes. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
these reasons, I would read into the private treble-damages provision a
&quot;proximate-cause&quot; limitation that places outside the provision ha=
rms
that are traceable to&nbsp;[**2013]&nbsp; an unlawful act only through a fo=
rm
of legitimate competitive activity. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Applying
this approach to the present case, I would hold that neither of Ideal's cou=
nts
states a RICO private treble-damages claim.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>National is a legitimate business.
Another private plaintiff (the State of New York) is available.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The question is whether Ideal asse=
rts a
harm caused directly by something other than ordinary competitive activity,=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, lower prices, a better produ=
ct, a
better distribution system, or a better&nbsp;[****64]&nbsp; production meth=
od. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ideal's
second count claims injury caused by National's (1) having taken customers =
(2)
attracted by its new store (3) that it financed in part through profits
generated by the tax fraud scheme, and the financing is the relevant violat=
ion.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The opening of a distribution outl=
et is
a legitimate competitive activity.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>It benefits the firm that opens it by making it more convenient for
customers to purchase from that supplier. That ordinary competitive process=
 is
all the complaint describes.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
And
for the reasons I have given &nbsp;[*487]&nbsp; in Part II, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra,</i> I believe that the financin=
g of a
new store--even with funds generated by unlawful activities--is not suffici=
ent
to create a private cause of action as long as the activity funded amounts =
to
legitimate competitive activity.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Ideal must look for other remedies, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>e.g.</i>,
bringing the facts to the attention of the United States Attorney or the St=
ate
of New York. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Ideal's
first count presents a more difficult question.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It alleges that National filed fal=
se
sales tax returns to the State of New York.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As an action indictable under the
federal mail fraud statute, that action is a predicate act under RICO.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[****65]&nbsp; (2000 ed., Supp. III).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>National passed these savings on t=
o its
cash customers by not charging them sales tax, thereby attracting more cash
customers than it would have without the scheme.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Is this a form of injury caused, n=
ot by
ordinary competitive activity, but simply by the predicate act itself? </sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
my view, the answer to this question is &quot;no.&quot; The complaint alleg=
es
predicate acts that amount simply to the facts that National did not
&quot;charge&quot; or &quot;pay&quot; sales taxes or accurately
&quot;report&quot; sales figures to the State.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>National did not tell its customer=
s,
&quot;We shall not pay sales taxes.&quot; Rather, it simply charged the
customer a lower price, say, $100 rather than $100 plus $8 tax.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Consider a retailer who advertises=
 to
the customer a $100 table and adds, &quot;We pay all sales taxes.&quot; Suc=
h a
retailer &nbsp;[***748]&nbsp; is telling the customer that he will charge t=
he
customer a lower price by the amount of the tax, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, about $92.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
The
retailer implies that he, the retailer, will pay the tax to the State, taki=
ng
the requisite amount owed to the State from the $100 the customer paid for =
the
item. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
defendants here have done no more.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>They have in effect cut the price of the item by the amount of the
sales&nbsp;[****66]&nbsp; tax and then kept the money instead of passing it=
 on
to the State.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>They funded the=
 price
cut from the savings, but the &nbsp;[*488]&nbsp; source of the savings is, =
in
my view, beside the point as long as the price cut itself is legitimate.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>I can find nothing in the complain=
t that
suggests it is not. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal29 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
these reasons, I would reverse the decision of the Court of Appeals on both
counts.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

