<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_10_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[***1015]&nbsp;Each year t=
he
Cook County Treasurer's Office holds a public auction to sell its tax liens=
 on
delinquent taxpayers' property.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an>To
prevent any one buyer from obtaining a disproportionate share of the liens,=
 the
county adopted the &quot;Single, Simultaneous Bidder Rule&quot; (Rule), whi=
ch
requires each buyer to submit bids in its own name, prohibits a buyer from
using &quot;apparent agents, employees, or related entities&quot; to submit
simultaneous bids for the same parcel, and requires a registered bidder to
submit a sworn affidavit affirming its compliance with the Rule.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners and respondents regula=
rly
participate in the tax sales.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Respondents filed suit, alleging that petitioners fraudulently obtai=
ned
a disproportionate share of liens by filing false compliance attestations.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As relevant here, they claim that
petitioners violated and conspired to violate the Racketeer Influenced and
Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO) through a pattern of racketeering activity
involving mail fraud, which occurred when petitioners sent property owners
various notices required by Illinois law.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&=
nbsp;
</span>The District Court &nbsp;[****2]&nbsp;dismissed the RICO claims for =
lack
of standing, finding that respondents were not protected by the mail fraud
statute because they did not receive the alleged misrepresentations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Reversing, the Seventh Circuit bas=
ed
standing on the injury respondents suffered when they lost the chance to ob=
tain
more liens, and found that respondents had sufficiently alleged proximate c=
ause
because they were immediately injured by petitioners' scheme.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The court also rejected petitioner=
s'
argument that respondents are not entitled to relief under RICO because they
had not &nbsp;[***1016]&nbsp;received, and therefore had not relied on, any
false statements. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_11_4"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
<span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Held:</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> A
plaintiff asserting a RICO claim predicated on mail fraud need not show, ei=
ther
as an element of its claim or as a prerequisite to establishing proximate
causation, that it relied on the defendant's alleged misrepresentations. Pp.
647-661.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_12_4"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) In </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, RICO provides a private right of action for treble dam=
ages
to &quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by reason of a
violation,&quot; as pertinent here, of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which makes it &quot;unlawful for any person employed by or associated
with&quot; a qualifying enterprise &quot;to conduct or participate . . . in
&nbsp;[****3]&nbsp;the conduct of such enterprise's affairs through a patte=
rn
of racketeering activity,&quot; including &quot;mail fraud,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)(B)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mail fraud, in turn, occurs whenev=
er a
person, &quot;having devised or intending to devise any scheme or artifice =
to
defraud,&quot; uses the mail &quot;for the purpose of executing such scheme=
 or
artifice.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1341</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The gravamen of the offense is the
scheme to defraud, and any &quot;mailing . . . ,incident to an essential pa=
rt
of the scheme' . . . satisfies the mailing element,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Schmuck</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 489 U.S. 70=
5,
712, 109 S. Ct. 1443, 103 L. Ed. 2d 734</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, even if the mail=
ing
&quot;contain[s] no false information,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., at 715, 10=
9 S.
Ct. 1443, 103 L. Ed. 2d 734</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Once the relationship among these
statutory provisions is understood, respondents' theory of the case is
straightforward.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners
nonetheless argue that because the alleged pattern of racketeering activity=
 is
predicated on mail fraud, respondents must show that they relied on
petitioners' fraudulent misrepresentations, which they cannot do because the
misrepresentations were made to the county.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Nothing on the statute's face impo=
ses
such a requirement.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Using the=
 mail
to execute or attempt to execute a scheme to defraud is indictable as mail
fraud, and hence a predicate racketeering act under &nbsp;[****4]&nbsp;RICO,
even if no one relied on any misrepresentation, see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Neder</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 527 U.S. 1,
24-25, 119 S. Ct. 1827, 144 L. Ed. 2d 35</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; and one can cond=
uct
the affairs of a qualifying enterprise through a pattern of such acts witho=
ut
anyone relying on a fraudulent misrepresentation.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Thus, no reliance showing is requi=
red to
establish that a person has violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> by
conducting an enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering activi=
ty
predicated on mail fraud.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Nor=
 can a
first-party reliance requirement be derived from </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which, by providing a right of action to &quot;[a]ny person&quot; injured b=
y a
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
suggests a breadth of coverage not easily reconciled with an implicit
first-party reliance requirement.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Moreover, a person can be injured &quot;by reason of&quot; a pattern=
 of
mail fraud even if he has not relied on any misrepresentations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>For example, accepting respondents'
allegations as true, they were harmed by petitioners' scheme when they lost
valuable liens they otherwise would have been awarded. Pp. 647-650.</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_13_4"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(b) None of petitioners'
arguments&#8212;that under the &quot;common-law meaning&quot; rule, Congress
should be presumed to have made reliance an element of a civil RICO claim
predicated on a violation of &nbsp;[****5]&nbsp;the mail fraud statute; tha=
t a
plaintiff bringing a RICO claim based on mail fraud must show reliance on t=
he
defendant's misrepresentations in order to establish proximate cause; and t=
hat
RICO should be interpreted to require first-party reliance for fraud-based
claims in order to avoid the &quot;overfederalization&quot; &nbsp;[***1017]=
&nbsp;of
traditional state-law claims&#8212;persuades this Court to read a first-par=
ty
reliance requirement into a statute that by its terms suggests none. Pp.
650-660.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_14_4"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N3H-TGY0-0038-X0DD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>477 F.3d 928<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
affirmed. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><a
