style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*647]&nbsp;
II </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
begin by setting forth the applicable statutory provisions.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>RICO's private right of action is
contained in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which &nbsp;[****15]&nbsp;provides in relevant part th=
at <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_29"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN3_1_5"></a>=
[3]
&quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by reason of a viola=
tion
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this chapter may sue therefor in any appropriate United States district cou=
rt
and shall recover threefold the damages he sustains and the cost of the sui=
t,
including a reasonable attorney's fee.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
contains RICO's criminal prohibitions.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>Pertinent here is <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_29"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN4_1_5"></a>[4]</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which makes it &quot;unlawful for any person employed by or associated
with&quot; an enterprise&nbsp;[**2138]&nbsp; engaged in or affecting inters=
tate
or foreign commerce &quot;to conduct or participate, directly or indirectly=
, in
the conduct of such enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering
activity.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The term
&quot;racketeering activity&quot; is defined to include a host of so-called
predicate acts, including &quot;any act which is indictable under . . . </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1341<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(relating to mail fraud).&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)(B)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ48T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The upshot is that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_26"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN5_1_4"></a>=
[5] RICO
provides a private right of action for treble damages to any person injured=
 in
his business or property by reason of the conduct of a qualifying enterpris=
e's
affairs through a pattern of acts indictable as mail fraud.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mail fraud, in turn, occurs whenev=
er a
person, &quot;having devised or intending to &nbsp;[****16]&nbsp;devise any
scheme or artifice to defraud,&quot; uses the mail &quot;for the purpose of
executing such scheme or artifice or attempting so to do.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1341</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The gravamen of the offense is the
scheme to defraud, and any &quot;mailing that is incident to an essential p=
art
of the scheme satisfies the mailing element,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ48S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Schmuck</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 489 U.S. 70=
5,
712, 109 S. Ct. 1443, 103 L. Ed. 2d 734 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citation and internal quotation marks omitted), even if the mailing itself
&quot;contain[s] no false information,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4910000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BVS0-003B-4333-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 715, 109=
 S.
Ct. 1443, 103 L. Ed. 2d 734</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Once
the relationship among these statutory provisions is understood, respondent=
s'
theory of the case is straightforward.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>They allege that petitioners devised a scheme to defraud
&nbsp;[*648]&nbsp; when they agreed to submit false attestations of complia=
nce
with the Single, Simultaneous Bidder Rule to the county.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In furtherance of this scheme,
petitioners used the mail on numerous occasions to send the requisite notic=
es
to property owners.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Each of t=
hese
mailings was an &quot;act which is indictable&quot; as mail fraud, &nbsp;[*=
**1021]&nbsp;and
together they constituted a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity.&quot;<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>By conducting the affairs of their
enterprise through this pattern of racketeering activity, petitioners viola=
ted </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As a result, respondents lost the
opportunity &nbsp;[****17]&nbsp;to acquire valuable liens.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Accordingly, respondents were inju=
red in
their business or property by reason of petitioners' violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and RICO's plain terms give them a private right of action for treble damag=
es. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Petitioners
argue, however, that because the alleged pattern of racketeering activity
consisted of acts of mail fraud, respondents must show that they relied on
petitioners' fraudulent misrepresentations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>This they cannot do, because the a=
lleged
misrepresentations&#8212;petitioners' attestations of compliance with the
Single, Simultaneous Bidder Rule&#8212;were made to the county, not
respondents.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>county</i> may well have relied on
petitioners' misrepresentations when it permitted them to participate in the
auction, but <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>respondents</i>, never
having received the misrepresentations, could not have done so.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, respondents do not even al=
lege
that they relied on petitioners' false attestations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Thus, petitioners submit, they fai=
l to
state a claim under RICO. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4940000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>If petitioners' proposed require=
ment
of first-party reliance seems to come out of nowhere, there is a reason:<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Nothing on the face of the relevant
statutory provisions imposes such a requirement.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_=
25"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN6_1_4"></a>[6] Using the mail to execute or
&nbsp;[****18]&nbsp;attempt to execute a scheme to defraud is indictable as
mail fraud, and hence a predicate act of racketeering under RICO, even if no
one relied on any misrepresentation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4930000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Neder</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 527 U.S. 1,
24-25, 119 S. Ct. 1827, 144 L. Ed. 2d 35 (1999)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;The common-law requiremen[t] of 'justifiable reliance' . . . plainly
ha[s] no place &nbsp;[*649]&nbsp; in the [mail, wire, or bank] fraud
statutes&quot;).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And one can
conduct the affairs of a qualifying enterprise through a pattern of such
acts&nbsp;[**2139]&nbsp; without anyone relying on a fraudulent
misrepresentation. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ49C0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>It thus seems plain&#8212;and in=
deed
petitioners do not dispute&#8212;that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_23"=
></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN7_1_4"></a>[7] no showing of reliance is required to
establish that a person has violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> by
conducting the affairs of an enterprise through a pattern of racketeering
activity consisting of acts of mail fraud.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>See </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4950000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S., at
476, 126 S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Thomas, J.,
concurring in part and dissenting in part) (&quot;Because an individual can
commit an indictable act of mail or wire fraud even if no one relies on his
fraud, he can engage in a pattern of racketeering activity, in violation of=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
without proof of reliance&quot;).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>If reliance is required, then, it must be by virtue of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which provides the right of action.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>But it is difficult to derive &nbsp;[****19]&nbsp;a first-party reli=
ance
requirement from </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which states simply that &quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or prop=
erty
by reason of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
may sue for treble damages.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>T=
he
statute provides a right of action to &quot;[a]ny person&quot; injured by t=
he
violation, suggesting a breadth of coverage not easily reconciled with an
implicit requirement that the plaintiff show reliance in addition to injury=
 in
his business or property. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_22"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN8_1_4"><=
/a>[8] a
person can be injured &quot;by reason of&quot; a pattern of mail fraud even=
 if
he has not relied on any misrepresentations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>This is a case in point.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Accepting their allegations as tru=
e,
respondents clearly were injured by petitioners' scheme:<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As a &nbsp;[***1022]&nbsp;result of
petitioners' fraud, respondents lost valuable liens they otherwise would ha=
ve
been awarded.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And this is tru=
e even
though they did not rely on petitioners' false attestations of compliance w=
ith
the county's rules.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Or, to ta=
ke
another example, suppose an enterprise that wants to get rid of rival
businesses mails misrepresentations about them to their customers and
suppliers, but not to the rivals themselves.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If the rival businesses lose money=
 as a
result &nbsp;[*650]&nbsp; of the misrepresentations, it would certainly
&nbsp;[****20]&nbsp;seem that they were injured in their business &quot;by
reason of&quot; a pattern of mail fraud, even though they never received, a=
nd
therefore never relied on, the fraudulent mailings.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Yet petitioners concede that, on t=
heir
reading of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the rival businesses would have no cause of action under RICO, Tr. of Oral =
Arg.
4, even though they were the primary and intended victims of the scheme to
defraud. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Lacking
textual support for this counterintuitive position, petitioners rely instea=
d on
a combination of common-law rules and policy arguments in an effort to show
that Congress should be presumed to have made first-party reliance an eleme=
nt
of a civil RICO claim based on mail fraud.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>None of petitioners' arguments persuades us to read a first-party
reliance requirement into a statute that by its terms suggests none. </span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
