if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Neder, supra<=
/span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WNV-2M50-004C-2006-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 23, 119 =
S.
Ct. 1827, 144 L. Ed. 2d 35</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It has long been settled, they con=
tend,
that only the recipient of a fraudulent misrepresentation may recover for
common-law fraud, &nbsp;[****21]&nbsp;and that he may do so &quot;if, but o=
nly
if . . . he relies on the misrepresentation in acting or refraining from
action.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso=
-bidi-font-style:
normal'>4 Restatement (Second) of Torts &sect; 537 (1977)</i>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Given this background rule of comm=
on
law, petitioners maintain, Congress should be presumed to have adopted a
first-party reliance requirement when it created a civil cause
of&nbsp;[**2140]&nbsp; action under RICO for victims of mail fraud. </span>=
</p>

