href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[***102=
6]&nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46R7-3560-0038-X3MN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 104</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That &quot;specialized condition,&=
quot;
whether characterized as an element of the claim or as a prerequisite to
establishing proximate causation, simply has no place in a remedial scheme
keyed to the commission of mail fraud, a statutory offense that is distinct
from common-law fraud and that does not require proof of reliance. </span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
while it may be that first-party reliance is an element of a common-law fra=
ud
claim, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_20"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN=
10_1_1"></a>[10]
there is no general common-law principle holding that a fraudulent
misrepresentation can cause legal injury only to those who rely on it.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Restatement provision cited by
petitioners certainly does not support that proposition.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It provides only that the plaintif=
f's
loss must be a foreseeable result of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>someone's</i>
reliance on the misrepresentation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn403'
href=3D"#_ftn403" name=3D"_ftnref403" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It does not say that only those wh=
o rely
on the misrepresentation can suffer a legally cognizable injury.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And any such notion
&nbsp;[****31]&nbsp;would be contradicted by the long line of cases in which
courts have permitted a plaintiff directly injured by a fraudulent
misrepresentation to recover even though it was a third party, and not the
plaintiff, who relied on the defendant's misrepresentation.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn404' href=3D"#_ftn404" name=3D"_ftnref404" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, so well established is the
defendant's liability &nbsp;[*657]&nbsp; in such circumstances that the
Restatement (Second) of Torts sets forth as a &quot;[g]eneral [p]rinciple&q=
uot;
that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_18"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN11=
_1_0"></a>[11]
&quot;[o]ne who intentionally causes injury to another is subject to liabil=
ity
to the other for that injury, if his conduct is generally culpable and not
justifiable under the circumstances.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 870</i>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As an illustration, the Restatement
provides the example of a defendant who &quot;seeks to promote his own
interests by telling a known falsehood to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>or
about</i> the plaintiff or his product.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Id.,
Comment h</i> (emphasis added).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an>And
the Restatement specifically recognizes &quot;a cause of action&quot; in fa=
vor
of the injured party where the defendant &quot;defrauds another for the pur=
pose
of causing pecuniary harm to a third person.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Id.</i>,
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&sect; 435A, Comment a</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners' contention that proxi=
mate
cause has traditionally incorporated&nbsp;[**2144]&nbsp; a first-party reli=
ance
requirement for claims based &nbsp;[***1027]&nbsp;on fraud cannot be
&nbsp;[****32]&nbsp;reconciled with these authorities. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nor
is first-party reliance necessary to ensure that there is a sufficiently di=
rect
relationship between the defendant's wrongful conduct and the plaintiff's
injury to satisfy the &nbsp;[*658]&nbsp; proximate-cause principles articul=
ated
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> and <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Anza.</i><span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Again, this=
 is a
&nbsp;[****34]&nbsp;case in point. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I0CD5SY7NKN0007C7W40=
000F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0CD5SY7BNN0007C7W40000D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DM0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Respondents' alleged injury&#8212;t=
he
loss of valuable liens&#8212;is the direct result of petitioners' fraud. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DS0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>It was a foreseeable and natural
consequence of petitioners' scheme to obtain more liens for themselves that
other bidders would obtain fewer liens.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>And here, unlike in</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DK00004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
there are no independent factors that account for respondents' injury, ther=
e is
no risk of duplicative recoveries by plaintiffs removed at different levels=
 of
injury from the violation, and no more immediate victim is better situated =
to
sue.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Indeed, both the District
Court and the Court of Appeals concluded that respondents and other losing
bidders were the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>only</i> parties i=
njured
by petitioners' misrepresentations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DS0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>App. to Pet. for Cert. 17a; </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4N3H-TGY0-0038-X0DD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>477 F.3d, at =
931</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners quibble with that
conclusion, asserting that the county would be injured too if the taint of
fraud deterred potential bidders from participating in the auction.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But that eventuality, in contrast =
to
respondents' direct financial injury, seems speculative and remote. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Of course, none of this is to say
that a RICO plaintiff who alleges injury &quot;by reason of&quot; a pattern=
 of
mail fraud can prevail without showing that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>someone</i>
relied &nbsp;[****35]&nbsp;on the defendant's misrepresentations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Cf. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRC-25N0-003B-R163-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Field</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRC-25N0-003B-R163-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRC-25N0-003B-R163-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mans</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRC-25N0-003B-R163-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 516 U.S. 59,
66, 116 S. Ct. 437, 133 L. Ed. 2d 351 (1995)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;No one, of course, doubts that some degree of reliance is required to
satisfy the element of causation inherent in the phrase 'obtained by'&quot;=
 in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>11 U.S.C. &sect; 523(a)(2)(A)</i>, whi=
ch
prohibits the discharge of debts for money or property &quot;obtained by&qu=
ot;
fraud).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In most cases, the
plaintiff will not be able to establish even but-for causation if no one re=
lied
on the misrepresentation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If,=
 for
example, the county had not accepted petitioners' false attestations of
compliance with the Single, Simultaneous Bidder Rule, and as a result had n=
ot
permitted petitioners to participate in the auction, respondents' injury wo=
uld
never have materialized.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In
addition, the complete absence of reliance may prevent the plaintiff
&nbsp;[*659]&nbsp; from establishing proximate cause.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Thus, for example, if the county k=
new
petitioners' attestations were false but nonetheless permitted them to
participate in the auction, then arguably the county's actions would consti=
tute
an intervening cause breaking the chain of causation between petitioners'
misrepresentations and respondents' injury. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DX0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Accordingly, it may well be that=
 a
RICO plaintiff alleging injury &nbsp;[****36]&nbsp;by reason of a pattern of
mail fraud must establish at least third-party reliance in order to prove
causation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&quot;But the fact=
 that
proof of reliance is often used to prove an element of the plaintiff's caus=
e of
action, such as the element of causation, does not transform reliance itself
into an element of the cause of action.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S., at
478, 126 S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Thomas, J.,
concurring in part and dissenting in part).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Nor does it transform first-party
reliance into an indispensable requisite of proximate causation. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0CD5SY81TC0007C7W40000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4F00000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Proof that the plaintiff relied on =
the
defendant's misrepresentations may in some cases be sufficient to establish
proximate cause, but there is no sound reason to conclude
&nbsp;[***1028]&nbsp;that such proof is always necessary.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>By the same token, the absence of
first-party reliance may in some cases tend to show that an injury was not
sufficiently direct to satisfy </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
proximate-cause requirement, but it is not in and of itself dispositive.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A contrary holding&nbsp;[**2145]&n=
bsp;
would ignore</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4DY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'
instruction that proximate cause is generally not amenable to bright-line
rules. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a last resort, petitioners contend that we should interpret RICO to require
first-party reliance for fraud-based claims in order to avoid
&nbsp;[****37]&nbsp;the &quot;over-federalization&quot; of traditional
state-law claims. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4F20000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>In petitioners' view, respondents' =
claim
is essentially one for tortious interference with prospective business
advantage, as evidenced by count V of their complaint.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Such claims have traditionally been
handled under state law, and petitioners see no reason why Congress
&nbsp;[*660]&nbsp; would have wanted to supplement traditional state-law
remedies with a federal cause of action, complete with treble damages and
attorney's fees, in a statute designed primarily to combat organized
crime.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4F10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, </span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>supra</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 471-475,=
 126
S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Ti=
mes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Thomas, J., concurring in part=
 and
dissenting in part); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4F30000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4048-9TB0-004B-Y032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Beck</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4048-9TB0-004B-Y032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 529 U.S., at
496-497, 120 S. Ct. 1608, 146 L. Ed. 2d 561</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A first-party reliance requirement=
, they
say, is necessary &quot;to prevent garden-variety disputes between local
competitors (such as this case) from being converted into federal racketeer=
ing
actions.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Reply Brief for
Petitioners 3. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal30 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4TXM1RC0K1MNJ4F60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Whatever the merits of petitione=
rs'
arguments as a policy matter, we are not at liberty to rewrite RICO to refl=
