k'>Justice
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Thomas</b> delivered the opinion o=
f the
Court. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal31 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
this case, we decide whether a federal court of appeals has jurisdiction to
review a district court's order that remands a case to state court after
declining to exercise supplemental jurisdiction over state-law claims under=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1367(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Cour=
t of
Appeals for the Federal Circuit held that appellate review of such an order=
 is
barred by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1447(d=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
because it viewed the remand order in this case as resting on the District
Court's lack of subject-matter jurisdiction over the state-law claims.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We&nbsp;[**1865]&nbsp; disagree and
reverse the judgment of the Court of Appeals. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal31 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal31 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2005, respondents filed a complaint against petitioner and others in Califo=
rnia
state court, alleging that petitioner had violated state and federal law in
connection with a patent dispute. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C10000=
400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Petitioner removed the case to the =
United
States District Court for the Central District of &nbsp;[****4]&nbsp;Califo=
rnia
pursuant to </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_30"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN1_1_6"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1441(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which allows removal of an &quot;entire case&quot; when it includes at least
one claim over which the federal district court has original jurisdiction.<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioner then filed a motion to
dismiss the only federal claim in the lawsuit, which arose under the Racket=
eer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-1968</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, for failure &nbsp;[*637]&nbsp;=
 to
adequately allege a pattern of racketeering. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C30000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>HIF Bio, Inc.=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Yung Shin
Pharmaceuticals Indus. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 508 F.3d 65=
9,
662 (CA Fed. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C30000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The District Court agreed that
respondents had failed to state a RICO claim upon which relief could be gra=
nted
and dismissed the claim pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The District Court also declined to
exercise supplemental jurisdiction over the remaining state-law claims purs=
uant
to </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_30"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LED=
HN2_1_6"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1367(c)(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which provides that a district court &quot;may decline=
 to
exercise supplemental jurisdiction over a claim&quot; if &quot;the district
court has dismissed all claims over which it has original
jurisdiction.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Distr=
ict
Court then remanded the case to state court as authorized by this Court's
decision in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FXP0-003B-413X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Carnegie-Mell=
on
Univ.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FXP0-003B-413X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FXP0-003B-413X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cohill</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FXP0-003B-413X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 484 U.S. 34=
3,
108 S. Ct. 614, 98 L. Ed. 2d 720 (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal31 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0FM0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0FP0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Petitioner appealed
&nbsp;[****5]&nbsp;to the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal
Circuit, arguing that the District Court should have exercised supplemental
jurisdiction over the state-law claims because they implicate federal
patent-law rights.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0C40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>508 F.3d at 6=
63</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court of Appeals dismissed the
appeal, finding that the remand order could &quot;be colorably characterize=
d as
a remand based on lack of subject matter jurisdiction&quot; and, therefore,
could not be reviewed under<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_30"></a> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN3_1_6"></a>[3] </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;
1447(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(d)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which provide in part that remands for &quot;lack of subject matter
jurisdiction&quot; are &quot;not reviewable on appeal or otherwise.&quot;<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0FN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R47-WB10-TXFN-63BD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 667</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
&nbsp;[***848]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal31 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0FS0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This Court has not yet decided
whether a district court's order remanding a case to state court after
declining to exercise supplemental jurisdiction is a remand for lack of
subject-matter jurisdiction for which appellate review is barred by </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;
1447(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(d)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0J60000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0FR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P0M-FYM0-004C-101R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powerex Corp.=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P0M-FYM0-004C-101R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P0M-FYM0-004C-101R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reliant Energy
Services, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P0M-FYM0-004C-101R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 551 U.S. 22=
4,
235, n. 4, 127 S. Ct. 2411, 168 L. Ed. 2d 112 (2007)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;We have never passed on whether <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Cohill</i>
remands are subject-matter jurisdictional for purposes of . . . </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1447(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0JP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1447(d=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;).
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0J60000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>We granted certiorari to resolve th=
is
question, <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4WF8GNX0K1MNJ0J50000400"></a><i style=3D'mso=
-bidi-font-style:
normal'>555 U.S. 943, 129 S. Ct. 395, 172 L. Ed. 2d 284 (2008)</i>,
&nbsp;[1355]&nbsp; and now hold that such remand orders are not based
