<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_15_4"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) This Court assumes, without
deciding, that Iqbal's </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim is actionable in a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i>
action, see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4JTG-G8N0-004B-Y03F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hartman</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4JTG-G8N0-004B-Y03F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4JTG-G8N0-004B-Y03F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4JTG-G8N0-004B-Y03F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S. 25=
0,
254, n. 2, 126 S. Ct. 1695, 164 L. Ed. 2d 441</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Because vicarious liability is
inapplicable to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i> and </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1983</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
suits, see, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g., </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SP0-003B-S1RH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Monell</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SP0-003B-S1RH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SP0-003B-S1RH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York City
Dept. of Social Servs.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8SP0-003B-S1RH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 436 U.S. 65=
8,
691, 98 S. Ct. 2018, 56 L. Ed. 2d 611</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, the plaintiff in=
 a
suit such as the present one must plead &nbsp;[****4]&nbsp;that each
Government-official defendant, through his own individual actions, has viol=
ated
the Constitution.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Purposeful
discrimination requires more than &quot;intent as volition or intent as
awareness of consequences&quot;; it involves a decisionmaker's undertaking a
course of action &quot;'because of,' not merely 'in spite of,' [the action'=
s]
adverse effects upon an identifiable group.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8410-003B-S1CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Personnel
Administrator of Mass.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8410-003B-S1CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8410-003B-S1CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Feeney</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8410-003B-S1CX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 442 U.S. 25=
6,
279, 99 S. Ct. 2282, 60 L. Ed. 2d 870</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Iqbal must plead sufficient factual
matter to show that petitioners adopted and implemented the detention polic=
ies
at issue not for a neutral, investigative reason, but for the purpose of
discriminating on account of race, religion, or national origin. Pp. 675-67=
7</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_16_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(b) Under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 8(a)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, a complaint must contain a
&quot;short and plain statement of the claim showing that the pleader is
entitled to relief.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&quot;[D]etailed factual allegations&quot; are not required, </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Twombly</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 550 U.S., at
555, 127 S. Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, but the Rule does
call for sufficient factual matter, accepted as true, to &quot;state a clai=
m to
relief that is plausible on its face,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 570, 127=
 S.
Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A claim has facial plausibility wh=
en the
pleaded factual content allows the court to draw the reasonable inference
&nbsp;[****5]&nbsp;that the defendant is liable for the misconduct alleged.=
<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 556, 127=
 S.
Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Two working principles underlie <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Twombly.</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>First, the tenet that a court must
accept a complaint's allegations as true is inapplicable to threadbare reci=
tals
of a cause of action's elements, supported by mere conclusory statements.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 555, 127=
 S.
Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Second, determining whether a comp=
laint
states a plausible claim is context-specific, requiring the &nbsp;[*664]&nb=
sp;
reviewing court to draw on its experience and common sense.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 556, 127=
 S.
Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A court considering a motion
&nbsp;[***875]&nbsp;to dismiss may begin by identifying allegations that,
because they are mere conclusions, are not entitled to the assumption of tr=
uth.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>While legal conclusions can provid=
e the
complaint's framework, they must be supported by factual allegations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>When there are
well-pleaded&nbsp;[**1941]&nbsp; factual allegations, a court should assume
their veracity and then determine whether they plausibly give rise to an
entitlement to relief. Pp. 677-680</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_17_1"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(c) Iqbal's pleadings do not com=
ply
with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
under <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Twombly.</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Several of his allegations--that
petitioners agreed to subject him to harsh conditions as a matter of policy,
solely on account of discriminatory factors and for no legitimate penologic=
al
interest; that &nbsp;[****6]&nbsp;Ashcroft was that policy's &quot;principal
architect&quot;; and that Mueller was &quot;instrumental&quot; in its adopt=
ion
and execution--are conclusory and not entitled to be assumed true.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Moreover, the factual allegations =
that
the FBI, under Mueller, arrested and detained thousands of Arab Muslim men,=
 and
that he and Ashcroft approved the detention policy, do not plausibly suggest
that petitioners purposefully discriminated on prohibited grounds.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Given that the September 11 attack=
s were
perpetrated by Arab Muslims, it is not surprising that a legitimate policy
directing law enforcement to arrest and detain individuals because of their
suspected link to the attacks would produce a disparate, incidental impact =
on
Arab Muslims, even though the policy's purpose was to target neither Arabs =
nor
Muslims.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Even if the complain=
t's
well-pleaded facts gave rise to a plausible inference that Iqbal's arrest w=
as
the result of unconstitutional discrimination, that inference alone would n=
ot
entitle him to relief:<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>His cl=
aims
against petitioners rest solely on their ostensible policy of holding detai=
nees
categorized as &quot;of high interest,&quot; but the complaint does not con=
tain
facts plausibly showing that their policy was &nbsp;[****7]&nbsp;based on
discriminatory factors. Pp. 680-684</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_18"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(d) Three of Iqbal's arguments a=
re
rejected. Pp. 684-687</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_19"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(i) His claim that <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Twombly</i> should be limited to its
antitrust context is not supported by that case or the Federal Rules.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Because <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Twombly</i> interpreted and applied </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which in turn governs the pleading standard &quot;in all civil actions,&quo=
t; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8JD7-3YB2-8T6X-706W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 1</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the case applies to antitrust and discrimination suits alike, see </span><a
