<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*666]&nbsp;&nbsp;[**1942]&nbsp;
Justice <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>Kennedy</b> delivered the
opinion of the Court. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Javaid
Iqbal (hereinafter respondent) is a citizen of Pakistan and a Muslim.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In the wake of the September 11, 2=
001,
terrorist attacks he was arrested in the United States on criminal charges =
and
detained by federal officials.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Respondent claims he was deprived of various constitutional protecti=
ons
while in federal custody.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>To
redress the alleged deprivations, respondent filed a complaint against nume=
rous
federal officials, including John Ashcroft, the former
&nbsp;[****9]&nbsp;Attorney General of the United States, and Robert Muelle=
r,
the Director of the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Ashcroft and Mueller are the petit=
ioners
in the case now before us.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As=
 to
these two petitioners, the complaint alleges that they adopted an
unconstitutional policy that subjected respondent to harsh conditions of
confinement on account of his race, religion, or national origin. </span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the District Court petitioners raised the defense of qualified immunity and
moved to dismiss the suit, contending the complaint was not sufficient to s=
tate
a claim against them.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Dis=
trict
Court denied the motion to dismiss, concluding the complaint was sufficient=
 to
state a claim despite petitioners' official status at the times in
question.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Petitioners brought=
 an
interlocutory appeal in the Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The court, without discussion, ass=
umed
it had jurisdiction over the order denying the motion to dismiss; and it
affirmed the District Court's decision. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Respondent's
account of his prison ordeal could, if proved, demonstrate unconstitutional
misconduct by some governmental actors.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>But the allegations and pleadings with respect to these actors are n=
ot
before us here.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&nbsp;[****10]&nbsp;This case instead turns on a narrower question:<=
span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Did respondent, as the plaintiff i=
n the
District Court,&nbsp;[**1943]&nbsp; plead factual matter that, if taken as
true, states a claim that petitioners deprived him of his clearly establish=
ed
constitutional rights.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We hold
respondent's pleadings are insufficient. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*667]&nbsp;
I </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Following
the 2001 attacks, the FBI and other entities within the Department of Justi=
ce
began an investigation of vast reach to identify the assailants and prevent
them from attacking anew.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The=
 FBI
dedicated more than 4,000 special agents and 3,000 support personnel to the
endeavor.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>By September 18 &qu=
ot;the
FBI had received more than 96,000 tips or potential leads from the
public.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Dept. of Justic=
e,
Office of Inspector General, The September 11 Detainees:<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A Review of the Treatment of Alien=
s Held
on Immigration Charges in Connection with the Investigation of the Septembe=
r 11
Attacks 1, 11-12 (Apr. 2003),
&nbsp;[***877]&nbsp;http://www.usdoj.gov/oig/special/0306/full.pdf?bcsi_sca=
n_61073EC0F747
59AD0&amp;bcsi_scan_filenamefull.pdf (as visited May 14, 2009, and availabl=
e in
Clerk of Court's case file). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the ensuing months the FBI questioned more than 1,000 people with suspected
links to the attacks &nbsp;[****11]&nbsp;in particular or to terrorism in
general.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>Id.</i>, at 1.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Of tho=
se
individuals, some 762 were held on immigration charges; and a 184-member su=
bset
of that group was deemed to be &quot;of 'high interest'&quot; to the
investigation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'ms=
o-bidi-font-style:
normal'>Id.,</i> at 111.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The
high-interest detainees were held under restrictive conditions designed to
prevent them from communicating with the general prison population or the
outside world.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'ms=
o-bidi-font-style:
normal'>Id.,</i> at 112-113. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Respondent was one of the
detainees.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>According to his
complaint, in November 2001 agents of the FBI and Immigration and
Naturalization Service arrested him on charges of fraud in relation to
identification documents and conspiracy to defraud the United States. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4050000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Iqbal</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hasty</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 490 F.3d 14=
3,
147-148 (CA2 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4050000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Pending trial for those crimes,
respondent was housed at the Metropolitan Detention Center (MDC) in Brookly=
n,
New York.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Respondent was desi=
gnated
a person &quot;of high interest&quot; to the September 11 investigation and=
 in
January 2002 was placed in a section of the MDC known as the Administrative
Maximum Special Housing Unit &nbsp;[*668]&nbsp; (ADMAX SHU).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 148</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As the facility's name indicates, =
the
ADMAX SHU incorporates the maximum security conditions allowable under Fede=
ral
Bureau of Prisons &nbsp;[****12]&nbsp;regulations.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Ibid.</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>ADMAX SHU detainees were kept in
lockdown 23 hours a day, spending the remaining hour outside their cells in
handcuffs and leg irons accompanied by a four-officer escort.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Ibid.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ40Y0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Respondent pleaded guilty to the
criminal charges, served a term of imprisonment, and was removed to his nat=
ive
