or
national origin. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Respondent
disagrees.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>He argues that, un=
der a
theory of &quot;supervisory liability,&quot; petitioners can be liable for
&quot;knowledge and acquiescence in their subordinates' use of discriminato=
ry
criteria to make classification decisions among detainees.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Iqbal Brief 45-46.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That is to say, respondent believe=
s a
supervisor's mere knowledge of his subordinate's discriminatory purpose amo=
unts
to the supervisor's violating the Constitution.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We reject this argument.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Respondent's conception of
&quot;supervisory liability&quot; is inconsistent with his accurate stipula=
tion
that petitioners may not be held accountable for the misdeeds of their
agents.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0712-D6RV-H526-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1983</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
suit or a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i> action--where
masters do not answer for the torts of their &nbsp;[****28]&nbsp;servants--=
the
term &quot;supervisory liability&quot; is a misnomer.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_=
23"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN9_1_6"></a>[9] Absent vicarious liability, each Gover=
nment
official, his or her title notwithstanding, is only liable for his or her o=
wn
misconduct.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In the context of
determining whether there is a violation of a clearly established right to
overcome qualified immunity, purpose rather than knowledge is required to
impose <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i> liability on the
subordinate for unconstitutional discrimination; the same holds true for an
official charged with violations arising from his or her superintendent
responsibilities. </span></p>

