k'>
should be tempered where, as here, the Court of Appeals has &quot;instructed
the district court to cabin discovery in such a way as to preserve&quot;
petitioners' defense of qualified immunity &quot;as much as possible in
anticipation of a summary judgment motion.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Iqbal Brief 27.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We have &nbsp;[****40]&nbsp;held,
however, that the question presented by a motion to dismiss a complaint for
insufficient pleadings does not turn on the controls &nbsp;[*685]&nbsp; pla=
ced
upon the discovery process.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4N00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Twombly</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, </span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>supra</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 559, 127=
 S.
Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;It is no answer to say t=
hat
a claim just shy of a plausible entitlement to relief can, if groundless, be
weeded out early in the discovery process through careful case management g=
iven
the common lament that the success of judicial supervision in checking
discovery abuse has been on the modest side&quot; (internal quotation marks=
 and
citation omitted)). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4N80000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our rejection of the
careful-case-management approach is especially important in suits where
Government-official defendants are entitled to assert the defense of qualif=
ied
immunity.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The basic thrust of=
 the
qualified-immunity doctrine is to free officials from the concerns of
litigation, including &quot;avoidance of disruptive discovery.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4N70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KSR0-003B-R16H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Siegert</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KSR0-003B-R16H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KSR0-003B-R16H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gilley</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KSR0-003B-R16H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 500 U.S. 22=
6,
236, 111 S. Ct. 1789, 114 L. Ed. 2d 277 (1991)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Kennedy, J., concurring in judgment). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4NB0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>There are serious and legitimate re=
asons
for this.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If a Government off=
icial
is to devote time to his or her duties, and to the formulation of sound and
responsible policies, it is counterproductive to require the substantial
diversion that &nbsp;[****41]&nbsp;is attendant to participating in litigat=
ion
and making informed decisions as to how it should proceed. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4NB0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Litigation, though necessary to ens=
ure
that officials comply with the law, exacts heavy costs in terms of efficien=
cy
and expenditure of valuable time and resources that might otherwise be dire=
cted
to the proper execution of the work of the Government.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The costs of diversion are only
magnified when Government officials are charged with responding to, as Judge
Cabranes aptly put it, &quot;a national and international security emergency
unprecedented in the history of the American Republic.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4WGBBPY0K1MNJ4N90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NYV-G060-0038-X36S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>490 F.3d at 1=
79</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is no answer to these concerns to say that discovery for petitioners can be
deferred while pretrial proceedings continue for other defendants.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It is quite likely that, when disc=
overy
as to the other parties proceeds, it would prove necessary for petitioners =
and
their counsel to participate in the process to ensure the case does not dev=
elop
in a misleading or slanted way that causes prejudice to their position.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Even &nbsp;[*686]&nbsp; if petitio=
ners
are not yet themselves subject to discovery orders, then, they would not be
free from the burdens of discovery. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
decline respondent's invitation to relax &nbsp;[****42]&nbsp;the pleading
requirements on the&nbsp;[**1954]&nbsp; ground that the Court of Appeals
promises petitioners minimally intrusive discovery.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That promise provides especially c=
old
comfort in this pleading context, where we are impelled to give real conten=
t to
the concept of qualified immunity for high-level officials who must be neit=
her
deterred nor detracted from the vigorous performance of their duties.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Because respondent's complaint is
deficient under &nbsp;[***889]&nbsp;</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
he is not entitled to discovery, cabined or otherwise. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3 </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Respondent
finally maintains that the Federal Rules expressly allow him to allege
petitioners' discriminatory intent &quot;generally,&quot; which he equates =
with
a conclusory allegation.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Iqbal
Brief 32 (citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. Rule Civ.
Proc. 9</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It foll=
ows,
respondent says, that his complaint is sufficiently well pleaded because it
claims that petitioners discriminated against him &quot;on account of [his]
religion, race, and/or national origin and for no legitimate penological
interest.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Complaint P 9=
6,
App. to Pet. for Cert. 172a-173a.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Were we required to accept this allegation as true, respondent's com=
plaint
would survive petitioners' motion to dismiss.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>But the Federal Rules do not requi=
re
courts to credit a complaint's &nbsp;[****43]&nbsp;conclusory statements
without reference to its factual context. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is true that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_10"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LEDHN15_1_1"></a>[15]</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires particularity when pleading &quot;fraud or mistake,&quot; while
allowing &quot;[m]alice, intent, knowledge, and other conditions of a perso=
n's
mind [to] be alleged generally.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>But &quot;generally&quot; is a relative term.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In the context of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it is to be compared to the particularity requirement applicable to fraud or
mistake.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
merely excuses a party from pleading discriminatory intent under an elevated
pleading standard.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It does no=
t give
him license &nbsp;[*687]&nbsp; to evade the less rigid--though still
operative--strictures of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See 5A C. Wright &amp; A. Miller,
Federal Practice and Procedure &sect; 1301, p 291 (3d ed. 2004) (&quot;[A]
rigid rule requiring the detailed pleading of a condition of mind would be
undesirable because, absent overriding considerations pressing for a
specificity requirement, as in the case of averments of fraud or mistake, t=
he
general 'short and plain statement of the claim' mandate in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8(a)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> .
. . should control the second sentence of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>And </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 8</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not empower respondent to plead the bare elements of his cause of acti=
on,
affix the label &quot;general allegation,&quot; and expect his
&nbsp;[****44]&nbsp;complaint to survive a motion to dismiss. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>V </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
hold that respondent's complaint fails to plead sufficient facts to state a
claim for purposeful and unlawful discrimination against petitioners.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court of Appeals should decide=
 in
the first instance whether to remand to the District Court so that responde=
nt
can seek leave to amend his deficient complaint. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
judgment of the Court of Appeals is reversed, and the case is remanded for
further proceedings consistent with this opinion. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal32 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is so ordered. </span></p>

