:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ante</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 677, 173=
 L.
Ed. 2d, at 883</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5002D4H0K1MNJ1JY0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The majority says that in a <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i> action, &quot;where masters=
 do
not answer for the torts of their servants,&quot; &quot;the term 'superviso=
ry
liability' is a misnomer,&quot; and &nbsp;[*693]&nbsp; that &quot;[a]bsent
vicarious liability, each Government official, his or her title notwithstan=
ding,
is only liable for his or her own misconduct.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Ibid.</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Lest there be any mistake, in these
words the majority is not narrowing the scope of supervisory liability; it =
is
eliminating <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bivens</i> supervisory
liability entirely.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The natur=
e of a
supervisory liability theory is that the supervisor may be liable, under
certain conditions, for the wrongdoing of his subordinates, and it is this =
very
principle that the majority rejects.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5002D4H0K1MNJ1JX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ante</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 683, 173=
 L.
Ed. 2d, at 887</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;[P]etitioners cannot be held liable unless they
