style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Turkette</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. 57=
6,
583, 101 S. Ct. 2524, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, the District Cou=
rt
instructed the jury that to establish a RICO association-in-fact
&quot;enterprise,&quot; the Government must prove (1) an ongoing organizati=
on
with a framework, formal or informal, for carrying out its objectives, and =
(2)
that association members functioned &nbsp;[****2]&nbsp;as a continuing unit=
 to
achieve a common purpose.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The=
 court
also told the jury that an association-in-fact's existence is often more
readily proved by what it does than by abstract analysis of its structure, =
and
denied Boyle's request for an instruction requiring the Government to prove
that the enterprise had &quot;an ascertainable structural hierarchy distinct
from the charged predicate acts.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbs=
p;
</span>Boyle was convicted, and the Second Circuit affirmed. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_11_7"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
<span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Held:</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_12_7"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. An association-in-fact enterp=
rise
under RICO must have a &quot;structure,&quot; but the pertinent jury
instruction need not be framed in the precise language Boyle proposes, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.,</i> as having &quot;an ascertain=
able
structure beyond that inherent in the pattern of racketeering activity in w=
hich
it engages.&quot; Pp. 943-951.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_13_6"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) In light of RICO's broad
statement that an enterprise &quot;includes any . . . group of individuals
associated in fact although not a legal entity,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(4=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and the requirement that RICO be &quot;liberally construed to effectuate its
remedial purposes,&quot; note following </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Turkette</i> explained that
&quot;enterprise&quot; reaches &quot;a group of persons associated together=
 for
a common purpose of engaging in a course of conduct,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>452 U.S., at =
583,
101 S. Ct. 2524, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:=
"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and &quot;is proved
&nbsp;[****3]&nbsp;by evidence of an ongoing&nbsp;[**2240]&nbsp; organizati=
on,
&nbsp;[*939]&nbsp; formal or informal, and by evidence that the various
associates function as a continuing unit.&quot;<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>ibid.</i>
Pp. 943-945.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_14_6"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(b) The question presented by th=
is
case is whether an association-in-fact enterprise must have &quot;an
ascertainable structure beyond that inherent in the pattern of racketeering
activity in which it engages.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Pet. for Cert. i.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>This
question can be broken into three parts.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span>First, the enterprise must have a &quot;structure&quot; that, under
RICO's terms, has at least three features:<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>a purpose, relationships among the associates, and longevity suffici=
ent
to permit the associates to pursue the enterprise's purpose.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Turkette,</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 452 U.S., at
583, 101 S. Ct. 2524, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The instructions need not actually=
 use
the term &quot;structure,&quot; however, so long as the relevant point's
substance is adequately expressed.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Second, because a jury must find the existence of elements of a crime
beyond a reasonable doubt, requiring a jury to find the existence of a
structure that is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ascertainable</i>=
&nbsp;[***1272]&nbsp;would
be redundant and potentially misleading.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span>Third, the phrase &quot;beyond that inherent in the pattern of
racketeering activity&quot; is correctly interpreted to mean that the enter=
prise's
existence is a separate &nbsp;[****4]&nbsp;element that must be proved, not
that such existence may never be inferred from the evidence showing that the
associates engaged in a pattern of racketeering activity.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:normal'>ibid.</i>
Pp. 945-947.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_15_5"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(c) Boyle's argument that an
enterprise must have structural features additional to those that can be fa=
irly
inferred from RICO's language--<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g.=
,</i>
a hierarchical structure or chain of command; fixed roles for associates; a=
nd
an enterprise name, regular meetings, dues, established rules and regulatio=
ns,
disciplinary procedures, or induction or initiation ceremonies--has no basi=
s in
the statute's text.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Turkette</i> said, an association-in-f=
act
enterprise is simply a continuing unit that functions with a common
purpose.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The breadth of RICO's
&quot;enterprise&quot; concept is highlighted by comparing the statute with
other federal laws having much more stringent requirements for targeting
organized criminal groups:<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>E.g.,</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1955(b=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
defines an &quot;illegal gambling business&quot; as one that &quot;involves
five or more persons who conduct, finance, manage, supervise, direct, or own
all or part of such business.&quot; Pp. 947-949.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_16_4"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(d) Rejection of Boyle's argument
does not lead to a merger of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
crime and other federal offenses.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>&nbsp;[****5]&nbsp;For example, proof that a defendant violated </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1955</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not necessarily establish that he conspired to participate in a gambli=
ng
enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Rather, that would require the
prosecution to prove either that the defendant committed a pattern of </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1955</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
violations or a pattern of state-law gambling crimes.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Pp. 949&#8211;950.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_17_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(e) Because RICO's language is
clear, the Court need not reach Boyle's statutory purpose, legislative hist=
ory,
or rule-of-lenity arguments. Pp. 950&#8211;951.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_18_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*940]&nbsp; 2. The
instructions below were correct and adequate.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>By explicitly telling jurors they =
could
not convict on the RICO charges unless they found that the Government had
proved the existence of an enterprise, the instructions made clear that this
was a separate element from the pattern of racketeering activity.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The jurors also were adequately to=
ld
that the enterprise needed the structural attributes that may be inferred f=
rom
the statutory language.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Final=
ly,
the instruction that an enterprise's existence &quot;is oftentimes more rea=
dily
proven by what is [sic] does, rather than by abstract analysis of its
structure&quot; properly conveyed <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>T=
urkette</i>'s
point that proof of a pattern &nbsp;[****6]&nbsp;of racketeering activity m=
ay
be&nbsp;[**2241]&nbsp; sufficient in a particular case to permit an inferen=
ce
of the enterprise's existence. Pp. 949-951.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_19_0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R5N-55T0-TXFX-41XH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>283 Fed. Appx.
825</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>, affirmed. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal33 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Counsel_32"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span
