<p class=3DNormal34 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
a final effort to save its claim, the City has shifted course before this
Court.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In its second amended
complaint and RICO statement, the City relied solely on Hemi's failure to f=
ile
Jenkins Act reports with the State to form the basis of the predicate act m=
ail
and wire frauds.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See Second A=
mended
Compl. PP 99, 101, 118, 125; Record A980-A982.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Before this &nbsp;[***956]&nbsp;Co=
urt,
however, the City contends that Hemi made affirmative misrepresentations to
City residents, which, the City now argues, comprise part of the RICO predi=
cate
mail and wire frauds.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See Bri=
ef for
Respondent 42-43.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The City's
counsel pressed the point at oral argument, asserting that the City's injur=
y was
&quot;caused by the seller's misrepresentation, which encourages the purcha=
sers
not to pay taxes.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Tr. o=
f Oral
Arg. 44. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal34 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*17]&nbsp;
The City, however, affirmatively disavowed below any reliance on
misrepresentations to form the predicate RICO violation.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The alleged false statements, the =
City
there stated, &quot;are evidence of the scheme to defraud, but are not part=
 of
the &nbsp;[****28]&nbsp;fraud itself. . . .<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>[T]he scheme to defraud would exis=
t even
absent the statements.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
Record
A980.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The City reiterated the
point:<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&quot;The scheme consi=
sts of
the interstate sale of cigarettes and the failure to file Jenkins Act repor=
ts
indentifying those sales.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>&quot;Related to the fraud, but no=
t a
circumstance 'constituting' the fraud, the defendants inform customers that
[their] purchases will be concealed, and also seek to convince their custom=
ers
that no taxes are owed by claiming, falsely, that the sales are
tax-free.&quot;<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.,</i> at A982.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Not only did the City disclaim any
reliance upon misrepresentations to the customers to form the predicate acts
under RICO, but the City made clear in its second amended complaint that its
two RICO&nbsp;[**994]&nbsp; claims rested solely on the Jenkins Act violati=
ons
as the predicate acts.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>See Se=
cond
Amended Compl.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>PP 118, 125.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Because the City defined the predi=
cate
act before the District Court as Hemi's failure to file the Jenkins Act
reports, and expressly disavowed reliance on the alleged misrepresentations
themselves as predicate acts, we decline to consider Hemi's alleged
misstatements as predicate acts at this late stage. </span></p>

