if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The &quot;pattern of racketeering&=
quot;
at issue here consists of repeated instances of mail fraud, which in turn
consist largely of violations of the federal Jenkins Act, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:61XJ-4HH3-GXJ9-33PC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 375-378</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>That Act seeks to help States coll=
ect
tobacco taxes by requiring out-of-state cigarette sellers, such as Hemi, to
file reports with state tobacco tax administrators identifying the names and
addresses of in-state customers and the amounts they purchased.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The violations consist of Hemi's
intentional failure to do so. </span></p>

