<p class=3DNormal34 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
majority asks whether New York City stated a valid cause of action in alleg=
ing
that it lost tobacco tax revenue &quot;by reason of&quot; Hemi's unlawful
misrepresentations.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The facts=
 just
set forth make clear that we must answer that question affirmatively.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>For one thing, no one denies
&nbsp;[****36]&nbsp;that Hemi's misrepresentation was a &quot;but-for&quot;
condition of New York City's loss.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>In the absence of the misrepresentation, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>i.e.</i>, had Hemi told New York State the truth about its New York
City customers, New York City would have written letters to the purchasers =
and
obtained a significant share of the tobacco taxes buyers owed. </span></p>

