:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at ____ - _=
___,
175 L. Ed. 2d, at 952-953</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6T2853CCD200002D6700028"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4YD38400K1MNJ4CG0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>But courts used this concept of
directness in tort law to expand liability (for direct consequences) <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>beyond</i> what was foreseeable, not to
eliminate liability for what was foreseeable. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4YD38400K1MNJ4CG0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4YD38400K1MNJ4CD0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>&nbsp;[****42]&nbsp;Thus, under the
&quot;directness&quot; theory of proximate causation, there is liability for
both &quot;all 'direct' (or 'directly traceable') consequences <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>and those indirect consequences that a=
re
foreseeable.</i>&quot; Prosser and Keeton &sect; 42, at 273 (emphasis
added);&nbsp;[*26]&nbsp; see also <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i=
d.,</i>
&sect; 43, at 294, and n 17 (citing </span><a
