<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*578]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[**571]&nbsp; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31R0000400"></a>&nbsp=
;[***290]&nbsp;
Under New York law, the standard provision in a contract of primary liabili=
ty
insurance requiring the insured to give the insurer prompt notice of a
potential claim operates as a condition precedent; thus, for failure to give
notice, the primary insurer &quot;need not show prejudice before it can ass=
ert
the defense of noncompliance&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
( </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VM90-003C-C1WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Security Mut.
Ins. Co. v Acker- Fitzsimons Corp.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VM90-003C-C1WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 31 NY2d 436,
440)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. For reasons which follow, we hold that this &quot;no
prejudice&quot; rule does not apply to a failure to comply with the prompt
notice requirement in a contract of reinsurance. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31T0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This appeal, pursuant to section
500.17 of this Court's Rules of Practice (22 NYCRR), certifies to us the
central legal issue in a declaratory judgment action commenced in the United
States District Court for the Southern District of New York by Unigard Secu=
rity
Insurance Company, Inc. (Unigard) against&nbsp;[****6]&nbsp; North River
Insurance Company (North River).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The question is whether Unigard must honor its commitment
&nbsp;[**572]&nbsp; &nbsp;[***291]&nbsp; under a &nbsp;[*579]&nbsp; faculta=
tive
reinsurance certificate </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn421' href=3D"=
#_ftn421"
name=3D"_ftnref421" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> issued to North River covering a
high layer excess insurance policy issued by North River to Owens-Corning
Fiberglass Corp. (Owens-Corning) <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(s=
ee, </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DHT0-001T-726B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Unigard Sec. =
Ins.
Co. v North Riv. Ins. Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DHT0-001T-726B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 762 F Supp 5=
66</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
[SD NY, Sweet, J.]). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Unigard contends that it is automat=
ically
relieved from its obligations under the reinsurance certificate because Nor=
th
River failed to give prompt notice of an &quot;occurrence or accident which
appear[ed] likely to involve [the] reinsurance&quot; as&nbsp;[****7]&nbsp;
required by clause C of the certificate. Any question of prejudice, it argu=
es,
is irrelevant.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>North River has
filed a counterclaim seeking damages and a declaration that Unigard is
obligated to pay under the certificate. North River maintains that before a=
 reinsurer
may avoid its responsibility on account of late notice, it must show that it
has suffered prejudice and that, since concededly no prejudice has been sho=
wn,
Unigard's defense of late notice must fail. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
North River policy--for which Unigard issued its certificate of
reinsurance--provided $ 30 million in excess liability insurance for two
successive policy periods for amounts recovered against Owens-Corning over =
$ 76
million of underlying primary and lower level excess policies provided by
various insurers. Under its certificate, Unigard agreed &quot;to reinsure&q=
uot;
North River in respect to these policies &quot;subject to the terms,
conditions, limits of liability, and Certificate provisions set forth&quot;=
.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The terms and provisions include t=
he
following: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;C.
Prompt notice shall be given by the company to the Underwriting Managers on
behalf of the Reinsurers of any occurrence or accident which appears likely=
 to
involve this reinsurance&nbsp;[****8]&nbsp; and while the Underwriting Mana=
gers
or the Reinsurers do not undertake to investigate or defend claims or suits,
the Underwriting Managers directly, or through its representatives and/or
counsel, shall nevertheless have the right and be given the opportunity to
associate with the Company and its representatives at the Reinsurers' expen=
se
in the defense and control of any claim, suit or &nbsp;[*580]&nbsp; proceed=
ing
which may involve this reinsurance with the full cooperation of the
Company.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Unigard,
as the agreed payment for its reinsurance commitment, became entitled to
receive one sixth of the premium paid to North River, less the standard 25%
brokerage commission. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
dispute over the Unigard reinsurance coverage stemmed from the asbestos cla=
im
crisis which began in the early 1980's and resulted in hundreds of claims
against various asbestos products manufacturers including Owens-Corning.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On April 1, 1987, North River beca=
me
aware that its excess policies were being penetrated and that there was a
likelihood that the Unigard reinsurance would be involved.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In August 1987, North River sent a
precautionary notice to Unigard advising it of potential asbestos-related l=
osses
under its&nbsp;[****9]&nbsp; excess policies. Unigard did not receive this
notice until September 2, 1987. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31Y0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The District Court, after a nine=
-day
bench trial, found: (1) that North River's obligation to send a notice to
Unigard arose in April 1987; and (2) that, under an objective standard, the
notice sent in August and received on September 2, 1987 was untimely.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It rejected Unigard's late notice
defense, however, holding that New York law required a reinsurer to demonst=
rate
prejudice. Because there was no showing of prejudice, the Court rendered
judgment in favor of </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ31X0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DHT0-001T-726B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>North River (=
762
F Supp 566</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra).</i> </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3210000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On appeal to the Second Circuit
Court of Appeals, that court determined: (1) that the only New York decisio=
n on
the issue of whether a reinsurer is required to demonstrate prejudice is an
unpublished opinion <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(General Ins. C=
o. v
Nutmeg Ins. Co.,</i> Sup Ct, NY County, July 24, 1987, Shainswit, J.); and =
(2)
that there is a split of authority on the issue within the Southern Distric=
t of
New York <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(compare, </i>&nbsp;[**573=
]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[***292]&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3200000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7W80-0054-407W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Christiana Ge=
n.
Ins. Corp. v Great Am. Ins. Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7W80-0054-407W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 745 F Supp 1=
50</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
[SD NY 1990] [holding that reinsurer must demonstrate prejudice to prevail =
on
late-notice defense], &nbsp;[****10]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>appeal
pending</i> F2d [2d Cir, Sept. 23, 1991, No. 91-7912], <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>with </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3220000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9D10-0054-41WW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Travelers Ins.
Co. v Buffalo Reinsurance Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9D10-0054-41WW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 735 F Supp 4=
92</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
[reinsurer need not prove prejudice], <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>vacated
in part</i> </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3240000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-8D90-0054-43MD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>739 F Supp 20=
9</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
[SD NY 1990]). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3310000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Second Circuit noted that the
&quot;determination of the legal requirements for the assertion of a
late-notice claim in the reinsurance context is important to purchasers and
sellers of reinsurance issued in New York and to the reinsurance &nbsp;[*58=
1]&nbsp;
industry&quot; (</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3260000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76C0-008H-V3Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>949 F2d 630, =
632)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, it certified to us the following question: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3330000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;Must a reinsurer prove
prejudice before it can successfully invoke the defense of late notice of l=
oss
by the reinsured?&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>( </i></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3320000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76C0-008H-V3Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.,</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-76C0-008H-V3Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 632</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.) </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
answer the question in the affirmative. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3350000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ33S0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_35"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is settled New York law that the notice provision for a primary insurer
operates as a condition precedent and that the insurer need not show prejud=
ice
to rely on the defense of late notice <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>(see,
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3340000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VM90-003C-C1WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Security Mut.
Ins. Co. v Acker-Fitzsimons Corp.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VM90-003C-C1WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 31 NY2d 436,=
 440</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra).</i> The question is whether th=
is
same rule should be applied in the context of reinsurance. The New York
&quot;no prejudice&quot; rule for primary insurers--as
remarked&nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; by the District Court herein <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
