k'>).<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>There is nothing in this provision=
 or
elsewhere in the North River certificate indicating that the parties intend=
ed
that the giving of notice should operate as a condition precedent <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(compare, </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ35X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DS70-0039-P4X6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Liberty Mut. =
Ins.
Co. v Gibbs,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DS70-0039-P4X6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 773 F2d 15, =
16-
17</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'> [1st Cir 1965] [where the certificate specifically prov=
ided
that notice &quot;is a condition precedent to any liability under this
policy&quot;]).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>If the ordina=
ry
rules of contract were applied, the prompt notice provision in the North Ri=
ver
certificate would not be construed as a condition precedent. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III.
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>There
are significant and basic differences between primary insurance and
reinsurance. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_35"></a>A certificate of
reinsurance--unlike a contract of primary insurance--is not a contract under
which the company agrees to indemnify the insured from losses up to a stated
limit&nbsp;[****14]&nbsp; upon the happening of specified contingencies.<sp=
an
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>A certificate of reinsurance is a
contract between two insurance companies in which the reinsured company agr=
ees
to cede part of its risk to the reinsurer in return for a percentage of the
premium. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3610000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>A reinsurance contract operates sol=
ely
between the reinsurer and the ceding company.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>It confers no rights on the insure=
d <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(see,</i> 19 Couch, Insurance 2d &sect=
; 80:1
[1983]).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>As we recently state=
d, a
reinsurer's &quot;only obligation is to indemnify the primary insurer&quot;=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>( </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3600000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-82B0-003V-B0F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Matter of Mid=
land
Ins. Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-82B0-003V-B0F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 79 NY2d 253,=
 258</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see, </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3620000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Security Mut.
Cas. Co. v Century Cas. Co., supra,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 977</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
13A Appleman, Insurance Law and Practice &sect; 7681 [1976]). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*583]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3650000400"></a>These differences in the
contractual undertakings of reinsurers and primary insurers have consequenc=
es
of critical importance, as courts in other jurisdictions have found <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(see, e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3640000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Security Mut.
Cas. Co. v Century Cas. Co., supra,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 978</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3660000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-003B-51MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Insurance Co.=
 v
Associated Intl. Ins. Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-003B-51MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 922 F2d 516,
523-524</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra).</i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_35"></a>A reinsurer is not responsible for
providing a defense, for investigating the claim or for attempting to get
control of the claim in order to effect an early settlement.
&nbsp;[****15]&nbsp; Unlike a primary insurer, it may not be held liable to=
 the
insured for a breach of these duties.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>Settlements, as well as the investigation and defense of claims are =
the
sole responsibility of the primary insurer; and settlements made by the pri=
mary
insurer are, by express terms of the reinsurance certificate, binding on the
reinsurer. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn422' href=3D"#_ftn422"
name=3D"_ftnref422" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Thus, failure to give the requi=
red
prompt notice is of substantially less significance for a reinsurer than fo=
r a
primary insurer. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3690000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, the interests of a
reinsurer and the ceding primary insurer with respect to a pending claim are
generally identical.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The
&quot;follow the fortunes&quot; clause in most reinsurance agreements leave=
s reinsurers
little room to dispute&nbsp;[****16]&nbsp; the reinsured's conduct of the
case.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In addition, the intere=
sts of
both parties are furthered through the primary insurer's efficient
investigation and defense of the claim and through the resolution of the cl=
aim
on the best terms possible <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(see, </=
i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ3680000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Security Mut.
Cas. Co. v Century Cas. Co., supra,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-25K0-0039-M0X2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 978</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
[rejecting rule under Colorado law and noting that a reinsurer had &quot;as
much reason&quot; as a primary insurer to see that claims &quot;were proper=
ly
investigated and defended&quot; and that as between &quot;the two insurers,
there was little danger of fraud or imposition&quot;]; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ36B0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-003B-51MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Insurance Co.=
 v
Associated Intl. Ins. Co., supra,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-003B-51MK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 523-524</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
By contrast, the interests of a primary insurer and its insured may often be
adverse.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>There may be dispute=
s over
cooperation or coverage or over claimed collusion on the part of the insure=
d.
These factors make prompt notice of the claim and expeditious processing and
control of it a matter of vital concern to the primary insurer. Such
considerations have &nbsp;[**575]&nbsp; &nbsp;[***294]&nbsp; greatly dimini=
shed
application to the reinsurer. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Unigard
asserts, however, that a reinsurer's &quot;right to associate&quot; </span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn423' href=3D"#_ftn423" name=3D"_ftnref423" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[*584]&nbsp; with the ceding insurance company makes it
necessary&nbsp;[****17]&nbsp; for the reinsurer to have prompt notification=
 of
any claim likely to involve the reinsurance. The &quot;right to associate&q=
uot;
involves the right to consult with and advise the reinsured in its handling=
 of
a claim.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Unigard argues that =
this
right gives it an interest similar to that of a primary insurer and that it,
therefore, must have early notice so that it may itself investigate the cla=
im
and foreclose the possibility of fraud.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span>We agree that there are cases in which the reinsurer's right to
associate may be impaired by late notice from the reinsured. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ36F0000400"></a>Nonetheless, because of the
critical distinctions between a primary insurer's right to control the
investigation and defense of a claim and a reinsurer's &quot;right of
association&quot; with the ceding companies, we cannot agree with Unigard's
contention that the risk of such impairment is sufficiently grave to warrant
applying a presumption of prejudice. Accordingly, there is no sound reason =
to
depart from the general contract law principle that a breach will excuse
performance only if it is material or demonstrably prejudicial. Indeed, it =
has
been noted that reinsurers seldom have occasion to exercise their right to
associate <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>(see, Christiana</i>
&nbsp;[****18]&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ36D0000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7W80-0054-407W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Gen. Ins. Corp. v Great Am. Ins. Co=
.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7W80-0054-407W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 745 F Supp 1=
50</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
159, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra; </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FFM8FF0K1MNJ36G0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9D10-0054-41WW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Travelers Ins.
Co. v Buffalo Reinsurance Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-9D10-0054-41WW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 735 F Supp 4=
92,
499</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra).</i> </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>This is not to suggest that a reins=
urer
may never assert late notice as a ground for avoiding its obligations under=
 a
reinsurance contract.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>All we =
hold
here is that the reinsurer must demonstrate how it was prejudicial and may =
not
rely on the presumption of prejudice that applies&nbsp;[****19]&nbsp; in the
late notice disputes between primary insurers and their insureds. </span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we conclude that the question certified should be
answered in the affirmative. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal36 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*585]&nbsp;
Following certification of a question by the United States Court of Appeals=
 for
the Second Circuit and acceptance of the question by this Court pursuant to
section 500.17 of the Rules of Practice of the New York State Court of Appe=
als (</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5WYW-NFS1-JJSF-2104-00009-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>22 NYCRR 500.=
17</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>),
and after hearing argument by counsel for the parties and consideration of =
the
briefs and the record submitted, certified question answered in the
affirmative.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

