<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_5_23"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[***478]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[*593]&nbsp; Seeking relief from his accident-related chronic pain,
Douglas Horn purchased and began taking &#8220;Dixie X,&#8221; a purportedly
THC-free, non-psychoactive CBD tincture produced by Medical Marijuana, Inc.=
 A
few weeks later, however, Horn&#8217;s employer selected him for random drug
screening, and Horn tested positive for THC. After he refused to participat=
e in
a substance abuse program, his employer fired him. Horn then sued Medical
Marijuana under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (RICO)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which creates a cause of action for &#8220;[a]ny person injured in his busi=
ness
or property&#8221; by reason of a criminal RICO violation. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The District Court granted summary judgment to Medical
Marijuana. Horn&#8217;s lost employment derived from a personal injury
(ingesting THC), the court reasoned. And in the court&#8217;s view, </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
forecloses recovery not only for personal injuries, but also for business or
property harms that result from such injuries. The Second Circuit reversed,
concluding that Horn had been &#8220;injured in his business&#8221; when he
lost his job. In so holding, the Second Circuit rejected the
&#8220;antecedent-personal-injury&nbsp;[****2]&nbsp; bar,&#8221; a rule ado=
pted
by several circuits that &nbsp;[***479]&nbsp; precludes recovery for busine=
ss
or property losses that derive from a personal injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_6"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><sp=
an
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Held</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>:
Under civil RICO, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
plaintiff may seek treble damages for business or property loss even if the
loss resulted from a personal injury. Pp. 600-614.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_7"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(a) The sole question before the
Court is whether civil RICO categorically bars recovery for business or
property losses that derive from a personal injury. The Court does not addr=
ess
issues implicated by this case but outside the scope of the question presen=
ted,
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.,</i> whether Horn suffered a
personal injury when he consumed THC, whether the term &#8220;business&#822=
1;
encompasses all aspects of &#8220;employment,&#8221; and what &#8220;injure=
d in
his . . . property&#8221; means for purposes of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Pp. 600.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_8_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(b) </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides that &#8220;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by re=
ason
of a violation of [RICO] may sue . . . .&#8221; The ordinary meaning of
&#8220;injure&#8221; is to &#8220;cause harm or damage to&#8221; or to
&#8220;hurt.&#8221; American Heritage Dictionary 676. So the meaning of </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
straightforward: A plaintiff has been &#8220;injured in his business or
property&#8221; if his business or property has been harmed or damaged.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_9_0"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*594]&nbsp; Even so, </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not allow recovery for all harms. By explicitly permitting recovery for
harms to business and property, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
implicitly excludes recovery for harm to one&#8217;s person. But
the&nbsp;[****3]&nbsp; business or property requirement operates with respe=
ct
to the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>kinds</i> of harm for which =
the
plaintiff can recover, not the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>caus=
e</i>
of the harm for which he seeks relief. For example, a gas station owner bea=
ten
in a robbery cannot recover for his pain and suffering. But if injuries from
the robbery force him to shut his doors, he can recover for the loss of his
business. A plaintiff can seek damages for business or property loss, in ot=
her
words, regardless of whether the loss resulted from a personal injury. Pp.
600-601.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_10_8"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(c) Medical Marijuana argues that
while &#8220;injury&#8221; ordinarily means harm, it can also refer to the
&#8220;invasion of a legal right.&#8221; Ballentine&#8217;s Law Dictionary =
627.
Seizing on the latter definition, Medical Marijuana asserts that &#8220;inj=
ured
in his business or property&#8221; means &#8220;suffered an invasion of a
business or property right&#8221;&#8212;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>i.e.</i>,
a business or property tort. And Medical Marijuana contends that the invasi=
on
of a personal right never gives rise to a RICO claim. So if a personal-inju=
ry
tort causes a business or property harm, the plaintiff &#8220;cannot
recast&#8221; his harm &#8220;as the basis for a RICO suit.&#8221;</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_11_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Medical Marijuana in effect trie=
s to
make a term-of-art argument without the term of art.&nbsp;[****4]&nbsp; Tru=
e,
&#8220;injury&#8221; can mean the &#8220;invasion of a legal right,&#8221; =
but
even legal dictionaries confirm that &#8220;injury&#8221; often means
&#8220;harm&#8221; or &#8220;damage.&#8221; In any event, when a word carri=
es
both an ordinary and specialized meaning, context determines the choice bet=
ween
them. Here, context favors ordinary meaning. The statute uses
&#8220;injured,&#8221; not &#8220;injury,&#8221; and the dictionary Medical
Marijuana relies on defines &#8220;injured&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>only</i> according to its ordinary meaning. And Medical
Marijuana&#8217;s argument based on the presence of the word
&#8220;damages&#8221; in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
untenable. The phrase &#8220;threefold the damages he sustains&#8221; refer=
s to
monetary redress&#8212;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, a
&nbsp;[***480]&nbsp; plaintiff may recover triple the amount that makes him
whole. Pp. 602-604.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_clspara_12_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(d) Medical Marijuana ignores the
many cases in which the Court has used the words &#8220;injury,&#8221;
&#8220;harm,&#8221; and other terms connoting loss interchangeably. See, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>e.g., </i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S. P.=
 R.
L.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Imrex Co.</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
