<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
this case implicates several issues that fall outside the scope of the ques=
tion
presented, we begin by emphasizing what we do <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>not</i> decide.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First,
we express no view on whether Horn suffered an antecedent personal injury w=
hen
he consumed THC. In the courts below, Horn characterized his injury as
exclusively to his business and property&nbsp;[****11]&nbsp; because the
defendants hurt his livelihood, not his body. Whatever the merits of this
theory, the Second Circuit did not address it, and neither party asks us to
revisit the District Court&#8217;s conclusion that Horn suffered a personal
injury. So like the Second Circuit, we proceed on the understanding that he
did.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Second,
we do not decide whether the Second Circuit correctly interpreted
&nbsp;[***484]&nbsp; &#8220;business&#8221; to encompass
&#8220;employment&#8221; for purposes of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
This interpretation may or may not be right. But because Medical Marijuana =
has
not challenged it, we leave the issue for another day.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB28T4020040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Finally, we do not opine on what=
 it
means for a plaintiff to be &#8220;injured in his . . . property&#8221; und=
er </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The parties suggest that this phrase covers all pecuniary loss. We need not
engage this argument, however, because Horn&#8217;s claim does not depend on
it. After concluding that Horn was &#8220;injured in business,&#8221; the
Second Circuit expressly reserved the question &#8220;whether Horn suffered=
 an
injury to property when he lost his job.&#8221; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB28T4020030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>80 F. 4th, at
136, n. 3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We follow suit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
only question we address is the one squarely before us: whether civil RICO =
bars
recovery for all business or property harms that derive from a personal inj=
ury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_36"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides that &#8220;[a]ny person <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i=
njured
in his business or property</i> by reason of a violation of [RICO] may
&nbsp;[*601]&nbsp; sue. . . .&#8221; (Emphasis added.) The ordinary meaning=
 of
&#8220;injure&#8221; is to &#8220;cause harm or damage to&#8221; or to
&#8220;hurt.&#8221; American Heritage Dictionary 676 (1969); Webster&#8217;s
Third New International Dictionary&nbsp;[****12]&nbsp; 1164 (1971) (&#8220;=
to
impair the soundness of &#8221;; &#8220;to inflict material damage or loss
on&#8221;); Ballentine&#8217;s Law Dictionary 626 (3d ed. 1969) (&#8220;[t]o
harm; to hurt; to wound&#8221;). &#8220;Injury,&#8221; which shares a common
root, ordinarily refers to &#8220;[d]amage of or to a person, property,
reputation, or thing.&#8221; American Heritage Dictionary, at 676; see also
Webster&#8217;s Third New International Dictionary, at 1164. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn426' href=3D"#_ftn426" name=3D"_ftnref426" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The meaning of the relevant &nbsp;[**939]&nbsp; phrase is therefore
straightforward: A plaintiff has been &#8220;injured in his business or
property&#8221; if his business or property has been harmed or damaged. </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires nothing more.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB2D6MRY0030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc3_36"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Even
so, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not allow recovery for all harms. Instead, by explicitly permitting
recovery for harms to business and property, it implicitly excludes recovery
for harm to one&#8217;s person. See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB2D6MRY0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco, =
Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>European
Community</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 579 U. S. 3=
25,
350, 136 S. Ct. 2090, 195 L. Ed. 2d 476 (2016)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
see also A. Scalia &amp; B. Garner, Reading Law &sect;10, p. 107 (2012)
(&#8220;[S]pecification of the one implies exclusion of the other&#8221;). =
But
the &#8220;business or property&#8221; requirement operates with respect to=
 the
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>kinds</i> of harm for which the pla=
intiff
can recover, not the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cause</i> of t=
he
harm for which he seeks relief. For example, if the owner of a gas station =
is
beaten in a robbery, he cannot recover for his pain and suffering. But if h=
is
injuries force him to shut his doors, he can recover for the loss of his
business. In short,&nbsp;[****13]&nbsp; a plaintiff can seek damages for
business or property loss regardless of whether the loss resulted from a
personal injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*602]&nbsp; B</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB2D6MRY0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Medical Marijuana resists this
conclusion, &nbsp;[***485]&nbsp; contending that &#8220;injured in his busi=
ness
or property&#8221; carries a specialized meaning. While &#8220;injury&#8221;
ordinarily means harm, it can also refer to the &#8220;invasion of a legal
right.&#8221; Ballentine&#8217;s Law Dictionary, at 627; see also <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Restatement (Second) of Torts &sect;7(=
1)</i>
(1964). Seizing on the latter definition, Medical Marijuana, echoed by the
principal dissent, argues that &#8220;injured in his business or
property&#8221; means &#8220;suffered an invasion of a business or property
right&#8221;&#8212;that is, a business or property tort. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn427' href=3D"#_ftn427" name=3D"_ftnref427" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The invasion of a personal right, they assert, never gives rise to a cause =
of
action under RICO. See Brief for Petitioners 11, 15, 35; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFB2D6MRY0040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>post</i>, at 631 (opinion of <span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps=
'>Kavanaugh,
J.</span>). So if a personal-injury tort causes a business or property harm,
the plaintiff &#8220;cannot recast&#8221; his harm &#8220;as the basis for a
RICO suit.&#8221; Brief for Petitioners 15.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is true that &#8220;injury&#8221; can mean &#8220;invasion of a legal
right.&#8221; But even in the language of lawyers, this specialized definit=
ion
is not exclusive. Ballentine&#8217;s, for example, defines the full phrase
&#8220;injury in his property&#8221; to mean either &#8220;[a]n injury to h=
is
property&#8221; or &#8220;[h]arm or damage resulting to his property direct=
ly
or indirectly.&#8221; Ballentine&#8217;s Law Dictionary, at 627. And
Black&#8217;s defines &#8220;injury&#8221; to mean &#8220;[a]ny wrong <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>or damage</i> done to another, either =
in his
person, rights, reputation, or &nbsp;[**940]&nbsp; property.&#8221;
Black&#8217;s Law Dictionary &nbsp;[*603]&nbsp; 924 (rev. 4th ed. 1968)
(emphasis added). So in legal dictionaries, as in lay ones,
&#8220;injury&#8221; often means &#8220;harm&#8221; or &#8220;damage.&#8221=
;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFC28T40F0040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc5_32"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
a word carries both an ordinary and specialized meaning,&nbsp;[****14]&nbsp=
; we
look to context to choose between them. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFC28T40F0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68HX-DGM1-FBFS-S0RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68HX-DGM1-FBFS-S0RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68HX-DGM1-FBFS-S0RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hansen</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:68HX-DGM1-FBFS-S0RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 599 U. S. 7=
62,
775, 143 S. Ct. 1932, 216 L. Ed. 2d 692 (2023)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Here, context cuts decisively in favor of ordinary meaning. As an initial
matter, the statute uses the word &#8220;injured&#8221; rather than
&#8220;injury.&#8221; The word choice is notable, because while the legal
dictionary on which Medical Marijuana primarily relies includes the special=
ized
meaning (&#8220;invasion of a legal right&#8221;) in the possible definitio=
ns
of &#8220;injury,&#8221; it defines &#8220;injured&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>only</i> according to its ordinary meaning: &#8220;[h]urt, damaged,
[or] wounded.&#8221; Ballentine&#8217;s Law Dictionary, at 627. It is hard =
to
make a term-of-art argument without the term of art.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFC2N1RM50010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>And although Medical Marijuana
argues otherwise, the presence of the word &#8220;damages&#8221; does not
suggest that &#8220;injured&#8221; conveys a specialized meaning. Recall th=
at </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
allows &#8220;[a]ny person <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injured<=
/i> in
his business or property by reason of &#8221; a RICO violation to
&#8220;recover threefold the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>damage=
s</i>
he sustains.&#8221; (Emphasis added.) Medical Marijuana insists that the
definitions of &#8220;injured&#8221; and &#8220;damages&#8221; must be
different, because &#8220;Congress&#8217; use of &#8216;certain language in=
 one
part of &nbsp;[***486]&nbsp; the statute and different language in
another&#8217; can indicate that &#8216;different meanings were
intended.&#8217;&#8221; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFC28T40F0050000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57JR-J1D1-F04K-F51X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sebelius</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57JR-J1D1-F04K-F51X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57JR-J1D1-F04K-F51X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Auburn Region=
al
Medical Center</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:57JR-J1D1-F04K-F51X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 568 U. S. 1=
45,
156, 133 S. Ct. 817, 184 L. Ed. 2d 627 (2013)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFC2N1RM50020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CRG-3TY0-004C-000F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
