k'>.
Perhaps due to these difficulties, the Court today issues a narrow opinion =
that
elides the parties&#8217; primary disputes. Rather than opine on ancillary
issues, I would dismiss the writ of &nbsp;[**947]&nbsp; certiorari as
improvidently granted. I respectfully dissent.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff
Douglas Horn lost his job as a commercial truck driver after a random drug =
test
revealed tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) in his system. Horn asserts that the THC
came from his ingestion of Dixie X, an over-the-counter medicinal product
manufactured and sold by Medical Marijuana, Inc. </span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn434' href=3D"#_ftn434" name=3D"_ftnref434" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>*</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Medical Marijuana advertised Dixie X as THC-free, but a third-party laborat=
ory
detected THC in the product.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Horn
sued Medical Marijuana in Federal District Court, alleging various state-law
claims and a violation&nbsp;[****27]&nbsp; of civil RICO. To establish a ci=
vil
RICO violation, a plaintiff must prove that a defendant engaged in a
&#8220;pattern of racketeering activity&#8221; that caused the plaintiff to=
 be
&#8220;injured in his business or property.&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;1=
962</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
1964(c). Horn alleged that Medical Marijuana engaged in mail and wire
fraud&#8212;forms of racketeering activity under RICO&#8212;and that, as a
result, he suffered a business or property injury in the form of lost
employment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Medical
Marijuana moved for summary judgment on some of Horn&#8217;s state-law clai=
ms
on the ground that Horn had not suffered a personal injury, an essential
element of those claims. Specifically, Medical Marijuana asserted that Horn=
 had
&#8220;failed to produce any evidence or testimony that [he] suffered any
bodily injury from ingesting Dixie X.&#8221; Memorandum &nbsp;[***494]&nbsp;
&nbsp;[*616]&nbsp; of Law in Support of Defendants et al. in No. 15-cv-701
(WDNY, Aug. 30, 2018), ECF Doc. 62-1, p. 27. In Medical Marijuana&#8217;s v=
iew,
&#8220;[t]he only loss&#8221; Horn even &#8220;attempted to establish throu=
gh
discovery&#8221; was that he lost his &#8220;jo[b] and . . . source of
income.&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VD"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFJ2HM63K0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The District Court accepted Medi=
cal
Marijuana&#8217;s argument and rejected some of Horn&#8217;s state-law clai=
ms
based on his failure to establish a personal injury. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFJ2HM63K0040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>383 F. Supp. 3d 114, 134 (WDNY 2019)</i>. The court explained that
although Horn seeks damages for &#8220;economic losses,&#8221; he does
&#8220;not claim that [he] suffered any personal injury . . . as a result of
[Medical Marijuana&#8217;s] conduct.&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Ibid.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The&nbsp;[****28]&nbsp;
District Court&#8217;s summary judgment ruling left two claims for trial: a
state-law fraudulent inducement claim and the civil RICO claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
the eve of trial, Medical Marijuana argued for the first time that Horn&#82=
17;s
civil RICO claim failed as a matter of law because it was based on a
&#8220;personal injury.&#8221; Memorandum of Law in Reply to Motion <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>In Limine</i> in No. 15-cv-701 (WDNY, =
Aug.
2, 2021), ECF Doc. 200, p. 4. It asserted that the only injury Horn alleged=
 was
the unwanted &#8220;ingest[ion]&#8221; of &#8220;trace amounts of THC,&#822=
1;
and that this kind of injury &#8220;is, fundamentally, a type of &#8216;per=
sonal
injury.&#8217;&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>, at 1=
0, 12.
Horn&#8217;s lost wages, according to Medical Marijuana, were merely
&#8220;damages&#8221; that &#8220;derive from a personal injury,&#8221; and
thus &#8220;are not recoverable under . . . civil RICO.&#8221; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>, at 5 (capitalization and bold=
face
deleted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VC"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK2N1RNS0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VB"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK2N1RNS0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Notwithstanding its earlier
determination that Horn had <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>not </i=
>suffered
a personal injury for purposes of the state-law claims, the District Court
adopted Medical Marijuana&#8217;s new theory and held that Horn&#8217;s civ=
il
RICO claim was based on an impermissible &#8220;personal injury: the bodily
invasion that [Horn] suffered when he unwittingly ingested THC.&#8221; </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK2N1RNS0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:63M3-2N21-F27X-6549-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 174740, 2021 WL 4173195, *3</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:63M3-2N21-F27X-6549-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[**948]=
&nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:63M3-2N21-F27X-6549-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (WDNY, Sept.=
 14,
2021)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. And, because civil RICO precludes suits based on perso=
nal
injuries, the District Court determined that Horn&#8217;s civil
RICO&nbsp;[****29]&nbsp; claim &#8220;fail[ed] as a &nbsp;[*617]&nbsp; matt=
er
of law.&#8221; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK2N1RNS0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:63M3-2N21-F27X-6549-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2021 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 174740, [WL], at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The District Court granted par=
tial
final judgment on the civil RICO claim to allow Horn to appeal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Before
the Second Circuit, Horn did not contest the District Court&#8217;s holding
that civil RICO excludes suits based on economic harms resulting from perso=
nal
injuries. Rather, he argued that he had not suffered a personal injury in t=
he
first place.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000V9"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK28T4240010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000V8"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK28T4240030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Second Circuit declined to
decide whether Horn had suffered a personal injury. Instead, the court reso=
lved
what it described as the &#8220;logically antecedent legal question&#8221;:
whether civil RICO &#8220;bars a plaintiff from suing for injuries to busin=
ess
or property simply because they flow from, or are derivative of, a personal
injury.&#8221; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK2N1RNS0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>80 F. 4th 130,
135, n. 2 (2023)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Deepening a Circuit split, the
Second Circuit held that civil RICO permits a plaintiff to sue for injuries=
 to
business or property that &#8220;flow from, or are derivative of, an antece=
dent
personal injury.&#8221; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK28T4240020000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 135</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Medical
Marijuana petitioned this Court for certiorari, asking us to decide whether
economic harms resulting from personal injuries are injuries to &#8220;busi=
ness
or property&#8221; under civil &nbsp;[***495]&nbsp; RICO. Pet. for Cert. i.=
 It
assured us that &#8220;[t]his case cleanly tees that circuit split up for
resolution,&#8221; and that the case would &#8220;tur[n] on a narrow,
dispositive, and discrete question of statutory interpretation that requires
little analysis of the underlying facts.&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Id.</i>, at 4 (internal quotation marks omitted). We granted
certiorari. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>601 U. S. ___, 144 S. C=
t.
1454, 218 L. Ed. 2d 688 (2024)</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I
would dismiss the writ of certiorari as improvidently granted. The question
presented has divided the Circuits and merits this Court&#8217;s attention.
But, the parties dispute an&nbsp;[****30]&nbsp; important, factbound, thres=
hold
issue that the court below did not pass upon. And, the intertwined question=
 of
how to define a civil RICO injury is inadequately briefed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*618]&nbsp; A</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
took this case to decide whether economic losses flowing from personal inju=
ries
are <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injuries</i> to business or pro=
perty
for purposes of civil RICO, or merely <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>damages</i>.
That question necessarily assumes the existence of a personal injury as the
starting point.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VJ"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK28T4240050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Yet, in this case, the parties
vigorously contest whether Horn suffered a personal injury at all. Despite =
its
earlier theory that Horn had not suffered a personal injury, see <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFK28T4240040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>supra</i>, at 616-617, Medical Marijuana now argues that &#8220;Horn
suffered a quintessential personal injury&#8221; by &#8220;ingesting an
unwanted substance.&#8221; Brief for Petitioners 20. Horn, by contrast,
contends that he &#8220;did not suffer any harm to his person,&#8221; and t=
hat
his injuries were only economic in nature. Tr. of Oral Arg. 67; Brief for
Respondent 29.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000V7"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2N1RNT0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VH"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2N1RNT0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Second Circuit expressly
sidestepped the question whether Horn suffered a personal injury by ingesti=
ng
Dixie X. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2N1RNT0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>80 F. 4th, at
135, n. 2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The only court to have passed on the issue&#8212;the
District Court&#8212;expressed different views at different points. See <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2N1RNT0030000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>supra</i>, at 616-617.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000V6"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2SF7VN0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I would not decide whether losses
flowing from personal injuries are injuries to business or property in a ca=
se
where no one knows whether the plaintiff suffered a personal injury in the
first place. If Horn did not suffer a personal injury, then our resolution =
of
the question presented will have no bearing on this case. Because federal
courts may not &#8220;render advisory opinions . . .&nbsp;[**949]&nbsp;
advising what the law would&nbsp;[****31]&nbsp; be upon a hypothetical stat=
e of
facts,&#8221; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2N1RNT0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BGH0-003B-S1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Preiser</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BGH0-003B-S1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BGH0-003B-S1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Newkirk</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BGH0-003B-S1WM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 422 U. S. 3=
95,
401, 95 S. Ct. 2330, 45 L. Ed. 2d 272 (1975)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted), we ordinarily do not decide matters unl=
ess
we know that our decision will have some import for the parties before us.
Given the lack of clarity on whether Horn suffered a personal injury, we ca=
nnot
be assured that our resolution of the question presented would affect the
parties here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000V5"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2SF7VN0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Even if resolving a question that
might not affect these parties is &#8220;a permissible course, it does not
strike me as the &nbsp;[*619]&nbsp; most sensible one.&#8221; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFM2SF7VN0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6C0C-1NY3-S9D5-J4RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Warner Chappe=
ll
Music, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6C0C-1NY3-S9D5-J4RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6C0C-1NY3-S9D5-J4RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nealy</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6C0C-1NY3-S9D5-J4RB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 601 U. S. 3=
66,
376, 144 S. Ct. 1135, 218 L. Ed. 2d 363 (2024)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<span
