<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Court&#8217;s opinion underscores why we should dismiss the writ of certior=
ari
as improvidently granted. <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VN"></=
a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W50020000400"></a>Perhaps due&nbsp;[****35]&nb=
sp; to
the &nbsp;[**951]&nbsp; difficulties described above, the Court declines to
resolve the question presented. It also declines to offer definitive guidan=
ce
on what it means for a plaintiff to be &nbsp;[***498]&nbsp; &#8220;injured =
in
his business or property.&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Instead, as <span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Justice Kavanaugh</span>
explains, &#8220;the Court charts an unusual middle way.&#8221; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W50010000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Post</i>, at 626 (dissenting opinion). It affirms a component of the
Second Circuit&#8217;s decision that no party seems to dispute. It then def=
ines
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>one word</i> within the disputed
statutory phrase, leaving the most critical and outcome-determinative issues
for another day. If these limited holdings are the most we can offer, then =
our
grant of certiorari was plainly improvident.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VP"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W50040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000TS"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2HM64C0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court affirms the Second
Circuit&#8217;s conclusion that civil RICO &#8220;does not preclude recovery
for all economic harms that result from personal injuries.&#8221; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W50030000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 614. In other words, the Court affirms the Second
Circuit&#8217;s determination that civil RICO does not contain a so-called
&#8220;antecedent-personal-injury bar.&#8221; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W50050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>80 F. 4th, at=
 137</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
But, as far as I can tell, no party has ever contested that point.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
&#8220;antecedent-personal-injury bar&#8221; appears to be a concept invent=
ed
by the Second Circuit. The bar, if applied to civil RICO, would preclude a
plaintiff&nbsp;[****36]&nbsp; from recovering for an injury to business or
property simply because the injury happens to follow a personal injury in t=
he
causal chain. In other words, the theory goes, once the plaintiff suffers a
personal &nbsp;[*623]&nbsp; injury, he may not recover for any subsequent
injuries to his business or property that follow or flow from it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VR"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2HM64C0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VS"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2HM64C0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In my view, the majority
misunderstands Medical Marijuana to be advocating for that rule. See <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2HM64C0020000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>ante</i>, at 609. The majority describes the &#8220;only
question&#8221; before us as &#8220;whether civil RICO bars recovery for all
business or property harms that derive from a personal injury.&#8221; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2HM64C0040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 600. But, that reformulation of the question presented
miscasts Medical Marijuana&#8217;s argument. Medical Marijuana concedes that
some harms to business or property are actionable under civil RICO, even if
they follow or flow from a personal injury. See Brief for Petitioners 33-34.
Its point is that not <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>all</i> harms=
 to
business or property are <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injuries</=
i> to
business or property under civil RICO. In its view, some economic
harms&#8212;such as medical expenses, or, as relevant here, some forms of l=
ost
wages&#8212;are better conceived of as <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>damages</i>.
Thus, the question squarely before us is whether the economic harms in this
case&#8212;Horn&#8217;s lost wages&#8212;constitute a cognizable <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injury</i> to business or property und=
er
civil RICO or are instead personal-injury <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>damages</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VT"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I agree with the Court&#8217;s
conclusion that civil RICO does not bar recovery for <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>all</i> economic harms that result from a personal injury. So do the
other dissenters. <a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000TP"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0010000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Post</i>, at 644 (opinion of <span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps=
'>Kavanaugh,
J.</span>). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0040000400_2"></a>And, as far=
 as I
can tell, so do all the parties and the court below. See Brief for Petition=
ers
34; Brief for Respondent 18-21; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:690H-9651-JXNB-60B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>80 F. 4th, at=
 137</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We do not ordinarily take a case to settle an issue on which there is no
disagreement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VV"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2D6MVH0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court&#8217;s other holding =
is
that &#8220;injured&#8221; in civil RICO means &#8220;harmed.&#8221; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP28T42P0050000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 600-601. But, defining one word within the phrase
&#8220;injured in his business or property,&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
sidesteps the core of the&nbsp;[****37]&nbsp; parties&#8217; disagreement.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[***499]&nbsp;
The parties dispute the meaning of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>phrase
</i>&#8220;injured in his business or property.&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Although the parties &nbsp;[*624]&nbsp; would define &#8220;injured&#8221; =
in
slightly different terms, their biggest disagreement concerns <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>what</i> is injured or harmed. Accordi=
ng to
Horn, a plaintiff can bring a civil RICO suit so long as his
business&#8212;broadly defined to mean &nbsp;[**952]&nbsp;
&#8220;everything&#8221; about employment&#8212;is harmed. Brief for Respon=
dent
12 (internal quotation marks omitted). And, according to Medical Marijuana,=
 a
plaintiff can bring a civil RICO suit only if his legally protected right in
his business or property is invaded (or, in other words, injured or harmed).
See Brief for Petitioners 11. The modest conclusion that &#8220;injured&#82=
21;
means &#8220;harmed&#8221; leaves the scope of the relevant harm unresolved=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VW"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2D6MVH0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000VX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2D6MVH0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court&#8217;s limited holdin=
g is
bound to &#8220;leave substantial confusion and litigation in its wake.&#82=
21; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2D6MVH0020000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Post</i>, at 648 (opinion of <span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps=
'>Kavanaugh,
J.</span>). Notwithstanding its decision to affirm, the Court is unwilling =
to
say that Horn has alleged a cognizable injury to his business under civil R=
ICO.
The Court makes clear that it is not deciding whether the Second Circuit
correctly interpreted &#8220;business&#8221; to encompass
&#8220;employment.&#8221; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2D6MVH0040000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i>, at 600. The Court also makes=
 clear
that it is not deciding the definition of &#8220;property.&#8221; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i> I appreciate the hesitation =
to
define an entire phrase in a case where neither party offers a complete
definition. But, rather than define one word in isolation, I would wait for=
 a
better case.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>***</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
decision below implicates a genuine Circuit split, but the
parties&nbsp;[****38]&nbsp; dispute a factbound, threshold issue that direc=
tly
bears on the question presented. And, the related issue of how to define
&#8220;injured in his business or property,&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
was not decided below and is inadequately briefed. I would therefore dismiss
the writ of certiorari as improvidently granted. I respectfully dissent.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;font-varia=
nt:
small-caps;color:black'>Justice Kavanaugh</span><span style=3D'font-family:=
"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, with whom <span style=3D'font-=
variant:
small-caps'>The Chief Justice</span> and <span style=3D'font-variant:small-=
caps'>Justice
Alito</span> join, dissenting.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000W0"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFP2SF7W60020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, known as RICO,
provides that any &#8220;person <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inj=
ured</i>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in his &nbsp;[*625]&nbsp; business =
or
property</i> by reason of a violation&#8221; of the Act &#8220;shall recover
threefold the damages he sustains.&#8221; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U. S. C.
&sect;1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
