<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Business
injury such as unfair competition and tortious interference with contract
eventually developed into its own standalone tort-law category, distinct fr=
om
personal or property injury. By the late 19th and early 20th centuries, cou=
rts
and commentators embraced the general principle that &#8220;to interfere wi=
th a
man&#8217;s trade by a malicious act is actionable&#8221; because it violat=
es
&#8220;a concrete right as distinct as his right to his lands and chattels,=
 one
which imposes on his fellows a correlative duty, the breach of which is a
tort.&#8221; E. McClennen, Some of the Rights of Traders and Laborers, 16 H=
arv.
L. Rev. 237, 237-238, 241 (1903); cf. F. Cooke, The Law of Combinations,
Monopolies and Labor Unions &sect;7 (2d ed. 1909). And by 1938, the First
Restatement included a division titled &#8220;Interference with Business
Relations&#8221; that &nbsp;[*630]&nbsp; described trade-related wrongs like
unfair competition&nbsp;[****45]&nbsp; and tortious interference with contr=
act.
See <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Restatement (First) of Torts, d=
iv. 9</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
short, tort law has long distinguished personal-injury suits from
business-injury or property-injury suits. And RICO incorporated that
traditional distinction into the statutory text. Like some of the States,
Congress could have decided to authorize RICO suits for any person who has =
been
&#8220;injured,&#8221; period, which would have covered personal-injury sui=
ts
as well as business- or property-injury suits. See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6348-FV61-DYB7-W1GS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ga. Code Ann.
&sect;16-14-6(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (2024); </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5C24-MT31-6SKW-D0X9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fla. Stat.
&sect;772.104(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (2023). But Congress instead
decided &nbsp;[***503]&nbsp; to limit civil RICO suits to plaintiffs who ha=
ve
been &#8220;injured in&#8221; their &#8220;business or property.&#8221;</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>More
specifically, RICO&#8217;s private right of action is available to a person=
 who
has suffered a business or property injury &#8220;by reason of a
violation&#8221; of RICO. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
And a RICO violation generally requires the defendant to have engaged in a
&#8220;pattern of racketeering activity,&#8221; where &#8220;racketeering
activity&#8221; includes conduct chargeable as any of a host of state- and
federal-law crimes, such as money laundering, extortion, and mail or wire
fraud, to take some common examples. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1962</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;&sect;1=
961(1)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(5)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The question for a court under civil RICO is simply whether the plaintiff w=
as
&#8220;injured in his business or property by reason of &#8221; whatever the
defendant did to violate RICO. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH8003RRRM1000WD"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFT2HM6500030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>One further important point: Und=
er
tort law, injury is distinct from the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>losses
or damages</i> that result from an injury. Since before the Founding, courts
have distinguished &#8220;injury&#8221; from &#8220;damage.&#8221; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFT2HM6500020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:625D-Y6T1-JT99-2472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Uzuegbunam</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:625D-Y6T1-JT99-2472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:625D-Y6T1-JT99-2472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Preczewski</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:625D-Y6T1-JT99-2472-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 592 U. S. 2=
79,
286, 141 S. Ct. 792, 209 L. Ed. 2d 94 (2021)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citing <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cable</i> v. <i style=3D'ms=
o-bidi-font-style:
normal'>Rogers</i>, 3 Bulst. 311, 312, 81 Eng. Rep. 259 (K. B. 1625)). Stat=
ed
simply, injury is &#8220;the illegal invasion of a legal right; damage is t=
he
loss, hurt, or harm which results from&nbsp;[****46]&nbsp; the injury.&#822=
1;
Ballentine&#8217;s, at 303; see Black&#8217;s Law Dictionary 466 (rev. 4th =
ed.
1968).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*631]&nbsp;
For example, negligently driving a car into a pedestrian inflicts a legal
injury on the pedestrian&#8212;wrongful invasion of the pedestrian&#8217;s
physical safety. The pedestrian&#8217;s losses or damages resulting from the
injury typically will include his lost wages and medical expenses, among ot=
her
things like pain and suffering. The injury (the hit from the negligently dr=
iven
car) gives the pedestrian a right to sue; the lost wages, medical expenses,=
 and
pain and suffering that follow are damages that a plaintiff &nbsp;[**956]&n=
bsp;
may be able to recover for the injury. See <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Restatement
(Second) of Torts &sect;&sect;910</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>912,
Comment a</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>All
of that means that when a victim suffers only a personal injury (such as fr=
om a
car accident or defective product), his lost wages and medical expenses are
merely the losses or damages that result from that personal injury, not
themselves a separate business or property injury&#8212;that is, not a dist=
inct
infringement of a legal right in one&#8217;s business or property.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Therefore,
a victim who suffers only a personal injury &#8220;by reason of &#8221; a R=
ICO
violation has not been &#8220;injured&#8221; in his &#8220;business or
property,&#8221; even if that personal injury leads him to lose wages or in=
cur
medical expenses. That victim may not sue under RICO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
Court&#8217;s antitrust precedents further confirm that RICO&#8217;s exclus=
ion
of personal-injury suits means what it says and cannot be circumvented
by&nbsp;[****47]&nbsp; recharacterizing personal-injury losses or damages (=
such
as lost wages or medical expenses) as their own standalone business or prop=
erty
injuries.</span></p>

