yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 603. But the Court&#8217;s nifty turn of phrase has no
substance behind it. Every one of the Court&#8217;s dictionaries&#8212;its
legal dictionaries, and even its generalist ones&#8212;includes the
rights-violation definition of &#8220;injure&#8221; or &#8220;injured,&#822=
1;
not just of &#8220;injury.&#8221; See Ballentine&#8217;s, at 627
(&#8220;injured party&#8221;); Black&#8217;s, at 924 (to &#8220;injure&#822=
1;);
Webster&#8217;s New International Dictionary 1111 (1913)
(&#8220;injured&#8221;); American Heritage Dictionary 676 (1969) (to
&#8220;injure&#8221;); Webster&#8217;s Third New International Dictionary 1=
164
(1971) (&#8220;injured&#8221;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFY2HM65G001000040=
0_2"></a>Those
sources do not draw the distinction that the Court creates in order to avoid
RICO&#8217;s exclusion of personal injuries. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn438' href=3D"#_ftn438" name=3D"_ftnref438" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*639]&nbsp;
Simply put, the Court&#8217;s attempt to find a key difference between
&#8220;injured&#8221; and &#8220;injury&#8221; rings hollow. And the
Court&#8217;s textual gymnastics do not end there. If the Court were correct
that injured simply means having suffered harm or losses or damages, then t=
he
statutory term &#8220;injured&#8221; would refer to the same thing as the
statutory term &#8220;damages.&#8221; But the statute distinguishes
&#8220;injured&#8221; from &#8220;damages&#8221; and makes clear they are n=
ot
the same thing. See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1964(c)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&#8220;Any person <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injured</i> in h=
is
business or property by reason of a violation&#8221; of RICO &#8220;shall
recover threefold the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>damages</i> he
sustains&#8221; (emphasis added)). The text instead makes clear that damages
are the losses that a victim sustains from an injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM10010F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFY2HM65G0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Faced with the
problem&nbsp;[****56]&nbsp; that its definition of injured refers to the sa=
me
thing as damages in this statute, the Court simply redefines
&#8220;damages&#8221; not to mean losses, but rather to mean only
&#8220;monetary redress&#8221; to be paid for the losses. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYFY2HM65G0020000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 603-604.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Court is surely correct that &#8220;damages&#8221; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>can</i> mean (i) losses suffered or (ii) monetary redress from a
lawsuit for those losses. In fact, the term &#8220;damages&#8221; is often =
used
in both ways. But in this statutory context, only the former
definition&#8212;losses&#8212;makes any sense. As used in the RICO statute,
&#8220;damages&#8221; are something &nbsp;[***509]&nbsp; that a victim
&#8220;sustains.&#8221; And the term &#8220;sustains&#8221; means to
&#8220;experience or suffer (loss or injury).&#8221; American Heritage, at
1296. A plaintiff cannot suffer or sustain &#8220;monetary redress,&#8221; =
as
the Court seems to think. But he <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ca=
n</i>
sustain losses.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>So
the term &#8220;damages&#8221; in RICO means losses, but that is exactly wh=
at
the Court &nbsp;[**961]&nbsp; says the term &#8220;injured&#8221; means. The
Court affords the same meaning to those different terms. In this context, t=
hat
is another clue that the Court&#8217;s analysis of the term
&#8220;injured&#8221; has gone off track. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn439' href=3D"#_ftn439" name=3D"_ftnref439" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*640]&nbsp;
In short, to reach its conclusion that RICO allows personal-injury suits
involving losses or damages to business or property, the Court reasons that
&#8220;injured&#8221;&nbsp;[****57]&nbsp; means something different from
&#8220;injury.&#8221; And it changes the statutory definition of
&#8220;damages&#8221; to avoid that word carrying the same meaning as the
Court&#8217;s reading of &#8220;injured.&#8221; Neither of those efforts is
persuasive or permissible as an interpretation of RICO&#8217;s text, in my
view.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000Y6"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG02D6MWS0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The correct reading is instead t=
he
textually straightforward one: A person &#8220;injured in his business or
property&#8221; is a person who has suffered injury in a business or proper=
ty
right as traditionally defined&#8212;the violation of a legal right in his
business or property. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000Y7"=
></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG02D6MWS0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG02D6MWS0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4390-003B-H24X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Keogh</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4390-003B-H24X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 260 U. S., =
at
163, 43 S. Ct. 47, 67 L. Ed. 183</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Civil RICO allows a person to
&#8220;recover damages&#8221; if the damages &nbsp;[*641]&nbsp; result from=
 a
business or property injury, but not if the damages result from a personal
injury. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG02D6MWS0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG02D6MWS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4390-003B-H24X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4390-003B-H24X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 162, 43 =
S.
Ct. 47, 67 L. Ed. 183</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn440' href=3D"#_ftn440" name=3D"_ftnref440" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM10010H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM10010J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG128T4420010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Court also contends that
precedent compels us to read &#8220;injured&#8221; as meaning &#8220;harmed=
 or
damaged,&#8221; not as a tort-law term of art meaning invasion
&nbsp;[***510]&nbsp; of a legal right. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG12N1RRM0050000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Ante</i>, at 601; see <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG128T4420020000400"=
></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ante</i>, at 604-606. But the Court do=
es not
cite any cases &nbsp;[**962]&nbsp; actually holding as much. Instead, the C=
ourt
offers out-of-context citations of isolated mentions of &#8220;harm&#8221; =
in
other cases.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YC"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG128T4420050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In relying on passages pulled ou=
t of
context from judicial opinions, the Court makes the mistake of parsing
&#8220;the language of&nbsp;[****58]&nbsp; an opinion&#8221; &#8220;as thou=
gh
we were dealing with language of a statute.&#8221; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG128T4420040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reiter</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sonotone Corp=
.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-83V0-003B-S18D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 442 U. S. 3=
30,
341, 99 S. Ct. 2326, 60 L. Ed. 2d 931 (1979)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court seizes in particular on the word &#8220;harm,&#8221; which is not=
 in
the RICO statute. The Court says that injury simply means harm as ordinarily
understood. So any harm to business or property is its own distinct busines=
s or
property <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>injury</i>, the Court says=
. But
that is wrong as a matter of elementary tort law. As the Restatement plainly
says, &#8220;harm, which is merely personal loss or detriment, gives rise t=
o a
cause of action only when it results from the invasion of a legally protect=
ed
interest, which is to &nbsp;[*642]&nbsp; say an injury.&#8221; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Restatement (Second) of Torts &sect;7,
Comment d</i>. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn441' href=3D"#_ftn441"
name=3D"_ftnref441" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YK"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG12SF7XM0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YM"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YN"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG12SF7XM0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YT"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YV"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YW"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG22N1RRX0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, contrary to the
Court&#8217;s telling, multiple civil RICO precedents confirm that to be
&#8220;injured&#8221; in civil RICO means to have suffered &#8220;legal
injury,&#8221; not merely to have suffered harm or loss or damage of some k=
ind.
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG12SF7XM0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> v. </span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Phoenix Bond
&amp; Indemnity Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 553 U. S. 6=
39,
656, 128 S. Ct. 2131, 170 L. Ed. 2d 1012 (2008)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bridge</i>, for example, the Cou=
rt
explained that whether an element is required in order to state a civil RICO
claim turns on whether that element is necessary to show &#8220;legally
cognizable injury&#8221; under &#8220;general common-law principle[s].&#822=
1; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ibid.</i> </span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn442' href=3D"#_ftn442" name=3D"_ftnref442" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG12SF7XM0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4048-9TB0-004B-Y032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Beck v. </spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4048-9TB0-004B-Y032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Prupis</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the Court likewise held that when civil RICO says that anyone
&#8220;injured&#8221; by reason of a
&#8220;violation&#8221;&nbsp;[****59]&nbsp; may sue, it means that a plaint=
iff
may recover only if &#8220;injured&#8221; by a &#8220;violation&#8221; under
&#8220;well-established &nbsp;[*643]&nbsp; common law&#8221; principles.
&nbsp;[***511]&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG128T4460010000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4048-9TB0-004B-Y032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>529 U. S. 494,
500-501, 506, 120 S. Ct. 1608, 146 L. Ed. 2d 561 (2000)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn443' href=3D"#_ftn443" name=3D"_ftnref443" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM10010K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG228T44J0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Those <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Bridge </i>and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Beck </i>pre=
cedents
directly contradict the Court&#8217;s test today &nbsp;[**963]&nbsp; that
RICO&#8217;s private right of action &#8220;requires nothing more&#8221; th=
an
that a plaintiff &#8217;s &#8220;business or property has been harmed or
damaged.&#8221; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG22SF7XW0050000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i>, at 601. Under this Court&#82=
17;s
precedents, more is certainly required. Specifically, the plaintiff must ha=
ve
suffered a business or property injury as those terms are understood under
general tort principles&#8212;meaning an invasion of a legal right in his
business or property.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal37 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Court further says that distinguishing personal injuries from business or
property injuries, as the text of RICO requires, would be difficult at time=
s. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM1000YY"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_II08GRN6YPH9003RRRM100100"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG228T44J0030000400"></a>To be sure, as with almost
everything in the law, there may be close calls at the margins&#8212;here,
about whether a plaintiff plausibly alleges a business or property injury as
distinct from a personal injury. But that inquiry is at least a familiar ju=
dicial
exercise. Cf. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I6FGFYG228T44J0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RJ6-FCN0-003B-R0KW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
</span></i></a><a
