<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
DiFolco referred in her complaint to her e-mails to Kaplan of August 23, 20=
05,
and August 24, 2005, the District Court could deem them incorporated in the
complaint and therefore subject to consideration in its review of the adequ=
acy
of &nbsp;[**20]&nbsp;the complaint. Although DiFolco wrote in her e-mail of
August 23 that she wished to &quot;discuss [her] exit from the shows&quot; =
and
to give MSNBC &quot;ample time to replace [her],&quot; these statements can=
 be
taken as indicative of her desire to get out from under the direction of the
people with whom she had problems rather than leaving MSNBC altogether. Als=
o,
her statement that she wanted to &quot;be a part of [MSNBC's] team for a lo=
ng
time to come&quot; and that she asked for a meeting with Kaplan to discuss
matters further may point in the direction of her desire to continue employ=
ment
on a different level. DiFolco's e-mail to Kaplan on the very next day, Augu=
st
24, in which DiFolco inquired about the cancellation of her fashion week
assignment, included the following:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>[T]o
be clear, I did not resign yesterday and was really giving you significant
notice of my intention so you could begin thinking about alternatives for n=
ext
year. [A]s always, count on my continued professionalism.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kaplan's
e-mail response of August 28, 2005, ignored the foregoing altogether and st=
ated
the following: &quot;My complete impression is that you had resigned . .
.&quot; and &quot;your obvious intent is to leave.&quot; Kaplan may well ha=
ve
put this &nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;&quot;spin&quot; on the earlier e-mail in an att=
empt
to rid MSNBC of an employee he considered troublesome.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J570030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In any event, we do not agree wi=
th
the District Court that &quot;[p]laintiff's 8/23 e-mail is . . . unambiguou=
s in
expressing [DiFolco's] intention to leave,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J570020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiFolco</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2007 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 23440, 2007 WL 959085, at *3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, as a matter of law. There are =
at
least factual issues as to whether DiFolco had made a final and definite
communication of an intent to forego performance or had indicated her
&nbsp;[*113]&nbsp; refusal to perform in a clear and unqualified way such a=
s to
justify a conclusion that she had repudiated her contract.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
further support for its conclusion that DiFolco had repudiated her contract,
the District Court referred to an e-mail communication from DiFolco to Kapl=
an,
dated August 31, 2005, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see id</i>.,
following Kaplan's August 28 e-mail that included language that it would be
&quot;[b]est for all&quot; that DiFolco leave &quot;quickly&quot; and that
&quot;sooner is better.&quot; The August 31 e-mail was not attached to the
complaint, was not incorporated by reference in the complaint, and was not
integral to the complaint. Neither was it relied upon in any way in DiFolco=
's
claim for breach of contract. Moreover, it was sent after Kaplan's August 24
e-mail &nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;setting forth his &quot;complete impression&quot; =
that
DiFolco had resigned and terminated her employment. The District Court
therefore erred in considering the August 31 e-mail.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
the August 31 e-mail was ambiguous as well. On the one hand, as the District
Court noted, DiFolco wrote: &quot;It was my intention to give you long-term
notice that I was terribly disappointed in the way I was treated and was not
planning on returning for the second year of my contract.&quot; On the other
hand, DiFolco wrote in the same e-mail: &quot;So we went from your agreemen=
t to
meet with me to discuss the issues to you having a 'complete impression' of=
 my
resignation, which was never given. . . . Even if you were unclear from my
original e-mail, . . . what part of 'I did not resign yesterday and was mer=
ely
giving you significant notice of my intention so you could begin thinking a=
bout
alternatives for next year' in my follow-up did you not understand? No noti=
ce
was given, no formal letter of resignation was sent. No time frames were
offered.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J570050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The August 31 e-mail is a lengthy
one and cannot as a matter of law be said to manifest a clear and unequivoc=
al
repudiation. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_33"></a>In ruling on a motion
pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, &nbsp;[**23]&nbsp;the duty of a court &quot;is merely =
to
assess the legal feasibility of the complaint, not to assay the weight of t=
he
evidence which might be offered in support thereof.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J570040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SCG-RJS0-0038-X37G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cooper v. Par=
sky</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SCG-RJS0-0038-X37G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 140 F.3d 43=
3,
440 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal citation and quotation
marks omitted). The District Court here &quot;assay[ed] the weight of the
evidence&quot; of repudiation and improperly chose between reasonably compe=
ting
interpretations. Because we find that a claim has been stated for a breach =
of
contract and that repudiation has not been established as a matter of law, =
we
reinstate DiFolco's breach of contract claim as well as her corresponding c=
laim
brought pursuant to the provisions of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6D37-T4K3-RS5V-X0NF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York Labor
Law &sect;&sect; 190-199-a</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> for failure to pay wages.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.
The Defamation Causes of Action</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J580020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The District Court determined th=
at
two of the allegedly defamatory statements -- the statement on the &quot;In=
side
Cable&quot; website that DiFolco had resigned from MSNBC &quot;in the middl=
e of
her contract&quot; and the statement on the &quot;NewsBlues&quot; website t=
hat
Claudia DiFolco has quit MSNBC in the middle of her contract&quot; --
&quot;[we]re true . . . and thus not actionable.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J580010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiFolco</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2007 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 23440, 2007 WL 959085, at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. This determination was based on
the court's conclusion that &nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;DiFolco repudiated her contra=
ct
as a matter of law, a conclusion that we have rejected as premature.
Accordingly, we are constrained to reinstate the defamation claims, which a=
re
pleaded properly to state claims in any event. The complaint alleges that t=
he
statements were untrue and defamatory and were published with malice and
&quot;with knowledge of their falsity and/or with a reckless disregard for
their truth or falsity.&quot; DiFolco further alleges that these
&nbsp;[*114]&nbsp; statements were &quot;willful&quot; and &quot;intended to
seriously harm [her]. . . career.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J580040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>According to the complaint, the
defamatory statements &quot;impugn Ms. DiFolco's honesty, trustworthiness,
dependability, and professional fitness and abilities by falsely charging h=
er
with conduct that would tend to injure her in her trade or business.&quot;
These and other allegations in the complaint are adequate to support a claim
based on a &quot;writing which tends to disparage a person in the way of [h=
er]
office, profession, or trade.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J580030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-XGY0-003C-C21H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nichols v. It=
em
Publishers, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-XGY0-003C-C21H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 309 N.Y. 5=
96,
132 N.E.2d 860, 862 (N.Y. 1956)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"T=
imes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J590010000400"></a>The complaint describes stat=
ements
that, &quot;if true, would tend to prove [DiFolco] unfit to continue [her]
calling.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. (internal
quotation marks and citations omitted). &nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;The complaint sets
forth the necessary elements to make out a claim for defamation in New York,
including the element of malice. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5900100004=
00_2"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See generally </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J580050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-7R30-003V-B1T2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Liberman v.
Gelstein</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-7R30-003V-B1T2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 80 N.Y.2d 4=
29,
605 N.E.2d 344, 349-50, 590 N.Y.S.2d 857 (N.Y. 1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J590030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J590050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As to the third alleged defamato=
ry
statement -- the TVSpy website statement that DiFolco relied on cleavage and
makeup to advance her career, ignored directions, refused &quot;alternate
takes,&quot; pouted, and was not a team player -- the District Court also w=
as
premature in its dismissal. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5B0040000400"><=
/a>The
District Court determined that &quot;a reasonable reader would conclude from
both the broader context and the immediate context that the third allegedly
defamatory statement consists of opinions, not facts.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J590020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiFolco</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2007 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 23440, 2007 WL 959085, at *8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. However, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_32"></a>&quot;'[o]pinions based on false fac=
ts are
actionable . . . against a defendant who had knowledge of the falsity or
probable falsity of the underlying facts.'&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J590040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JNC0-0039-P00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Davis v. Ross=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JNC0-0039-P00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 754 F.2d 80=
, 86
(2d Cir. 1985)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5B00100=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-11W0-0039-M40J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hotchner v.
Castillo-Puche</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-11W0-0039-M40J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 551 F.2d 91=
0,
913 (2d Cir. 1977)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5B0040000400_2"></a>Such knowledge of falsity =
is
properly alleged here, and DiFolco is entitled to show that the &quot;negat=
ive
characterization&quot; presented on the website &quot;is coupled with a cle=
ar
but false implication that &nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;the author is privy to facts a=
bout
the person that are unknown to the general reader.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5B0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JNC0-0039-P00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Davis</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JNC0-0039-P00S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 754 F.2d at
85-86</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C.
The Tortious Interference with Prospective Business Relations Claim</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0BHT5YYWX1000G44YV003SX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc7_30"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to recover for tortious interference with prospective business relati=
ons,
a plaintiff must plead and prove:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0010000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(1) business relations with a th=
ird
party; (2) the defendant's interference with those business relations; (3) =
the
defendant acted with the sole purpose of harming the plaintiff or used
dishonest, unfair or improper means; and (4) injury to the business relatio=
nship.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal38 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0010000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5B0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YXR-FVN0-0038-X2Y9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nadel v.
Play-By-Play Toys &amp; Novelties, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YXR-FVN0-0038-X2Y9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 208 F.3d 3=
68,
382 (2d Cir. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0030000400_2"></a>Noting the statement of co=
unsel
&quot;that Plaintiff relied solely on defamation as the wrongful act
predicating the claim of tortious interference&quot; and that it had dismis=
sed
the defamation claims, the District Court found no further basis for procee=
ding
with the tortious interference claim and dismissed it for failure to state a
claim. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiFolco</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4ND7-5XY0-TVW3-P2DM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2007 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 23440, 2007 WL 959805, at *8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51BFP3N0K9J5C0050000400_2"></a>Our reinstatement of the
defamation claim eliminates the District Court's rationale for dismissal of=
 the
