<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*62]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Miner</span>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants-appellants
appeal from judgments of conviction and sentence entered in the United Stat=
es
District Court for the Northern District of New York (Mordue, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>C.J</i>.), for violations of the Racke=
teer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-68</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. All were convicted under a
one-count indictment charging conspiracy to violate the provisions of </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> (prohibiting participation in the affairs of an enterpr=
ise
through a pattern of racketeering activity). The conspiracy was charged as a
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;1962(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Two of the defendants-appellants entered into plea
agreements &nbsp;[**3]&nbsp;prior to trial. A jury found that the remaining
five appellants agreed to participate in a pattern of racketeering activity
that included conspiracy to distribute and/or possess with intent to distri=
bute
50 or more grams of crack cocaine. The district court sentenced all
defendants-appellants to terms of imprisonment. The terms ranged from 214
months to life. The sentences included terms of supervised release of four =
to
five years and $100 special assessments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I82R1BM6T5R000077XR00003"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG760N0BBV0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On appeal, the defendants-appell=
ants
now bring a host of challenges to their convictions and sentences. We exami=
ne
with particularity three of the arguments raised by them: (1) whether the
existence of a RICO enterprise is a necessary element that the government m=
ust
prove to establish a RICO conspiracy in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>; (2) whether there was sufficient evidence to prove an
agreement that an enterprise would be established; and (3) whether the dist=
rict
court's failure to require unanimity as to which predicate acts the defenda=
nts
agreed to commit was reversible error, the district court having instructed=
 the
jury that it must find that each defendant agreed to commit at least two
racketeering acts and agree &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp;unanimously on the type of
racketeering acts each defendant agreed to commit. Because all of the
defendants' claims are ultimately without merit, for the reasons that follo=
w we
affirm the judgments of conviction in all respects but remand
defendant-appellant Gregory Thomas's case to the district court for
resentencing in light of </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG760N0BBV001000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4RB0-9510-TXFX-12KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kimbrough v.
United States</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4RB0-9510-TXFX-12KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 552 U.S. 85,
128 S. Ct. 558, 169 L. Ed. 2d 481 (2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I. <u>Prior
Proceedings</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
case arises out of the involvement of defendants-appellants in the Elk Block
gang. The gang's territory, located in Syracuse, New York, was bordered by
South Salina Street on the west, Interstate 81 on the east, Colvin Street on
the north, &nbsp;[*63]&nbsp; and McAllister Avenue on the south. On July 20,
2005, the seven defendants-appellants, Charmish Singletary, Dennis Jones,
Jerrawn Thomas, Gregory Thomas, William Robinson, Ismail Pierce, Ronnie Par=
nell
(collectively, the &quot;appellants&quot;), along with nine co-conspirator
defendants, Billy J. Applins, James Kelly, Nathan Speights, Joseph Derby,
Lonnie Singletary, Gregory Griffin, Andre Applins, Tyler Willis, Skyler Wil=
lis
(collectively, the &quot;defendants&quot;) (the defendants and appellants,
collectively, the &quot;Elk Block Gang&quot; or &quot;Elk Block&quot;), were
named &nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;in the indictment returned by a grand jury in the Un=
ited
States District Court for the Northern District of New York. Those named in=
 the
indictment were accused of being &quot;members and associates of a criminal
organization in Syracuse, New York, known as Elk Block, whose members and
associates engaged in murders, attempted murders, drug trafficking, witness
tampering </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn449' href=3D"#_ftn449"
name=3D"_ftnref449" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and other crimes within the
Northern District of New York and elsewhere.&quot; The indictment alleged t=
hat
the Elk Block Gang, including its &quot;leadership, members, and associates,
constituted an enterprise as defined by [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>]&quot; and that its members conspired to participate in=
 the
affairs of the Elk Block enterprise through a pattern of racketeering activ=
ity.
The indictment also alleged, with regard to the history and development of =
Elk
Block, that</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>[b]eginning
in or about 1995, the exact date being unknown to the grand jury, and
continuing thereafter up to the date of the indictment, in the Northern
District of New York and elsewhere, the defendants . . . together with othe=
rs
known and unknown to the grand jury, being persons employed by and associat=
ed
with the enterprise known as Elk Block, described in paragraphs
&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp;1 through 11 of this indictment, which enterprise engaged =
in,
and the activities of which affected, interstate and foreign commerce,
unlawfully, knowingly and intentionally, did combine, conspire, confederate,
and agree together and with each other and with others known and unknown to=
 the
grand jury, to violate [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;]1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, that is, to conduct and participate, directly and
indirectly, in the conduct of the affairs of the enterprise through a patte=
rn
of racketeering activity, as that term is defined in [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;]
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1961(5)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
consisting of multiple acts involving: (1) murder, in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6B90-WFN3-RWJT-S1FN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York Penal
Law sections 125.25</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N21-6RDJ-851D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>110.05</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N21-6RDJ-8515-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>105.17</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and (2) conspiracy to possess with intent to distribute more than 50 grams =
of
cocaine base (crack) and marijuana, as well as possession with intent to
distribute and distribution of more than 50 grams of cocaine base (crack) a=
nd
marijuana, in violation of [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G94-2YS3-RSC1-G3B3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>21 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect;] 841(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SDD-0C82-8T6X-73J2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>846</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
and (3) witness tampering, in violation of [</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0FY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;]
1512(b)(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
was &nbsp;[**7]&nbsp;part of the conspiracy that each defendant agreed that=
 a
conspirator would commit at least two acts of racketeering activity in the
conduct of the affairs of the enterprise.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
indictment described fifty-four racketeering acts, including multiple occas=
ions
of narcotics possession and distribution, firearms possession, shootings, a=
nd
murder.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pursuant
to a plea agreement executed March 21, 2006, appellant Charmish Singletary
pleaded guilty to the conspiracy charged in the indictment and admitted to
involvement in seven racketeering acts. By virtue of his plea, Singletary
admitted &nbsp;[*64]&nbsp; to having been responsible either directly or
through relevant conduct of his co-conspirators for the distribution of more
than 50 but less than 150 grams of crack-cocaine and for possession of a
dangerous weapon in connection with his drug trafficking activity. On May 4,
2007, Singletary received a sentence of a 214-month term of imprisonment, a
five-year term of supervised release, and a $100 special assessment. Judgme=
nt
was entered on May 9, 2007, and Singletary timely appealed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pursuant
to a sealed plea &nbsp;[**8]&nbsp;agreement executed February 27, 2006,
appellant Ronnie Parnell also pleaded guilty to the conspiracy charge. On M=
ay
9, 2007, Parnell received a sentence of a 45-month term of imprisonment, a
four-year term of supervised release, and a $100 special assessment. Parnel=
l's
term of imprisonment was reduced to 37 months on April 10, 2008, pursuant t=
o an
amendment to the crack-cocaine guidelines set forth in <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>U.S.S.G. &sect; 2D1.1(c)</i>. Parnell served his 37-month term of
imprisonment, but on November 20, 2008, the district court held a revocation
hearing at which Parnell admitted to violating five conditions of supervised
release. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG760N0BBV0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG760N0BBW0010000400"></a>The court then imposed a
fifty-month term of imprisonment for Parnell's violations of supervised
release. Judgment was entered December 1, 2008, and Parnell timely appealed=
 on
December 3, 2008. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn450' href=3D"#_ftn4=
50"
name=3D"_ftnref450" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition to appellants Singletary and Parnell, nine co-defendants, Billy J.
Applins, James Kelly, Nathan Speights, Joseph Derby, Lonnie Singletary, Gre=
gory
Griffin, Andre Applins, Tyler Willis, and Skyler Willis, pleaded guilty and
were sentenced to terms of imprisonment ranging from 37 to 121 months, term=
s of
supervised release of 4 to 5 years, and a $100 special assessment.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
remaining five appellants &#8212; Dennis Jones, Jerrawn Thomas, Gregory Tho=
mas,
William Robinson, and Ismail Pierce &#8212; proceeded to trial on the
indictment. The trial commenced on November 20, 2006. Cooperating defendant=
s,
who were former Elk Block gang members, and numerous law enforcement witnes=
ses
testified at trial regarding Elk Block's geographic territory, gang signs,
graffiti, clothing, tattoos, drug dealing, use of firearms, and utilization=
 of
violence for the protection and maintenance of control over the cocaine base
(crack) sales in Elk &nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;Block territory. At trial, there was
also evidence of a multitude of shootings and several murders. On December =
19,
2006, following four weeks of trial and two days of deliberations, the jury
rendered guilty verdicts as to all five defendants including findings that
Dennis Jones, Jerrawn Thomas, Gregory Thomas, William Robinson, and Ismail
Pierce each agreed to a pattern of racketeering activity that included
conspiracy to distribute and/or possess with intent to distribute 50 grams =
or
more of cocaine base.</span></p>

