<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
this case, we agree with the appellants that the district court's instructi=
ons
were conflicting and, in part, erroneous. Appellants requested the erroneous
instruction that the government was required to prove the existence of an
enterprise. The court gave the instruction as requested. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCP0040000400"></a>Although one of the appellan=
ts'
&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp;attorneys (Pierce) mentioned potential jury confusion, th=
ere
was never any suggestion as to how the inconsistency might be resolved.
Further, the court gave defense counsel its charge in writing before readin=
g it
to the jury, and no such suggestion to resolve the inconsistency was made.
Indeed, the appellants had an obvious interest in retaining the erroneous
&nbsp;[*76]&nbsp; portion of the instruction even if it was inconsistent wi=
th
the correct portion, since the erroneous instruction elevated the governmen=
t's
burden beyond what was required by law and what was set forth in the
indictment. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCR0010000400"></a><u>See, e.g.,=
 </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCP0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:404P-K710-0038-X119-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Naiman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:404P-K710-0038-X119-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 211 F.3d 40=
, 51
(2d Cir. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (stating that even with respect to preserved error,
reversal is required on the basis of error in jury instructions only if the
instructions, viewed as a whole, caused the defendant prejudice). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCR0010000400_2"></a>Nevertheless, we are not
persuaded that the defendants were prejudiced by the court's conflicting
instructions. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn453' href=3D"#_ftn453"
name=3D"_ftnref453" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
