<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.
<u>Sufficiency of the Evidence Challenges</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCR0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The appellants also contend that
there was insufficient evidence to support a finding of an agreement to for=
m an
enterprise. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_31"></a>As to sufficiency-of-the-evidence
challenges, we must credit every inference that could have been drawn in the
government's favor, and we must view the evidence as a whole. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0040000400"></a><u>See, e.g., </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4904-GB30-0038-X0M5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Hamilton</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4904-GB30-0038-X0M5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 334 F.3d 17=
0,
179 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2KM0-003B-P45P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Podlog</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2KM0-003B-P45P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 35 F.3d 699,
705 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0010000400"></a>Accordingly, &quot;[i]n
challenging the sufficiency of the evidence to support his conviction, a
defendant bears a heavy burden.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4904-GB30-0038-X0M5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hamilton</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4904-GB30-0038-X0M5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 334 F.3d at=
 179</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0030000400"></a>This Court &quot;defer[s] to=
 the
jury's determination of the weight of the evidence and the credibility of t=
he
witnesses, and to the jury's choice of the competing inferences that can be
drawn from the evidence.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TCW-KHV0-0038-X0SF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Morrison</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3TCW-KHV0-0038-X0SF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 153 F.3d 34=
, 49
(2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0010000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0050000400"></a>A conviction must be upheld =
if
&quot;any rational trier of fact could have found the essential elements
&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp;of the crime beyond a reasonable doubt.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCS0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8100-003B-S11K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Jackson v.
Virginia</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8100-003B-S11K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 443 U.S. 30=
7,
319, 99 S. Ct. 2781, 61 L. Ed. 2d 560 (1979)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0050000400_2"></a>If the court &quot;conclud=
es
that either of the two results, a reasonable doubt or no reasonable doubt, =
is
fairly possible, [the court] must let the jury decide the matter.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WWK-6S70-0038-X4F7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Guadagna</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WWK-6S70-0038-X4F7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 183 F.3d 12=
2,
129 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0050000400_3"></a>&quot;In cases of conspira=
cy,
deference to the jury's findings is especially important . . . because a
conspiracy by its very nature is a secretive operation, and it is a rare ca=
se
where all aspects of a conspiracy can be laid bare in court with the precis=
ion
of a surgeon's scalpel.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCT0040=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KTW-P8F0-0038-X4S3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Snow</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KTW-P8F0-0038-X4S3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 462 F.3d 55=
, 68
(2d Cir. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (citation and internal quotation marks omitted). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_29"></a>As to RICO conspiracy, the Supreme C=
ourt
has held that</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>[a]
conspirator must intend to further an endeavor which, if completed, would
satisfy all of the elements of a substantive criminal offense, but it suffi=
ces
that he adopt the goal of furthering or facilitating the criminal endeavor.=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCV0020000400"></a>He may do so in any number o=
f ways
short of agreeing to undertake all of the acts necessary for the crime's
completion. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52FCG780N0BCW0030000400"></a>One can be a
conspirator by agreeing to facilitate only some of the acts leading to the
substantive &nbsp;[**46]&nbsp;offense.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal39 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
