<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*229]&nbsp; <u>OPINION &amp; ORDER</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>PAUL
A. ENGELMAYER, District Judge:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
decision resolves a motion to dismiss a putative class action brought under=
 the
federal securities laws against defendants Coinbase Global, Inc., Coinbase,
Inc., and Brian Armstrong (&quot;Armstrong&quot;) (collectively with
Coinbase&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; Global, Inc., and Coinbase, Inc.,
&quot;Coinbase&quot; or &quot;defendants&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Coinbase
operates two online digital trading platforms on which users can transact in
the cryptoeconomy. The Amended Complaint (&quot;AC&quot;), Dkt. 43, alleges
that among the assets that Coinbase enables customers to buy and sell on th=
ese
platforms are ones qualifying as securities. It alleges that these include =
79
digital assets known as the &quot;Tokens.&quot; It alleges that,
notwithstanding Coinbase's practice of transacting in these securities with
plaintiffs and other users, Coinbase is not registered with the U.S. Securi=
ties
and Exchange Commission (&quot;SEC&quot;) as an exchange or broker-dealer.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
this basis, lead plaintiffs here&#8212;each of whom transacted in the Token=
s on
the Coinbase platform&#8212;bring three sets of claims: (1) under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-753X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 12(a)=
(1)
of the Securities Act of 1933 (the &quot;Securities Act&quot;)</span></i></=
a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
for damages arising from Coinbase's sale or solicitation of unregistered
securities, AC &para;&para; 931-41; (2) under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 29(b)=
 of
the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (the &quot;Exchange Act&quot;)</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
arising from illegal contracts Coinbase entered into with its users to purc=
hase
and sell securities in violation of the Exchange Act's registration
requirements, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.</i> &para;&para;
956-88; and (3) under state law, based on Coinbase's sale of unregistered
securities and failure to register as a broker-dealer, <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>id.</i> &para;&para;&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; 1016-1106. They also bring
control-person claims against Coinbase Global and its Chief Executive Offic=
er,
Armstrong, who allegedly orchestrated Coinbase's strategy to profit by
violating the securities laws, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id.<=
/i>
&para;&para; 942-55, 1002-15, 1038-49, 1094-1106. These claims are brought =
on
behalf of a nationwide class consisting of all persons or entities who
transacted in the Tokens on the Coinbase trading platforms between October =
8,
2019 and the AC's filing on March 11, 2022 (the &quot;class period&quot;), =
and
of subclasses keyed to citizens of three states who so traded during that
period.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*230]&nbsp;
Pending now is defendants' motion to dismiss the AC for failure to state a
claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rules=
 of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>8(a)</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Dkt. 69. For the reasons that follow, the Court grants in full the motion to
dismiss. The Court dismisses the federal claims with prejudice and the state
claims without prejudice.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Background</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. Factual Background</span></b>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn456' href=3D"#_ftn456" name=3D"_ftnref456" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. Crypto-Assets and Blockchains=
</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
case involves the modern financial concoctions known as crypto-assets. Also
sometimes called &quot;cryptocurrencies,&quot; &quot;tokens,&quot; or
&quot;crypto-securities,&quot; these are digital assets created and traded =
in
the digital world. AC &para; 19. Crypto-assets often rely on a technology k=
nown
as &quot;blockchain&quot;&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; to &quot;secure transactions,
control the creation of additional units, and verify their transfer.&quot; =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> Blockchain is a digital le=
dger
system that tracks the ownership and transfer of different kinds of
crypto-assets. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> &para;&p=
ara;
20-21.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Users
on the blockchain have a &quot;public key&quot; and a &quot;private key.&qu=
ot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> &para;&para; 24-26. A user=
 can
publicly identify herself to other users with her &quot;public key,&quot; w=
hich
is akin to a conventional bank account number an individual might share with
another who wishes to send the individual funds. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See id.</i> &para;&para; 24-25. A user also retains a &quot;private
key,&quot; which is akin to a pin code associated with the &quot;bank
account&quot; public key. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</=
i> The
blockchain makes use of the public keys and unique addresses associated
therewith to document transactions. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>See
id.</i> &para;&para; 20-21. For example, with the well-known crypto-asset k=
nown
as &quot;Bitcoin,&quot; &quot;[a] transfer of Bitcoin is public to the exte=
nt
that anyone can see the transferor's Bitcoin address, the recipient's Bitco=
in
address, and the quantity of assets transferred.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>Id.</i> &para; 27. By tracking who owns any given unit of a
crypto-asset at any given time, the blockchain allows for the secure exchan=
ge
of crypto assets, enabling users to&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; verify each transaction
involving that particular unit. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.=
</i>
&para; 22. This system, by documenting ownership, aims to prevent
counterfeiting. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>2. Crypto-Exchanges</span></b></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
crypto-asset ecosystem depends on digital exchanges known as
&quot;crypto-exchanges.&quot; &quot;Crypto-exchanges emerged to enable smoo=
ther
and faster trading between investors, just as stock and commodities exchang=
es
emerged to enable easy trading of securities.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Id.</i> &para; 28. Crypto-exchanges come in two primary forms:
decentralized and centralized. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.<=
/i>
&para; 29.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Decentralized
exchanges use different approaches. &quot;[W]hat they have in common is that
the crypto-assets are transferred between individual accounts.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> &para; 30. Such exchanges may =
be
analogized to Craigslist.com, the website that allows users to match direct=
ly
with other users to enable individualized transactions. <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>See id.</i> &para; 31. Decentralized exchanges, &quot;like Craigsli=
st,
do not own or hold the assets in question&#8212;they simply provide a platf=
orm
for exchanges between users, along with some features designed to facilitate
trading (for example, Craigslist's creation and maintenance of message boar=
ds
organized by product type or a decentralized exchange's smart contracts),
possibly in exchange&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; for advertising revenue or a transact=
ion
fee.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Centralized
exchanges play a more substantial role in transactions between individuals.
&nbsp;[*231]&nbsp; First, a customer must &quot;create an account&quot; on =
the
centralized exchange. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> &para=
; 32.
&quot;The exchange will then provide that customer with a deposit address t=
hat
the exchange controls.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>=
, Then
&quot;[w]hen the customer deposits crypto-assets into that deposit address,=
 the
exchange will credit her trading account with the corresponding
crypto-asset.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> &quot;T=
he
exchange will typically then transfer the crypto-assets into one of its oth=
er
addresses for storage.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i=
> When
that customer wants to make a transaction&#8212;say, to transfer one Bitcoi=
n to
the address of someone else&#8212;the centralized exchange &quot;then debits
the [customer's] account and transfers a corresponding amount of crypto-ass=
et
from the exchange's reserves to that address&quot; of the user with whom he=
 or
she wishes to transact. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> &pa=
ra;
42.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
