supplemental jurisdiction over these claims. These claims are dismissed wit=
hout
prejudice.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>D. Dismissals With and Without
Prejudice</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, the Court dismisses the AC.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal40 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
to plaintiffs' federal-law claims, the Court's assessment is that granting
leave to amend would be futile. Plaintiffs have already had an opportunity =
to
amend their complaint, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> Dkts=
. 1,
43, and as reviewed above, they did so by adding numerous allegations that
directly contradicted their initial Complaint. And plaintiffs have made onl=
y a
perfunctoty request for leave to amend anew in the event of the AC's dismis=
sal.
In their opposition to the motion to dismiss, they ask that &quot;permissio=
n to
replead be granted&quot; should the Court dismiss the claims. Pl. Opp. at 3=
0. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM28T3VC0040000400"></a>But plaintiffs have not iden=
tified
any concrete amendments or additions, let alone ones that might cure the
deficiencies afflicting their Securities Act and Exchange Act claims. This
supports&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; denial of leave to amend. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM2D6N0R0030000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See <a name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM28T3VC0030000400"></a>Gregory v. Pr=
oNAi
Therapeutics Inc., 757 F. App'x 35, 39 (2d Cir. 2018)</i> (affirming denial=
 of
leave to amend where &quot;plaintiffs sought leave to amend in a footnote at
the end of their opposition to defendants' motion to dismiss&quot; and
&quot;included no proposed amendments&quot;); <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM28T3VC0050000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>F5 Capital, 856 F.3d at 89</i> (affirming denial of leave to amend =
on
futility grounds where plaintiff provided &quot;no clue as to how the
complaint's defects would be cured through an amendment&quot; (internal
quotation marks omitted)). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM2D6N0R0030000400_2">=
</a>And
given the analysis above, it is difficult to see how any revised such claims
could overcome plaintiffs' factual averments in the Complaint and the terms=
 of
the User Agreement. &quot;In the absence of any identification of how a fur=
ther
amendment would improve upon the Complaint, leave to amend must be denied as
futile.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I67G74WM2D6N0R0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BDM-Y7H0-0038-Y00F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re WorldCo=
m,
Inc. Sec. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BDM-Y7H0-0038-Y00F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
