<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Kagan and the Klotsman defendants argue that State Farm's RICO claims must =
be
dismissed because New York's No-Fault regime preempts the application of the
RICO laws. These defendants also argue that the RICO claims are time-barred.
Finally, these defendants argue that plaintiff's pleadings concerning the R=
ICO
violations are defective because plaintiff has failed to properly allege: (=
i)
&quot;racketeering activity&quot;; (ii) that defendants proximately caused
plaintiff's injury; (iii) detrimental reliance on fraudulent representation=
s;
and (iv) that the &quot;enterprise&quot; affected interstate commerce. These
arguments are addressed in turn.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<u><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>Preemption</span></u></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_18"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1700000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The McCarran-Ferguson Act, </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-714W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1011-1015</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, is meant to protect an individ=
ual
state from inadvertent &nbsp;[**31]&nbsp;federal intrusion into the state's
sovereign right to regulate the business of insurance within its borders. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0XRG3THNMP000M333V00209"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1770000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ16Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RYJ-TWK0-003B-R1WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Barnett Bank =
v.
Nelson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RYJ-TWK0-003B-R1WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 517 U.S. 25,
39, 116 S. Ct. 1103, 134 L. Ed. 2d 237 (1996)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Under McCarran-Ferguson, a federal statute is preempted if (i) the statute =
does
not relate specifically to insurance; (ii) the acts challenged under the
statute constitute the &quot;business of insurance&quot;; (iii) the state h=
as
enacted laws which regulate the challenged acts; and (iv) the state laws wo=
uld
be &quot;invalidate[d], impaired or supersede[d]&quot; by application of the
federal statute. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1770000400_2"></a><u>See=
 </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1710000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3VKR-SDV0-004B-Y00N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Humana, Inc. =
v.
