<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
the Amended Complaint describes, in some detail, a complex, multi-layered
scheme involving over 80 defendants who allegedly devised, facilitated and/=
or
participated in the plan to defraud an insurance company of monies through =
the
submission of fraudulent medical bills. Given the nature of the scheme alle=
ged,
it is clear that each moving defendant against whom a RICO claim is made ei=
ther
directly used the mails or could have reasonably foreseen the use of the ma=
ils
to execute the alleged scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff
satisfies </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> by
pleading, with sufficient detail, hundreds of statements alleged to be
fraudulent. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1H90000400"></a>Plaintiff has
identified the &quot;speakers&quot;, that is, creators of the false insuran=
ce
claims, &nbsp;[**41]&nbsp;and has proffered an explanation as to why these
speakers' statements are fraudulent. Further, plaintiff provides, in multip=
le
exhibits to its Amended Complaint, an extensive sampling of statements alle=
ged
to be fraudulent, including dates of mailing, corresponding claim numbers,
entities which submitted many of the claims, the price allegedly paid by the
submitting entity and the price charged to plaintiff. Although the exhibits=
 to
the Amended Complaint list many but not all of the individual statements
alleged to be fraudulent, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24_5"></a>&quot;<=
/span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not require that each specific misrepresentation be identified where an
ongoing fraudulent scheme is alleged&quot;; rather, &quot;the purpose of </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
to give defendants fair notice of the fraud alleged against them.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1H80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VC9-0HN0-TXFR-J2VR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Liguori</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VC9-0HN0-TXFR-J2VR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 589 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 237</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I0XRG3TJGKJ000M333V002BN"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1HC0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J00000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <u>Sterling National Bank v. =
A-1
Hotels International, Inc</u>., Judge Lynch considered an alleged scheme, in
violation of RICO laws, where defendants submitted to plaintiff &quot;in ex=
cess
of two hundred . . . fraudulent and unauthorized charges.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1HB0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42N3-0G80-0038-Y1HK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>No. 00-CV-735=
2,
2001 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 2997, 2001 WL 282687, at *3, *12 (S.D.N.Y. Mar. 22, 2=
001)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Although the <u>Sterling National</u> Complaint alleged over two hundred
fraudulent statements, it &nbsp;[**42]&nbsp;provided only &quot;nine specif=
ic
examples&quot; to demonstrate the pattern of fraud and then alleged the
participation of each member in that scheme. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1HY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:42N3-0G80-0038-Y1HK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2001 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 2997, [WL] at *3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J20000400"></a>Similarly, here, plaintiff h=
as
alleged, in great detail, the fraudulent conduct, replete with examples of
fraudulent statements, explanations for why the statements were fraudulent =
and
the identity of the particular fraudulent statements provided. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J20000400_2"></a>Further, plaintiff has all=
eged
each defendant's, including the Klotsman defendants', role in those stateme=
nts.
When plaintiff's detailed examples are &quot;viewed in conjunction with the
conduct described in the complaint (<u>i.e</u>., how defendants routinely
submit[ted] claims for unnecessary, expensive cervical collars . . .), the
specific fraud is evident.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4HMG-J0K0-TVW3-P2YJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Olmecs</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4HMG-J0K0-TVW3-P2YJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2005 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 29666, 2005 WL 3710370, at *12</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <u>see also</u> =
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S7T-JY40-TXFR-J1V7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Grafman</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S7T-JY40-TXFR-J1V7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 04-CV-2=
609,
slip op. at 12, 2007 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 96751 (Gold, M.J.)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the Amended Complaint sets forth facts supporting a strong inference of
fraudulent intent. Accepting plaintiff's allegations as true for the purpos=
es
of this motion, plaintiff's allegations that the Klotsman defendants repeat=
edly
sought reimbursement for medical treatment, tests and durable medical equip=
ment
at artificially high prices or for &nbsp;[**43]&nbsp;equipment or tests that
were not medically necessary or, in some cases, never performed at all, sho=
ws
intentional misbehavior.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
the Klotsman defendants' contention that plaintiff has not properly alleged
&quot;racketeering activity&quot; is without merit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Proximate Causation</span></i></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc25_3"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1J=
R0000400"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
plaintiff can succeed on a RICO claim only if it is injured &quot;by reason
of&quot; a defendant's racketeering activity. &nbsp;[*229]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. In the RICO context, proximate cause requires that the=
re
be some direct relation between the injury asserted and the injurious condu=
ct
alleged. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1JP0000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza v. Ideal
Steel Supply Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 547 U.S. 4=
51,
461, 126 S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720 (2006)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Kagan and the Klotsman defendants argue that plaintiff's RICO claims must f=
ail
because plaintiff cannot show that the alleged scheme was the proximate cau=
se
of plaintiff's losses. Specifically, these defendants contend that State Fa=
rm's
profits are regulated by the State of New York and argue that, because some
aspect of plaintiff's profit is pre-approved by the State, even if plaintiff
had not paid the allegedly fraudulent charges, &quot;it would not result in
additional profits for State Farm&quot;; rather, defendants argue, in the
absence of fraudulent charges, consumers may &nbsp;[**44]&nbsp;have received
lower premiums. Therefore, defendants contend, the actual victims of the
alleged scheme are patients insured by plaintiff. Relying on <u>Anza v. Ide=
al
Steel, id</u>., defendants argue that plaintiff's claims fail because (i)
plaintiff has not suffered a direct injury; (ii) plaintiff's RICO claims
&quot;create problems in apportioning damages among potential victims&quot;;
and (iii) other victims could better &quot;vindicate the policies underlying
RICO.&quot; These arguments are without merit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1JT0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants' reliance on <u>Anza<=
/u>
is misplaced. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I0XRG3TJX30000M333V002BS"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KD0000400"></a>In <u>Anza</u>, the plaintif=
f's
RICO claim was based on an allegation that the defendant had defrauded the =
New
York State Department of Taxation and Finance and, in so doing, had injured
defendant's competitors by charging artificially low prices. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1JS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 457-58</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KD0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KG0000400"></a>The <u>Anza</u> court reject=
ed the
plaintiff's claims, holding that the injury to competitors was too attenuat=
ed,
as &quot;[i]t was the State that was being defrauded and the State that lost
tax revenue as a result.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 458</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KG0000400_2"></a>The situation in <u>Anza</=
u> is
inapposite, indeed, almost opposite, to the allegations here where defendan=
ts,
through the control, by certain defendants such as Kagan, of medical provid=
ers,
retailers and wholesalers &nbsp;[**45]&nbsp;are alleged to have submitted f=
alse
and fraudulent claims for reimbursement directly to plaintiff, and plaintiff
relied on receipt of these fraudulent claims in paying monies directly to
defendants, an injury far more direct than any alleged in <u>Anza</u>. The
Amended Complaint alleges that the plaintiff suffered &quot;damages of more
than $ 780,000&quot; as a result of the defendants' conspiracy to commit the
predicate acts of mail fraud. Therefore, &quot;[t]his argument must be reje=
cted
out of hand.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KF0000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPT Med. Svcs=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 2008 U.S.
Dist. LEXIS 71156, 2008 WL 4146190, at *13</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that &quot;State Farm's financial losses flow directly from the
fraudulent scheme, and it is inconsequential that State Farm may be able to
recoup some of those losses through an increase in future premium&quot;). T=
he
Amended Complaint alleges a direct injury to State Farm and does not create=
 a
problem in apportioning damages.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, the court rejects defendant Kagan's argument that plaintiff has n=
ot
pled proximate cause because plaintiff was not injured by Kagan so long as
services were provided to Insureds. Plaintiff's allegations against defenda=
nt
Kagan are not limited to an allegation that Kagan induced medical professio=
nals
to permit him ownership and control &nbsp;[**46]&nbsp;of medical providers
which are required by New York law to be owned and operated by medical
personnel (which has been held sufficient for a claim of common law fraud u=
nder
New York law, <u>supra</u>). Rather, the allegations of Kagan's role in
fraudulent incorporation must be taken together with allegations that he
improperly controlled medical providers, dictated the medical services
provided, allowed particular health care providers to &nbsp;[*230]&nbsp;
service patients in exchange for kickbacks and improperly siphoned profits =
to
himself under the guise of management fees. These facts, if true, plausibly
imply that State Farm was damaged by paying for improper or unnecessary
services and supplies, which were requested, at least in part, by non-medic=
al
personnel. Therefore, plaintiff has sufficiently pled that defendant Kagan
proximately caused injury for the purposes of a RICO violation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Detrimental Reliance</span></i><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KR0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendant Kagan, without
accompanying explanation or argument, states that plaintiff's RICO allegati=
ons
must be dismissed because plaintiff does not allege &quot;detrimental
reliance&quot; on the fraudulent conduct of defendants. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KW0000400"></a><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn471'
href=3D"#_ftn471" name=3D"_ftnref471" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc26_3"></a>While a plaintiff is required to est=
ablish
&quot;'reasonable reliance' on the defendants' &nbsp;[**47]&nbsp;purported
misrepresentations&quot; in order to prevail in a civil RICO action, </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BRC-VHM0-0038-X450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bank of China,
N.Y. Branch v. NBM LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4BRC-VHM0-0038-X450-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 359 F.3d 17=
1,
178 (2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, an allegation of reliance &quo=
t;is
subject to the liberal pleading requirement set forth in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P12-XYK0-TVW3-P2Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Repub. of
Colombia</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P12-XYK0-TVW3-P2Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 531 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 382</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Therefore, so long as plaintiff has alleged facts that
&quot;plausibly&quot; constitute its reasonable reliance on defendants'
misrepresentations, those allegations will be sufficient. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KW0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1KV0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Twombly</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 550 U.S. at=
 570</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal41 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
plaintiff alleges that defendant Kagan induced physicians to falsely repres=
ent
that they owned and operated certain medical companies. Plaintiff contends =
that
defendant Kagan secretly owned and operated these medical companies in
violation of New York law and that, under Kagan's control, these companies
submitted numerous fraudulent medical claims to plaintiff. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1M40000400"></a>Plaintiff also alleges that =
Kagan
controlled, or participated in the operation of, Retailers which engaged in=
 a
secret kickback system to facilitate the &nbsp;[**48]&nbsp;submission of cl=
aims
for reimbursement at artificially inflated prices. Plaintiff alleges that,
relying on the misrepresentations of Kagan and his companies, it paid bills=
 it
was not obligated to pay. According to plaintiff, because of defendants'
attempts to conceal the fraud, it &quot;should not have reasonably
discovered&quot; the fraud until shortly before it filed suit. These
allegations amply support a plausible scenario of reasonable reliance on
fraudulent claims. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1M30=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VWB-TVB0-TXFR-J283-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Halima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VWB-TVB0-TXFR-J283-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2009 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 22443, 2009 WL 750199, at *4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XFDYHJ0K1MNJ1M50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPT Med Servs=
</span></i></a><a
