style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*86]&nbsp;
MESKILL, Circuit Judge:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
defendant Paul Mazzei (Mazzei) appeals from the judgment of the
United&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; States District Court for the Eastern District of N=
ew
York, Bramwell, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>J.,</i> convicting =
him,
after a four week jury trial, of conspiracy to violate the Racketeer Influe=
nced
and Corrupt Organizations Act, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>et seq.</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (1976 &amp;
Supp. V 1981)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (RICO), conspiracy to commit sports bribery, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5032-D6RV-H523-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
224 (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and interstate travel with the intent to commit briber=
y, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1952 (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Mazzei was sentenced to ten years imprisonment on the =
RICO
count, and concurrent five year prison terms on the remaining two counts.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Facts</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mazzei's
conviction arises from his involvement in the Boston College (B.C.) <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ46Y0000400"></a>&quot;point shaving&quot;
scheme.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The facts pertinent t=
o this
criminal enterprise, which gained national prominence in February of 1981 w=
hen
Sports Illustrated (SI) published &nbsp;[*87]&nbsp; an article exposing the
scheme, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn475' href=3D"#_ftn475"
name=3D"_ftnref475" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> are discussed extensively in the
opinion that has been filed simultaneously with this decision.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ46X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0SW0-003B-G3TX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Burke,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0SW0-003B-G3TX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 700 F.2d 70 =
(2nd
Cir. 1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We refer the reader to that opinion for a more complete
discussion of this scandal and therefore limit our discussion of the facts =
to
those implicating Mazzei in the B.C. conspiracy.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp;
The evidence presented at trial revealed that three general groups of
individuals were responsible for devising and implementing the point shaving
scheme.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The &quot;Pittsburgh
Connection&quot; consisted of Rocco Perla, his brother Anthony Perla, and
appellant Mazzei.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>They appear=
 to
have initially conceived of the point shaving concept and were able to enli=
st
the cooperation of Richard Kuhn, a B.C. basketball player who was a high sc=
hool
friend of Rocco Perla.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The
&quot;New York Connection&quot; consisted of Henry Hill and James Burke, who
were responsible for creating a bookmaking syndicate to bet on B.C. games a=
nd
arranging &quot;protection&quot; for the conspirators.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The third group of individuals
implicated in the scheme were the &quot;insiders,&quot; those members of the
B.C. basketball team who agreed to &quot;shave points&quot; in certain
preselected games. The players received payments, usually $2,500 per game, =
in
exchange for ensuring that B.C. did not beat the &quot;point spread&quot; in
those games where the betting syndicate wagered against B.C.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mazzei
acted largely as a &quot;middleman&quot; in this conspiracy. He was linked =
to
the Perla brothers in Pittsburgh and interceded on their behalf to enlist h=
is
&quot;friends&quot; in New York, including&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; Henry Hill, to
provide support and protection for the criminal enterprise.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mazzei had befriended Hill while b=
oth
individuals were serving time in prison and he apparently used this connect=
ion
to gain access to major New York gambling circles.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Henry
Hill ultimately proved to be an unreliable friend.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In exchange for full immunity, Hill
exposed the point shaving scheme and implicated Mazzei in this criminal
enterprise.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mazzei was jointly
indicted and tried with James Burke, Anthony Perla, Rocco Perla, and Richard
Kuhn, each of whom were found guilty under RICO and the bribery statutes.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mazzei has decided to appeal his
conviction separate from his co-defendants, who pursued a joint appeal.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mazzei raises a variety of claims,=
 some
of which are identical to those argued by his co-defendants, others of which
are distinct.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>To the extent t=
hat
the claims are identical, they have been considered and rejected in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Burke</i> opinion. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn476' href=3D"#_ftn476" name=3D"_ftnref476" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This decision addresses only those claims that are unique to Mazzei.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Discu=
ssion</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Enterprise Element -- RICO</i></span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Mazzei's
principal contention on appeal is that the district court improperly instru=
cted
the jury on the elements of the RICO offense.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Specifically, Mazzei argues that t=
he
district court failed to explain to the jury that an &quot;enterprise,&quot=
; as
defined in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4) (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn477' href=3D"#_f=
tn477"
name=3D"_ftnref477" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> must be separate and distinct f=
rom
its &quot;pattern of racketeering activity,&quot; as defined in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c) (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>None
of the defendants below, including Mazzei, submitted requests to charge with
regard to the definition of enterprise, nor was any exception taken to that
portion of the charge. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ47B0000400"></a><s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Accordingly, Mazzei's claim must fa=
il
unless there was &nbsp;[*88]&nbsp; plain error in the charge given.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Mazzei's contention that the
district&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; court failed to charge an essential element of the
RICO offense, if established, would amount to plain error. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4790000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YKX0-0039-X2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
DeMarco,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YKX0-0039-X2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 488 F.2d 828,
832 (2d Cir. 1973)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ47C0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3PS0-0039-X1MJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Fields,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3PS0-0039-X1MJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 466 F.2d 119,
121 (2d Cir. 1972)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
indictment charged the defendants with a violation <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_42"></a>of RICO, 18 U.S.C. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(1976), which makes unlawful &quot;the conduct of [an] enterprise's affairs
through a pattern of racketeering activity.&quot; Included within the statu=
tory
definition of enterprise is a &quot;group of individuals associated in fact
although not a legal entity.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4) (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The indi=
ctment
alleged an enterprise consisting of &quot;a group of individuals associated=
 in
fact and utilizing, among other things, interstate travel and facilities in
interstate commerce to influence by means of bribery the outcome of basketb=
all
games involving the Boston College varsity basketball team and to profit
therefrom by wagering on those games.&quot; App. of Appellant at B1.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The alleged pattern of racketeering
activity was the defendants' efforts during 1978 and 1979 to influence the
outcome of B.C. basketball games in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5032-D6RV-H523-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
224 (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and the defendants' travel&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; in interst=
ate
commerce with the intent to commit bribery in order to influence the outcom=
e of
B.C. basketball games in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1952 (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ48R0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Mazzei claims that to establish a
violation of RICO, there must be proof that the alleged enterprise was dist=
inct
from the alleged pattern of racketeering activity. According to Mazzei, the
government's indictment alleged an enterprise identical to the alleged patt=
ern
of racketeering activity, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>to wit,</=
i> a
conspiracy formed for the sole purpose of shaving points in B.C. basketball
games. To support this contention, he relies principally on </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ48P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Turkette,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 452 U.S. 576=
, 69
L. Ed. 2d 246, 101 S. Ct. 2524 (1981)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ48S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3P70-003B-G0PH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Bledsoe,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3P70-003B-G0PH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 674 F.2d 647
(8th Cir. 1982)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4920000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Turkette,</i> the Supreme Court was asked to decide &quot;whether t=
he
term 'enterprise ' as used in RICO encompasses both legitimate and illegiti=
mate
enterprises or is limited in application to the former.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4910000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>452 U.S. at 5=
78</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Turkette</i> Court reviewed the
definition of enterprise found in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4) (1976)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> and concluded that the statute did not exclude illegiti=
mate
organizations.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court foun=
d that
the legislative history of&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; RICO compelled the same conclus=
ion.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>In response to criticism that incl=
uding
illegitimate organizations in the definition of enterprise would render
redundant the element of a pattern of racketeering activity, the Court
reasoned:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_42"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>That </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>That
a wholly criminal enterprise comes within the ambit of the statute does not
mean that a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; is an
&quot;enterprise.&quot; In order to secure a conviction under RICO, the
Government must prove both the existence of an &quot;enterprise&quot; and t=
he
connected &quot;pattern of racketeering activity.&quot; The enterprise is an
entity, for present purposes a group of persons associated together for a
common purpose of engaging in a course of conduct.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The pattern of racketeering activi=
ty is,
on the other hand, a series of criminal acts as defined by the statute.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1) (1976 ed., Supp. III)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The former is proved by evidence o=
f an
ongoing organization, formal or informal, and by evidence that the various
associates function as a continuing unit.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&=
nbsp;
</span>The latter is proved by evidence of the requisite number of acts of
racketeering committed by the participants in the enterprise.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_=
41"></a>While
the proof used to establish these separate elements&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; may in
particular cases coalesce, proof of one does not necessarily establish the
other.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The &quot;enterprise&q=
uot;
is not the &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot;; it is an entity
separate and apart from the pattern of activity in which it engages. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4940000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The existence of an enterprise at a=
ll
times remains a separate element which must be proved by the Government.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4940000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4930000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 583</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(footnote omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*89]&nbsp;
We agree that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Turkette</i> requires=
 the <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_38"></a>government to prove both the existen=
ce of
an &quot;enterprise&quot; and a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity.&quo=
t;
We do not, however, read <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Turkette</=
i> to
hold that proof of these separate elements be distinct and independent, as =
long
as the proof offered is sufficient to satisfy both elements.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>There is nothing in the language or
legislative history of the Act to support the appellant's view.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Moreover, it does not make sense to
impose a &quot;distinctness&quot; requirement in RICO cases.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The appellant would have us rule t=
hat
his actions are beyond the purview of RICO because he engaged only in point
shaving and did not commit criminal acts other than those specifically
contemplated in the conspiracy. Mazzei's interpretation would lead to the
anomalous result that&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; a large scale underworld operation
which engaged solely in trafficking of heroin would not be subject to RICO's
enhanced sanctions, whereas small-time criminals jointly engaged in infrequ=
ent
sales of contraband drugs and illegal handguns arguably could be prosecuted
under RICO.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The Court will not
place its <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>imprimatur</i> on such a
counter-productive interpretation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ49F0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>Turkette</span></i><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> expressly provides that the pro=
of
used to establish the &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; element
&quot;may in particular cases coalesce&quot; with the proof offered to
establish the &quot;enterprise&quot; element of RICO. 452 U.S. at 583. In o=
ur
judgment, the proof of these elements coalesce comfortably in the present
action.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The government here p=
roved
an enterprise that was a &quot;group of individuals associated in fact&quot;
with evidence establishing a common or shared purpose among the individuals=
 and
evidence that they functioned as a continuing unit.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ49D0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-75Y0-0039-W46K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Errico,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-75Y0-0039-W46K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 635 F.2d 152,
156 (2d Cir. 1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>cert. denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ49G0000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>453 U.S. 911, 69 L. Ed. 2d 994, 101 S.=
 Ct.
3142 (1981)</i>. The prosecution showed that the B.C. conspirators shared a
common purpose -- illegally shaving points on B.C. games to maximize their
chances&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; of betting successfully on those games -- and that
they functioned as a continuing unit, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>i.e.,</i>
during the 1978-79 B.C. basketball season.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>The government also proved that the enterprise derived profit throug=
h a
&quot;pattern of racketeering activity,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>to wit,</i> &quot;fixing&quot; nine B.C. basketball games.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
