name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4DV0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Errico,</i> we held that a network of jockeys and bettors, joined
together for the &quot;single, illegal purpose&quot; of betting on
&quot;fixed&quot; horse races, constituted an &quot;enterprise&quot; for
purposes of RICO. 635 F.2d at 156. In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>Altese,</i>
the gravamen of the indictment was that the &quot;defendants had conducted a
large scale gambling business through a pattern of racketeering activity and
the collection of unlawful debts, as defined in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(5)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(6)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4DT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1VF0-0039-M3P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>542 F.2d at 1=
05</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Altese</i> defendants claimed on
appeal that RICO applied only to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>le=
gitimate</i>
enterprises that were&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; conducted through a pattern of
racketeering activity and did not extend to businesses conducted for solely
illegal purposes.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>They assert=
ed
that RICO was not intended to provide added criminal sanctions in situations
where the alleged enterprise was formed for an exclusively illegal purpose,=
 in
their case, gambling.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Altese</i> Court focused on the bro=
ad
language of the RICO statute, particularly the legislature's regular use of=
 the
term &quot;any&quot;:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
first note that each of the four paragraphs of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
begins with the all inclusive phrase: &quot;It shall be unlawful for any pe=
rson
. . . .&quot; who has received <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any<=
/i>
income derived from <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> pattern=
 of
racketeering activity, etc., to use <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>any</i>
part of such income in the acquisition of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>&quot;any</i>
enterprise engaged in . . . . interstate or foreign commerce.&quot; (emphas=
is
supplied).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The word &quot;any=
&quot;
is explicit. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F30000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>In addition, we note that in </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1961<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the Congress in defining the words &quot;person&quot; and
&quot;enterprise&quot; again uses the word <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>&quot;any&quot;.</i>
In the light of the continued repetition of the word &quot;any&quot; we can=
not
say that &quot;a reading of the statute&quot; evinces a Congressional inten=
t to
eliminate illegitimate businesses from the orbit of&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; the A=
ct.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>On the contrary we find ourselves
obliged to say that Title IX in its entirety says in clear, precise and
unambiguous language -- the use of the word &quot;any&quot; -- that all
enterprises that are conducted through a pattern of racketeering activity or
collection of unlawful debts fall within the interdiction of the Act.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F30000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4DW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1VF0-0039-M3P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>542 F.2d at 1=
06</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(footnote omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F50000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>The Court observed that Congress co=
uld
have included limiting language in the RICO statute if it had intended to
restrict the application of RICO to certain types of enterprises. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F70000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Rather, the Congress declared that =
the
Act should &quot;be liberally construed to effectuate its remedial
purposes.&quot; Pub. L. No. 91-452, &sect; 904(a), 84 Stat. 941, 947 (1970)=
.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>Citing this legislative mandate, t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Altese</i> Court concluded that RICO w=
as
intended to provide enhanced sanctions against both legitimate and illegiti=
mate
businesses when they are conducted through a pattern of racketeering activi=
ty. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1VF0-0039-M3P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>542 F.2d at 1=
06</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F70000400_2"></a>The Court also ruled, albe=
it
implicitly, that a group formed for the sole purpose of illegal gambling and
the collection of debts constituted an &quot;enterprise&quot; under RICO.<s=
pan
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4F60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1VF0-0039-M3P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1VF0-0039-M3P3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 107</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Van Graafeiland, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>J.,</i> dissentin=
g). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp;
RICO is primarily focused on the threat to society posed by organized crime=
. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn480' href=3D"#_ftn480" name=3D"_ftnref480" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Crime is no less organized where its purposes are singular.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The district court properly charge=
d each
element of the RICO offense, and Mazzei has pointed to no omission in the
charge that affected his substantive rights.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>We reject this claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Factual Determination of Enterprise</i=
></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4FG0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Mazzei also asserts that the
district court removed from the jury the factual &nbsp;[*91]&nbsp;
determination of enterprise.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>=
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4FF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-VC40-0039-M52T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Huber,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-VC40-0039-M52T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 603 F.2d 387,
394-95 (2d Cir. 1979)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>cert. denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FPXWCR0K1MNJ4FH0000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>445 U.S. 927, 63 L. Ed. 2d 759, 100 S.=
 Ct.
1312 (1980)</i>. The portion of the charge that Mazzei objects to provided:=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
first element which I told you must find in order to convict a defendant on
Count One is the existence of an enterprise consisting of an association or
group of persons which was associated&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; to influence by bri=
bery
the outcome of Boston College basketball games. Under the relevant legal
definition contained in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1961(=
4)
Title 18 of the United States Code</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, an enterprise includes and I
quote: &quot;any . . . . group of individuals associated in fact although n=
ot a
legal entity.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is of course your responsibility as jurors to determine whether the Governm=
ent
has proved the existence of the enterprise <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>as
alleged,</i> and my instructions to you are in no way intended to indicate =
how
you should find on this question of fact. However, I instruct you that as a
matter of law the type of association alleged by the Government, if proven,
would constitute an enterprise within the meaning of the statute.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>App.
of Appellant at C2669-70 (emphasis added).<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>=
&nbsp;
</span>The government's indictment alleged an enterprise, namely, a group of
individuals sharing a common purpose -- influencing the outcome of B.C.
basketball games to secure a profit.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>The indictment also alleged a continuing unit -- throughout the 1978=
-79
team season.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The district cou=
rt did
not remove the factual determination of enterprise from the jury, rather it
only defined the appropriate legal standard.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>The jury was&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; lef=
t to
evaluate the government's proof to determine whether its allegations were
sustained.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal44 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
judgment of the district court is affirmed.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

