ed
States. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. As to these targets, additional
mechanisms were used to influence both formulary decisions and prescribing
decisions. In 1994, in a memo discussing the promotion of Neurontin as an
anti-convulsant, Parke-Davis's marketing team listed Kaiser as second on its
list of &quot;Top 10 HMOs Targeted for Neurontin.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *11</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In 2004, Pfizer developed an
&quot;Operating Plan&quot; for marketing a number of drugs, including
Neurontin, to Kaiser; tellingly, the plan featured, as a strategy,
&quot;develop[ing] relationships with [decision makers affiliated with Kais=
er] <u>who
are not considered whistle blowers</u>.&quot; <u>Id</u>. (emphasis added)
(internal quotation marks omitted). Pfizer also employed physicians associa=
ted
with Kaiser to serve on speakers' bureaus and publish misleading articles a=
bout
Neurontin. <u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>Kaiser's
Management of Neurontin on Its Formularies</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGV0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGV0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Kaiser is composed of two separa=
te
corporations: the Kaiser Foundation Health Plan, which owns six regional he=
alth
plans and directly provides medical coverage to beneficiaries in California=
 and
Hawaii, providing medical &nbsp;[**11]&nbsp;insurance to about 8.6 million
members; and Kaiser Foundation Hospitals, which operates health care facili=
ties
and pharmacies. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGV0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The Kaiser Foundation Health P=
lan
and its subsidiaries do not employ physicians themselves, but have exclusive
contractual relationships with regional Permanente Medical Groups
(&quot;PMGs&quot;). </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGV0030000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Each
PMG has its own Pharmacy and Therapeutics (&quot;P &amp; T&quot;) Committee
which manages each PMG's formulary, or list of medications that treating
physicians may prescribe. <u>Id</u>. Representatives from both
&nbsp;[*29]&nbsp; entities sit on the P &amp; T Committees and participate =
in
formulary management. Kaiser Foundation Hospitals has a Drug Information
Service (&quot;DIS&quot;) that researches and communicates information about
drugs, including monographs about new drugs or new drug uses, to physicians=
 and
P &amp; T Committees. <u>Id</u>. DIS monographs summarize available evidenc=
e --
including publicly available evidence and unpublished information obtained =
from
pharmaceutical manufacturers -- on drug safety and efficacy, and P &amp; T
Committees rely heavily on these monographs in making formulary decisions. =
<u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>PMG formularies may list drugs (=
1)
without restrictions; (2) with restrictions limiting prescribing
&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp;to a particular group of physicians; or (3) with guidelin=
es
for appropriate prescribing. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGV00500=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Kaiser will pay for off-formul=
ary
prescriptions and no prior authorization is required for any prescription.
Nonetheless, an internal Kaiser study found that 95% of prescriptions writt=
en
by PMG physicians comply with formularies. <u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
the FDA approved Neurontin for epilepsy in 1993, the P &amp; T Committee of
each regional PMG added Neurontin to its formulary, with one regional PMG --
Hawaii -- not adding Neurontin to its formulary until 2000. <u>Id</u>. The
Southern California PMG initially restricted prescribing of Neurontin to
neurologists. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0030000400"></a><u>Id</u>. =
In
September of 1997, however, its P &amp; T Committee permitted anesthesiolog=
ists
to prescribe Neurontin for reflex sympathetic dystrophy, a particular pain
syndrome. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0050000400"></a><u>Id</u>. In J=
une of
1999, the Committee removed prescribing restrictions on Neurontin and added
guidelines reserving its use for neuropathic pain patients who were
unresponsive to or intolerant of other treatments. <u>Id</u>. Then, in
September of 1999, the P &amp; T Committee removed all remaining formulary
restrictions on Neurontin. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0020000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0050000400_2"></a>Prescriptions of Neurontin
increased dramatically thereafter. &nbsp;[**13]&nbsp;.</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *31</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BR0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court found that
&quot;Kaiser relied on Pfizer's misrepresentations and omissions during the
development of drug monographs in both June and September 1999,&quot; </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BR0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *29</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and that Pfizer's
misrepresentations &quot;directly affected decisions about Neurontin's
placement on formulary without restrictions,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BR0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>Physicians'
Prescribing Behavior as to Neurontin</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGW0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGW0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The jury and court found that the
prescribing of Neurontin had in fact been causally affected by the fraudule=
nt
marketing scheme, which included the sponsorship of CME events attended by
physicians and direct marketing to physicians. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BR0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *12</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Defendants stress that no
physician in this case, or in the Neurontin MDL as a whole, testified that =
he
or she prescribed Neurontin because of defendants' fraudulent off-label
marketing. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGW0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *32</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. But Kaiser presented other
evidence as to causation, and evidence as to why such individual testimony =
was
unreliable.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGW0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The primary evidence was the exp=
ert
testimony of Dr. Meredith Rosenthal, who holds a Ph.D. in health economics =
from
Harvard University and is a professor at the Harvard School of Public Healt=
h. <u>Id</u>.
Dr. Rosenthal &quot;use[d] aggregate data and statistical approaches
&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp;to link patterns in promotional spending</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn485' href=3D"#_ftn485" name=3D"_ftnref485" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
patterns in prescribing for the drug.&quot; <u>Id</u>. (internal quotation =
mark
omitted). Her regression &nbsp;[*30]&nbsp; analysis found a causal connecti=
on
between the fraudulent marketing and the quantity of prescriptions written =
for
off-label indications. She also testified as to why Pfizer's proposed physi=
cian-by-physician
analysis of causation was not a scientifically valid approach to causation.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68S0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68S0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Dr. Rosenthal used &quot;gold
standard&quot; national data on Neurontin prescriptions, and employed the
assumptions that (1) &quot;Kaiser's patient population and physician
distribution are similar to the national mix,&quot; and (2) &quot;promotion=
al
spending on off-label marketing was the same as the promotional spending on=
 <u>fraudulent</u>
off-label marketing.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68S0010000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *32-33</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The district court found both
assumptions to be reasonable. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68S0030=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *32-33</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
is customary for such experts, Dr. Rosenthal testified that she &quot;assum=
ed
that the allegations in the complaint are true&quot; for purposes of conduc=
ting
&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;her analysis, but offered no view as to whether or not th=
ere
had been a fraudulent marketing scheme. She further explained that her
assignment was only to calculate the percentage of prescriptions caused by
Pfizer's fraudulent off-label marketing and not to convert that percentage =
into
a damages number for Kaiser, which was the task of another expert witness, =
Dr.
Raymond Hartman, Ph.D.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68T0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Dr. Rosenthal explained the
difference between correlation and causation and stated that her analysis
established causation by performing a regression analysis on sales informat=
ion
against promotional spending on detailing, professional journal advertising,
and the retail value of samples, while controlling for other variables. Her
analysis excluded the many off-label prescriptions by physicians who receiv=
ed
legitimate on-label promotion. She concluded that the &quot;percentage[s] of
Neurontin prescriptions that were caused by Pfizer's fraudulent marketing of
Neurontin&quot; were, by off-label indication, as follows: 99.4% of
prescriptions for bipolar disorder; 70% of prescriptions for neuropathic pa=
in;
27.9% of prescriptions for migraine; and 37.5% of prescriptions for doses o=
ver
1800 mg/day. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68S0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *33</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, &nbsp;[**16]&nbsp;three =
out
of ten Neurontin prescriptions written by neurologists for migraine would n=
ot
have been written or filled but for the alleged misconduct. As for Neurontin
prescriptions written by psychiatrists for bipolar disorder between November
1995 and December 2004, 99.4% would not have been written had there been no
fraud. Dr. Rosenthal testified that it was her opinion &quot;to a reasonable
degree of scientific certainty that these calculations are the best way to
estimate the number of prescriptions and the share of prescriptions that we=
re
affected by the alleged misconduct.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id=
:ftn486'
href=3D"#_ftn486" name=3D"_ftnref486" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Turning
to Pfizer's insistence that only doctor-by-doctor evidence could prove
causation, Dr. Rosenthal testified as to the well-recognized unreliability =
in
the field of healthcare economics of asking doctors individually whether th=
ey
were influenced by the many methods of off-label marketing. She said that
self-reporting from physicians about patterns of practice that may be
controversial shows both conscious reluctance and unconscious bias, which l=
ead
them to deny being influenced. As a result, it is preferable &quot;[t]o exa=
mine
objectively &nbsp;[**17]&nbsp;the causal association between promotion and
sales using . . . econometric models.&quot; Dr. Rosenthal utilized the stan=
dard
practice of using &quot;aggregate data and . . . statistical approaches to =
link
patterns in promotional spending to patterns in prescribing for the drug.&q=
uot;
Dr. Rosenthal testified that it was &quot;neither standard nor appropriate =
to
look physician by physician.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*31]&nbsp;
In opposition to Dr. Rosenthal's expert testimony, Pfizer introduced the ex=
pert
testimony of Dr. Michael C. Keeley, Ph.D., who testified as to alleged flaw=
s in
Dr. Rosenthal's methodology. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68T0030000400">=
</a>Dr.
Keeley testified that when he re-ran Dr. Rosenthal's regression analysis wi=
th
different assumptions, he did not find a statistically significant relation=
ship
between Pfizer's promotion of Neurontin and prescriptions of Neurontin. Dr.
Keeley did not present his own causation or damages model, however. The cou=
rt
rejected Dr. Keeley's criticisms and accepted Dr. Rosenthal's calculations.=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68T0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *58</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68T0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The court also found that subsid=
iary
evidence tended to show a causal link. For example, PMG physicians attended
conferences where Neurontin was promoted for off-label uses, and after one =
such
conference, in May 1999, new starts of Neurontin &nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;increase=
d by
62%. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68T0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>D. <u>Criminal
Proceedings and Related Proceedings Against the Defendants Concerning Neuro=
ntin</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Dr. David Franklin was employed =
as a
medical liaison at Parke-Davis for about five months in 1996; on August 13,
1996, he filed a sealed qui tam action against Parke-Davis under the False
Claims Act (&quot;FCA&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SCB-BFD2-8T6X-70GG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>31 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 3729-3733</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 ex
rel. Franklin v. Parke-Davis, Div. of Warner-Lambert Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 147 F. Sup=
p.
2d 39, 43-44, 46 (D. Mass. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0040000400_2"></a>Franklin alleged that
Parke-Davis engaged in a fraudulent scheme to promote off-label uses of
Neurontin, and that this campaign caused false claims to be submitted to the
Veterans Administration and to the federal government for Medicaid
reimbursement. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 43</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0010000400_2"></a>Franklin's suit remained u=
nder
seal for more than three years, as the government considered whether to
intervene, and was then unsealed on December 21, 1999, with the government
participating only as an amicus curiae. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2HM68V0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43HK-MYX0-0038-Y01V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 46</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
On June 16, 2004, Franklin, Parke-Davis, Pfizer, and the United States ente=
red
into a stipulation of dismissal, under which Franklin received a relator's
share of $24,640,000.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On May 13, 2004, the U.S. Depart=
ment
of Justice filed a criminal &nbsp;[**19]&nbsp;information charging
Warner-Lambert with illegal off-label promotion of Neurontin. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *11</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Pfizer caused
Warner-Lambert to plead guilty to two felony counts of marketing Neurontin =
for
unapproved uses, with Warner-Lambert &quot;expressly and unequivocally
admit[ting]&quot; that it promoted the sale and use of Neurontin for
neuropathic pain, bipolar disorder, and migraine. <u>Id</u>. To be clear, t=
his
plea did not admit to <u>fraudulent</u> marketing. Warner-Lambert agreed to=
 pay
a $240 million criminal fine, and Pfizer paid $190 million in additional ci=
vil
fines. <u>Id</u>. News of this action, plea, and settlement caused Kaiser to
take certain steps, as described below.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>E. <u>Kaiser's
Actions To Reduce Neurontin Prescriptions</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Neurontin prescriptions written =
by
PMG physicians increased dramatically after September 1999 (the fraudulent
marketing campaign began in 1997). This notable increase led some Kaiser
regions to &quot;examine their members' use of Neurontin&quot; and make eff=
orts
to limit it. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51S0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *31</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. By the spring of 2002, the
Northern California PMG had barred Pfizer drug representatives from detaili=
ng
its physicians regarding Neurontin, and the same PMG's Drug Utilization Gro=
up
(&quot;DRUG&quot;) &nbsp;[**20]&nbsp;began a campaign to promote only the
appropriate use of Neurontin, which other regional PMGs joined. <u>Id</u>.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
late 2002, Kaiser learned about Franklin's qui tam action and escalated its
efforts to limit prescribing of Neurontin &nbsp;[*32]&nbsp; for neuropathic
pain, bipolar disorder, migraine, and nociceptive pain. <u>Id</u>. Kaiser
shared materials about Neurontin produced by DRUG and the Southern Californ=
ia
PMG's Drug Utilization Action Team (&quot;DUAT&quot;) with all regional PMG=
s.
The district court found that though Neurontin use continued to increase
nationally, Kaiser's efforts to limit its use &quot;result[ed] in a 33-34%
decrease in new starts of Neurontin.&quot; <u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The P &amp; T Committees did not
remove Neurontin from their formularies or impose restrictions on its use a=
fter
learning about the allegations of defendants' fraudulent off-label marketin=
g of
Neurontin. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0040000400"></a>Favorable
information about using Neurontin to treat neuropathic pain remained on
Kaiser's website until the eve of trial. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The district court found, howe=
ver,
that Kaiser employees did not know about the full scope of defendants' frau=
d. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0040000400_2"></a>Rather, they learned of th=
e full
scope of the fraud through (1) discovery in this suit, and (2) the publicat=
ion,
in &nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;November of 2009, of an article in the <u>New England
Journal of Medicine</u> reporting defendants' use of scholarly publications=
 to
disseminate misleading information about Neurontin. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *31, *7 &amp; n.4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F. <u>Injury
and Damages Sustained by Kaiser Due to Defendants' Fraud</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court and the jury found that Kaiser had suffered both injury and quantifia=
ble
damages as a result of defendants' actions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BS0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BS0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>After reviewing the evidence at
trial -- including the results of DBRCTs and other clinical trials, anecdot=
al
accounts of clinical success, regulatory approval in other countries, and
expert opinions, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51T0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *34-45</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> -- the district court found that
&quot;there is no reliable scientific evidence that Neurontin is effective =
for
bipolar disorder, migraine, or at high doses,&quot; and that although there=
 was
evidence that Neurontin was effective in treating some kinds of neuropathic
pain, &quot;there is no reliable scientific evidence to support a broad
indication of neuropathic pain,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BS0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *34</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The court also found that
&quot;PMG physicians would have almost certainly prescribed alternative
medication to their patients had they not prescribed Neurontin.&quot; </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *33</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BT0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In addition to Dr. Rosenthal's
expert testimony on causation &nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;and injury, Kaiser presented
testimony by a second expert, Dr. Hartman, who provided evidence as to the
damages incurred by Kaiser. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BT0020000400"><=
/a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BT0040000400_2"></a>His analysis used a list</=
span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn487' href=3D"#_ftn487" name=3D"_ftnref487" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
alternative drugs that &quot;were more appropriate for each off-label
indication than Neurontin&quot; in order to determine the average cost of t=
he
alternative medications that would have been prescribed in the absence of
defendants' fraud. <u>Id</u>. Dr. Hartman then multiplied the quantity of
affected prescriptions (as determined by Dr. Rosenthal) by the average exce=
ss
cost of each Neurontin prescription as compared to alternative medications.=
 <u>Id</u>.
He concluded that Kaiser's damages from defendants' fraud totaled $62,457,0=
82,
with Kaiser sustaining the following damages from fraud-induced prescriptio=
ns
for each off-label indication: $17,822,647 for bipolar disorder; $39,774,623
for neuropathic pain; $1,260,464 for migraine; and $3,599,348 for doses over
1800 mg/day. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BT0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *34</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In fact, the total awarded by =
the
jury was less than this sum.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Dr.
Keeley, Pfizer's expert, testified that Dr. Hartman's calculations were fla=
wed
&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp;because he did not have data that permitted &nbsp;[*33]&n=
bsp;
him to determine which alternative drugs would have been prescribed in plac=
e of
Neurontin. Dr. Keeley did not present his own estimate of Kaiser's damages,
however.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51V0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Pfizer argued to the jury that
Neurontin was effective for the off-label uses at issue, and that as a resu=
lt,
(1) Pfizer's promotional campaign involved no misrepresentations about
Neurontin's effectiveness; (2) even if Pfizer made misrepresentations, Kais=
er
doctors prescribed Neurontin for off-label uses because it was effective in
their clinical experience, not because of Pfizer's misrepresentations; and =
(3)
because Kaiser's damages theory was based on Neurontin's complete
ineffectiveness for off-label uses, Kaiser's damages calculations were inva=
lid
if Neurontin was sometimes effective for these uses. The jury rejected Pfiz=
er's
arguments and awarded Kaiser $47,363,092 in damages, which the court treble=
d to
$142,089,276. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BT0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer
argued to the district court that since doctors consider &quot;multiple
sources, types, and levels of scientific evidence&quot; in making treatment
decisions, and the effectiveness of a drug is a patient-specific inquiry, t=
he
court should not confine its &nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;analysis of Neurontin's
effectiveness for off-label uses to whether DBRCTs demonstrated efficacy.
Kaiser responded that DBRCTs were the &quot;gold-standard for determining
efficacy&quot; and that &quot;[l]ower-tier evidence is insufficient, especi=
ally
in place of existing DBRCTs.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51V0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Pfizer further argued to the cou=
rt
that because Neurontin was not &quot;completely and categorically
ineffective&quot; for off-label uses, Pfizer had not misled Kaiser about
Neurontin's efficacy and Kaiser had not proved that it suffered economic
injury. Pfizer also argued that Dr. Rosenthal's and Dr. Hartman's testimony=
 was
flawed and hence not probative of causation or damages. The court rejected
Pfizer's arguments and accepted Dr. Rosenthal's and Dr. Hartman's calculati=
ons
as the basis for its own damages award of $95,286,518. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51V0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *58-60</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>III.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer
seeks to vacate the court and jury findings of liability and damages on a
number of theories. It argues that Kaiser's claims fail as a matter of law,
that the evidence was insufficient, and that there were trial errors. At the
heart of the appeal is the claim that, as a matter of law, Kaiser cannot me=
et
the RICO or UCL causation requirements, and so Pfizer was entitled to a
directed &nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;verdict. On appeal, Pfizer does not challenge the
conclusions of the jury and district court that it engaged in a fraudulent
scheme with respect to its promotion of Neurontin for off-label uses.</span=
><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn488' href=3D"#_ftn488" name=3D"_ftnref488" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>RICO
Causation</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_43"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The civil damages provision of R=
ICO
provides that &quot;[a]ny person injured in his business or property by rea=
son
of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this chapter may sue therefor . . . and shall recover threefold the damages=
 he
sustains and the cost of the suit, including a reasonable
attorney's&nbsp;[*34]&nbsp; fee.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. &nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;In relevant part, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
prohibits &quot;any person employed by or associated with any enterprise
engaged in, or the activities of which affect, interstate or foreign
commerce&quot; from &quot;conduct[ing] or participat[ing], directly or
indirectly, in the conduct of such enterprise's affairs through a pattern of
racketeering activity.&quot; <u>Id</u>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. A
&quot;racketeering activity&quot; can consist of a wide range of predicate
offenses, including, as alleged in this case, mail and wire fraud, <u>see i=
d</u>.
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(1=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and a &quot;pattern&quot; of such activity requires at least two racketeeri=
ng
acts, <u>id</u>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(5=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBF15H00000YTK00004"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBF42000000YTK00005"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBF7P800000YTK00006"></a><a
