name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51V0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGX0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our RICO causation analysis is
controlled by the Supreme Court's decisions in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T28T51V0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes v.
Securities Investor Protection Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 503 U.S. 2=
58,
112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532 (1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and its progeny.=
</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn489' href=3D"#_ftn489" name=3D"_ftnref489" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BV0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza v. Ideal
Steel Supply Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 547 U.S. 4=
51,
126 S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720 (2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BV0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge v. Pho=
enix
Bond &amp; Indem. Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 553 U.S. 6=
39,
128 S. Ct. 2131, 170 L. Ed. 2d 1012 (2008)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BV0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hemi Grp., LL=
C v.
City of New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 559 U.S. 1,=
 130
S. Ct. 983, 175 L. Ed. 2d 943 (2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In <u>Holmes</u>,
the Supreme Court held that the civil RICO provision's &quot;by reason of&q=
uot;
language contains both but-for causation and proximate causation requiremen=
ts. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGX0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
68</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In our view, these are two quite distinct questions. Here, the harm to Kais=
er
plainly was foreseeable, &nbsp;[**27]&nbsp;and foreseeability is needed for,
but does not end the inquiry as to, proximate causation. The proximate
causation question in this appeal concerns whether the chain of events betw=
een
Pfizer's misrepresentations and Kaiser's payment for the prescriptions is so
attenuated that, for legal and policy reasons, Kaiser's claim for recovery
should be denied. The but-for causation question, in contrast, is whether,
absent Pfizer's fraud, Kaiser would have paid for fewer off-label Neurontin
prescriptions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer's
primary argument is that, as a matter of law, there is no proximate causati=
on
in this case because there are too many steps in the causal chain connecting
its misrepresentations to the injury to Kaiser, particularly because that
injury rests on the actions of independent actors -- the prescribing doctor=
s.
As to but-for causation, Pfizer argues that its evidence at trial
&quot;falsified&quot; Kaiser's theories of causation, and that some of the
evidence Kaiser presented to prove but-for causation was inadmissible.
&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp;We take these arguments in sequence.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>Proximate
Causation</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGX0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BW0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <u>Holmes</u>, the Supreme Co=
urt
upheld entry of summary judgment for the defendant on RICO claims brought b=
y a
plaintiff who was subrogated to the rights of others, based on the plaintif=
f's
failure to meet the proximate cause requirement. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGX0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 262-64,
271-74</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0010000400"></a>The =
<u>Holmes</u>
plaintiff alleged that the defendant had engaged in an enterprise to manipu=
late
the prices of certain stocks, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BW0010=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 261</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and complained that this conduct caused the plaintiff to have to pay the cl=
aims
of customers of two broker-dealers that had become insolvent once the fraud=
 was
revealed, <u>see </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BW0030000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 262-63</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0010000400_2"></a>The Court determined that,=
 even
if this plaintiff were allowed to stand in the shoes of a better-situated
plaintiff (namely, the customers), the link was too remote between the alle=
ged
stock manipulation scheme and the harm to the customers, because that harm =
was
itself contingent on the harm suffered by the broker-dealers who had purcha=
sed
the manipulated&nbsp;[*35]&nbsp; stock. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2SF8BW0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 271</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The only connection between the RICO conduct and the claimed harm was the
broker-dealers' insolvency. <u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The <u>Holmes</u> Court stated t=
hat,
&quot;[a]t bottom, the notion of proximate cause &nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;reflects
'ideas of what justice demands, or of what is administratively possible and
convenient.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0020000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 268</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting W. Keeton, et al., <u>Prosser &amp; Keeton on Law of Torts</u> &se=
ct;
41, at 264 (5th ed. 1984)). As a result, the Court explained, it was
&quot;us[ing] 'proximate cause' to label generically the judicial tools use=
d to
limit a person's responsibility for the consequences of that person's own
acts.&quot; <u>Id</u>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Because of &quot;the infinite
variety of claims that may arise&quot; in which a court must analyze proxim=
ate
causation, it is &quot;virtually impossible to announce a black-letter rule
that will dictate the result in every case.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2D6NCW0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 272 n.20=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4T2N1RGY0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated Ge=
n.
Contractors of Cal., Inc. v. Cal. State Council of Carpenters</span></i></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. 51=
9,
536, 103 S. Ct. 897, 74 L. Ed. 2d 723 (1983))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). Instead, the Court set out certain
principles, derived from the common law and from interpretations of analogo=
us
statutes, to govern the proximate cause inquiry under RICO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
