<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
our view, Kaiser has met both the direct relationship and functional tests
articulated in <u>Holmes</u> and its progeny. We reject Pfizer's core defen=
se
that there are too many steps in the causal chain between its
misrepresentations and Kaiser's alleged injury to meet the proximate cause
&quot;direct relation&quot; requirement as a matter of law. Pfizer characte=
rizes
this causal relationship as involving at least four steps: Pfizer communica=
ting
tainted information about Neurontin to Kaiser's DIS; the DIS producing
monographs that rely on the misrepresentations; those monographs influencing
the PMGs in their formulary decisions; and the prescribing physicians (who
exercise independent medical judgment) acting within the formulary to issue=
 the
prescriptions. We think this characterization misconstrues the way in which=
 the
Court has framed the direct relation test. Moreover, the adoption of
&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;Pfizer's view would undercut the core proximate causation
principle of allowing compensation for those who are directly injured, whose
injury was plainly foreseeable and was in fact foreseen, and who were the i=
ntended
victims of a defendant's wrongful conduct.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn493' href=3D"#_ftn493" name=3D"_ftnref493" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
fact, the causal chain in this case is anything but attenuated. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH30030000400"></a>Pfizer has always known that,
because of the structure of the American health care system, physicians wou=
ld
not be the ones paying for the drugs&nbsp;[*39]&nbsp; they prescribed. Pfiz=
er's
fraudulent marketing plan, meant to increase its revenues and profits, only
became successful once Pfizer received payments for the additional Neurontin
prescriptions it induced. Those payments came from Kaiser and other TPPs. <=
u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH30020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 128 S. Ct. =
at
2144</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (noting that other auction bidders, not the county
officials who immediately relied on defendants' misrepresentations, were the
intended victims of defendants' RICO conduct); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH30040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>BCS Servs</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 637 F.3d at
756</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>. Kaiser sought only economic recovery in this case, and=
 its
economic injury occurred when it paid for fraudulently induced Neurontin
prescriptions.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn494' href=3D"#_ftn494"
name=3D"_ftnref494" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to the mechanisms by which Pfizer marketed Neurontin to PMG doctors
through detailing and educational programs, Pfizer fraudulently marketed to
physicians with the intent that those physicians would write prescriptions =
paid
for by Kaiser. The fraudulent scheme worked as intended, inducing a huge
increase in Neurontin prescriptions for off-label uses. Pfizer now argues t=
hat
because doctors exercise independent medical judgment in making decisions a=
bout
prescriptions, the actions of these doctors are independent intervening cau=
ses.
But Pfizer's scheme relied on the expectation that physicians would base th=
eir
prescribing decisions in part on Pfizer's fraudulent
&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp;marketing. The fact that some physicians may have conside=
red
factors other than Pfizer's detailing materials in making their prescribing
decisions does not add such attenuation to the causal chain as to eliminate
proximate cause. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND10010000400"></a>Rather t=
han
showing a lack of proximate causation, this argument presents a question of
proof regarding the total number of prescriptions that were attributable to
Pfizer's actions. This is a damages question. <u>Cf</u>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5200050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S. at=
 466</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Thomas, J., concurring in part and dissenting in part) (&quot;Proximate ca=
use
and certainty of damages, while both related to the plaintiff's responsibil=
ity
to prove that the amount of damages he seeks is fairly attributable to the
defendant, are distinct requirements for recovery in tort.&quot;).</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBFM6K00000YTK00009"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND10030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The doctrine of proximate cause,=
 as
Judge Posner has noted, &quot;does its work&quot; in situations where</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND10030000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>too many unexpected things had to
happen between the defendant's wrongdoing and the plaintiff's injury, in or=
der
for the injury to occur -- so many unexpected things that the defendant
couldn't have foreseen the effect of his wrongdoing and therefore couldn't =
have
been influenced, in deciding how much care to employ in the activity that
produced the &nbsp;[**44]&nbsp;wrongful act, by the prospect of inflicting =
such
an injury as occurred.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND10030000400_3"></a><a
