if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 756</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The district court correctly concluded that Kaiser met the proximate causat=
ion
requirement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>But-For
Causation</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kaiser
introduced several categories of evidence at trial which clearly demonstrat=
ed
but-for causation. It produced evidence that (1) its employees directly rel=
ied
on Pfizer's misrepresentations in preparing monographs and formularies, whi=
ch,
in turn, influenced doctors' prescribing decisions; and (2) Pfizer's fraudu=
lent
off-label marketing directed to physicians caused PMG doctors to issue more
Neurontin prescriptions than they would have absent such marketing. The
&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp;latter type of evidence came from Dr. Rosenthal's report<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn495' href=3D"#_ftn495" name=3D"_ftnref495" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> as
well as inferences from other data. Pfizer has argued both that the direct
reliance evidence was insufficient and that Dr. Rosenthal's aggregate evide=
nce
was inadmissible and insufficient. Pfizer's insufficiency claims rest on the
argument that certain evidence, introduced at trial and considered by the j=
ury
and district court, &quot;falsified&quot; Kaiser's theories of causation. We
reject both of Pfizer's arguments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <u>But-For
Reliance Evidence</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C10030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Kaiser presented ample evidence =
of
the ways in which its reliance on Pfizer's misrepresentations regarding the
effectiveness of Neurontin for the four relevant off-label uses met the but=
-for
causation requirement. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C10050000400"></a>Ka=
iser
received Pfizer's misrepresentations through Pfizer's contacts with Kaiser's
DIS, which disseminated information throughout the Kaiser organization. <u>=
See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C10020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *3-4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C10050000400_2"></a>The DIS also relied on pub=
licly
available information about Neurontin, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C10040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, which, because of Pfizer's
publication strategy, omitted important information about negative study
results, <u>see </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5210010000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *7-8</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. A reasonable fact finder could
readily conclude that misinformation received by the DIS would be widely
disseminated, utilized, and relied upon throughout the Kaiser organization =
to
cause but-for injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5210040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Kaiser specifically presented
&nbsp;[**47]&nbsp;evidence that the DIS shared with all regions at least two
monographs that recommended Neurontin for bipolar disorder and that recomme=
nded
removal of any formulary restrictions on Neurontin. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH40010000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5210030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *28-29</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. These monographs were compiled
without Pfizer having disclosed certain adverse material information. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH40010000400_2"></a><u>Id</u>. &quot;In making
formulary decisions, P &amp; T Committees rely heavily on DIS's
monographs,&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5210050000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and PMG physicians comply with=
 the
formulary at a 95 percent rate, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH40020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH40050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>There was also evidence that PMG
physicians received and acted upon Pfizer's misrepresentations, both through
information&nbsp;[*41]&nbsp; sent through the DIS and information provided =
to
them at Pfizer-sponsored events. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T52200200004=
00"></a>For
one, when DIS answered physicians' questions through its inquiry service, D=
IS
relied on half-truths communicated to it by Pfizer. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH40040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *29</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220040000400"></a>Second, after PMG physicians
attended a medical education conference in May 1999, new Neurontin
prescriptions increased by 62 percent. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220040000400_2"></a>And significantly, when Ka=
iser
conducted the DRUG and DUAT campaigns to reduce Neurontin usage after the
negative information about Neurontin came to light, new prescriptions of
Neurontin fell by about 33 percent. &nbsp;[**48]&nbsp;At the same time, such
prescriptions continued to rise nationally. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *31</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>From
this evidence, the district court concluded that</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>[t]he
publication strategies and the other communications between Pfizer and Kais=
er
directly affected decisions about Neurontin's placement on formulary without
restrictions. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C30010000400"></a>In addition=
, the
direct communications to PMG physicians caused Kaiser injury because it
reimbursed for Neurontin rather than less costly alternatives. Because Kais=
er
has a 95% compliance rate with its formulary, formulary restrictions
necessarily affect the number of prescriptions written for any given drug. I
find that Kaiser was injured as a result of its reliance on Pfizer's
intentional misrepresentations and omissions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C30010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5220050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. This finding was not clearly
erroneous. Further, a reasonable jury could have reached the same conclusio=
n.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer
argues that Kaiser's DRUG and DUAT campaigns to reduce prescriptions of
Neurontin were not evidence of but-for causation because they were motivate=
d by
the desire to contain costs, not by concerns about Neurontin's efficacy for
off-label uses. Pfizer also argues that once evidence of the DRUG and DUAT
campaigns is properly discounted, there &nbsp;[**49]&nbsp;is no evidence th=
at
the Kaiser PMGs took steps to restrict Neurontin on their formularies, which
&quot;falsifies&quot; Kaiser's causal theory of direct reliance.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C30030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Pfizer did present evidence that
Kaiser continued to permit and even recommend the prescription of Neurontin=
 for
certain off-label uses after it became aware of Pfizer's fraud, as well as
evidence that Kaiser's efforts to limit Neurontin prescriptions were driven=
 in
part by its cost. But Kaiser presented evidence that it did not learn the f=
ull
scope of Pfizer's fraud until November 2009, </span><a
