mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer
argues that Dr. Rosenthal's analysis is nonetheless unreliable in this inst=
ance
because it did not account for other factors &nbsp;[**53]&nbsp;that may have
led a doctor to prescribe Neurontin for off-label use, particularly because=
 the
model did not include a &quot;time trend.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn496' href=3D"#_ftn496" name=3D"_ftnref496" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Pfizer also argues that&nbsp;[*43]&nbsp; the methodology must be unsound
because the data contradict the results of Dr. Rosenthal's regression in th=
ree
ways: (1) gabapentin prescriptions continued to grow after October 2004, wh=
en
marketing spending plummeted as Neurontin lost patent protection; (2) the m=
odel
improperly controlled for a spike in promotional spending in 2003, when
Neurontin prescriptions remained relatively flat; and (3) the model attribu=
ted
85% of Neurontin prescriptions for nociceptive pain to alleged fraudulent
marketing, but the fact finders found that there was no fraudulent marketing
for that indication.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND60010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court acted well wi=
thin
its discretion in concluding that Dr. Rosenthal's methods met the scientific
validity standard under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J3-8173-RRP5-G44P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 702</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_34"></a>&quot;So long as &nbsp;[**54]&nbsp;an
expert's scientific testimony rests upon 'good grounds, based on what is
known,' it should be tested by the adversarial process, rather than excluded
for fear that jurors will not be able to handle the scientific complexities=
.&quot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND50050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FF-7XD1-652P-Y04B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Milward</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FF-7XD1-652P-Y04B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 639 F.3d at=
 15</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citation omitted) (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND6002000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XDR0-003B-R3R6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Daubert</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XDR0-003B-R3R6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 509 U.S. at=
 590</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
Pfizer's own expert witness admitted that peer-reviewed, published studies =
do
not always contain time trends. Moreover, Dr. Rosenthal explained her reason
for declining to use a time trend: because the case involved only a single =
drug
(as opposed to other studies involving multiple drugs), the time trend would
likely be a confounding variable, because its inclusion would produce resul=
ts
showing that promotional spending had no statistically significant effect on
prescriptions -- a conclusion that would not comport with basic economics.
Indeed, Pfizer's own documents and testimony show that it expected and beli=
eved
that off-label marketing of Neurontin would increase off-label prescription=
s,
and that its marketing had that result. The choice not to use a time trend =
did
not make Dr. Rosenthal's methodology unreliable.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer's
objections regarding data that allegedly contradict the
&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp;reliability of the model also do not show that the distri=
ct
court abused its discretion. These objections presented a question for the
jury. The post-October 2004 increase in gabapentin prescriptions does not
render the regression analysis inadmissible. Indeed, the increase can be
explained by the fact that gabapentin became a generic drug at that time, a=
nd
the generic's lower price would be expected to increase gabapentin sales ev=
en though
marketing efforts for Neurontin had ceased. This change in circumstances do=
es
not negate the causal relationship between marketing and prescriptions that=
 the
model revealed for the pre-October 2004 period.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>There
was also nothing methodologically suspect about Dr. Rosenthal's controlling=
 for
a spike in promotional spending in 2003, because that spike was likely the
result of &quot;strategic interaction&quot; between the marketing efforts f=
or
Neurontin and for Pfizer's launch of a new anti-epileptic drug, Lyrica. As =
Dr.
Rosenthal explained, this was the most plausible reason why promotional
spending for Neurontin would increase even as it neared the end of its pate=
nt
life.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Pfizer's argument about the 85% figure for nociceptive pain misunderstands =
the
&nbsp;[**56]&nbsp;structure of the model. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND60050000400"></a>In conducting her analysis, =
Dr.
Rosenthal assumed -- at the plaintiffs' direction -- that&nbsp;[*44]&nbsp; =
all
off-label marketing was fraudulent,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn49=
7'
href=3D"#_ftn497" name=3D"_ftnref497" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> then analyzed the
relationship between marketing and prescriptions. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6940040000400"></a>Such <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_32"></a>an approach to proving injury from an
underlying assumption of unlawful behavior (to be proven to the fact-finder=
) is
well accepted in the antitrust context from which RICO has drawn many of its
causation principles. <u>See, e.g., </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND60040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Associated Ge=
n.
Contractors</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 459 U.S. at
528, 535-46</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (noting that appellate court had &quot;properly
assumed&quot; that defendant's alleged conduct &quot;might violate the
antitrust laws,&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6940010000400">=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-003B-S0SB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 528</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
then going on to separately evaluate whether plaintiff had sufficiently all=
eged
antitrust injury). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6940040000400_2"></a>Ulti=
mately,
Pfizer's attacks on Dr. Rosenthal's methodology were all grist for the trie=
r of
fact; they warranted &quot;test[ing] by the adversarial process, rather than
exclu[sion].&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6940030000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FF-7XD1-652P-Y04B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Milward</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FF-7XD1-652P-Y04B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 639 F.3d at=
 15</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
to the &quot;fit&quot; between Dr. Rosenthal's model and the facts at issue=
 in
the case, Pfizer objects that: (1) Dr. Rosenthal did not analyze the effect=
 of
the distorted studies or educational events on prescriptions,
&nbsp;[**57]&nbsp;but rather the effect of promotional spending on
prescriptions; (2) she did not analyze the effect of formulary expansion on=
 the
number of prescriptions written; (3) the analysis used national drug
utilization data, as opposed to drug utilization data of Kaiser; (4) the
analysis assumes all off-label marketing expenditures for Neurontin were for
fraudulent marketing; and (5) the diagnostic codes used to determine what
condition the drug was prescribed for indicate a patient's primary conditio=
n,
so Neurontin could have been prescribed for an on-label use, but appear to =
be
off-label. The basic thrust of Pfizer's argument is that Dr. Rosenthal's
analysis does not provide insight into the quantity of prescriptions writte=
n as
a result of Pfizer's alleged fraudulent marketing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6950010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>None of these arguments demonstr=
ate
that the district court abused its discretion under the &quot;fit&quot;
criterion in admitting Dr. Rosenthal's testimony. The use of promotional
spending as a variable was a reasonable &quot;fit&quot; to represent Pfizer=
's
fraud because Pfizer targeted its promotional activities toward PMG physici=
ans
and toward Kaiser itself, and the money it spent on promotion helped to
implement its fraudulent publication &nbsp;[**58]&nbsp;strategy. <u>See </u=
></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6940050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *11-28</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The analysis did=
 not
require Kaiser to quantify the &quot;publication strategy&quot; as distinct
from other promotional activities in order to effectively model the causal
relationship. In fact, if publications and CME events did exert an effect
independent of detailing (for instance, an effect on decisions about the
formulary), the model would have <u>underestimated</u> the impact of the fr=
aud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6950030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Next, the use of national drug d=
ata
was reasonable, and the district court did not abuse its discretion in so
holding. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6950020000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *32</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Dr. Rosenthal used data that w=
as
prepared by independent consulting companies, and this type of data is used=
 by
Pfizer itself in its own strategic planning and marketing efforts. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6950050000400"></a>Kaiser did not independently=
 keep
track of the usage for which each prescription was written, so Dr. Rosenthal
used what she considered the best alternative, derived from national databa=
ses
that the district court described as the &quot;gold standard.&quot; <u>Id</=
u>.
Pfizer does not challenge the district court's determination that it was
reasonable to assume that Kaiser's patient population and physician
distribution are similar to the national mix. <u>See id</u>.
&nbsp;[**59]&nbsp;The district court also permissibly found a &quot;fit&quo=
t;
in Dr. Rosenthal's use of the databases' diagnostic codes (particularly with
respect to bipolar disorder)&nbsp;[*45]&nbsp; to determine the percentage of
prescriptions written for each indication. Indeed, Pfizer's own estimate was
that bipolar disorder accounted for 14.7 percent of Neurontin prescriptions,
which is &quot;quite close&quot; to Dr. Rosenthal's estimate of 16 percent.=
 <u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6950040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2011 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 99593, [WL] at *32 n.20</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
that Dr. Rosenthal's report assumed all of Pfizer's off-label marketing was
fraudulent marketing is not a basis to find that the district court erred in
admitting the report. Pfizer is incorrect that this assumption means that D=
r.
Rosenthal was &quot;assum[ing] the very conclusion she was attempting to
prove.&quot; Dr. Rosenthal's analysis sought to determine whether Pfizer's
marketing had a causal effect on prescribing behaviors, not whether the
marketing was in fact fraudulent. Pfizer's objection does not go to the
question of whether Dr. Rosenthal's regression had a close enough
&quot;fit&quot; to satisfy <u>Daubert</u>; rather, it is a question of dama=
ges.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b. <u>Sufficiency
of Aggregate Evidence</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Having
found that Dr. Rosenthal's testimony was admissible, we turn to Pfizer's
argument &nbsp;[**60]&nbsp;that it was insufficient evidence to support the
jury's and district court's findings of causation. We reject the argument,
while pointing out that her testimony was not the only evidence of but-for
causation.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer
insists that Dr. Rosenthal's testimony cannot be credited because it does n=
ot
take into account the patient-specific, idiosyncratic decisions of individu=
al
prescribing physicians. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH80020000400"></a>T=
hus,
according to Pfizer, the report was legally insufficient proof of causation.
Indeed, Pfizer purports to find support for its position in the district
court's rulings entering summary judgment against Aetna and Harden. <u>See =
</u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH80010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51P9-KDG1-652H-V000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Neurontin Cla=
ss
SJ</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51P9-KDG1-652H-V000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 754 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 310-11</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH80030000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XHW-WWS0-YB0N-8014-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Neurontin
Coordinated SJ</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XHW-WWS0-YB0N-8014-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 677 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 485, 494-95</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5250010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5250050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc9_30"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
tort plaintiff need not &quot;prove a series of negatives; he doesn't have =
to
'offer evidence which positively exclude[s] every other possible cause of t=
he
accident.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH80050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>BCS Servs</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 637 F.3d at
757</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (alteration in original) (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5250020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5BP0-0039-Y29S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Carlson v.
Chisholm-Moore Hoist Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5BP0-0039-Y29S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 281 F.2d 7=
66,
770 (2d Cir. 1960)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Friendly, J.)). &quot;Once a
plaintiff presents evidence that he suffered the sort of injury that would =
be
the expected consequence of the defendant's wrongful conduct,&quot; the bur=
den
&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp;shifts to the defendant to rebut this causal inference. <=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5250040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 758</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
