mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Se. Laborers
Health &amp; Welfare Fund v. Bayer Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4X06-XCR0-TXFP-K277-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 655 F. Sup=
p.
2d 1270 (S.D. Fla. 2009)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. But we disagree with Pfizer's
characterization of these cases and find them either supportive of our resu=
lt
or inapposite. We see no split in authority.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C50040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5260010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In particular, Pfizer leans heav=
ily
on the Second Circuit's decision in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>UFCW Local 17=
76
v. Eli Lilly &amp; Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 620 F.3d 1=
21</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which reversed a district court's certification of a class of TPP plaintiffs
who claimed that Eli Lilly's fraudulent marketing of Zyprexa caused them to=
 pay
an inflated price for that drug and to pay for prescriptions that would not
have otherwise been written. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBG03800000YTK=
0000D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5260030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C50050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 123, 137=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
To begin, the district court in <u>Eli Lilly</u> granted class certificatio=
n on
the former (excess pricing) claim, and the Second Circuit reversed on that
basis. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5260030000400_2"></a><u>See </u></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5260020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 133</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5260050000400"></a>By contrast, the claimed inj=
ury to
Kaiser resembles the latter (excess quantity) theory. The Second Circuit fo=
und
a lack of but-for causation &nbsp;[**64]&nbsp;only on the excess pricing
theory, because doctors do not generally consider the price of a drug when =
they
make prescribing decisions. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T526004000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 133-34</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
On the other hand, doctors would certainly consider information about the
efficacy of a drug when deciding whether to prescribe it for their patients=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As to the excess quantity theory,
the Second Circuit described the plaintiffs' aggregate evidence of causatio=
n as
involving only an extrapolation from the fact that the number of off-label
prescriptions for Zyprexa fell after Eli Lilly's fraud became known. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970040000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 135</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
This does not come close to resembling Dr. Rosenthal's evidence, which exam=
ined
contemporaneous data that reflected what was actually happening with regard=
 to
spending and prescriptions while Pfizer's fraud was ongoing. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970040000400_2"></a>Finally, the Second Circuit
specifically noted that, &quot;while [the excess quantity] theory cannot
support class certification, it is not clear that the theory is not viable =
with
respect to individual claims by some TPPs.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 136</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Kaiser's case, of course, is just such an individual claim by a TPP.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBG52P00000YTK0000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The other cases on which Pfizer
relies are distinguishable. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60030000400"><=
/a>The
Eleventh Circuit, addressing alleged fraudulent &nbsp;[**65]&nbsp;marketing
claims involving the drug Seroquel, specifically declined to decide the cas=
e on
causation grounds. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6970050000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52CP-JM01-JCNJ-6038-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ironworkers</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52CP-JM01-JCNJ-6038-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 634 F.3d at
1359-60</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60050000400"></a>Instead, that court held tha=
t the
TPP plaintiffs had failed to show economic injury because the prescriptions=
 at
issue &nbsp;[*47]&nbsp; were merely less cost-effective than the alternativ=
es,
rather than being &quot;medically unnecessary or inappropriate.&quot;</span=
><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn498' href=3D"#_ftn498" name=3D"_ftnref498" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52CP-JM01-JCNJ-6038-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ironworkers</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52CP-JM01-JCNJ-6038-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 634 F.3d at
1360</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60050000400_2"></a>Ka=
iser,
in contrast, staked much of its case on proving that Neurontin was ineffect=
ive
for the promoted off-label uses, and the district court so found. <u>See </=
u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C60040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *34-45</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBG7P500000YTK0000H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5270040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Third Circuit addressed the
causation question as a matter of Article III standing rather than RICO
doctrine. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C80010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5270030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55N7-0PB1-F04K-K0CW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Schering
Plough</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55N7-0PB1-F04K-K0CW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 678 F.3d at=
 246</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
It also did not address the use of aggregate evidence at all, finding merely
that the TPP plaintiff in &nbsp;[**66]&nbsp;that case had not connected the
pharmaceutical company's alleged fraudulent marketing scheme as to two drug=
s to
the TPP's payment for a third drug owned by the same company. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C80010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5FHPVBGBVN00000YTK0000J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C80030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V28T5270050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55N7-0PB1-F04K-K0CW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55N7-0PB1-F04K-K0CW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 247-48</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C80030000400_2"></a>The Ninth Circuit, in an
unpublished decision, did not mention aggregate evidence. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C80020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5192-CGY1-652R-800F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United Food &=
amp;
Commercial Workers Cent. Pa. &amp; Reg'l Health &amp; Welfare Fund v. Amgen,
Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5192-CGY1-652R-800F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 400 F. App=
'x
255, 257-58 (9th Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn499' href=3D"#_ftn499" name=3D"_ftnref499" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Courts'
treatment of aggregate evidence is not as Pfizer represents. Earlier we cit=
ed
to the use of such aggregate evidence to show causation under several cause=
s of
&nbsp;[**67]&nbsp;action. We see no reason to reach a different conclusion =
for
the specific subset of RICO claims based on fraudulent marketing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>IV.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
trial, Pfizer argued that it had not committed fraud because Neurontin was
effective for the off-label uses at issue. The jury and court rejected the
argument, and on appeal Pfizer does not contest the finding of fraud.
Nonetheless, it uses the question of Neurontin's effectiveness to argue that
Kaiser failed to prove that it suffered economic injury. Pfizer contends th=
at
because Neurontin was actually effective for the off-label uses at issue,
Kaiser suffered no economic injury from paying for prescriptions for these
uses. Pfizer claims that the court applied an erroneous burden of proof and=
 an
erroneous medical standard in making its findings as to Neurontin's
effectiveness.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn500' href=3D"#_ftn500"
name=3D"_ftnref500" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We disagree.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH90020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Pfizer asserts that the district
court erroneously shifted the &nbsp;[**68]&nbsp;burden of proof to it when =
the
court allowed Kaiser to prove its economic injury by showing that &quot;the=
re
is no reliable scientific evidence that Neurontin is effective&quot; for the
conditions at issue, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH90010000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *34</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, rather than
requiring Kaiser to show that Neurontin was actually ineffective
for&nbsp;[*48]&nbsp; these conditions in all cases.</span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn501' href=3D"#_ftn501" name=3D"_ftnref501" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <u>See,
e.g.,</u> </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RH90030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YNM-W5R1-2RHM-100C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Schering
Plough Corp. Intron/Temodar Consumer Class Action</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YNM-W5R1-2RHM-100C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No.
2:06-cv-5774, 2010 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 56621, 2010 WL 2346624, at *4 (D.N.J. J=
une
9, 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;[A] lack of data or evidence affirmatively provi=
ng
that a Subject Drug was effective in treating a condition [is] not the same=
 as
the actual ineffectiveness of the Subject Drug.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RHC0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court did not place=
 the
burden on Pfizer to show that Neurontin was effective. Kaiser produced expe=
rt
witnesses and evidence showing that Neurontin was no more effective than
placebo for the indications at issue -- i.e., that it was ineffective. <u>S=
ee </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RHC0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *35-45</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (reviewing such
evidence). Pfizer then produced its own evidence to attempt to rebut Kaiser=
's
evidence.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Pfizer's
second argument asserts that the district court rested its conclusion on the
FDA approval standard -- two positive DBRCTs showing efficacy -- to determi=
ne
whether Neurontin was effective, and that this meant the court's conclusion=
 was
fatally flawed. Pfizer argues that the proper standard was the standard
governing the practice of medicine, not the standard for FDA approval.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn502' href=3D"#_ftn502" name=3D"_ftnref502" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
clinical practice, Pfizer argues, FDA-type trials are not dispositive;
&nbsp;[**70]&nbsp;instead, physicians rely on their own experience, other
doctors' positive clinical experiences, and other evidence. Relatedly, Pfiz=
er
argues that Kaiser's use of &quot;negative&quot; studies to show
ineffectiveness was not legally sufficient because such studies do not
&quot;establish the drug's inefficacy for treating the condition in all oth=
er
patients and circumstances.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kaiser
responds that the district court did not frame the issue of ineffectiveness
only in terms of DBRCTs, but rather considered a number of different types =
of
evidence, including clinical trials that did not meet the DBRCT requirements
and reports of clinical judgments such as case studies. The court was aware=
 of
Pfizer's critique of DBRCTs; it was also aware that, due to the placebo eff=
ect,
some patients would report improvements regardless of whether the drug was
scientifically effective for their conditions, making non-DBRCT evidence le=
ss
probative of effectiveness. Kaiser argues that the court properly chose the
weight to give each type of medical evidence. Kaiser's is the more accurate
description of the record. &nbsp;[**71]&nbsp;We conclude that the totality =
of
the evidence supported the district court's ultimate conclusion that Kaiser=
 met
its burden of showing that Neurontin was ineffective for the four off-label
indications.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Randomized
controlled studies like DBRCTs are widely accepted as &quot;ideally
suited&quot; for showing causation and as a &quot;good measure of the treat=
ment
effect.&quot; D. Kaye &amp; D. Freedman, <u>Reference Guide on Statistics, =
in</u>
Federal Judicial Center, <u>Reference Manual on Scientific Evidence</u>
&nbsp;[*49]&nbsp; 211, 218, 220 (3d ed. 2011). Where, as here, numerous DBR=
CTs
indicate that a drug is ineffective, that provides powerful scientific evid=
ence
of inefficacy, particularly as compared to anecdotal experiences, which can=
 be
tainted by the placebo effect. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RHC0040000400=
"></a>As
one witness in this trial testified, &quot;the default position [in medical
decision making] is that a drug is ineffective unless it's proven
otherwise.&quot; Experiments start with a null hypothesis that the drug is =
no
more effective than placebo. In this case, DBRCTs repeatedly showed that th=
ere
was not enough evidence to reject the null hypothesis for the indications at
issue. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RHC0030000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2011 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 99593, 2011 WL 3852254, at *35-45</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Pfizer's claimed
evidence of Neurontin's efficacy &nbsp;[**72]&nbsp;came from less convincing
sources.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
the totality of the evidence strongly supports a conclusion that Neurontin =
was
not effective for the four off-label conditions as to which the district co=
urt
and jury found liability. We need not address what the standard for efficacy
would be if there were no DBRCTs in existence, or if the results of DBRCTs =
were
equivocal, or if there were a different mix of DBRCT and non-DBRCT evidence=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>V.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal45 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C90010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C90050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Because Kaiser met both causation
requirements with legally sufficient evidence and proved that it suffered
economic injury from Pfizer's fraudulent scheme, we move to the separate
challenges to the amount of damages awarded. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_29"></a>&quot;On that phase of the case the
plaintiff has a more relaxed burden of proof . . . , especially if as in th=
is
case the defendants' conduct has made it difficult for the plaintiff to pro=
ve
the precise extent of his damages.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2N1RHC0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>BCS Servs</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:52FW-1VM1-F04K-R033-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 637 F.3d at
759</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>; <u>see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C90020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-008H-V3TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thermo Electr=
on
Corp. v. Schiavone Constr. Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-008H-V3TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 958 F.2d 1=
158,
1166 (1st Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Under such circumstances, dama=
ges
do not need to be proven &quot;with mathematical certainty, provided an awa=
rd
has a rational basis in the evidence.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2SF8C90040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-008H-V3TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Thermo Electr=
on</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5500-008H-V3TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 958 F.2d at
1166</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6ND800100=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0650-003B-G4GB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Jay Edwards, =
Inc.
v. New Eng. Toyota Distrib., Inc</span></i></a><a
