8,
63 (1st Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The damages inquiry does not a=
llow
a defendant to benefit from the scope of its wrongdoing; this is why
&quot;[e]ven 'speculation has its place in estimating &nbsp;[*50]&nbsp;
damages, and doubts should be resolved against the wrongdoer.'&quot; </span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2D6NDB0030000400"></a><a
