mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1404</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, arguing for the first time that transfer was favored by
(1) Kaiser's residence in California, (2) California's greater interest in =
the
litigation, (3) the greater familiarity of California federal courts with t=
he
California UCL, and (4) the convenience of witnesses. The district court, w=
ith
years of experience in the case, denied this motion, explaining that (1) Ka=
iser
did not wish to transfer venue; (2) transfer would result in considerable d=
elay
as any transferee judge familiarized herself with the case; and (3) defenda=
nts
would not be prejudiced, since they had access to videotaped deposition
testimony of non-party witnesses. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5889J4V2HM6980020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:8332-2RB1-652H-V13D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kaiser Findin=
gs</span></i></a><a
