<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. <u>The Facts</u></span></b></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
facts alleged in the Complaint are assumed to be true for purposes of this
motion and may be summarized as follows:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. <u>The Parties</u></span></b>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
Monique Sykes, Ruby Colon, Rea Veerabadren, Fatima Graham, Kelvin Perez, Sa=
udy
Rivera, Paula Robinson, and Enid Roman (&quot;plaintiffs&quot;)
&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp;are New York City residents who allege that defendants
conspired to fraudulently secure default judgments against them. Each named
plaintiff was sued by defendants in state debt collection actions commenced
between 2006 and 2009. (Compl. &para;&para; 120-37, 142-59, 164-81, 194-212,
222-39, 254-73, 277-94, 298-315). Default judgments were obtained against t=
hem.
(<u>Id</u>.). All except Graham deny having received actual notice of the
commencement of the actions against them. (<u>Id</u>.). Plaintiffs propose a
class action on behalf of all victims of this purported scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
three sets of defendants are a debt-buying company, a law firm, and a proce=
ss
service company, and their respective affiliates and associates.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
L-Credit, LLC, LR Credit, LLC, LR Credit 10, LLC, LR Credit 12, LLC, LR Cre=
dit
14, LLC, LR Credit 18, LLC, and LR Credit 19, LLC are wholly-owned subsidia=
ries
of defendant Leucadia National Corporation (&quot;LNC&quot;). (<u>See id</u=
>.
&para;&para; 28-34). All eight entities are primarily engaged in the busine=
ss
of purchasing and collecting on defaulted debts. (<u>Id</u>.). They, along =
with
affiliated individuals, comprise the &quot;Leucadia defendants.&quot;</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Mel S. Harris and Associates, LLC (&quot;Mel Harris, LLC&quot;) is a
&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp;Manhattan &nbsp;[*419]&nbsp; law firm primarily engaged in
debt collection litigation on behalf of the Leucadia defendants and other
debt-buyer clients. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 3, 18-25). Also named as
defendants are its principals and affiliated individuals (together, the
&quot;Mel Harris defendants&quot;). (<u>Id</u>.).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendant
Samserv, Inc. (&quot;Samserv&quot;) is a process serving agency located in
Brooklyn, New York. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 36-44). Its chief executive
officer, five individual process servers, and affiliated individuals are al=
so
named as defendants (together, the &quot;Samserv defendants&quot;). (<u>Id<=
/u>.).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>2. <u>The Debt-Buying Business</=
u></span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Debt-buying
companies typically purchase &quot;portfolios&quot; of defaulted debts for
pennies on the dollar and then attempt to collect the full face value of the
debts for themselves. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 46). The debts are priced based up=
on
recency: debt-buyers must pay more for &quot;freshly charged-off&quot; debts
than older debts, which often include debts that others have unsuccessfully
tried to collect. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 47). An active market exists even for
debts that are beyond the statute of limitations. (<u>Id</u>.).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
debt portfolio customarily contains account information for each consumer,
including her name, account number, Social Security number, last known
&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;address and telephone number, charge-off date, date and am=
ount
of last payment, and the alleged amount owed. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 48). When
debt-buyers acquire these portfolios, however, they generally do not purcha=
se
documentation of the indebtedness between the original creditor and consume=
r,
or they may purchase the documentation for only a small fraction of the acc=
ounts.
(<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 48-49). Thus, many debt-buyers have limited proof =
of
the validity of these debts. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 50).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>3. <u>The Alleged Scheme</u></sp=
an></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
allege that the Leucadia and Mel Harris defendants entered into joint ventu=
res
to purchase debt portfolios, pursued debt collection litigation <u>en masse=
</u>
against the alleged debtors, and sought to collect millions of dollars in
fraudulently obtained default judgments. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 1, 3, 95,
97). In 2006, 2007, and 2008, they filed a total of 104,341 debt collection
actions in New York City Civil Court. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 96). </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn518' href=3D"#_ftn518" name=3D"_ftnref518" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Assuming 260 business days a year, they filed an average of 133 debt collec=
tion
actions per day.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Leucadia and Mel Harris defendants regularly hired Samserv to serve process=
. (<u>Id</u>.
&para;&para; 4, 98). &nbsp;[**6]&nbsp;They paid Samserv only for service
attempts that were reported as completed and paid nothing for service attem=
pts
that were not reported as completed. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 73). More than 90% =
of
the individuals they sued did not appear in court; most defaulted because t=
hey
were not actually served. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 3-4; <u>see also id</u>.
&para;&para; 3-4 69, 107).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Sewer
service was integral to this scheme. After a consumer failed to appear in
court, the Leucadia and Mel Harris defendants applied for a default judgmen=
t by
providing the court with proof of service; proof of additional mailed notic=
e to
the consumer; an affidavit attesting to whether the consumer was in the
military; and an &quot;affidavit of merit&quot; attesting to their personal
knowledge of facts substantiating their legal claims to the court. (<u>Id</=
u>.
&para;&para; 86-90, 108-10).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Leucadia
had limited proof to substantiate its claims because it typically did not
&nbsp;[*420]&nbsp; purchase documentation of the consumers' indebtedness to=
 the
original creditors. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 46-50). Nonetheless, the Mel
Harris defendants' &quot;designated custodian of records,&quot; Todd Fabach=
er,
signed the vast majority of the approximately 40,000 affidavits of merit th=
ey
filed each year. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 110-17). Fabacher averred to havi=
ng
&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp;personal knowledge of the key facts establishing that the =
debt
in each collection action was due and owing. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 113). Assum=
ing
260 business days a year, Fabacher had to have personally (and purportedly
knowledgeably) issued an average of twenty affidavits of merit per hour, i.=
e.,
one every three minutes, over a continuous eight-hour day.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
obtaining the default judgments, the Leucadia and Mel Harris defendants
proceeded to restrain plaintiffs' bank accounts, threatened to garnish their
wages or seize their property, caused them to incur litigation costs, and
impaired their credit, making it difficult for plaintiffs to obtain housing,
employment, and loans. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 7-8, 140, 162, 192, 212-17,
219, 240, 242, 244-46, 252, 275, 294-96, 315-17).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Government
agencies have recognized that abusive debt collection practices are a public
concern. In 2008, the New York City Department of Consumer Affairs held a
public hearing on unethical debt-collector fee arrangements with process
servers. (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 70, 72). Last year, the Federal Trade
Commission issued a report that identified and discussed, <u>inter alia</u>,
industry-wide problems with debt-buyers failing to substantiate their claims
against &nbsp;[**8]&nbsp;consumers. (<u>Id</u>. &para; 50).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. <u>Prior Proceedings</u></spa=
n></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Sykes
commenced this action on October 6, 2009 against some of the Leucadia, Mel
Harris, and Samserv defendants, alleging only FDCPA and New York GBL claims=
. On
December 28, 2009, Colon, Veerabadren, and Graham joined the action as
plaintiffs, and class allegations and RICO claims were added. Plaintiffs fi=
led
the &quot;Complaint&quot; on March 31, 2010, adding Perez, Rivera, Robinson,
and Roman as plaintiffs and a Judiciary Law claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>These
three motions to dismiss followed, on behalf of each set of defendants.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><u><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></u></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First,
I address whether plaintiffs have sufficiently pled claims under the FDCPA,
RICO, and state law. Second, I consider defendants' remaining arguments
regarding the <u>Rooker-Feldman</u> doctrine, absolute privilege, the <u>No=
er-Pennington</u>
doctrine, and piercing the corporate veil.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. <u>Plaintiffs' Claims</u></sp=
an></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. <u>The FDCPA Claims</u></span=
></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
move to dismiss plaintiffs' FDCPA claims pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for failure to state a claim upon which relief may be granted. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn519' href=3D"#_ftn519" name=3D"_ftnref519" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The FDCPA forbids &nbsp;[*421]&nbsp; &quot;debt collectors&quot; from, <u>i=
nter
alia</u>: (1) engaging in &quot;any conduct the natural consequence of whic=
h is
to harass, oppress, or abuse any person,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692d</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(2)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp;making a &quot;false, deceptive, or misleading
representation,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692e</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>, or </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(3)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
using &quot;unfair or unconscionable means&quot; to attempt to collect a de=
bt, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692f</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
