<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
contest the applicability of the FDCPA, arguing that: (1) the statute of
limitations has expired for all or some of the plaintiffs who allege FDCPA
claims; </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn520' href=3D"#_ftn520"
name=3D"_ftnref520" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (2) they are not &quot;debt
collectors&quot; within the meaning of the statute; and (3) their alleged
actions are not prohibited by the FDCPA. I address each argument in turn.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>a) <u>Statute of Limitations</u>=
</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
argue that some or all of the FDCPA claims are time-barred. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn521' href=3D"#_ftn521" name=3D"_ftnref521" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> To
be timely, an FDCPA claim must be brought &quot;within one year from the da=
te
on which the violation occurs.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692k(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Plaintiffs counter that the equitable tolling doctrine
preserves their claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2780PXTTG0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The first FDCPA violations alleg=
edly
occurred when the Leucadia and Mel Harris defendants filed the state debt
collection actions. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2780PXTTT0050000400"></a>Defen=
dants
plausibly violated the FDCPA again when they subsequently applied for defau=
lt
judgments against plaintiffs. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn522'
href=3D"#_ftn522" name=3D"_ftnref522" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Even using the default judgment
application dates, the claims of Sykes, Graham, and Perez would be time-bar=
red
because those dates &nbsp;[**12]&nbsp;were more than a year before December=
 28,
2009, when the class action allegations were asserted. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2780PXTTT0050000400_2"></a><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:ftn523'
href=3D"#_ftn523" name=3D"_ftnref523" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (<u>See</u> Compl. &para;&para;
128, 203, 230). &nbsp;[*422]&nbsp; Thus, it appears that absent equitable
tolling, their claims would be untimely.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I3R5DYT3Y5F000G4J8N000NC"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTV30040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Complaint plausibly alleges =
that
equitable tolling applies, as to most of the plaintiffs' FDCPA claims. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVB0030000400"></a>A statute of limitations may=
 be
tolled in extraordinary circumstances, if a plaintiff establishes that: (1)=
 the
defendant concealed from him the existence of his cause of action; (2) he
remained in ignorance of that cause of action until some length of time wit=
hin
the statutory period before commencement of his action; and (3) his continu=
ing
ignorance was not attributable to lack of diligence on his part. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTV30030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2TW0-001B-K4RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State of N.Y.=
 v.
Hendrickson Bros., Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-2TW0-001B-K4RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 840 F.2d 1=
065,
1083 (2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <u>see also </u></span><a
