gue
that they fall within the FDCPA's special process server exemption.</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I conclude, as a matter of law, =
that
the Leucadia defendants are &quot;debt collectors.&quot; The Second Circuit=
 has
interpreted the FDCPA's definition of &quot;debt collector&quot; to include=
 an
entity that attempts to collect debts in default and does not
&quot;service&quot; the debt, even though it nominally owns the debt and is
collecting it for itself. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVJ00500004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48W4-0BK0-0038-X1T6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Alibrandi v. =
Fin.
Outsourcing Servs., Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48W4-0BK0-0038-X1T6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 333 F.3d 8=
2,
83-85 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692a(6)(F)(iii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>). First, the Complaint alleges =
that
the Leucadia defendants are principally in the business of buying defaulted
debts and seeking to collect on them, as they have filed more than 100,000
&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp;debt collection actions in state courts since 2006. Secon=
d,
the Complaint alleges numerous instances in which the Leucadia defendants u=
sed
interstate wires, to prepare non-military affidavits and to freeze plaintif=
fs'
bank accounts, among other acts. Thus, they fall squarely within the first
prong of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1692a=
(6)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
definition of &quot;debt collector.&quot; To the extent that the Leucadia
defendants argue that they are not &quot;debt collectors,&quot; that the FD=
CPA
does not apply to employees or subsidiaries of a &quot;creditor&quot;
collecting its own debts, or that their principal business is not the
collection of debts, their motion to dismiss is denied.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Samserv defendants argue that
they are not &quot;debt collectors&quot; because the FDCPA expressly exempts
any &quot;person&quot; engaged in &quot;serving or attempting to serve legal
process on any other person in connection with the judicial enforcement of =
any
debt.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-71SC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1692a(6)(D)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus, process servers whose involvement is merely
&quot;limited to serving the [debt collection] communication on the consumer
&#8212; in effect, to being messengers&quot; &#8212; are exempt. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V91-9PG0-0038-X2WF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Romea v.
Heiberger &amp; Assocs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V91-9PG0-0038-X2WF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 163 F.3d 1=
11,
117 (2d Cir. 1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting S. Rep. No. 95-382, at=
 3-4
(1977), <u>as reprinted &nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;in</u> 1977 U.S.C.C.A.N. 1695,
1697-98). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0050000400_2"></a>But a process
server who goes &quot;beyond being merely being a messenger . . . and engag=
es
in prohibited abusive or harassing activities to force an individual to rep=
ay a
debt&quot; cannot claim the exemption's protections. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F2790PXTVR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4788-STH0-0038-Y44X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Flamm v. Sarn=
er
&amp; Assoc., P.C</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4788-STH0-0038-Y44X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., No. 02 Civ.
4302 (LAR), 2002 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 22255, 2002 WL 31618443, at *5 (E.D. Pa. =
Nov.
6, 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; <u>see </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27B0PXTVX0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XS3-GDX0-YB0N-Y010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McNall v. Cre=
dit
Bureau of Josephine County</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XS3-GDX0-YB0N-Y010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 689 F. Supp=
. 2d
1265, 1277-78 (D. Or. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Because the FDCPA protects pro=
cess
servers only &quot;while&quot; they serve process, the Samserv defendants'
alleged failure to serve plaintiffs process and provision of perjured
affidavits of service remove them from the exemption. On these facts, their
