span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
challenge the GBL &sect; 349 claim on the grounds of sufficiency (all
defendants) and mootness (Leucadia), and the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-13T1-6RDJ-8461-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Judiciary Law
&sect; 487</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> claim on the ground of sufficiency (Mel Harris).</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I3R5DYT4GG1000G4J8N000NG"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27C0PXTX60040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>GBL &sect; 34=
9</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
plaintiff must show that: (1) the defendant's conduct is
&quot;consumer-oriented&quot;; (2) &nbsp;[**33]&nbsp;the defendant is engag=
ed
in a &quot;deceptive act or practice&quot;; (3) the plaintiff was injured by
this practice; and (4) &quot;the acts or practices have a broader impact on
consumers at large.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27C0PXTX600300004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51D1-22D1-652R-0003-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wilson v. Nw.
Mut. Ins. Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51D1-22D1-652R-0003-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 625 F.3d 5=
4,
64 (2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27C0PXTX60050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-6F40-003V-B43S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Oswego Labore=
rs'
Local 214 Pension Fund v. Marine Midland Bank, N.A</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-6F40-003V-B43S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 85 N.Y.2d =
20,
24-27, 647 N.E.2d 741, 623 N.Y.S.2d 529 (1995))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, even though the default judgments against plaintiffs have been vacate=
d by
the state courts or by agreement with defendants, plaintiffs' claim for dam=
ages
cannot be moot. For the same reasons discussed <u>supra</u>, plaintiffs have
alleged facts sufficient to survive a motion to dismiss as to all defendants
except Waldman, Orlando, and Cannella. Thus, I grant the defendants' motions
only as to these three individuals.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Judiciary
Law </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-13T1-6RDJ-8461-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 487</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides that any attorney who has engaged in &quot;any deceit or collusion=
, or
consents to any deceit or collusion, with intent to deceive the court or any
party&quot; is guilty of a misdemeanor and liable for damages. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-13T1-6RDJ-8461-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Jud. L.
&sect; 487</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Plaintiffs' allegations regarding the fraudulent
