<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. <u>Defendants' Remaining
Arguments</u></span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. <u>The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Rooker-Feldman</i> Doctrine</u></span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal47 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27C0PXTX80030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Leucadia and Mel Harris
defendants claim that this Court lacks subject matter jurisdiction over all=
 or
some of the claims under the <u>Rooker-Feldman</u> doctrine because plainti=
ffs
are effectively appealing from a state-court judgment. The <u>Rooker-Feldma=
n</u>
doctrine applies only if, <u>inter alia</u>, a plaintiff invites a district
court to review and reject an adverse state-court judgment. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I521F27C0PXTX80020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H17-DXP0-0038-X3RC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hoblock v. Al=
bany
County Bd. of Elections</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4H17-DXP0-0038-X3RC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 422 F.3d 77=
, 85
(2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. This argument fails, as plaintiffs assert claims
