<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
themselves succinctly and accurately summarize these alleged predicate acts=
 as
consisting of &quot;litigation activities.&quot; (<u>See, e.g., id</u>.
&para;&para; 58, 60, 68, 72, 88, 113.) Plaintiffs further characterize these
&quot;litigation activities&quot; as &quot;multiple instances of mail fraud=
 in
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.&quot; (<u>Id</u>. &para;&para; 156, 166, 172, 178, 184,
192, 202, 208, 214, 219.) Additionally, plaintiffs portray the alleged
&quot;numerous&quot; phone calls from Turansky in Connecticut to Bushman in=
 New
York (<u>id</u>. &para;&para; 113(H), 115(A)) and Nichols' use of wire
transmissions to fax copies of letters to various New York State Court judg=
es
and other counsel (<u>id</u>. &para; 130 (A-F)) as &quot;multiple instances=
 of
wire fraud in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1343</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&quot; (<u>id</u>. &para;&para; 156, 166, 172, 178, 184,
192, 202, 208, 214, 219). These allegations fail to state a claim upon which
relief may be granted for several reasons.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Even
a cursory review of the predicate acts alleged in the Complaint reveals that
plaintiffs' summary of these actions as &quot;litigation activities&quot; is
apt. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn553' href=3D"#_ftn553" name=3D"_=
ftnref553"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>20</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (<u>See, e.g</u>., Compl. &para;&para; 58, 60, 68, 72, =
88,
113.) Thus, the predicate acts alleged in the Complaint all involve
&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp;the mailing of litigation documents such as pleadings (for
example, a Reply to Counterclaims (<u>id</u>. &para; 58(A)), discovery noti=
ces
(for example, Plaintiff's First Notice for Discovery (<u>id</u>. &para; 58(=
B)),
&nbsp;[*171]&nbsp; requests for adjournments (for example, a letter regardi=
ng
an adjournment dated January 10, 2008 (<u>id</u>. &para; 64(A)), and other
ministerial documents (for example, letters regarding service via fax or
submitting a replacement copy of a brief (<u>id</u>. &para;&para; 78(B),
81(A)). Indeed, as the Bushman defendants correctly point out, each and eve=
ry
mailing specified in the Complaint &quot;involves the service, filing, or
exchange of documents related to a pending legal action.&quot; (Bushman Mem=
. at
11.) The same appears to be true regarding the alleged wire fraud.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
understanding &#8212; that the alleged RICO predicate acts are no more than
&quot;litigation activities&quot; alone &#8212; brings into focus plaintiff=
s'
rather striking theory of the case. Plaintiffs essentially allege that any
client with the impudence to contest the Curtis Law Firm's legal fees, and
further, to litigate in court that client's obligation to pay those fees or
challenge through a malpractice action the professional conduct of the Curt=
is
Law Firm, and any attorney who represents such a client, is a racketeer and
liable for treble damages. The gravamen of the Complaint is thus that
defendants' have violated RICO by defending against plaintiffs' fee claims =
or
initiating malpractice &nbsp;[**44]&nbsp;actions against plaintiffs and the=
reby
forcing plaintiffs to litigate allegedly &quot;phony&quot; and
&quot;frivolous&quot; lawsuits in state court. This theory cannot withstand=
 a
motion to dismiss because it fails to state a claim upon which relief may be
granted.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPDBR000024W200015"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRK0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPHCW000024W200016"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPKXG000024W200017"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPP4T000024W200018"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRM0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPSRK000024W200019"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPVV7000024W20001B"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRN0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVPXY2000024W20001C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRP0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>First, persuasive authority in t=
his
and other jurisdictions suggests that the litigation activities alleged in =
this
Complaint cannot properly form the basis for RICO predicate acts. <u>See, e=
.g.,
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRK0030000400"></a>Gunn v. Palmieri</u>, No.
87-cv-1418, 1989 WL 119519, at *1 (E.D.N.Y. Sept. 29, 1989), <u>aff'd</u>, =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>904 F.2d 33 (2d Cir. 1990)</i>, <u>cer=
t.
denied</u>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>498 U.S. 1049, 111 S. C=
t.
758, 112 L. Ed. 2d 777 (1991)</i> (rejecting &quot;untenable&quot;
interpretation of RICO which would permit litigation activities to be const=
rued
as RICO predicate acts). Thus, on similar facts, a number of courts have fo=
und
that allegations such as those here more properly may be classified as clai=
ms
sounding in abuse of process </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn554'
href=3D"#_ftn554" name=3D"_ftnref554" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> or malicious
prosecution. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn555' href=3D"#_ftn555"
name=3D"_ftnref555" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <u>See, e.g., </u=
></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRK0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Daddona v. Ga=
udio</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 156 F. Supp=
. 2d
153, 162 (D. Conn. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (finding allegations &quot;at b=
est
amount to vague abuse of process or malicious prosecution claims&quot; where
complaint lists &quot;a &nbsp;[**45]&nbsp;variety of 'predicate acts,' all =
of
which involve the filing of complaints and other legal documents&quot;); </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRM0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nakahara v. B=
al</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 97-cv-2=
027,
1998 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 825, at *20-21, *27 (S.D.N.Y. Jan. 30, 1998)</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(finding that plaintiffs' mail and wire fraud claims are at most &quot;a
potential yet still inchoate claim for malicious prosecution or abuse of
process&quot; where &quot;the gravamen of [plaintiffs'] Complaint . . . is
&nbsp;[*172]&nbsp; patently directed at [the defendant's] filing of, or
participation in, the various legal actions pending against
[plaintiffs]&quot;); </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRM0040000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Von Bulow v. =
Von
Bulow</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 657 F. Supp.
1134, 1140-42 (S.D.N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (finding that the &quot;essence=
 of
the stated [fraud] claim&quot; sounds in malicious prosecution where
plaintiffs' allegations &quot;closely parallel[ed] the elements of malicious
prosecution claim).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRP0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly, here, although plaint=
iffs
have styled their allegations as mail and wire fraud, plaintiffs' allegatio=
ns
in fact focus entirely upon the &quot;litigation activities&quot; involved =
in
defendants' allegedly &quot;phony&quot; and &quot;frivolous&quot; litigation
with plaintiffs. Plaintiffs' allegations thus loosely track the elements fo=
r a
malicious prosecution claim in that (1), plaintiffs allege an action against
plaintiffs, (plaintiffs allege defendants' commencement of various malpract=
ice
actions (<u>id</u>. &para; 1(C)), (2) begun with malice (plaintiffs allege
prosecution &nbsp;[**47]&nbsp;of such actions by &quot;acts of champerty,&q=
uot;
&quot;corruption&quot; and &quot;deceitful&quot; schemes employing
&quot;suborned perjury and deceit of the court&quot; (<u>id</u>. &para;
141-142)), and (3) without probable cause (plaintiffs allege defendants'
prosecution of &quot;knowingly false legal malpractice claims&quot; and
challenge of &quot;the right to earned legal fees&quot; (<u>id</u>. &para;
142)). <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRP0020000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SXB-VXM0-0038-X2GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Engel</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SXB-VXM0-0038-X2GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 145 F.3d at=
 502</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>However,
plaintiffs fail to allege that the state court litigation underlying their
allegations has terminated in plaintiffs' favor, or even terminated at all,=
 a
required element of any malicious prosecution claim. <u>See id</u>. Indeed,=
 to
the contrary, plaintiffs affirmatively allege that the fee disputes are
ongoing. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRP0050000400"></a>(<u>See</u> Comp=
l.
&para;&para; 88(E), 124(F), and 130(G).) Further, plaintiffs fail to allege=
 any
special injury. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRP0040000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SXB-VXM0-0038-X2GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Engel</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3SXB-VXM0-0038-X2GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 145 F.3d at=
 502</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, despite plaintiffs' creative attempts to plead mail and wire fraud, t=
he
court finds that plaintiffs' incomplete allegations at best attempt to make=
 out
a malicious prosecution claim. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn556'
href=3D"#_ftn556" name=3D"_ftnref556" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>23</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRR0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Without more, such allegations t=
hat
the state court litigation is frivolous, fraudulent, or baseless- essential=
ly
claims of malicious prosecution - without more, cannot constitute a viable =
RICO
predicate act. <u>See, e.g., </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRR0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Daddona</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 156 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 162</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;Attempts to characterize abuse of process or
malicious prosecution claims as mail and wire fraud violations for RICO
purposes have been scrutinized by the courts, and have been rejected where =
the
only allegedly fraudulent conduct relates to the filing of documents in
litigation.&quot;); <u>see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRR0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C250-0039-R2XS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Park South
Assocs. v. Fischbein</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C250-0039-R2XS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 626 F. Supp.
1108, 1114 (S.D.N.Y. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, &nbsp;[**49]&nbsp;<u>aff'd</u>=
, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>800 F.2d 1128 (2d Cir. 1986)</i> (reje=
cting
plaintiffs' RICO claims on various grounds including that plaintiff &quot;h=
as
not pled&quot; a RICO predicate act although &quot;[p]laintiffs' allegations
may make out a state court action for abuse of process&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Further, and even more compelling
than the persuasive authority discussed above, plaintiffs' claims must be
rejected because finding otherwise - and allowing malicious prosecution cla=
ims
such as those attempted to be alleged here to suffice as RICO &nbsp;[*173]&=
nbsp;
predicate acts - would lead to absurd results. First, if routine litigation
activities such as defending against a fee claim or prosecuting a malpracti=
ce
action against a former attorney is a violation of RICO, then almost every
state or federal action could lead to corollary federal RICO actions. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0030000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRR0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4072-CF80-0038-Y1CV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kashelkar v.
Rubin &amp; Rothman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4072-CF80-0038-Y1CV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 97 F. Supp.=
 2d
383, 392 (S.D.N.Y. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Garden-variety pleading
errors and the filing of routine motions do not constitute RICO predicate a=
cts.
To hold otherwise would turn every state court lawsuit into a predicate for=
 a
subsequent federal RICO action.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0030000400_2"></a>Plaintiffs' interpretation=
 of
RICO is untenable and would result in the inundation of federal courts with
civil RICO &nbsp;[**50]&nbsp;actions that could potentially subsume all oth=
er
state and federal litigation in an endless cycle where any victorious litig=
ant
immediately sues opponents for RICO violations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0050000400"></a><u>See, e.g., <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0020000400"></a>Gunn</u>, 1989 WL 119519, at=
 *1
(&quot;If serving and filing an answer or a motion by any defendant . . . c=
ould
be considered . . . <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0050000400_2"></a>[a =
RICO
predicate act], this Court would be flooded with motions to amend complaint=
s by
plaintiffs seeking to add RICO claims based upon mail fraud and obstruction=
 of
justice as soon as an answer was served. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0020000400"></a>Such an interpretation of th=
e RICO
statute is untenable.&quot;); <u>see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX70YFPRS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CGN0-0054-44TY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morin v. Trup=
in</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CGN0-0054-44TY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 711 F. Supp.
97, 106 (S.D.N.Y. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The Court finds absurd
plaintiffs' apparent suggestion that a lawyer's act in posting a letter whi=
ch
states a client's legal position in a dispute can constitute mail fraud. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0020000400_2"></a>If such were the situation,
every dispute in which the parties' counsel exchanged letters could give ri=
se
to RICO litigation. Such activity is simply not fraudulent.&quot;) (quoting=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B570-0039-R45P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Paul S. Mullin
&amp; Assocs., Inc. v. Bassett</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B570-0039-R45P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 632 F. Supp.
532, 540 (D. Del. 1986))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Indeed, as Levitt correctly po=
ints
out, were this court to permit plaintiffs' Complaint in this action to move
forward, defendants &nbsp;[**51]&nbsp;could conceivably countersue plaintif=
fs
for RICO conspiracy violations based upon the many allegations and statemen=
ts
by plaintiffs in this action which defendants might similarly contend are
frivolous or false. (<u>See</u> Levitt Mem. at 6.)</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Additionally, this absurd result
would chill litigants and lawyers and frustrate the well-established public
policy goal of maintaining open access to the courts. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0010000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WV3-D690-0038-X3GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Engel</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3WV3-D690-0038-X3GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 182 F.3d at=
 129</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting generally &quot;strong public policy of open access to the courts f=
or
all parties and [need] to avoid ad infinitum [litigation] with each party
claiming that the opponent's previous action was malicious and meritless&qu=
ot;)
(internal quotation and citation omitted). If any litigant's or attorney's
pleading and correspondence in an unsuccessful lawsuit could lead to drastic
RICO liability in a private right of action, litigants might hesitate to av=
ail
themselves of the courts and available legal remedies or be unable to find
representation to help vindicate their rights. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0010000400_2"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRT0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CGN0-0054-44TY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morin</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-CGN0-0054-44TY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 711 F. Supp=
. at
105</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;Congress could not have intended that the mail a=
nd
wire fraud statute sweep up correspondence between attorneys, dealing at ar=
m's
length on behalf of their parties, concerning &nbsp;[**52]&nbsp;an issue in
pending litigation . . . . Subjecting [such] letter[s] . . . to the mail fr=
aud
statute would chill an attorney's efforts and duty to represent his or her
client in the course of pending litigation.&quot;) (internal quotation and
citation omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
allowing the federal RICO statute to usurp underlying legitimate state court
litigation as proposed by plaintiffs here would inappropriately bypass the
state tribunal where the action is pending and which properly controls that
proceeding. Should plaintiffs' vague allegations of &quot;phony&quot; and
&quot;frivolous&quot; litigation, &quot;suborned perjury&quot; and &quot;de=
ceit
of court&quot; have merit, plaintiffs' should direct such claims to the sta=
te
courts where these acts are &nbsp;[*174]&nbsp; allegedly occurring and where
the underlying litigation is still pending. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0030000400"></a>This court flatly rejects
plaintiffs' contention that &quot;there is . . . no remedy to be found in s=
tate
courts merely by suing Bushman based on only one, or even several, of the s=
even
underlying cases . . . .&quot; (Pl. Bushman Mem. at 28 n.23.) <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0050000400"></a>Rather, this court has full
confidence that the New York State courts where the underlying litigation is
currently pending are fully competent to address any such wrongs and this
&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp;court is unconvinced that such issues should be re-litiga=
ted
in federal court under the guise of a RICO action. <u>See, e.g., </u></span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Daddona</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4418-K2C0-0038-Y1H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 156 F. Supp.=
 2d
at 162</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;If a suit is groundless or filed in bad faith, t=
he
law of torts may provide a remedy. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV005000=
0400_2"></a>Resort
to a federal criminal statute is unnecessary.&quot;) (citation omitted); <u=
>see
also </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRV0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nakahara</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1998 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 825, at *31</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The claims of 'fraud'
advanced here can be asserted as effectively, if not more so, in each of [t=
he]
ongoing [underlying] proceedings. Indeed, this RICO action adds nothing exc=
ept
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in terrorem</i> effect of a tre=
ble
damage claim and the expense of conducting litigation over these same issue=
s in
yet another forum.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Finally, the Congressional inten=
t in
enacting RICO does not square with the absurd consequences and contraventio=
ns
of well-settled public policy which would inevitably result from allowing R=
ICO
predicate acts to be based upon the type of malicious prosecution claims
alleged here. Certainly, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_21"></a>&quot;R=
ICO is
to be read broadly.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW00100004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.R=
.L.
v. Imrex Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 473 U.S. 4=
79,
497-98, 105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that &quot;the lesson&quot; of RICO's broad interpretation is evide=
nt
&quot;not only [from] . . . <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW0040000400"><=
/a>Congress'
self-consciously expansive language &nbsp;[**54]&nbsp;and overall approach,=
 but
also . . . [from] its express admonition that RICO is to 'be liberally
construed to effectuate its remedial purposes'&quot;) (internal citation
omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW0040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRX0010000400"></a>However, there can be no dis=
pute
that &quot;Congress did not intend to effect a wholesale preemption of state
civil law in its enactment of RICO.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Von Bulow</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 657 F. Supp=
. at
1143</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRX0010000400_2"></a>Pe=
rmitting
the &quot;litigation activities&quot; alleged here to serve as RICO predica=
te
acts would lead to just such an absurd result, in contravention of RICO's
admittedly broad remedial purpose. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRW0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Eisen</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 974 F.2d 24=
6,
254 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting the &quot;understandable
reluctance&quot; on the part of Congress &quot;to use federal criminal law =
as a
back-stop for all state court litigation&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVR16C000024W20001D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVR3HG000024W20001F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVR5PW000024W20001G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVR7TS000024W20001H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVRBYV000024W20001J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVRFXY000024W20001K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRX0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>For all of these reasons, this c=
ourt
joins a long line of cases in finding, as a matter of law, that the
&quot;litigation activities&quot; pleaded in the Complaint cannot constitute
predicate acts for the purposes of RICO. <u>See, e.g., </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRX0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G3V-CMJ0-TVW3-P244-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Directv, Inc.=
 v.
Lewis</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G3V-CMJ0-TVW3-P244-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 03-cv-6=
241,
2005 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 8187, at *22 (W.D.N.Y. Apr. 29, 2005)</span></i></a><=
span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;courts have held that serving litigation documents by mail cannot be=
 a
predicate act to establish mail fraud under the RICO statute&quot;);
&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp;</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPRX0040000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4072-CF80-0038-Y1CV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kashelkar</sp=
an></i></a><a
