<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS00050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs' response to the Bush=
man
Memorandum's discussion of these multiple persuasive authorities (<u>see</u>
Bushman Mem. at 10-12) amounts to simply alleging that even legitimate legal
documents can amount to mail fraud because to prove mail fraud, a plaintiff
need not show that the mailings themselves were fraudulent if the mailings =
were
part of an overall scheme to defraud. (<u>See</u> Pl. Bushman Mem. at 23-31=
.)
Certainly, this is a correct statement of the law. <u>See, e.g., </u></span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS00040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 553 U.S. at=
 647</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(noting that any mailing that is incident to an essential part of an overall
scheme to defraud &quot;satisfies the mailing element [for mail fraud] . . .
even if the mailing itself contains no false information&quot;) (internal
quotations and citations omitted). However, this argument does nothing to
address the weight of authority discussed above. Indeed, notwithstanding
plaintiffs' lengthy recitation of the elements and components of the two
different alleged schemes to defraud here, plaintiffs' memoranda in opposit=
ion
to defendants' &nbsp;[**57]&nbsp;motions to dismiss fail to discuss a single
case contrary to the long line of cases finding that defendants'
&quot;litigation activities&quot; alone, cannot, as a matter of law, suffic=
e as
predicate acts to support a RICO claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2FT9SVRJ67000024W20001M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS10020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Finally, in a footnote to
plaintiffs' reply memorandum in support of plaintiffs' motion to again amend
the Complaint, plaintiffs seek to respond to this authority by citing to a
single Second Circuit case, <u>United States v. Eisen</u>, for the proposit=
ion
that litigation activities can indeed rise to the level of RICO predicate a=
cts
and constitute a &quot;pattern of racketeering activity&quot; as required by
the RICO statutes. (<u>See</u> ECF No. 185, Reply Mem. in Further Support of
Pl. Mot. for Leave to Amend at 5 n.2 (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS10010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eisen</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 974 F.2d 246
(2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.) However, <u>Eisen</u> is inapposite here. First, the
holding in <u>Eisen</u> did not reach the issue here regarding whether
litigation activities alone can suffice as RICO predicate acts of mail and =
wire
fraud. Second, the facts of <u>Eisen</u> are plainly distinguishable from t=
he
facts here and therefore provide no support for plaintiffs' position.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS10040000400"></a><u><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Eisen</span></u><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
involved the criminal prosecution of seven attorneys, investigators, and of=
fice
personnel of a Manhattan &nbsp;[**58]&nbsp;law firm (&quot;the Eisen
Firm&quot;) which specialized in bringing personal injury suits on behalf of
plaintiffs. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS10030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Eisen</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1RB0-008H-V0P0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 974 F.2d at=
 251</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The evidence at trial in that case showed that the defendants conducted the
affairs of the Eisen Firm through a &quot;pattern of mail fraud and witness
bribery by pursuing counterfeit claims&quot; by, among other things
&quot;pressuring accident witnesses to testify falsely, paying individuals =
to
testify falsely . . ., paying unfavorable witnesses not to testify, and
creating false . . . evidence for use before and during trial.&quot; <u>Id<=
/u>.
Thus, in <u>Eisen</u>, the defendant attorneys &quot;went well beyond their
capacities as legal representatives&quot; in conducting their fraudulent
scheme. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20010000400_2"></a><u>See </u></sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS10050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DHW-K9M0-0038-Y4GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Morrow v.
Blessing</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4DHW-K9M0-0038-Y4GP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 04-cv-1=
161,
2004 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 20318, at *16 (E.D.Pa. Sept. 20, 2004.)</span></i></a=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*176]&nbsp; Accordingly, =
the
predicate acts in <u>Eisen</u> amounted to far more than mere &quot;litigat=
ion
activities,&quot; and instead involved an extensive and broader scheme to
defraud defendants in the personal injury lawsuits commenced by the Eisen F=
irm.
<u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nakahara</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3D-HYH0-0038-Y116-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1998 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 825 at *30</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The fraudulent criminal
scheme underlying the predicate mail fraud offenses in <u>Eisen</u> was
entirely external to, and independent of, &nbsp;[**59]&nbsp;any of the
particular disputes between the litigants in the civil actions that were
improperly filed and litigated by the <u>Eisen</u> defendants in execution =
of
their scheme.&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20050000400"></a>By
contrast, here, the allegedly &quot;frivolous&quot; and &quot;phony&quot;
litigation activities themselves and the merits of the underlying state cou=
rt
lawsuits form the gravamen of plaintiffs' RICO and state law claims and the
framework for the overall scheme to defraud alleged in the Complaint. <u>Se=
e id</u>.
(&quot;[T]he RICO claim in this case . . . seeks to have this Court in effe=
ct
decide the merits of lawsuits or proceedings that are already pending betwe=
en
these same parties in several other jurisdictions . . . .&quot;) Accordingl=
y,
the court finds unavailing plaintiffs' reliance on <u>Eisen</u> and reaffir=
ms
that plaintiffs' allegations based on defendants' allegedly
&quot;frivolous&quot; and &quot;phony&quot; litigation activities cannot,
alone, give rise to viable predicate acts of mail and wire fraud. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20050000400_2"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS20040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Von Bulow</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-D3T0-003B-6146-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 657 F. Supp=
. at
1145</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (finding that &quot;a complaint based on nothing more t=
han
a party's filing of unjustified suits cannot fulfill the requirement that a
RICO plaintiff plead a predicate act&quot; but declining to &quot;address t=
he
situation where allegedly unjustified &nbsp;[**60]&nbsp;suits form a part of
some more extensive scheme of racketeering activity, such as extortion&quot=
;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS30020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs' arguments are thus
unavailing, and this court finds that as a matter of law, the allegations in
the Complaint are insufficient to plead a pattern of racketeering activity,=
 and
therefore the substantive RICO claims should be dismissed. <u>See </u></spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS30010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S36-MPT0-TXFX-42Y2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Spool</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S36-MPT0-TXFX-42Y2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 520 F.3d at=
 183</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;[U]nder any prong of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
plaintiff in a civil RICO suit must establish a 'pattern of racketeering
activity'&quot; and adequately allege such &quot;pattern of racketeering
activity&quot; in the complaint in order to withstand a motion to dismiss).=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>b) Failure to Plead Fraud with
Particularity</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX80YFPS30040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, even assuming, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>arguendo</i>, that plaintiffs' allegat=
ions
do not fail to state RICO predicate acts as a matter of law, plaintiffs' RI=
CO
claims fail because the allegations of mail and wire fraud do not meet the
heightened pleading requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_17"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires that RICO allegations of mail and wire fraud be pleaded with
particularity. <u>See</u> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
