<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Motion to
Amend/Correct/Supplement the Complaint</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_11"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under the Federal Rules of Civil
Procedure, a court should &quot;freely give leave [to amend the pleadings] =
when
justice so requires.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
15(a)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The rationale underlying this rule is that &quot;[i]f =
the
underlying facts or circumstances relied upon by a plaintiff may be a proper
subject of relief, [the plaintiff] ought to be afforded an opportunity to t=
est
his claim on the merits&quot; through an amended pleading. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H6D0-003B-S3WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Foman v. Davi=
s</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H6D0-003B-S3WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 371 U.S. 17=
8,
182, 83 S. Ct. 227, 9 L. Ed. 2d 222 (1962)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSM0030000400"></a>Conversely, however, where t=
he
underlying facts and circumstances &nbsp;[**73]&nbsp;could never be a
&quot;proper subject of relief,&quot; denial of leave to amend is within a
court's discretion. <u>See id</u>.; <u>see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V88-G4V0-TXFX-427D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Burch v. Pion=
eer
Credit Recovery, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V88-G4V0-TXFX-427D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 551 F.3d 1=
22,
126 (2d Cir. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;While </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 15(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> states that leave to amend shou=
ld
be granted 'when justice so requires,' motions to amend should generally be
denied in instances of futility, undue delay, bad faith or dilatory motive,
repeated failure to cure deficiencies by amendments previously allowed, or
undue prejudice to the nonmoving party.&quot;) (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSK0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H6D0-003B-S3WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Foman</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H6D0-003B-S3WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 371 U.S. at=
 182</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSM0030000400_2"></a>Thus, &quot;[i]t is well
established that leave to amend a complaint need not be granted where amend=
ment
would be futile.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSM0020000400"=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4945-17S0-0038-X1XH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ellis v. Chao=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4945-17S0-0038-X1XH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 336 F.3d 11=
4,
127 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Having
thoroughly reviewed plaintiffs' seventy-page proposed second amended compla=
int
and sixty-one proposed accompanying exhibits, </span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn560' href=3D"#_ftn560" name=3D"_ftnref560" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>27</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the court &nbsp;[*181]&nbsp; finds that plaintiffs' proffered amendments wo=
uld
not affect the court's analysis as to the failure of both the Complaint and=
 the
proposed second amended complaint, as a matter of law, to adequately plead =
any
predicate acts. (<u>See</u> ECF Nos. 174-1-9, Pl. Proposed Second Amended
Complaint and proposed accompanying exhibits.) &nbsp;[**74]&nbsp;The propos=
ed
amendments embellish details of the allegations, add additional
&quot;non-party&quot; participants, and even add additional alleged predica=
te
acts in the form of additional court filings and litigation-related
correspondence. (<u>See id</u>.) <a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSM00500004=
00"></a>However,
the proposed amendments leave unchanged the nature of the alleged predicate
acts which remain &quot;litigation activities&quot; and which cannot, as a
matter of law, suffice as predicate acts. (<u>See id</u>.) Therefore, becau=
se
the proposed amendments would have no impact on the basis for the court's
dismissal and would accordingly be futile, further leave to amend is denied=
. <u>See,
e.g., </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSM0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V88-G4V0-TXFX-427D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Burch</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V88-G4V0-TXFX-427D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 551 F.3d at=
 126</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(affirming district court's denial of leave to amend on basis of futility).=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal48 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
prior to making their proposed amendments, plaintiffs had the benefit of
reviewing defendants' fully briefed motions to dismiss the Complaint which
detailed the grounds upon which defendants attacked the pleadings - includi=
ng
the very basis relied upon by this court for dismissal. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSN0020000400"></a>Despite this fair notice of =
the
deficiencies in the Complaint, plaintiffs' proposed amendments fail to corr=
ect
these issues. Under such circumstances, further leave to amend is
inappropriate. <u>See, e.g., </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I51WCTX90YFPSN0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4M9X-YCP0-0038-X4CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Curtis v.
Citibank, N.A</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4M9X-YCP0-0038-X4CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 204 Fed. A=
ppx.
929 (2d Cir. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &nbsp;[**76]&nbsp;(finding
dismissal with prejudice proper where plaintiff has notice of complaint's
