Times;color:black'>. Our fear was that use of the proximate cause language =
in
discussions of statutory standing could lead to confusion with its common-l=
aw
counterpart. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> As <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Abrahams</i> recognized, however, RICO
proximate causation is a distinct concept--one that sometimes overlaps with
zone-of-interests analysis. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4Y50000400"><=
/a>Both
of these statutory standing principles are grounded on similar policy
considerations, imposing limitations on the types of injuries that are
sufficiently related to core RICO concerns as to be cognizable. Thus, the
reasonably foreseeable victim of a RICO enterprise will often be,
unsurprisingly, the type of victim the RICO statute seeks to protect. Indee=
d,
Justice Scalia has implied that proximate causality, as used in the statuto=
ry
context, may simply be an element of the zone-of-interests test. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4F2RG080K1MND4XY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-size:9.0pt;mso-bid=
i-font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077C=
C;
background:white'>Holmes, 503 U.S. at 287</span></i></a><span style=3D'font=
-size:
9.0pt;mso-bidi-font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'> n.* (Scalia, J., concurring). In subsequent cases, we h=
ave
deemed it prudent to revert to the proximate cause terminology employed in =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> when speaking of this same
causation principle. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i>Labore=
rs
Local 17, 191 F.3d at 234 n.3. Here, we limit our discussion to proximate
causation. </span></p>

