name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CN0K47PT0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Accordingly, to plead a claim for
relief based on fraudulent incorporation, the &nbsp;[*265]&nbsp; Plaintiffs
were not required to plead that the material misrepresentations were for
fraudulent medical services. Rather, the Plaintiffs were only required to
allege with specificity that the material misrepresentations were fraudulent
because the Defendants were not entitled to recover the No-Fault benefits. =
<u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CN0K47PT0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4X91-46F0-TXFR-J2D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Grafman</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4X91-46F0-TXFR-J2D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 655 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 221</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (holding that when pleading a cause of action for
fraudulent incorporation where the &quot;damages stem from the alleged
fraudulent misrepresentation that the [defendants] were properly
incorporated&quot; a plaintiff is not required in the complaint to &quot;de=
tail
the injury suffered, including the nature of the claims in question,
&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;by date of service, claim number, or even patient name&qu=
ot;)
(internal quotation marks omitted); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CN0K47PT0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51FP-9091-652J-D001-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rabiner</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:51FP-9091-652J-D001-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2010 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 119961, 2010 WL 4363903, at * 6-7</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that to
state a claim for unjust enrichment based on No-Fault insurance payments ma=
de
to an improperly licensed professional corporation, the plaintiff was not
required to allege &quot;that the medical services that were the subject of=
 the
challenged claims were medically unnecessary or not rendered&quot;).</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>2. Whether the Plaintiffs' Have
Adequately Pled the Defendants' Involvement in the Fraudulent Scheme</span>=
</b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As the Court has previously held,
the Plaintiffs adequately pled in the Initial Complaint a detailed descript=
ion
of the underlying fraudulent scheme; YMA's specific role in that scheme; and
YMA's fraudulent misrepresentations in furtherance of that scheme. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0040000400"></a><u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V8B-1FD0-TXFR-J1V2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rozenberg</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V8B-1FD0-TXFR-J1V2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 590 F. Supp=
. 2d
at 390</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;The Plaintiffs have described the allegedly
fraudulent scheme, and each Defendant's role in the scheme, in detail. In f=
act,
the complaint includes exhibits detailing the dates and specific instances =
of
fraudulent submissions mailed by the Defendants. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0040000400_2"></a>The complaint also alleges=
 that
each of the Defendants are connected to the scheme such that each defendant
could have &nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;reasonably foreseen that use of the mail was an
essential element of the alleged fraud.&quot;) (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BBF0-003B-515J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Bortnovsky</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BBF0-003B-515J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 879 F.2d 30=
, 36
(2d Cir. 1989))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Furthermore,
the Defendants do not appear to dispute, nor could they, that the Plaintiffs
have adequately pled that the alleged fraud was perpetrated using the mail.=
 In
the Amended Complaint, the Plaintiffs identify the statements that they con=
tend
were fraudulent. Specifically, the Plaintiffs allege that, among other thin=
gs,
the material misrepresentations were contained in insurance claim forms
(&quot;NF-3 forms&quot;), as well as other forms of medical documentation s=
uch
as medical billing invoices and medical records, that were mailed to the
Plaintiffs demanding payment for the medical procedures purportedly perform=
ed
at YMA. (Am. Compl., &para;&para; 696, 699).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In addition, it is also beyond
dispute that the Plaintiffs have sufficiently identified where and when the
statements were made. In this regard, the Plaintiffs attached a number of
exhibits to the Amended Complaint, which the Court can properly consider on=
 a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion to dismiss. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CR0K47RY0050=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5161-VXH1-652R-0002-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiFolco v. MS=
NBC
Cable L.L.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5161-VXH1-652R-0002-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 622 F.3d 10=
4,
111 (2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclsc=
c10_0"></a>(&quot;In
considering a motion to &nbsp;[**27]&nbsp;dismiss for failure to state a cl=
aim
pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
district court may consider the facts alleged in the complaint, documents
attached to the complaint as exhibits, and documents incorporated by refere=
nce
in the complaint.&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90030000400"></a=
>These
exhibits included a chart showing selected examples of dates when NF-3 forms
were sent to the Plaintiffs from a PC Defendant for payment (Am. Compl., Ex.
12). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90030000400_2"></a>In addition, the
exhibits include three charts showing payments from the Plaintiffs to the PC
Defendants, including YMA, in response to NF-3 forms and other medical
documentation &nbsp;[*266]&nbsp; that the PC Defendants submitted to the
Plaintiffs for medical expenses and/or services (Am. Compl., Exs. 13-15).
Although these charts are not necessary to adequately plead a RICO claim ba=
sed
on mail fraud, <u>see</u>, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90020000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State Farm Mu=
t.
Automobile Ins. Co. v. CPT Med. Servs., P.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 04-CV-5=
045,
2008 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 71156, 2008 WL 4146190, at *12 (E.D.N.Y. Sept. 5, 200=
8)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
they provide an additional layer of particularity that support the sufficie=
ncy
of the Amended Complaint under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Thus,
the relevant issue before the Court is whether the Plaintiffs have adequate=
ly
alleged that Sheynkman and Artek owned and controlled YMA, and that they
&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp;were involved in the underlying fraudulent scheme. The
Defendants argue that the allegations in the Amended Complaint with respect=
 to
their relationship with YMA and association with the underlying scheme are
conclusory and fail to allege that the revenue they received from YMA was n=
ot
for legitimate business transactions. The Court disagrees.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>YMA
was identified in the Initial Complaint as one of the medical clinics of wh=
ich
Rozenberg falsely purported to be the sole owner, officer, and director. The
Plaintiffs contend that they learned during the discovery process that YMA =
was
actually controlled by certain non-medical professionals and managements
companies, namely First Neptune, which was owned and controlled by Ilya
Grinberg and Vladimir Kutsyk, and by Artek, which was owned and controlled =
by
Sheynkman (collectively the &quot;YMA Managers&quot;). Accordingly, the
Plaintiffs amended the complaint to assert that the YMA Managers, as part of
the overall scheme, used YMA to do indirectly what they, as non-medical
professionals and management companies, were forbidden from doing directly
under New York Insurance Law, specifically: (1) employing physicians and ot=
her
licensed healthcare professionals; &nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;(2) controlling their
practices; and (3) charging for their services. (Am. Compl., &para; 178.) In
the Amended Complaint, the Plaintiffs have set forth in detail the descript=
ion
of the underlying scheme; the Defendants involvement in that scheme; and how
the scheme was facilitated through medical records, invoices, and other
documentation sent through the mail.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to the Defendants' ownership and control of YMA, the Plaintiffs all=
ege,
among other things, that: (1) since 2002, Sheynkman a non-licensed layperson
used his management company Artek as a tool to illegally own and control YMA
through the use of management and/or consulting agreements; (2) since at le=
ast
2003, First Neptune and Artek, through their respective owners including
Sheynkman, controlled YMA and used it to employ physicians and other licens=
ed
healthcare professionals, to control their practices, and charge for their
services; and (3) through their ownership and control of YMA, Sheynkman and
Artek illegally siphoned-off revenues of $1,243,256.00 generated by YMA thr=
ough
billing submitted to the Plaintiffs for healthcare services.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, the Plaintiffs set forth a number of allegations detailing how the
defendants, &nbsp;[**30]&nbsp;including Sheynkman and Artek, used their con=
trol
of the PC Defendants to facilitate the fraudulent billing. For example, the
Plaintiffs state in the Amended Complaint that the YMA Managers, including
Sheynkman and Artek, installed non-medical personnel at YMA to &quot;enforc=
e a
pre-established regimen of treatment without regard to medical necessity, f=
or
the sole purpose of maximizing YMA's billings so that the revenue could be
siphoned-off to [the YMA Managers].&quot; (Am. Compl., &para; 182.) The
Plaintiffs then explained the details of how all of the defendants, which w=
ould
include Sheynkman &nbsp;[*267]&nbsp; and Artek, allegedly implemented the
fraudulent billing scheme through billing for &quot;substantially similar
evaluations, treatments, and testing regarding of the nature of the alleged
injuries.&quot; (Am. Compl., &para; 772.) In addition, the defendants were
alleged to have devised a system where upon a patient's initial office
consultation at the PC Defendants, it would trigger &quot;a series of inter=
nal
billing practices and procedures in which the PC Defendants would bill for
services . . . regardless of necessity of the individualized medical needs =
of
the patient.&quot; (Am. Compl., &para; 773.) Also, the initial consultation
would &nbsp;[**31]&nbsp;allegedly trigger &quot;automatic billing for follo=
w-up
examinations, testing and treatment regimen that the PC Defendants would bi=
ll
for regardless of whether the patients appeared, or the examinations, testi=
ng
and treatments were medically necessary.&quot; (Am. Compl. &para; 774.)</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Accepting these facts as true, w=
hich
the Court must on a motion to dismiss, the Plaintiffs have met their burden=
 of
plausibly alleging the Defendants role in the mail fraud both with respect =
to
fraudulent billing and fraudulent incorporation. The Plaintiffs have allege=
d in
detail that the Defendants, who were non-licensed medical professionals, as
opposed to Rozenberg, owned and controlled YMA for the purpose of circumven=
ting
New York's No-Fault laws. The Plaintiffs further alleged in detail how the =
Defendants'
services to YMA were fraudulent and not legitimate arms length business
transactions both through the allegations detailing how the fraudulent bills
were created, and the allegation that they siphoned-monies from YMA. Viewed=
 in
the context of the detailed description of the fraudulent scheme as a whole,
these allegations are sufficient to plausibly allege that the Defendants ow=
ned
and controlled YMA and the Defendants &nbsp;[**32]&nbsp;involvement in the
fraudulent scheme. <u>See </u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7CV0K47T90040=
000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4X91-46F0-TXFR-J2D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State Farm Mu=
t.
Auto. Ins. Co. v. Grafman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4X91-46F0-TXFR-J2D7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 655 F. Supp=
. 2d
212 (E.D.N.Y. 2009)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;To the contrary, the
allegations that defendants siphoned monies, and collected kickbacks, from
Milan, especially when viewed in light of the detailed description of how a=
nd
why Milan was fraudulently incorporated, are sufficient to survive a motion=
 to
dismiss.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Defendants assert that the plausibility that they were involved in the frau=
d is
undermined by the fact that the Amended Complaint contains fewer factual
allegations regarding their involvement in the fraud than that alleged as to
other defendants, specifically those that were criminally indicted for their
role in the scheme. As Artek and Sheynkman correctly note, they are the only
defendants without specific allegations of money laundering levied against
them. However, these arguments go to the relative role that the Defendants
played in the scheme, not the plausibility of their involvement in the sche=
me.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HF0K491Y0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, that Artek and
Sheynkman are not alleged to have been the sole owners and managers of YMA,
does not change the fact that they were allegedly complicit in and derived
revenue from the invoices &nbsp;[**33]&nbsp;submitted to the Plaintiffs und=
er
the auspices of a medical company owned and operated by a licensed physicia=
n. <u>See
</u></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HF0K491Y0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPT Med. Serv=
s.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TH6-SDC0-TXFR-J2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2008 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 71156, 2008 WL 4146190, at *12</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that the
plaintiff had adequately plead fraudulent intent as to all defendants under
similar factual circumstances where &quot;the motive shared by all defendan=
ts
was to induce State Farm to pay claims that were otherwise not reimbursable,
and for their efforts they received payments, either directly from State Fa=
rm,
or indirectly as kickbacks disguised as 'management fees'&quot;); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HF0K491Y0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VWB-TVB0-TXFR-J283-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allstate Ins.=
 Co.
v. Halima</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VWB-TVB0-TXFR-J283-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 06-CV-1=
316,
2009 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 22443, 2009 WL 750199, at *5 (E.D.N.Y. March 19, 2009=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(holding that allegations nearly identical to those at issue here
&quot;sufficiently set forth the role of the [specific management
&nbsp;[*268]&nbsp; defendants] and the acts of fraud they are alleged to ha=
ve
committed&quot;). As the court noted in <u>Halima</u>, the conduct that
Sheynkman and Artek to alleged to have committed &quot;is exactly the sort =
of
conduct sought to be discouraged by the requirement that only physicians own
and operate medical professional corporations&quot; and therefore
&quot;requiring Plaintiffs to plead with more particularity would not furth=
er
any of the policy goals of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp;<u>Id.</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, the Defendants contend that the fact that $1,243,256.00, the amou=
nt
received by Artek and Sheynkman from YMA, breaks down to receiving
approximately $150,000 per year, which &quot;amounts to less than the salar=
y of
a first year associate at a major law firm on an annual basis . . . practic=
ally
disproves fraud.&quot; (Defs.' Reply Br. at 3, 5.) However, as even a
&quot;first year associate at a major law firm&quot; would know, there is no
profit threshold required to adequately allege fraud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the Defendants seek to have the Court accept Inna Polack's deposition testi=
mony
in which she disclaimed knowledge of Artek as evidence that Artek was not
involved in fraud. However, Ms. Polack's deposition transcript was attached=
 as
an exhibit to the Plaintiffs' fourth motion for prejudgment attachment, not=
 to
the Amended Complaint. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_0"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4G85C555Y300079JF40017P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HG0K492X0010000400"></a>It is well settled that a Co=
urt
cannot consider documents outside the pleadings on a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion to dismiss unless the documents are incorporated by reference in the
complaint or are integral to the complaint. &quot;To be incorporated by
reference, the complaint must make a clear, definite and substantial refere=
nce
to the documents. . . . [and] [t]o be integral &nbsp;[**35]&nbsp;to a
complaint, the plaintiff must have (1) actual notice of the extraneous
information and (2) relied upon the documents in framing the complaint.&quo=
t; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HF0K491Y0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XS3-HNN0-YB0N-V05B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DeLuca v.
AccessIT Group, Inc.</span></i></a><a
