<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><u><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C. Motion for Prejudgment Attach=
ment
of Assets</span></u></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
January 26, 2009, and in subsequent amendments, the Court granted the
Plaintiffs' motion for prejudgment attachment as to Inna Polack, Alexander
Polack, Emmanuel Kucherovsky, the Initial PC Defendants, and the Management
companies. Relying on the Court's findings in the January 26, 2009 Order, t=
he
Plaintiffs seek an &nbsp;[*269]&nbsp; order attaching the assets of Prestig=
e,
Artek, and Sheynkman.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_0"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4G85C55SHC00079=
JF400005"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HW0K497K0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under New York law, to obtain an
order of attachment, the moving party must demonstrate that (1) it has a ca=
use
of action for a money judgment, (2) there is a probability of success on the
merits, (3) one or more of the enumerated statutory grounds for attachment
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPLR &sect; 6=
201</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
exists, and (4) the amount demanded exceeds the amount of all counterclaims
known to the party seeking attachment.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HW0K497K0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KN1-BJX0-0038-Y3MW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nanjing Texti=
les
Imp/Exp Corp. v. NCC Sportswear Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KN1-BJX0-0038-Y3MW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 06-CV-5=
2,
2006 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 56111, 2006 WL 2337186, at *4 (S.D.N.Y. Aug. 11, 2006=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp;(citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. CPLR
&sect;&sect; 6201</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-850H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>6212(a)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
The statutory ground under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPLR &sect; 6=
201</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applicable in the instant case is </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 6201(=
3)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
which provides that an order of attachment may be granted when:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HW0K497K0040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4G85C563FC00079JF400007"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7J30K499C0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>the defendant, with intent to
defraud his creditors or frustrate the enforcement of a judgment that might=
 be
rendered in plaintiff's favor, has assigned, disposed of, encumbered or
secreted property, or removed it from the state or is about to do any of th=
ese
acts</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7J30K499C0010000400_2"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. C.P.L.R.
&sect; 6201(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (McKinneys 2010). Under the provision of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPLR. &sect;
6201(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, a plaintiff must prove two elements: (1) that defendant
either is about to or has assigned, disposed of, encumbered, or secreted
property, or removed it from the state, and (2) that defendant has acted or
will act with the intent to defraud her creditors or to frustrate the
enforcement of a judgment that might be rendered in plaintiff's favor.&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I52MT7HW0K497K0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4J4J-VDN0-TVW3-P2FH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Encore Credit
Corp. v. LaMattina</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4J4J-VDN0-TVW3-P2FH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 05-CV-5=
442,
2006 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 2935, 2006 WL 148909, at *3 (E.D.N.Y. Jan. 18, 2006)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Central
to the Court's January 26, 2009 decision to order the prejudgment attachmen=
t of
the assets of Inna Polack, Alexander Polack, Emmanuel Kucherovsky and the
Initial PC Defendants was the evidence of the money laundering
&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp;scheme. Specifically, this Court noted that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to the evidence of money laundering, the Plaintiffs have shown that
once the PC Defendants received payment for medical services, Inna Polack
directed these funds to the Management Companies. The Plaintiffs have also
shown that the Management Companies in turn funneled the money to shell
companies created by Emmanuel Kucherovsky who instructed Polack and the
Management Companies to structure their transactions to as to avoid currency
reporting requirements. . . . <u>By presenting evidence of this money
laundering scheme</u>, the Plaintiffs have made a prima facie showing that =
the
Polacks, the PC Defendants, the Management Companies, and Kucherovsky secre=
ted
assets for the purpose of evading creditors.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>January
26, 2009 Order at 13. (emphasis added). Thus, this Court held that the evid=
ence
of the money laundering scheme, in connection with evidence of specific act=
ions
taken by Inna and Alexander Polack that provided circumstantial evidence of=
 the
Polacks' intent to move their assets in order to frustrate a judgment, was
sufficient to satisfy the Plaintiffs' burden under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CPLR &sect;
6201(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to Prestige, the Plaintiffs have established &nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;a
likelihood of success on the merits. The relationship between Prestige, Inna
Polack, and AR is set forth in exacting detail in the Amended Complaint. Gi=
ven
the sufficiency of the Amended Complaint with respect to Prestige, the Court
finds that its analysis in the January 26, 2009 Order with respect to
likelihood of success on the merits as to Ms. Polack and AR is also applica=
ble
to Prestige. Furthermore, the Court finds that the Plaintiffs have shown th=
at
the attachment of Prestige's assets are permissible under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8508-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
