<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*163]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Jos&eacute; A. Cabranes</span>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
appeal presents the question&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; of whether plaintiffs, victim=
s of
South African apartheid, have plausibly alleged relevant conduct committed
within the United States that is sufficient to rebut the Alien Tort Statute=
's
presumption against extraterritoriality.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
hold that they have not.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>AFFIRM</b> the August 28, 2014 =
order
of the United States District Court for the Southern District of New York
(Shira A. Sheindlin, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Judge</i>).</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X00010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Nearly a decade and a half ago,
plaintiffs filed suit under the Alien Tort Statute (&quot;ATS&quot;)</span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn21' href=3D"#_ftn21" name=3D"_ftnref21" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
against various corporations</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn22'
href=3D"#_ftn22" name=3D"_ftnref22" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> for allegedly aiding and abetti=
ng
crimes proscribed by &quot;the law of nations&quot; (also called
&quot;customary international law&quot;)</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn23'
href=3D"#_ftn23" name=3D"_ftnref23" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> committed during apartheid by t=
he
South African government against South Africans within South Africa's sover=
eign
territory.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCT0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCV0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87F0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87F0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1C0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The long and complicated procedu=
ral
history of this consolidated case involves rulings from all three levels of=
 the
federal judiciary.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn24' href=3D"#_ftn24"
name=3D"_ftnref24" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> As relevant here, the District
Court, on April 8, 2009, held that plaintiffs may proceed against defendants
Ford and IBM (the &quot;Companies&quot;) on an agency theory of liability f=
or
apartheid era crimes allegedly committed by their subsidiaries. Thereafter,=
 the
Companies sought a writ of mandamus in this Court.&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; On
September &nbsp;[*164]&nbsp; 17, 2010, while this case remained pending, we
held, in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel v. Royal Dutch Petr=
oleum
Co.</i> (&quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel I</i>&quot;),=
 that
the ATS does not confer jurisdiction over claims pursuant to customary
international law against corporations.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:f=
tn25'
href=3D"#_ftn25" name=3D"_ftnref25" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Supreme Court granted
certiorari and, on April 17, 2013, affirmed our judgment, while explicitly
declining to reach the corporate liability question (&quot;<i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Kiobel II</i>&quot;).</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn26'
href=3D"#_ftn26" name=3D"_ftnref26" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Instead, the Court held that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_1"></a>&quot;the presumption against
extraterritoriality applies to claims under the ATS&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn27' href=3D"#_ftn27" name=3D"_ftnref27" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and thus the statute cannot be applied &quot;to conduct in the territory of
another sovereign.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn28' href=3D"#=
_ftn28"
name=3D"_ftnref28" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1C0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1C0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Two days after the Supreme Court
released its ruling in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel II</i=
>, we
requested supplemental briefing from the parties on the impact of that deci=
sion
on the present case. Thereafter, on August 21, 2013, in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1C0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:595R-CNV1-F04K-J000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Balintulo v.
Daimler AG</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:595R-CNV1-F04K-J000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 727 F.3d 17=
4,
188 (2d Cir. 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>Balintulo I</i>&quot;), we denied the Companies' request for a writ=
 of
mandamus and remanded to the District Court where the Companies would be ab=
le
to &quot;seek the dismissal of all of the plaintiffs' claims, and prevail,
prior to discovery,&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; through a motion for judgment on the
pleadings.&quot; In so doing, we rejected plaintiffs' theory of vicarious
liability for the Companies based on actions taken within South Africa by t=
heir
South African subsidiaries and concluded that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Kiobel II</i> &quot;forecloses the plaintiffs' claims because the
plaintiffs have failed to allege that any relevant conduct occurred in the
United States.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn29' href=3D"#_ftn=
29"
name=3D"_ftnref29" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GG0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GG0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87G0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On remand, the Companies moved f=
or a
judgment in their favor. The District Court ordered the Companies to brief =
the
question of whether corporations can be held liable under the ATS following=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel II</i>. On April 17, 2014, the
District Court held that the Supreme Court in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Kiobel II</i>, which, as noted earlier, expressly declined to addre=
ss
the question of corporate liability under customary international law, had
nonetheless overruled the holding of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Kiobel
I</i> and thus altered the law of the Circuit in that respect.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn30' href=3D"#_ftn30" name=3D"_ftnref30" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The District Court also permitted plaintiffs to move to amend their complai=
nts
in order to allege facts sufficient to overcome the ATS's presumption again=
st
extraterritoriality.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn31' href=3D"#_ftn=
31"
name=3D"_ftnref31" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> After plaintiffs
submitted their proposed amended complaints, the District Court held that t=
he
proposed amendments were futile&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; because the &quot;relevant
conduct&quot; alleged &quot;all occurred abroad&quot; and because plaintiff=
s'
theory of liability was foreclosed by this Court's decision in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Balintulo I</i>.</span><a style=3D'mso=
-footnote-id:
ftn32' href=3D"#_ftn32" name=3D"_ftnref32" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87G0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87G0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GH0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GH0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X10030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc3_1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
generally review a district court's decision to permit or deny leave to ame=
nd a
complaint for abuse of discretion, &quot;keeping in mind that leave to amend
should be freely granted when justice so requires.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn33' href=3D"#_ftn33" name=3D"_ftnref33" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
However, when denial of leave to file a revised pleading is based on a legal
interpretation, such as futility, a reviewing court conducts a <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> review.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn34' href=3D"#_ftn34" name=3D"_ftnref34" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> A
proposed amendment to a complaint &nbsp;[*165]&nbsp; is futile when it
&quot;could not withstand a motion to dismiss.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-=
footnote-id:
ftn35' href=3D"#_ftn35" name=3D"_ftnref35" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_1"></a>In order to survive a motion to dismi=
ss,
&quot;a complaint must contain sufficient factual matter, accepted as true,=
 to
'state a claim to relief that is plausible on its face.'&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn36' href=3D"#_ftn36" name=3D"_ftnref36" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
And while a court must accept all of the allegations contained in a complai=
nt
as true, &quot;that tenet is inapplicable to legal conclusions, and threadb=
are
recitals of the elements of a cause of action, supported by mere conclusory
statements, do not suffice.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn37'
href=3D"#_ftn37" name=3D"_ftnref37" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. The ATS Claims</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, plaintiffs claim that they have alleged extensive new
facts&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; demonstrating that the Companies' U.S.-based actions
constituted unlawful aiding and abetting of crimes in violation of the law =
of
nations. They allege that the Companies' &quot;specialized product developm=
ent,
sales of such tailored products, and provision of expertise and training&qu=
ot;
were aimed at facilitating abuses committed in South Africa.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn38' href=3D"#_ftn38" name=3D"_ftnref38" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Specifically, plaintiffs allege that defendant Ford (1) provided specialized
vehicles to the South African police and security forces to enable these fo=
rces
to enforce apartheid,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn39' href=3D"#_ft=
n39"
name=3D"_ftnref39" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and (2) shared
information with the South African regime about anti-apartheid and union
activists, thereby facilitating the suppression of anti-apartheid activity.=
</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn40' href=3D"#_ftn40" name=3D"_ftnref40" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> As
for IBM, plaintiffs claim that the company (1) designed specific technologi=
es
that were essential for racial separation under apartheid and the
denationalization of black South Africans;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn41' href=3D"#_ftn41" name=3D"_ftnref41" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2) bid on, and executed, contracts in South Africa with unlawful purposes =
such
as &quot;denationalization&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn42'
href=3D"#_ftn42" name=3D"_ftnref42" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> of black South
Africans;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn43' href=3D"#_ftn43" name=3D=
"_ftnref43"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>23</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> and (3) provided training, support, and expertise to the
South African government in using IBM's specialized technologies.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn44' href=3D"#_ftn44" name=3D"_ftnref44" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>24</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
turn, the Companies assert that the District Court properly denied plaintif=
fs'
motion for leave to amend their complaints because (1) plaintiffs cannot
satisfy the ATS's territoriality and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>mens
rea</i> requirements; (2) corporations cannot be sued under the ATS; and (3)
there is no aiding and abetting liability under the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Jurisdiction Under the ATS</=
span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PT6PXSFPP000M2NRW0039J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X10050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our inquiry begins by assessing
whether the ATS grants us jurisdiction over plaintiffs' action. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_1"></a>The Alien Tort Statute contains numer=
ous
jurisdictional predicates, each of which must be satisfied before a court m=
ay
properly assume jurisdiction over an ATS claim.</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn45' href=3D"#_ftn45" name=3D"_ftnref45" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>25</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Thus, at the outset, a court must assure itself that: &quot;(1) the complai=
nt
pleads a violation of the law of nations; (2) the presumption against the
extraterritorial application of the ATS, announced by the Supreme Court in =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel</i> [<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>II</i>], does not bar the claim; (3) customary international law
recognizes [the asserted] liability [of a] defendant; and (4) the theory
&nbsp;[*166]&nbsp; of liability alleged by plaintiffs (<i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, aiding and abetting, conspiracy)&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; is
recognized by customary international law [or 'the law of nations'].&quot;<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn46' href=3D"#_ftn46" name=3D"_ftnref46" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>26</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
And while a defect in any of these jurisdictional predicates would be fatal=
 to
a plaintiff's claims, courts retain discretion regarding the order and mann=
er
in which they undertake these inquiries.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn47'
href=3D"#_ftn47" name=3D"_ftnref47" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>27</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
we begin by addressing the question of whether plaintiffs, in their proposed
amended complaints, allege sufficient conduct to displace the ATS's presump=
tion
against extraterritoriality. Because we agree with the District Court's
conclusion that they do not, we need not address the other jurisdictional
predicates.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn48' href=3D"#_ftn48"
name=3D"_ftnref48" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>28</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. ATS and the Presumption Again=
st
Extraterritoriality</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X20010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PT6PXSKHV000M2NRW0039K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X20030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X20050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As noted above, the Supreme Cour=
t in
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel II</i> made clear that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_1"></a>claims under the ATS cannot be brough=
t for
violations of the law of nations occurring within the territory of a sovere=
ign
nation other than the United States.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn4=
9'
href=3D"#_ftn49" name=3D"_ftnref49" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>29</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Court explain=
ed
that it was dismissing the plaintiffs-claims because &quot;all the relevant
conduct took place outside the United States.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn50' href=3D"#_ftn50" name=3D"_ftnref50" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>30</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The wholly extraterritorial nature of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>Kiobel</i>
plaintiffs' claims was &quot;a dispositive fact&quot; for the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel II</i> Court and so it had no r=
eason
to explore how courts should proceed where, as here, some of the &quot;rele=
vant
conduct&quot;&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; occurred in the United States.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn51' href=3D"#_ftn51" name=3D"_ftnref51" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>31</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GJ0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GJ0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Mastafa v. Chevron Corporation</i>, we applied the Supreme Court's
rulings in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Morrison v. National Aus=
tralia
Bank Limited</i></span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn52' href=3D"#_ftn52"
name=3D"_ftnref52" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>32</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Kiobel II</i> to clarify that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_1"></a>the &quot;focus&quot; of the ATS inqu=
iry is
on the nature and location of the conduct constituting the alleged offenses
under the law of nations.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn53' href=3D"=
#_ftn53"
name=3D"_ftnref53" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>33</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Accordingly, to
determine whether specific claims can be brought under the ATS, a court must
isolate the &quot;relevant conduct&quot; of a defendant&#8212;conduct that =
is
alleged to be either a direct violation of the law of nations or the aiding=
 and
abetting of another's violation of the law of nations&#8212;in a complaint =
and
then conduct a two-step jurisdictional analysis.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCW0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&nbsp;[*167]&nbsp; Step one is a
determination of whether that &quot;relevant conduct&quot; sufficiently
&quot;touches and concerns&quot; the United States so as to displace the
presumption against extraterritoriality. Step two is a determination of whe=
ther
that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>same</i> conduct states a clai=
m for
a violation of the law of nations or aiding and abetting
another's&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; violation of the law of nations.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn54' href=3D"#_ftn54" name=3D"_ftnref54" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>34</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCW0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCX0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCX0040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc10_1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to satisfy the second step of this analysis, a plaintiff stating a cl=
aim
under an aiding and abetting theory must demonstrate that the defendant
&quot;'(1) provides practical assistance to the principal which has a
substantial effect on the perpetration of the crime, and (2) does so with t=
he
purpose of facilitating the commission of that crime.'&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn55' href=3D"#_ftn55" name=3D"_ftnref55" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>35</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>mens rea</i> standard for acces=
sorial
liability in ATS actions is &quot;purpose rather than knowledge alone.&quot=
;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn56' href=3D"#_ftn56" name=3D"_ftnref56" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>36</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Knowledge of or complicity in the perpetration of a crime&#8212;without
evidence that a defendant purposefully facilitated the commission of that
crime&#8212;is thus insufficient to establish a claim of aiding and abetting
liability under the ATS.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn57' href=3D"#=
_ftn57"
name=3D"_ftnref57" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>37</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. Analysis of Plaintiffs'
Complaints</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Turning
to the complaints in the instant case, plaintiffs assert that the following
conduct by defendant Ford is sufficient to displace the ATS's presumption
against extraterritoriality: (1) Ford provided specialized vehicles to the
South African security forces that enabled these forces to violently suppre=
ss
opposition to apartheid;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn58' href=3D"#=
_ftn58"
name=3D"_ftnref58" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>38</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and (2) Ford was
responsible for aiding and abetting the suppression of its own workforce in
South Africa.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn59' href=3D"#_ftn59"
name=3D"_ftnref59" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>39</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for IBM, plaintiffs allege that (1) IBM employees trained employees of the
South African government on how to use their hardware and software to create
identity documents&#8212;&quot;the very means by which black South Africans
were deprived of their South African nationality&quot;;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn60' href=3D"#_ftn60" name=3D"_ftnref60" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>40</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2) IBM bid on contracts in South Africa with unlawful purposes such as
denationalizing black South Africans;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn=
61'
href=3D"#_ftn61" name=3D"_ftnref61" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>41</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and (3) IBM desig=
ned
specific technologies that&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; were essential for racial
separation under apartheid and the denationalization of black South African=
s.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn62' href=3D"#_ftn62" name=3D"_ftnref62" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>42</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1G0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Balintulo I</i>, we reasoned that the Companies' alleged domestic
conduct lacked a clear nexus to the human rights abuses occurring in South
Africa.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn63' href=3D"#_ftn63" name=3D"_=
ftnref63"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>43</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Here too, &nbsp;[*168]&nbsp; plaintiffs' amended pleadi=
ngs
do not establish federal jurisdiction under the ATS because they do not
plausibly allege that the Companies themselves engaged in any &quot;relevant
conduct&quot; within the United States to overcome the presumption against
extraterritorial application of the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. Allegations Against Ford</spa=
n></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PT6PXSSMP000M2NRW0039M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2D6N1G0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GK0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Beginning with the allegations
against Ford, plaintiffs only allege &quot;relevant conduct&quot; that occu=
rred
in South Africa, thus failing to satisfy step one of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Mastafa</i>'s two-step jurisdictional analysis.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn64' href=3D"#_ftn64" name=3D"_ftnref64" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>44</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
was Ford's subsidiary in South Africa, not Ford, that is alleged to have
assembled and sold the specialized vehicles to South Africa's government, w=
ith
parts shipped principally from Canada and the United Kingdom&#8212;not from=
 the
United States.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn65' href=3D"#_ftn65"
name=3D"_ftnref65" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>45</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Similarly, it was
Ford's South African subsidiary,&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; not Ford, that allegedly
provided information to the apartheid government about anti-apartheid activ=
ists
in South Africa.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn66' href=3D"#_ftn66"
name=3D"_ftnref66" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>46</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Although plaintif=
fs
repeatedly allege&#8212;no less than six times in their proposed amended
complaint</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn67' href=3D"#_ftn67" name=3D=
"_ftnref67"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>47</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>&#8212;that Ford controlled their South African subsidia=
ry,
we have previously rejected a vicarious liability theory based on allegatio=
ns
materially identical to those asserted here.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn68' href=3D"#_ftn68" name=3D"_ftnref68" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>48</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GK0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X30010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs contend that their
amended pleadings demonstrate that the Companies controlled their South Afr=
ican
subsidiaries from the United States such that they could be found
directly&#8212;and not just vicariously&#8212;liable for their subsidiaries=
' conduct
under the ATS. But holding&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; Ford to be directly responsible
for the actions of its South African subsidiary, as plaintiffs would have us
do, would ignore <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_1"></a>well-settled
principles of corporate law, which treat parent corporations and their
subsidiaries as legally distinct entities.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn69' href=3D"#_ftn69" name=3D"_ftnref69" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>49</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
While courts occasionally &quot;pierce the corporate veil&quot; and ignore a
subsidiary's separate legal status, they will do so only in extraordinary
circumstances, such as where the corporate parent excessively dominates its
&quot;subsidiary in such a way as to make it a 'mere instrumentality' of the
parent.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn70' href=3D"#_ftn70"
name=3D"_ftnref70" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>50</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
plaintiffs present no plausible allegations&#8212;indeed, they present no
allegations&#8212;that would form any basis for us to &quot;pierce [Ford's]
corporate veil.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn71' href=3D"#_ft=
n71"
name=3D"_ftnref71" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>51</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The complaints do=
 not
suggest that Ford's control over its subsidiaries differed from that of most
companies headquartered in the United States with subsidiaries abroad. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_0"></a>Allegations of general corporate
supervision are insufficient to rebut the presumption against territoriality
and establish aiding and abetting liability under the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*169]&nbsp; <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>2.
Allegations Against IBM</b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X30030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X30050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs' first&nbsp;[**17]&nb=
sp;
allegation against IBM also fails because the &quot;relevant conduct&quot; =
all
occurred within South Africa and so they cannot satisfy step one of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mastafa</i>'s two-step jurisdictional
analysis.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn72' href=3D"#_ftn72" name=3D=
"_ftnref72"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>52</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> Just as in the case of Ford, it is IBM's South African
subsidiary&#8212;not IBM&#8212;that is alleged to have trained South African
government employees to use IBM hardware and software to create identity
materials.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn73' href=3D"#_ftn73"
name=3D"_ftnref73" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>53</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> These allegations
cannot rebut the presumption against extraterritoriality as they do not
sufficiently &quot;tie[ ] the relevant human rights violations to actions t=
aken
within the United States.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn74'
href=3D"#_ftn74" name=3D"_ftnref74" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>54</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs'
second allegation against IBM&#8212;that the company bid on contracts meant=
 to
further the denationalization of South African blacks&#8212;falls short of
alleging a violation of the law of nations for a simple reason: IBM did not=
 win
the contract for the only bid specifically alleged to have been made by IBM,
rather than IBM's South African subsidiary.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn75' href=3D"#_ftn75" name=3D"_ftnref75" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>55</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Indeed, even according to plaintiffs, another company, ICL, won the passboo=
ks
contract over IBM.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn76' href=3D"#_ftn76"
name=3D"_ftnref76" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>56</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_0"></a>It is simply not a violation of the =
law of
nations to bid on, and lose, a contract that arguably would help a sovereign
government perpetrate&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; an asserted violation of the law of
nations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
final allegation against IBM, on the other hand, appears to &quot;touch and
concern&quot; the United States with sufficient force to displace the
presumption against extraterritoriality. Their proposed amended complaint
reads, in relevant part, as follows:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the United States, IBM developed both the hardware and the software&#8212;b=
oth
a machine and a program&#8212;to create the Bophuthatswana ID. Once IBM had
developed the system, it was transferred to the Bophuthatswana government f=
or
implementation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn77' href=3D"#_ftn77"
name=3D"_ftnref77" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>57</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S28T4GM0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87K0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Identity documents, like those
allegedly created by IBM and transferred to the Bophuthatswana government, =
were
an essential component of the system of racial separation in South Africa.<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn78' href=3D"#_ftn78" name=3D"_ftnref78" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>58</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
And so, designing particular technologies in the United States that would
facilitate South African racial separation would appear to be both
&quot;specific and domestic&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn79'
href=3D"#_ftn79" name=3D"_ftnref79" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>59</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> conduct that would
satisfy the first of the two steps of our jurisdictional analysis.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn80' href=3D"#_ftn80" name=3D"_ftnref80" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>60</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Accordingly, if this allegation is able to also satisfy the second prong of=
 our
extraterritoriality &nbsp;[*170]&nbsp; inquiry&#8212;that is, if such condu=
ct
aided and abetted a violation of the law of nations&#8212;the
presumption&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; against extraterritoriality would be displaced
and we would be able to establish jurisdiction for this particular claim un=
der
the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87K0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2SF87K0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Upon an initial review of the
&quot;relevant conduct&quot; in the complaint,&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; however, we
conclude that plaintiffs' claim against IBM does not meet the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>mens rea</i> requirement for aiding and
abetting liability established by our Court. While the complaint must
&quot;support [] an inference that [IBM] acted with the 'purpose' to advance
[South Africa's] human rights abuses,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn81' href=3D"#_ftn81" name=3D"_ftnref81" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>61</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> it
plausibly alleges, at most, that the company acted with knowledge that its =
acts
might facilitate the South African government's apartheid policies. But, as=
 we
noted earlier, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_0"></a>mere knowledge wit=
hout
proof of purpose is insufficient to make out the proper <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>mens rea</i> for aiding and abetting liability.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn82' href=3D"#_ftn82" name=3D"_ftnref82" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>62</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X40020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2HM6X40040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GRBF9S2N1RCY0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, where the language in =
the
complaint seems to suggest that IBM acted purposefully,</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn83' href=3D"#_ftn83" name=3D"_ftnref83" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>63</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;it does so in conclusory terms and fails to establish even a baseline
degree of plausibility of plaintiffs' claims.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn84' href=3D"#_ftn84" name=3D"_ftnref84" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'f=
ont-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>64</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_0"></a>A complaint will not &quot;suffice i=
f it
tenders naked assertions devoid of further factual enhancement.&quot;</span=
><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn85' href=3D"#_ftn85" name=3D"_ftnref85" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>65</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Indeed, plaintiffs do not&#8212;and cannot&#8212;plausibly allege that by
developing hardware and software to collect innocuous population data, IBM's
purpose was to denationalize black South Africans and further the aims of a
brutal regime.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn86' href=3D"#_ftn86"
name=3D"_ftnref86" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>66</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> This absence of a
connection between IBM's &quot;relevant conduct&quot; and&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;=
 the
alleged human rights abuses of the South African government means that
plaintiffs, even if allowed to amend their complaint, will be unable to sta=
te a
valid ATS claim against IBM.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
because plaintiffs fail plausibly to plead that any U.S.-based conduct on t=
he
part of either Ford or IBM aided and abetted South Africa's asserted violat=
ions
of the law of nations, their claims cannot form the basis of our
jurisdiction&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; under the ATS. We therefore affirm the Distr=
ict
Court's denial of plaintiffs' motion for leave to file an amended complaint
because the proposed amendments are futile as a matter of law.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
summarize, we hold that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(1)
Knowledge of or complicity in the perpetration of a crime under the law of
nations (customary international law)&#8212;absent evidence that a defendant
purposefully facilitated the commission of that crime&#8212;is insufficient=
 to
establish a claim of aiding and abetting liability under the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(2)
It is not a violation of the law of nations to bid on, and lose, a contract
&nbsp;[*171]&nbsp; that arguably would help a sovereign government perpetra=
te
an asserted violation of the law of nations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(3)
Allegations of general corporate supervision are insufficient to rebut the
presumption against extraterritoriality and establish aiding and abetting
liability under the ATS.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(4)
Here, plaintiffs' amended pleadings do not establish federal jurisdiction u=
nder
the ATS because they do not plausibly allege that the Companies themselves
engaged in any &quot;relevant conduct&quot; within the United States to
overcome the presumption against extraterritorial application of the ATS.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>a.
Holding Ford to be directly responsible&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; for the actions of
its South African subsidiary, as plaintiffs would have us do, ignores
well-settled principles of corporate law, which treat parent corporations a=
nd
their subsidiaries as legally distinct entities.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b.
Plaintiffs have plausibly alleged some specific, domestic conduct in the
complaint&#8212;namely, that IBM in the United States designed particular
technologies in the United States that facilitated South African apartheid.
This conduct satisfies the first prong of our extraterritoriality analysis =
as
it &quot;touches and concerns&quot; the United States.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>c.
Plaintiffs' complaint against IBM fails on the second prong of the required
jurisdictional analysis: it does not plausibly allege that IBM's conduct
purposefully aided and abetted South Africa's alleged violations of customa=
ry
international law.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>d.
Accordingly, the alleged conduct cannot state a claim for aiding and abetti=
ng
liability under the ATS and cannot form the basis for our jurisdiction.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(5)
Because we decide the case on the basis of the presumption against
extraterritoriality, we need not address whether plaintiffs' complaint
satisfies the ATS's other jurisdictional predicates, including whether the
complaint pleads a violation&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; of the law of nations; wheth=
er
customary international law recognizes the asserted liability of the Compan=
ies;
and whether the theory of liability alleged by plaintiffs is recognized by
customary international law.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the reasons set forth above, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>AF=
FIRM</b>
the August 28, 2014 order of the District Court.</span></p>

