mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d 75=
, 80
(2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and they were allegedly &quot;link[ed] . . . in . . .
specific way[s] to . . . fraudulent representation[s] or omission[s]&quot; =
made
in those offering documents. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM6390050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM6390040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiVittorio</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 822 F.2d at
1249</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Even
under the heightened pleading standard of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
Plaintiffs are not obliged to disaggregate these affiliates to pursue their
fraud claim. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_2"></a>Where a plural author=
 is
implied by the nature of the representations&#8212;for instance, where, as
here, (1) the alleged fraud is based on statements made in the offering
materials and (2) the complaint gives grounds for attributing the statement=
s to
the group&#8212;group pleading may satisfy the source identification requir=
ed
by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Following <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Luce</i>, we hold that th=
ere is
no fixed requirement in such circumstances to identify a single entity with=
in
the group on pain of dismissal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
in the instant case, each Wachovia entity was a member of a corporate subgr=
oup
that operated together and communicated with Plaintiffs under a shared trade
name: &quot;Wachovia Securities.&quot;&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; J.A. at 263, 309, =
466.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn94' href=3D"#_ftn94" name=3D"_ftnref94" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Each employee involved in the CDO transactions was listed on a Wachovia
Securities phone list without reference to a specific entity in the subgrou=
p. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGF0020000400"></a>And the logo emblazoned on t=
he
marketing materials was that of Wachovia Securities. As a result, Plaintiff=
s'
designation in the complaint of these three defendants by a group shorthand
rather than their individual entity names amounts, at most, to excusable
mislabeling. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGF0010000400"></a>Datskow v. Teledyne, Inc., C=
ont'l
Prods. Div., 899 F.2d 1298, 1300-01 (2d Cir. 1990)</i>. And the costs of su=
ch
mislabeling are better borne in this situation by those who authored the
offering documents, which were characterized by that same slippage between =
the
collective trade name and the entities acting under it. It would be strange=
 indeed
to demand greater precision of Plaintiffs in pleading the author's identity
than they received as readers of these documents.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, given that the alleged fraud focuses (1) on specific misrepresentation=
s in
the CDO offering documents and (2) on the coordinated activity by specific
Wachovia affiliates in constructing and offering these CDOs, Plaintiffs'
identification of the group suffices&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; to meet the
particularity of attribution required by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Wachovia's own lack of transparency in identifying which entity was
communicating to prospective investors only bolsters our conclusion in this
regard.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. Material Misrepresentations a=
nd
Omissions</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGF0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Having concluded that the compla=
int
sufficiently identifies Wachovia as the source of many of the statements at
issue, we next consider whether &quot;the complaint assert[s] facts that
plausibly support the inference of fraud.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGF0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cohen</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 711 F.3d at=
 360</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Because the substance of the fraud alleged with respect to Octans and
Sagittarius &nbsp;[*174]&nbsp; differs in kind from that alleged with respe=
ct
to Longshore, we treat the two types of fraud allegations separately. We
discuss the constellation CDOs in this section and the next, Section I.C
(Scienter), before turning to Longshore in Section I.D.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGG0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As to Octans and Sagittarius, the
district court determined that Plaintiffs had inadequately pleaded a materi=
al
misrepresentation or omission. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See =
</i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGF0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5849-2RY1-F04F-0231-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wells Fargo</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5849-2RY1-F04F-0231-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2013 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 49665, 2013 WL 1294668, at *10-12</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGG0030000400"></a>In so doing the district cou=
rt
erred, in our view, by imposing its own reading of Plaintiffs' substantive
fraud allegations without considering them in the light most favorable to
Plaintiffs. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGG0030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_2"></a>While </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires that &quot;the circumstances constituting fraud&quot;
be&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; &quot;state[d] with particularity,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
9(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, it does not require factual pleadings that demonstrate=
 the
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>probability</i> of wrongdoing. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR4228T4YJ0020000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGG0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Iqbal</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 556 U.S. at=
 678</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;The plausibility standard is not akin to a 'probability requirement'=
 . .
. .&quot; (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGG0040000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bell Atl. Cor=
p.
v. Twombly</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 550 U.S. 54=
4,
556, 127 S. Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929 (2007))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR4228T4YJ0020000400_2"></a>At the pleadings stage, the
alleged fraud need only be <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>plausibl=
e</i>
based on the complaint; it need not be more likely than other possibilities=
. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR4228T4YJ0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Twombly</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 550 U.S. at=
 556</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_2"></a>&quot;[A] well-pleaded complaint may
proceed even if it strikes a savvy judge that actual proof of those facts is
improbable, and that a recovery is very remote and unlikely.&quot; (citation
and internal quotation marks omitted)); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>cf.
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR4228T4YJ0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cohen</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 711 F.3d at=
 360</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Iqbal</i> . . . requires
assertions of facts supporting a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pl=
ausible</i>
inference of fraud&#8212;not of facts which can have no conceivable other
explanation.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Upon
our <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> review, we conclude=
 that
the facts asserted in the complaint are sufficiently particular and plausib=
ly
support the existence of a material misrepresentation or omission with resp=
ect
to Wachovia and Harding, but not with respect to SAI.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
Wachovia and Harding</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
gravamen of Plaintiffs' complaint is that, as to the two constellation CDOs,
the offering documents (1) misrepresented that SAI and Harding would serve =
as
judicious collateral managers, who would&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; employ certain
selection and monitoring procedures, and (2) omitted both Magnetar's role in
selecting the collateral and Magnetar's adverse position relative to the CD=
Os.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint plausibly suggests that these alleged misrepresentations were mad=
e by
both Wachovia and Harding. First and foremost, Wachovia (as a group) author=
ed
the Octans and Sagittarius offering documents in which the allegedly mislea=
ding
statements appeared. In addition, Harding itself stated that it &quot;w[oul=
d]
be responsible for selecting and monitoring [Octans'] collateral,&quot; wit=
h no
mention of Magnetar. J.A. at 122. And this statement came from a page of the
Octans Offering Circular prepared by <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Harding</i>,
at the top of which Harding claimed &quot;responsibility for the information
contained in this section&quot; and expressly represented that it had &quot=
;not
omit[ted] anything likely to affect the import of such information.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 253.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
allege that had they known that the collateral managers would not exercise
independent judgment and would instead accede to the desires of a powerful
short investor, they would not have invested in either CDO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63B0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>It is not for us to say at this
stage whether Plaintiffs' account of Magnetar's role and of
Defendants'&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; sleights of hand regarding that role is true,=
 nor
is it for us to say whether, at a later stage, a judge or jury might find t=
hat
such misrepresentations were immaterial to sophisticated investors
&nbsp;[*175]&nbsp; like Plaintiffs. The question properly before us is whet=
her
the facts pleaded are sufficiently detailed to satisfy </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&#8212;in
particular, whether these facts &quot;plausibly support the inference of
fraud.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR4228T4YJ0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cohen</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583W-FYW1-F04K-J0JV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 711 F.3d at=
 360</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Our
conclusion that they do rests, first, on the numerous alleged exchanges bet=
ween
Magnetar and Defendants. As to Octans, for example, various exchanges in Au=
gust
and September 2006 between Prusko and Chau&#8212;senior officers of Magnetar
and Harding, respectively&#8212;may be read to suggest (1) Magnetar's
considerable influence over the CDO and (2) high-level discussions regarding
Magnetar's long-short strategy. An email from a Wachovia trader to Prusko a=
lso
refers to &quot;a few trades [Wachovia] did on behalf of Magnetar,&quot; wh=
ile,
in another email, a Harding employee asks Prusko to &quot;let [them] know if
[he] plan[s] on shorting any names into any of the [Octans] transactions.&q=
uot;
J.A. at 125. Together, such emails plausibly indicate not only a cozy relat=
ionship
with Magnetar but also specific actions taken by Wachovia and&nbsp;[**35]&n=
bsp;
Harding at Magnetar's direction based on Magnetar's short positions, such as
selecting certain CDSs for inclusion in Octans.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Exchanges
between Magnetar and Wachovia pertaining to Sagittarius support a similar
reading of Magnetar's undisclosed role with respect to this CDO. For exampl=
e,
Prusko emailed Wachovia's managing director a request to make SAI more
&quot;user friendly.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> =
at
131. In another email to a team of Wachovia employees a few months later,
Prusko attached a graph showing higher profits to Magnetar from the CDO's
failure and described Sagittarius as &quot;not a pretty bond.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 134.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, both Octans and Sagittarius had built-in features conducive to
Magnetar's alleged strategy, and the presence of these features lends furth=
er
support to Plaintiffs' account of Magnetar's role. For example, Plaintiffs
allege that Magnetar received sourcing fees for every CDS that it chose for=
 the
asset bundle, and that Magnetar also negotiated for favorable waterfall rul=
es,
which would permit equity holders like itself to continue receiving payments
longer in the face of early signs of default. While at least some of these
features were disclosed to investors and may not form a basis for fraud in
themselves,&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; they may be read to suggest favoritism towards
Magnetar and thereby to put Plaintiffs' other allegations in context.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Construed
in the light most favorable to Plaintiffs, the emails regarding Octans and
Sagittarius&#8212;together with the structural elements advantageous to
Magnetar&#8212;plausibly support an inference that the offering documents
materially misled investors by falsely holding out the skill and rigorous a=
sset
selection methods of the respective collateral managers while failing to
disclose Magnetar's antagonistic influence.</span></p>

