<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*180]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGJ0050000400"></a>Whatever cognizance of
secondhand&nbsp;[**48]&nbsp; allegations courts may take at the pleading st=
age,
it seems to us clear that the portions of the SEC order quoted in the compl=
aint
are in the nature of <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_1"></a>allegations
&quot;upon information and belief,&quot; which cannot ordinarily form the b=
asis
of a fraud claim &quot;except as to matters peculiarly within the opposing
party's knowledge.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGJ004000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Luce</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 802 F.2d at=
 54
n.1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (citation and internal quotation marks omitted). Even a=
s to
the latter, a fraud plaintiff must generally state the facts upon which her
belief is founded. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>While
a complaint that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>merely</i> recites
others' allegations may therefore be insufficient, we are satisfied that in
this case Plaintiffs do also allege non-conclusory facts and that these
additional factual pleadings are sufficient to render unproblematic any imp=
lied
reliance on the SEC findings. In particular, the complaint asserts that:</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(1)
Wachovia had been preparing two CDO deals in February 2007, one of which be=
came
Longshore while the other was canceled;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(2)
because Wachovia was the warehouse provider for both deals, assets from the
canceled CDO remained on its books;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(3)
based on superior insider knowledge, &quot;Wachovia was aware of significant
problems in the []MBS sector,&quot; J.A. at 137;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(4)
despite Defendants'&nbsp;[**49]&nbsp; representations that all acquisitions=
 for
Longshore would be carried out in arm's length-type transactions, the assets
from the canceled CDO deal &quot;were sold to Longshore for $4.6 million ov=
er
their then-current market value,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Id.</i>
at 137-38; and</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(5)
this decline in value would otherwise have been borne by Wachovia.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>These
allegations&#8212;albeit clearly overlapping with the SEC order&#8212;are m=
ade
directly by Plaintiffs, and were signed by Plaintiffs' counsel subject to t=
he
requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 11</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
11(b)(3)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Taken together, they are, in our view, adequate to sur=
vive
a motion to dismiss.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW10020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants object that the compl=
aint
requires the court to infer their knowledge of a decline in the value of the
specific assets transferred into Longshore from broader conditions in the
market. That inference, however, seems to us reasonable under the circumsta=
nces
and, thus, is fair to draw in Plaintiffs' favor at the Rule 12(b)(6) stage.=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW10010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BH8-DK71-F04K-J1NN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nielsen</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BH8-DK71-F04K-J1NN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 746 F.3d at=
 62</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
This is not a case in which a securities fraud plaintiff alleges a mere
downturn in the market, asking the court to infer defendant's knowledge of =
the
poor performance of a given company. Plaintiffs pleaded scienter by asserti=
ng
that Wachovia was aware of a high probability that at the time the assets
were&nbsp;[**50]&nbsp; transferred into Longshore, the original acquisition
cost no longer represented those assets' fair market value. The basis for t=
hat
alleged knowledge is not vague; it is Wachovia's status as a major particip=
ant
in the MBS market, a participant who was, consequently, aware of a broad dr=
op
in value in the very class of securities to which the transferred assets
belonged.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, while the complaint could be more detailed as to the timeline and
valuation of the securities in question, there is enough particularity to
withstand Defendants' Rule 12(b)(6) motion. Plaintiffs' direct allegations =
(1)
that Wachovia knew the MBS market well, and knew that it was in decline over
the &quot;months&quot; in question, J.A. at 137, and (2) that Wachovia
nevertheless transferred assets on its books at their original cost basis w=
hile
telling investors that SAI would ensure &nbsp;[*181]&nbsp; that the transac=
tions
were conducted as if between unrelated parties, suffice to raise a plausible
inference of material misrepresentation by Wachovia as well as a strong
inference of scienter.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
SAI</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
contrast to the allegations regarding Wachovia's role, Plaintiffs' claims
regarding SAI's role are less detailed and fail to satisfy the particularity
required&nbsp;[**51]&nbsp; by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Indeed, although the Longshore scheme differs in kind from the constellation
CDOs scheme, as to SAI, the complaint suffers from the same infirmity that =
we
found earlier when dealing with the constellation CDOs&#8212;namely, that
Plaintiffs have alleged neither a fraudulent misrepresentation by SAI nor, =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>a fortiori</i>, scienter on SAI's part=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the case of Longshore, the plausibly fraudulent statement in the offering
documents is that SAI, as the collateral manager, would &quot;cause any
acquisition or sale&quot; of assets &quot;to be conducted on an arm's length
basis,&quot; or at least &quot;on terms as favorable to [Longshore] as woul=
d be
the case&quot; in a transaction between unrelated parties.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 136. That statement is clea=
rly
attributable to Wachovia, as the author of the offering documents. But as we
noted before in the case of the constellation CDOs, the problem here is that
Plaintiffs have not adequately pleaded any such misrepresentation <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>by SAI. Cf. supra</i> Section I.B.2. A=
nd
Plaintiffs' allegations regarding the close relationship between SAI and
Wachovia lack particulars from which it would be reasonable to infer that S=
AI
itself misled investors about its role in the acquisition of assets by
Longshore.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn98' href=3D"#_ftn98"
name=3D"_ftnref98" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Absent
facts plausibly indicating a misrepresentation by SAI, the complaint
necessarily lacks allegations giving rise to a strong inference of scienter=
 on
SAI's part. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. supra</i> Section I=
.C.
Hence, as to SAI, the complaint fails to state a claim in connection with
either the constellation CDOs or Longshore. To proceed against SAI, Plainti=
ffs
must replead their claim with sufficient particularity to give rise to the
requisite inferences of misrepresentation and scienter.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to all three CDOs, Plaintiffs lost millions of dollars. But that lo=
ss
coincided with the recent financial crisis, which affected large swaths of
securities in and beyond the MBS market. For that reason, the question aris=
es
whether Defendants' misstatements met the requirements of loss causation as
well as of transaction causation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn99'
href=3D"#_ftn99" name=3D"_ftnref99" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Loss causation was
not &nbsp;[*182]&nbsp; pleaded in&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp; great detail below, but
neither was its absence relied on by the district court in dismissing the
complaint. Nevertheless, Defendants argue on appeal, as they argued before =
the
district court, that Plaintiffs' failure to plead it is fatal to the compla=
int.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PN0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>At the outset of our review of
Plaintiffs' pleadings as to loss causation are two unsettled questions: (1)
whether this element must be&nbsp;[**54]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>pleaded</i> (rather than simply supported by evidence at a later st=
age)
and, if so, (2) with what level of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
particularity</i>.
Although loss causation must certainly be pleaded to state a claim for fede=
ral
securities fraud, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PN0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lentell v.
Merrill Lynch &amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 396 F.3d 16=
1,
172 (2d Cir. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, our Circuit has not had occasi=
on
to decide whether a plaintiff making an analogous claim under state common =
law
must likewise plead this element.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn100'
href=3D"#_ftn100" name=3D"_ftnref100" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Private
Securities Litigation Reform Act (&quot;PSLRA&quot;), which codified loss
causation as a separate element of federal securities fraud actions, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-7025-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78u-4(b)(4)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, draws on&#8212;but also departs from&#8212;the common =
law.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PN0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G05-93F0-004B-Y01S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dura Pharms.,
Inc. v. Broudo</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G05-93F0-004B-Y01S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 544 U.S. 33=
6,
341, 125 S. Ct. 1627, 161 L. Ed. 2d 577 (2005)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;[A] private damages action [for securities fraud under federal law]
resembles, but is not identical to, common-law tort actions for deceit and
misrepresentation.&quot;). It is thus scarcely clear that pleading is ident=
ical
in the two contexts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63F0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We recently assumed the existenc=
e of
a requirement that loss causation be pleaded in a similar common-law case in
which loss causation had, in fact, been pleaded in considerable detail. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63F0040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63F0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5FS3-V9K1-F04K-J03V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fin. Guar. In=
s.
Co. v. Putnam Advisory Co., LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5FS3-V9K1-F04K-J03V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 783 F.3d 39=
5,
402-05 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
