 Law
of Torts</i>, 43 U. Chi. L. Rev. 69, 72 (1975).)</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
requirements of transaction and loss causation are exactly analogous to but=
-for
cause and causal tendency in this classic torts case. Suppose a real estate
company misrepresents that a certain house belonged to Abraham Lincoln, and=
 a
buyer purchases the house because of this. Suppose the buyer can show that,
absent the lie, she would not have bought the house and would instead have
bought a house in another part of town. Subsequently, a flood destroys her
house and others in the neighborhood, while leaving the &quot;other part of
town&quot; unscathed. The loss to her would not have occurred but for the
fraud. And yet, so long as the neighborhood was not more prone to flooding,=
</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn101' href=3D"#_ftn101" name=3D"_ftnref101" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>lie</i> in no way increased the
chances of the actual &nbsp;[*184]&nbsp; damage to her house. Transaction
causation is present; loss causation is not. (To be sure, if the buyer paid=
 a
premium for <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Lincoln's</i> house, th=
at
premium&#8212;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, the amount =
above
the fair market value of the house in light of its true, <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>non</i>-presidential provenance&#8212;represents a separate harm wh=
ose
causal&nbsp;[**59]&nbsp; linkage to the lie is evident.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn102' href=3D"#_ftn102" name=3D"_ftnref102" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span>=
