<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63G0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Some authors have treated loss
causation as if it were part of&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp; proximate cause, whereas
transaction causation is treated as part of cause-in-fact. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See</i> Jill E. Fisch, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Caus=
e for
Concern: Causation and Federal Securities Fraud</i>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:4WBS-KBS0-02BM-Y1P2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>94 Iowa L. Re=
v.
811, 816-17 (2009)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Subsequent courts have
analogized loss causation to proximate or legal cause, while they analogize
transaction causation to 'but-for' or factual cause.&quot;); <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 817 n.23 (citing examples).=
</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn103' href=3D"#_ftn103" name=3D"_ftnref103" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
truth, proximate cause is separate, and transaction causation and loss
causation alike, to the extent that they are required in a given case, are =
each
subject to the further requirement of proximity. Lack of proximity between =
the
wrongful activity and the transaction will preclude liability, just as
liability will not attach if the causal tendency&#8212;the thing that incre=
ases
the chances of the actual occurrence of the harm&#8212;is too remote. In su=
m,
proximity applies to both transaction and loss causation, and for both elem=
ents
the additional element of proximity must be present before liability may be
imposed. It is only because some of the considerations &nbsp;[*185]&nbsp;
relevant to showing proximate cause (nature of the risk and existence of
intervening causes) typically pertain to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>loss</i>
that courts frequently, albeit unnecessarily, analyze loss causation under =
the
rubric&nbsp;[**62]&nbsp; of proximate cause.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn104' href=3D"#_ftn104" name=3D"_ftnref104" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Some
writers have also suggested that while the requirement of transaction causa=
tion
(but-for cause) may occasionally be waived, as in cases of multiple or
