452, 454 (2d Cir. 2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63H0040000400_2"></a>The occasional
&quot;deviation&quot; case that does not require causal tendency can be
explained in similar fashion: a carrier is held liable for the destruction =
of
goods in transit, having shipped them by a route other than that
&quot;specified in the contract or reasonably within the contemplation of t=
he
parties,&quot; even though the destructive event was not made likelier by t=
he
choice of route. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63H0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YTG-XJP0-00KR-F28S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Green-Wheeler
Shoe Co. v. Chicago, R.I. &amp; P.R. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YTG-XJP0-00KR-F28S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 130 Iowa 12=
3,
106 N.W. 498 (1906)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (applying this approach to a
shipping delay).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGN0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The relaxed requirements in suits
brought under the Federal Employers' Liability Act bear a family resemblanc=
e to
these cases, and indeed have been criticized on this very ground, as a
departure from the&nbsp;[**65]&nbsp; common-law requirement of causal link.=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63H0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H5V0-003B-S3J4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gallick v.
Baltimore &amp; Ohio R.R. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H5V0-003B-S3J4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 372 U.S. 10=
8,
126-27, 83 S. Ct. 659, 9 L. Ed. 2d 618 (1963)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(Stewart &amp; Goldberg, JJ., dissenting); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGN0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5358-GCB1-F04K-F4N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CSX Transp., =
Inc.
v. McBride</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5358-GCB1-F04K-F4N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 131 S. Ct.
2630, 2645, 180 L. Ed. 2d 637 (2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Roberts, C.J.,
dissenting) (&quot;The test the Court would substitute&#8212;whether neglig=
ence
&nbsp;[*186]&nbsp; played any part, even the slightest, in producing the
injury&#8212;is no limit at all. It is simply 'but for' causation.&quot;).<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Where
does all of this leave us in the present case? <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGN0050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc20_0"></a>In
the securities fraud context in general, an investor may buy shares of a
certain stock because her broker falsified&#8212;or neglected to
mention&#8212;some detail but then suffer a loss due to a nationwide recess=
ion.
Loss causation is lacking unless the fraudulent statement that induced her =
to
invest can also be shown to have made her investment, in fact, more dispose=
d to
suffer the alleged harm&#8212;a catastrophic market collapse&#8212;than
honestly described alternative investments. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGN0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powers v. Bri=
tish
Vita, P.L.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 57 F.3d 176,
189 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. Plaintiffs' Loss Pleadings</s=
pan></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
Plaintiffs clearly allege transaction causation, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>i.e.</i>, that they would not have invested in the three CDOs <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>but for</i> Defendants' misrepresentat=
ions.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn105' href=3D"#_ftn105" name=3D"_ftnref105" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
They also allege that the CDOs were all in some way <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>designed</i> to fail and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>di=
d</i>
fail. Specifically, Plaintiffs assert that contrary to the picture painted =
in
the offering documents of purportedly independent&nbsp;[**66]&nbsp; collate=
ral
managers selecting high-quality assets for the benefit of long investors, t=
oxic
assets were purposefully chosen for each CDO&#8212;in the case of the
constellation CDOs, in order to advance Magnetar's long-short strategy; in =
the
case of Longshore, in order to offload these assets from Wachovia's own boo=
ks.
Defendants contend, however, that the subsequent market crash was of such
dramatic proportions that Plaintiffs' losses would have occurred at the same
time and to the same extent regardless of the alleged fraud.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW50040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>If Defendants are right, and the
alleged fraud in no way increased the chance of &nbsp;[*187]&nbsp; Plaintif=
fs'
ultimate losses, then loss causation is lacking. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23_0"></a>Fraud claims under New York law bein=
g an
area in which loss causation is required, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>see
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:580P-2691-F04J-7200-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Greentech
Research LLC v. Wissman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:580P-2691-F04J-7200-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 104 A.D.3d =
540,
961 N.Y.S.2d 406, 407 (1st Dep't 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fa=
mily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, Plaintiffs must
ultimately prove it; that is, they bear the burden of convincing the finder=
 of
facts that this element was present. But the question at this preliminary s=
tage
is whether the pleadings suffice to withstand Defendants' Rule 12(b)(6) cha=
llenge.
We conclude that they do.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGR0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGR0030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc24_0"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Assuming
both that loss causation must be pleaded in fraud actions brought under sta=
te
common law, as in federal securities fraud actions, and that the two pleadi=
ng
requirements are similar, we note that Plaintiffs' burden is not a heavy on=
e. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW50050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G05-93F0-004B-Y01S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dura</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G05-93F0-004B-Y01S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 544 U.S. at=
 347</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The complaint must simply give Defendants &quot;some indication&quot; of the
actual loss suffered and of a plausible causal link between that loss and t=
he
alleged misrepresentations. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>=
; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5FS3-V9K1-F04K-J03V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fin. Guar.</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5FS3-V9K1-F04K-J03V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 783 F.3d at=
 404</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGR0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGS0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGS0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>What sort of pleading is suffici=
ent
will depend on the factual circumstances of the case. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lentell</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 396 F.3d at=
 174</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(&quot;Loss causation is a fact-based inquiry and the degree of
difficulty&nbsp;[**69]&nbsp; in pleading will be affected by circumstances.=
 . .
.&quot;). In the federal securities fraud context, we have held that &quot;=
when
the plaintiff's loss coincides with a marketwide phenomenon causing compara=
ble
losses to other investors,&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id=
.</i>
(internal quotation marks omitted), the plaintiff may be required to plead
facts from which it would be reasonable to infer that the risks which
materialized in her loss were risks concealed by the fraud rather than risks
evident on the face of the investment disclosures. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>see </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGS0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 172-78</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
At the same time, we have observed that where the question, at bottom, is o=
ne
of intervening events&#8212;a consideration properly analyzed under <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>proximate cause</i>&#8212;&quot;the ch=
ain of
causation is a matter of proof at trial and not to be decided on a Rule
12(b)(6) motion to dismiss.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422D6NGS0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4F98-0CY0-0038-X3SH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 174</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citation, ellipsis, and internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
think it possible to distinguish here three broad types of fraud complaints.
Our list is not meant to exhaust the possibilities but only to illustrate
considerations that a court may deem relevant at different stages of the
litigation. To adapt the prior real estate example, assume three houses are
destroyed in an earthquake. The first is the one already
encountered&#8212;the&nbsp;[**70]&nbsp; house that Lincoln was falsely said=
 to
have owned. This situation is most clearly akin to the tree and speeding
trolley car in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Berry</i>. The causal
connection, if any exists, is not readily apparent. Hence, in assessing the
facial plausibility of the claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
court may be unwilling to permit the complainant to proceed without additio=
nal
facts from which it would be reasonable to infer that the defendant's wrong=
ful
activity in some way increased the likelihood of the eventual harm.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn106' href=3D"#_ftn106" name=3D"_ftnref106" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
second and third types of fraud involve, in contrast,
misrepresentations&nbsp;[**71]&nbsp; as to the solidity of the house and th=
us
differ &nbsp;[*188]&nbsp; in kind from the first in a way that bears direct=
ly
on loss causation in the present case. In the second type of fraud complain=
t,
the misrepresentation is that the house was well built. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PS0030000400"></a>In the third&#8212;a further
variation of the second&#8212;the buyer is told that the house was, in fact,
&quot;earthquake proof.&quot; It may turn out that shoddy and well-built ho=
uses
alike were destroyed in the earthquake. It may even be that the earthquake =
was
so vast as to destroy houses that were &quot;earthquake proof,&quot; as
&quot;earthquake proof&quot; is normally defined. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Cf. </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PS0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7FY0-003B-544K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bastian</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7FY0-003B-544K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 892 F.2d at
685-86</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (describing hypothetical loss on investment represented=
 by
broker to be &quot;risk-free&quot;). In both such situations, however, it is
enough at the pleading stage for the plaintiff to allege the particular
misrepresentation and the destruction of the house in an earthquake. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf.</i> Fisch, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>supra</i>, at 852 n.223 (&quot;Similarly, it is plausible that
companies involved in fraud are especially susceptible to ruinous harm upon=
 the
occurrence of adverse economic events. Indeed, many of the companies that
collapsed due to the bursting of the dot-com bubble&#8212;as opposed to
weathering it&#8212;were those engaged in financial accounting manipulations
and similar practices.&quot;).&nbsp;[**72]&nbsp; It then falls to the defen=
dant
to proffer facts indicating that a well-built house, or even an earthquake-=
proof
one, would have been destroyed in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>t=
his</i>
earthquake.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PS0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>What we are here describing is t=
he
burden of pleading, which has clearly been met by a plaintiff's allegation =
of a
misrepresentation that goes to how well the house was built, as well as the
burden of introducing some counterevidence (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>onus
procedendi</i>), which has shifted to the defendant. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Cf. </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3W07-G870-0038-X3VV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Liriano v. Ho=
bart
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3W07-G870-0038-X3VV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 170 F.3d 26=
4,
272 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;This shifting of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>onus procedendi</i> has long been
established in New York.&quot;). The essential point for present purposes is
that where a potential causal link is evident, it is not necessary for the
plaintiff to plead loss causation in detail to render her fraud claim plaus=
ible
at the motion-to-dismiss stage.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
instant suit lies somewhere between these second and third cases in which t=
he
sufficiency of the loss causation pleadings follows from the nature of the
fraud alleged and the harm suffered. In that way, this case fundamentally
differs from the speeding trolley car and the Lincoln house cases, and the
issue of causal link is consequently distinctly easier. Moreover, as the
difference between the second and third is a matter of&nbsp;[**73]&nbsp;
degree, or of the relative strength of causal tendency inferable from the t=
ype
of fraud and surrounding circumstances, that difference will principally be
relevant at later phases of the litigation, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>e.g.</i>,
on a motion for summary judgment, in the court's instruction on causation to
the jury, or in a post-trial challenge to the sufficiency of the evidence.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
while Defendants by no means represented the CDOs to be free of risk, they
represented the CDOs to be more than simply &quot;well built,&quot; and far
from what Plaintiffs claim the CDOs actually were: designed to fail. Indeed,
according to the complaint, Defendants hid from investors (1) that Magnetar=
 was
actively undermining the constellation CDOs by selecting marginal collatera=
l to
capitalize on eventual defaults and (2) that Wachovia was dumping into
Longshore worsening assets at their original cost to recoup the losses and =
pass
the risk to investors. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422SF8PT0020000400"></a>Wh=
ether
Plaintiffs can prove these allegations&#8212;and whether defendants in turn=
 can
proffer evidence that the CDOs would have collapsed regardless, due to the
larger crash in the MBS market&#8212;are evidentiary matters for later phas=
es
of this lawsuit. It is sufficient under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
