<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The present case combines a comp=
lex
commercial reality with a long, multi-prong complaint. In such situations,
pleading defects may not only be latent, and easily missed or misperceived
without full briefing and judicial resolution; they may also be borderline,=
 and
hence subject to reasonable dispute. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0030=
000400"></a>As
discussed in Part I, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>, dis=
missal
was partly based on the district court's determination that Plaintiffs' fra=
ud
allegations raised neither plausible inferences of material misrepresentati=
ons
nor strong inferences of scienter. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422N1RW60050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5849-2RY1-F04F-0231-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2013 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 49665, [WL] at *10-15</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0030000400_2"></a>These determinations entail
judgment calls on which reasonable minds can differ in a not insignificant
number of cases. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Iqbal</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 556 U.S. at=
 679</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc31_0"></a>&quot;Determining whether a complaint
states a plausible claim for relief will . . . be a context-specific task t=
hat
requires the reviewing&nbsp;[**81]&nbsp; court to draw on its judicial
experience and common sense.&quot;). The district court's rejection of
Plaintiffs' position that the strength-of-inference requirements had been m=
et
by the facts set forth in the original complaint was, without more,
insufficient reason to bar Plaintiffs from repleading. Indeed, that our opi=
nion
today partially vindicates Plaintiffs' position is, we think, some measure =
of
the potential for reasonable disagreement here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Yet even were we not persuaded
that&#8212;at least as to some elements&#8212;the district court erred in i=
ts
dismissal under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
its denial of leave to amend would still, under the present circumstances,
necessitate our remanding the case with instructions to grant the requested
leave. As to SAI, there may remain hurdles in pleading fraud that Plaintiffs
cannot clear. By not giving Plaintiffs a chance to replead, however, the
district court not only &quot;violated the liberal spirit of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,&quot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5GPPR422HM63N0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82XR-RSN1-652R-01H3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Williams</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82XR-RSN1-652R-01H3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 659 F.3d at=
 214</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
it also skirted the key issue of futility. At this stage, the district court
has not identified any issue as to which (i) the complaint is deficient and
(ii) amendment would be futile. Neither, on the record before us, do we
identify any such issue.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we instruct the&nbsp;[**82]&nbsp; district court to grant Plaintiffs leave =
to
amend their complaint in light of the remaining pleading defects as to SAI
identified by our opinion today. It is too early for us to say whether
amendment will cure these defects. That question should be addressed to the
district court in the first instance. That court denied Plaintiffs the
opportunity to demonstrate that their claims deserve to be decided on the
merits, and Plaintiffs should be given that opportunity now.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn108' href=3D"#_ftn108" name=3D"_ftnref108" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal3 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REVERSE<=
/b> so
much of the district court's &nbsp;[*192]&nbsp; judgment as concerns the fr=
aud
claim against Wachovia and Harding; we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:nor=
mal'>VACATE</b>
the judgment as to the remainder of Plaintiffs' claims; and we <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REMAND</b> the case for further proce=
edings
consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

