outside the complaint are generally off-limits on a motion to dismiss, but =
it
considered these sources nonetheless, on the theory that each was integral =
to
the complaint.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn112' href=3D"#_ftn112"
name=3D"_ftnref112" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292HM6V40030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GMD-JV31-F04F-035J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2015 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 103612, [WL] at *8-9</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S10030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We cannot agree. It is true, as
defendants emphasize, that plaintiffs &quot;expressly allege[d] that [a]
substantial portion of the facts described [in the complaint] was learned in
the [New York action],&quot; Bunge Defs.' Br. at 32 (internal quotation mar=
ks
omitted) (second alteration in original); true also, as the District Court
stressed, that plaintiffs &quot;filled the Complaint with allegations relyi=
ng
on and referencing the court filings and 'sworn testimony' from [the New Yo=
rk
action]&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; in order to detail . . . how the scheme worked,&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S10020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GMD-JV31-F04F-035J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Goel</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GMD-JV31-F04F-035J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2015 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 103612, 2015 WL 5037002, at *9</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. But neither
observation supports the District Court's consideration of Goel's deposition
testimony and affidavit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for the first, we fail to apprehend its significance. That Goel learned abo=
ut
the &nbsp;[*560]&nbsp; particulars of defendants' alleged fraud during the =
New
York lawsuit simply hasn't a thing to do with whether his complaint relies
heavily on the effect of deposition testimony he gave in connection with th=
at
case.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for the second, though plaintiffs' complaint indeed refers to &quot;sworn
testimony&quot; from the New York action, it does not refer to <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Goel's</i> sworn testimony. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S10050000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See, e.g.</i>, J.A. 32 &para; 73 (referring to &quot;the testimony =
of
Bunge Ltd. officers&quot;); J.A. 36 &para; 93 (referring to &quot;the testi=
mony
of Bunge representatives&quot;); J.A. 36 &para; 96 (referring to &quot;sworn
Bunge Ltd. testimony&quot;); J.A. 37 &para; 97 (referring to &quot;[d]ocume=
nts
produced by Bunge&quot;). Much less does it so heavily rely on the terms and
effect of Goel's testimony that the deposition transcript may fairly be dee=
med
integral to the complaint.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn113'
href=3D"#_ftn113" name=3D"_ftnref113" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclsc=
c5_3"></a>A
complaint that alleges facts related to or gathered during a separate
litigation does not open the door to consideration, on a motion to dismiss,=
 of
any and all documents&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; filed in connection with that
litigation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S10040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KJ1-RYM0-0038-X21W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Global Network
Commc'ns</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KJ1-RYM0-0038-X21W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 458 F.3d at=
 156</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(complaint's reference to plaintiff's guilty pleas in separate proceedings =
did
not permit consideration of &quot;the content of his testimony proffered in
exchange for the pleas, as the nexus between the two [was] too attenuated to
render that testimony integral to the complaint&quot;). A contrary rule wou=
ld
permit the improper transformation of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
inquiry into a summary-judgment proceeding&#8212;one featuring a bespoke
factual record, tailor-made to suit the needs of defendants.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S20020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Of course, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_3"></a>the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure
contemplate that when a district court is presented with materials outside =
the
pleadings at the motion-to-dismiss stage, circumstances sometimes favor the=
ir
consideration. If the court wishes to take account of such materials, howev=
er,
the Rules oblige it to &quot;treat[ ] [the motion] as one for summary judgm=
ent
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F165-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 56</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
and give all parties &quot;a reasonable opportunity to present all the mate=
rial
that is pertinent to the motion.&quot;&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(d)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i><=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S20010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:456H-9WR0-0038-X4H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Chambers</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:456H-9WR0-0038-X4H7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 282 F.3d at=
 154</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S20040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In this case, the District Court
&quot;considered converting [defendants'] motion[s],&quot; but ultimately
decided against it. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5JV6R292SF8S20030000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GMD-JV31-F04F-035J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Goel</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GMD-JV31-F04F-035J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2015 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 103612, 2015 WL 5037002, at *8 n.5</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Because neither
Goel's deposition testimony nor his affidavit can properly be deemed integr=
al
to the complaint, that decision was in error. The appropriate occasion to
consider these documents, which may be relevant to the timeliness of the RI=
CO
claims, is on summary judgment, not a motion to dismiss. Accordingly, the
judgment must be vacated and the cause remanded.</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn114' href=3D"#_ftn114" name=3D"_ftnref114" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal4 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, we conclude that the District Court erred by relying on materials outs=
ide
the pleadings in deciding defendants' motions to dismiss. We thus <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>VACATE</b> the judgments of August 7,=
 2015
and August 27, 2015 and <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REMAND</b>=
 for
such further proceedings as may be appropriate in the circumstances.</span>=
</p>

