<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_6"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF742D6MYB004=
0000400"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>New
York's no-fault &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp;insurance law was passed &quot;to create a
simple, efficient system that would provide prompt compensation to accident
victims without regard to fault, and in that way reduce costs for both cour=
ts
and insureds.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF742D6MYB0030000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CN6-8PR0-0038-X3BJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>State Farm Mu=
t.
Auto. Ins. Co. v. Mallela</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CN6-8PR0-0038-X3BJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 372 F.3d 50=
0,
502 (2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Under the law, automobile
insurance providers are required to include in their policies coverage for
injuries arising from car accidents, irrespective of who is to blame for the
accident. The no-fault &nbsp;[*366]&nbsp; scheme thus &quot;supplant[s] the
state's common law tort remedies for most injuries associated with automobi=
le
accidents.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. The law re=
quires
car insurance providers to reimburse injured persons for &quot;basic econom=
ic
loss,&quot; including medical expenses, and it sets forth a schedule of
permissible charges for specific services. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>Id</i>.
(citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6JXH-MP93-S445-X000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Ins. Law
&sect;&sect; 5102</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11P1-6RDJ-84HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>5108</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
An injured person who seeks medical treatment may assign her right to no-fa=
ult
benefits to her medical provider, and such assignment is typical.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>According
to the well-pleaded allegations in the Complaint,</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn124' href=3D"#_ftn124" name=3D"_ftnref124" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
defendants were involved in a massive conspiracy to defraud Allstate for
benefits under the no-fault law. The conspiracy consisted of several discre=
te
&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;clusters of actors linked together by Lyons. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. &para;&para; 93-137, 505-516. =
The
central entity within each cluster was a professional corporation
(&quot;PC&quot;) that purported to provide health care services for individ=
uals
injured in car accidents. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>.
&para;&para; 37-65. All of the PCs involved were owned on paper by licensed
medical doctors (&quot;paper owners&quot;), as required by New York law. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. &para;&para; 6-9, 37-65. Howev=
er,
the PCs were in fact controlled by other individuals or entities that were =
not
doctors (&quot;actual owners&quot;). <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Id</i>.
&para;&para; 394-504.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Lyons
served as a radiologist for each of the PCs and purported to analyze Magnet=
ic
Resonance Imaging (&quot;MRIs&quot;) they performed for their injured patie=
nts.
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. &para;&para; 505-16. Howeve=
r, the
reports he produced were fabricated. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>Id</i>.
&para;&para; 93-137. Some were based on MRIs of such poor quality that they
could not serve any legitimate diagnostic purpose, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>id</i>. &para; 98; some were falsely duplicated for multiple patien=
ts, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id</i>. &para; 95; some identified
conditions that did not appear on the corresponding MRIs, <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>id</i>. &para; 96; some otherwise diagnosed conditions that did not
exist, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id</i>. &para; 97; and some
ignored conditions &nbsp;[**6]&nbsp;that were apparent from the MRIs, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>id</i>. &para; 135.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
PCs provided these worthless MRIs and fake MRI reports, as well as other
medically unnecessary services purportedly recommended or justified by the =
fake
MRI reports, to individuals eligible for no-fault benefits. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. &para;&para; 93-137, 126, 137.=
 After
receiving assignment of their patients' no-fault benefits, the PCs then bil=
led
Allstate by mail for these medically unnecessary services under the no-fault
law. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. &para;&para; 517-524. =
In
such billings, the PCs misrepresented that they were organized in accordance
with New York law and that the medical treatment for which they sought paym=
ent
was medically necessary and compensable under the no-fault law. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. Allstate remitted payment as
demanded to the PCs in sums totaling more than $4 million. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Id</i>. &para; 16.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
