lear
Supreme Court precedent, this poses no problem under the distinctness rule.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Allegations of the Pattern of Racketee=
ring
Activity</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_4"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I3R5F36TJGW000G4=
J8T0006M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T40020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>RICO defines &quot;racketeering
activity&quot; as &quot;any act or threat&quot; involving a number of crimes
and offenses, including murder, kidnapping, gambling, and mail fraud. </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. A &quot;pattern&quot; &nbsp;[**14]&nbsp;of racketeering
activity requires, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>inter alia</i>, =
at
least two acts of racketeering activity. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(5=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
As the Supreme Court has explained, in order to properly plead a pattern of
racketeering activity, a complaint must set forth factual allegations from
which two things may be reasonably inferred: that the racketeering acts that
purport to make up the pattern (the &quot;predicate acts&quot;) are related=
 to
each other and that those acts &quot;amount to or pose a threat of continued
criminal activity.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T4001000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J. Inc. v. =
Nw.
Bell Tel. Co</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 492 U.S. 2=
29,
239, 109 S. Ct. 2893, 106 L. Ed. 2d 195 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Complaint alleges that defendants' pattern of racketeering activity consist=
ed
of repeated acts of mail fraud. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See=
</i>
Compl. &para;&para; 517-24; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see als=
o</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (mail fraud statute). Specifically, Allstate alleges th=
at
defendants committed mail fraud by submitting numerous claims for payment of
no-fault benefits in which they fraudulently misrepresented (1) that the PCs
were eligible for reimbursement, despite the fact that they were fraudulent=
ly
incorporated and therefore ineligible for reimbursement under the no-fault =
law;
and (2) that the services for which the PCs requested reimbursement were
medically necessary.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T40040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>While defendants appear to
&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;acknowledge that the alleged predicate acts satisfy the
relatedness requirement, they suggest that those acts fail either to amount=
 to
or to pose a threat of continued criminal activity. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_4"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR00=
10000400"></a>In
order to plead predicate acts that amount to continued criminal activity (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e</i>., in order to plead
&quot;closed-ended continuity&quot;), Allstate must plead a &quot;series of
related predicates extending over a substantial period of time.&quot; </spa=
n><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T40030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 492 U.S. at
242</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>. No single rule exists for how to plead predicate acts =
that
pose a threat of continued criminal activity (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>i.e</i>., &quot;open-ended continuity&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR0010000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id</i>. Rather, open-ended continuity &quot;depends on the specific
facts of each case.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. A=
t a
minimum, however, it is clear that a &quot;threat of continuity is sufficie=
ntly
established where the predicates can be attributed to a defendant operating=
 as
part of a long-term association that exists for criminal purposes&quot; or
where &quot;the predicates are a regular way of . . . conducting or
participating in an ongoing and legitimate RICO enterprise.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T40050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 242-43</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Putting
aside the question of closed-ended continuity for the purposes of this moti=
on,
there is no question that Allstate's Complaint properly alleges open-ended
continuity. In &nbsp;[**16]&nbsp;light of the Complaint's allegations of
fraudulent incorporation and &nbsp;[*370]&nbsp; of the very numerous and
regular inflated billings, it is reasonable to infer that the PCs operated =
by
defendants existed for criminal purposes. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR0030000400"></a>And for the same reasons, ev=
en if
the PCs were legitimate businesses, it would be reasonable to infer that
defendants' acts of mail fraud constituted a regular way of conducting the
affairs of those businesses. Defendants' contention that their acts of fraud
were &quot;isolated, terminable defects&quot; fails to take seriously <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_3"></a>the court's obligation at this stage=
 to
assume the truth of the well-pleaded factual allegations in the Complaint a=
nd
to draw all reasonable inferences in favor of Allstate. <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FPB0-001T-D2T1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bolt Elec., I=
nc.
v. City of New York</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-FPB0-001T-D2T1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 53 F.3d 465,
469 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Atlantic defendants argue that even if I find open-ended continuity with
respect to all other defendants in this case, Atlantic Radiology and McCart=
hy
should be viewed differently. Because Atlantic Radiology is now defunct, and
because McCarthy is not a paper owner of any existing PC, defendants argue =
that
they do not pose a threat of continued criminal activity.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This argument is without merit. =
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYD0020000400"></a>First, Atlantic Radiology
&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp;is not a charged RICO defendant and thus the Complaint ne=
ed
not establish the relevant RICO elements with respect to it. With respect to
McCarthy, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_3"></a>a RICO plaintiff relyin=
g on
open-ended continuity is not required to allege a currently-existing threat
that the pattern of racketeering activity will continue. It is sufficient t=
hat
a threat of continuity inhered in the alleged racketeering activity, even if
that activity lasted only a brief time and is indisputably over. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 492 U.S. at
241</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (holding that open-ended continuity refers to &quot;past
conduct that by its nature projects into the future with a threat of
repetition&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYD0020000400_2"></a>&quo=
t;Subsequent
events are irrelevant to the continuity determination . . . because 'in the
context of an open-ended period of racketeering activity, the threat of
continuity must be viewed at the time the racketeering activity
occurred.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYD0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:54X3-KXY1-F04K-P0B5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heinrich v.
Waiting Angels Adoption Servs</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:54X3-KXY1-F04K-P0B5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., No. 09-247=
0,
668 F.3d 393, 2012 U.S. App. LEXIS 2390, 2012 WL 371947, at *12 (6th Cir. F=
eb.
7, 2012)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYD00300=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CD40-008H-V0FS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Busacca</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CD40-008H-V0FS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 936 F.2d 23=
2,
238 (6th Cir. 1991))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>accord </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYD0050000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4GXW-1KV0-TVW3-P1XK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
