3,
827 N.E.2d 758, 794 N.Y.S.2d 700 (2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_3"></a>the New York Court of Appeals upheld=
 an
insurance regulation that renders ineligible to be reimbursed under the
no-fault law any health care provider that &quot;fails to meet any applicab=
le
New York State &nbsp;[**20]&nbsp;or local licensing requirement.&quot; </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5WYW-NFM1-JT99-23X4-00009-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Comp. Co=
des
R. &amp; Regs. tit. 11 &sect; 65-3.16(a)(12)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
New York licensing requirements in turn &quot;prohibit nonphysicians from
owning or controlling medical service corporations.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T50010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4FTR-4BW0-0039-437B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mallela</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4FTR-4BW0-0039-437B-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 4 N.Y.3d at=
 321</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T50040000400"></a>Under <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Mallela</i> then, a health care provider that is fraudulently licen=
sed
&#8212; because it is in fact owned or controlled by non-physicians &#8212;
makes a misrepresentation when it claims eligibility for reimbursement. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T50040000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id</i>. By extension, when individuals engage in a scheme by which =
they
seek reimbursement for services provided by a fraudulently incorporated hea=
lth
care provider, and that scheme is carried out through the mail, those
individuals commit mail fraud. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See =
</i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3XC2-6N60-0038-X1XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Walker</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3XC2-6N60-0038-X1XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 191 F.3d 32=
6,
334 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (identifying <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_2"></a>the elements of mail fraud as &quot;=
(1) a
scheme to defraud victims of (2) money or property, through the (3) use of =
the
mails&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>There
is no dispute that the defendants represented that the PCs were eligible to
receive reimbursement for medical services they purported to provide or that
they made these representations through the mail. The debate here centers
solely on whether Allstate's Complaint sufficiently alleges that the PCs we=
re
&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;fraudulently incorporated &#8212; that is, whether Allsta=
te
has sufficiently alleged that the PCs were owned or controlled by
non-physicians. Defendants contend that Allstate's allegations demonstrate =
that
the PCs simply &quot;delegate[d] . . . responsibility in non-medical
administrative matters&quot; to the actual owners in this case, but that the
paper owners ultimately maintained ownership and control of the PCs. Defs.'
Mem. Supp. Mot. Dismiss 11, ECF No. 83.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Again,
defendants fail to appreciate the standard of review on a motion to dismiss.
Construing the factual allegations of the Complaint as true and drawing all
inferences in favor of the plaintiffs, it is easy to find that Allstate has
adequately pled fraudulent incorporation here. With regard to the Atlantic
Imaging cluster, the Complaint alleges that the paper owner (Stern) had no
knowledge of Lyons's compensation, did not know whether Lyons was an employ=
ee
or an independent contractor, was unable to identify Lyons, had met Lyons o=
nly
once, could not identify the PC's landlord, did not know the type of equipm=
ent
the PC leased, admitted that he was an absentee owner, did not hire the PC's
management company and was instead approached by the &nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;mana=
gement
company (which was operated by the alleged actual owners Korenblit, Atbaysh=
an,
and Zharov), allowed his name to be signed by the management company, did n=
ot
invest any money in the PC, could not identify the attorneys or law firms
retained &nbsp;[*372]&nbsp; to pursue collections on behalf of the PC, could
not meaningfully discuss how legal settlements or arbitration awards were
allocated, and could not identify the PC's accountants. Compl. &para;&para;
450-62. These factual allegations are more than sufficient to raise the
reasonable inference that Atlantic Imagining was actually owned and control=
led
not by Stern, who is a physician, but by non-physicians Korenblit, Atbaysha=
n,
and Zharov.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Furthermore,
as discussed above, the factual allegations in the Complaint permit the
reasonable inference that Atlantic Radiology, in effect, simply renamed its=
elf
Atlantic Imaging. Thus in light of the factual allegations just discussed w=
ith
respect to Atlantic Imaging, the Complaint adequately alleges that Atlantic
Radiology was fraudulently incorporated.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn127'
href=3D"#_ftn127" name=3D"_ftnref127" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
regard to the Right Aid cluster, the Complaint alleges that the paper owner
Kalika never met Lyons and that she did not know his first name. Compl.
&para;&para; 424-25. Although this is a closer case, I conclude that these
facts, considered against the full backdrop of Right Aid's scheme as allege=
d in
the Complaint, are sufficient to reasonably raise the inference that Kalika
lacked true ownership &nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;or control over Right Aid and inste=
ad
ceded control to non-physician third parties.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83D0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In addition, defendants find fau=
lt
with Allstate's mail fraud allegations premised upon defendants'
misrepresentations that the medical services for which they sought
reimbursement were medically necessary and compensable under the no-fault l=
aw
(&quot;bad medicine&quot; fraud). According to defendants, Allstate fails to
plead bad medicine fraud with the requisite level of particularity under </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b) of =
the
Federal Rules of Civil Procedure</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Specifically, they contend (1)
that Allstate failed to satisfy its burden of alleging that each defendant =
had
the required scienter for fraud, and (2) that Allstate failed to specify
&quot;the contents of the communications, who was involved, where and when =
they
took place, and . . . why they were fraudulent.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83D0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9T70-003B-P25N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mills v. Polar
Molecular Corp</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9T70-003B-P25N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 12 F.3d 11=
70,
1176 (2d Cir. 1993)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83D0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants' arguments are
unpersuasive. Considering their second contention first, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_2"></a>&quot;[i]n the RICO context, </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
calls for the complaint to specify the statements it claims were false or
misleading, give particulars as to the respect in which plaintiffs contend =
the
statements were fraudulent, state &nbsp;[**25]&nbsp;when and where the
statements were made, and identify those responsible for the statements.&qu=
ot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83D0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moore v.
PaineWebber, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 189 F.3d 1=
65,
173 (2d Cir. 1999)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83D0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-47K0-008H-V4DN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McLaughlin v.
Anderson</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-47K0-008H-V4DN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 962 F.2d 18=
7,
191 (2d Cir. 1992))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
Allstate has conformed to these requirements, explaining in detail the cont=
ours
of the fraudulent scheme it alleges. It provides several representative
examples of the thousands of claims at issue in this case. In each example,
Allstate sets forth facts specifying the ways in which the MRI
&nbsp;[*373]&nbsp; report purportedly analyzed in connection with the claim=
 for
payment was grossly misrepresentative. Additionally, Allstate attaches to i=
ts
Complaint a series of charts that include each of the charges submitted by =
the
defendants that it believes were fraudulent. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>See</i> Compl. Exs. 14-15, 19-20. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF7528T42M0030000400"></a>The charts detail the entity=
 that
submitted each claim, as well as the corresponding claim number, the year
Allstate paid the claim, and the amount paid by Allstate. Such information
clearly directs defendants to the specific misrepresentations Allstate is
alleging. Under the circumstances, the specificity requirement of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9(b)</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires no more regarding the &nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;who, what, where, when, ho=
w,
and why of the alleged fraud in this case. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:=
normal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF7528T42M0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>PaineWebber</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3X8F-R000-0038-X0DT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 189 F.3d at=
 173</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF7528T42M0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6M9GTCFRSV00009BK2002HR"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6M9GTCFT6N00009BK2002HS"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83F0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, there is no question
that the Complaint alleges that the defendants had the scienter required for
fraud. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc20_2"></a>To adequately allege scien=
ter
for mail fraud, one must &quot;allege facts that give rise to a strong
inference of fraudulent intent.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>Id</i>.
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF7528T42M0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-55N0-006F-M0F6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>San Leandro
Emergency Med. Grp. Profit Sharing Plan v. Philip Morris Cos</span></i></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-55N0-006F-M0F6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 75 F.3d 80=
1,
812 (2d Cir. 1996))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
This may be done either &quot;(a) by alleging facts to show that defendants=
 had
both motive and opportunity to commit fraud, or (b) by alleging facts that
constitute strong circumstantial evidence of conscious misbehavior or
recklessness.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83F0010000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KKW-NP60-0038-X2R0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner v. Fle=
et
Bank, N.A</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4KKW-NP60-0038-X2R0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 459 F.3d 2=
73,
290-91 (2d Cir. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83F0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5M50-003B-P1SM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Shields v.
Citytrust Bancorp, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5M50-003B-P1SM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 25 F.3d 11=
24,
1128 (2d Cir. 1994))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
Even putting aside circumstantial evidence that defendants consciously
misbehaved and/or were reckless, which Allstate certainly pleads, the Compl=
aint
sufficiently establishes that defendants had both the motive and opportunit=
y to
commit fraud. According to the Complaint, defendants &nbsp;[**27]&nbsp;gain=
ed
millions of dollars through their alleged fraud; surely this constitutes
motive. And the Complaint alleges that the defendants together entered into=
 a
scheme whereby they provided bogus MRIs and then requested reimbursement for
false charges stemming from those MRIs &#8212; certainly an opportunity for
fraud. The Complaint thus raises the strong inference that defendants posse=
ssed
fraudulent intent, satisfying </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>c. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Allegations of Injury and Causation</i=
></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21_2"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I3R5F36V3TF000G4=
J8T0006R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF7528T42N0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In order to bring a civil RICO
claim, a private plaintiff must demonstrate that &quot;the RICO violation at
issue was a proximate cause of the injury to the plaintiff's business or
property for which redress is sought.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83F0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Terminate Con=
trol
Corp. v. Horowitz</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-4P40-003B-P2NG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 28 F.3d 133=
5,
1344 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The Atlantic defendants argue
Lyons's fraud was the source of Allstate's injury and that they did nothing=
 to
facilitate or conceal Lyons's fraud. Therefore, these defendants conclude, =
the
Complaint fails to allege that the Atlantic defendants' conduct was a proxi=
mate
cause of any injury Allstate suffered.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Viewing
the Complaint in the light most favorable to Allstate, I must reject the
Atlantic defendants' argument. Read in that light, the Complaint
&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp;clearly alleges that the Atlantic defendants joined with
Lyons to perpetrate the fraudulent scheme that injured Allstate. Compl.
&para;&para; 505-16. Together, each cluster of defendants entered into an
agreement with Lyons to bilk money from Allstate by (1) misrepresenting that
the PCs were eligible to receive reimbursement under the no-fault law and (=
2)
requesting payment for services purportedly provided by the PCs that were n=
ot
medically necessary or compensable. The alleged RICO violations of each
defendant proximately caused Allstate's financial &nbsp;[*374]&nbsp; injury=
 and
therefore Allstate may properly bring this suit.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>d. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ripeness</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
