the
PCs were eligible for reimbursement under the no-fault law and that they so=
ught
reimbursement &nbsp;[**34]&nbsp;for medically legitimate services.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
argue that Allstate has failed to plead a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
because their alleged misrepresentations were not consumer-oriented. Rather,
they were made by one private party to another, &nbsp;[*376]&nbsp; neither
acting in a consumer role, and are therefore outside of the scope of conduct
that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
was intended to reach.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Although defendants' argument has
some traction, I conclude that the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claims survive. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc26_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0030000400"></a>A plaintiff establishes
consumer-oriented conduct by showing that &quot;the acts or practices have a
broader impact on consumers at large&quot; in that they are &quot;directed =
to
consumers&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>or</i> that they
&quot;potentially affect similarly situated consumers.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T60050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-6F40-003V-B43S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Oswego</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-6F40-003V-B43S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 85 N.Y.2d at
25, 27</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Indeed, so long as the conduct is consumer-oriented, e=
ven
a defendant's business competitor may bring a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
provided the competitor is incidentally harmed by the defendant's deceptive
conduct. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RVK-8KT0-001T-D0DW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Securitron
Magnalock Corp. v. Schnabolk</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RVK-8KT0-001T-D0DW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 65 F.3d 256,
264 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;The critical question [u=
nder
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>]
is whether the matter affects the public interest in New York, not whether =
the
suit is brought by a consumer or a competitor.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0050000400"></a>Here, the defendants' scheme=
 is
alleged to have &nbsp;[**35]&nbsp;unlawfully stripped millions of dollars f=
rom
Allstate, which has likely increased the premiums of consumers. In light of=
 the
resulting burden on the public &#8212; a broad impact on consumers at large
&#8212; I conclude that defendants' alleged misrepresentations were
sufficiently consumer-oriented to fall within the ambit of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752D6MYH0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V8B-1FD0-TXFR-J1V2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allstate Ins.=
 Co.
v. Rozenberg</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4V8B-1FD0-TXFR-J1V2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 590 F. Supp=
. 2d
384, 394-95 (E.D.N.Y. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (adopting similar logic); <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>but see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T70010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5340-42S1-652J-D0WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Allstate Ins.=
 Co.
v. Bogoraz</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5340-42S1-652J-D0WJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 10 Civ.
5286, 818 F. Supp. 2d 544, 2011 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 63721, 2011 WL 2421045, at=
 *6
(E.D.N.Y. June 10, 2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (finding upon similar facts that
plaintiff failed to demonstrate that defendants' alleged conduct was
consumer-orientated). I therefore conclude that Allstate's claims for
violations of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6HKK-NR83-SHV7-X0WS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 349</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
are legally sufficient.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>5. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Unjust Enrichment</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc27_2"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_I3R5F370CPK000G4=
J8T001FT"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T70040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To state a claim for unjust
enrichment, a plaintiff must allege &quot;(1) that the defendant benefitted;
(2) at the plaintiff's expense; and (3) that equity and good conscience req=
uire
restitution.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T70030000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K0M-VP60-0038-X1HM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Beth Israel M=
ed.
Ctr. v. Horizon Blue Cross &amp; Blue Shield of N.J</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K0M-VP60-0038-X1HM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 448 F.3d 5=
73,
586 (2d Cir. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Allstate's claims for unjust
enrichment are premised on defendants' alleged misrepresentations, which ca=
used
Allstate to remit payment to &nbsp;[**36]&nbsp;defendants for claims for wh=
ich
they were not entitled to reimbursement. Compl. &para;&para; 758-62.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83G0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants assert that I must
dismiss Allstate's unjust enrichment claims because Allstate's insurance
contracts govern its relationships with the defendants, which they believe
precludes any quasi-contract recovery for their actions. In this case, howe=
ver,
Allstate's unjust enrichment claims are &quot;predicated on conduct not cov=
ered
by the contract.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T70050000400"=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-43M0-0039-42K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sergeants
Benevolent Ass'n Annuity Fund v. Renck</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G9M-43M0-0039-42K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 19 A.D.3d 1=
07,
796 N.Y.S.2d 77, 81 (App. Div. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Rather, they stem
from defendants' misrepresentations, and they are therefore properly assert=
ed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83G0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In addition to being inapplicable
here, the rule defendants would have me adopt &#8212; that an unjust enrich=
ment
claim is never cognizable where a valid contract exists between the parties
&#8212; is too broadly stated. For example, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclsc=
c28_2"></a>where
one party to a contract accidentally pays another more than the contract
requires, the overpayer has an unjust enrichment claim to recover the exces=
s. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752SF83G0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4FFR-CFY0-0039-4117-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kirby McInern=
ey
&amp; Squire, LLP v. Hall Charne Burce &amp; Olson, S.C</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4FFR-CFY0-0039-4117-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 15 A.D.3d =
233,
790 N.Y.S.2d 84, 85 (App. Div. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. That the parties
remain bound by their contract in other respects does not bar a
&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp;claim to recoup the mistaken overpayment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Motion To Compel Arbitration</i></span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Atlantic defendants also move to compel arbitration. They argue that </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York
Insurance Law &sect; 5106(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Time=
s",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and the individual insurance
contracts governing &nbsp;[*377]&nbsp; the allegedly fraudulent billings at
issue (the &quot;Allstate insurance contracts&quot;) give them the option to
resolve the instant dispute through arbitration. I find that the scope of t=
he
arbitration clause under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 5106(b=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and the individual contracts does not reach any of the claims before me exc=
ept
those that Allstate has not yet paid. I therefore deny the motion to compel
arbitration as to all claims but those still pending before Allstate.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 5106(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the New York no-fault insurances law requires that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
