mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 5106</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
creates &quot;[f]air claims settlement&quot; procedures to achieve the
statutory goal of the no-fault law: to speedily route compensation to people
injured in car accidents. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See gener=
ally</i>
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5WYW-NFR1-JB2B-S13N-00009-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Comp. Co=
des
R. &amp; Regs. tit. 11 &sect; 65-3.2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Claim prac=
tice
principles to be followed by all insurers: (a) Have as your basic goal the
prompt and fair payment to all automobile accident victims.&quot;); </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T90020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R5X-HBW0-TX4N-G13J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hosp. for Joi=
nt
Diseases</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4R5X-HBW0-TX4N-G13J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 9 N.Y.3d at=
 317</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[*380]&nbsp; (&quot;New York's no-fault automobile insurance system is
designed to ensure prompt compensation for losses incurred by accident vict=
ims
. . . .&quot;) (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752HM6T90040000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:472B-XWR0-0039-41NP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Med. So=
c'y</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:472B-XWR0-0039-41NP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 298 A.D.2d =
255,
749 N.Y.S.2d 227, 227 (2003))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
Allowing the suit before me to proceed outside of the strictures of </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 5106</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not significantly interfere with the important goals of the no-fault l=
aw.
If successful, affirmative actions based on fraud may claw back money alrea=
dy
paid, in effect slowing down the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>fi=
nal</i>
resolution of certain claims under the no-fault &nbsp;[**47]&nbsp;law. Howe=
ver,
in these cases the underlying insurance claims will have been already
processed, and payment on those claims promptly made. Moreover, affirmative
suits for fraud are quite rare. Thus, the goal of the no-fault law is still
substantially achieved: Injured parties' claims for benefits meet quick
resolutions, the vast majority of which are conclusive.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
alternative &#8212; interpreting the 30-day rule of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> to
apply to and effectively bar affirmative actions for fraud &#8212; would
sacrifice too much in the name of efficiency. Slow-to-detect fraud would be
irremediable, a result difficult to reconcile with </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc40"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11N1-6RDJ-851C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York
Insurance Law &sect; 409</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, which clearly evinces the goal=
 of
combating insurance fraud. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11N1-6RDJ-851C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Ins. Law
&sect; 409</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (requiring insurance companies to adopt plans &quot;for=
 the
detection, investigation and prevention of fraudulent insurance
activities&quot;). In light of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-11N1-6RDJ-851C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 409</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, I
am not prepared to attribute to the legislature a single-minded purpose to
promote efficiency at any expense. My interpretation of the scope of </span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
strikes a reasonable balance between the conflicting values evinced by the
statutory scheme as a whole.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCX0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Atlantic defendants emphasize
that the arbitration &nbsp;[**48]&nbsp;clause of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (b=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
either expressly or constructively appears in the Allstate insurance contra=
cts.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCX0040000400"></a>They thus contend that the
broad policy favoring arbitration under the Federal Arbitration Act
(&quot;FAA&quot;), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6572-SW63-GXF6-82NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>9 U.S.C &sect=
; 2</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
compels me to read any ambiguities in the arbitration clause, insofar as th=
ey
exist, in favor of arbitration. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See,
e.g., </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCX0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:54P4-JGF1-F04K-F1NH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CompuCredit C=
orp.
v. Greenwood</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:54P4-JGF1-F04K-F1NH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 132 S. Ct. =
665,
669, 181 L. Ed. 2d 586 (2012)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Tim=
es",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCX0040000400_2"></a>However, as the Supreme Co=
urt
has made clear, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc41"></a>courts should
&quot;appl[y] the presumption favoring arbitration, in FAA . . . cases, only
where it reflects, and derives its legitimacy from, a judicial conclusion t=
hat
arbitration of a particular dispute is what the parties <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>intended</i> because their express agreement to arbitrate was valid=
ly
formed and . . . is legally enforceable and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>best
construed to encompass the dispute</i>.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I554PF752N1RCX0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YSN-SHB0-YB0V-90M6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Granite Rock =
Co.
v. Int'l Bhd. of Teamsters</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YSN-SHB0-YB0V-90M6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 130 S. Ct.
2847, 2859-60, 177 L. Ed. 2d 567 (2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (emphasis added).
Here, local law mandates that disputes be arbitrable at the option of the
claimant, and the parties' agreements &#8212; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>i.e</i>., the Allstate insurance contracts &#8212; merely adhere to
this mandate, parroting the words of the statute or silently adopting its
provisions. Thus the parties cannot be &nbsp;[**49]&nbsp;said to have evinc=
ed
an intention or bargained for the right to arbitrate affirmative fraud clai=
ms
through their private agreements. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>S=
ee id</i>.
Rather, the manifest intention of the parties, insofar as any of their
agreements explicitly included an arbitration clause, was simply to adopt a=
nd
comply with local law, and I find that the best construction of the </span>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (b=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
excludes from its scope affirmative suits for fraud. I thus deny defendants'
motion to compel arbitration of these claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>However,
defendants' motion to compel arbitration is materially different with respe=
ct
to claims that Allstate has not yet paid. For those claims &#8212; with res=
pect
to which Allstate seeks a declaration that it need not remit payment &#8212=
; I
find that the obligations set forth in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (a=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
squarely apply. By extension, disputes regarding &nbsp;[*381]&nbsp; first-p=
arty
benefits that arise with respect to those claims are subject to the arbitra=
tion
clause of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69YT-VDB3-RRT0-43KY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>subsection (b=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn132' href=3D"#_ftn132" name=3D"_ftnref132" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> I
therefore grant defendants' motion to compel arbitration of this limited cl=
ass
of claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the reasons set forth herein, the motions to dismiss are denied in their
entirety. The Atlantic defendants' motion to compel is granted with respect=
 to
all claims for reimbursement that Allstate has not yet paid and denied with
respect to all other claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal7 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>So
ordered.</span></p>

