 a
legally cognizable injury. In this case, it is a missed chance for innocent
enjoyment of a fraud. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_5"></a>A court of equity &quot;will not lend=
 its
power to assist or protect a fraud.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MP0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JKP0-003B-H101-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kitchen v.
Rayburn</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JKP0-003B-H101-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 86 U.S. 254,
263, 22 L. Ed. 64 (1873)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20020000400_2"></a>We therefore decline to me=
asure
loss based on the amount of other investors' money that the Plan could have
withdrawn had it maximized its potential gains in Madoff's Ponzi scheme. Cf=
. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82YT-KP71-652R-01HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Bernard=
 L.
Madoff Inv. Sec. LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82YT-KP71-652R-01HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 654 F.3d 22=
9,
235 (2d Cir. 2011)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;Use of the Last Statement
Method in this case would have the absurd effect of treating fictitious and
arbitrarily assigned paper profits as real and would give legal effect to
Madoff's machinations.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a practical matter, the Trustees are correct that had they withdrawn the ne=
arly
$31 million in fictitious profits from the Plan's BLMIS account in December
1998, they would have been able to keep it. But that is not because the
transfer would have been non-fraudulent; it is because the
law&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; values finality. By the time the BLMIS bankruptcy tru=
stee
attempted to claw back funds from net winners in 2010, any recovery from the
Plan for the benefit of victims was defeated by invocation of the statute of
limitations. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20040000400"></a>In fact, this
statute of limitations served to protect the nearly $33 million in profit t=
he
Plan withdrew from BLMIS prior to 2006 from clawback by the BLMIS bankruptcy
trustee. The transfer would likewise have been shielded from avoidance in
bankruptcy because it would have been classified as a &quot;settlement
payment&quot; made &quot;in connection with a securities contract&quot; und=
er </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S7X-D412-D6RV-H2NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 546(e=
) of
the Bankruptcy Code</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S7X-D412-D6RV-H2NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>11 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
546(e)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20030000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5DST-RCD1-F04K-J0VN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Picard</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5DST-RCD1-F04K-J0VN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 773 F.3d at
421-22</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitted). As this Court has
explained:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-botto=
m:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>These statutes of limitations
reflect that, at a certain point, the need for finality is paramount even in
light of countervailing equity considerations. Similarly, by enacting </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S7X-D412-D6RV-H2NT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 546(e)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
Congress provided that, for a very broad range of securities-related transf=
ers,
the interest in finality is sufficiently important that they cannot be avoi=
ded
by a bankruptcy trustee at all, except as actual fraudulent transfers under=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S7X-D412-D6RV-H2NW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
548(a)(1)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2N1PX20050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5DST-RCD1-F04K-J0VN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5DST-RCD1-F04K-J0VN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 423</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The funds the Trustees would like to have withdrawn in 1998 were not withdr=
awn.
No interest (equity, finality, or the&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; merits) is served by
giving real effect to a fraud because an innocent party would have gotten a=
way
with it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
therefore does not matter whether (as the complaint claims) the Plan would =
have
received greater returns by withdrawing the full $36,629,757 stated value of
its BLMIS account in December 1998 and investing the money in a prudent
alternative investment. Of course, the Plan did have a right to its net
investment, which in December 1998 was $5,725,258. But the Trustees do not
allege that had <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>this</i> money been
withdrawn and invested elsewhere, the profits earned would have exceeded the
Plan's BLMIS profits, which ultimately totaled $32,974,742 in 2005 (after w=
hich
no further investments or withdrawals were made).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Even if the Trustees had explici=
tly
alleged in their complaint that the investment of $5,725,258 in a prudent
alternative investment would have earned more than the $32,974,742 earned in
profit from BLMIS, such a claim would fail the test of plausibility. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8_5"></a>A
cause of action under <u>Donovan's</u> prudent alternative investment theory
requires pleading that the ERISA-protected plan suffered a loss as a result=
 of
certain funds being invested in an imprudent manner and that, had
the&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; funds been available for other investments, those
investments would have <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>earned more<=
/i>
than &nbsp;[*569]&nbsp; the imprudent investment <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>actually earned</i>. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG00200=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JJN0-0039-P4VM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Donovan, 754 =
F.2d
at 1056</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0050000400_2"></a>As=
 we
have previously observed, however, &quot;the profits recorded over time [by
BLMIS] on the customer statements were after-the-fact constructs that were =
. .
. rigged to reflect a steady and upward trajectory in good times and
bad[.]&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2D6NPG0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82YT-KP71-652R-01HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Bernard=
 L.
Madoff Inv. Sec. LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:82YT-KP71-652R-01HH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 654 F.3d at=
 238</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In other words, Madoff's Ponzi scheme was successful because BLMIS was post=
ing
far higher returns than any other investment, returns which the Trustees we=
re
able to realize by withdrawing nearly $33 million in pure profit from their
investment before the Ponzi scheme was revealed. Since Madoff was able to p=
ost
such high returns only because of his fraud, it would be implausible to read
the complaint here to allege that any of the Plan's non-fraudulent alternat=
ive
investments would have realized higher returns between December 1998 and
December 2005.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Indeed,
any such comparable profit would have been extremely difficult to achieve. A
generous 10 percent annual compounded return on a $5,725,258 investment in =
1998
would have yielded a profit of about $5.4 million by 2005 -- approximately
$27.5 million less than what the Plan&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; pocketed. Even an
astronomical 25 percent annual return on investment would have fallen rough=
ly
$11.4 million short.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn135' href=3D"#_ft=
n135"
name=3D"_ftnref135" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Trustees have not claimed t=
hat
any of the Plan's alternative investment options offered returns as high as=
 25
percent every year between 1998 and 2005. Nor is such a rate of return
plausible. The valid measure is a prudent alternative investment, not an
alternative Ponzi scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4680020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>True, this ruling affords no rem=
edy
on this claim notwithstanding the alleged breach of fiduciary duty in the
rendering of investment advice. But there is no cognizable investment loss.=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_5"></a>And a breach of fiduciary duty under =
ERISA
in and of itself does not &quot;constitute an injury-in-fact sufficient for
constitutional standing.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4680010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VXF-BB30-TXFX-426W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kendall</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VXF-BB30-TXFX-426W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 561 F.3d at=
 121</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, the Plan has failed to allege facts sufficient to show Article=
 III
standing.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. Performance Fees and Legal
Expenses</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Trustees claim as additional losses: (1) the $1.8 million in performance fe=
es
paid to Ivy in connection with the Plan's BLMIS investment after 1998; and =
(2)
the costs incurred responding to the unsuccessful clawback action filed by =
the
Madoff bankruptcy trustee and to the subpoenas issued by the United States
Department of Labor and the Attorney&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; General of the State=
 of
New York in their actions against Ivy, Simon, and Wohl.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn136' href=3D"#_ftn136" name=3D"_ftnref136" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4680040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MR0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc10_5"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
the common law of trusts -- which &quot;offers a starting point for analysi=
s of
ERISA unless it is inconsistent with the language of the statute, its
structure, or its purposes,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4680030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40G8-Y5K0-004B-Y039-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Harris Tr. &a=
mp;
Sav. Bank v. Salomon Smith Barney Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40G8-Y5K0-004B-Y039-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 530 U.S. 23=
8,
250, 120 S. Ct. 2180, 147 L. Ed. 2d 187 (2000)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks and alterations omitted) -- the &quot;loss occasi=
oned
by one breach of trust&quot; is not offset by &quot;a gain which has accrued
through another and &nbsp;[*570]&nbsp; distinct breach of trust.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Restatement (Second) of Trusts &sect; =
213</i>
(1959). However, where, as here, the alleged &quot;breaches of trust are not
distinct, the trustee is accountable only for the net gain or chargeable on=
ly
with the net loss resulting therefrom.&quot; <u>Id.; see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4680050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43NX-Y3S0-0038-X39S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cal. Ironwork=
ers
Field Pension Tr. v. Loomis Sayles &amp; Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43NX-Y3S0-0038-X39S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 259 F.3d 10=
36,
1047 (9th Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;[A] fiduciary is liable =
for
the total aggregate loss of all breaches of trust and may reduce liability =
for
the net loss of multiple breaches only when such multiple breaches are so
related that they do not constitute separate and distinct breaches.&quot;).=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
the performance fees and the legal and compliance costs that the Trustees s=
eek
to recoup arise from the same alleged breach of trust that imprudently left=
 the
Plan invested in BLMIS. The question becomes whether the performance fees a=
nd
the legal and compliance costs, added together, exceed&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; the
profit that the Plan derived in excess of what it would have made from a
prudent alternative investment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
last investment or withdrawal made in connection with the Plan's BLMIS acco=
unt
occurred in December 2005 (the stated value that remained, which was imagin=
ary
anyway, was erased when the Ponzi scheme was uncovered in December 2008). B=
ased
on the total investments and withdrawals (<u>i.e.</u>, through December 200=
5),
the Plan earned a profit of $32,974,742. As discussed above, the Trustees h=
ave
not alleged, nor is it plausible, that if they had been duly warned about B=
LMIS
in December 1998 and then withdrawn the $5,725,258 principal to which they =
were
entitled, the Plan could have obtained a profit anywhere near this large
through a prudent alternative investment. Even an implausible 25 percent an=
nual
return would have fallen short over $11 million.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>It
is also wholly implausible that the performance fees and legal expenses the
Trustees claim as losses could have amounted to more than a few million
dollars. Although the exact costs incurred responding to the clawback action
and subpoenas are not stated in the complaint, one can safely conclude that
these costs, when combined with the&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; $1.8 million paid in
performance fees, do not come close to matching the extraordinary profits m=
ade
by the Plan's BLMIS investment over and above what it could have made throu=
gh a
prudent alternative investment. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MR003000040=
0"></a>Indeed,
it is inconceivable that legal expenses relating to a clawback action that =
was
barred by the statute of limitations and subpoenas asking for information a=
bout
an investment would amount to many millions of dollars. Therefore, because =
the
Trustees have not plausibly alleged losses in excess of their profits, they
have not pleaded an injury in fact sufficient for Article III standing. <u>=
See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MR0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ashcroft v. I=
qbal</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4W9Y-4KS0-TXFX-1325-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 556 U.S. 66=
2,
678, 129 S. Ct. 1937, 173 L. Ed. 2d 868 (2009)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc11_5"></a>requiring a complaint to &quot;conta=
in
sufficient factual matter, accepted as true, to state a claim to relief tha=
t is
plausible on its face&quot; (internal quotation marks omitted)).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C. Increased Pension Fund Benefi=
ts</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Trustees claim that the Plan suffered an additional loss when pension fund
benefits were increased in 1999 in partial reliance on the stated performan=
ce
of the BLMIS investment. It is alleged that this increase was unwarranted
because it was predicated on fictitious stated values of investments that
eventually became worthless, and that it reduced Plan assets and
compromised&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; the Plan's ability to pay promised retirement
benefits. Since, according to the complaint, the Trustees would not have in=
creased
pension fund benefits had they known that investing with BLMIS was impruden=
t,
they hold Ivy, Simon, and Wohl responsible for the cost of the benefit incr=
ease
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70PP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1109(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*571]&nbsp;
The complaint does not allege that the Plan has been or will be unable to p=
ay
the increased benefits to its participants. Although the Plan may have less=
 of
a surplus than the Trustees expected when they increased benefits in 1999, =
no participants
are alleged to have been harmed. Nor was the Plan itself harmed; the Plan
received funds far in excess of its entitlement. Therefore, the increase in
pension benefits does not constitute a cognizable loss.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MR0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court rejected this
alleged loss for a different reason, namely that Ivy, Simon, and Wohl could=
 not
be held liable because they had no involvement in the decision to increase
benefits. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2HM6MR0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GXY-D6S1-F04F-00M0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Trs. of the
Upstate N.Y. Eng'rs Pension Fund</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GXY-D6S1-F04F-00M0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 131 F. Supp=
. 3d
at 127</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Because we conclude that the increase in pension benef=
its
does not constitute a cognizable loss, we need not decide that additional
issue.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>D. Disgorgement of Simon and Woh=
l's
$200 Million</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Trustees further seek disgorgement of the $200 million that
Simon&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; and Wohl shared when BNY Mellon acquired Ivy. The
Trustees argue that this money was the fruit of Simon and Wohl's breach of
fiduciary duty because Ivy's value as an acquisition target depended in par=
t on
its assets under management, which Simon and Wohl feared would decrease if =
they
disclosed to the Trustees their complete and honest conclusions about Madof=
f. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4690020000400"></a>However, although the compla=
int
alleges that the Trustees would have divested from BLMIS if Simon and Wohl =
had
disclosed these conclusions, it does not allege that the Trustees would have
removed the assets from Ivy's management. There is therefore no reasonable
inference that Simon and Wohl's concealment of information about Madoff
affected Ivy's acquisition price. <u>See</u> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc12_5"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70PP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1109(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> (permitting disgorgement of a fiduciary's profits only
where there is a causal connection between those profits and use of plan as=
sets
by the fiduciary); <u>see also </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4690010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bell Atl. Cor=
p.
v. Twombly</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NSN-8840-004C-002M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 550 U.S. 54=
4,
555, 127 S. Ct. 1955, 167 L. Ed. 2d 929 (2007)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> (<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_4"></a>&quot;Factual allegations must be en=
ough
to raise a right to relief above the speculative level[.]&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the Trustees allege that BNY Mellon, a non-fiduciary, knowingly participate=
d in
Ivy, Simon, and Wohl's breach of fiduciary duty &quot;[b]y virtue of its
acquiescence and its receipt of the investment&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; advisory f=
ees
paid by the Plan.&quot; App'x 90-91.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4690040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2SF8J30030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc14_4"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;The
well-settled elements of a cause of action for participation in a breach of
fiduciary duty are 1) breach by a fiduciary of a duty owed to plaintiff, 2)
defendant's knowing participation in the breach, and 3) damages.&quot; </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4690030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1FT0-008H-V491-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Diduck v.
Kaszycki &amp; Sons Contractors, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1FT0-008H-V491-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 974 F.2d 27=
0,
281-82 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <u>abrogated on other grounds =
by </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR28T4690050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48MR-S6X0-0038-X3T3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gerosa v. Sav=
asta
&amp; Co., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48MR-S6X0-0038-X3T3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 329 F.3d 317
(2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Although the complaint adequately alleges that BNY Mel=
lon
knew of the breach of fiduciary duty, it fails to plead facts demonstrating
that BNY Mellon &quot;affirmatively assist[ed], help[ed] conceal, or by vir=
tue
of failing to act when required to do so enable[d] [the breach of fiduciary
duty] to proceed.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5MJ0BJR2SF8J30020000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1FT0-008H-V491-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Diduck, 974 F=
.2d
at 284</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Accordingly, the complaint fails to state a claim agai=
nst
BNY Mellon for participation in a breach of fiduciary duty by Ivy, Simon, a=
nd
Wohl.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal8 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, the district court's dismissal of the Trustees'
complaint is affirmed.</span></p>

