<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ16W0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On remand, the district court
received further briefing on two issues raised in defendants' initial motio=
n to
dismiss but not addressed in the court's first decision: standing and statu=
te
of limitations. Thereafter, on September 3, 1986, the district court issued=
 an
order (1) upholding the complaint as to Rhoades and Braten; (2) dismissing =
the
claims of substantive RICO violations against Soifer as time-barred;
&nbsp;[*1100]&nbsp; but (3) upholding the claim that Soifer conspired with =
the
other defendants to violate RICO. On the standing issue, the district court
specifically held that Bankers had asserted &quot;injuries * * * in its own
right&quot;, not merely injuries to BAC which might be recoverable only by a
bankruptcy trustee.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ16V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-8H90-0039-R1KM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Trust=
 Co.
v. Feldesman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-8H90-0039-R1KM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 648 F. Supp=
. 17
(S.D.N.Y. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
moved to reargue, and on December 22, 1987, the district court reversed its=
elf
in part and dismissed Bankers' &nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; complaint as to all
defendants. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1810000400"></a>Specifically,=
 the
district court held that Bankers lacked standing to pursue a civil RICO act=
ion
based on defendants' common-law fraud, perjury and bankruptcy fraud--all
alleged to be part of defendants' attempt to deplete BAC assets--because
Bankers had suffered no injury from these illegal acts. Rather, the court
stated, only BAC, as the corporation whose assets were fraudulently
transferred, was injured by defendants' actions. In addition, applying a
three-year statute of limitations, the district court held the bribery clai=
ms
time-barred because the last injury to Bankers occurred on January 18, 1979,
over three years before it commenced its RICO action in federal court.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1800000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B310-003B-62YP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Trust=
 Co.
v. Feldesman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B310-003B-62YP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 676 F. Supp.
496 (S.D.N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
appeal followed. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>II.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>DISCUSSION </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Bankers'
argument on appeal is two-fold. First, it argues that it has standing to pu=
rsue
a civil RICO claim directly against the individual defendants, and that the
district court's holding to the contrary was error. Second, Bankers contends
that the district court erred in dismissing on statute of limitations groun=
ds.
We agree with both contentions. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Bankers'
Standing &nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; to Bring a RICO Claim</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Determining
that formulation of an acceptable plan of reorganization &quot;would be all=
 but
impossible if a creditor could employ RICO to recover misappropriated assets
from the bankrupt's corporate officers&quot; during the pendency of the
bankruptcy proceedings, the district court held that Bankers had no standing
&quot;to sue for any injury attributable to the depletion of BAC's corporate
assets.&quot; Such analysis correctly perceives a legitimate problem--if
Bankers is allowed to bring its RICO claim (and collect treble damages plus
costs and attorney's fees), there may well be nothing left for the bankrupt=
's
estate to recover--but it uses the wrong mechanism to ensure that problem's
solution. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I686JKNB4450008CX1G004MP"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1830000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I686JKNB9710008CX1G004MR"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1920000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_8"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
civil RICO statute, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> provides that &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>any
person</i> injured in his business or property by reason of a violation of =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
this chapter may sue therefor * * * and shall recover threefold the damages=
 he
sustains * * *.&quot; (emphasis added). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1940000400"></a>This language contains no sp=
ecial
limitation on standing; all that is required is that plaintiff suffer injur=
y in
fact, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1820000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima v. Imr=
ex
Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
495, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346, 105 S. Ct. 3275 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(the only standing requirement under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; is that the plaintiff be &quot;injured in his business or
property by the conduct constituting the violation&quot;), and that the inj=
ury
be caused by defendants' RICO violation. Thus, as another panel of this cou=
rt
recently recognized: <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_8"></a>&quot;A defen=
dant
who violates </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
not liable for treble damages to everyone he might have injured by other
conduct, but only to anyone whose injuries were caused by reason of a viola=
tion
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1840000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sperber v. Bo=
esky</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0RT0-001B-K309-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 849 F.2d 60=
, 64
(2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (citation omitted). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1940000400_2"></a>Significantly, this injury=
 is
not limited to damages suffered from the RICO violation as a whole, but also
includes injuries suffered from each predicate act. Those acts are, when
committed in the circumstances delineated in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
&quot;an activity which RICO was designed to deter&quot;, and recoverable
damages &quot;will flow from the commission of the[se] predicate acts.&quot=
; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1930000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. at=
 497</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1960000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>With these principles in mind, a=
long
with congress's instructions that we broadly construe the statute, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> Pub. L. 91-452, Title IX, Sect=
ion
904, 84 Stat. 941, we hold that the district court erred when it applied
&nbsp;[*1101]&nbsp; an additional, special requirement of standing to Banke=
rs'
civil RICO claim. &nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; Put simply, if Bankers was injured by
defendants' acts, as its complaint adequately alleges in this case, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1950000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-WC80-003B-G1J9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>741 F.2d at
515-16</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>, it has standing to bring a RICO claim, regardless of t=
he
fact that a bankrupt BAC might also have suffered an identical injury for w=
hich
it has a similar right of recovery. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I686JKNBY310008CX1G004MW"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1BJ0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1C70000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This conclusion is not contrary =
to
our decision in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1BH0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3JX0-0039-P4W3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rand v.
Anaconda-Ericsson, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3JX0-0039-P4W3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 794 F.2d 84=
3</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied</i>, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ1BK0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>479 U.S. 987, 93 L. Ed. 2d 582, 107 S. Ct. 579 (1986)</i>, where we
