<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Having
determined generally that plaintiff may recover for any injury caused by
defendants' RICO violation if plaintiff discovered or should have discovered
that injury within four years of the commencement of this action, we now ap=
ply
this standard to Bankers' specific injury claims. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Bankers
commenced its action on August 24, 1982; hence, it may recover for any inju=
ry
it discovered or should have discovered on or after August 24, 1978. In its
third amended complaint, Bankers alleges multiple injuries which may be gro=
uped
into three general categories: (a) past legal fees and other expenses, (b) =
loss
of a legitimate debt and related expenses, and (c) future legal fees and
expenses. We shall discuss each in turn. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>a.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Past
legal fees and other expenses</i>. Several injuries claimed by Bankers fall
within this category: (1) an unspecified amount in legal fees and other
expenses incurred in fighting defendants' &nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; frivolous laws=
uits
in New York state court; (2) $ 100,000 in legal fees and other expenses spe=
nt
in overcoming bribe-induced decisions in a similar lawsuit in South Carolin=
a;
and (3) $ 100,000 in legal fees and other expenses incurred in obtaining a
revocation of the initial reorganization plan. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ23M0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under our rule of separate accru=
al,
Bankers may recover any of these expenses which it discovered or should have
discovered on or after August 24, 1978. We therefore instruct the district
court that it may dismiss from this action any claim for legal fees and oth=
er
expenses which Bankers discovered or should have discovered before that dat=
e.
In so instructing, we note our agreement with Judge Conner when he held that
Bankers suffered injury as to each expense when it became obligated to pay =
that
expense, and not at some later date when it actually made the payment.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJNC820K1MNJ22H0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B310-003B-62YP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bankers Trust=
 Co.
v. Feldesman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B310-003B-62YP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 676 F. Supp.
496, 504 (S.D.N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;If Bankers was able to
postpone accrual of its claim until it had paid its legal expenses, then
Bankers could keep its claim open indefinitely simply by protracting the
litigation.&quot;). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal9 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Loss
of a legitimate debt and related expenses</i>. In this second
&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; category, Bankers claims (1) loss of a legitimate debt in
1976 when, relying on defendants' misrepresentations, it accepted a bankrup=
tcy
reorganization plan that would have allowed Bankers to recover only 17.5% of
its allowed claim; (2) loss of the use of those &nbsp;[*1106]&nbsp; funds f=
rom
1976 to present; and (3) an additional loss in funds which might have been =
used
