k'>Prior
History:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;Retired officers of Booz Allen Hamilton (&quot;Booz
Allen&quot;) allege that they received insufficient payment in connection w=
ith
Booz Allen's sale of a division to another company. They sue Booz Allen and
others in the United States District Court for the Southern District of New
York (Kaplan, J.), asserting liability under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Employee
Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (&quot;ERISA&quot;), 29 U.S.C. &sect;
1001 et seq.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), 18 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1961 et seq.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, federal securities law, and common law. Each claim was
resolved adversely to plaintiffs, either by dismissal or by denial of leave=
 to
amend. We affirm the district court's dismissal of the ERISA claims because=
 the
plan through which Booz Allen distributed its stock to plaintiffs is not an
employee pension benefit plan within the meaning of ERISA. However, we vaca=
te
the judgment to the extent that it denied the motion by one plaintiff for l=
eave
to amend to add securities-fraud claims. Leave to amend was denied on the
ground that the claim was waived by a release, and on the ground of delay. =
That
release, however, is unenforceable by virtue of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 29(a)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;
of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4V2-8T6X-7566-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Securities
Exchange Act of 1934</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and there is no other sustaina=
ble
basis on which to deny leave&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; to amend. As to all the remai=
ning
claims, we affirm.</span></p>

