<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*165]&nbsp;
JACOBS, <u>Circuit Judge</u>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs are retired officers of Booz Allen Hamilton, a privately-held
corporation. Bruce Pasternack, Reginald Boudinot, and Paul Kocourek allege =
that
they were improperly denied compensation when, after their retirement, Booz
Allen Hamilton sold one of its divisions to the Carlyle Group (the
&quot;Carlyle Transaction&quot;). They sued Booz Allen Hamilton and several=
 of
its officers (collectively &quot;Booz Allen&quot;), as well as various
individuals and entities involved in the Carlyle Transaction. Numerous amen=
ded
complaints were filed&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; by each plaintiff. On appeal, we rev=
iew
the legal sufficiency of various claims under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Employee
Retirement Income Security Act of 1974 (&quot;ERISA&quot;), 29 U.S.C. &sect;
1001 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>et seq.</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), 18 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1961 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>et seq.</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
federal securities law, and common law. In general, all three plaintiffs al=
lege
that Booz Allen improperly discriminated among different Booz Allen officers
and violated the duties of due care, loyalty, and good faith, in violation =
of
ERISA. Kocourek separately argues that Booz Allen fraudulently induced him =
and
others to sell Booz Allen stock at prices below fair value, in violation of
federal securities regulations and other law.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
United States District Court for the Southern District of New York (Kaplan,=
 <u>J.</u>)
dismissed the ERISA claims on the ground that Booz Allen's stock-distributi=
on
program was not a pension plan within the meaning of ERISA, and denied as
futile leave to amend to &quot;augment&quot; the ERISA claims with new
allegations. The RICO claims were dismissed on the ground that they were ba=
rred
by the Private Securities Litigation Reform Act of 1995 (&quot;PSLRA&quot;)=
, <u>see</u>
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Kocourek's request to amend his complaint to add
securities-fraud &nbsp;[*166]&nbsp; claims was denied on the
grounds&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; of futility and undue delay. It appears that the
district court did not directly address Kocourek's proposed common law clai=
ms;
so Kocourek asks us to remand to the district court for reconsideration of
those claims. No other claim is before us on appeal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
affirm the district court in all respects save one. We vacate the judgment =
of
the district court to the extent it denied Kocourek leave to amend to add
securities-fraud causes of action, and we remand the case to the district c=
ourt
to consider those claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Booz
Allen is a privately-owned corporation that operates in all material respec=
ts
like a partnership. The company has no outside investors, avoids outside de=
bt,
and is wholly owned by its officers, who are referred to internally as
partners.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Booz
Allen allocates its stock via its Stock Rights Plan (the &quot;SRP&quot;). =
For
purposes of this opinion, Booz Allen stock can be classed as &quot;common
stock&quot; or &quot;Class B stock,&quot; although the actual operation of =
the
SRP is more complicated. The SRP operates as follows. In an officer's first
participation year, the officer is given the option to purchase a block of
shares, of which 10% is common stock and 90% is Class B. The officer pays b=
ook
value&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; for the common stock tranche, and a nominal amount f=
or
the Class B tranche. In each successive year, the officer exchanges some of=
 the
Class B stock for common stock, so that the percentage of common stock in t=
he
initial grant increases annually by 10%. Thus, at the end of the second yea=
r,
the block is composed of 20% common stock and 80% Class B stock; at the end=
 of
the third year, 30% common and 70% Class B; and so on, until at the end of =
ten
years, the block is 100% common stock. The officer pays for the incremental
conversions to common stock at the price of half of book value at the time =
of
the initial grant.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
new, parallel, ten-year process begins every year, as the officer continuou=
sly
receives new amounts of both common stock and Class B stock.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
an officer separates from Booz Allen, the disposition of the officer's
remaining Class B stock depends on how the officer leaves. If the officer is
terminated for cause or leaves the company before retirement, the officer l=
oses
the right to exchange the Class B stock for common stock and must return the
Class B stock to Booz Allen. If the participant retires with Booz Allen's
approval, all outstanding Class B stock may be converted&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; i=
nto
common stock. Retirees are allowed to continue holding their stock for two
years into retirement, after which Booz Allen has the option of repurchasing
the retiree's common stock at the current book value.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn137' href=3D"#_ftn137" name=3D"_ftnref137" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Common stock and Class B stock have voting rights in the management of Booz
Allen, and the common stock pays a small annual dividend.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a matter of policy, the only owners of Booz Allen stock are the company its=
elf
and its partners. Partners can sell their stock only to Booz Allen. Althoug=
h at
least one SRP policy document states that partners may sell their common st=
ock
back to Booz Allen at any time, the plaintiffs allege that partners were
effectively unable to do so until retirement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*167]&nbsp;
The SRP is a lucrative arrangement that allows participants to realize hand=
some
profits when they sell their common stock. The relevant measure of the comm=
on
stock's worth is its book value, i.e., Booz Allen's net assets divided by t=
he
number of outstanding shares of common stock. The company undertakes to
increase the actual book value of the common stock by 10% annually, and has
done so every year since 1978. Participants buy shares either at book value
(measured at the time of the initial&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; grant) for the 10%
portion of the annual grant composed of common stock, or at half of book va=
lue
(again measured at the time of issuance) for the remaining 90% of those sha=
res
that begin as Class B stock and convert to common stock over the ensuing ni=
ne
years. Because the book value appreciates at a compounded rate of 10% annua=
lly,
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>current</i> book value in any y=
ear
will be higher than the purchase price--generally much higher. A participant
who received an initial grant of 100 shares in 2001, with a book value of $=
100
per share, would ultimately pay $5,500 by 2010 to receive 100 shares of com=
mon
stock, which by then would be worth $23,579--a 329% return on investment wi=
th
nominal risk.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
Pasternack, Boudinot, and Kocourek are all former officers of Booz Allen. T=
hey
retired with the company's blessing in 2004, 2005, and 2007, respectively. =
They
all participated in the SRP.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2008, Booz Allen sold its Government Contracting Division to an affiliate of
the Carlyle Group. The Carlyle Transaction was approved by a supermajority =
of
Booz Allen stockholders (i.e., participants in the SRP). Then-current
stockholders received $763 per share of common stock, an amount far in
excess&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; of book value.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
the time of the Carlyle Transaction, Pasternack and Boudinot had no common
stock because Booz Allen exercised its option under the SRP to repurchase t=
heir
shares two years after their retirement. Since Kocourek's two-year
post-retirement period had not elapsed, Kocourek still held common stock, w=
hich
he sold in the Carlyle Transaction for approximately $20 million.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>D</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
three plaintiffs filed individual complaints in 2009. Initially, there was =
no
allegation that the SRP was governed by ERISA; but each complaint was amend=
ed
(with the defendants' consent) to add ERISA claims. The three complaints we=
re
consolidated.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Most
of the claims in the consolidated complaint were dismissed on motion, inclu=
ding
the ERISA claims and RICO claims. Plaintiffs' motion for leave to amend the=
ir
ERISA claims was denied. Kocourek's separate request for leave to add
securities-fraud claims was denied on the grounds of futility and undue del=
ay.
A few other claims, not before us on appeal, were dismissed at summary
judgment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>All
three plaintiffs appeal the dismissal of the ERISA-based claims, which were
dismissed on the ground that the SRP was not covered by ERISA. The plaintif=
fs
also appeal the&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; denial of their motion to amend their ERISA
claims. Kocourek separately appeals from the district court's dismissal of =
his
RICO claims and the denial of leave to amend to add securities-fraud claims.
Kocourek also seeks to revive his common-law claims for breach of fiduciary
duty.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42D0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court did not reach=
 the
merits of the ERISA claims because it &nbsp;[*168]&nbsp; concluded that the
plaintiffs failed to allege facts demonstrating that the SRP is covered by
ERISA. We agree.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn138' href=3D"#_ftn138"
name=3D"_ftnref138" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs assert that the SRP is <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_9"></a>=
an
ERISA-protected &quot;employee pension benefit plan,&quot; defined as:</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>[A]ny
plan, fund, or program . . . to the extent that by its express terms or as a
result of surrounding circumstances such plan, fund, or program--</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(i)
provides retirement income to employees, or</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(ii)
results in a deferral of income by employees for periods extending to the
termination of covered employment or beyond,</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-left:20.0pt;text-align:justify;line-hei=
ght:
15.0pt'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>regardless of the method of calculating the contributions made=
 to
the plan, the method of calculating the benefits under the plan or the meth=
od
of distributing benefits from the plan.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKDXMJ000YV8YK0000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WB0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_9"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
the Fifth Circuit has observed, this statutory provision is &quot;not to be
read as an elastic girdle that can be stretched to cover&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; =
any
content that can conceivably fit within its reach.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42D0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-KBF0-0039-W51V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Murphy v. Ine=
xco
Oil Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-KBF0-0039-W51V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 611 F.2d 57=
0,
575 (5th Cir. 1980)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The question is whether the re=
ach
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1002(2=
)(A)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
extends to the SRP.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
the district court found, the SRP has little to do with retirement. Rather,=
 as
described in an internal Booz Allen memorandum discussing the SRP, it is the
means of ensuring that Booz Allen is entirely &quot;owned by the partners, =
with
no outside control,&quot; and that the firm &quot;not use long-term debt for
working capital needs.&quot; J. App'x at 613. Since every enterprise must r=
aise
capital somehow, the obvious recourse was for &quot;[p]artners [to be]
responsible for providing the firm with adequate capital.&quot; <u>Id.</u>
Partners &quot;me[t] their financial obligation to the firm by annually
investing cash via the [SRP],&quot; which &quot;provide[d] the working capi=
tal
necessary to operate the firm.&quot; <u>Id.</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
maintain ownership by current partners over time, Booz Allen needed a way to
return capital to partners who leave. The SRP does this by having the compa=
ny
buy out the ownership stake accumulated by a separating partner. This proce=
ss
ensures a renewing stream of capital, allows for the creation of new partne=
rs,
accounts for the separation of present partners, maintains ownership
exclusivity by partners, and achieves independence from outside
debt.&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
particulars of the SRP appear unique: Booz Allen is a large corporation that
distributes stock in a way that makes the corporation operate like a
partnership. But the driving principle behind the arrangement is unexceptio=
nal.
At bottom, the SRP is akin to the financing mechanism utilized by ordinary
partnerships and by other enterprises in which capital is contributed to a
venture in exchange for an ownership stake.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
question remains whether the SRP is an employee pension benefit plan within=
 the
meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_9"></a>The two
subparagraphs of that section set out independent tests to determine whethe=
r a
plan is protected by ERISA. We begin with the second subparagraph, which
extends ERISA coverage to plans that &quot;result[] in a deferral of income=
 by
employees . . . to the termination of covered &nbsp;[*169]&nbsp;
employment.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)(ii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
SRP does not qualify under this test because &quot;income&quot; is not
&quot;deferred.&quot; The salient benefit that an SRP participant receives =
in
exchange for a capital injection is an ownership stake in Booz Allen. And
because Booz Allen is owned entirely by its officers, that ownership stake
entails the right to actively direct the management of the enterprise. Those
benefits accrue during the SRP&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; participants' tenure at Bo=
oz
Allen, not at retirement. True, the ownership stake can be liquidated into =
cash
only after retirement, but the dominant benefits are the ownership stake in=
 the
enterprise and the corresponding rights of management--and these rights were
exercised before retirement and ended with the retirement process. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_9"></a>We do not think that the statute, whi=
ch
speaks of a &quot;deferral of income . . . to the termination of covered
employment,&quot; is meant to include instances in which benefits enjoyed <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>before</i> retirement were converted t=
o cash
after retirement. Such benefits were not &quot;deferred.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>No
doubt, there are arrangements in which the distinction is blurry between the
grant of stock as deferred income and the deployment of present assets to
acquire a working share in a business. In this case, however, the distincti=
on
is crisp and unambiguous. The plaintiffs received present benefits rather t=
han
a deferral of income.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn139' href=3D"#_f=
tn139"
name=3D"_ftnref139" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nor
does the SRP constitute an employee pension benefit plan under <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_8"></a>the statute's alternative test, which
extends ERISA coverage to plans that &quot;provide[] retirement income to
employees.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)(i)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WB0030000400"></a>The
statutory phrase &quot;provides retirement income&quot; does not cover every
instance&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; in which a person cashes out an investment after
retirement, even though a participant will have anticipated this income when
planning for retirement. The very fact that the provision is an alternative=
 to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
1002(2)(A)(ii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which explicitly asks whether a plan &quot;results&quo=
t;
in deferred income, suggests that the phrase &quot;provides retirement
income&quot; considers the plan's primary purpose rather than its result. S=
ee </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WB0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-KBF0-0039-W51V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Murphy, 611 F=
.2d
at 575</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> (noting that &quot;[t]he words 'provides retirement inc=
ome'
patently refer only to plans designed for the purpose of paying retirement
income&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
primary purposes of the SRP are to provide working capital for the company =
and
maintain management's control of it. True, SRP participants receive cash wh=
en
they sell their shares after retirement. However (as described above), each
participant had already received and enjoyed the present benefit from their
contributions <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>before retirement</i>=
. The
later receipt of cash on sale of the asset after retirement does not mean t=
hat
the SRP &quot;provides retirement income&quot; within the meaning of the
statute. Moreover, as an internal Booz Allen memorandum states,
&quot;[l]iquidation of stock at retirement is a return of capital rather th=
an a
source of retirement income.&quot; J. App'x at 839. The SRP's primary
purpose&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; is simply not the provision of retirement income.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Consequently,
the SRP satisfies neither test of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Our analysis and conclusion finds support in <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_8"></a>the Department of Labor regulation wh=
ich
&nbsp;[*170]&nbsp; states that &quot;partnership buyout agreements . . . wi=
ll
not be subject to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>title I [of
ERISA]</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6C0T-8TK3-RRRN-F05Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 C.F.R. &se=
ct;
2510.3-3(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (full text in the margin).</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn140' href=3D"#_ftn140" name=3D"_ftnref140" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-70N0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>[T]itle I</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
of ERISA includes </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which describes &quot;employee pension benefit
plans.&quot; Consequently, partnership buyout agreements are not employee
pension benefit plans. Because Booz Allen's re-purchase of an SRP participa=
nt's
stock is in effect a partnership buyout agreement, the SRP is not an
&quot;employee pension benefit plan.&quot; And since the plaintiffs' sole c=
laim
to ERISA coverage is that the SRP is an employee pension benefit plan, the
ERISA causes of action fail.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
be sure, the regulation purports to apply only to &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>partnership</i> buyout agreements,&quot; and Booz Allen is structur=
ed
as a corporation. But the underlying principle controls. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_7"></a>By its terms the regulation suggests =
that
it should be interpreted flexibly: it speaks of &quot;a general principle w=
hich
can be applied to a large class of plans to determine whether they constitu=
te
employee benefit plans.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6C0T-8TK3-RRRN-F05Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 C.F.R. &se=
ct;
2510.3-3(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The Booz Allen arrangement is self-evidently a way to
finance the company's operations by contributions of capital from persons w=
ho
own and direct it. In this respect, Booz Allen officers are analogous to
partners (and function as partners in many other respects as well). Although
the SRP involves the purchase of corporate stock, it functioned as a
partnership buyout in all respects material to this appeal. The principle
embodied in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6C0T-8TK3-RRRN-F05Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 C.F.R. &se=
ct;
2510.3-3(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, that a partnership buyout is not covered&nbsp;[**16]&n=
bsp;
by ERISA, confirms the intuitive conclusion that ERISA does not extend to
agreements such as the SRP.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKF2FP000YV8YK0000H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WB0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Ninth Circuit has also concl=
uded
that the Booz Allen SRP is not covered by ERISA, though it got there by ano=
ther
route. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WB0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5JVG-8H21-F04K-V0BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rich v. Shrad=
er</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5JVG-8H21-F04K-V0BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 823 F.3d 12=
05
(9th Cir. 2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. We decline to follow the Ninth
Circuit's analysis, but it is quite evidently a product of the same insight:
that the Booz Allen arrangement is principally a mechanism for raising capi=
tal
for the running of the enterprise by its active managers, rather than a plan
for their retirement.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn141' href=3D"#_f=
tn141"
name=3D"_ftnref141" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*171]&nbsp;
In sum, the SRP neither &quot;(i) provides retirement income&quot; nor
&quot;(ii) results in a deferral of income . . . to the termination of cove=
red
employment.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(2)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Since the SRP is therefore not an &quot;employee pensi=
on
benefit plan&quot; within the meaning of ERISA, the district court correctly
dismissed all of the plaintiffs' claims that depended on a finding that the=
 SRP
was covered by ERISA.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed Kocourek's RICO claims and common law claims and
denied leave to amend to add securities-fraud claims. We conclude that the
district court should have allowed Kocourek leave to amend his complaint to=
 add
securities-fraud causes of action. We otherwise affirm the district court's
dismissal&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; of Kocourek's claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2SF84T0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Among the reasons advanced by the
district court for denying Kocourek's motion to add securities-fraud claims=
 is
that Kocourek waived his right to assert them, and that amendment would be
futile. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_7"></a>We review that ruling de n=
ovo. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2SF84T0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55R4-KH11-F04K-J0F9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Panther Partn=
ers
Inc. v. Ikanos Commc'ns, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:55R4-KH11-F04K-J0F9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 681 F.3d 11=
4,
119 (2d Cir. 2012)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Since
Kocourek still had shares when the Carlyle Transaction was done, he was
eligible to profit from it, and did. In order to receive a payout from the
Carlyle Transaction, stockholders were required to sign a &quot;Letter of
Transmittal&quot; (the &quot;Letter&quot;), surrendering their shares. The
district court determined that the following clause in the Letter (the
&quot;Release Clause&quot;) waived Kocourek's claims:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
undersigned . . . to the fullest extent permitted by applicable law, hereby
releases and forever discharges [Booz Allen and its affiliates, directors a=
nd
officers] . . . from any and all claims, demands, proceedings, causes of ac=
tion
. . . whether known or unknown, suspected or unsuspected . . . by reason of,
relating to or arising from the fact that the undersigned is or was a
stockholder of [Booz Allen] . . . or any other rights with respect to or wi=
th a
value derived from or other interest in any equity of [Booz Allen] . . . .<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>J.
App'x at 1028-29.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kocourek
does not dispute that&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; he signed the Letter, or that the
wording recites the waiver of the securities claims he is asserting. Instea=
d,
he argues that the Release Clause is invalidated by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 29(a)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the Securities Exchange Act of 1934 (codified at </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78cc(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>):</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Any
condition, stipulation, or provision binding any person to waive compliance
with any provision of [the Exchange Act] or of any rule or regulation
[promulgated] thereunder . . . shall be void.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>15 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
78cc(a)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKF514000YV8YK0000J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc9_7"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
antiwaiver provision generally invalidates blanket releases of liability th=
at
accompany the purchase or sale of securities. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKF8V8000YV8YK0000K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310050000400"></a>Accordingly, &quot;we do not=
 give
effect to contractual language . . . purporting to be a general waiver or
release of [securities-fraud] liability altogether.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2SF84T0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TKJ-35C0-TX4N-G089-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Vacold LLC v.
Cerami</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4TKJ-35C0-TX4N-G089-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 545 F.3d 11=
4,
122 (2d Cir. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. &quot;The statutory framework =
of
the 1933 and 1934 Acts compels the conclusion that individual securityholde=
rs
may not be forced to forego their rights under the federal securities laws =
due
to a contract provision.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C290-001T-D490-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McMahan &amp;=
 Co.
v. Wherehouse Entm't, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C290-001T-D490-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 65 F.3d 104=
4,
1051 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310050000400_2"></a>Relatedly, in <u>Harsco
Corporation v. Segui</u>, we upheld (in the face of a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 29</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
challenge) a contractual clause that limited the representations upon which=
 the
purchaser could bring a fraud claim, but we did so only after observing that
the purchaser &quot;ha[d] not waived its rights to bring &nbsp;[*172]&nbsp;=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> suit resulting
from&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; this deal.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N310040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1GJ0-006F-M1XC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>91 F.3d 337, =
344
(2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Letter was essentially a contract for the sale of Kocourek's securities, and
the Release Clause purported to waive all claims arising from Kocourek's st=
atus
as a Booz Allen stockholder. It is therefore invalid, absent an exception to
the general rule that blanket releases accompanying the sale of securities =
are
void.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Booz
Allen argues that there is such an exception. The company contends [1] that=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 29(a)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies only to &quot;anticipatory&quot; waivers of compliance, [2] that
Kocourek's agreement here was a &quot;retrospective&quot; release from
liability, and [3] that such retrospective releases are not voided by </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 29(a)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2N1R5K0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The cases Booz Allen cites for t=
his
proposition, however, all arose out of agreements in which the parties sett=
led
existing or contemplated litigation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn1=
42'
href=3D"#_ftn142" name=3D"_ftnref142" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Booz Allen is correct that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_7"></a>a waiver signed in the context of a
settlement agreement may release securities-fraud claims. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0040000400"></a>See, e.g., </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0H50-0039-M1P2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Locafrance U.=
S.
Corp. v. Intermodal Sys. Leasing, Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0H50-0039-M1P2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 558 F.2d 111=
3,
1114-15 (2d Cir. 1977)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. And it is certainly possible t=
hat
a release signed outside the context of settlement could serve the same
function. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0040000400_2"></a>See </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y6F0-0039-M02C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Goodman v.
Epstein</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y6F0-0039-M02C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 582 F.2d 38=
8,
394, 402-403 &amp; n.42 (7th Cir. 1978)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42F0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43VK-5XS0-0038-X079-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moseman v. Van
Leer</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:43VK-5XS0-0038-X079-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 263 F.3d 12=
9,
133-34 (4th Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKFH6V000YV8YK0000N"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4XVMGKFN20000YV8YK0000P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2SF84V0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc11_7"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>But
such instances, properly understood, are not &quot;exceptions.&quot; &quot;=
What
the antiwaiver provision of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-W4W2-8T6X-702K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 29(a)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
forbids is enforcement of agreements to waive 'compliance'&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp;
with the provisions of the statute.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WC0050000400"></a><a
