k'>).
A litigant may be &quot;prejudiced&quot; within the meaning of the rule if =
the
new claim would: &quot;(i) require the opponent to expend significant
additional resources to conduct discovery and prepare for trial; (ii)
significantly delay the resolution of the dispute; or (iii) prevent the
plaintiff from bringing a timely action in another jurisdiction.&quot; </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N330020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Block</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 988 F.2d at=
 350</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N330050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42G0020000400"></a>However, &quot;[m]ere delay,=
 . . .
absent a showing of bad faith or undue prejudice, does not provide a basis =
for
a district court to deny the right to amend.&quot; <u>Id.</u> (quoting <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2D6N330040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'><u>State Teachers Ret. Bd. v. Fluor Corp.</u>, 654 F.2d 843, 856 (2d
Cir. 1981))</i>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42G0020000400_2"></a>Nor can
complaints of &quot;the time, effort and money . . . expended in litigating
[the] matter,&quot; without more, constitute prejudice sufficient to warrant
denial of leave to amend. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42G00100004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 351</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
denial of leave to amend, based solely on delay and litigation expense, was=
 an
abuse of discretion. The district court's explanation cited the years of
litigation and concluded: &quot;[The] defendants have spent a vast amount of
money litigating the sufficiency of various complaints in this case. This is
not something unworthy of consideration. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42G0040000400"></a>It is surely
prejudice&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; . . . .&quot; J. App'x at 1317. But delay (and =
its
necessary consequence, litigation expense) does not, without more, constitu=
te
undue prejudice. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42G0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Block</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-HCT0-003B-P4M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 988 F.2d at
350-51</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Perhaps it should; but it does not. Moreover, essentia=
lly
no discovery has been undertaken in this case; Kocourek's proposed amended
complaint would be the first complaint to be considered after the district
court decided a motion to dismiss; and it does not appear that there is any
allegation of untimeliness based on a scheduling order.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn146' href=3D"#_ftn146" name=3D"_ftnref146" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Under the circumstances, amendment should not be precluded on the ground of
untimeliness.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>C</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court also dismissed Kocourek's securities-fraud claims as futile
because the &quot;allegations in the proposed amended complaint do not come
remotely close to pleading fraud with the particularity required by [</span=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure] 9(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &nbsp;[*175]&nbsp; and the Priv=
ate
Securities Litigation Reform Act [the 'PSLRA'].&quot; J. App'x at 1319.
However, the district court did not sufficiently explain why the allegations
were deficient under the heightened pleading standards of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and the PSLRA--presumably because the district court had already concluded =
that
Kocourek's claims were untimely and waived.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42H0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>But there is no need to parse
Kocourek's allegations here. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc17_3"></a>&quo=
t;Complaints
dismissed under&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
are 'almost always' dismissed with leave to amend.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42H0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Luce v. Edels=
tein</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0JJ0-0039-P06C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 802 F.2d 49=
, 56
(2d Cir. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; <u>see </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K28T42H0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P5K-2K30-TXFX-43CP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>ATSI, Commc'n=
s.,
Inc. v. Shaar Fund, Ltd.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4P5K-2K30-TXFX-43CP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 493 F.3d 87,
108 (2d Cir. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;District courts typically
grant plaintiffs at least one opportunity to plead fraud with greater
specificity when they dismiss under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;).
Kocourek presented his proposed securities-fraud claims to the district cou=
rt a
single time (on his motion to amend). At a minimum, Kocourek should be allo=
wed
on remand to plead with greater specificity. As to the merits, we have no v=
iew.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>D</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Booz
Allen also argues that Kocourek's securities-fraud claims are time-barred. =
The
claims here are subject to a five-year statute of repose. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WD0020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc18_3"></a>In
general, statutes of repose impose stricter timeliness requirements than
statutes of limitation. For example, the time limit for a statute of
limitations is measured from the time the plaintiff discovers or should have
discovered the injury, whereas the time limit for a claim subject to a stat=
ute
of repose is measured from the last culpable act of the defendant (meaning =
that
the plaintiff's discovery of the injury is irrelevant for a statute of repo=
se).
Statutes of limitation are normally subject to equitable tolling; statutes =
of
repose normally are not. See generally </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WD0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CD0-MWX1-F04K-F00T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>CTS Corp. v.
Waldburger, 134 S. Ct. 2175, 2182-84, 189 L. Ed. 2d 62 (2014)</span></i></a=
><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Booz
Allen argues that Kocourek's claim is&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; untimely because he=
 did
not file his amended complaint within the statute of repose. Although Kocou=
rek
had filed his <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>motion</i> to amend w=
ithin
that time period (with the proposed amended complaint attached), Booz Allen
argues that is insufficient.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WD0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Under Booz Allen's theory, a
plaintiff that requests leave to amend a complaint years in advance of the
expiration of the statue of repose would still be barred from bringing the =
suit
if the district court sat on the motion for years without fault of the
plaintiff. However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_3"></a>for purposes =
of a
statute of repose, when a plaintiff moves for leave to amend to add claims
within the limitations period and attaches a proposed amended complaint to =
the
motion, the claims are timely. <u>Cf. </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WD0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40PV-KK70-0038-X2BH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rothman v. Gr=
egor</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:40PV-KK70-0038-X2BH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 220 F.3d 81=
, 96
(2d Cir. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;When a plaintiff seeks to add a new defendant in=
 an
existing action, the date of the filing of the motion to amend constitutes =
the
date the action was commenced for statute of limitations purposes.&quot;
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5P67J7K2HM6WD0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C690-001T-71W4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nw. Nat'l Ins.
Co. v. Alberts</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C690-001T-71W4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 769 F. Supp.
498, 510 (S.D.N.Y. 1991))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal10 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we vacate the judgment of the district court to the
extent it denied Kocourek's request for leave to amend his complaint to add
claims sounding in securities fraud. We remand to the district court to all=
ow
Kocourek to amend his&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; complaint. In all other respects, t=
he
judgment is affirmed.</span></p>

