<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Prior_History_9"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Prior
History:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;Appeal from the United States
District Court for the Southern District of New York.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
question presented in this appeal is whether the plaintiffs have plausibly
alleged a &quot;domestic injury&quot; to their business or property within =
the
meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)
of the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the provision commonly referred to as civil RICO. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. This question is one of first impression&#8212;in this=
 (or
any) Court of Appeals&#8212;arising from the Supreme Court's decision in </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco, =
Inc.
v. European Community, 136 S. Ct. 2090, 195 L. Ed. 2d 476 (2016)</span></i>=
</a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
RJR Nabisco, the Supreme Court held, among other things, that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)
of the RICO</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> statute, which gives a private right of action to &quot=
;any
person injured in his business or property by reason of a violation of [RIC=
O's
substantive provisions],&quot; does not apply extraterritorially. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 2111</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, the Supreme Court explained that &quot;</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>[s]ection 196=
4(c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires a civil RICO plaintiff to allege and prove a domestic injury to
business or property and does not allow recovery for foreign injuries.&quot;
Id. (emphasis added). The Supreme Court did not explain, however, how to
determine whether an alleged injury is domestic or foreign.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff-appellant
Jorge Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, a citizen and resident of Chile, brought an
action under civil RICO in the United States&nbsp;[**2]&nbsp; District Court
for the Southern District of New York (George B. Daniels, Judge) against his
cousin, defendant-appellee Daniel Elsaca, also a citizen and resident of Ch=
ile.
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged that Elsaca, who had power of attorney over
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's finances, stole millions of dollars from
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n through several fraudulent financial schemes.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the District Court, Elsaca moved to dismiss Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's compla=
int
on the ground that he failed to allege a domestic injury as required by RJR
Nabisco. The District Court granted the motion and, characterizing
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's injury broadly as a $64 million &quot;economic
loss,&quot; held that, because individual plaintiffs suffer economic injuri=
es
at their place of residence and because Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n was a reside=
nt
of Chile, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged only foreign injuries. On appeal,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n argues that the District Court erred in relying
exclusively on his place of residence to determine that he alleged only for=
eign
injuries.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
conclude that, to the extent Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged injuries to
property located within the United States, he satisfied civil RICO's domest=
ic
injury requirement. But to the extent Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged injuri=
es
to property located outside of the United States,&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; the fact
that Elsaca or his co-defendants transferred those stolen funds to (or thro=
ugh)
the United States fails to transform an otherwise foreign injury into a
domestic one.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we REVERSE the District Court's order granting Elsaca's motion to dismiss, =
we
VACATE the District Court's order denying Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's motion f=
or
leave to file a second amended complaint, and we REMAND the cause to the
District Court for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span>=
</p>

