rmal'>Judge</i>)
against his cousin, defendant-appellee Daniel Elsaca, also a citizen and
resident of Chile. Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged that Elsaca, who had powe=
r of
attorney over Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's finances, stole millions of dollars =
from
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n through several fraudulent financial schemes.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the District Court, Elsaca moved to dismiss Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's compla=
int
on the ground that&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; he failed to allege a domestic injury as
required by <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nabisco</i>. The Di=
strict
Court granted the motion and, characterizing Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's injury
broadly as a $64 million &quot;economic loss,&quot; held that, because
individual plaintiffs suffer economic injuries at their place of residence =
and
because Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n was a resident of Chile, Bascu&ntilde;&aacut=
e;n
alleged only foreign injuries. Its holding set forth, in sum and substance,
&nbsp;[*810]&nbsp; the following rule: a foreign plaintiff who suffered an
&quot;economic loss&quot; due to a RICO violation cannot, absent extraordin=
ary
circumstances, allege a domestic injury. On appeal, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n
argues that the District Court erred in relying exclusively on his place of
residence to determine that he alleged only foreign injuries. He asserts th=
at,
because he alleged injuries to property located within the United States, he
satisfied civil RICO's domestic injury requirement. We agree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>While
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n claims a total loss of $64 million, he alleged, in p=
art,
the misappropriation of specifically identifiable property that was located=
 in
the United States when it was stolen. In particular, he alleged the
misappropriation of about $3 million held in a bank account in New York, and
the theft of bearer&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; shares, worth roughly $40 million, fro=
m a
safety deposit box also in New York. Because this property was located with=
in
the United States when it was stolen, we conclude that Bascu&ntilde;&aacute=
;n
has plausibly alleged a domestic injury notwithstanding the fact that he is=
 a
citizen and resident of Chile.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2YRP0000JKS40000J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52P0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To be clear, we do not hold that=
 a
plaintiff's place of residence is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>n=
ever</i>
relevant to the domestic injury inquiry required by </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52P0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>.</span></i><=
/a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Nor do we hold that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> contact=
 with
the United States suffices to make an injury domestic. Indeed, with respect=
 to
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's alleged injuries involving property located outsid=
e of
the United States, the fact that Elsaca or his co-defendants transferred th=
ose
stolen funds to (or through) the United States fails to transform an otherw=
ise
foreign injury into a domestic one. As noted, however, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute=
;n
has alleged two injuries that have a sufficient relationship to the United
States to qualify as &quot;domestic&quot; under the circumstances presented
here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REVERSE</b> the District Court's
order granting Elsaca's motion to dismiss, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-wei=
ght:
normal'>VACATE</b> the District Court's order denying Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;=
n's
motion for leave to file a second amended complaint, and we <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REMAND</b> the cause&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp;=
 to
the District Court for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Factual Overview</span></b></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52R0020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_10"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> the grant of a m=
otion
to dismiss, &quot;accept[ing] all factual allegations in the complaint as t=
rue
and draw[ing] inferences from those allegations in the light most favorable=
 to
the plaintiff.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn153' href=3D"#_ft=
n153"
name=3D"_ftnref153" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The relevant facts, as presente=
d in
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's Amended Complaint, are as follows:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n,
an only child, inherited a substantial fortune (the &quot;Estate&quot;) from
his parents after their deaths in the 1990s. The Estate includes various
companies and assets owned (directly and indirectly) by Bascu&ntilde;&aacut=
e;n,
including shares in Banco de Credito e Inversiones (&quot;BCI&quot;), the
third-largest bank in Chile, of which his father had been president.</span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn154' href=3D"#_ftn154" name=3D"_ftnref154" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> At
the time of his parents' death, and for years afterward,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, who was afflicted with a number of emotional and
physical ailments including depression and acquired immunodeficiency syndro=
me
(&quot;AIDS&quot;), was unable to manage his own finances. He relied instead
&nbsp;[*811]&nbsp; on a financial manager originally hired by his parents.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
1999, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n appointed his cousin Elsaca as a new financial
manager to oversee the Estate. Elsaca, who was eight years
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's senior, was a trusted family&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; memb=
er
with a master of business administration (&quot;MBA&quot;) degree from the
London School of Economics and extensive financial experience. According to
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's complaint, Elsaca was &quot;a licensed accountant,
prominent Chilean economist, and [formerly] the head of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Superintendencia de Valores y Seguros<=
/i> (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de Chile</i>), Chile's equivalent to t=
he
U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn155' href=3D"#_ftn155" name=3D"_ftnref155" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n ultimately granted Elsaca a broad power of attorney,
which included the power to engage in self-dealing transactions without
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's prior authorization.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Over
the next ten years, and until Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n fired him in 2010, Els=
aca
and his co-defendants</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn156' href=3D"#_f=
tn156"
name=3D"_ftnref156" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> allegedly engaged in a number of
fraudulent financial schemes against Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, illegally
transferring about $64 million from the Estate to entities and accounts und=
er
their own control. Specifically, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n claimed that Elsaca
perpetrated four schemes: (1) the New York Trust Account Scheme; (2) the
General Anacapri Investment Fraud Scheme; (3) the BCI Share Theft; and (4) =
the
Dividend Scheme. We describe each scheme in turn, focusing on the injuries
purportedly caused by each.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>The
New York Trust Account Scheme</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
1998, before he hired Elsaca to manage&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; his finances,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n established the so-called Afghan Trust with money fr=
om
the Estate. The Afghan Trust, a vehicle for Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's charit=
able
giving, was organized under the laws of the Cayman Islands and is administe=
red
by the New York office of J.P. Morgan. Importantly, its funds are held in N=
ew
York in a J.P. Morgan bank account.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2001, Elsaca, then in control of Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's finances, created=
 a
second trust: the Capri Star Trust. According to Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, the
Capri Star Trust&#8212;the stated purpose of which was also to finance
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's charitable goals&#8212;was entirely redundant of t=
he
already-existing Afghan Trust, and &quot;served no purpose other than to
generate sham fees&quot; for his cousin.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn157'
href=3D"#_ftn157" name=3D"_ftnref157" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The only difference between the=
 two
trusts was that the Capri Star Trust named Elsaca as an &quot;Investment
Advisor,&quot; which entitled him to receive an &quot;Investment Advisor&qu=
ot;
fee of 1% of the total value of the Capri Star Trust's assets each year,
notwithstanding the fact that UBS (not Elsaca) actively managed the Trust.<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn158' href=3D"#_ftn158" name=3D"_ftnref158" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
According to Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, Elsaca transferred funds from the Afgh=
an
Trust's New York bank account into the Capri Star Trust solely to earn sham
investment fees ($2.7 million in total), and to pay&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; sham
legal fees ($390,000 in total) to Jos&eacute; Pedro Silva Prado, Elsaca's
personal attorney and alleged co-conspirator.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*812]&nbsp;
Like the Afghan Trust, the Capri Star Trust was established in the Cayman
Islands and administered by the New York office of a banking and financial
services institution (in this instance, UBS AG).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>The
General Anacapri Investment Fraud Scheme</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
next purported scheme, which Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n dubbed the Anacapri
Investment Fraud Scheme, involved several byzantine sub-schemes and resulte=
d in
Elsaca, and others, illegally transferring at least $60 million from the Es=
tate
to accounts and entities under their control. Simply put, Elsaca created a
private investment fund in Chile&#8212;the Anacapri Fund (or &quot;the
Fund&quot;)&#8212;that took in a substantial amount of money from the Estate
and paid back very little: it returned to the Estate only $7.5 million of t=
he
approximately $48 million it had under management. According to
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, Elsaca and his associates simply pocketed most of t=
he
Estate assets controlled by the Anacapri Fund; that is, they transferred la=
rge
sums of money to themselves several times during the Fund's eight-year
existence and retained most of the assets after they
liquidated&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; the Fund in 2009.</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn159' href=3D"#_ftn159" name=3D"_ftnref159" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The Estate also paid Elsaca investment management fees amounting to
approximately 30% of the total value of the assets contributed to the Anaca=
pri
Fund, or about $16 million.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn160'
href=3D"#_ftn160" name=3D"_ftnref160" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Anacapri Fund was financed with assets contributed by three foreign entities
controlled by the Estate. The Amended Complaint does not describe where the
money belonging to those foreign entities was held. Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n =
did
allege that, after misappropriating assets from the Anacapri Fund, Elsaca
laundered those assets through bank accounts in New York and elsewhere.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>Theft
of BCI Shares</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>One
of the Anacapri sub-schemes&#8212;the BCI Share Theft&#8212;requires a some=
what
more detailed description. As mentioned above, the Estate included a 1.47%
stake in BCI. An entity called Tarascona Corp. (&quot;Tarascona&quot;) dire=
ctly
controlled the shares comprising that stake, and Tarascona was itself wholly
owned by Hofstra Corp. (&quot;Hofstra&quot;), an entity belonging to the
Estate. Both Tarascona and Hofstra were corporations organized under the la=
ws
of the British Virgin Islands (&quot;BVI&quot;) and Hofstra's interest in
Tarascona was represented by bearer shares stored in a J.P. Morgan safety
deposit box in New York.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2007, Elsaca, or an&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; agent acting on his behalf, traveled =
to
New York and, using the authority granted him in the power of attorney, rem=
oved
the bearer shares from the safety deposit box. Elsaca then arranged for a
Panamanian law firm to cancel Hofstra's bearer shares and re-register them =
in
the name of a new entity created and controlled by him, Nueva T Corp. (or
&quot;New Tarascona&quot;), a BVI corporation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn161' href=3D"#_ftn161" name=3D"_ftnref161" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This maneuver effectively transferred control of Tarascona and its only ass=
et,
the BCI shares, from the Estate to Elsaca.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*813]&nbsp;
At the last step of the BCI Share Theft sub-scheme, Elsaca caused the Anaca=
pri
Fund to use Estate assets to purchase New Tarascona, and thus (re)purchase =
the
BCI shares, from him for $43 million.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>D. <u>The
Dividend Scheme</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged that Elsaca stole over $1.8 million in divid=
end
payments earned by the Estate on its BCI shares. Between 2007 and 2010, a
Tarascona account held at BCI in Chile received over $3.5 million in divide=
nd
payments. Elsaca diverted a portion of those funds from the Tarascona accou=
nt
to his personal investment accounts at Morgan Stanley in New York. This
purported scheme was rather crude by comparison with the others: Elsaca
withdrew funds derived from the&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; dividend payments by writ=
ing
checks out of the Tarascona account, he endorsed those checks in his own na=
me,
and then he deposited the funds into his own accounts.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Procedural History</span></b=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
March 17, 2015, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n filed an initial complaint, followed=
 by
an amended complaint on August 24, 2015, accusing Elsaca and his co-defenda=
nts
of violating RICO by continuously and systematically breaching the mail fra=
ud,
wire fraud, bank fraud, anti-money-laundering, and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Travel Act</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
statutes through their actions involving the Estate. On December 22, 2015, =
the
defendants moved to dismiss Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's action on several grou=
nds,
including for failure to state a claim upon which relief can be granted und=
er </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52R0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Six months later, after the Dist=
rict
Court heard oral argument on the defendants' fully-briefed motion, the Supr=
eme
Court issued its decision in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Na=
bisco</i>,
which for the first time required a plaintiff bringing a private action und=
er
RICO to allege a &quot;domestic injury.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn162' href=3D"#_ftn162" name=3D"_ftnref162" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
response to the Supreme Court's decision, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n sought lea=
ve
to file a second amended complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
District Court denied as futile his motion for leave to amend and
simultaneously granted the defendants' pending motion to
dismiss,&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; solely on the ground that Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n
failed to meet civil RICO's new &quot;domestic injury&quot; requirement.</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK30010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In determining whether
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's complaint set forth a domestic injury, the District
Court characterized &quot;[t]he RICO injury alleged [as] an economic loss of
approximately $64 million.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn163'
href=3D"#_ftn163" name=3D"_ftnref163" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It did not consid=
er
whether each of the four fraudulent schemes alleged by Bascu&ntilde;&aacute=
;n
caused separately cognizable RICO injuries to property or business, but
concluded instead that Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n suffered a single &quot;econo=
mic
loss.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn164' href=3D"#_ftn164"
name=3D"_ftnref164" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK30050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Then, in order to decide where in
geographic terms it should locate that &quot;economic loss,&quot; the Distr=
ict
Court drew an analogy to the tort claim accrual rules under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-08C1-6RDJ-8458-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 202 of
the New York Civil Practice Law and Rules</span></i></a><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;NYCPLR&quo=
t;),
New York State's so-called &quot;borrowing statute.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn165' href=3D"#_ftn165" name=3D"_ftnref165" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
observed that, &quot;[w]hen applying &nbsp;[*814]&nbsp; this statute to
determine where an economic injury accrued, courts typically ask two
common-sense questions: [1] who became poorer, and [2] where did they become
poorer.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn166' href=3D"#_ftn166"
name=3D"_ftnref166" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> That inquiry,
according to the District Court, &quot;usually focuses upon where the econo=
mic
impact of the injury was ultimately felt,&quot; which &quot;is normally the
state of plaintiffs['] residence.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:f=
tn167'
href=3D"#_ftn167" name=3D"_ftnref167" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF84Y0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In reliance on that analogy, the
District Court held that &quot;[a]ll of the funds at issue . . . were
purportedly owned by Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n, and thus, he is the person that
ultimately suffered the loss. And as a Chilean citizen and resident, he suf=
fered
the losses in Chile.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn168'
href=3D"#_ftn168" name=3D"_ftnref168" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It then dismissed
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's RICO action for failure to allege a domestic injur=
y.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8500010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8500030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The District Court favored this
residency-based test primarily because it &quot;focuse[d] on the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>plaintiff</i> and where the alleged in=
jury
was <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>suffered</i>,&quot; as opposed =
to
focusing on the defendant's conduct.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn1=
69'
href=3D"#_ftn169" name=3D"_ftnref169" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It read <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nabisco</i> as standing for the
proposition that &quot;the location where the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>plaintiff suffered</i> the alleged injury dictates whether the
plaintiff may pursue a private right of action under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn170' href=3D"#_ftn170" name=3D"_ftnref170" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
sole question on appeal, subject to our <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>de
novo</i> review, is whether Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n plausibly alleged &quot;=
a <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>domestic</i> injury to business or
property.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn171' href=3D"#_ftn171"
name=3D"_ftnref171" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>23</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> No Court of Appea=
ls
has&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; yet to consider how to determine whether a civil RICO
injury is &quot;domestic&quot; or &quot;foreign.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
explained more fully below, we hold, as an initial matter, that where a civ=
il
RICO plaintiff alleges separate schemes that harmed materially distinct
interests to property or business, each harm&#8212;that is to say, each
&quot;injury&quot;&#8212;should be analyzed separately for purposes of this
inquiry. Next, we hold, contrary to the District Court, that a plaintiff wh=
o is
a foreign resident may nevertheless allege a civil RICO injury that is
domestic. At a minimum, when a foreign plaintiff maintains tangible propert=
y in
the United States, the misappropriation of that property constitutes a dome=
stic
injury. With respect to some of the schemes in the Amended Complaint,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n has alleged the misappropriation of tangible property
located in the United States and thus, to that extent, has &nbsp;[*815]&nbs=
p;
alleged a domestic injury. We therefore reverse the judgment of the District
Court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. RICO's Statutory Framework</s=
pan></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J10040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK20010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In enacting RICO, which is Title=
 IX
of the Organized Crime Control Act of 1970, Congress &quot;establish[ed] new
penal prohibitions, and . . . provid[ed] enhanced sanctions and new
remedies&quot; in order to thwart and punish individuals seeking to&nbsp;[*=
*17]&nbsp;
use illegal means and ends &quot;to infiltrate and corrupt legitimate
business.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn172' href=3D"#_ftn172"
name=3D"_ftnref172" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>24</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Although commonly
associated with the national effort to eradicate organized crime in America,
&quot;Congress drafted RICO broadly enough to encompass a wide range of
criminal activity, taking many different forms and likely to attract a broad
array of perpetrators operating in many different ways.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn173' href=3D"#_ftn173" name=3D"_ftnref173" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>25</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK20030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK20050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Specifically, RICO created
&quot;four new criminal offenses involving the activities of organized crim=
inal
groups in relation to an enterprise.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn174' href=3D"#_ftn174" name=3D"_ftnref174" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>26</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Those offenses are &quot;founded on the concept of racketeering activity,&q=
uot;
which &quot;[t]he statute defines . . . to encompass dozens of state and
federal offenses, known in RICO parlance as predicates.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn175' href=3D"#_ftn175" name=3D"_ftnref175" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>27</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> A
party commits a RICO violation when he engages in a &quot;pattern of
racketeering activity&#8212;a series of related predicates that together
demonstrate the existence or threat of continued criminal
activity&quot;&#8212;in order to &quot;infiltrate, control, or operate a[n]
enterprise which is engaged in, or the activities of which affect, intersta=
te
or foreign commerce.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn176'
href=3D"#_ftn176" name=3D"_ftnref176" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>28</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Those
four specific prohibitions, which are set forth in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962 =
of
RICO</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, were authoritatively summarized as follows:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
a)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
makes it&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; unlawful to invest income derived from a pattern=
 of
racketeering activity in an enterprise. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J20020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
makes it unlawful to acquire or maintain an interest in an enterprise throu=
gh a
pattern of racketeering activity. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
makes it unlawful for a person employed by or associated with an enterprise=
 to
conduct the enterprise's affairs through a pattern of racketeering activity.
Finally, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
d)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
makes it unlawful to conspire to violate any of the other three prohibition=
s.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn177' href=3D"#_ftn177" name=3D"_ftnref177" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>29</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1963(=
a)
of RICO</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> makes any violation of those four prohibitions subject =
to
criminal penalties. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sections 1964=
(a)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(b)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
authorize the Attorney General of the United States to bring civil proceedi=
ngs
to enforce those prohibitions.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn178'
href=3D"#_ftn178" name=3D"_ftnref178" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>30</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> And </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_10"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)
of RICO</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, which is the focus of this appeal, permits &quot;[a]ny
person injured in his business or property by reason of a violation of </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
to bring &nbsp;[*816]&nbsp; a private civil suit in federal district court =
and
authorizes the recovery of treble damages, attorney's fees, and costs.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn179' href=3D"#_ftn179" name=3D"_ftnref179" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>31</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. RICO and Issues of
Extraterritoriality</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>The
Supreme Court's Decision in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nab=
isco</i></u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J20040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52S0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>RJR Nabisco</i>, the Supreme Court considered whether the RICO stat=
ute
applies extraterritorially. The Court determined, as an initial matter, that
&quot;[t]he question of RICO's extraterritorial application really involves=
 two
questions&quot;: (1) &quot;do RICO's substantive prohibitions, contained in=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
apply to conduct that occurs in foreign countries?&quot; and (2) &quot;does
RICO's private right of action, contained in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
apply to injuries that are suffered in foreign countries?&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn180' href=3D"#_ftn180" name=3D"_ftnref180" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>32</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
answering both questions, the Court applied the presumption against
extraterritoriality:&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; a canon of statutory construction th=
at
requires courts to construe federal laws &quot;to have only domestic
application&quot; unless there is a clear and unmistakable indication that
Congress intended the law to apply abroad.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn181' href=3D"#_ftn181" name=3D"_ftnref181" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>33</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52S0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>With respect to the first questi=
on,
the Court held that &quot;RICO applies to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>some</i>
foreign racketeering activity,&quot; explaining that &quot;[a] violation of=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
may be based on a pattern of racketeering that includes predicate offenses
committed abroad, provided that each of those offenses violates a predicate
statute that is itself extraterritorial.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn182' href=3D"#_ftn182" name=3D"_ftnref182" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>34</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52T0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On the second question, the one
directly relevant to this appeal, the Court concluded that </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_10"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the civil RICO provision, &quot;[i]rrespective of any extraterritorial
application of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,&quot;
does not overcome the presumption against extraterritoriality and,
consequently, a plaintiff must allege a domestic injury.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn183' href=3D"#_ftn183" name=3D"_ftnref183" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>35</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52T0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8510030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In answering the second question,
the Court made a point of separately applying the presumption against
extraterritoriality to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
It concluded that the presumption against extraterritoriality applies not j=
ust
to &quot;the question of what conduct falls within a statute's purview&quot;
but also to a statute's creation of a private right of action, because
&quot;providing a private&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; civil remedy for foreign conduct
creates a potential for international friction beyond that presented by mer=
ely
applying U.S. substantive law to that foreign conduct.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn184' href=3D"#_ftn184" name=3D"_ftnref184" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>36</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
For instance, drawing a comparison &nbsp;[*817]&nbsp; to antitrust law's tr=
eble
damages remedy, the Court noted that it had previously been told by foreign
governments that applying U.S. civil remedies to foreign conduct &quot;would
unjustifiably permit [foreign] citizens to bypass their own [nation's] less
generous remedial schemes.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn185'
href=3D"#_ftn185" name=3D"_ftnref185" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>37</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Nevertheless, the
Supreme Court stated that the &quot;domestic injury&quot; requirement of </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&#8212;more
specifically, the fact that RICO's private right of action lacks language
expressly providing recovery for injuries to foreign persons&#8212;&quot;do=
es
not mean that foreign plaintiffs may not sue under RICO.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn186' href=3D"#_ftn186" name=3D"_ftnref186" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>38</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8510050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Ultimately, because the plaintif=
fs
had stipulated that they waived their damages claims for any domestic injur=
ies,
the Court did not explain how to identify a &quot;domestic injury&quot; and
noted only that &quot;[t]he application of this rule in any given case will=
 not
always be self-evident, as disputes may arise as to whether a particular
alleged injury is 'foreign' or 'domestic.'&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn187' href=3D"#_ftn187" name=3D"_ftnref187" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>39</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>The
&quot;Domestic Injury&quot; Determination</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB32B30000JKS40000K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK60020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The guidance from&nbsp;[**22]&nb=
sp;
the Supreme Court in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK60010000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
regarding what constitutes a domestic injury is admittedly sparse. The Court
held that &quot;[s]ection 1964(c) requires <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc=
5_9"></a>a
civil RICO plaintiff to allege and prove a domestic injury to business or
property,&quot; but it did not indicate what factors a court should examine=
 to
determine whether a plaintiff's alleged injury is foreign or domestic.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn188' href=3D"#_ftn188" name=3D"_ftnref188" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>40</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> We
will now turn to the schemes alleged here and their accompanying injuries.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK60040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8520010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8520030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8520050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Before doing so, however, we note
that the District Court erred in its preliminary approach to discerning
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's injuries. The District Court's generic
characterization of Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's alleged injuries as &quot;an
economic loss of approximately $64 million&quot; is not helpful in determin=
ing
whether, for purposes of the presumption against extraterritoriality, his
particular injuries were foreign or domestic.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn189' href=3D"#_ftn189" name=3D"_ftnref189" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>41</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
All civil RICO injuries are, by the terms of the statute itself, economic
losses of one kind or another. A plaintiff bringing a civil RICO claim must
allege an injury to his &quot;business or property&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn190' href=3D"#_ftn190" name=3D"_ftnref190" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>42</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
he cannot, for example, recover for &quot;personal injuries.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn191' href=3D"#_ftn191" name=3D"_ftnref191" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>43</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> As
we have explained, &quot;[t]he requirement that the injury be to the
plaintiff's business or property means&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; that the plaintiff
must show a proprietary type of damage,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn192' href=3D"#_ftn192" name=3D"_ftnref192" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>44</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
or, in other words, an &quot;economic injury.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn193' href=3D"#_ftn193" name=3D"_ftnref193" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>45</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2J030000JKS40000C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J30020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6_9"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to determine where the economic losses alleged by a civil RICO plaint=
iff
are located geographically, courts must examine more closely the specific t=
ype
of injuries alleged. It is not enough simply to label the business or prope=
rty
injuries, tautologically, as &quot;economic&quot; injuries. Because, as the
Supreme Court explained, &nbsp;[*818]&nbsp; &quot;[t]he application of th[e
domestic injury] rule in any given case will not always be self-evident,&qu=
ot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn194' href=3D"#_ftn194" name=3D"_ftnref194" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>46</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
this analysis will, as a general matter, depend on the particular facts all=
eged
in each case. In addition, if a plaintiff alleges more than one injury, cou=
rts
should separately analyze each injury to determine whether any of the injur=
ies
alleged are domestic. If one of the alleged injuries is domestic, then the
plaintiff may recover for that particular injury even if all of the other
injuries are foreign.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn195' href=3D"#_f=
tn195"
name=3D"_ftnref195" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>47</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we begin our analysis with the schemes that are easiest to analyze: the
Dividend Scheme and the General Anacapri Investment Fraud Scheme. We then
examine the more complex issues raised by the New York Trust
Account&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; Scheme and the BCI Share Theft, in turn.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>Elsaca's
Alleged Use of Domestic Bank Accounts</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
conclude that both the Dividend Scheme and the General Anacapri Investment
Fraud Scheme failed to allege a &quot;domestic&quot; injury. With respect to
the Dividend Scheme, Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged that Elsaca stole funds
that were legally owned by a foreign corporation (and beneficially owned by
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n himself, a foreign citizen and resident) and held in=
 a
foreign bank account. The only domestic element alleged is that Elsaca
transferred these foreign funds to his own accounts in New York. Similarly,=
 the
only domestic elements of the General Anacapri Investment Fraud Scheme pert=
ain
to Elsaca's laundering of stolen money using bank accounts in the United St=
ates
and elsewhere.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn196' href=3D"#_ftn196"
name=3D"_ftnref196" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>48</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB34NY0000JKS40000M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J30040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>That is not enough to allege a
domestic injury. Indeed, one could argue that such allegations are insuffic=
ient
under a straightforward application of </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J30030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
as allegations similar to Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's were levied in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nabisco</i> itself.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn197' href=3D"#_ftn197" name=3D"_ftnref197" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>49</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n does not even expressly argue that transferring mone=
y to
(or through) the &nbsp;[*819]&nbsp; United States makes his alleged injuries
&quot;domestic.&quot; Instead, he seems to argue only that &quot;where the
alleged RICO injury&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; is the misappropriation of property
located within the territorial jurisdiction of the United States, [plaintif=
fs]
have plainly alleged a domestic RICO injury to their business or
property.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn198' href=3D"#_ftn198"
name=3D"_ftnref198" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>50</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB372R0000JKS40000N"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J40050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We ultimately conclude that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_8"></a>an injury to tangible property is gen=
erally
a domestic injury only if the property was physically located in the United
States, and that a defendant's use of the U.S.&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; financial
system to conceal or effectuate his tort does not, on its own, turn an
otherwise foreign injury into a domestic one. We thus hold that the use of =
bank
accounts located within the United States to facilitate or conceal the thef=
t of
property located outside of the United States does not, on its own, establi=
sh a
domestic injury. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK40020000400"></a>To hold
otherwise would subvert the intended effect of the &quot;domestic injury&qu=
ot;
requirement articulated by the </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J40040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Court. Because of the primacy of American banking and financial institution=
s,
particularly those in New York, a transnational RICO case is often likely to
involve in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>some</i> way, however
insignificant, financial transactions with American institutions. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK40020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK40040000400"></a>Holding that a <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>defendant's</i> mere use of a domestic=
 bank
account could transform an otherwise foreign injury into a domestic one mig=
ht
well effectively eliminate the effect of the domestic injury requirement in=
 a
large number of cases.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn199' href=3D"#_=
ftn199"
name=3D"_ftnref199" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>51</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In addition, and
importantly, the only domestic connections alleged here were acts of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>the defendant</i>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK40040000400_2"></a>Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n and=
 his
relevant property always remained abroad, and these injuries did not arise =
from
any preexisting connection between Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n and the United
States.&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; To allow such a plaintiff to recover treble damag=
es
would thus &quot;unjustifiably permit [foreign] citizens to bypass their own
[nation's] less generous remedial schemes.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn200' href=3D"#_ftn200" name=3D"_ftnref200" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>52</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
at least insofar as they are pleaded in the Amended Complaint, these schemes
fail to allege a domestic injury.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn201'
href=3D"#_ftn201" name=3D"_ftnref201" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>53</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[*820]&nbsp; D. <u>Foreign-Owned Property Located Within=
 the
United States</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
New York Trust Account Scheme and the BCI Share Theft, on the other hand,
allege that certain property&#8212;although belonging to a foreign
owner&#8212;was located within the United States when it was stolen. As
explained below, we conclude that the District Court erred in holding that
these schemes caused only foreign injuries.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
The New York Trust Account Scheme</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK50020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK50040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We start with the New York Trust
Account Scheme, in which Elsaca misappropriated funds held in a New York ba=
nk
account at J.P. Morgan. This alleged injury is an injury to property; money=
, as
has been observed in a variety of other contexts, is property.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn202' href=3D"#_ftn202" name=3D"_ftnref202" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>54</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Moreover, this injury is analogous to an injury to tangible property, by wh=
ich
we mean property that can be fairly said to exist in a precise location.</s=
pan><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn203' href=3D"#_ftn203" name=3D"_ftnref203" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>55</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
While money is, of course,&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; ultimately fungible, the money
allegedly stolen as a result of this scheme was situated in a specific
geographic location at the time of injury such that we can treat it as tang=
ible
property for purposes of this inquiry.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2MKV0000JKS40000D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK70010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2R0V0000JKS40000F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK70030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Accordingly, we consider this sc=
heme
to have involved the misappropriation of tangible property located within t=
he
United States.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn204' href=3D"#_ftn204"
name=3D"_ftnref204" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>56</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_8"></a>Where the injury is to tangible prope=
rty,
we conclude that, absent some extraordinary circumstance, the injury is
domestic if the plaintiff's property &nbsp;[*821]&nbsp; was located in the
United States when it was stolen or harmed, even if the plaintiff himself
resides abroad.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn205' href=3D"#_ftn205"
name=3D"_ftnref205" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>57</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Several
considerations support such a rule.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB39740000JKS40000P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK70050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB3FPH0000JKS40000S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52W0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52W0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>First and foremost, this rule
accords with </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK70040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and furthers the principles animating the presumption against
extraterritoriality. The principal justification for the domestic injury
requirement, according to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nabis=
co</i>,
is the need to avoid &quot;international friction.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn206' href=3D"#_ftn206" name=3D"_ftnref206" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>58</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Foreign persons and entities that own private property located within the
United States expect that our laws will protect them in the event of damage=
 to
that property. That modest expectation is entirely justified, especially wh=
en
we consider that a foreign resident's&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; property located in=
 the
United States is otherwise subject to all of the regulations imposed on pri=
vate
property by American state and federal law. We see no reason, in the text of
the RICO statute or in the </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52W0040000=
400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52W0020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2N1RK70050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G28T52W0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
decision, why we should exclude private property of this kind from the reme=
dial
benefits conferred by RICO's private right of action.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8540030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2SF8540050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Few things could be more destruc=
tive
to the comity underlying the international system than legal rules that
penalize international economic cooperation and deter foreign investment si=
mply
because such activity involves &quot;foreign&quot; counterparties. Indeed, =
the
majority in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RJR Nabisco</i> took ca=
re to
make plain that its opinion should <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
not</i>
be taken to &quot;mean that foreign plaintiffs may not sue under RICO.&quot=
;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn207' href=3D"#_ftn207" name=3D"_ftnref207" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>59</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
That, of course, is exactly the effect of the District Court's residency-ba=
sed
test. Our rule, on the other hand, does not discriminate against foreigners=
 who
own property in the United States: it ensures that both foreign and domestic
plaintiffs can obtain civil RICO's remedy for damage to their property, but
only if their property was located within the territorial jurisdiction of t=
he
United States.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn208' href=3D"#_ftn208"
name=3D"_ftnref208" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>60</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In so doing, it
protects the interest each&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; sovereign has in regulating the
private property situated in its own &nbsp;[*822]&nbsp; territory without
extending the reach of American law or discriminating against foreign
plaintiffs.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn209' href=3D"#_ftn209"
name=3D"_ftnref209" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>61</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Accordingly, our
holding <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>reduces</i> the possibility=
 of
international discord.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
also draw guidance from the approach taken by the Restatement (Second) of
Conflicts of Laws in resolving substantive choice-of-law issues in the cont=
ext
of trans-jurisdictional torts, specifically torts involving &quot;Injuries =
to
Tangible Things.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn210' href=3D"#_=
ftn210"
name=3D"_ftnref210" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>62</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Second
Restatement's presumptive choice-of-law rule regarding &quot;Injuries to
Tangible Things&quot; directs that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_8"></a=
>&quot;the
local law of the state <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>where the in=
jury
occurred to the tangible thing</i> will usually be applied to determine most
issues involving the tort . . . on the rare occasions when conduct and the
resulting injury to the thing occur in different states.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn211' href=3D"#_ftn211" name=3D"_ftnref211" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>63</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Our holding, treating injuries involving tangible property located within t=
he
jurisdiction of the United States as &quot;domestic,&quot; accords with that
presumptive rule.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
consider that rule to be an appropriate reference for determining the locat=
ion
of Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's alleged&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; injuries because the
interests considered by the Second Restatement mirror the concerns underlyi=
ng
the presumption against extraterritoriality. The Second Restatement explains
that &quot;[t]he rights <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc10_8"></a>and liabi=
lities
of the parties with respect to an issue in tort are determined by the local=
 law
of the state which, with respect to that issue, has the most significant
relationship to the occurrence and the parties under the principles stated =
in
&sect; 6.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn212' href=3D"#_ftn212"
name=3D"_ftnref212" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>64</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Those principles
include, among others, &quot;the needs of the . . . international system[
],&quot; &quot;the relevant policies of other interested states and the
relative interests of those states in the determination of the particular
issue,&quot; and &quot;the protection of justified expectations.&quot;</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn213' href=3D"#_ftn213" name=3D"_ftnref213" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>65</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Elsaca's
principal counterarguments are unpersuasive.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First,
Elsaca argues that our rule is inappropriate here because
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's alleged injuries are a special type of property
injury: an injury to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>financial prop=
erty</i>.
He argues that &quot;different rules may apply in cases involving damage to=
 a
business or non-financial property, such as real-estate or chattels,&quot; =
than
apply when the alleged damage is to financial property.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn214' href=3D"#_ftn214" name=3D"_ftnref214" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>66</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This suggested distinction between financial and
non-financial&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; property is unhelpful in the circumstances
presented here. The fact that the property at issue was money, rather than =
real
property or chattels, is of no moment. Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n plausibly all=
eged
that the funds stolen were held in a specific and identifiable bank account=
 in
New York. In that sense, the Afghan Trust resembled real property or chatte=
ls
in the &nbsp;[*823]&nbsp; only way relevant to our decision&#8212;that is, =
the
trust funds could be located within the jurisdiction of the United States at
all times relevant to the complaint. Elsaca cannot change that critical fact
simply by labeling Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's injury &quot;financial.&quot;</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2T6P0000JKS40000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J50020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Next, Elsaca relies on <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Atlantica Holdings</i> to argue that &=
quot;a
residence-based rule [ ] conforms with this Court's approach to determining
where a plaintiff has allegedly suffered a financial injury for purposes of
other federal statutes.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn215'
href=3D"#_ftn215" name=3D"_ftnref215" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>67</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Based on the
circumstances presented here, however, we hold that our <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>Atlantica Holdings</i> decision is inapposite.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn216' href=3D"#_ftn216" name=3D"_ftnref216" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>68</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J50040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB2WKC0000JKS40000H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J60010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In that case, U.S. plaintiffs
brought securities fraud claims against entities from Kazakhstan, alleging =
that
the defendants drastically misrepresented the value of shares that the
plaintiffs had purchased.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn217'
href=3D"#_ftn217" name=3D"_ftnref217" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>69</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In considering
whether the plaintiffs had alleged&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; a direct effect for
purposes of the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Foreign Sover=
eign
Immunities Act</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, we relied there, too, on choice-of-law rules for torts
(albeit the First Restatement), and held that there was a &quot;direct
effect&quot; in the United States because that was where the plaintiffs
resided.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn218' href=3D"#_ftn218"
name=3D"_ftnref218" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>70</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Although we looke=
d to
the plaintiffs' residence in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Altant=
ica
Holdings</i>, we do not think a similar method is appropriate here because =
of
the different property interests at issue. The injury alleged in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Atlantica Holdings</i> involved the
diminished value of ownership interest in a company, for which the clear
locational nexus was the shareholder's place of residence. On the contrary,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n alleged an injury&#8212;the theft of specific assets
from bank accounts located in New York&#8212;for which the locus of the inj=
ury
is clearly domestic.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Elsaca argues that conflict-of-laws rules, of which New York's borrowing
statute is a part and on which the District Court relied, provide an
appropriate framework for analyzing civil RICO's domestic injury requirement
precisely because &quot;they serve the same policies as the presumption aga=
inst
extraterritoriality.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn219'
href=3D"#_ftn219" name=3D"_ftnref219" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>71</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We agree with the
general proposition that the &quot;most important function&quot; of
conflict-of-laws&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; rules, like the presumption against
extraterritoriality, &quot;is to make the interstate and international syst=
ems
work well,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn220' href=3D"#_ftn220"
name=3D"_ftnref220" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>72</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> but the particular
conflict-of-law rule relied on by Elsaca and the District Court is inapposi=
te.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J60030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2HM6J60050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>New York's &quot;borrowing
statute&quot;&#8212;part of that state's choice-of-law rules regarding claim
accrual&#8212;is designed to protect <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>defendants</i>
from suits that would be barred by shorter statutes of limitations in other
jurisdictions where a plaintiff could have brought suit.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn221' href=3D"#_ftn221" name=3D"_ftnref221" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>73</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
does not account for the interest a foreign sovereign may have in the
application (or not) of a particular jurisdiction's substantive or remedial
law.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn222' href=3D"#_ftn222" name=3D"_f=
tnref222"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>74</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> In contrast, the substantive choice-of-laws
&nbsp;[*824]&nbsp; rules set forth in the Second Restatement, the goal of w=
hich
is to select the law with the most significant relationship to the legal is=
sues
and parties before the court, consider a range of factors to identify the
appropriate law.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn223' href=3D"#_ftn223"
name=3D"_ftnref223" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>75</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Those
conflict-of-laws rules <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>do</i> prote=
ct the
interest of foreign jurisdictions. That is, in part, why we considered the
substantive conflict-of-laws rule applicable to &quot;Injuries to Tangible
Things&quot; to be helpful in determining whether Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's
alleged injuries were foreign or domestic.&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn224' href=3D"#_ftn224" name=3D"_ftnref224" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>76</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5DFBFB3M8Y0000JKS40000V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK70020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To be clear, we do not hold that=
 a
plaintiff's place of residence is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>n=
ever</i>
relevant to the domestic injury inquiry required by </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5R1GH6G2D6NK70010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5K27-42B1-F04K-F1B2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>RJR Nabisco</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. A
plaintiff's residence may often be relevant&#8212;perhaps even
dispositive&#8212;in determining whether certain types of business or prope=
rty
injuries constitute a domestic injury. But with respect to the particular t=
ype
of property injury alleged here&#8212;the misappropriation of
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's trust funds from a specific bank account located in
the United States&#8212;we conclude that the location of the property and n=
ot
the residency of the plaintiff is the dispositive factor.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2.
The BCI Share Theft</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
light of the foregoing, the question presented with respect to the BCI Share
Theft is whether the misappropriation of the bearer shares, located in a sa=
fety
deposit box in New York, also constitutes the misappropriation of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>tangible</i> property. We conclude it =
does.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Importantly,
Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n does not allege that Elsaca's RICO activity caused a
drop in the economic value of these shares.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn225' href=3D"#_ftn225" name=3D"_ftnref225" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>77</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> He
contends instead that these shares were, in effect, stolen&#8212;<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>physically</i> stolen&#8212;from a saf=
ety
deposit box in New York. Bearer shares are a form of stock &quot;that has no
recorded ownership&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; information.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn226' href=3D"#_ftn226" name=3D"_ftnref226" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>78</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> As
a result, &quot;the physical bearer of the stock certificate is presumed to=
 be
the owner.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn227' href=3D"#_ftn227"
name=3D"_ftnref227" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>79</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It is thus fair to
say that, by fraudulently taking possession of the bearer shares (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>i.e.</i>, by taking them from the New =
York
safety deposit box), Elsaca defrauded Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n out of his
interest in BCI. At the motion to dismiss stage, that is enough to plausibly
allege an injury to tangible property within the United States, and thus a
&quot;domestic injury&quot; within the meaning of the civil RICO statute.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I60F218K2HM6B10010000400"></a><b style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-weight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
summarize, we hold:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(1)
The fact that a defendant used bank accounts located within the United Stat=
es
to facilitate or conceal the theft of property located outside of the United
States, on its own, does not establish that a civil RICO plaintiff has suff=
ered
a domestic injury;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(2)
The misappropriation of tangible property located in the United States, on =
the
other hand, causes a &quot;domestic injury&quot; for purposes of civil RICO,
even if the owner of the property resides abroad. Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's
alleged injuries involving the misappropriation of trust funds held in a ba=
nk
account in &nbsp;[*825]&nbsp; New York, and the theft of bearer shares from=
 a
safety deposit box also located in New&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; York, were domesti=
c.
The District Court thus erred in dismissing Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's Amended
Complaint on the grounds that he alleged only foreign injuries;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(3)
Because it was based on the same legal error, the District Court's order
denying Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's motion for leave to file a second amended
complaint must be vacated. In the event that Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n seeks l=
eave
to file another amended complaint, the District Court should determine, in
light of our ruling, whether any such amendment is futile.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal11 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the reasons set out above, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REVE=
RSE</b>
the District Court's order granting Elsaca's motion to dismiss, we <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>VACATE</b> the District Court's order
denying Bascu&ntilde;&aacute;n's motion for leave to file a second amended
complaint, and we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REMAND</b> the c=
ause
for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

