<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span>EXCLUSION OF EXHIBIT G </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
indictment alleged as the third predicate act that Braz's insurance claim of
February, 1980 for the theft of sheepskin coats from the store was fraudule=
nt
because the coats were not delivered until&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; September, 198=
0. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ01S0000400"></a>In response to evidence intr=
oduced
by the government in support of its argument, Braz sought to introduce
defendant's exhibit G, the report of the insurance adjuster. The report sta=
ted
in relevant part: &quot;We questioned a Mr. David Belsky of the firm Damer
Sheepskin Fashions [the supplier] who advised that the goods were delivered=
 to
the assured on January 19, 1980, but to date they had not been paid for by =
the
assured.&quot; The district court held that the document was not admissible
under either </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed.R.Evid.
803(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> as a business record, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed.R.Evid.
803(8)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'> as a public record, or </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed.R.Evid.
803(24)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> or </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6D9P-GMB3-RRYK-4349-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>804(b)(5)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which provide generally for admission of documents not covered by other
exceptions &quot;but having equivalent circumstantial guarantees of
trustworthiness.&quot; We conclude that this exclusion was not, as Braz arg=
ues,
an abuse of discretion. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn233' href=3D"=
#_ftn233"
name=3D"_ftnref233" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ01R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9KR0-001B-K41S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Salvador</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9KR0-001B-K41S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 820 F.2d 55=
8,
561</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (2d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert.
denied</i>, <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ01T0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>484 U.S. 966, 108 S. Ct. 458, 98 L. Ed=
. 2d
398 (1987)</i>. Nor was the decision denying Braz's Rule 33 motion -- for a=
 new
trial based on the document's exclusion or, in the alternative, for a heari=
ng
as to its reliability -- &nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; reversible error. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
Braz speaks of the entire document, the real issue is the admissibility not=
 of
exhibit G itself, but of the statement of Belsky it contains. Therefore, to
prevail on his argument, Braz must establish not only that the report falls
within an exception to the hearsay rule, but also that Belsky's statement is
covered by such an exception. Braz has failed to do the latter. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ01X0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0270000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Although the adjuster's report m=
ight
otherwise qualify as a business record within the meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 803(6)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
Belsky's statement does not satisfy the rule's requirements because there w=
as
no showing that he had a duty to report the information he was quoted as ha=
ving
given. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ02K0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ01W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0T30-001B-K33V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Meyers</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0T30-001B-K33V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 847 F.2d 14=
08,
1412 (9th Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding exhibit
summarizing&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; surveillance activity was admissible because
there was no evidence report &quot;improperly incorporated the statements of
