ny,
stating&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; that the proofs of loss and supporting documentat=
ion
were insufficient, conflicted with, rather than furthered, the scheme to
defraud the insurance company. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0BK0000400=
"></a>The
court stated, in words fully applicable to the case at hand, that the defen=
dant
&quot;was continuing to try to collect, and these letters helped give him
another chance.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>
Sapperstein's letter similarly gave Braz and Bortnovsky another chance. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0BK0000400_2"></a>This letter is not qualita=
tively
different from that of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Draiman</i> =
simply
because the &quot;other chance&quot; in this case required the defendants to
pursue a new avenue of recovery. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf=
.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0BJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8FJ0-0039-N0WK-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Lane</span></i></a><a
