<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;
color:black'>&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; E.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;
</span>SENTENCING </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Bortnovsky
and Braz claim that their terms of imprisonment for mail fraud, probation, =
and
restitution do not conform with &nbsp;[*42]&nbsp; the terms of applicable
statutes. Braz also contends that the district court abused its discretion =
when
it imposed a longer sentence on him after this trial than after the first. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
government concedes that Bortnovsky and Braz's sentences for mail fraud
exceeded the term permitted by law. In addition, the probationary term for =
both
defendants does not conform to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H1FP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
3651</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (repealed effective Nov. 1, 1986), which requires that,=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_9"></a>where a probationary term follows
incarceration, the term of incarceration may not exceed six months.
Accordingly, the case is remanded to the district court to correct the term=
s of
the sentences for mail fraud and to review the periods of probation. </span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
defendants, each of whom was ordered to pay restitution of $ 5,225, a sum
representing half the amount Braz claimed as losses sustained in the burgla=
ry
of February 1, 1980, also contend that the sentence of restitution is inval=
id
because&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; the Victim and Witness Protection Act (&quot;the
Act&quot;) pursuant to which it was imposed, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H1FW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 3663-64</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, applies only to crimes committ=
ed
after January 1, 1983. The government answers that, because the defendants'
conviction for participation in a RICO enterprise included an act occurring
after January 1, 1983, the defendants can be sentenced to pay restitution f=
or
any of the acts underlying the RICO conviction. This court has not previous=
ly
addressed the question and the circuits which have are divided. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal12 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I10P0NP1XW2000DGGFB0030M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I10P0NP292S000DGGFB004X2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0GJ0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>At least three circuits have held
that restitution may be ordered only for acts committed after January 1, 19=
83. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0GS0000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0G70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FV0-001B-K164-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Corn</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FV0-001B-K164-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 836 F.2d 88=
9,
895-96 (5th Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0GK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9FF0-001B-K39S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Oldaker</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9FF0-001B-K39S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 823 F.2d 77=
8,
781-82 (4th Cir. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FJ6B470K1MNJ0GN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-7020-0039-P4RH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
