style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Prior
History:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;This appeal arises from the denial of plaintiffs'
motion to vacate the judgment of dismissal and for leave to file a first
amended complaint. The United States District Court for the Western Distric=
t of
New York (Siragusa, J.) construed plaintiffs' motion as arising under </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 60(b)(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and denied the motion as untime=
ly
per the applicable one-year filing window. The court held in the alternative
that plaintiffs' motion fails under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which does not have a strict time limit, but which requires a showing of
extraordinary circumstances to merit relief from judgment. For the reasons
explained herein, we conclude that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(1)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> is
inapplicable, and the unique facts of this case necessitate post-judgment
relief under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;.
Accordingly, we REVERSE the denial of Plaintiffs' motion to vacate the judg=
ment
of dismissal and for leave to file a first amended complaint, and REMAND for
further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

