style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
start, the ground for dismissing the Complaint&#8212;that racial disparitie=
s in
national arrest and incarceration rates do not necessarily persist among
persons with the skill set required for the jobs in question&#8212;is not
self-evident. Skills are acquired by adults; nobody is born with a resume.
Every qualified candidate for employment was once a kid, youngster, and
teenager. Plaintiffs reasonably could have assumed that they need not allege
the persistence of disparate patterns of arrest and conviction among people
seeking skilled employment, because even skilled persons are assumed to have
lived life before becoming credentialed.</span></p>

