if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 371 U.S. at=
 182</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
for depriving Plaintiffs of the chance to obtain relief from judgment and f=
ile
an amended complaint for the first time. There is no plausible contention of
bad faith or dilatory motive, nor that vacatur would unduly prejudice the
Defendant. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MD0010000400"></a>There was no u=
ndue
delay, for the reasons explained above. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MD0050000400"></a>And the district court did n=
ot
conclude that Plaintiffs' proposed amendments would be futile. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MD0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MD0010000400_2"></a>To the contrary, the court
acknowledged the relevance of the amici's data indicating that racial
disparities in criminal outcomes persist at higher levels&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp;=
 of
education, and noted that in the <u>in banc</u> memoranda, several judges of
this Court &quot;suggested these statistics 'might' have rendered Plaintiff=
s'
claims plausible had they been included in the original pleadings.&quot; </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF2D6MXG0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:647S-MK11-FBV7-B0JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Mandala IV</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:647S-MK11-FBV7-B0JG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
