if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion, noting courts &quot;should not dismiss without granting leave to am=
end
at least once when a liberal reading of the complaint&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; giv=
es
any indication that a valid claim might be stated&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, this is one of the exceptional cases necessitating relief from judgmen=
t:
Plaintiffs have yet to be afforded a single opportunity to amend their
pleading; the original dismissal of the Complaint was premised on grounds
subject to reasonable, actual, and vigorous debate; Plaintiffs diligently
prosecuted their case at all times; and Plaintiffs' proposed amendments add=
ress
the sole pleading deficiency identified by the district court. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VHXX028T4R00010000400"></a>On these facts, the court's
contrary holding was not a proper exercise of discretion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
