if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies in this case, I would still &nbsp;[*366]&nbsp; affirm because I agr=
ee
with&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; the district court that Plaintiffs failed to demonst=
rate
extraordinary circumstances warranting relief. For these reasons, I would
affirm the district court's order denying Plaintiffs' motion to vacate the
judgment.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I7CPF3X2JYW00006P2B0001V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MF0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As an initial matter, I emphasize
that the standard of review in this appeal is abuse of discretion. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MF0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:664S-6YM1-JXNB-60FX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>ExxonMobil Oil
Corp. v. TIG Ins. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:664S-6YM1-JXNB-60FX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 44 F.4th 16=
3,
172 (2d Cir. 2022)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. A district court abuses its
discretion only when &quot;its decision [(1)] rests on an error of law or a
clearly erroneous factual finding; or (2) cannot be found within the range =
of
permissible decisions.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><u>Id.=
</u></i>
(internal quotation marks omitted). The relevant inquiry is therefore not
whether Plaintiffs might have &quot;reasonably . . . assumed that they need=
 not
allege&quot; certain facts in their complaint in order to state a claim, Ma=
j.
Op. at 11, but rather whether the district court's decision is erroneous or
cannot be found within the range of permissible decisions. Given this
deferential standard of review, I am hard pressed to see how the district
court's decision could constitute an abuse of discretion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal13 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I7CPF3X2GG100006P2B0001T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PF28T4MF0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I7CPF3X2BGM00006P2B0001S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I7CPF3X284F00006P2B0001R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I69VS1PG28T4MP0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In my view, the district court d=
id
not err in construing Plaintiffs' motion as one for relief under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(1)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
rather than </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Recognizing the concern that &quot;parties may attempt to use </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
to&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; circumvent the one-year time limitation in other
subsections of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,&quot;
we have long recognized that &quot;</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)(1)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
