erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, arguing that their prior acts,=
 as
part of litigation they brought against the plaintiff, do not constitute
predicate acts for purposes of RICO. Kim subsequently moved to disqualify
defendant Michael S. Kimm as counsel for the defendants and sought leave to
further amend his amended complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
United States District Court for the Eastern District of New York (Allyne R.
Ross, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Judge</i>) dismissed Kim's ac=
tion
for failure to state a claim, agreeing with the defendants that Kim could n=
ot
sustain a RICO action based on the defendants' prior litigation activities.=
 The
district court &nbsp;[*101]&nbsp; also denied Kim's motion for leave to ame=
nd
and to disqualify Kimm as counsel for the defendants. The defendants then m=
oved
for sanctions pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rules=
 of
Civil Procedure Rule 11</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The district court denied the
motion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kim,
now proceeding <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pro se</i>, appeals =
the
district court's judgment dismissing his action and the district court's de=
nial
of his motions for leave to amend and to disqualify&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; Kimm. =
Kimm
and his co-defendants cross-appeal, challenging the district court's denial=
 of
sanctions against Kim. We agree with the district court that the alleged
litigation activities do not constitute RICO predicate acts. We also conclu=
de
that the district court did not abuse its discretion in denying Kim leave to
amend, Kim's motion to disqualify, and the defendants' motion for sanctions.
The judgment of the district court is therefore affirmed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal14 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
