t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;Seven former participants in onli=
ne
discount membership programs allege violation of federal privacy statutes a=
nd a
racketeering conspiracy between online retailers and loyalty club businesse=
s to
defraud customers of &quot;membership fees&quot; for rewards programs they
unwittingly joined. They allege that Trilegiant Corporation conspired with
e-merchant retailers such as Buy.com, Orbitz, and Priceline to enroll the
retailers' customers in the discount programs via deceptive post-transaction
marketing and datapass techniques. We conclude that, because the appellants
fail to raise a material issue of fact as to whether they consented to
enrollment in the membership programs, the prohibitions of the Electronic
Communications Privacy Act do not apply; and because the appellants identif=
y no
actionable fraud, they cannot proceed on a theory of racketeering.</span></=
