<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*101]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>John M. Walker, Jr.</span>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiff-appellant class participates in retirement plans administered by =
New
York University (NYU) and the NYU School of Medicine. Plaintiffs brought th=
is
suit against NYU in its capacity as the fiduciary of plaintiffs' retirement
plans, alleging a number of breaches of NYU's fiduciary duties under the
Employment Retirement Income Savings Act (ERISA). Following a bench trial in
the United States District Court for the Southern District of New York
(Katherine B. Forrest, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>J.</i>) and
post-trial motions (Analisa Torres, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>J.</i>),
they appeal from the entry of judgment in defendant-appellee NYU's favor and
the denial of post-trial motions. On appeal, plaintiffs challenge: (1) the
dismissal of their claim that NYU breached its &nbsp;[*102]&nbsp; duty of
prudence by offering particular share classes of mutual funds in the retire=
ment
plans, (2) the denial of leave to amend their complaint to name additional
defendants, (3) the striking of their&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; demand for a jury tr=
ial,
(4) the use of written declarations rather than live examination for direct
testimony in the bench trial, (5) some of the district court's findings in
NYU's favor after the bench trial, and (6) the denial of their motion for a=
 new
trial, which argued that the judge presiding over the trial (Forrest, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>J.</i>) should have been disqualified.=
 We
find merit in the first two of these challenges, but none in the remainder.
Accordingly, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>AFFIRM</b> in part=
, <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>VACATE</b> in part, and <b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>REMAND</b> for further proceedings
consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plaintiffs represent a class of NYU and NYU School of Medicine employees who
are suing the University for breach of fiduciary duty in its administration=
 of
their retirement plans under ERISA. Plaintiffs participate in either the NYU
Retirement Plan for Members of the Faculty, Professional Research Staff, and
Administration (the Faculty Plan) or the NYU School of Medicine Retirement =
Plan
for Members of the Faculty, Professional Research Staff, and Administration
(the Medical Plan). The Faculty Plan covers most of NYU's faculty, research
staff, and administrative staff, while the Medical Plan serves employees of=
 the
School of Medicine.&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
NYU Retirement Plan Committee (the Committee) is the nine-member fiduciary
entity responsible for administering both plans, having been designated as =
the
Plan Administrator by NYU's Board of Trustees. The Committee is made up of
senior University and Medical Center administrators, including NYU's Chief
Investment Officer, the Senior Vice Presidents of Finance of NYU and the
Medical Center, the Medical Center's Controller, the Vice Presidents of Hum=
an
Resources of NYU and the Medical Center, the Directors of Benefits of NYU a=
nd
the Medical Center, and NYU's Provost (or its designee).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Both
the Faculty Plan and Medical Plan (the Plans) are defined contribution plan=
s,
as set forth in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67T2-3NK3-GXF6-82R4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1002(34)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and are tax6 qualified under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67RH-G1T1-JCH0-Y1YM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>26 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
403(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Defined contribution plans are retirement plans in whi=
ch
the employee contributes directly to her individual account, and the benefi=
ts
that will ultimately accrue to the employee are a function of the amount she
contributes to investments in the plan and the market performance of those
investments, minus the expenses of plan administration.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn264' href=3D"#_ftn264" name=3D"_ftnref264" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Plans that operate under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:67RH-G1T1-JCH0-Y1YM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 403(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
beneficial tax scheme are retirement plans administered by certain qualifyi=
ng
non-profits, including universities,&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; that offer mutual fund
and annuity investment options to participants.</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn265' href=3D"#_ftn265" name=3D"_ftnref265" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Participants
in NYU's Plans had a range of investment options offered by either TIAA-CRE=
F or
the Vanguard Group, the two retirement investment firms under contract with
NYU. The Faculty Plan offered 103 investment options (25 from TIAA-CREF; 78
from Vanguard) to plan participants during the class period. The Medical Pl=
an
offered 84 options (11 from TIAA-CREF; 73 from Vanguard). Both Plans offered
investment options that included &nbsp;[*103]&nbsp; fixed annuity contracts
(meaning the investment returns at a contractually specified minimum intere=
st
rate), variable annuities (returns at a variable interest rate), and mutual
funds. Participants could also choose from both actively and passively mana=
ged
index funds, with actively managed funds charging higher fees for that serv=
ice.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>TIAA-CREF
and Vanguard are referred to in the industry as the Plans'
&quot;recordkeepers.&quot; They provide investment and administrative servi=
ces,
for which they charge investment fees and recordkeeping fees, respectively.=
 For
mutual funds, the investment fees are charged as a percentage of each fund's
assets (the &quot;expense ratio&quot;). The fees can differ depending on
the&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; share class of the fund: a &quot;retail&quot; share (t=
he
share class that is marketed to individuals with small amounts to invest)
typically has a higher expense ratio than an &quot;institutional&quot; share
(the share class that is available to institutional investors, including la=
rge
retirement plans, with large amounts to invest) of the same fund. These fees
are measured in &quot;basis points,&quot; with each basis point equaling 0.=
01%
of the fund's assets. The administrative (recordkeeping) fees are charged
either (1) as a flat fee, in which case each fund participant pays a set
amount, or (2) by revenue sharing. Under the revenue-sharing model, a fund =
pays
the recordkeeper a set portion of the fund's expense ratio.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
2016, plaintiffs brought this suit under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6JH3-K443-SBKB-44R7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1132(a)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn266' href=3D"#_ft=
n266"
name=3D"_ftnref266" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> alleging that NYU breached its
fiduciary duties of loyalty and prudence and engaged in prohibited
transactions, which caused the Plans to incur excessive costs and unreasona=
ble
performance losses. The breach allegedly occurred because the defendants: p=
ermitted
TIAA-CREF to mandate inclusion of specific proprietary accounts, requiring =
use
of TIAA-CREF as the recordkeeper, in the Plans (Counts I and II); incurred
unreasonable recordkeeping fees (Counts&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; III and IV); incur=
red
unreasonable investment fees, unnecessary marketing and distribution fees a=
nd
mortality and expense risk fees, and thus caused unreasonable performance
losses (Counts V and VI); and failed to monitor the investments (Count VII)=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHND2SF8PT0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHND2SF8PT0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNF2D6NWX0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNF2D6NWX0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On August 25, 2017, the district
court granted in part and denied in part NYU's motion to dismiss, dismissing
Counts I, II, IV, VI, and VII in their entirety and Counts III and V in par=
t.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn267' href=3D"#_ftn267" name=3D"_ftnref267" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The district court's order dismissed all claims alleging that NYU breached =
its
duty of loyalty under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SN7-CJF2-D6RV-H131-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
404(a)(1)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; that NYU engaged in prohibited transactions under </sp=
an><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SN7-CJF2-D6RV-H131-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
404(a)(1)(A)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SN7-CJF2-D6RV-H131-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(C)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SN7-CJF2-D6RV-H131-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(D)</span></i=
></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
and that NYU failed to monitor the investments.</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn268' href=3D"#_ftn268" name=3D"_ftnref268" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The order also dismissed some of the plaintiffs' claims alleging a breach of
the duty of prudence under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SN7-CJF2-D6RV-H131-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
404(a)(1)(B)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. First, the court dismissed the imprudence claim under
Count I, which alleged that NYU mandated inclusion of specific accounts and
required the use of TIAA-CREF as recordkeeper.</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn269' href=3D"#_ftn269" name=3D"_ftnref269" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Second, the court dismissed in part the imprudence claims under Count V to =
the
extent they arose from allegations that NYU offered more expensive retail c=
lass
shares rather than the lower-cost &nbsp;[*104]&nbsp; institutional class sh=
ares
of the same mutual funds (the share-class claim),&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; or incur=
red
unnecessary and unreasonable layers of fees.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn270' href=3D"#_ftn270" name=3D"_ftnref270" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNF2D6NWX0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNF2D6NWY0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The only claims that survived
dismissal were the imprudence claims in Count III and one of the imprudence
claims in Count V. Specifically, Count III survived dismissal on the ground=
s of
imprudence regarding incurring excessive recordkeeping costs (the recordkee=
ping
claim); employing a revenue-sharing method to pay recordkeepers (the
revenue-sharing claim); and failing to consolidate to a single recordkeeper=
 for
each Plan (the recordkeeper-consolidation claim).</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn271' href=3D"#_ftn271" name=3D"_ftnref271" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Count V survived on the ground of imprudence in continuing to include the
underperforming CREF Stock Account and TIAA Real Estate Account as investme=
nt
options (the investment-retention claim).</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id=
:ftn272'
href=3D"#_ftn272" name=3D"_ftnref272" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Thus, those portions of Counts =
III
and V were permitted to proceed to trial.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNF2D6NWY0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On September 8, 2017, plaintiffs
moved both (1) for reconsideration of the district court's dismissal of the
share-class and failure to monitor claims and (2) for leave to amend the
complaint to add seventeen individuals who were Committee members during the
class period as named defendants and to replead the dismissed claims. On
October 17, the district court denied the motion for leave to amend and
deferred&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; consideration of the request to replead the
dismissed claims until resolution of the pending motion for reconsideration.
The district court denied the motion for reconsideration two days later,
relying on different reasoning from that supporting the dismissal of the
share-class claim.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn273' href=3D"#_ftn2=
73"
name=3D"_ftnref273" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
the parties were preparing for trial, NYU successfully moved to strike
plaintiffs' jury demand. The district court also established trial manageme=
nt
rules that specified that all direct testimony would be taken by written
declarations (the court's standing practice for bench trials) and that each
side would have 25 hours of trial time to present its case.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNG2N1R390010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court held a bench
trial on the surviving claims from April 16-26, 2018. On July 31, 2018, the
district court issued its written decision finding in favor of NYU on all
remaining claims.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn274' href=3D"#_ftn27=
4"
name=3D"_ftnref274" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
August 14, 2018, plaintiffs filed a motion for amended or additional trial
findings under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-22N1-6N19-F032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 52(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and to alter or amend the judgm=
ent
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 59(e)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
seeking findings that individual Committee members had failed to adequately
perform their fiduciary duties and removal of those individual Committee
members as fiduciaries, despite the overall judgment for NYU.
Plaintiffs&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; also appealed to this court on September 11, 2=
018,
but we held the appeal in abeyance pending the district court's resolution =
of
the post-trial motions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNG2N1R390030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Meanwhile, by mid-July 2018, it =
had
become public knowledge that Judge Forrest would be leaving the bench. She
resigned from the bench effective September 11, 2018, and returned to her p=
rior
firm, Cravath, Swaine &amp; Moore LLP, the following day. On October 1, 201=
8,
plaintiffs moved for a new trial pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> on
the ground that Judge Forrest should have been disqualified from the case b=
ased
on a connection to NYU through a colleague at Cravath. On July 1, 2019, Jud=
ge
Torres, to &nbsp;[*105]&nbsp; whom the case had eventually been reassigned,
denied plaintiffs' various post-trial motions.</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn275' href=3D"#_ftn275" name=3D"_ftnref275" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, plaintiffs argue that: (1) the district court erred in dismissing t=
he
share-class claim; (2) the district court erred in denying the motion to am=
end
the complaint to add individual Committee members as defendants, an error t=
hat
later prejudiced two of their post-trial motions;</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn276' href=3D"#_ftn276" name=3D"_ftnref276" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(3) they were entitled to a jury trial under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T4S2-D6RV-H37V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Seventh Amend=
ment</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
(4) the use of written declarations for all direct testimony violated the
Federal Rules of Civil Procedure and denied them&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; a fair
trial; (5) the district court's trial findings in NYU's favor on the
recordkeeper-consolidation claim and the investment-retention claim were
clearly erroneous; and (6) Judge Forrest should have been disqualified from
presiding over this case.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
agree with respect to the first two challenges, and accordingly vacate the
dismissal of the share-class claim, vacate the denial of leave to amend, and
vacate the denial of the prejudiced post-trial motions. We otherwise affirm=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Dismissal of the share-class
claim was error</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
the district court granted NYU's motion to dismiss a number of claims for
failure to state a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the only such claim relevant on appeal is plaintiffs' allegation in Count V
that the Plans' fiduciary breached its duty of prudence by offering
retail-class shares of certain mutual funds rather than lower-cost
institutional-class shares of the same funds (i.e., the share-class claim).=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
allege that &quot;the only difference between the various share classes is
fees,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn277' href=3D"#_ftn277"
name=3D"_ftnref277" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and that large
investors like the Plans &quot;can obtain [institutional] share classes with
far lower costs than retail mutual fund shares.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn278' href=3D"#_ftn278" name=3D"_ftnref278" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>15</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
They allege that &quot;[e]ven if a jumbo plan&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; does not me=
et
the minimum investment thresholds for an institutional share class, fund
companies will routinely waive those minimums for billion dollar plans if
merely requested.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn279' href=3D"#=
_ftn279"
name=3D"_ftnref279" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>16</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Supported by a
lengthy and detailed chart, plaintiffs make specific allegations regarding =
the
basis point differences in costs between retail and institutional shares of
each of dozens of mutual funds offered in the Faculty and/or Medical Plans.
They allege that fiduciaries can readily obtain this data on cost-different=
ials
from the prospectus for each fund.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNG2N1R390050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNG28T4YY0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In granting the motion to dismis=
s,
the district court found that &quot;prudent fiduciaries may very well choos=
e to
offer retail class shares over institutional class shares . . . because ret=
ail
class shares necessarily offer higher liquidity than institutional investme=
nt
vehicles.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn280' href=3D"#_ftn280"
name=3D"_ftnref280" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>17</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> It also found that
plaintiffs' allegations of imprudence in this respect were insufficient bec=
ause
&quot;the fees &nbsp;[*106]&nbsp; offered for the sixty-three identified re=
tail
funds included in NYU's Options ranged from 4-77 basis points&#8212;a lower
range than that permitted by the Third, Seventh, and Ninth Circuits.&quot;<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn281' href=3D"#_ftn281" name=3D"_ftnref281" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNG2D6NX40050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH2SF8R70020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH28T5060010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In responding to plaintiffs' mot=
ion
for reconsideration&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; of the share-class claim, the district
court changed its reasoning for dismissing the claim.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn282' href=3D"#_ftn282" name=3D"_ftnref282" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
affirmed the dismissal on the basis that &quot;the 'prudence of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>each</i> investment is not assessed in
isolation but, rather, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>as the inves=
tment
relates to the portfolio as a whole</i>,'&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn283' href=3D"#_ftn283" name=3D"_ftnref283" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and therefore &quot;it must consider the mix rather than the prudence of any
individual option when assessing a prudence claim.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn284' href=3D"#_ftn284" name=3D"_ftnref284" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
found the allegations to be deficient, noting that &quot;plaintiffs do not
allege that, taken as a whole, the mixes of options in the Plans were impru=
dent
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>because</i> of the inclusion of the=
se
retail class shares.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn285'
href=3D"#_ftn285" name=3D"_ftnref285" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>22</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> To withstand
dismissal, the district court stated that &quot;[t]he retail class shares w=
ould
have to be so prevalent that an entire Plan was tainted.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn286' href=3D"#_ftn286" name=3D"_ftnref286" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>23</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> It
found that, in this case, plaintiffs' allegations that the Plans offered re=
tail
rather than institutional shares in 63 funds&#8212;out of 103 offered by the
Faculty Plan and 84 offered by the Medical Plan&#8212;were insufficient to =
meet
that standard as a matter of law.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn287'
href=3D"#_ftn287" name=3D"_ftnref287" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>24</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
appeal, plaintiffs argue that their allegations are sufficient to generate a
plausible inference of imprudence, and that the district court misconstrued=
 our
precedent in finding&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; otherwise.</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn288' href=3D"#_ftn288" name=3D"_ftnref288" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>25</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
NYU disagrees on the merits, but it argues principally that, even if dismis=
sal
was error, the claim should not be reinstated because the district court's
later trial findings rendered the dismissal harmless.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn289' href=3D"#_ftn289" name=3D"_ftnref289" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>26</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the reasons we now explain, we find that the share-class claim was adequate=
ly
pled and that we cannot conclude, on the present record, that its dismissal=
 was
harmless.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>Standard
of review</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH28T5060030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7CF9X0000021K0000R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH28T5060050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7C9GS0000021K0000P"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH2N1R3R0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNH2N1R3R0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2N1R3T0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2N1R3T0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We review the grant of a motion =
to
dismiss a claim under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> de
novo.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn290' href=3D"#_ftn290" name=3D"_=
ftnref290"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>27</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> We apply the well-established pleading standard articul=
ated
in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bell Atlantic Corp. v. Twombly</=
i></span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn291' href=3D"#_ftn291" name=3D"_ftnref291" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>28</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ashcroft v. Iqbal</i>:</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn292' href=3D"#_ftn292" name=3D"_ftnref292" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>29</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;To survive a motion to dismiss, a complaint must contain sufficient
factual matter, accepted as true, to 'state a claim to relief that is plaus=
ible
on its face.'&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn293' href=3D"#_ftn=
293"
name=3D"_ftnref293" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>30</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In assessing the
complaint, we must construe it liberally, accepting all factual allegations
therein as true and drawing all reasonable &nbsp;[*107]&nbsp; inferences in=
 the
plaintiffs' favor.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn294' href=3D"#_ftn2=
94"
name=3D"_ftnref294" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>31</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> However, we disre=
gard
conclusory allegations, such as &quot;formulaic recitation[s] of the elemen=
ts
of a cause of action.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn295'
href=3D"#_ftn295" name=3D"_ftnref295" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>32</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2SF8RH0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We have cautioned that &quot;the
nature of . . . allegations under ERISA calls for particular care in applyi=
ng
this . . . inquiry in order to ensure&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; that the . . .
[c]omplaint alleges <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>nonconclusory</=
i>
factual content raising a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>plausible=
</i>
inference of misconduct and does not rely on the vantage point of
hindsight.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn296' href=3D"#_ftn296"
name=3D"_ftnref296" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>33</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> On the other hand=
, we
are cognizant that &quot;ERISA plaintiffs generally lack the inside informa=
tion
necessary to make out their claims in detail unless and until discovery
commences.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn297' href=3D"#_ftn297"
name=3D"_ftnref297" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>34</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> So, as is true in
many contexts, a claim under ERISA may withstand a motion to dismiss based =
on
sufficient circumstantial factual allegations to support the claim, even if=
 it
lacks direct allegations of misconduct.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:f=
tn298'
href=3D"#_ftn298" name=3D"_ftnref298" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>35</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>The
share-class claim for breach of the fiduciary duty of prudence was adequate=
ly
pled</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ28T50B0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>ERISA imposes a &quot;prudent man
standard of care&quot; on retirement plan fiduciaries in order &quot;to pro=
tect
beneficiaries of employee benefits plans.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn299' href=3D"#_ftn299" name=3D"_ftnref299" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>36</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> As
relevant to the share-class claim, fiduciaries must &quot;discharge [their]
duties . . . with the care, skill, prudence, and diligence under the
circumstances then prevailing that a prudent man acting in a like capacity =
and
familiar with such matters would use in the conduct of an enterprise of a l=
ike
character and with like aims.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn30=
0'
href=3D"#_ftn300" name=3D"_ftnref300" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>37</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ28T50B0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2SF8RM0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The prudence of a fiduciary &quo=
t;is
measured according to&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; the objective prudent person standa=
rd
developed in the common law of trusts.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn301' href=3D"#_ftn301" name=3D"_ftnref301" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>38</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
ERISA instructs us to assess a fiduciary's prudence &quot;under the
circumstances then prevailing,&quot; so we must &quot;judge a fiduciary's
actions based upon information available to the fiduciary at the time of ea=
ch
investment decision and not from the vantage point of hindsight.&quot;</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn302' href=3D"#_ftn302" name=3D"_ftnref302" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>39</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;[T]his standard focuses on a fiduciary's conduct in arriving at an
investment decision, not on its results, and asks whether a fiduciary emplo=
yed
the appropriate methods to investigate and determine the merits of a partic=
ular
investment.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn303' href=3D"#_ftn30=
3"
name=3D"_ftnref303" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>40</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*108]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2HM65H0010000400"></a>A claim for breach of the=
 duty
of prudence will &quot;survive a motion to dismiss if the court, based on
circumstantial factual allegations, may reasonably infer from what is alleg=
ed
that the process was flawed&quot; or &quot;that an adequate investigation w=
ould
have revealed to a reasonable fiduciary that the investment at issue was
improvident.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn304' href=3D"#_ftn3=
04"
name=3D"_ftnref304" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>41</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> That is the situa=
tion
here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2HM65H0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNJ2HM65J0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The complaint sets forth cost
differentials of specified basis points for the dozens of mutual funds as to
each of which, they claim, NYU should have offered lower-cost institutional
shares instead of higher-cost retail shares. Plaintiffs&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp;
allege that this information was included in fund prospectuses and would ha=
ve
been available to inquiring fiduciaries when the fiduciaries decided to off=
er
the funds in the Plans. In sum, plaintiffs have alleged &quot;that a superi=
or
alternative investment was readily apparent such that an adequate
investigation&quot;&#8212;simply reviewing the prospectus of the fund under
consideration&#8212;&quot;would have uncovered that alternative.&quot;</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn305' href=3D"#_ftn305" name=3D"_ftnref305" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>42</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> On
review of a motion to dismiss, we must draw reasonable inferences from the
complaint in plaintiffs' favor.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn306'
href=3D"#_ftn306" name=3D"_ftnref306" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>43</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Upon doing so, wi=
th
respect to the share-class allegations, we believe that plaintiffs have
sufficiently alleged that NYU acted imprudently in offering the number of
retail-class shares identified in the complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RS0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RS0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RS0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RT0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Although the district court
abandoned its initial rationale for dismissing this claim, we note two prob=
lems
in its order. First, the notion that &quot;prudent fiduciaries may very well
choose to offer retail class shares over institutional class shares&quot;
because retail shares offer greater liquidity provides no basis to dismiss
pleadings that otherwise generate plausible inferences of the claimed
misconduct. Such an argument &quot;goes to the merits&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; and=
 is
misplaced at this early stage.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn3=
07'
href=3D"#_ftn307" name=3D"_ftnref307" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>44</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> While the
plausibility standard requires that facts be pled &quot;permit[ting] the co=
urt
to infer more than the mere possibility of misconduct,&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn308' href=3D"#_ftn308" name=3D"_ftnref308" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>45</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> we
do not require an ERISA plaintiff &quot;to rule out every possible lawful
explanation for the conduct he challenges.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn309' href=3D"#_ftn309" name=3D"_ftnref309" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>46</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> To
do so &quot;would invert the principle that the complaint is construed most
favorably to the nonmoving party&quot; on a motion to dismiss.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn310' href=3D"#_ftn310" name=3D"_ftnref310" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>47</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RT0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, we caution against
overreliance on cost ranges from other ERISA cases as benchmarks. While such
comparisons may sometimes be instructive, their utility is limited because =
the
assessment of any particular complaint is a &quot;context-specific&nbsp;[*1=
09]&nbsp;
task.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn311' href=3D"#_ftn311"
name=3D"_ftnref311" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>48</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We cannot rule out
the possibility that a fiduciary has acted imprudently by including a
particular fund even if, for example, the fees that fund charged are lower =
than
a fee found not imprudent in another case.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court's order denying the motion for reconsideration similarly off=
ers
no compelling ground for affirmance. In it, the court faulted plaintiffs for
not alleging (1) that &quot;the mixes of options in the Plans were
imprudent,&quot; or (2) that the Plans were tainted in their entirety
because&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; the retail shares were included. Neither ground f=
or
dismissal is persuasive.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXR0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXS0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXS0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As for the &quot;mix&quot; of fu=
nds,
we agree with the general principle that &quot;the prudence of each investm=
ent
is not assessed in isolation but, rather, as the investment relates to the
portfolio as a whole.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn312'
href=3D"#_ftn312" name=3D"_ftnref312" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>49</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> But this principle
alone cannot support the district court's dismissal of the share-class clai=
m in
this case. As we have suggested previously, allegations concerning the mix =
of
investments are more centrally relevant to claims of imprudence based on the
riskiness of funds or the risk-profile of a portfolio as a whole.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn313' href=3D"#_ftn313" name=3D"_ftnref313" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>50</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Here, with respect to the share-class claim, the alleged imprudent choice h=
as
nothing to do with the funds' risk profiles; the choice was simply between
higher-or lower-cost shares of the same fund. In some cases where the 'mix'
analysis is appropriate, there is uncertainty as to what investment option
would have been included if the questioned investment option had not been
included. Here, however, there was a binary choice between the retail shares
and the institutional shares; had the funds not included the former, they w=
ould
have included the latter, to some extent. Even if this were not the
case,&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; the principle that a portfolio should be assessed
holistically does not preclude critical assessment of individual funds.</sp=
an><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn314' href=3D"#_ftn314" name=3D"_ftnref314" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>51</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Fiduciaries cannot shield themselves from liability&#8212;much less
discovery&#8212;simply because the alleged imprudence inheres in fewer than=
 all
of the fund options. If the prudence of a particular investment offering wi=
ll
become clear only in the context of the portfolio as a whole, that argument
cannot resolve a motion on the pleadings; it goes to the merits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
to whether the Plans were tainted in their entirety, we do not suggest that=
 a
holistic assessment of the Plans is irrelevant to the share-class
claim&#8212;we simply think that plaintiffs have pled enough on that claim =
to
withstand dismissal at the pleading stage. They allege that 63 of the funds
included in the 103-fund and 84-fund Plans charged excessive (retail share)
fees, each of which plaintiffs set forth with specificity. The district cou=
rt
appears to have faulted plaintiffs for failing to calculate what 63/104 or
63/84 would be as a percentage of each plan, and then to allege
&nbsp;[*110]&nbsp; that those percentages were high enough to taint each pl=
an
as a whole. But plaintiffs' non-conclusory allegations&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp;
plainly pointed the way to these obvious inferences in plaintiffs' favor.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Drawing
all reasonable inferences in their favor, plaintiffs have plausibly alleged
that offering these retail shares rather than institutional shares was
imprudent. This claim should have been, and now must be, litigated on the
merits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>The
share-class claim's dismissal was not harmless</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>NYU
urges us to decline to reinstate the share-class claim on the basis that any
error in dismissing it was harmless because of two findings the district co=
urt
made at trial. The first finding was that plaintiffs failed to prove a brea=
ch
of fiduciary duty in using revenue sharing to fund recordkeeping costs. The
second finding was that plaintiffs failed to prove loss resulting from eith=
er
of the revenue-sharing or recordkeeping claims. NYU 's reliance on these
findings is premised on its argument that selecting higher-cost retail shar=
es
was necessary to pay recordkeeping fees through revenue sharing. For the
reasons that follow, however, we are not persuaded that these findings comp=
el
the conclusion that dismissal of the share-class claim on the pleadings was
harmless.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>i. <u>The
district court's revenue-sharing finding</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
first address&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; NYU's argument that because the use of reve=
nue
sharing was found not imprudent at trial, and because the difference in cos=
ts
between retail and institutional shares supplies the funds for the
revenue-sharing arrangement to pay the recordkeepers, the dismissal of the
share-class claim on the pleadings was harmless. We think NYU's argument ta=
kes
the district court's trial findings too far.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RV0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RV0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The revenue-sharing claim presen=
ted
at trial was not concerned with the specific expense ratios of each fund th=
at
generated revenue for the recordkeepers. Rather, the district court was ask=
ed
to focus on two other issues: (1) whether it was imprudent not to cap the
per-participant cost of revenue sharing, and (2) whether it was imprudent to
use revenue sharing at all instead of employing a flat fee billed to each
participant.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn315' href=3D"#_ftn315"
name=3D"_ftnref315" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>52</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The court's rejec=
tion
of this claim relied only on evidence pertaining to these general questions=
 in
relation to the Plans as a whole.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn316'
href=3D"#_ftn316" name=3D"_ftnref316" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>53</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
cannot be sure what would have happened at trial (or on summary judgment) h=
ad
the share-class claim survived dismissal. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RV0050000400"></a>Importantly, the district co=
urt's
rejection of the revenue-sharing claim was specific to plaintiffs' claim th=
at
the cost should&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; have been capped per-participant; the
district court did not explicitly find that the revenue-sharing costs thems=
elves
were prudent. When pressed at argument to direct us to such a finding in the
district court opinion, NYU's counsel could point only to the district cour=
t's
brief rejection of plaintiffs' separate recordkeeping claim. But in rejecti=
ng
the recordkeeping claim, the district court simply rejected the plaintiffs'
expert opinion on what would have been a reasonable per-participant amount =
to
charge for recordkeeping services.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn317'
href=3D"#_ftn317" name=3D"_ftnref317" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>54</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
decline to foreclose the reinstatement of a wrongly dismissed claim on the
basis of a record that was developed with respect to a different question a=
nd
that is underdeveloped in the context of the harmlessness argument that
defendants &nbsp;[*111]&nbsp; now press. For example, there is a set of
Committee meeting minutes in the record, upon which the dissent heavily rel=
ies,
that appears relevant to the relationship between retail-share costs and
revenue sharing.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn318' href=3D"#_ftn318"
name=3D"_ftnref318" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>55</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> But the document =
is
unaccompanied by any testimony, is lacking in specificity, and does not com=
pel
the dissent's conclusion that the erroneous dismissal of the share-class cl=
aim
was necessarily harmless. The&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; fact that one document purp=
orts
to memorialize a discussion about whether or not to offer retail shares does
not establish the prudence of that discussion or its results as a matter of
law.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
dissent states that &quot;if revenue sharing is prudent, so too is offering
retail shares.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn319' href=3D"#_ft=
n319"
name=3D"_ftnref319" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>56</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We have no quarrel
with the general concept of using retail shares to fund revenue sharing. Bu=
t,
there was no trial finding that the use here of all 63 retail shares to ach=
ieve
that goal was not imprudent. Simply concluding that revenue sharing is appr=
opriate
does not speak to how the revenue sharing is implemented in a particular ca=
se.
We do not know, for example, whether revenue sharing could prudently be
achieved with fewer retail shares.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
dissent also insists that this &quot;numerical claim&quot; is nonetheless
foreclosed by the findings at trial because NYU arrived at this number thro=
ugh
&quot;a deliberative process for adopting the revenue-sharing model.&quot;<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn320' href=3D"#_ftn320" name=3D"_ftnref320" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>57</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> We
cannot agree. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2N1R450020000400"></a>While the a=
bsence
of a deliberative process may be enough to demonstrate imprudence, the pres=
ence
of a deliberative process does not, contrary to the dissent's suggestion,
suffice in every case to demonstrate prudence. Deliberative
processes&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; can vary in quality or can be followed in bad
faith. In assessing whether a fiduciary fulfilled her duty of prudence, we =
ask
&quot;whether a fiduciary employed the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>appropriate</i>
methods to investigate and determine the merits of a particular
investment,&quot; not merely whether there were any methods whatsoever.</sp=
an><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn321' href=3D"#_ftn321" name=3D"_ftnref321" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>58</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Of
course, because this claim has not been litigated on the merits, we offer no
opinion on the precise deliberations at issue here. Discovery should take
place&#8212;and it may turn out to be minimal&#8212;before the claim is
dispensed with. At the same time, any incentive to future parties to seek
discovery as to dismissed claims will be avoided.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>ii.
<u>The district court's no-loss findings</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>NYU's
second argument in favor of harmlessness is that the district court's findi=
ngs
that plaintiffs failed to prove loss on two of the tried claims&#8212;the
revenue-sharing and recordkeeping claims&#8212;foreclose a showing of loss =
on
the share-class claim. We are unpersuaded.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2N1R450040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>First, regarding the revenue-sha=
ring
claim, the district court's no-loss finding was at most implied; the court =
made
no explicit findings about why plaintiffs had failed to prove loss on that
claim. In contrast to the district court's detailed discussion&nbsp;[**27]&=
nbsp;
of the evidence supporting the finding that NYU had not breached its fiduci=
ary
duty by using revenue sharing, the district court's only statement regarding
plaintiffs' failure to show loss relevant to the revenue-sharing claim is a=
 blanket
statement in a footnote rejecting their efforts to show &nbsp;[*112]&nbsp; =
loss
generally.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn322' href=3D"#_ftn322"
name=3D"_ftnref322" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>59</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> That the district
court did not discuss loss in the specific context of the revenue-sharing c=
laim
is understandable&#8212;it separately concluded there was no breach of
fiduciary duty. However, that absence of reasoning leaves us with no way to
assess whether the share-class claim is foreclosed in the way NYU argues.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RW0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Second, as to the recordkeeping
claim, the district court's discussion of its no-loss finding was unpersuas=
ive.
The full extent of the district court's findings on this front was a brief
rejection of plaintiffs' proposed alternative range for recordkeeping fees.=
</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn323' href=3D"#_ftn323" name=3D"_ftnref323" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>60</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
This finding that plaintiffs had not come up with a credible alternative is
distant from saying that the fees charged affirmatively resulted in no loss,
and a further distance still from saying that each of the retail-class shar=
es
selected was necessary to pay the recordkeeping costs and none of them
resulted&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; in lost opportunity costs.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Moreover,
we are hard-pressed to rely on the discussion of loss that the district cou=
rt
did undertake because the discussion was somewhat unclear in several respec=
ts.
It conflated loss with damages, appeared to answer a question the court cla=
imed
to leave undecided, and effectively misallocated the burden of proof on
damages.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RW0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2SF8RW0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK28T50P0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK28T50P0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The first thing that perplexes u=
s is
the district court's conflation of &quot;loss&quot; with &quot;damages.&quo=
t;
The court stated expressly that, because it found that plaintiffs had not s=
hown
loss, it had no occasion to confront the subsequent question of damages.</s=
pan><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn324' href=3D"#_ftn324" name=3D"_ftnref324" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>61</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
However, the court then proceeded to describe the evidence exclusively in t=
erms
of damages, crediting NYU's &quot;<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>d=
amages</i>
rebuttal expert&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn325' href=3D"#_f=
tn325"
name=3D"_ftnref325" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>62</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and concluding th=
at
plaintiffs &quot;ha[d] not met their burden of proof as to <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>damages</i> for excessive recordkeeping fees.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn326' href=3D"#_ftn326" name=3D"_ftnref326" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>63</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> To
be clear, these terms are not interchangeable. Loss is measured in this con=
text
by &quot;a comparison of what the [p]lan actually earned on the . . .
investment with what the [p]lan would have earned had the funds been availa=
ble
for other [p]lan purposes. If the latter amount is greater than the former,=
 the
loss is the difference between the two.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnot=
e-id:
ftn327' href=3D"#_ftn327" name=3D"_ftnref327" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>64</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;
The question of how much money should be awarded to the plaintiffs in damag=
es
is distinct from, and subsequent to, whether they have shown a loss. The
district court's conflation of the two concepts saps our confidence in its
analysis on this subject.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXV0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXV0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We are further puzzled because, =
in
stating that it had no need to address damages, the district court explicit=
ly
declined to resolve which party would bear the burden of proof during a dam=
ages
analysis</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn328' href=3D"#_ftn328"
name=3D"_ftnref328" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>65</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&#8212;but
nevertheless it went on to resolve that exact question, and did so incorrec=
tly.
It stated that &quot;plaintiffs fail to demonstrate by a preponderance of t=
he
evidence that their proposed fee ranges were the only plausible or prudent
ones,&quot; and so &quot;[p]laintiffs thus have not met their burden of pro=
of
as to damages for excessive recordkeeping fees.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn329' href=3D"#_ftn329" name=3D"_ftnref329" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>66</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
These statements indicate that the district court believed the plaintiffs
would, in addition to proving loss, bear the burden of
proving&nbsp;[*113]&nbsp; the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>amoun=
t</i>
of damages. That allocation of the burden was erroneous.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2D6NXV0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2N1R470020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2HM65W0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNK2HM65W0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNM28T50W0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Although plaintiffs bear the bur=
den
of proving a loss,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn330' href=3D"#_ftn3=
30"
name=3D"_ftnref330" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>67</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> the burden under
ERISA shifts to the defendants to disprove any portion of potential damages=
 by
showing that the loss was not caused by&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; the breach of
fiduciary duty.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn331' href=3D"#_ftn331"
name=3D"_ftnref331" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>68</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> This approach is
aligned with the Supreme Court's instruction to &quot;look to the law of
trusts&quot; for guidance in ERISA cases.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id=
:ftn332'
href=3D"#_ftn332" name=3D"_ftnref332" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>69</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Trust law
acknowledges the need in certain instances to shift the burden to the trust=
ee,
who commonly possesses superior access to information.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn333' href=3D"#_ftn333" name=3D"_ftnref333" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>70</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Even in the context of the share-class claim, where plaintiffs have alleged=
 the
known cost-differentials between retail and institutional shares, &quot;it
makes little sense to have the plaintiff hazard a guess as to what the
fiduciary would have done had it not breached its duty in selecting investm=
ent
vehicles, only to be told [to] guess again.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foo=
tnote-id:
ftn334' href=3D"#_ftn334" name=3D"_ftnref334" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>71</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
considering the potential opportunity cost to the plaintiff of the investme=
nt,
&quot;[i]t makes much more sense for the fiduciary to say what it claims it
would have done and for the plaintiff to then respond to that.&quot;</span>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn335' href=3D"#_ftn335" name=3D"_ftnref335" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>72</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2N1R4H0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>By requiring the plaintiffs here=
 to
prove that the alternative fee ranges proposed by their expert were &quot;t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>only</i> plausible or prudent ones,&qu=
ot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn336' href=3D"#_ftn336" name=3D"_ftnref336" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>73</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
the district court failed to shift the burden onto the defendant. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2N1R4H0040000400"></a>Had plaintiffs been able to =
prove
that the charged fees were&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; imprudent, and had the plainti=
ffs
shown a prudent alternative, the burden would have shifted to the defendant=
 to
disprove that the entire amount of loss should be awarded as damages. Put
differently, if a plaintiff proved that it was imprudent to pay $100 for
something but that it would have been prudent to pay $10, it is not the
plaintiff's burden to prove that it would also have been imprudent to pay e=
very
price between $11 and $99. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2N1R4H0040000400_2">=
</a>It
is &nbsp;[*114]&nbsp; on the defendant to prove that there is some price hi=
gher
than $10 that it would have been prudent to pay.</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn337' href=3D"#_ftn337" name=3D"_ftnref337" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>74</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Against
this backdrop, we decline to foreclose the share-class claim on the basis of
the district court's loss findings. Accordingly, we vacate dismissal of that
claim in Count V and order its reinstatement for further proceedings.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Leave to amend was denied un=
der
the wrong legal standard, and denial was not harmless</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
argue that the district court erred when it denied their motion to amend the
complaint to add the Committee members as named defendants. We agree, becau=
se
the district court denied the motion to amend with reference to the wrong l=
egal
standard. We therefore vacate the denial of leave to amend and remand for
consideration&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; under the correct legal standard. Plaintiffs
further argue that this first error led to another, when the district court
refused to order the removal of two of the members as fiduciaries. We agree
that the outcome of the motion to amend may have affected the outcome of
plaintiffs' post-trial motions for removal of specific Committee members, a=
nd
therefore we also vacate the relevant rulings on those post-trial motions.<=
/span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court, on December 5, 2016, entered a scheduling order in which the
dates seem to have been proposed by the parties that provided, in relevant
part: &quot;Amended pleadings may not be filed, and no party may be joined,
without leave of Court more than 10 days after the filing of this Order or =
the
filing of a responsive pleading, whichever occurs first.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn338' href=3D"#_ftn338" name=3D"_ftnref338" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>75</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Nine months later, on September 8, 2017, shortly after resolution of the mo=
tion
to dismiss and nearly three months before fact discovery closed, plaintiffs
sought leave of court to amend the complaint to add the individual Committee
members as named defendants.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2SF8S80010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court denied the mo=
tion
with citations to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 16(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and the scheduling order.
Specifically, it found that &quot;[t]he time for amending the&nbsp;[**33]&n=
bsp;
complaint as of right has passed&quot; and so &quot;without [plaintiffs]
demonstrating good cause, the Court may dismiss this untimely motion. Plain=
tiffs
have not demonstrated good cause for their failure to include the defendants
whom they now propose to add.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn33=
9'
href=3D"#_ftn339" name=3D"_ftnref339" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>76</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2SF8S80030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2SF8S80050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We review denial of leave to ame=
nd
for abuse of discretion,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn340' href=3D"=
#_ftn340"
name=3D"_ftnref340" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>77</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and will find it =
when
the district court's &quot;decision rests on an error of law (such as the
application of the wrong legal principle).&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-foot=
note-id:
ftn341' href=3D"#_ftn341" name=3D"_ftnref341" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>78</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
The district court here applied the wrong Federal Rule of Civil Procedure to
the motion to amend, so we must vacate the ruling.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*115]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNN2SF8SC0020000400"></a>The ability of a plaintif=
f to
amend the complaint is governed by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rules 15</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
