style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
language of the scheduling order in this case set the deadline (ten days) f=
or
amending <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>without</i> leave of court=
. It
set no expiration date after which all amendments were prohibited, which wo=
uld
have triggered the stricter </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)(4)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;good cause&quot; standard thereafter. Thus, if plaintiffs wanted to a=
mend
after the stated deadline, they only needed the court's leave&#8212;under <=
/span><a
