<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First
of all, we have found nothing defective in the order itself, only in the
district court's ensuing ruling on the motion for leave to amend, which app=
lied
the &nbsp;[*116]&nbsp; wrong legal standard. Other district courts that have
issued an identical scheduling order, and evaluated a motion for leave to a=
mend
under that order pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)(2)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
would have no cause for concern.&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; District courts wishing =
to
evaluate motions for leave to amend under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
after a particular date need only write their scheduling orders consistent =
with
that intent, and state that no amendment will be permitted after that date =
in
order to trigger the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
standard. And secondly, even if the dissent's speculation that other distri=
ct
courts may have committed similar interpretive missteps is true, that provi=
des
no reason for us to review this decision more deferentially. The efficacy of
our appellate review should not be affected by the possible existence of ot=
her
similarly worded scheduling orders in other cases.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2D6NYF0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We cannot find this error harmle=
ss
because the resulting denial of leave to amend may have later affected
plaintiffs' post-trial motions. In the district court's trial findings, it =
had
harshly criticized as incompetent the performance of two Committee members&=
#8212;Margaret
Meagher and Nancy Sanchez&#8212;who were among the fiduciaries that plainti=
ffs
had sought (through their motion to amend) to name as defendants.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn348' href=3D"#_ftn348" name=3D"_ftnref348" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>85</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Thereafter, in their proper role of policing the Plans' fiduciaries, plaint=
iffs
pressed for Meagher and Sanchez's removal.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-i=
d:
ftn349' href=3D"#_ftn349" name=3D"_ftnref349" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>86</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2SF8SG0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2SF8SG0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs' effort was thwarted,=
 in
major&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; part, because Meagher and Sanchez had not been name=
d as
defendants. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2HM66D0010000400"></a>Judge Torres =
denied
plaintiffs' post-trial motions on the ground that Judge Forrest had previou=
sly
considered and rejected ordering their removal from the Committee.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn350' href=3D"#_ftn350" name=3D"_ftnref350" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>87</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
However, we see no such ruling by Judge Forrest, even implicitly, in the tr=
ial
findings. The only question put to the district court was the Committee's
