<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*118]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2SF8SH0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2SF8SH0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2HM66J0010000400"></a>Plaintiffs did not, upon bei=
ng
alerted by the district court's December 19 order, move for reconsideration=
 of
the order denying a jury trial.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn357'
href=3D"#_ftn357" name=3D"_ftnref357" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>94</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Nor did they obje=
ct
at the pretrial conference, at which the parties and court turned their
attention to the forthcoming bench trial. And finally, the plaintiffs
thereafter&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; &quot;participat[ed] in a bench trial without
objection[, which alone] constitutes waiver of the jury trial right.&quot;<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn358' href=3D"#_ftn358" name=3D"_ftnref358" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>95</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Under these circumstances, &quot;[i]t would be patently unfair and, in effe=
ct,
an ambush of the trial judge on appeal if appellant were allowed to lodge an
early demand for a jury, participate in a bench trial without objection, and
then assign as error the failure to honor the jury demand.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn359' href=3D"#_ftn359" name=3D"_ftnref359" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>96</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV. The district court's use of
written direct testimony was not an abuse of discretion</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court followed its bench-trial practice of taking direct testimony=
 by
written submissions, followed by live cross-examination and live redirect.
Plaintiffs argue that this practice violated the Federal Rules of Civil
Procedure and denied plaintiffs a fair trial. These arguments lack merit.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
claim to have lodged this objection with the district court, but the letter
they point to as evidence of that objection contains no such argument.</spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn360' href=3D"#_ftn360" name=3D"_ftnref360" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>97</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> In
that letter, they argued only that they needed more trial time for oral
cross-examination of witnesses <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in l=
ight
of</i> the court's practice of taking direct testimony by written declarati=
on.
Having acknowledged the practice without objection, they
effectively&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; consented to it.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2HM66J0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNP2HM66J0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNR2D6NYP0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>It is a &quot;well-established
general rule that a court of appeals will not consider an issue raised for =
the
first time on appeal.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn361'
href=3D"#_ftn361" name=3D"_ftnref361" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>98</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In any event, we =
have
approved of the practice of taking direct testimony by written submissions =
in
bench trials.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn362' href=3D"#_ftn362"
name=3D"_ftnref362" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>99</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> While in certain
cases this practice might exceed a district court's discretion, there is
nothing in this record indicating that the district court abused its discre=
tion
to manage trials efficiently.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn363'
href=3D"#_ftn363" name=3D"_ftnref363" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>100</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>V. The district court did not er=
r in
ruling for NYU on the tried claims</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
appeal the district court's entry of judgment for NYU after trial, and
&nbsp;[*119]&nbsp; specifically make arguments that the trial court erred w=
ith
respect to: (A) the recordkeeper-consolidation claim, and (B) the
investment-retention claim.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn364'
href=3D"#_ftn364" name=3D"_ftnref364" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>101</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We reject these
arguments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNR2D6NYW0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNR2D6NYW0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;We review the district cou=
rt's
findings of fact after a bench trial for clear error and its conclusions of=
 law
de novo.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn365' href=3D"#_ftn365"
name=3D"_ftnref365" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>102</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The clear error
standard permits us to set aside a district court's factual findings only i=
f we
are &quot;left with the definite and firm conviction that a mistake has been
committed.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn366' href=3D"#_ftn366"
name=3D"_ftnref366" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>103</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The district cour=
t's
determination as to whether NYU breached its fiduciary duty rests on an
&quot;application&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; of those facts to draw conclusions of l=
aw,
including a finding of liability, [and so] is subject to <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>de novo</i> review.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn367'
href=3D"#_ftn367" name=3D"_ftnref367" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>104</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Because we discer=
n no
clear error in the district court's factual findings, we have little diffic=
ulty
agreeing that NYU did not breach its fiduciary duties in the ways argued by
plaintiffs at trial.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2HM66W0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Turning first to the
recordkeeper-consolidation claim: plaintiffs argued that it was imprudent f=
or
NYU to use multiple recordkeepers for the Plans rather than consolidating to
one recordkeeper. The Medical Plan contracted with TIAA-CREF, Vanguard, and=
 Prudential
as recordkeepers until 2013, when it consolidated with TIAA-CREF, while the
Faculty Plan contracted with both TIAA-CREF and Vanguard throughout the cla=
ss
period.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn368' href=3D"#_ftn368"
name=3D"_ftnref368" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>105</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2HM66W0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2HM66X0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2HM66X0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court found that, w=
hile
plaintiffs were correct that &quot;[c]onsolidation may lead to lower
recordkeeping fees[,] . . . [t]he evidence at trial support[ed] defendant's
contention that technical and other requirements prevented immediate consol=
idation.&quot;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn369' href=3D"#_ftn369" name=3D"_ftnref369" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>106</span=
></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Specifically, the district court credited testimony from several Committee
members to the effect that a lengthy and resource-intensive change of the
computer systems used&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp; for payroll, finance, student recor=
ds,
and human resources at the Washington Square campus (where Faculty Plan mem=
bers
work) precluded the Committee from consolidating recordkeepers during that
time.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn370' href=3D"#_ftn370" name=3D"_=
ftnref370"
title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-f=
amily:
Times'>107</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'> The court credited NYU's belief that &quot;any recordke=
eper
switch could not be completed without risk of significant errors or additio=
nal
&nbsp;[*120]&nbsp; changes prior to completion of this global update of NYU=
's
systems and technology.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn371'
href=3D"#_ftn371" name=3D"_ftnref371" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>108</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The switchover of=
 the
University's IT systems created this hurdle because &quot;[a] change in
recordkeepers would entail significant coordination with and changes to the=
 new
systems being implemented,&quot; due to the fact that the recordkeepers' sy=
stems
must interface with NYU's systems in order to allow participants to access
their account information.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn372'
href=3D"#_ftn372" name=3D"_ftnref372" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>109</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2N1R5B0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2N1R5B0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Plaintiffs argue on appeal, as t=
hey
did before the district court, that the IT justification for not consolidat=
ing
recordkeepers sooner is not credible because the contemporaneous Committee
meeting minutes lack references to these technical difficulties.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn373' href=3D"#_ftn373" name=3D"_ftnref373" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>110</span=
></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
But &quot;clear error review mandates that we defer to the district court's
factual findings, particularly those involving credibility
determinations.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn374' href=3D"#_f=
tn374"
name=3D"_ftnref374" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>111</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> In light of the
district court's&nbsp;[**44]&nbsp; extensive discussion of witness testimony
that persuaded it to credit the IT justification, we find no clear error in=
 the
factual findings forming basis of the court's rejection of the
recordkeeper-consolidation claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Having
accepted the district court's factual findings, we agree that NYU did not
breach its fiduciary duty of prudence by failing to consolidate recordkeepe=
rs
any faster than it did. In light of the technical challenges NYU was facing,
including the risk that participants would suffer disrupted account access,=
 we
cannot conclude that a hypothetical prudent fiduciary would have acted any
differently.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Turning
next to the investment-retention claim: plaintiffs argue that NYU breached =
its
duty of prudence by retaining two particular funds&#8212;the CREF Stock Acc=
ount
and the TIAA Real Estate Account&#8212;in the Plans beyond when it should h=
ave
because NYU was using inadequate benchmarks to decide which funds to retain=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2N1R5B0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS28T51Y0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS28T51Y0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2D6P020010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNS2D6P020030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court found after
detailed analysis, however, that the benchmarks used by the Committee to
evaluate these two funds were appropriate in light of these funds' unique a=
nd
complex characteristics and that the retention of these funds was valuable =
in
diversifying&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; the plans.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id=
:ftn375'
href=3D"#_ftn375" name=3D"_ftnref375" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>112</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The district court
found that the fact the Committee changed the benchmarks employed to monitor
the TIAA Real Estate Account during the relevant period demonstrated careful
attention to the fund's performance.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn3=
76'
href=3D"#_ftn376" name=3D"_ftnref376" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>113</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Similarly, the
district court found that the Committee &quot;focused on the difficulties w=
ith
benchmarking that the CREF Stock Account presented,&quot; held
&quot;specialized discussions&quot; about it, and was &quot;actively
engaged&quot; with monitoring this fund's benchmarks.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn377' href=3D"#_ftn377" name=3D"_ftnref377" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>114</span=
></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
With respect to its evaluation of the funds' performance, the district court
found that these funds were performing as well as could be expected from
contemporaneous assessments.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn378'
href=3D"#_ftn378" name=3D"_ftnref378" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>115</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The district court
specifically discredited plaintiffs' expert testimony &nbsp;[*121]&nbsp;
regarding these funds' performance.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn37=
9'
href=3D"#_ftn379" name=3D"_ftnref379" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>116</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We see no clear e=
rror
in these factual findings.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Based
on the foregoing findings, we also agree that NYU did not breach its duty of
prudence by failing to remove the CREF Stock Account and TIAA Real Estate
Account from the Plans. The facts found at trial demonstrated that the
Committee paid special attention to these funds and retained them on the
strength of their performance against legitimate benchmarks. We agree with =
the
district court&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp; that a hypothetical prudent fiduciary would
have made similar choices if presented with these circumstances.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>VI. Judge Forrest's attenuated
connection to NYU did not require disqualification</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
argue, as they did before Judge Torres, that Judge Forrest was disqualified
from presiding over this case due to her connection to one of NYU's board
members, and that Judge Torres erred by denying plaintiffs' motion for a new
trial on that ground following Judge Forrest's resignation from the bench. =
We
review the district court's denial of a motion for new trial for abuse of
discretion.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn380' href=3D"#_ftn380"
name=3D"_ftnref380" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>117</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT28T5210010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT28T5210030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Federal law provides that: &quot=
;Any
justice, judge, or magistrate judge of the United States shall disqualify
himself in any proceeding in which his impartiality might reasonably be
questioned.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn381' href=3D"#_ftn38=
1"
name=3D"_ftnref381" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>118</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> And we have said:
&quot;[P]hrased differently, would an objective, disinterested observer ful=
ly
informed of the underlying facts, entertain significant doubt that justice
would be done absent recusal?&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn38=
2'
href=3D"#_ftn382" name=3D"_ftnref382" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>119</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Here, we think no=
t.
Judge Forrest's connection to NYU is the sort of &quot;remote, contingent, =
or
speculative&quot; relationship that &quot;is not the kind of interest which
reasonably brings into question a judge's impartiality.&quot;&nbsp;[**47]&n=
bsp;</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn383' href=3D"#_ftn383" name=3D"_ftnref383" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>120</span=
></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
argue that Judge Forrest was disqualified because of her employment, both
before taking the bench and after leaving the bench, at Cravath, Swaine &am=
p;
Moore LLP. They assert that because Judge Forrest left the bench for Cravath
six weeks after issuing her trial findings, and because Cravath's chairman =
Evan
Chesler, who was a mentor to Judge Forrest before she took the bench and wo=
uld
be a close colleague after she left, serves on the NYU Board of Trustees, s=
he
had a &quot;prospective financial relationship&quot; with NYU that called h=
er
impartiality into question.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn384'
href=3D"#_ftn384" name=3D"_ftnref384" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>121</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Upon a close look,
this argument does not hold water.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Chesler
was one of NYU's sixty-one voting Board members, and he was one of more than
eighty partners at Cravath. Chesler had no personal financial interest in t=
his
case, and his conduct is not at issue. Significantly, he did not sit on the
NYUBoard's Retirement Committee. Nonetheless, plaintiffs claim that because
Chesler had a &quot;personal strong charitable interest in raising money for
NYU's endowment,&quot; &nbsp;[*122]&nbsp; there is an appearance of bias on
Judge Forrest's part.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn385' href=3D"#_f=
tn385"
name=3D"_ftnref385" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>122</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Their theory is t=
hat
Judge Forrest would want to enter judgment in NYU's favor in order to prote=
ct
&quot;donor confidence&nbsp;[**48]&nbsp; in NYU's treatment of employees and
retirees&quot; and therefore benefit Chesler's personal charitable interest=
.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn386' href=3D"#_ftn386" name=3D"_ftnref386" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>123</span=
></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Plaintiffs also theorize that, as a personal matter, Judge Forrest &quot;wo=
uld
be reluctant to strain [her] relationship&quot; with Chesler &quot;by
condemning an institution to which Mr. Chesler has major ties and holds deep
affection.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn387' href=3D"#_ftn387"
name=3D"_ftnref387" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>124</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
believe that plaintiffs' theories of impropriety are too far-fetched to
reasonably call Judge Forrest's impartiality into question. Her prospective
financial relationship was with Cravath, not Chesler individually. Cravath =
was
never involved in this case, and Chesler's involvement is limited to his
membership on a large Board of Trustees in his personal capacity.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
we agree with plaintiffs that the appearance of judicial impartiality is of=
 the
utmost importance, parties who dislike court rulings cannot later rely upon
first-time assertions of tenuous, preexisting alleged conflicts of interest=
 to
avoid those rulings. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT28T5210050000400"></a>Plai=
ntiffs
had similar arguments before Judge Forrest ruled against them, but never ma=
de
those arguments. Even though plaintiffs did not know that Judge Forrest wou=
ld
leave the bench for Cravath until she did so, the knowledge
that&nbsp;[**49]&nbsp; she had come to the bench from Cravath and had
previously been close to Chesler at the firm was readily ascertainable at a=
ll
stages of the litigation. Chesler's attendance at Judge Forrest's Senate
confirmation hearing was a matter of public record&#8212;a fact plaintiffs
themselves rely upon in arguing for her disqualification on appeal.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn388' href=3D"#_ftn388" name=3D"_ftnref388" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>125</span=
></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
And yet, they made no attempt to move for Judge Forrest's disqualification
&quot;at the earliest possible moment,&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn389' href=3D"#_ftn389" name=3D"_ftnref389" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>126</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> as
they are required to do.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Under
these circumstances, we discern no reasonable questions about the appearanc=
e of
Judge Forrest's impartiality. Judge Torres's denial of plaintiffs' motion f=
or a
new trial was not an abuse of discretion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>* *
*</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
sum, we hold that plaintiffs adequately pled a breach of the fiduciary duty=
 of
prudence in Count V's share-class claim, and we cannot find the district
court's dismissal of this claim harmless on the present record. We therefore
vacate its dismissal and reinstate the claim for further proceedings. We al=
so
find that the district court erred in denying plaintiffs' motion to amend to
name individual Committee members as defendants.&nbsp;[**50]&nbsp; We there=
fore
vacate denial of leave to amend and vacate denial of the ensuing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-22N1-6N19-F032-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 52(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>59(e)</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motions for post-trial findings concerning two of those individuals. We rej=
ect
the remainder of plaintiffs' arguments on appeal, affirming the trial of th=
eir
claims without a jury, the use of written direct testimony at that trial, t=
he
entry of judgment for NYU on the tried claims, and the &nbsp;[*123]&nbsp;
denial of their </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16F-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 60</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion for a new trial based upon Judge Forrest's alleged disqualification.=
 We
remand for further proceedings consistent with this opinion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, we <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>AFFIRM</=
b> in
part, <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>VACATE</b> in part, and <b
