LBqPyKRyyWw6n9BoDyCtWq/YrHbL7Xq/4LB4SKCaB+c0eq1us9/uOHwur9Pv9jx+r+/z/36BgIO=
ChYSHhomIi4qNjI+OkZCTkpWUcAkAOw=3D=3D" v:shapes=3D"_x0000_s1080"></span><br
style=3D'mso-ignore:vglayout' clear=3DALL>
</p>

<p class=3DNormal16><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;font-varia=
nt:
small-caps;color:black'>Menashi</span><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Circuit Judge</i>, dissenting in part:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I
join the opinion of the court insofar as it affirms the judgment of the
district court, and I dissent insofar as the court vacates and remands that
judgment. I would not remand for further proceedings on the share-class cla=
im,
and I do not believe the district court abused its discretion in denying le=
ave
to amend the complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court appears to entertain two versions of the share-class claim: a categor=
ical
version (that NYU acted imprudently by including <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>any</i> retail&nbsp;[**51]&nbsp; shares) and a numerical version (t=
hat
NYU acted imprudently by including <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
too
many</i> retail shares). Neither version of the claim can prevail based on =
the
trial record. The categorical claim is foreclosed by the district court's
decision that the revenue-sharing model was prudent&#8212;a judgment that t=
he
plaintiffs do not even appeal. Because retail shares enable revenue sharing=
, if
revenue sharing is not imprudent, then neither is the inclusion of retail
shares. The numerical claim is foreclosed by the uncontested determination =
that
NYU followed a deliberative process for adopting the revenue-sharing model =
that
includes the retail shares. ERISA requires prudence, meaning that an employ=
er
must follow a deliberative process in making its decisions, even if the
decisions are imperfect. Here, NYU followed a deliberative process for deci=
ding
which retail shares to offer; therefore, NYU acted prudently even if the
plaintiffs or the court could imagine a better ultimate decision. For these
reasons, both versions of the share-class claim are foreclosed by the distr=
ict
court's judgment after trial, and therefore the district court's purportedly
erroneous dismissal of the share-class claim was harmless.&nbsp;[**52]&nbsp=
;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Like
all retirement plans, NYU's retirement plans require the service of
recordkeepers. Recordkeepers calculate and track account balances and
investment performance and prepare and deliver enrollment materials, notice=
s,
and other materials to plan participants. For these services, recordkeepers
must be paid. There are two ways to pay recordkeepers: based on the number =
of
participants in the plan (a flat per-participant fee) or based on the assets
under management (an asset-based fee).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>NYU
chose the latter. NYU paid the recordkeeping fees through a method called
&quot;revenue sharing,&quot; in which NYU would offer retail
shares&#8212;rather than institutional shares&#8212;of investment products =
as
investment options for the plan participants. Many of the investment options
NYU offered are available in two classes of shares: retail shares and
institutional shares. The only difference between the two classes of shares=
 is
the cost; the underlying asset is the same. Retail shares have higher expen=
se
ratios.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn390' href=3D"#_ftn390"
name=3D"_ftnref390" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Institutional &nbsp;[*124]&nbsp;
shares have lower expense ratios. Just as sellers in other industries offer
wholesale prices to large purchasers, investment managers offer lower-priced
&quot;institutional&quot; shares to large clients.&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp; As one=
 of
the largest defined-contribution plans in the country, NYU could obtain
institutional shares for its plan participants.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Yet
for sixty-three funds, NYU offered participants the higher-priced retail sh=
ares
rather than the lower-priced but otherwise identical institutional shares. =
The
court suggests that, based on the pleadings, the &quot;reasonable
inference[]&quot; is that the inclusion of retail shares was due to NYU's
neglect and therefore its imprudence. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>Ante</i>
at 17. The court says that an &quot;adequate investigation,&quot; consistin=
g of
&quot;simply reviewing the prospectus of the fund under consideration,&quot;
would have &quot;uncovered&quot; the &quot;superior alternative
investment&quot; of institutional shares&#8212;and that the plaintiffs have
plausibly alleged that NYU failed to conduct that adequate investigation. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>But
the trial record reveals that NYU in fact investigated its alternatives and
made a considered decision to offer retail shares rather than institutional
shares. NYU did so for a perfectly reasonable reason: the excess cost of the
retail shares paid for the recordkeeping fees under NYU's revenue-sharing
model. Under revenue sharing, administrative fees are not charged
separately&#8212;as a flat per-participant&nbsp;[**54]&nbsp; fee would
be&#8212;but are covered by the higher expense ratios of the retail-share
offerings. Plan participants who buy the retail shares pay more, and the
investment manager then transfers a portion of the excess expense ratios to=
 the
recordkeeper. The revenue-sharing model and the retail-share offerings cann=
ot
be viewed in isolation because the latter enables the former: revenue shari=
ng
works by offering higher-priced retail shares.</span><a style=3D'mso-footno=
te-id:
ftn391' href=3D"#_ftn391" name=3D"_ftnref391" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7C5JS0000021K0000N"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT2SF8T80040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Consequently, if revenue sharing=
 is
prudent, so too is offering retail shares. The district court concluded that
the revenue-sharing plan was prudent. It found that revenue-sharing
arrangements were &quot;common&quot; and that NYU had &quot;du[ly] consider=
[ed]
... the appropriate pros and cons&quot; in rejecting the plaintiffs' favored
alternative&#8212;&quot;a flat per-participant model&quot;&#8212;because the
flat fee would not be &quot;fair&quot; to participants with &quot;relatively
small account balance[s]&quot; and because &quot;flat dollar fees cannot be
assessed against the TIAA and CREF annuity account balances in the Plans.&q=
uot;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT2SF8T80030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5SXN-MPN1-F4NT-X11Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sacerdote v. =
New
York Univ.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5SXN-MPN1-F4NT-X11Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 328 F. Supp=
. 3d
273, 305-06 (S.D.N.Y. 2018)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The plaintiffs do not challenge
this ruling on appeal. Therefore, because the inclusion of retail shares is=
 the
mechanism by which revenue sharing operates, the district&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp;
court's determination that the revenue-sharing model was prudent forecloses=
 the
categorical version of the share-class claim: if revenue sharing is prudent,
the inclusion of retail shares must also be prudent.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court rejects this justification for including retail shares&#8212;that
&quot;the difference in costs between retail and institutional shares suppl=
ies
the funds for the revenue-sharing arrangement to pay the
recordkeepers&quot;&#8212;by suggesting that NYU violated ERISA's standard =
of
prudence because it could have bargained for a better &nbsp;[*125]&nbsp; de=
al
on &quot;the specific expense ratios of each fund that generated revenue for
the recordkeepers.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i> a=
t 22.
The court thus shifts its argument from the categorical version of the
share-class claim, according to which NYU failed to investigate the possibi=
lity
of institutional shares,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn392' href=3D"=
#_ftn392"
name=3D"_ftnref392" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> and defends the numerical versi=
on
instead. According to the numerical version of the share-class claim, even =
if
the inclusion of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>some</i> number of
retail shares would not be imprudent, sixty-three retail shares was too man=
y.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
numerical claim is also foreclosed by the district court's findings at tria=
l.
NYU did not act imprudently by including sixty-three retail shares in
its&nbsp;[**56]&nbsp; retirement plan because NYU arrived at that number
through a deliberative process&#8212;the deliberative process through which=
 it
adopted the revenue-sharing model. A deliberative process is what ERISA
requires.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court implies that an employer might be imprudent under ERISA if it makes a
considered decision but fails to get the best deal possible. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7C1MV0000021K0000M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0010000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See ante</i> at 25-26 (describing the standard for the share-class
claim as requiring a showing that &quot;each of the retail-class shares
selected was necessary to pay the recordkeeping costs and none of them resu=
lted
in lost opportunity costs&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7952R0000021K00=
00J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0030000400"></a>But ERISA's standard of prud=
ence
requires the fiduciary to follow an appropriate <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>process</i> leading to its decision, not to make a perfect decision=
. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHNT2SF8T80050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583N-GS61-F04K-J0JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pension Ben.
Guar. Corp. (PBGC) v. Morgan Stanley Inv. Mgmt. Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583N-GS61-F04K-J0JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 712 F.3d 70=
5,
718 (2d Cir. 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting that a claim for breach=
 of
fiduciary duty under ERISA depends on showing that &quot;the process was
flawed&quot; such that the fiduciaries failed to conduct &quot;an adequate
investigation&quot; and that it is not &quot;necessarily sufficient to show
that better investment opportunities were available at the time of the rele=
vant
decisions&quot;). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0030000400_2"></a>The c=
ourt
itself even recognizes that ERISA's &quot;standard focuses on a fiduciary's
conduct in arriving at an investment decision, not on its&nbsp;[**57]&nbsp;
results, and asks whether a fiduciary employed the appropriate methods to
investigate and determine the merits of a particular investment.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i> at 16-17 (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583N-GS61-F04K-J0JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>PBGC</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583N-GS61-F04K-J0JM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 712 F.3d at=
 716</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
ERISA does not require an employer to obtain the best possible result as lo=
ng
as the employer acts prudently by following a deliberative process.</span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV78WWC0000021K0000G"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A fiduciary acts imprudently whe=
n it
fails to follow a deliberative process or fails to conduct an adequate
investigation <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>at all</i>.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn393' href=3D"#_ftn393" name=3D"_ftnref393" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> A
district court case, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0040000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YXN-57D1-652H-7004-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tibble v. Edi=
son
Int'l</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YXN-57D1-652H-7004-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No. 07-CV-5=
359,
2010 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 69119, 2010 WL 2757153 (C.D. Cal. July 8, 2010)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
is instructive. In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Tibble</i>, the
district court concluded that the employer was imprudent in the
&nbsp;[*126]&nbsp; selection of retail shares because the employer did not
consider the relative benefits of institutional shares <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>at all</i>. In other words, the fiduciary acted imprudently because=
 it
undertook <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>no</i> process to investi=
gate
its options:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2HM69S0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV78FF10000021K0000C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Investments Staff simply
recommended adding the retail share classes of these three funds without any
consideration of whether the institutional share classes offered greater
benefits to the Plan participants. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W2002000=
0400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7855P0000021K00009"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20040000400"></a>Thus, the Plan fiduciaries
responsible for selecting the mutual funds (the Investment Committees) were=
 not
informed about the institutional share classes and did not conduct a thorou=
gh
investigation.&nbsp;[**58]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20020000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YXN-57D1-652H-7004-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2010 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 69119, [WL] at *25</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Moreover, &quot;[i]n the one
instance in which the Plan fiduciaries actually reviewed the different share
classes of one of these three funds, the fiduciaries realized that it would=
 be
prudent to invest in the institutional share class rather than the retail s=
hare
class,&quot; indicating that the presence of retail shares was not the resu=
lt
of a deliberative process. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20030000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7YXN-57D1-652H-7004-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>2010 U.S. Dis=
t.
LEXIS 69119, [WL] at *26</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>By
contrast, the trial evidence in this case shows that NYU followed a
deliberative process through which it made a considered decision to offer t=
he
sixty-three retail shares to finance its recordkeeping fees through
revenue-sharing. As an initial matter, the presence of institutional shares=
 in
the plan offerings in this case shows that, unlike the employer in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Tibble</i>, NYU was not ignorant of the
existence of institutional shares&#8212;and it is not plausible that NYU fa=
iled
to &quot;simply review[] the prospectus&quot; to &quot;uncover[] that
alternative.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i> at 17. =
To the
contrary, NYU made a considered decision to maintain a revenue-sharing model
that required the sixty-three retail-share offerings. Among other things, t=
he
minutes of the Retirement Committee's meeting of January 10,
2011,&nbsp;[**59]&nbsp; illustrate the deliberative process by which the
offerings were determined:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Committee next discussed the availability of lower cost share classes as
communicated to NYU by Vanguard, and the possibility of implementing a lower
share class in the NYU program. CLC [Cammack LaRhette Consulting, an outside
investment adviser] noted that although the share class reduction seems to =
be
an offer of a fee reduction, it actually offers plan sponsors the opportuni=
ty
to decide how to structure the fees of the plan. That is, Vanguard would al=
low
the plan to utilize the lower share classes as long as Vanguard continues to
receive the required revenue necessary to administer the program. A plan
sponsor could choose to use a lower cost share class for the program, but,
because Vanguard would still require a certain amount of revenue for its se=
rvices
to NYU, Vanguard would require that any revenue lost from the lower cost sh=
are
class be made up by either a per participant fee or direct payments from NY=
U.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Committee sought additional clarification as to whether NYU could utilize t=
he
lower share classes in its program. CLC confirmed that NYU could choose to
utilize the lower share class. However,&nbsp;[**60]&nbsp; because the lower
share class funds do not return any recordkeeping revenue to Vanguard, they
would need to make up this revenue, either by issuing a per participant fee=
 or
by the plan sponsor paying to offset the cost. The existing share class in =
the
NYU program provides 13 [basis points] of recordkeeping revenue to Vanguard;
this would need to be made up by NYU or plan participants if the switch is =
made
to the lower cost share class.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Committee agreed that since a change in share classes would not result in an
actual fee reduction for plan participants, &nbsp;[*127]&nbsp; it did not m=
ake
sense to change share classes at this time.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV781RK0000021K00008"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2N1R870010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>App'x 959-62 (NYU Retirement
Committee Meeting Minutes, Jan. 10, 2011). When the employer weighs the
relevant variables and arrives at a considered decision&#8212;as NYU did
here&#8212;the employer has not violated its fiduciary duty of prudence even
if, in hindsight, the decision could have been better. <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD28T3W20050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-WN20-003B-G2MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Katsaros v. C=
ody</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-WN20-003B-G2MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 744 F.2d 27=
0,
279 (2d Cir. 1984)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (noting that the decision must =
be
evaluated &quot;from the perspective of the 'time of the [challenged] decis=
ion'
rather than from the 'vantage point of hindsight'&quot;). Because NYU made a
considered decision by following a deliberative process, the plaintiffs can=
not
prevail on remand.&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn3=
94'
href=3D"#_ftn394" name=3D"_ftnref394" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
short, the plaintiffs have already received a trial on whether NYU acted
prudently when it implemented the revenue-sharing model by offering sixty-t=
hree
retail shares to plan participants. That trial showed that NYU followed a
deliberative process that demonstrates prudence. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV77R7V0000021K00006"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2N1R870050000400"></a>Therefore, even if the distr=
ict
court erred in dismissing the share-class claim, that error was harmless
because the trial effectively disposed of the claim. For that reason, I wou=
ld
not remand. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPD2N1R870040000400"></a>Wilson v. Hanrahan, 804 F. A=
pp'x
58, 61 (2d Cir. 2020)</i> (affirming dismissal of a claim because, even if =
the
dismissal were an error, &quot;that error was harmless&quot; because the
subsequent verdict meant that the claim &quot;would necessarily have
failed&quot;).</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn395' href=3D"#_ftn395"
name=3D"_ftnref395" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court also vacates the district court's denial of leave to amend the compla=
int
because the district court relied on </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
&quot;good cause&quot; standard instead of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
liberal standard. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i> at 29-34=
. That
is incorrect. The district court referenced the correct standard when it de=
nied
leave to amend.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court acknowledges that &quot;[t]he period of 'liberal' amendment ends if t=
he
district court issues a scheduling order setting a date after which no
amendment will permitted.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.=
</i>
at 32. The district court issued&nbsp;[**62]&nbsp; a pro forma scheduling o=
rder
setting a date after which it would not entertain amendments without leave:=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Amended
pleadings may not be filed, and no party may be joined, without leave of Co=
urt
more than 10 days after the filing of this Order or the filing of a respons=
ive
pleading, whichever occurs first.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*128]&nbsp;
Special App'x 147. The obvious implication of this order is that the distri=
ct
court would not liberally grant leave to amend after the date it set.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Today's
opinion, however, refuses to acknowledge this obvious implication. Instead =
of
adhering to the ordinary meaning of the order, the court insists that the
district court set no deadline to seek leave to amend <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>with</i> leave of court after the ten-day period.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn396' href=3D"#_ftn396" name=3D"_ftnref396" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
According to the court, therefore, the district court intended to cut off
amendment as of right but for some reason still intended to liberally and
freely grant leave to amend.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV77FJ70000021K00004"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2N1R880040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We should not read district court
orders so tendentiously. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2N1R880030000400"></a>Kanter v. Barr, 919 F.3d 437=
, 454
(7th Cir. 2019)</i> (Barrett, J., dissenting) (&quot;[J]udicial opinions are
not statutes, and we don't dissect them word-by-word as if they were.&quot;=
).
The meaning of the scheduling order is plain. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7797C0000021K00003"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2D6MT70010000400"></a>It indicates that in the nor=
mal
course, no pleadings&nbsp;[**63]&nbsp; may be amended; however, where there=
 is
good cause, pleadings may be amended if the court grants leave. We have said
that the &quot;lenient standard of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;
does not apply when a party seeks to amend after the deadline set in a
scheduling order; under those circumstances, a party must show &quot;good
cause&quot; under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2N1R880050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Parker v.
Columbia Pictures Indus.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 204 F.3d 32=
6,
340 (2d Cir. 2000)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The language of the scheduling
order effectively communicates that the district court intended to invoke t=
hat
restriction.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn397' href=3D"#_ftn397"
name=3D"_ftnref397" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &quot;[W]ithout leave of the
court&quot; does not indicate that anything goes <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>with</i> leave of the court. Special App'x 147.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
reasonable understanding of the scheduling order accords with the structure=
 of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rules 15(a)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>16(b)</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not mention scheduling orders. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7CVV20000021K=
0000V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2D6MT70030000400"></a>Scheduling orders are issued
pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
which sets out the requirements for issuing scheduling orders. For this rea=
son,
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies in the absence &nbsp;[*129]&nbsp; of a scheduling order, but when a
scheduling order is issued, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
applies. We have explained that &quot;the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
'good cause' standard, rather than the more liberal standard of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
governs a motion to amend filed after the deadline a district court has set=
 for
amending&nbsp;[**64]&nbsp; the pleadings&quot; because, &quot;if we conside=
red
only </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
without regard to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
we would render scheduling orders meaningless and effectively would read </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and its good cause requirement out of the Federal Rules of Civil
Procedure.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2D6MT70020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Parker</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 204 F.3d at=
 340</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(alteration omitted). Here, the district court issued a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
scheduling order, and this court finds &quot;nothing defective in the order
itself.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ante</i> at 33. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7CPYR0000021K0000T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2D6MT70050000400"></a>Yet the court holds that even
though the district court properly issued a scheduling order pursuant to </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it nevertheless abused its discretion by following the &quot;good cause&quo=
t;
standard of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
rather than the liberal standard of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
In doing so, the court renders the scheduling order meaningless; in its vie=
w,
the district court was obliged to freely permit amendment despite its issua=
nce
of a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6G8Y-9H73-RS41-G0S7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 16(b)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
scheduling order. I would instead adhere to the rule that &quot;a district
court does not abuse its discretion in denying leave to amend the pleadings
after the deadline set in the scheduling order where the moving party has
failed to establish good cause.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2D6MT70040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Parker</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3YP5-55C0-0038-X2GX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 204 F.3d at=
 340</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court's decision today could have unexpected consequences. The scheduling o=
rder
in question was one of Judge Forrest's pro forma scheduling orders. She app=
ears
to have used it regularly,&nbsp;[**65]&nbsp; and perhaps other judges have =
used
the same or similar language. Are all those orders now defective? <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KBYV7CK080000021K0000S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2SF7MB0020000400"></a>In light of today's opinion,
district judges must beware. Instead of reading the scheduling order in the
stilted fashion on which the court insists, I would read it reasonably and
affirm the judgment. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf. </i></span=
><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I63DBHPF2SF7MB0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6319-HJR1-F60C-X03M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lombardo v. C=
ity
of St. Louis</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:6319-HJR1-F60C-X03M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 141 S. Ct.
2239, 2242, 210 L. Ed. 2d 609 (2021)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Alito, J.,
dissenting) (&quot;If we expect the lower courts to respect our decisions, =
we
should not twist their opinions to make our job easier.&quot;).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>* *
*</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal16 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
share-class claim is foreclosed by the district court's judgment after tria=
l.
The categorical version of the claim necessarily fails because if revenue
sharing is not imprudent, neither is the inclusion of retail shares. The
numerical version of the claim necessarily fails because the deliberative
process by which NYU adopted its revenue-sharing model satisfies ERISA's du=
ty
of prudence. I therefore would not remand the case because the dismissal of=
 the
share-class claim, even if erroneous, was harmless. The district court also=
 did
not abuse its discretion in denying leave to amend. Accordingly, I dissent =
in
part.</span></p>

