<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Proceedings
regarding the Estate are underway in Connecticut Probate Court, as we have
described. That they have been &nbsp;[*94]&nbsp; ongoing for more than thir=
ty
years, however, and that the Forbearance Agreement is in place (<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> Background Part II.B, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i>), unavoidably raises the que=
stion
whether they will ever end, and whether their pendency can reasonably be
treated as we have treated parallel&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; proceedings in the ca=
ses
just cited: that is, as grounds to preclude the related RICO suit.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nonetheless,
the problem identified by the District Court and recognized in our case law
remains: the amount of Virginia's ultimate distribution from the
Estate&#8212;and, thus, the amount of her damages, as measured by the
difference between any distribution she actually receives and the distribut=
ion
she should have received&#8212;is remarkably uncertain. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6GBJN4YHT500005X060000T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6GBJN4YSPR00005X060000V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PK0050000400"></a>The value of the Estate is n=
ot
static: David and Larry are still authorized, as co-executors, to conduct t=
he
Estate's business, to buy and sell its real estate and businesses, and ther=
eby
to control the Estate's net value. In fact, many of the &quot;schemes&quot;
identified in the Complaint are examples of David's misappropriating for
himself lucrative business opportunities that should have been treated as
belonging to the Estate and the legatees. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6=
_14"></a>We
have held in the civil RICO setting that defendants are &quot;not liable for
all losses that <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>may occur</i>, but =
only
for those <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>actually suffered</i>.&qu=
ot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PK0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4835-WTN0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Motorola Cred=
it
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4835-WTN0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 322 F.3d at=
 136</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PM0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FN0-003B-P0RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Nationw=
ide
Bank</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5FN0-003B-P0RW-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 27 F.3d at =
768</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>)
(emphasis in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Motorola</i>). Applyin=
g this
standard, we cannot escape the conclusion that Virginia's lost debt claim is
not ripe because she cannot even estimate with reasonable&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp;
certainty the amount of her anticipated distributional share.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Virginia
argues that there is no &quot;realistic&quot; possibility that the amount of
her damages will fluctuate in light of circumstances described in the
Complaint, Appellant's Br. 67, because these have rendered the Estate
&quot;hopelessly insolvent,&quot; App. 66 (Am. Compl. &para; 27). In her vi=
ew,
the likelihood that she will receive <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>any</i>
distributional share when the Estate closes is nil, no matter what interim
fluctuations in value the Estate's assets might experience.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
argument has some force, but we are skeptical that the law requires us to
accept at face value the claim that the Estate's alleged insolvency is
&quot;hopeless[],&quot;given the variability of the Estate's assets and
liabilities and the unpredictability of the market forces at play. For exam=
ple,
Virginia has pleaded that the Estate's liabilities outstrip its assets, but=
 she
also alleges that David himself&#8212;through Red Knot&#8212;holds $100 mil=
lion
of the Estate's debt. That liability, accordingly, seems amenable to decrea=
se
or even elimination. Moreover, Virginia asserts that the Estate has an
equitable interest in Silver Knot, because the latter was funded with moneys
stolen from the Estate.&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; A balance sheet that takes into
account the Estate's entitlement to some portion of the $455 million cash
payment that Silver Knot received in 2014 might reflect a more accurate
assessment of the Estate's solvency.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PM0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Virginia's assessment of the
Estate's condition also does not recognize that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_13"></a>the Connecticut Probate Court is emp=
owered
to alter the Estate's balance of assets and liabilities in at least two
potentially effective ways. First, the Probate Court may assess a significa=
nt
surcharge against David for any fiduciary breaches that the court identifie=
s. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PM0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46NX-D1X0-0039-438W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gaynor v. Pay=
ne</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:46NX-D1X0-0039-438W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 261 Conn. 5=
85,
596-97, 804 A.2d 170 (2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Virginia's allegations suggest
that David would have access to assets sufficient to satisfy such a surchar=
ge.
Second, that court is authorized in certain circumstances to declare prior
asset transfers null and void, and to impose a constructive trust on assets
wrongfully transferred from an estate. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>See
</i></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5KD2-T7J0-00CV-M18T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In the Matter=
 of
Edwin A. Jarmoc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5KD2-T7J0-00CV-M18T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 29 Quinnipi=
ac
Prob. L. J. 443, 451-52 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5KD2-T7J0-00CV-M18T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*95]&n=
bsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5KD2-T7J0-00CV-M18T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> (2016)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn406' href=3D"#_ftn406" name=3D"_ftnref406" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
These powers raise the possibility that some of the asset transfers identif=
ied
by Virginia as &quot;plundering&quot; could be revoked and the assets retur=
ned
to the Estate, increasing its net value and Virginia's proportionate share =
upon
distribution. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW70010000400"></a>Accordingly=
, even
those particular schemes for which a&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; loss amount is
theoretically calculable&#8212;such as the Honeyspot Road scheme&#8212;do n=
ot
yet give rise to clear and definite damages. Such acts of rectification by =
the
Connecticut Probate Court would doubtless not be easy to accomplish, but
because &quot;[t]hese contingencies, and other conceivable contingencies,
remain,&quot; our precedent teaches that Virginia's RICO claim for triple t=
he
value of her distributional share of the Estate is not ripe for adjudicatio=
n. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PM0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4835-WTN0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Motorola Cred=
it
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4835-WTN0-0038-X47T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 322 F.3d at=
 136</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Virginia
has also alleged that final assessments of the Estate's value and distribut=
ions
to legatees will simply never come to pass. She asserts that David intends,=
 and
is likely able, to keep the Estate open until her death (at 70, she is Davi=
d's
elder by 15 years), and that the bona fides of his threat are evidenced by
David's undeniable success in keeping the Estate open for more than thirty
years so far. Abiding by our RICO ripeness jurisprudence under these
circumstances will, in effect, improperly enable David to carry out his
unlawful plan, she insists: that is, he will keep the Estate unresolved unt=
il
Virginia's death, at which point her share will devolve to the Estate and be
divided equally among her surviving siblings.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
are&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; not enabled by these pleas to depart from our precede=
nt. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW70030000400"></a>Unfortunate as Virginia's
situation might be,<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_13"></a> the RICO stat=
ute as
construed in our Circuit simply does not provide a remedy before a plaintiff
has suffered reasonably ascertainable damages. Nor may a RICO plaintiff,
through predictions of a defendant's future plans, artificially ripen a cla=
im
that is unripe under our jurisprudence. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>Cf.
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW70020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CNW-T3R1-F04K-J0N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kurtz v. Veri=
zon
New York, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5CNW-T3R1-F04K-J0N5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 758 F.3d 50=
6,
516 (2d Cir. 2014)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (endorsing application of ripen=
ess
inquiry that governs </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T3X2-8T6X-731X-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
