k'>
takings claims to due process claims as well to &quot;prevent[] evasion of
ripeness test by artful pleading&quot;). Moreover, even accepting Virginia's
allegations as true, we are not convinced that&#8212;barring something
unexpected&#8212;the Estate is sure not to close until after Virginia's dea=
th.
Although Virginia alleges that David has kept the Estate open without
significant interference by the Connecticut Probate Court, the appointment =
of a
new Probate Judge in 2010 and the Connecticut legislature's substantial ref=
orm
of the Connecticut Probate Court system in 2011 raise the possibility that
closure will now in fact occur. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See
generally</i> Margaret E. St. John, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>The
Connecticut Probate Court System Reform: A Step in the Right Direction</i>,=
 </span><a
