<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#007=
7CC;
background:white'>III. Section 1962(b)</span></i></b></a><b style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-weight:
normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Ti=
mes;
color:black'> theory of recovery</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc14_8"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
makes it unlawful for a person, &quot;through a pattern of racketeering
activity[,] . . . to acquire or maintain, directly or indirectly, any inter=
est
in or control of any enterprise which is engaged in, or the activities of w=
hich
affect, interstate or foreign commerce.&quot; Our Circuit, like many others,
requires a plaintiff who brings a civil RICO claim for a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1962(b)</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
violation to demonstrate an injury&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; arising from the
defendants' acquisition of an interest in, or maintenance of control over, =
an
alleged enterprise. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> Jed S. =
Rakoff
and Howard W. Goldstein, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RICO: Civi=
l and
Criminal Law and Strategy</i>, &sect; 3.03[2], 28-29 &amp; n.23 (2011) (not=
ing
that, as of 2011, all circuits but the Fourth and Eighth require plaintiffs
alleging a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
violation to identify an &quot;acquisition or maintenance&quot; injury).</s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PN0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6GBJN50J6W00005X0600015"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H528T4NW0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The &quot;acquisition or
maintenance&quot; requirement in our Circuit stems from our decision in </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PN0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Discon, Inc. =
v.
NYNEX Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 93 F.3d 1055
(2d Cir. 1996)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>vacated on oth=
er
grounds by </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H528T4NW0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V9V-W320-004B-Y00G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NYNEX Corp. v.
Discon, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3V9V-W320-004B-Y00G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 525 U.S. 12=
8,
119 S. Ct. 493, 142 L. Ed. 2d 510 (1998)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In that case,
plaintiff &nbsp;[*98]&nbsp; Discon, a telephone equipment removal company,
alleged that the defendants had committed certain racketeering acts in
connection with their control of NYTel, a local telephone service provider.=
 </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW90010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H528T4NW0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>at 1057-58</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW90010000400_2"></a>We ruled that Discon faile=
d to
state a RICO claim arising out of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
primarily because it did not allege that defendants' acquisition or control
over NYTel was obtained through a pattern of racketeering activity. </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H528T4NW0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-15B0-006F-M3C0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 1063</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
At the same time, we observed that the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim was also flawed because Discon had failed to allege that its injuries
were caused by the defendants' acquisition or maintenance of NYTel, rather =
than
by the defendants' various racketeering acts. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Id.</i> This alternative holding has taken on significance over
time.&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Virginia
has certainly alleged in some detail that her collection expense injuries a=
re
traceable to Defendants' control over the Estate, and that Defendants' cont=
rol
was maintained through a pattern of racketeering activity. Although David is
not alleged to have &quot;acquire[d]&quot; his position as an Executor thro=
ugh
racketeering acts, the facts as stated in the Complaint provide a more than
sufficient basis from which to infer that David <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>maintained</i> his position (and its attendant control of the Estat=
e)
through the Red Knot forbearance scheme.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
David and Garvey created Red Knot, the Estate's largest secured creditors h=
ad
sought to remove David as an Executor. By replacing those creditors with an
entity that he is alleged to control, David neutralized a threat that could
have led to his removal as an Executor and fortified his position through t=
he
Forbearance Agreement, purportedly making his position impervious to attack.
And, later, when Cadle sought a court order removing him as an Executor, Red
Knot opposed that motion, invoking its status as the Estate's major secured
creditor to give weight to its support of David. Several of the
schemes&#8212;including two sets in particular (the allegedly&nbsp;[**39]&n=
bsp;
wrongful transfers of residential property from the Estate to David, Larry,=
 and
Mary Lou, and the Cadle suit settlement scheme, all of which directly remov=
ed
assets from the Estate)&#8212;occurred after the Red Knot forbearance scheme
had cemented David's hold on the Estate. The expense collection losses
attributable to those alleged breaches can reasonably be attributed to Davi=
d's
&quot;maintenance&quot; of control over the Estate.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW90030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Relying on <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Discon</i>, however, the District Court concluded that the expense
injuries attributable to Defendants' alleged acquisition or maintenance of
control over the Estate were insufficiently &quot;separate and distinct&quo=
t;
from the injuries that resulted from the predicate acts alleged in the
Complaint. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NW90020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5N4W-F6P1-F04C-W09R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>D'Addario</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5N4W-F6P1-F04C-W09R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2017 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 41795, 2017 WL 1086772, at *18</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. We disagree. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc15_6"></a>To successfully plead a RICO claim u=
nder </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;1962(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>, a
plaintiff must indeed allege distinct damages arising from the acquisition =
or
maintenance of control of the enterprise. In other words, those damages mus=
t be
different from the damages that flow from the predicate acts themselves. For
example, a racketeer might use a pattern of physical threats and violence,
including an act of arson against the plaintiff's property, to extort an
interest in the plaintiff's business. The cost&nbsp;[**40]&nbsp; of replaci=
ng
or repairing property damaged in the fire is a loss caused by the predicate
act, the arson, not by the ultimate acquisition of an interest in the
plaintiff's business. The &quot;separate and distinct&quot; damages caused =
by
the RICO violation, as opposed to by the predicate acts, is the value of the
share of the plaintiff's business that the owner turned over to the defenda=
nt.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*99]&nbsp;
Similarly, in this case, Virginia alleges losses specifically attributable =
to
the predicate acts of fraud, such as the loss of the estate assets that were
turned over to Red Knot. But that scheme also maintained David's control of=
 the
Estate, by making his position as Executor impregnable. At a minimum, that
entrenchment of control contributed to Virginia's collection damages, becau=
se
David's enhanced position meaningfully complicated her efforts to unseat hi=
m.
We conclude, therefore, that Virginia sufficiently pleaded a separate and
distinct &quot;acquisition or maintenance&quot; injury.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
question remains, however, whether Virginia has adequately pleaded such an
injury as to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>each</i> of the six
defendants: David, Mary Lou, Garvey, Vitti, Red Knot, and Silver Knot. Of t=
hese
defendants, only David as an Executor had a formal&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; positi=
on
through which he exerted control over the Estate. (Recall that Virginia did=
 not
name Larry, her brother and now the other Executor, as a defendant in this
suit.) We accept Virginia's argument that the Complaint plausibly asserts t=
hat,
along with David, Red Knot and Garvey also exerted significant control over=
 the
Estate, helping to perpetuate David's control and each contributing thereby=
 to
the requisite &quot;acquisition or maintenance&quot; injury. For example, t=
he
Complaint alleges that by purchasing the Estate's loans from the Bank Group,
Red Knot gained not only a standard secured creditor's interest in the Esta=
te's
assets, but also the contractual right under the Forbearance Agreement to
initiate potentially disastrous wholesale foreclosure proceedings upon Davi=
d's
removal. Red Knot's power to foreclose on &quot;virtually all&quot; of the =
Estate's
assets in the event of a management change plausibly represents a meaningful
form of &quot;control&quot; over the Estate. App. 77 (Am. Compl. P 59). And=
 it
would be imprudent to conclude, at this early stage in the proceedings, that
Gregory Garvey&#8212;as Red Knot's nominal owner&#8212;lacked any power or
control over Red Knot, or, through Red Knot, the Estate.
Accordingly,&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; Virginia has pleaded a viable claim under </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
against Red Knot and Garvey, as well as against David.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
remaining defendants, however, are not themselves alleged to have exerted a=
ny
direct control over the Estate's management, much less control that was
acquired or maintained through any alleged racketeering acts. Virginia alle=
ges
generally that Mary Lou, Silver Knot, and Vitti took part in (or, in Vitti's
case, advised David regarding) one or more of the various schemes by which
David looted the Estate. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc16_6"></a>Without =
more,
however, participation as a third party in a business transaction with the
Estate does not constitute either maintenance of an &quot;interest in&quot;=
 or
exercise of &quot;control over&quot; the Estate for purposes of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Accordingly, Virginia failed sufficiently to allege that Mary Lou, Silver K=
not,
or Vitti violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
b)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV. </span></b><a
