:contentItem:3S4X-69X0-003B-S0DS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. 57=
6,
583, 101 S. Ct. 2524, 69 L. Ed. 2d 246 (1981)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>All
six Defendants claim that, even accepting the Complaint's allegations, they
were not associated with an &quot;enterprise&quot; &nbsp;[*100]&nbsp; within
the meaning of the statute and, thus, that Virginia has not adequately plea=
ded
that they violated </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 1962(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Virginia identifies two possible &quot;enterprises&quot; with which all
Defendants purportedly associated: (1) an &quot;association-in-fact&quot;
consisting of all six Defendants, and (2) the Estate itself.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn408' href=3D"#_ftn408" name=3D"_ftnref408" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>Association-in-fact
of the six Defendants</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PP0040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc18_5"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
RICO statute defines &quot;enterprise&quot; as &quot;any individual,
partnership, corporation, association, or other legal entity, and any union=
 or
group of individuals associated in fact although not a legal entity.&quot; =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(4)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn409' href=3D"#_ft=
n409"
name=3D"_ftnref409" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> The Supreme Court=
 has
observed in this regard that &quot;[t]he term 'any' ensures that the defini=
tion
has a wide reach, and the very concept of an association in fact is
expansive.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PP0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Boyle v. Unit=
ed
States</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 556 U.S. 93=
8,
944, 129 S. Ct. 2237, 173 L. Ed. 2d 1265 (2009)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal citations omitted). It has further instructed that, in accordance
with the law's purposes, the RICO statute is to be &quot;liberally
construed,&quot; giving a broad and flexible reach to the term
&quot;association-in-fact.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id=
.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6GBJN50KMC00005X0600016"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52N1PRN0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In line with this&nbsp;[**44]&nb=
sp;
general approach, the Supreme Court has rejected attempts to graft onto the
statute formal strictures that would tend to exclude amorphous or disorgani=
zed
groups of individuals from being treated as RICO &quot;enterprises.&quot;
Accordingly, it has explained, RICO associations-in-fact need exhibit only
three structural features: (1) a shared purpose; (2) relationships among the
associates; and (3) &quot;longevity sufficient to permit these associates to
pursue the enterprise's purpose.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52HM5PP0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 946</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Defendants
do not meaningfully contest that the Complaint adequately alleges longevity,
inasmuch as Defendants' charged association in connection with the Estate h=
as
persisted for decades (and indeed, several of the individual schemes were
carried out over a period of years). They argue, however, that Virginia has
failed to allege sufficiently the existence of relationships among the
Defendants or a common purpose. Rather, for example, they highlight David's
alleged purpose&#8212;to enrich himself&#8212;and contrast it with each
defendant's self-regarding, and separate, individual purpose in individual
transactions: for example, Mary Lou's desire to obtain a particular residen=
tial
property and Garvey's profit-oriented investment&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; in the
aluminum can company through Silver Knot. Although not in the end dispositi=
ve, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see infra</i> Discussion Part IV.B, we=
 find
Defendants' argument on this point persuasive.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_4"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The concept of an
association-in-fact is protean, and, as such, variability is invited by the
statutory language and the Supreme Court's construction of that language. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52N1PRN0030000400"></a>District Courts and Courts of
Appeals &nbsp;[*101]&nbsp; have taken various paths towards providing some
predictable shape for the notion, often drawing on established conspiracy l=
aw
for analogy and contrast. A number of courts&#8212;although not our court&#=
8212;have
found that a group of individuals related by a structure that mimics so-cal=
led
&quot;rimless hub-and-spoke&quot; conspiracies cannot be considered a RICO
association-in-fact. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., </i=
></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52N1PRN0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:50T0-1R51-652R-1015-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Insuran=
ce
Brokerage Antitrust Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:50T0-1R51-652R-1015-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 618 F.3d 30=
0,
374-75 (3d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52N1PRN0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5NYR-1CN1-F04F-00C8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Moss v. BMO
Harris Bank, N.A.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5NYR-1CN1-F04F-00C8-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 258 F. Supp=
. 3d
289, 302-03 (E.D.N.Y. 2017)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NWB0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NS3-8FC0-0038-Y505-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cedar Swamp
Holdings, Inc. v. Zaman</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4NS3-8FC0-0038-Y505-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 487 F. Supp=
. 2d
444, 451-52 (S.D.N.Y. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times=
",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In other words, when each
defendant is alleged to have a relationship with a central figure, but the
defendants are not all alleged to be connected in some overarching way (suc=
h as
by &quot;an agreement to further a single design or purpose&quot;), these
courts have found no RICO association-in-fact. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See</i> Gregory P. Joseph, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
Civil
RICO: A Definitive Guide</i> 105-07 (3d ed. 2010). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I6GBJN50N3X00005X0600017"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NWB0040000400"></a>Without such a limitation, as
commentators have observed, one malefactor's&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp; series of
independent frauds could be cast as a RICO conspiracy, sweeping numerous ot=
her
individuals into a net of heightened liability under RICO, and doing so eve=
n if
each fraud was perpetrated quite independently of the others. Such a sweep
would seem to run afoul of the principle adopted by the Supreme Court in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Boyle</i> that individuals who act
&quot;independently and without coordination&quot; may not be treated as pa=
rt
of a RICO association-in-fact. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NWB0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Boyle</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4WGD-3S70-TXFX-12BB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 556 U.S. at=
 947
n.4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal17 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52N1PRP0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We agree further with Defendants
that, if proven, the facts alleged in the Complaint would establish that Da=
vid
engaged in a series of separate frauds involving different sets of individu=
als.
This, they say, is insufficient to make out relationships among &quot;the
defendants as a whole&quot; that would satisfy even <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Boyle</i>'s relaxed test for an association-in-fact. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6X5H52D6NWB0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5N4W-F6P1-F04C-W09R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>D'Addario</sp=
an></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5N4W-F6P1-F04C-W09R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 2017 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 41795, 2017 WL 1086772, at *19</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fami=
ly:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. That each defend=
ant
agreed to join forces with David to defraud the Estate in a particular way =
does
not support an inference that they all agreed to join forces with each othe=
r to
pursue a goal of defrauding the Estate over decades in a variety of ways.
Rather, at most, it suggests that Defendants (and in a few cases, perhaps, a
small subgroup of Defendants) each agreed with David to
engage&nbsp;[**47]&nbsp; in individual schemes.</span></p>

