<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*135]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Debra Ann Livingston</span>, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Circuit Judge</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
is a case about proximate cause under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;), 18 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
1961 </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>et seq.</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Plaintiff-Appellant Empire Merchants, LLC (&quot;Empire&quot;), a distribut=
or
of alcoholic beverages, is New York State's exclusive distributor for popul=
ar
brands like Johnnie Walker, Grey Goose, and Seagram's Gin. Empire alleges t=
hat
from at least 2008 to 2014, Defendant-Appellee Reliable Churchill LLLP
(&quot;Reliable&quot;) and (non-party) Republic National Distributing Compa=
ny
(&quot;RNDC&quot;), two of Maryland's largest liquor distributors, conspired
with retail liquor stores in Cecil County, Maryland (&quot;Cecil County
retailers&quot;) and New York City (&quot;New York retailers&quot;) to smug=
gle
liquor from Maryland to New York, in violation of New York liquor law. Empi=
re
sued Reliable and several Cecil County and New York retailers under RICO,
alleging that their bootlegging directly harmed Empire &quot;because every =
case
of alcohol smuggled into New York from Maryland was a lost sale by New York=
's
authorized distributors&#8212;of which Empire was the largest.&quot; J.A. 1=
72.
The defendants moved to dismiss&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, arguing in part that the smuggling operation did not
directly cause Empire to lose sales, and therefore that Empire had not
adequately alleged proximate cause under RICO. The district court agreed and
dismissed the case, and Empire appealed. Because Empire failed to allege
proximate cause adequately, we AFFIRM the judgment of the district court.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Factual Background</span></b>=
<a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn415' href=3D"#_ftn415" name=3D"_ftnref415" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
relevant here, the American alcohol industry consists of three different gr=
oups
of entities: (1) suppliers, who produce the alcohol in breweries, vineyards,
and distilleries; &nbsp;[*136]&nbsp; (2) distributors, who purchase liquor =
from
suppliers in bulk and sell it to retail liquor stores, restaurants, and bar=
s;
and (3) retailers, the liquor stores, restaurants, and bars that ultimately
sell liquor to consumers. Many suppliers and distributors enter into contra=
cts
with one another, giving the distributors &quot;the exclusive right to
distribute that supplier's products in-state.&quot; J.A. 200. Both the fede=
ral
government and New York State license suppliers and distributors, and many
states and municipalities tax the sale of liquor. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>See, e.g.</i>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S9D-WC42-D6RV-H1GJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>27 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
203(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT2-YFF1-6RDJ-84PD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Alco. Be=
v.
Cont. Law &sect; 62</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Empire
is the largest liquor distributor in the New York metropolitan area
and&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; has exclusive distribution contracts for that area with
some of the world's leading liquor suppliers, giving it exclusive rights to
distribute popular brands like Johnnie Walker and Smirnoff. It alleges that
from at least 2008 to 2014, Reliable and RNDC conspired with the Cecil Coun=
ty
and New York retailers to smuggle liquor from Maryland to New York, thus
violating state and federal law, interfering with Empire's exclusive New Yo=
rk
distribution rights, and depriving Empire of millions of dollars in lost sa=
les.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn416' href=3D"#_ftn416" name=3D"_ftnref416" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
scheme was simple. The New York retailers would make interstate phone calls=
 and
send interstate faxes and emails to Cecil County retailers requesting liquo=
r,
and the Cecil County retailers passed on the orders to Reliable and RNDC.
Reliable and RNDC sold the requested products to the Cecil County retailers=
 at
discount. The New York retailers paid for the liquor in cash (sometimes up =
to
$20,000 at a time) and smuggled it in vans and trucks from Maryland to New
York. The Cecil County retailers deposited the cash into bank accounts and
wrote checks from those accounts to pay Reliable and RNDC.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn417' href=3D"#_ftn417" name=3D"_ftnref417" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Empire
alleges that Reliable knew about the smuggling and that many of its
employees&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; encouraged and even helped coordinate the scheme.
Some Reliable employees helped Cecil County retailers remove Maryland stick=
ers
from its liquor crates to help smugglers evade detection, at least one Reli=
able
salesman lied to a Cecil County retailer who expressed concern that Reliable
was encroaching on Empire's exclusive rights, and Reliable may have even be=
en
in direct contact with the smuggling New York retailers. Both Reliable and =
RNDC
also track retailers' weekly sales and would have noticed the large purchas=
ing
discrepancies in rural Cecil County. And many Cecil County and New York
retailers, according to the Amended Complaint, have admitted to smuggling.<=
/span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn418' href=3D"#_ftn418" name=3D"_ftnref418" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span><=
/sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*137]&nbsp;
At all relevant times during the alleged smuggling operation, Maryland's ex=
cise
liquor tax was $1.50/gal, and the total excise tax in New York City (state =
and
local tax included) was $7.44/gal. In both Maryland and New York, distribut=
ors
are responsible for paying alcohol excise taxes. The operation &quot;was
predicated on&quot; this $6/gal tax difference. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Id.</i> at 204. Smuggling allowed the New York retailers to buy liq=
uor
at a discount and then sell it at New York prices. For the Cecil County
retailers, Reliable, and RNDC, the bootlegging scheme&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; open=
ed a
much larger market, as Cecil County has a population of 78,000 people, about
0.3% the population of metropolitan New York. Profits soared. One Cecil Cou=
nty
retailer sold $300,000 of liquor to New York in a nine-day period. Reliable
alone sold over 272,000 cases (5.8 million bottles) as part of this smuggli=
ng
operation, &quot;resulting in gross revenues of more than $40 million.&quot=
; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 181.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>But
the operation cost New York State, New York City, and, according to the Ame=
nded
Complaint, Empire. &quot;By willfully avoiding the payment of the much high=
er
New York State and New York City excise tax on alcohol,&quot; the Amended
Complaint alleges, the defendants deprived the State and City &quot;of mill=
ions
