k'>Second,
Empire argues that it was more directly injured than New York State because
&quot;the government lost tax revenue only because Empire lost sales that w=
ould
have generated that revenue.&quot; Reply Br. 7. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6SR9828T4NS0050000400"></a>This is incorrect. As explai=
ned
above, New York State's &quot;entitlement to tax revenue&quot; is a property
interest, and so it <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>was</i> deprive=
d of
that interest when Reliable directed&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; its sales to New York
without paying the New York excise taxes. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6SR9828T4NS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G1N-60S0-004C-0010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pasquantino</=
span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G1N-60S0-004C-0010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 544 U.S. at=
 357</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Empire, by contrast, alleged that it was injured because it <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>would have</i> sold more liquor but fo=
r the
smuggling operation. These hypothetical lost sales are thus far less direct
than defendants' abrogation of New York State's property interest.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn426' href=3D"#_ftn426" name=3D"_ftnref426" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Third,
Empire claims that it &quot;seeks recovery for its own unique injury (stolen
&nbsp;[*146]&nbsp; sales), not for unpaid taxes,&quot; and it is clearly
therefore the most &quot;directly injured victim[].&quot; Reply Br. 17. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PV0RV2DXD000RG2Y40052B"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6SR982N1PRJ0020000400"></a>But this just shows that Emp=
ire's
injury is <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>distinct</i> from New Yor=
k's,
not that its injury was <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>directly ca=
used</i>
by the defendants' racketeering. Indeed, the plaintiffs in <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Anza</i> also allegedly lost sales, but the Supreme Court still
determined that New York was the most &quot;immediate victim[]&quot; of the
defendants' racketeering scheme. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Se=
e </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5T6SR982N1PRJ0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anza</span></=
i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 547 U.S. at=
 460</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fourth,
Empire appears to contend that we should disregard, or at least pay little
attention to New York's injury when assessing whether Empire was directly
injured. Otherwise, Empire suggests, we would &quot;leave[] no space for
private RICO claims arising out of conduct that injures the government as w=
ell
as a private party.&quot; Reply Br. 19. This would cripple civil
RICO,&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; Empire adds, because &quot;over a dozen RICO predic=
ates
necessarily harm the government.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Id.</i>
at 9. But as in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bridge</i>, private
plaintiffs may still sue if their injuries are sufficiently <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>direct</i>. That is just not the case =
here.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal18 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5PV0RV2JRJ000RG2Y40052C"></a><a
