<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2Y20000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2Y40000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In their statement in support of
their motion for summary judgment, the defendants allege that &quot;the
Agricultural and Industrial Fair of Clinton County, Inc. established in 197=
6 a
'booth rule' which requires all political, charitable, religious, commercia=
l or
other vendors to conduct any solicitation at the Fair from booths rented for
that purpose.&quot; (L.R. 7.1(f) Statement, P 3). The Supreme Court defined=
 the
constitutionally permissible limits of booth rules in the 1981 case of </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2Y10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heffron v. In=
t'l
Soc. for Krishna Consciousness,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 452 U.S. 640=
, 69
L. Ed. 2d 298, 101 S. Ct. 2559 (1981)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The <u>Heffron</=
u>
Court stated that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_17"></a>&quot;the </spa=
n><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not guarantee the right to communicate one's views at all times and pl=
aces
or in any manner that may be desired,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2Y30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 647</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citations omitted), and held that activities protected by the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;are subject to reasonable time, place, and manner restrictions.&quot;=
 <u>Id</u>.
The <u>Heffron</u> Court considered three factors in assessing the
reasonableness of rules restricting the exercise of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
