:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>i. Does Clinton County's Booth R=
ule
Discriminate on the Basis of the Content or Subject Matter of Speech?</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to be valid, a rule restricting the exercise of activities protected =
by
the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
must be content neutral. Defendants allege that the County Fair's booth rule
applies to &quot;all political, charitable, religious, commercial or other
vendors&quot; who seek to &quot;conduct any solicitation.&quot; (L.R. 7.1(f)
Statement, P 3). Defendants further assert, via the uncontroverted affidavi=
t of
Fair Manager LePage, that &quot;booths were distributed on a straightforward
first-come, first-served basis to all comers and one was expressly offered =
to
plaintiff.&quot; (LePage Aff. P 6).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YF0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As characterized by defendants, =
and
uncontradicted in any form by plaintiff, Clinton County's booth rule clearly
satisfies the &quot;content-neutrality&quot; prong of the <u>Heffron</u> te=
st.
Indeed, Clinton County's rule is nearly identical to the Minnesota booth ru=
le
upheld in <u>Heffron</u>. First, like the rule in &nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; <u>Hef=
fron</u>,
Clinton County's rule does not discriminate against certain kinds of speech,
but &quot;applies evenhandedly to all who wish to distribute and sell writt=
en
materials or to solicit funds.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YD0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heffron</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. at=
 649</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Second, like the Minnesota rule, the &quot;method of allocating space is a
straightforward first-come, first-served system.&quot; <u>Id</u>. In <u>Hef=
fron</u>,
the Supreme Court expressly approved the &quot;first-come, first-served&quo=
t;
system of allocation on the grounds that such a system avoided the danger of
&quot;more covert forms of discrimination that may result when arbitrary
discretion is vested in some governmental authority.&quot; <u>Id</u>. </spa=
n><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn439' href=3D"#_ftn439" name=3D"_ftnref439" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>ii. Does Clinton County's Booth =
Rule
Serve a Significant Governmental Interest?</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YH0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc5_16"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp;
Second Circuit has held that although a rule restricting the exercise of </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
activities &quot;must be narrowly tailored to serve a significant governmen=
tal
interest, &nbsp;[*371]&nbsp; it need not be the least restrictive or least
intrusive means of serving that interest. Rather, it simply must promote a
substantial governmental interest that would be achieved less effectively
without the rule.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YG0000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y8H0-008H-V12R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Paulsen v.
Gotbaum,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-Y8H0-008H-V12R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 982 F.2d 825,
829 (2nd Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I3VWNFMX9RW000BSTP700352"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YK0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Defendants assert two justificat=
ions
in support of the validity of the Clinton County Fair booth rule. First, th=
ey
assert &quot;an interest in maintaining the orderly flow of pedestrian
traffic.&quot; Second, they assert an &quot;interest in preventing fraud,
duress or harassment in the solicitation of Fair patrons &#8230; by confini=
ng
such solicitation-type activities to booths in certain areas [where] sheriff
deputies could more easily monitor the situation.&quot; (LePage et al., Mem=
o.
et al., Memo. of Law, p. 7). Defendants' uncontroverted interests are ident=
ical
to those asserted by Minnesota fair officials in <u>Heffron</u>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YN0000400"></a>In that case, the Supreme Co=
urt
established that &quot;as a general matter &#8230; a State's interest in
protecting the 'safety and convenience' of persons&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; using a
public forum is a valid governmental objective.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heffron</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. at=
 650</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YN0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3030000400"></a>The Court also indicated tha=
t a
forum's &quot;special attributes,&quot; and the &quot;nature and function of
the particular forum involved&quot; were relevant to determining the
significance of the asserted interest. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3050000400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 650-51</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3030000400_2"></a>In particular, the Court
distinguished fairgrounds from city streets, observing that &quot;the flow =
of
the crowd and demands of safety are more pressing in the context of the
Fair,&quot; which &quot;is a temporary event attracting great numbers of vi=
sitors.&quot;
<u>Id.</u>, Finally, the Court noted that without the rule, &quot;there wou=
ld
be a much larger threat to the State's interest in crowd control.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ2YP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 653</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Since the government interests plausibly asserted by the defendants are nea=
rly
identical to those which the Supreme Court considered and approved of in <u=
>Heffron</u>,
it is clear that the defendants satisfy the requirement &quot;that a place =
or
manner restriction must serve a substantial state interest.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, at 654</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>iii.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3050000400_3"></a>Did the rule leave open
alternative channels for communication of the information?</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3070000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6_16"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
be valid as a place and manner restriction, it must &#8230; be sufficiently
clear that&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; alternative forums for the expression of
respondents' protected speech exist despite the effects of the Rule.&quot; =
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3060000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heffron</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. at=
 654</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ30G0000400"></a>Again noting the resemblance=
 of
Clinton County's booth rule to that of the Minnesota Fair rule upheld in <u=
>Heffron</u>,
there is little doubt that Clinton County's rule meets this requirement. Fi=
rst,
the rule did not ban plaintiff from soliciting signatures &quot;anywhere
outside the fairgrounds,&quot; nor did it prevent him from soliciting
signatures <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>within</i> the fairgroun=
ds,
provided that he rented a booth. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ30G0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ30F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id,</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 655</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Defendants also note that plaintiff's campaign workers &quot;could have min=
gled
with the crowd orally urging them to adopt his views.&quot; (LePage et al.,
Memo. of Law, p.9).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>iv. Conclusion:</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ30J0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As the Supreme Court noted in <u=
>Heffron</u>,
&quot;the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
protects the right of every citizen to 'reach the minds of willing listeners
and to do so there must be an opportunity to win their attention.'&quot; </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ30H0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Heffron</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-69V0-003B-S0DF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 452 U.S. at=
 655</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Nevertheless, reasonable restrictions can be imposed on the exercise of </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
activities in the furtherance of substantial countervailing government
interests. The foregoing analysis compels the conclusion&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; =
that
Clinton County's booth rule is a reasonable restriction on the exercise of =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
activities. First, the rule is content neutral and is applied evenhandedly.
Second, the rule furthers the substantial government interest in safety and
crowd control. Third, the rule leaves open ample alternative forums in which
plaintiff could carry on his protected activities.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
there is no material question of fact in dispute, and because plaintiff's
deposition testimony and defendants' affidavits, exhibits, and memoranda of=
 law
establish that no violation of plaintiff's </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-T2X2-D6RV-H374-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>First Amendme=
nt</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
rights occurred as a result of any defendants' actions, defendants' Motion =
for
Summary Judgment on plaintiff's amended first cause of action is GRANTED, a=
nd
that cause of action is DISMISSED.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*372]&nbsp;
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>b. Plaintiff's Third Amended Cause=
 of
Action:</b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
gravamen of plaintiff's third amended cause of action is identical to that =
of
his original second cause of action: that defendant McHugh (with the assist=
ance
of McHugh Independent Petition Does 1, 2 and 3) circulated petitions for an
independent McHugh candidacy at a time when &quot;defendants had already kn=
own
that defendant McHugh was going into the&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; primary [against=
 an
opponent] as republican candidates.&quot; Cmplt. P 75. Unfortunately,
plaintiff's claim as amended suffers from the same deficiency it evidenced =
as
initially drawn: it fails to provide any basis from which the Court can
identify a claim upon which relief could be granted. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn440' href=3D"#_ftn440" name=3D"_ftnref440" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;
Assuming all plaintiff's factual allegations are true (as the Court must wh=
en
contemplating dismissal under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>),
and that defendant McHugh did in fact circulate petitions as both the
Republican Party candidate and as an independent candidate of his Voters'
Rights Party, the Court nevertheless finds that plaintiff has failed to all=
ege,
and the Court can conceive of, no statutory, legal or equitable prohibition
against such &quot;dual track&quot; petitioning (as plaintiff styles it). In
short then, plaintiff fails to allege any basis for his assertion that McHu=
gh's
(or any other defendants') conduct in this regard is actionable. As a basis=
 for
relief plaintiff asserts only that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>78.
By allowing a party candidate to harbor also an independent candidacy is
improper and should be declared null and void by the Court when applied to
federal office holders.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>79.
That accordingly, plaintiff prays that the Court grant judgment declaring t=
he
&quot;dual track&quot; candidacy evidenced by the McHugh Republican and Rig=
ht
to Vote candidacy as repugnant and hence unlawful.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(Cmplt.
at PP 78-79).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Conclusory
terms such as &quot;improper, &quot;&quot;unlawful&quot; and
&quot;repugnant&quot; are simply insufficient&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; to place
defendants on notice of the violations of law, or of plaintiff's rights,
plaintiff has twice attempted to assert against them.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff's
failure to state the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>legal</i> natu=
re of
his claims against these defendants aside, plaintiff also fails to allege a=
ny
sufficient injury <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>in fact</i> suffe=
red by
him and attributable to these defendants. The closest plaintiff comes to
asserting <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>any</i> injury is that:</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>76.
[Defendants acted] with the knowledge that the second 'independent' petitio=
n,
while political safe harbor for defendant McHugh, would deplete the availab=
le
numbers of registered voters for other candidates. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ31Y0000400"></a>As the time for the Republic=
an
petitions came first in time, the independent petition would thus draw down=
 and
reduce that pool available to persons such as plaintiff. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn441' href=3D"#_ftn441" name=3D"_ftnref441" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>14</span>=
</sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ31Y0000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>(Cmplt. at P 76).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal19><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal19 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Once
again, even assuming that the pool of signatories available to &quot;persons
such as &nbsp;[*373]&nbsp; plaintiff&quot; was marginally reduced by defend=
ant
McHugh's petitioning as both a party and an independent candidate, the Court
finds that such an amorphous and insubstantial assertion of injury will not
give rise to a cognizable federal claim. <u>See </u></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ31X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FJM0-008H-F40C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hotel Syracus=
e,
Inc. v. Young,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FJM0-008H-F40C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[**28]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FJM0-008H-F40C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 805 F. Supp.
1073, 1081 (N.D.N.Y. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (quoting </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCBV6Y0K1MNJ3200000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BXY0-003B-54FC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ricketts v.
Midwest Nat'l Bank,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BXY0-003B-54FC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 874 F.2d 117=
7,
1180 (7th Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclsc=
c8_15"></a>(Under
&quot;substantiality doctrine,&quot; court is required to &quot;dismiss a
complaint seeking recovery under the Constitution or laws of the United Sta=
tes
if the alleged federal claim either 'clearly appears to be immaterial and
solely made for the purpose of obtaining jurisdiction or where such a claim=
 is <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>wholly and insubstantial and
frivolous.'&quot;)</i> (emphasis added) (citations omitted).</span></p>

