k'>Prior
History:</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>&nbsp;Appeal from a final judgment, entered on April 13,
2016, in the United States District Court for the Southern District of New =
York
(Griesa, J.), granting defendant Republic of Argentina's motion to dismiss =
with
prejudice pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Civ. =
P.
12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> and denying as moot plaintiffs' motion for injunctive
relief and expedited discovery. The district court dismissed plaintiffs'
Amended Complaint because plaintiffs failed to allege that they had entered
into valid, countersigned contracts with defendant. The language of the
purported contracts expressly required the defendant's countersignature for=
 the
contracts to be binding, but defendants never signed the contracts. We hold
under this Court's precedents and New York state law that, when an agreement
expressly requires a party's countersignature to be binding and the factors=
 set
out in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Winston v.
Mediafare Entertainment Corp., 777 F.2d 78, 80 (2d Cir. 1985)</span></i></a=
><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**1]&nbsp;
otherwise indicate that the parties did not intend to be bound, no valid
contract exists in the absence of a party's countersignature.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
we AFFIRM the judgment of the district court.</span></p>

