<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*827]&nbsp;
<span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Per Curiam</span>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
more than a decade of litigation, defendant-appellee the Republic of Argent=
ina
(the &quot;Republic&quot;) sought to settle with certain holders of its
defaulted bonds. Plaintiffs-appellants hold defaulted bonds and claim that =
they
entered into binding settlement agreements with the Republic. The Republic
responds that it was not bound by the purported agreements because it did n=
ot
countersign them. There is no dispute that the Republic never signed the
agreements. The question before us is whether the unexecuted agreements were
nonetheless binding.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32D6NH70020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32D6NH70040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our precedents and those of the =
New
York Court of Appeals supply a clear answer: <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFcls=
cc1_19"></a>&quot;[I]f
the parties to an agreement do not intend it to be binding upon them until =
it
is reduced to writing and signed by both of them, they are not bound and may
not be held liable until it has been written out and signed.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32D6NH70010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VX20-003C-C48N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Scheck v. Fra=
ncis</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-VX20-003C-C48N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 26 N.Y.2d 4=
66,
260 N.E.2d 493, 494, 311 N.Y.S.2d 841 (N.Y. 1970)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
We generally look to the four factors set&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; out in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32D6NH70030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Winston v.
Mediafare Entertainment Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 777 F.2d 78=
, 80
(2d Cir. 1985)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, to determine whether parties intended not to be bound
until the formalities of execution were satisfied. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX4TMB00003HJ50004X"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710010000400"></a>In the present case, the fir=
st
factor is most prominent. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710010000400_2"><=
/a>The
purported agreements between plaintiffs and the Republic expressly require =
the
Republic's countersignature before binding the parties. The other </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710010000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32D6NH70050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Winston</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
factors also indicate that the parties did not intend to be bound in the
absence of a countersigned agreement. Accordingly, we affirm the judgment of
the district court (Griesa, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>J.</i>)=
.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>BACKGROUND</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <u>The
Republic's Settlement Proposal</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In 2001, the Republic defaulted =
on
bonds issued pursuant to a 1994 Fiscal Agency Agreement. The Republic's def=
ault
spawned extensive litigation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, =
e.g., </i>&nbsp;[*828]&nbsp;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5965-B351-F04K-J00P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NML Capital, =
Ltd.
v. Republic of Argentina</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5965-B351-F04K-J00P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 727 F.3d 230
(2d Cir. 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J32SF8710040000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56X0-3TN1-F04K-J26C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NML Capital, =
Ltd.
v. Republic of Argentina</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:56X0-3TN1-F04K-J26C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 699 F.3d 246
(2d Cir. 2012)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. As part of this litigation, the district court entered=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctions barring the
Republic from making payments on certain other bonds unless it also made
payments on its defaulted bonds.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 5, 2016, the Republic published a settlement proposal (the
&quot;Proposal&quot;) to holders of its defaulted debt. The Proposal presen=
ted
two offers to holders of defaulted bonds. Bondholders without <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctions could recei=
ve the
amount of their original principal plus 50% of&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; that princi=
pal.
Bondholders with <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i>
injunctions could receive payment of any money judgment or accrued claim
against the Republic, less a discount of 30%; if bondholders with <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctions settled by
February 19, 2016, this discount would be reduced to 27.5%. The Proposal st=
ates
that it is &quot;subject . . . to the judicial decision ordering the liftin=
g of
the Pari Passu Injunctions.&quot; Joint App. 218 (boldface omitted).</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 17, 2016, the Republic supplemented the initial Proposal with three
documents: a set of Instructions for Bondholders to Accept its Settlement
Proposal (the &quot;Instructions&quot;), a Master Settlement Agreement, and=
 an
Agreement Schedule. The basic plan, as set forth in the Instructions, was f=
or
bondholders to complete an Agreement Schedule specific to their holdings,
including information about their bonds and the particular amount of money =
to
be paid. Once executed and exchanged, this bespoke Agreement Schedule would
become part of a Master Settlement Agreement, which contained standard terms
for all bondholders. Specifically, the Instructions state as follows:</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Holders
may become a party to a Settlement Agreement by executing and exchanging wi=
th
the&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; Republic a completed Agreement Schedule . . . . The ho=
lder
must complete, sign and send the Agreement Schedule to the Republic . . . .
That Agreement Schedule, when countersigned by the Republic, shall constitu=
te a
binding agreement between the parties to settle all claims in respect of the
bonds on the terms contained in the Master Settlement Agreement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Joint
App. 90-91.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
language of the Master Settlement Agreement and Agreement Schedule confirms=
 the
plan set out in the Instructions. The Master Settlement Agreement contains
standard provisions applicable to all settling bondholders, including a
provision that New York state law governs the agreement. The Master Settlem=
ent
Agreement also states:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
Master Settlement Agreement . . . is made, in accordance with the terms of =
the
Proposal . . . , between [the Republic] and the Holder identified in item (=
i)
of the Agreement Schedule signed by the parties in connection with this
Agreement (such Agreement Schedule, when executed and exchanged by the Repu=
blic
and the Holder, being an integral part of this Settlement Agreement).</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Joint
App. 92. The Agreement Schedule contains blanks for bondholders to provide
personal information and various&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; details about their bonds=
. It
also contains a blank for the monetary settlement amount above a statement =
that
&quot;[t]his Settlement Amount has been reconciled between the Republic and=
 the
Holder.&quot; Joint App. 97-98. The signature page of the Agreement Schedule
states in its entirety: &quot;By executing counterparts of this Agreement
Schedule in the space provided below and exchanging those counterparts, the
parties agree to be bound by the terms of the Settlement Agreement, as
completed by the information contained in this Agreement &nbsp;[*829]&nbsp;
Schedule.&quot; Joint App. 99. Below this text, there are two open lines ma=
rked
with &quot;/s/&quot; symbols. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</=
i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B. <u>The
District Court's Vacatur Order</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42D6NHJ0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Soon after the Republic posted t=
he
Proposal, it reached settlement agreements with several bondholders.
Thereafter, on February 11, 2016, the Republic moved to vacate the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctions. The distri=
ct
court lacked jurisdiction to lift some of the injunctions due to a pending
appeal, but, on February 19, 2016, it issued an Indicative Ruling explaining
that the district court would vacate these injunctions if two conditions we=
re
met: (1) the repeal of legislative obstacles to the Republic's settlement w=
ith
its bondholders and,&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; (2) &quot;full payment [by the Republ=
ic]
in accordance with the specific terms of each . . . agreement&quot; to
&quot;all plaintiffs that enter[ed] into agreements in principle with the
Republic on or before February 29, 2016.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See</i> Indicative Ruling at 23, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42D6NHJ0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5J47-V841-F04F-006W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NML Capital, =
Ltd.
v. Republic of Argentina</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5J47-V841-F04F-006W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No.
14-cv-8601-TPG, ECF No. 59, 2016 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 20633 (S.D.N.Y. Feb. 19,
2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Upon
remand after the Republic voluntarily dismissed two pending appeals, the
Republic moved the district court to enter the Indicative Ruling as an order
and to lift all of the injunctions. In making this request, the Republic
represented to the district court that it had already settled claims worth =
$6.2
billion and that &quot;[n]egotiations with other plaintiffs are ongoing.&qu=
ot;
Joint App. 120. The Republic also submitted seven settlement agreements in
principle to demonstrate its progress. Six of these agreements in principle
were countersigned by the Republic, but one, with Red Pines LLC, was not.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42D6NHJ0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>On March 2, 2016, the district c=
ourt
entered an order (the &quot;Vacatur Order&quot;) implementing its earlier
Indicative Ruling and vacating all of the injunctions upon the occurrence of
the two conditions set out in the Indicative Ruling, including that the
Republic make full payments to&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; all bondholders that entered
into agreements in principle before February 29, 2016. <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>See</i> Vacatur Order at 5, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42D6NHJ0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5J6T-GFT1-F04F-019D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NML Capital, =
Ltd.
v. Republic of Argentina</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5J6T-GFT1-F04F-019D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, No.
14-cv-8601-TPG, ECF No. 76, 2016 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 26355 (S.D.N.Y. March 2,
2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C. <u>The
Present Action</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs-appellants
in the present case are five holders of defaulted bonds. On or before Febru=
ary
29, 2016, per the Instructions, each plaintiff sent the Republic an Agreeme=
nt
Schedule via email. The Republic acknowledged receipt of each plaintiff's s=
ubmission
on February 29, 2016 or March 1, 2016. The Republic did not countersign
plaintiffs' Agreement Schedules, and no executed Agreement Schedules were
exchanged. On March 11, 2016, the Republic informed plaintiffs that it would
not agree to their Agreement Schedules.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
March 25, 2016, plaintiffs filed a Complaint seeking a declaratory judgment
that they had entered into binding agreements in principle with the Republi=
c by
February 29, 2016, such that the Republic had to pay them to lift the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctions under the t=
erms
of the Vacatur Order. Plaintiffs also sought injunctive relief barring the
Republic from notifying the district court that all settling bondholders had
received full payment, in&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; satisfaction of the Vacatur Orde=
r's
second condition precedent, without first paying plaintiffs. On April 6, 20=
16,
the Republic moved to dismiss plaintiffs' Complaint, and the following day
plaintiffs filed an Amended Complaint with the same claims for declaratory =
and
injunctive relief. The district court expedited briefing on the Republic's
motion to dismiss and, in an Opinion dated April 12, 2016, dismissed
plaintiffs' Amended Complaint in its entirety. &nbsp;[*830]&nbsp; The distr=
ict
court denied plaintiffs leave to amend their pleadings a second time.
Plaintiffs timely appealed.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;font-variant:small-caps;color:black'>Discussi=
on</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42SF8760010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J42SF8760030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_19"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&quot;We
review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> a district court=
's
dismissal of a complaint pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
