<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I
would remand for a determination by the district court regarding whether the
bonds owned by appellants are eligible to accept the Republic of Argentina's
offer of settlement of litigation. My colleagues avoid this issue, which is
minor in the overall context of this protracted litigation but at the heart=
 of
the present appeal, by broadly and adventurously holding that the detailed
&quot;offer,&quot; as labeled by the Republic itself, was not an
&quot;offer&quot; but rather merely an invitation to bondholders to each ma=
ke
their own offer, each of which the Republic was free to accept or refuse.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>a) <u>Summary</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
contract before us is not uncommon in contract law.&nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; It is
known in academic writings as a unilateral contract. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3G6K00003HJ500042"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J728T4WK0050000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See</i><span style=3D'font-variant:small-caps'>Arthur L. Corbin, </=
span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:62F8-0RK0-R03J-V47S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;font-variant:small-caps;color:#0077CC;backgro=
und:
white'>Corbin on Contracts</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Danalytical-materia=
ls&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:62F8-0RK0-R03J-V47S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;font-variant:small-caps;color:#0077CC;backgro=
und:
white'> &sect; 1.23</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (Joseph E. Murray, Jr. ed., 201=
8)
(&quot;A unilateral contract consists of a promise or group of promises mad=
e by
one of the contracting parties only, usually assented to by the other.&quot=
;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3HS200003HJ500043"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0020000400"></a>As relevant here, such a con=
tract
involves an offer by a promisor to confer a benefit on a promisee who accep=
ts
by a specified performance. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i=
></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J728T4WK0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-GR40-008H-F395-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Wachovia Bank=
 of
Georgia, N.A. v. Apex Tech of Georgia, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-GR40-008H-F395-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 144 B.R. 64=
9,
654 (S.D.N.Y. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3MW300003HJ500045"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3S0B00003HJ500047"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0040000400"></a>Such an offer is binding and
enforceable, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRM-R6Y0-003F-624M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>I. &amp; I.
Holding Corp. v. Gainsburg</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRM-R6Y0-003F-624M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 276 N.Y. 42=
7,
434, 12 N.E.2d 532 (N.Y. 1938)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Ti=
mes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, even though there is no meetin=
g of
the minds as in a bilateral contract, &nbsp;[*834]&nbsp; although the promi=
sor
can challenge the adequacy of the requisite performance. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0040000400_2"></a>An offer of a specified re=
ward
for finding a fugitive is enforceable, even though the promisor can claim t=
hat
the wrong person was discovered and refuse the benefit, subject to a judici=
al
adjudication. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0030000400"></a>Carlill v. Carbolic Smoke Ba=
ll Co.</i>,
1 Q.B. 256 (Court of Appeal, 1892); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WR0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JG00-003B-H0D1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Shuey v. Unit=
ed
States</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JG00-003B-H0D1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 92 U.S. 73,=
 23
L. Ed. 697, 11 Ct. Cl. 358 (1875)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:=
"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3W3N00003HJ500049"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WS0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX509700003HJ500051"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WS0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>My colleagues hold that the
Republic's &quot;offer&quot; was not binding because it did not meet the </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WS0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Winston v.
Medialive Entertainment Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 777 F.2d 78=
 (2d
Cir. 1985)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, test showing a meeting of the minds. But </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J828T4WS0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CNX0-0039-P2M3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Winston</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
does not apply to the enforceability of unilateral contracts, which does not
require a meeting of the minds.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
a promisor, the Republic of Argentina, made a detailed &quot;offer&quot; --=
 its
chosen word -- to certain known&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; and unknown holders of
Argentine bonds on which the Republic had defaulted. The offer contained
mathematical formulae for determining the settlement amount and details
regarding payment. The offer stated that its effectiveness was conditioned =
<u>only</u>
upon the passage of Argentinean legislation allowing the payments and the
lifting by the federal courts of <u>pari passu</u></span><a style=3D'mso-fo=
otnote-id:
ftn453' href=3D"#_ftn453" name=3D"_ftnref453" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
injunctions that had the effect of denying the Republic of Argentina access=
 to
international financial markets.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Each
bondholder could accept the offer by establishing its eligibility as a
designated promisee. To that end, bondholders had to fill out an application
expressly limited by the offer to information concerning: (i) each applican=
t's
ownership of certain Argentina-issued bonds, (ii) the identified bond's
eligibility status under the Republic's offer of settlement, and (iii) the
calculation of the cash due under the offer on the eligible bonds. The prom=
isor
could dispute the eligibility of a party to accept the offer but, if
eligibility was shown, the offer would be binding.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
offer stated that a bondholder's signed showing of eligibility to participa=
te
in the settlement had to be countersigned by the Republic.&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp=
; My
colleagues hold that this countersignature requirement authorized the Repub=
lic
to repudiate its offer in its entirety by withholding the countersignature =
and
leaving eligible bondholders free only to make their own offer to settle. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX3XNH00003HJ50004B"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J92N1RHT0040000400"></a>As demonstrated <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>infra</i>, this ruling is contrary to =
the
plain language of the offer. It also renders written page after page and
written provision after provision of the Republic's offer irrelevant nullit=
ies,
disregarding the fundamental principle of contact interpretation that every
provision be given meaning. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX53GJ00003HJ50=
0053"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J928T4WT0010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J92N1RHT0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-XGP0-003C-C1YM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Muzak Corp. v.
Hotel Taft Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRS-XGP0-003C-C1YM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1 N.Y.2d 42,
46, 133 N.E.2d 688, 150 N.Y.S.2d 171 (N.Y. 1956)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J928T4WT0010000400_2"></a>Moreover, the nullification
ignores the facts that these writings were the product of a federal court's
mandating of negotiations and that the creation of the writings was supervi=
sed
by a federal judicial officer, a Special Master. It also ignores the fact t=
hat
the Special Master,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn454' href=3D"#_ftn=
454"
name=3D"_ftnref454" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> the Republic,</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn455' href=3D"#_ftn455" name=3D"_ftnref455" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and the district&nbsp;[*835]&nbsp; court</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:=
ftn456'
href=3D"#_ftn456" name=3D"_ftnref456" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> all described the writings as
constituting a binding &quot;offer&quot; that bondholders could
&quot;accept.&quot; The district court even set a terminal date for
&quot;acceptance.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
writings contained now-nullified provisions in which the Republic
promised&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; to pay a specific percentage of either the monet=
ary
judgment amount or accrued claim value of designated bonds on which it had
defaulted. The writings also specifically stated that disputes over the
settlement agreement were to be resolved by the district court. These
provisions make no sense whatsoever if no binding offer exists.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX56MV00003HJ500055"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX586D00003HJ500056"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J928T4WT0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, the enforceability of
these writings was the explicit basis for the district court's lifting of t=
he
injunction, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2421-6N19-F16N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 62.1</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Indicative Ruling at 13, </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J928T4WT002000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5J47-V841-F04F-006W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>NML Capital L=
td.
v. The Republic of Argentina, 14-8601 Dkt. No. 59, 2016 U.S. Dist. LEXIS 20=
633
(Feb. 19, 2016)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and for this court's affirming=
 of
that lifting, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1J928T4WT0040000400"></a>Mandate of the U.S. Court of
Appeals for the Second Circuit, 14-8601 Dkt. No. 61 (Feb. 24, 2016). No iss=
ue
as to the enforceability of these provisions was raised until after the
injunction was lifted. No mention of the countersignature requirement was e=
ven
made before the injunction was lifted except for one occasion when it was
explained as a requirement to reflect the Republic's agreement as to the
eligibility of an application. The issue in this case, as originally posed =
by
appellees, was quite narrow: Are the bonds in question eligible under the
settlement offer given the fact that the bonds&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; by their o=
wn
terms were time-barred from enforceability?</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Indeed,
the Republic's performance under the agreement reflects its intent to bind
itself. The requisite legislation was passed, and the Republic paid $6.2
billion to bondholders who demonstrated eligibility. On the present record =
it
appears that every bondholder that owned indisputably eligible bonds and
accurately applied the offer's mathematical formulae was paid by the Republ=
ic.
To hold that payment of $6.2 <u>billion</u> and the lifting of an injunction
barring a large nation from international capital markets is, as a matter of
law, not even part performance indicating the enforceability of written ter=
ms
lacks logic but surely qualifies as a Guinness record.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
hold that the countersignature requirement repudiates the offer of a binding
settlement suggests a massive fraud intended by the Republic, and on, or
perhaps even by, the district court. And, because the descriptions of the
&quot;offer&quot; above were the basis aggressively argued by the Republic =
in
successfully seeking judicial relief, my colleagues' holding unmistakably
violates the doctrines of stare decisis and judicial estoppel.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>b) <u>Background</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
underlying litigation began in&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; December 2001 with Argenti=
na's
default on over $80 billion in external debt. Appellants are holders of bon=
ds
issued by Argentina between 1994 and 2001. In 2012, due to continuing
violations of the equal treatment provision of the Fiscal Agency
&nbsp;[*836]&nbsp; Agreement,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn457'
href=3D"#_ftn457" name=3D"_ftnref457" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> the district court began enteri=
ng
injunctions ordering that Argentina make ratable payments to other debthold=
ers
when it paid on so-called &quot;Exchange Bonds.&quot; Because no bank could
process payments without risking contempt proceedings, by late 2015, the
injunctions made it extremely difficult, actually impossible, for Argentina=
 to
access fully international capital markets. The district court ordered
negotiations between the parties that were supervised and facilitated by a
Special Master appointed by the court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX5C9D00003HJ500058"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2SF8810040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In late 2015, Mauricio Macri was
elected President of the Republic of Argentina and resolved to end the debt
crisis. In order to accomplish this, he needed an infusion of international
capital from new bond financing, but could not obtain it without relief from
the <u>pari passu</u> injunctions. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2SF881003000=
0400"></a>Complaint
at &para; 48, NML Capital Ltd. v. The Republic of Argentina, 14-8601 Dkt. N=
o. 1
(March 25, 2016). Therefore, on February 5, 2016, Argentina&nbsp;[**24]&nbs=
p;
issued a Spanish press release (&quot;Propuesta&quot; or &quot;Proposal&quo=
t;)
laying out a proposed framework for settling the claims of holders of defau=
lted
bonds. This framework was the product of the negotiations ordered by the
district court and facilitated by the Special Master. On February 17, the
Republic supplemented the Proposal by publishing three documents on its web=
site:
(a) a &quot;Master Settlement Agreement&quot;; (b) the &quot;Instructions f=
or
Bondholders to Accept its Settlement Proposal&quot;; and ( c) an
&quot;Agreement Schedule.&quot; Some bondholders were expected to use this
procedure to settle their dispute with the Republic, while others were expe=
cted
to seek more favorable terms by making counterproposals. Complaint at &para;
57. Because it was unknown whether the acceptance of the offer would be
sufficiently widespread to justify vacatur of the injunction, the Republic
stated its willingness to consider counteroffers if necessary.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>c) <u>The
Writings</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plain language -- and the very titles -- of the multiple writings constitut=
ing
the settlement agreement indicate beyond any doubt the specificity of the t=
erms
of the settlement offer and its intended enforceability.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
purpose of the &quot;Proposal,&quot; as stated by the
Republic,&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; was to &quot;propose[] a settlement to all hold=
ers
of the Republic's default bonds.&quot; The designated bonds were described =
as
the &quot;defaulted bonds in which a 'pari passu' injunction&quot; was issu=
ed
by the district court and which had the effect of denying access to
international capital markets by the Republic. The Proposal stated that hol=
ders
of such bonds could settle by agreeing</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>to
receive (i) in the case of such bonds for which a monetary judgment less a
discount of 30%, and (ii) in the case of such bonds for which no monetary
judgment has been rendered prior to February 1, 2016, the current accrued v=
alue
of the claims less a discount of 30%. The discounts referred to in (i) and =
(ii)
will be reduced to 27.5% on settlement agreements in principle executed thr=
ough
February 19, 2016.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*837]&nbsp;
The &quot;Proposal&quot; was subject to only two conditions. It stated:</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
proposal is subject to the approval of the Argentine Congress as well as a
decision of the U.S. District Court of the Southern District of New York to
lift the <u>pari passu</u> injunction following such approval by the Argent=
ine
Congress.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
countersignature requirement was not mentioned in the Proposal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 16, 2016, the Republic issued a long document&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp;
entitled &quot;Republic of Argentina Master Settlement Agreement.&quot; Like
the Proposal, it stated terms of the proposed settlement. Section 2 stated
&quot;Subject to satisfaction of the conditions set out in Section 6 below,=
 on
the Closing Date the Republic shall pay to the Holder, in full settlement of
the Holder's claims under the Bonds, the Settlement Amount.&quot; Section 6=
 is
entitled &quot;Conditions.&quot; It read:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
settlement and release contemplated by Sections 2 and 3 above are subject t=
o:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(i)
The repeal or abridgement of Law 26,017 (the &quot;<u>Lock Law</u>&quot;) a=
nd
the approval of the terms and conditions of this Agreement by the Argentine
Congress.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(ii)
The U.S. District Court of the Southern District of New York having permane=
ntly
lifted all <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>pari passu</i> injunctio=
ns
granted to certain holders of defaulted Argentine bonds.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(iii)
No action shall have been taken and no statute, rule, regulation or judicial
order shall have been enacted, adopted or issued by any government or
regulatory authority that would, as of the Closing Date, prevent any of the
actions set forth in this Settlement Agreement from taking place.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the event that the above conditions are not satisfied during the Closing
Period, a closing shall not occur, this Settlement Agreement
shall&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; terminate, and the parties shall have no further
obligations to each other under this Settlement Agreement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Section
6 made no mention of the Republic's retention of any discretion to reject an
acceptance of the settlement proposal by holders of eligible bonds so long =
as
the specified conditions are met. The Republic's countersignature was not s=
uch
a condition.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
third document, entitled &quot;Instructions for Bond Holders to Accept its
Settlement Proposal,&quot; was issued by the Republic of Argentina's Minist=
ry
of Finance and Public Credit. This related to a document attached to the Ma=
ster
Settlement Agreement entitled &quot;Agreement Schedule&quot; to be &quot;si=
gned
by the parties in connection with this Agreement.&quot; A form, Agreement
Schedule, was attached to the Master Settlement Agreement. It required that
owners of bonds identify themselves, provide specifics as to the bonds held=
, and
calculate the &quot;Settlement Amount.&quot; In that regard, the Instructio=
ns
stated:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
holder must provide the Republic with (i) the information about its bonds
called for by the attachment to the forms of Agreement Schedule contained in
the Master Settlement Agreement and (ii) the holder's calculation of the
Settlement Amount for its bonds&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; as called for by item 5
below.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Section
5 states:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Injunction
Bond Holders that execute and deliver to the email address included hereby =
an
Agreement Schedule prior to 5:00 pm New York time on February 19, 2016 will
have the Settlement Amount under options (ii) and (iii) of Section 4 above
calculated using a discount of 27.5%. Those Injunction Bond &nbsp;[*838]&nb=
sp;
Holders that execute and deliver to the email address below an Agreement
Schedule after 5:00 pm New York time on February 19, 2016 will have the
Settlement Amount under options (ii) and (iii) of Section 4 above calculated
using a discount of 30%.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
holder must &quot;complete, sign, and send the Agreement Schedule to the
Republic including the information set forth for in item 3 above. That
Agreement Schedule, when countersigned by the Republic, shall constitute a
binding agreement between the parties to settle all claims in respect of the
bonds on the terms contained in the Master Settlement Agreement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Section
4 of the Instructions states:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>If the holder is an Injunction B=
ond
Holder</span></i><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-=
family:
Times;color:black'>, it should also indicate how it wishes the Settlement
Amount for its Injunction Offer Bonds to be calculated. As described in the
February 5, 2016 Proposal, the options of such a holder are:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(i)
the Standard&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp; Offer (150% of the original principal amount=
 of
its bonds but not exceeding the value of any monetary judgment rendered by a
court in respect of those bonds, determined as of January 31, 2016),</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(ii)
if a monetary judgment has been entered in respect of its bonds, the origin=
al
amount of that monetary judgment updated by any applicable post-judgment
interest rate accrued through January 31, 2016, less a discount of 30%, and=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:40.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>(iii)
if a monetary judgment has not been entered in respect of the bonds, the
current accrued value of the claims (calculated through January 31, 2016) l=
ess
a discount of 30%.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
plain language, therefore, of the relevant documents stated very specific t=
erms
for settlement. The term &quot;Settlement Amount&quot; in the agreement
settlement documents did not, as my colleagues assert, allow an applicant to
offer any amount that it desired. Rather, the &quot;Instructions,&quot; sta=
ted
that that amount is &quot;the holder's calculation of the settlement amount=
 . .
. as called for by item 5&quot; in the &quot;instructions.&quot; Section 5
contained the formula for the settlement and is quoted above. The
&quot;Settlement Amount,&quot; therefore, was limited to a mathematical
calculation based on the offer. Indeed, the first paragraph&nbsp;[**30]&nbs=
p;
of Section 5 sets dates for acceptance of the offer by submitting an Agreem=
ent
Schedule based on the mathematical formulae set out in the offer.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Agreement Schedule contained spaces for the bondholder's signature and the
Republic's countersignature just below a statement that the signatures bind=
 the
signatories to the Settlement Agreement &quot;as completed by the informati=
on
[regarding ownership, eligibility of the bonds, and mathematical calculatio=
n of
the settlement] contained in this Agreement Schedule.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
countersignature requirement is, therefore, found only in a document issued=
 by
the Republic entitled &quot;Instructions for Bondholders to Accept its
Settlement Proposal.&quot; The Agreement Schedule was necessary to identify
relevant bondholders, to verify the eligibility of their bonds, and to
accurately apply the settlement formulae to those bonds. The Schedule
explicitly states that the countersignature requirement is intended to bind=
 the
Republic to the &quot;Settlement Agreement, <u>as completed by the informat=
ion
contained in this Agreement Schedule</u>.&quot; The countersignature
requirement was, therefore, intended only to reflect the Republic's agreeme=
nt
with &quot;the information contained&quot; in the relevant&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp;
document, not the intention of the &nbsp;[*839]&nbsp; Republic to be bound =
by
the offer. The Republic had the right to dispute the eligibility of the bon=
ds
described in a Schedule, to dispute the claims of ownership, and to challen=
ge
the application's math with regard to the mathematical formulae. The writin=
gs
explicitly provided that such disputes were to be resolved by the district
court. Until the <u>pari passu</u> injunction was lifted, no one on the pla=
net,
so far as I can tell, stated the settlement offer to be anything but
enforceable. Indeed, the countersignature requirement was of such a limited
nature that, in seeking to lift the <u>pari passu</u> injunction, the
Republic's motion was supported by an exhibit reflecting a purportedly
successful settlement with an Agreement Schedule that had not been
countersigned.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Clearly,
the requirement of a countersignature by the Republic of Argentina did not
reflect its discretionary power to accept a holder's offer of what the hold=
er
believed to be a fair settlement. Rather, the countersignature simply refle=
cted
the Republic's agreement with regard to the &quot;information&quot; provide=
d as
to the ownership of the bonds specified in the agreement schedule, their
eligibility under&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; -- inclusion within -- the settlement
offer, and the accuracy of the mathematical calculation of the amount accor=
ding
to the formulae quoted above in the instructions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX83H100003HJ50006M"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>There is also a record as to the
meaning of the countersignature requirement. On February 29, 2018, the Repu=
blic
of Argentina submitted a supplemental memorandum in support of its motion, =
by
order to show cause, to vacate the <u>pari passu</u> injunctions. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX5DVW00003HJ500059"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2SF8810050000400"></a>NML Capital v. The Republic =
of
Argentina, 14-8601, Dkt. No. 71 (Feb. 29, 2016). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0030000400_2"></a>Along with this memorandum=
, the
Republic submitted a Second Supplemental Declaration of Argentina's
Undersecretary of Finance, Santiago Bausili, dated February 29, 2016. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0020000400"></a>NML Capital v. The Republic =
of
Argentina, 14-8601, Dkt. No. 71(2) (Feb. 29, 2016).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX5J0600003HJ50005C"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX5KHV00003HJ50005D"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB28T4WW0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The declaration stated: &quot;As=
 a
result of the negotiation process overseen by the Special Master, the Repub=
lic
has entered into agreements in principle to settle claims made by numerous
bondholders.&quot; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB2HM6VG0040000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at &para; 6. He then went on to
append several such &quot;agreements in principle&quot; as exhibits. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1HPPHX5N9S00003HJ50005F"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB28T4WW0040000400"></a>Specifically, he stated,
&quot;Attached as exhibit 9 is a true and correct copy of the Agreement in
Principle between Red Pines LLC and the Republic of Argentina,
executed&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; as of February 28, 2016.&quot; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB28T4WW0010000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id.</i> at &para; 13. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB28T4WW0040000400_=
2"></a>This
document bore the signature of Red Pines, dated February 28, 2016, but lack=
ed a
countersignature. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JB28T4WW0030000400"></a>NML Cap=
ital
v. The Republic of Argentina, 14-8601, Dkt. No. 71(11) (Feb. 29, 2016). The
Republic explained:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
February 29, 2016, the Republic mistakenly included in the list of settleme=
nts
provided to the district court that it had reached an agreement in principle
with Red Pines LLC. In fact, while Red Pines signed a form of agreement, wh=
ich
it submitted to the Republic on February 28, 2016 (see A-2017-25), the Repu=
blic
subsequently determined that the submitted agreement provided for payment w=
ith
respect to claims that are timebarred. The Republic therefore did not
countersign the agreement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
sole ground asserted at that time -- the indicative ruling promising the
lifting of the injunction had not occurred -- concerned only the eligibilit=
y of
the bonds in question.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal21 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>d) <u>Contract
Law</u></span></p>

