ine of
judicial estoppel is governed by three factors: &quot;(1) that a party's new
position is 'clearly inconsistent' with its earlier position, (2) that the
party seeking to assert this new position previously persuaded a court to
accept its earlier position, and (3) that the party 'would derive an unfair
advantage or impose an unfair detriment on the opposing party if not
estopped.'&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5X9X1JG2N1RJ80010000400"></a><a
