BEA, Circuit Judge:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Today
we confront an issue of first impression in our circuit, and one that has
caused an apparent circuit split among four of our sister circuits: In civil
actions brought under the </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Racketeer
Influenced and Corrupt Organizations Act (&quot;RICO&quot;)</span></i></a><=
span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
against pharmaceutical companies, do patients and health insurance companies
who reimbursed patients adequately allege the required element of proximate
cause where they allege that, but for the defendant's omitted mention of a
drug's known safety risk, they would not have paid for the drug?</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. FACTUAL BACKGROUND</span></b>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
appeal arises from a putative class action against Takeda Pharmaceuticals U=
SA,
Inc., its parent company Takeda Pharmaceutical Company Ltd., and Eli Lilly
&amp; Co. (collectively, &quot;Defendants&quot;). Together, Defendants
developed and marketed a drug named Actos. Actos was intended to lower blood
sugar in type 2 diabetics. Defendants obtained Food and Drug Administration
(&quot;FDA&quot;) approval for Actos in 1999. The plaintiffs&nbsp;[**5]&nbs=
p;
allege that despite learning through multiple studies over the next several
years that Actos increased a patient's risk of developing bladder cancer,
Defendants refused to change Actos's warning label or otherwise inform the
public of such risk. Further, the plaintiffs allege that Defendants convinc=
ed
the FDA that studies revealing that Actos increased the risk of bladder can=
cer
were wrong. Defendants are alleged to have actively misled prescribing
physicians, consumers, and third-party payors into believing that Actos did=
 not
increase a person's risk of developing bladder cancer. Defendants did all of
this, the plaintiffs allege, simply to increase their profits from the sale=
 of
Actos.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
September 17, 2010, after further studies of Actos revealed an increased ri=
sk
of bladder cancer, the FDA announced that it was conducting a safety review=
 of
Actos. On June 15, 2011, the FDA released an official warning to the public
that Actos may be linked to bladder cancer in patients who use it over
prolonged periods of time. Following the FDA's official warning, Defendants
changed Actos's warning label to warn of a bladder cancer risk. The sales of
Actos are alleged to have dropped shortly after&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; the FDA is=
sued
its alert in 2010, and then again when the FDA issued its official warning =
in
2011, by a total of approximately 80%.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7928T4HJ0020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A group of patients who developed
bladder cancer after ingesting Actos and their family members then brought
personal injury and wrongful death claims against Defendants in the Western
District of Louisiana. After a 37-day trial in 2014, the jury returned a
verdict in favor of the plaintiffs, but the parties later agreed to a global
settlement program for all eligible personal injury claimants who used Actos
before December 1, 2011 and had been diagnosed with bladder cancer. </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7928T4HJ0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5P1V-XJJ1-F04D-C2TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Actos
(Pioglitazone) Prods. Liab. Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5P1V-XJJ1-F04D-C2TR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 274 F. Supp=
. 3d
485, 503 (W.D. La. 2017)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote=
-id:
ftn464' href=3D"#_ftn464" name=3D"_ftnref464" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
present action was also originally filed in the Western District of Louisia=
na.
But in late 2017, the parties stipulated to transfer the case to the Central
District of California. The plaintiffs in this case comprise five individual
patients from different states (collectively, &quot;Patients&quot;) and
Painters and Allied Trades District Council 82 &nbsp;[*1247]&nbsp; Health C=
are
Fund (&quot;Painters Fund&quot;) (together, &quot;Plaintiffs&quot;).</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Painters
Fund is a third-party payor (&quot;TPP&quot;) of health and welfare benefit=
s to
covered members and their families. As a TPP,&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; Painters Fund
reimburses its members' claims for drugs, including Actos, submitted by
pharmacies and healthcare providers covered by its plan. Painters Fund
&quot;relies on each member to submit claims for prescription medications t=
hat
are medically reasonable and necessary for treatment,&quot; with the
expectation that patients and their prescribing physicians will &quot;make
informed decisions about which drugs will be prescribed and, in turn, submi=
tted
to [Painters Fund] for reimbursement.&quot; Painters Fund &quot;has the
authority to determine which drugs are covered under its plan, although, [i=
t]
entrusts the administration of claims and formulary determinations to Prime
Therapeutics, LLC, based in Eagan, Minnesota.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-f=
ootnote-id:
ftn465' href=3D"#_ftn465" name=3D"_ftnref465" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Patients
are individuals with type 2 diabetes who were prescribed Actos by their
physicians and who took Actos to help lower their blood sugar. Each patient
paid an out-of-pocket sum for Actos. Patients each allege that neither they=
 nor
their physicians knew about Actos's risk of bladder cancer when they began
taking the drug and that they immediately stopped taking Actos once they
learned that it increased their risk of developing bladder cancer. Patients
also allege that they never would&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; have purchased Actos had
they known that it increased their risk of developing bladder cancer, and t=
hus,
that they never would have submitted claims for reimbursement for purchases=
 of
Actos to their respective TPPs. Only one patient, Annie Snyder from Califor=
nia,
alleges that prior to starting her prescription, she read and relied upon t=
he
Actos label. But Plaintiffs generally allege that Patients relied on
Defendants' misrepresentations about Actos, by act or omission, in purchasi=
ng
the drug, that physicians relied on such misrepresentations in prescribing
Actos for their patients, and that TPPs relied on such misrepresentations in
agreeing to pay for Actos prescriptions for their members.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiffs
seek to represent a class of similarly situated patients and TPPs &quot;who
paid or incurred costs for the drug Actos, for purposes other than resale,
between 1999, i.e., when the drug was approved, and the present,&quot;
excluding &quot;those consumers who are presently seeking a personal injury
claim arising out of their use of Actos.&quot; Plaintiffs argue that Defend=
ants
conspired to commit mail and wire fraud under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>1343</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> by
intentionally misleading physicians, consumers, and TPPs to believe that Ac=
tos
did&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; not increase a person's risk of developing bladder can=
cer.
Plaintiffs seek to recover economic damages under RICO for the payments they
made to purchase Actos under the assumption that it was a safe drug, which =
they
allege they would not have purchased had they known that Actos increases a
person's risk of developing bladder cancer (this is called the &quot;quanti=
ty
effect theory&quot; of damages).</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn466'
href=3D"#_ftn466" name=3D"_ftnref466" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Plaintiffs do not, however, see=
k to
recover economic or non-economic damages caused by any person's actual
ingestion of Actos.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court dismissed with prejudice Plaintiffs' RICO claims under
&nbsp;[*1248]&nbsp; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> in a single paragraph, holding =
that
Plaintiffs failed adequately to allege facts sufficient to establish that
Defendants' acts and omissions were the proximate cause of their claimed
damages. This appeal followed.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn467'
href=3D"#_ftn467" name=3D"_ftnref467" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. ANALYSIS</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A. Standard of Review</span></b>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7928T4HJ0040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_20"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> the district cou=
rt's
grant of a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
motion to dismiss. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7928T4HJ0030000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bain v. Cal. Teachers Ass'n, 891 F.3d =
1206,
1211 (9th Cir. 2018)</i>. We take all of Plaintiffs' factual allegations as
true, and we may affirm the dismissal &quot;only if it appears beyond doubt
that [Plaintiffs] can prove no set of facts in support of [their] claim[s]
which would entitle [them] to relief.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Id.</i>
(internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. Plaintiffs'</span></b><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;<b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'> RICO Claims</b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7B2N1R5B0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The crux of Plaintiffs' complaint
rests on their civil RICO claims. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7B2N1R5B0030000=
400"></a>Although
the RICO statute was originally enacted to combat organized crime, &quot;it=
 has
become a tool for everyday fraud cases brought against respected and legiti=
mate
enterprises.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7928T4HJ0050000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.R=
.L.
v. Imrex Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
499, 105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted). Broadly speaking, there are two parts t=
o a
civil RICO claim. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7B2N1R5B0030000400_2"></a>The c=
ivil
RICO violation is defined under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn468' href=3D"#_ft=
n468"
name=3D"_ftnref468" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>5</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> while &quot;RICO standing&quot;=
 is
defined under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The district court dismissed Plaintiffs' RICO claims o=
nly
for lack of standing, and thus we address only that portion of Plaintiffs' =
RICO
claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7B2N1R5B0050000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_20"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
allege civil RICO standing under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, a &quot;plaintiff must show: (1) that his alleged harm
qualifies as injury to his business or property; and (2) that his harm was =
'by
reason of' the RICO violation.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7B2N1R5B0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S3V-JNR0-TXFX-D2CG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Canyon County=
 v.
Syngenta Seeds, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4S3V-JNR0-TXFX-D2CG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 519 F.3d 96=
9,
972 (9th Cir. 2008)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Defendants do not dispute that
Plaintiffs have alleged an injury to their business or property. Rather, as=
 the
district court held, Defendants argue that Plaintiffs have failed to allege
that their harm was &quot;by reason of&quot; the alleged RICO violation bec=
ause
they have failed to allege the claimed RICO violation was the proximate cau=
se
of their claimed losses.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>1. Supreme Court Precedent</span=
></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4HX0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4HX0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc3_20"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Supreme Court has interpreted&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; the phrase &quot;by reason
of&quot; in </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'> to require, as elements for a civil RICO recovery, both
proximate and but-for causation.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn469'
href=3D"#_ftn469" name=3D"_ftnref469" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4HX0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes v. Sec.
Inv'r Prot. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. 25=
8,
268, 112 S. Ct. 1311, 117 L. Ed. 2d 532 (1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The requirement of proximate cause seeks to &quot;limit a person's
responsibility for the consequences of that person's own acts.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Put another way, &quot;the
proximate-cause requirement generally bars suits for alleged harm that is '=
too
remote' from the defendant's &nbsp;[*1249]&nbsp; unlawful conduct.&quot; </=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4HX0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BTS-W1F1-F04K-F003-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lexmark Int'l,
Inc. v. Static Control Components, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BTS-W1F1-F04K-F003-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 572 U.S. 11=
8,
133, 134 S. Ct. 1377, 188 L. Ed. 2d 392 (2014)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, it &quot;demand[s] . . . some direct relation between the injury asse=
rted
and the injurious conduct alleged.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4HX0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 268</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_20"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This &quot;direct relation&quot;
requirement is based upon three practical factors, stated in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>First,
the less direct an injury is, the more difficult it becomes to ascertain the
amount of a plaintiff's damages attributable to the violation, as distinct =
from
other, independent, factors. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2S7MV000076PF0002P">=
</a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20030000400"></a>Second, quite apart from pro=
blems
of proving factual causation, recognizing claims of the indirectly injured
would force courts to adopt complicated rules apportioning damages among
plaintiffs removed at different levels of injury from the violative acts, to
obviate the risk of multiple recoveries. And, finally, the need to grapple =
with
these problems is simply unjustified by the general interest
in&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; deterring injurious conduct, since directly injured
victims can generally be counted on to vindicate the law as private attorne=
ys
general, without any of the problems attendant upon suits by plaintiffs inj=
ured
more remotely.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 269-70</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal citations omitted). The Supreme Court has applied the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> factors, along with its dir=
ect
relation requirement, in each of its decisions addressing proximate cause f=
or
civil RICO claims.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SBRM000076PF0002S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Anza v. Ideal Steel Supply Corp.</i>, the plaintiff&#8212;a steel m=
ill
product retailer in New York City&#8212;alleged that one of its retail
competitors caused it economic harm by failing to charge customers applicab=
le
New York state sales taxes, thereby defrauding the New York state tax autho=
rity.
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7C28T4J20040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>547 U.S. 451,
457-58, 126 S. Ct. 1991, 164 L. Ed. 2d 720 (2006)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230020000400"></a>This conduct, the plaintiff
alleged, allowed the defendant to offer lower prices and attract more
customers, which in turn caused the plaintiff to lose customers and profit.=
 <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230040000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id.</i> The district court dismissed the plaintiff's complaint unde=
r </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for failure to plead proximate cause, but the Second Circuit vacated the
district court's judgment, holding that the plaintiff had adequately pleaded
that the defendant proximately caused its damages. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230020000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 455</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Supreme Court then reversed the Second Circuit's&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; judg=
ment
and held that the plaintiff failed to satisfy the requirement to allege
proximate cause under RICO because the &quot;direct victim of this conduct =
was
the State of New York, not [the plaintiff].&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K41-DS80-004C-200J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 458</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Indeed, &quot;[i]t was the State that was being defrauded and the State that
lost tax revenue as a result.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>Id.</i>
Although the plaintiff alleged that it suffered its own harms by losing
customers and profits through the defendant's failure to tax its customers,=
 the
plaintiff's asserted harms were &quot;entirely distinct from the alleged RI=
CO
violation (defrauding the state),&quot; and thus the plaintiff's allegations
failed the Supreme Court's direct relation requirement for the element of
proximate cause. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SD92000076PF0002T"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Likewise, in <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>Hemi Group, LLC v. City of New York</i>, the Supreme Court reversed=
 the
Second Circuit's holding that the plaintiffs had sufficiently alleged damag=
es
proximately caused by the defendants' actions under RICO to survive dismiss=
al
under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8230050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>559 U.S. 1, 4=
-5,
130 S. Ct. 983, 175 L. Ed. 2d 943 (2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240050000400"></a>There, the City of New York =
(the
&quot;City&quot;), which imposed a $1.50-per-pack tax on each pack of
cigarettes possessed within New York City for sale or use, sued a New Mexico
retailer that sold cigarettes online to residents in New York City. </span>=
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 4-6</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The City alleged&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; that the New Mexico &nbsp;[*1250]&nbsp;
retailer failed to comply with a federal law requiring out-of-state vendors=
 to
submit customer information to the states into which it ships cigarettes. <=
/span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2SF8240040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 4</span><=
/i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
That failure, the City argued, not only made it more difficult for the City=
 to
track down people who possessed cigarettes in New York City purchased
elsewhere, but also constituted mail and wire fraud under RICO, which caused
the City to lose millions of dollars in uncollected per-pack cigarette taxe=
s. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J50020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Supreme Court disagreed. It =
held
that the New Mexico retailer's failure to submit customer information to the
State of New York was too attenuated from the City's loss of cigarette
possession tax proceeds to satisfy the proximate cause allegation requireme=
nt. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J50010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 11</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Supreme Court explained that the conduct constituting the alleged fraud=
 was
the New Mexico retailer's failure to submit customer information to the Sta=
te
of New York, but &quot;the conduct directly responsible for the City's harm=
 was
the customers' failure to pay their taxes.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i> Thus, &quot;the conduct directly causing the harm was disti=
nct
from the conduct giving rise to the fraud,&quot; and therefore the City fai=
led
to satisfy the Supreme Court's direct&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; relation requiremen=
t. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SFVF000076PF0002V"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J50040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J80010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In contrast, in <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>Bridge v. Phoenix Bond &amp; Indemnity Co.</i>, the Supreme Court
affirmed the Seventh Circuit's reversal of the district court's order
dismissing the plaintiffs' complaint for failure sufficiently to allege the
proximate cause element under RICO. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J50030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>553 U.S. 639,
645, 661, 128 S. Ct. 2131, 170 L. Ed. 2d 1012 (2008)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The plaintiffs were bidders at a county tax lien auction. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J50050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 643</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J80030000400"></a>To ensure fair distribution =
of tax
liens during the auctions, the county enacted a &quot;Single, Simultaneous
Bidder Rule,&quot; requiring each &quot;tax [lien] buying entity&quot; to b=
id
in its own name and not to use agents or employees to submit simultaneous b=
ids
on its behalf. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> The plaintif=
fs
alleged that the defendants violated that rule by using agents to submit
simultaneous bids on the defendants' behalf and directing those agents to f=
ile
false attestations that they had complied with the county's rules. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J80020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 643-44</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J80050000400"></a>The plaintiffs alleged that =
this
deceptive practice resulted in the defendants receiving a disproportionately
higher share of tax liens at the county auction. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i> The plaintiffs further alleged that as a result of this
deceptive practice, they were deprived of their ability to obtain their fair
share of tax liens at the county auction. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D28T4J80040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 644</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2N1R5V0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7D2N1R5V0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The defendants
