name=3D"_ftnref472" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>9</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> we have never applied it
&nbsp;[*1253]&nbsp; to the situation at issue here&#8212;whether patients a=
nd
TPPs suing pharmaceutical companies for concealing an allegedly known safety
risk about a drug can satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement. </span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn473' href=3D"#_ftn473" name=3D"_ftnref473" titl=
e=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>10</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
But several of our sister circuits have addressed this question in similar
factual scenarios and have reached different results, creating an apparent
inter-circuit split. We look to their reasoning for guidance.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>a. Seventh Circuit</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SJYH000076PF0002X"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HB0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HB0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Sidney Hillman Health Center v. Abbott Laboratories</i>, two TPPs w=
ho
had paid to cover their patients' off-label</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-=
id:
ftn474' href=3D"#_ftn474" name=3D"_ftnref474" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>11</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
uses of&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; a prescription drug named Depakote sued the drug
manufacturer under RICO for concealing its role in promoting Depakote's
off-label uses to intermediaries, such as prescribing physicians. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HB0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>873 F.3d 574,=
 575
(7th Cir. 2017)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In relevant part, the district
court dismissed the TPPs' complaint for failure to allege that their damages
were proximately caused by the drug manufacturer's concealed off-label
promotion, and the Seventh Circuit affirmed. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HB0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 575, 578<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2D6N7B0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Seventh Circuit first noted
that, in some cases, an injury to one person caused by wrongs against anoth=
er
can satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement, as &nbsp;[*1254]&nbsp; the
Supreme Court held in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HB0050000400">=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 553 U.S. at=
 661</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2D6N7B0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>873 F.3d at 5=
76</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
However, the Seventh Circuit held that the TPPs in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>Sidney Hillman</i> were too far removed from the alleged RICO viola=
tion
to satisfy the proximate cause requirement. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>Id.</i>
The Seventh Circuit opined that while TPPs &quot;part with money . . . it is
not at all clear that they are the initially injured parties, let alone the
sole injured parties.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>=
 The
Seventh Circuit explained that patients may be the most directly injured
parties, as they incurred financial loss (if they paid a copayment to recei=
ve
Depakote) and personal injury damages if they suffered harmful effects from
using Depakote for an&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; unsafe off-label use. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Moreover, the Seventh Circuit =
noted,
the &quot;patients' health and financial costs come first in line temporall=
y;
that pharmacies then send bills to [TPPs], which cover the remainder of the
expense, does not make those [TPPs] the initial losers&quot; from the drug
manufacturer's unlawful promotion scheme. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>Id.</i>
The Seventh Circuit opined that prescribing physicians may also suffer loss,
though indirectly, because &quot;[i]f a physician prescribes an ineffective
medicine and so does not provide [patients] help, patients may turn
elsewhere.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2D6N7B0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Seventh Circuit next explain=
ed
that physicians make independent decisions when prescribing patients medici=
ne,
and it would be difficult to disentangle which physicians' decisions, if an=
y,
were influenced by the drug manufacturer's unlawful promotions. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2D6N7B0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 577-78</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S0020000400"></a>That, and other factors, suc=
h as
the fact that some patients may have benefited from using Depakote for an
off-label use, convinced the Seventh Circuit that it would be too difficult=
 to
calculate the plaintiffs' alleged damages. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S0020000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i> Thus, the Seventh Circuit held that the
TPPs&#8212;&quot;several levels removed in the causal sequence&quot; from t=
he
drug manufacturer's actions&#8212;could not satisfy RICO's&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp;
proximate cause requirement. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S00100=
00400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 576-78</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>b. Second Circuit</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SMPB000076PF0002Y"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S0040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HF0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly, in <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>UFCW Local 1776 v. Eli Lilly &amp; Co.</i>, TPPs and individual
patients brought a putative class action for civil RICO fraud against the
manufacturer of the drug Zyprexa, alleging that the manufacturer misreprese=
nted
Zyprexa's side effects and effectiveness to physicians and promoted Zyprexa=
 for
off-label uses when there was no evidence that Zyprexa was effective for
off-label uses. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>620 F.3d 121,
123, 129 (2d Cir. 2010)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The plaintiffs alleged two dam=
ages
theories: (1) the &quot;excess price theory&quot;&#8212;that they overpaid =
for
Zyprexa prescriptions because the manufacturer relied on its misrepresentat=
ions
to charge higher prices; and (2) the &quot;quantity effect
theory&quot;&#8212;that they paid for Zyprexa prescriptions &quot;that would
not have been issued but for the alleged misrepresentations.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> The district court certified a=
 class
of TPPs based upon their excess price theory for damages, but the Second
Circuit reversed. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2SF82S0050000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 123, 137<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HF0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G28T4JW0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G28T4JW0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As to the proximate cause
requirement under RICO, the Second Circuit held that the plaintiffs' injuri=
es
under both of their damages theories were too attenuated, as they &quot;res=
t[]
on the independent actions of third and even fourth parties.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HF0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 134</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G2HM6HF0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hemi Group</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:7XMP-8RB0-YB0V-912G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 559 U.S. at=
 15</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>).
The Second Circuit&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; was not persuaded by the plaintiffs'
argument that &quot;the ultimate source for the information on which doctors
based their prescribing decisions was [the manufacturer] and its consistent,
pervasive marketing plan,&quot; because the manufacturer was &quot;not . . .
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>only</i> source of information =
on
which doctors based prescribing decisions.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G28T4JW0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 135</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis in original). Rather, &quot;[a]n individual patient's diagnosis, =
past
and current medications being taken by the patient, &nbsp;[*1255]&nbsp; the
physician's own experience with prescribing Zyprexa, and the physician's
knowledge regarding the side effects of Zyprexa are all considerations that
would have been taken into account in addition to the alleged
misrepresentations distributed by [the manufacturer].&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Accordingly, the Second Circui=
t held
that the plaintiffs failed to allege that their damages were proximately ca=
used
by the drug manufacturer's wrongful conduct and reversed the district court=
's
certification order.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn475' href=3D"#_ft=
n475"
name=3D"_ftnref475" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>12</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7G28T4JW0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
