<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>c. First Circuit</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SP7W000076PF00030"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7G0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7G0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>To the contrary, in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>In re Neurontin Marketing &amp; Sales
Practices Litigation</i>, a jury awarded Kaiser Foundation Health Plan
(&quot;Kaiser&quot;), a TPP, damages&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; for the injury it
suffered in paying for off-label Neurontin prescriptions that were induced =
by
Pfizer's (the drug manufacturer) fraudulent scheme to misrepresent Neuronti=
n's
effectiveness for off-label conditions. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7G0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>712 F.3d 21,
25-26 (1st Cir. 2013)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The district court had found t=
hat
Kaiser relied on Pfizer's fraudulent marketing campaign in deciding to incl=
ude
Neurontin in its formulary&#8212;a list of medications its treating physici=
ans
were authorized to prescribe. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0010=
000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7G0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 28-29</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0010000400_2"></a>The district court subsequ=
ently
denied Pfizer's motion for a new trial, and Pfizer appealed. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7G0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 27</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Pfizer argued that Kaiser could =
not
satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement as a matter of law. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 34</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0020000400"></a>But the First Circuit disagr=
eed,
holding that &quot;Kaiser has met both the direct relationship and function=
al
tests articulated in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> and=
 its
progeny.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HJ0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 38</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0040000400"></a>Unlike the Second and Seventh
Circuits, the First Circuit rejected the argument that there were &quot;too
many steps in the causal chain between [Pfizer's] misrepresentations and
Kaiser's alleged injury&quot; to meet the &quot;direct relation&quot; requi=
rement.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0040000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0020000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i> Rather, the First Circuit held that &quot;the causal chain =
in
this case is anything but attenuated,&quot; because Pfizer &quot;has always
known that, because of the structure of the American&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; heal=
th
care system, physicians would not be the ones paying for the drugs they
prescribed.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0010000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 38-39</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Pfizer's fraudulent marketing scheme, which was meant to increase its sales=
 and
profits, &quot;only became successful once Pfizer received payments for the
additional Neurontin prescriptions it induced.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 39</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Those payments came from TPPs, including Kaiser. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7J0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The First Circuit also rejected
Pfizer's argument that &quot;because doctors exercise independent medical
judgment in making decisions about prescriptions, the actions of these doct=
ors
are independent intervening causes&quot; that cut off the chain of causatio=
n. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> The First Circuit explained th=
at
&quot;Pfizer's scheme relied on the expectation that physicians would base
their prescribing decisions in part on Pfizer's fraudulent marketing.&quot;=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> &quot;The fact that some physi=
cians
may have considered factors other than Pfizer's detailing materials in maki=
ng
their prescribing decisions does not add such attenuation to the causal cha=
in
as to eliminate proximate cause&quot;; rather, &quot;[t]his is a damages
question&quot; about the &quot;total number of prescriptions that were
attributable to Pfizer's actions.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>Id.</i>
Finally, the &nbsp;[*1256]&nbsp; First Circuit noted that &quot;[h]olding
Pfizer liable will&nbsp;[**27]&nbsp; have an effect in deterring wrongful
conduct,&quot; and thus it held that Kaiser had satisfied the proximate cau=
se
requirement under RICO. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7H0050000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:583X-49S1-F04K-H05P-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 39-40</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>d. Third Circuit</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SRTD000076PF00031"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7J0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7J0050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Similarly, in <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>In re Avandia Marketing, Sales Practices &amp; Product Liability
Litigation</i>, the Third Circuit held that the TPP plaintiffs sufficiently
alleged proximate cause for their civil RICO claims. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7J0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>804 F.3d 633,=
 634
(3d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. There, TPPs filed a putative class action against the
defendant alleging under RICO that the defendant misrepresented significant
heart-related safety risks associated with the drug Avandia. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2D6N7J0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 634-36</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM0020000400_2"></a>The plaintiffs alleged tha=
t they
included Avandia in their formularies and covered it at favorable rates for
their members in reliance on the defendant's misrepresentations about Avand=
ia's
safety. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 636</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM0040000400"></a>The plaintiffs also alleged =
that
physicians relied on the defendant's misrepresentations in deciding to
prescribe Avandia and that they would have prescribed it to fewer patients =
if
the defendant had not concealed its safety risks. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Id.</i> The district court held that the plaintiffs adequately alle=
ged
that the defendant proximately caused their damages but certified its decis=
ion
for interlocutory appeal. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM00300004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 637</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Third Circuit affirmed. Appl=
ying
the Supreme Court's&nbsp;[**28]&nbsp; direct relation requirement, the Third
Circuit held that &quot;[t]he conduct that allegedly caused [the] plaintiff=
s'
injuries is the same conduct forming the basis of the RICO scheme alleged in
the complaint&#8212;the misrepresentation of the heart-related risks of tak=
ing
Avandia that caused TPPs . . . to place Avandia in the formulary.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HM0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 644</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Next, looking to the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i> fac=
tors,
the Third Circuit noted that it would not be too difficult to distinguish
between the damages attributable to the defendant's alleged violation from
other independent factors, and that at the pleadings stage, the question of
damages was &quot;a question for another day.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>Id.</i> Further, the Third Circuit observed that the plaintiffs were
best situated to sue, as the plaintiffs' alleged injury &quot;is an economic
injury independent of any physical injury suffered by Avandia users,&quot; =
and
&quot;prescribing physicians did not suffer RICO injury from [the defendant=
's]
marketing of Avandia.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Third Circuit, like the First
Circuit, rejected the defendant's argument that &quot;the presence of
intermediaries, doctors and patients, destroys proximate cause because they
were the ones who ultimately decided whether to rely on [the defendant's] m=
isrepresentations.&quot;&nbsp;[**29]&nbsp;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 645</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0050000400"></a>The Third Circuit explained =
that
&quot;drug manufacturers are well aware that TPPs cover the cost of their
drugs&quot; and the defendant's &quot;fraudulent scheme could have been
successful only if [the] plaintiffs paid for Avandia, [which] is the very i=
njury
that [the] plaintiffs seek recovery for.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id.</i> Thus, the plaintiffs' alleged injury had a direct relation =
to
the alleged RICO violation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i>
Therefore, the Third Circuit affirmed the district court's holding that the
plaintiffs adequately alleged RICO proximate cause at the pleadings stage. =
</span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7H2HM6HN0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H7G-9R61-F04K-K01W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 645-46</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>e. Circuit Court Precedent Analy=
sis</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Although
each of these four circuit court opinions arises under similar factual
scenarios, factual and procedural distinctions exist between them. For exam=
ple,
the Third and Seventh Circuits' opinions confronted the issue whether the
plaintiffs could satisfy the proximate cause requirement under RICO at the
pleadings stage, whereas the Second Circuit considered the issue at the cla=
ss
certification stage, and the Third Circuit reviewed the issue post-trial.
&nbsp;[*1257]&nbsp; Further, while the Second, Third, and Seventh Circuit c=
ases
involved putative class actions, the First Circuit's opinion involved a sin=
gle
TPP. But these minor factual&nbsp;[**30]&nbsp; and procedural differences do
not help us resolve the central dispute between the Second and Seventh Circ=
uits'
reasoning and the First and Third Circuits' reasoning.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Indeed,
it seems <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_19"></a>the central dispute betw=
een
the Second and Seventh Circuits and the First and Third Circuits is whether=
 the
decisions of prescribing physicians and pharmacy benefit managers constitute
intervening causes that sever the chain of proximate cause between the drug
manufacturer and TPP.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn476' href=3D"#_f=
tn476"
name=3D"_ftnref476" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>13</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> We think the First
and Third Circuits have it right because their reasoning is more consistent
with the Supreme Court's direct relation requirement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
