<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HV0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>If we were to hold the
opposite&#8212;that prescribing physicians' and pharmacy benefit managers'
decisions constitute an intervening cause to sever the chain of proximate
cause&#8212;as the Second and Seventh Circuits have held, drug manufacturers
would be insulated from liability for their fraudulent marketing schemes, as
they could continuously hide behind prescribing physicians and pharmacy
benefit&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; managers. That is not the purpose the requirement
&nbsp;[*1258]&nbsp; of proximate cause is intended to serve. Proximate cause
exists to &quot;limit a person's responsibility for the consequences of that
person's own acts.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HV002000040=
0"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Holmes</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 503 U.S. at=
 268</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Here, Plaintiffs seek to hold Defendants liable for the consequences of the=
ir
own acts and omissions toward Plaintiffs: the money spent by Plaintiffs to
purchase Actos.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
