<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2SXGD000076PF00034"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K70040000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I84B43ND52000002TB1005NX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2SF8370010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>It was recognized in both <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Sidney Hillman</i> and <i style=3D'mso=
-bidi-font-style:
normal'>UFCW Local 1776</i> that the drug manufacturer's fraudulent promoti=
on
of a prescription drug for off-label uses was not the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>only</i> basis upon which the prescribing physicians relied in
prescribing the drug. In <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Sidney Hil=
lman</i>,
the Seventh Circuit noted that it would be too difficult to disentangle whi=
ch
physicians' prescribing decisions, if any, were influenced by the defendant=
s'
unlawful promotion of the prescription drug for off-label uses. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K70030000400"></a><a
