:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>873 F.3d at
577-78</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. Similarly, in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
UFCW
Local 1776</i>, the Second Circuit noted that the drug manufacturer's unlaw=
ful
promotion of the prescription drug&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; for off-label uses was=
 not
the only source of information upon which the prescribing physicians based
their decisions to prescribe the drug. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K70050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>620 F.3d at 1=
35</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2T1SR000076PF00036"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2SF8370030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I84B43NDYP700002TB1005P8"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I84B43NF1YY00002TB1005P9"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2SF8370050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Echoing the first factor of <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Holmes</i>, the failure to warn of the
bladder cancer risk in this case makes Plaintiffs' damages more clearly
&quot;attributable to [Defendants'] violation.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2SF8370020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-KJ40-003B-R4CC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>503 U.S. at 2=
69</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The damages claimed from off-label uses in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2SF8370040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sidney Hillma=
n</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HW0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5109-4R71-652R-0000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>UFCW Local 17=
76</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
are less directly attributable to the alleged false promotions. It is much =
more
likely that Actos's risk of causing a disease as serious as bladder cancer
would materially influence prescribing physicians' decisions whether to
prescribe Actos. Plaintiffs' allegations confirm this theory, as they allege
that a survey conducted by Defendants in 2003 showed that 75% of surveyed
physicians' interest in a different oral anti-diabetic drug declined
&quot;greatly&quot; once they learned that it carried a risk of causing bla=
dder
cancer. Further, Plaintiffs allege that those survey results are confirmed =
by
their allegation that sales of Actos decreased approximately 80% once the F=
DA
issued its official warning that Actos may be linked to bladder cancer in
patients who use it over a prolonged period of time.&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; Taki=
ng
those allegations as true, as we must, the question whether prescribing
physicians would not have been influenced by Defendants' alleged fraudulent
omission is less concerning in this case than it was to the Second and Seve=
nth
Circuits.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HW0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, the Seventh Circuit's
distinction that TPPs' injuries are too far removed from the drug
manufacturer's fraudulent scheme to satisfy the RICO proximate cause
requirement because they are not &quot;the sole injured parties&quot; and
because individual patients' &quot;health and financial costs come first in
line temporally&quot; misses the mark. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HW0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sidney Hillma=
n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5PPC-P271-F04K-R0V5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 873 F.3d at=
 576</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Supreme Court has never made a distinction about temporal proximity of =
the
plaintiffs to the damages caused to others when evaluating whether a plaint=
iff
has adequately alleged that the defendant proximately caused the plaintiff's
damages under RICO. Additionally, the fact that individual patients and TPPs
both suffered economic injuries from a drug manufacturer's fraudulent scheme
does not mean that one group of plaintiffs should be favored to recover over
the other, so long as they both suffered the same economic injuries from the
drug manufacturer's &nbsp;[*1259]&nbsp; same misconduct. Finally, the Seven=
th
Circuit's comment&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp; that prescribing physicians may suffer
indirect loss does not attenuate the chain of causation so far as to break =
it. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> Even if prescribing physic=
ians
suffer an indirect loss such as reputational harm for prescribing an
ineffective or unsafe drug, they are not out of pocket for the price of the
drug and thus do not suffer the same economic loss as do individual patient=
s and
TPPs. For these reasons, we agree with the First and Third Circuits that
Plaintiffs' damages are not too far removed from Defendants' alleged omissi=
ons
and misrepresentations to satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement.</span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>3. Reliance</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
a threshold matter, any argument that Patients have not alleged that they
relied on Defendants' misrepresentations and omissions lacks merit. Each
patient alleged that had he &quot;known that Actos increased the risk of
causing bladder cancer, he would never have purchased and ingested the
drug.&quot; Additionally, Patients alleged that they &quot;relied on
Defendants' . . . misrepresentations of Actos'[s] safety in purchasing the
drug.&quot; These statements are sufficient to allege that Patients relied =
on
Defendants' misrepresentations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T0010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Next, the Supreme Court has
explained that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_17"></a>if there is a
direct&nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; relationship between a defendant's wrongful conduct
and a plaintiff's alleged injury, a RICO plaintiff who did not directly rel=
y on
the defendant's omission or misrepresentation can still satisfy the require=
ment
of proximate causation of damages. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T003000=
0400"></a>Recall
that in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Bridge</i> the defendants'
misrepresentations that they complied with the county's &quot;Single,
Simultaneous Bidder Rule&quot; were made to the tax lien selling <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>county</i>, not to the plaintiff tax l=
ien <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>buyers</i>. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HW0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>553 U.S. at 6=
48</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
But the Supreme Court held that it was sufficient to establish proximate ca=
use
between the defendants' alleged wrongful conduct and the plaintiffs' alleged
injury that the county had relied on the defendants' false attestations. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T0030000400_2"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 658-59</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T0050000400"></a>What mattered most in the RI=
CO
proximate causation inquiry was whether there was a direct relationship bet=
ween
the alleged RICO violation and the plaintiffs' alleged injury. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i> And there was. The plainti=
ffs'
&quot;alleged injury&#8212;the loss of valuable [tax] liens&#8212;[was] the
direct result of . . . [the defendants'] scheme to obtain more liens for
themselves.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2D6N7T0040000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 658</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HX0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HX0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In so holding, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc8_17"></a>the Supreme Court expressly rejected=
 the
defendants' argument that &quot;[direct] reliance is an element of a civil =
RICO
claim predicated&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; on mail fraud.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HX0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>553 U.S. at
646-49</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K90010000400"></a>The
Supreme Court explained that the civil RICO statute has no reliance require=
ment
on its face, and a person may be injured &quot;by reason of&quot; another
person's fraud even if the injured party did not rely on any misrepresentat=
ion.
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HX0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 648-49</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K90010000400_2"></a>Nonetheless, the Supreme C=
ourt
noted that it &quot;may well be that a RICO plaintiff alleging injury by re=
ason
of a pattern of mail fraud must establish at least [indirect] reliance in o=
rder
to prove causation.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J2HM6HX00500004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>553 U.S. at
658-59</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. This is because, logically, a plaintiff cannot even
establish but-for causation if <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>no o=
ne</i>
relied on the defendant's alleged misrepresentation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>Id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4X29H2TN5D000076PF0003K"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K90030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Despite this precedent, Defendan=
ts
argue that Painters Fund failed to allege reliance on Defendants' omissions=
 of
Actos's bladder cancer risk, since Painters Fund expressly alleged that, as=
 a
TPP, it &quot;relies on [its] members and their prescribers to make informed
decisions about which drugs will be prescribed and, in turn, submitted to
Plaintiff Painters Fund for reimbursement.&quot; This argument is also
&nbsp;[*1260]&nbsp; meritless. Like in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5XNGX7J28T4K90020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4SPS-PSH0-TXFX-12X7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Bridge</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
where it was sufficient to satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement that =
the
county (a third party) had relied on the defendants' false attestations, he=
re,
it is sufficient&nbsp;[**38]&nbsp; to satisfy RICO's proximate cause
requirement that Painters Fund alleged that prescribing physicians (also th=
ird
parties, but not intervening causes) relied on Defendants' misrepresentatio=
ns
and omissions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Defendants argue that even if Painter's Fund has alleged indirect reliance,=
 its
general allegations of indirect reliance&#8212;i.e., that prescribing
physicians relied on Defendants' misrepresentations and omissions in
prescribing Actos for their patients, which Painters Fund then
reimbursed&#8212;are insufficient, because Painters Fund should have alleged
with specificity exactly which prescribing physicians were misled by
Defendants' alleged misrepresentations. Remembering that this case is befor=
e us
at the pleadings stage and without the benefit of discovery, we recognize t=
hat
it would be difficult for Painters Fund to determine with specificity exact=
ly
which doctors relied on Defendants' alleged misrepresentations. All that is
required of Painters Fund at this stage is to allege that <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>someone</i> in the chain of causation relied on Defendants' alleged
misrepresentations and omissions, which it has done here. Thus, we hold that
Plaintiffs have adequately alleged the reliance necessary&nbsp;[**39]&nbsp;=
 to
satisfy RICO's proximate cause requirement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III. CONCLUSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal22 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>While
we express no opinion on Plaintiffs' chances of success in this litigation =
as
it proceeds, we hold that Plaintiffs have satisfactorily alleged that
Defendants proximately caused their claimed damages at the pleadings stage.=
 We
reverse the district court's judgment dismissing Plaintiffs' RICO claims un=
der </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
for lack of RICO standing, and we remand to the district court for further
proceedings consistent with this disposition.</span></p>

