style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[**10]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 79 (2d Cir.
1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ05N0000400"></a>In
addition, pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
the complaint must allege the content of the fraudulent communication, who =
made
and received the communication, where and when it took place, and describe =
why
the information transmitted was fraudulent. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0540000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9T70-003B-P25N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>12 F.3d at 11=
76</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ05J0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>897 F.2d at 7=
9</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ05N0000400_2"></a>Finally, the complaint must
&quot;provide some minimal factual basis&quot; that gives rise to a strong
inference of fraudulent intent.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </sp=
an></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ05M0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Powers v. Bri=
tish
Vita, P.L.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DV20-001T-D124-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 57 F.3d 176,
184 (2d Cir. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The Wolff Defendants claim that
FIAC has failed to meet each of these elements. I disagree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4MGY21WRXV0005CHC70011N"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0630000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4MGY21WY1P0005CHC7003F7"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0650000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc5_20"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint in a RICO action need not be specific as to each allegation of ma=
il
or wire fraud when the nature of the RICO scheme is sufficiently pleaded so=
 as
to give notice to the defendants.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </=
span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ05P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiVittorio v.
Equidyne Extractive Indus.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 822 F.2d 12=
42,
1247 (2d Cir. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. This is especially true when t=
he
sufficiency of the complaint is being considered before discovery has taken
place and when the &quot;information is exclusively in the possession of th=
ose
participating in the fraud.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>I=
d.</i>;
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0640000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-V0J0-001T-546W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Constellation
Bank, N.A. v. C.L.A. Management Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-V0J0-001T-546W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 1995 U.S. D=
ist.
LEXIS 1105</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, No. 94 Civ 0989, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0660000400"></a>1995 WL 42285, at *22-23 (S.=
D.N.Y.
Feb. 1, 1995); &nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ06M0000=
400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>In re Ann Taylor Stores Sec. Litig., 8=
07 F.
Supp. 990, 1004 (S.D.N.Y. 1992)</i>. It is with these standards in mind tha=
t I
must view the sufficiency of FIAC's complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>FIAC
has adequately pleaded the content of several acts of mail and wire fraud. =
(<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g.</i>, Compl. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ06R0000400"></a>PP 30-31, 41-43, 71, 75 &amp;
101). In addition, when the complaint is read as a whole, and the inquiry is
not limited to specific paragraphs of the complaint viewed in isolation, it=
 is
sufficient to meet the pleading requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
FIAC has sufficiently alleged the content, speaker, date and nature of seve=
ral
of the fraudulent communications, and the complaint is sufficient to
&quot;inform [at least some of the defendants] of the nature of [their] all=
eged
participation in the fraud.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ06P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>DiVittorio</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93W0-001B-K2KF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 822 F.2d at
1247</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
complaint, however, does not sufficiently allege mail and wire fraud as to =
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>all</i> of the named defendants. In fa=
ct, a
number of the defendants are given no more than a brief mention in a few
paragraphs of the complaint. The only defendants who FIAC has properly alle=
ged
committed two acts of mail or wire fraud are Wolff (under a theory of aiding
and abetting to be discussed <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>infra<=
/i>),
CIC, &nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; Mark Karasick and Thomas Tyndall. (<i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See, e.g.</i>, Compl. PP 30-31, 33-35). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0750000400"></a>FIAC has failed to sufficien=
tly
allege that the other defendants participated in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>any</i> acts of mail or wire fraud. While, FIAC does allege that the
RICO enterprise was carried on by all defendants, general conclusory
allegations such as this are not sufficient under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-1WP1-6N19-F0YN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 9(b)</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ06S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ouaknine</spa=
n></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6PG0-003B-50TB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d at=
 79</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, Count I is dismissed as to all defendants except Wolff, CIC, Mark
Karasick, and Thomas Tyndall.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>a) <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>Aiding and Abetting</i></span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition to alleging that each of the defendants participated directly in t=
he
RICO violations, FIAC alleges in the alternative that each defendant aided =
and
abetted the RICO violations. (Compl. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0770=
000400"></a>P
3). <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_20"></a>A party can be found liable of
aiding and abetting a RICO violation only when that party had some part in
directing the affairs of the RICO enterprise.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0760000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S70-NFC0-003B-R50T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Reves v. Ernst
&amp; Young</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S70-NFC0-003B-R50T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 507 U.S. 17=
0,
179, 122 L. Ed. 2d 525, 113 S. Ct. 1163 (1993)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Merely being associated with the RICO enterprise is insufficient to establi=
sh
liability under RICO. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> In the
&nbsp;[*486]&nbsp; present case, FIAC has adequately alleged that Wolff was=
 not
only associated with the Enterprise in this case, but also&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp;
took part in directing its affairs. (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>See,
e.g.</i>, Compl. PP 3, 30, 34, 45-64). Thus, FIAC has sufficiently pleaded =
that
Wolff &quot;aided and abetted&quot; a violation of the RICO laws. The same
holds true for defendants CIC, Karasick, and Tyndall. (<i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>See, e.g.</i>, Compl. PP 30-31, 33-35).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>FIAC,
however, has failed to make this showing as to any of the remaining defenda=
nts.
FIAC has merely alleged that those defendants were associated with the
Enterprise. The complaint is lacking in any facts that suggest these defend=
ants
participated directly <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>or</i> indire=
ctly
in the RICO violations. While FIAC does allege that some of these defendants
were part of the Enterprise and may have profited from the RICO acts carrie=
d on
by others, they have not alleged the participation required to establish
liability under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Hence, FIAC has failed to properly plead &quot;aiding and abetting&quot; as=
 to
any defendants except Wolff, CIC, Mark Karasick, and Thomas Tyndall.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>2. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>&quot;Pattern of Racketeering Activity&quot; and continuity</i></sp=
an></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Wolff Defendants' next challenge to FIAC's complaint is that it fails to al=
lege
a &quot;pattern&quot; of racketeering activity. They claim that FIAC has
alleged a &quot;single scheme, with a single&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; victim,
extending over only a six month period&quot; that does not threaten continu=
ed
criminal activity. (Def. Mem. at 19). They argue, therefore, that FIAC has
failed to meet the requirements of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1961(5=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. I
disagree.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0790000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ07R0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc7_18"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1961(=
5)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
loosely defines a &quot;pattern&quot; as two or more related acts.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(5)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ07T0000400"></a>To be
related, the acts of mail and wire fraud must have the &quot;same or similar
purposes or results, participants, victims . . . and [can not be] isolated
events.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0780000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J. Inc. v.
Northwestern Bell Tel. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 492 U.S. 22=
9,
237, 106 L. Ed. 2d 195, 109 S. Ct. 2893 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0820000400"></a>The Supreme Court, however, =
has
interpreted </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
