mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 897 F.2d at=
 26
n.4</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>FIAC
meets these standards. The complaint alleges that (1) each of the defendants
agreed to join the conspiracy to defraud FIAC and others (by showing that e=
ach
defendant &quot;manifested . . . an assent 'to commit predicate acts of
racketeering and by showing the defendant's status in the Enterprise, (2) t=
hat
each of the defendants committed acts of mail and wire fraud in furtherance=
 of
the conspiracy, and (3) that the defendants knowingly participated in the s=
ame.
(<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> Compl. PP 2-4, 7-21, 49-59,
99-101, 103-04, &amp; 107). Thus, FIAC has adequately pleaded a claim under=
 </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962(d=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>III. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Pendent State Law Claims</i></span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition to the RICO claim, FIAC alleges nine separate state law claims aga=
inst
the defendants. The Wolff Defendants argue that FIAC has failed to sufficie=
ntly
plead&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; any of these claims. While the Wolff Defendants off=
er
no new arguments to substantiate this claim, they do reiterate some of the =
same
grounds they offered in support of their motion to dismiss the RICO count.
Hence, for the same reasons I discussed in relation to the RICO count, the
state law claims are dismissed as to all defendants except Wolff, CIC, Thom=
as
Tyndall, and Mark Karasick. The same facts and allegations that supported
FIAC's RICO claim against these four defendants, however, are also sufficie=
nt
to allow FIAC's state law claims to survive the motion to dismiss.</span></=
p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*489]&nbsp;
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'>IV. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:normal'>Motion
to Disqualify FIAC's Counsel.</i></b></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Wolff Defendants have also moved to disqualify Patterson, Belknap, Webb &am=
p;
Tyler LLP (&quot;PBW&amp;T&quot;) as FIAC's counsel. PBW&amp;T began
representing FIAC approximately two weeks prior to the closing of the first
deal between FIAC and CIC. (Gianninoto Aff. at PP 4-5). At that time, PBW&a=
mp;T
assumed an active role in the review of the loan and guaranty documents use=
d to
consummate the deal. (<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> at 4-=
7).
The Wolff Defendants allege that because of this representation, PBW&amp;T =
has
professional interests in this action that directly conflict with the inter=
ests
of FIAC. &nbsp;[**24]&nbsp; Specifically, the Wolff Defendants argue that F=
IAC
may have a malpractice claim against PBW&amp;T based on PBW&amp;T's review =
of
the closing documents. The Wolff Defendants also argue that since the
defendants may have a claim for contribution against PBW&amp;T, PBW&amp;T w=
ill
not be able to fairly and adequately represent FIAC in this lawsuit. I disa=
gree
with both arguments.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0D00000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0D20000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc13_10"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Disqualification of a party's ch=
osen
counsel is a &quot;drastic measure.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0CY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B860-003B-64CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Motown Record
Corp. v. Mary Jane Girls, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-B860-003B-64CR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 118 F.R.D. =
35,
38 (S.D.N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Motions to disqualify opposing
counsel are viewed with disfavor because they impinge on a party's right to
employ the counsel of its choice. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F40000=
400"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0D10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7HM0-001T-53PS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Stratavest Lt=
d.
v. Rogers</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-7HM0-001T-53PS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 903 F. Supp.
663, 666 (S.D.N.Y. 1995)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F40000400_2"></a>Additionally, the &quot;dr=
astic
remedy&quot; of disqualification is subject to strict scrutiny because of t=
he
strong potential to &quot;stall and derail the proceedings, redounding to t=
he
strategic advantage of one party over another.&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-XW20-003D-G418-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>S &amp; S Hot=
el
Ventures Ltd. Partnership v. 777 S.H. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-XW20-003D-G418-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 515 N.Y.S.2d
735, 738 (N.Y. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Hence, the moving party bears =
the
&quot;heavy burden [in] proving [the] facts required for
disqualification.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F30000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YND0-003B-G067-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Evans v. Artek
Sys. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YND0-003B-G067-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 715 F.2d 78=
8, </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YND0-003B-G067-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[**25]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-YND0-003B-G067-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 794 (2d Cir.
1983)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'>. The Wolff Defendants' motion to disqualify fails to
satisfy this heavy burden.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>PBW&amp;T's
involvement with the FIAC-CIC deals was limited to the review of the loan
agreements and guaranty. (Gianninoto Aff. at PP 4-8). PBW&amp;T was not
responsible for ensuring that defendants would not violate the RICO laws or
commit fraud. The crux of FIAC's complaint is that the defendants <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>intentionally</i> defrauded them. The =
Wolff
Defendants have alleged no facts that would demonstrate any negligence on t=
he
part of PBW&amp;T, much less satisfy the heavy burden required in a motion =
to
disqualify.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F60000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The Wolff Defendants' argument t=
hat
they might assert affirmative defenses alleging that PBW&amp;T was somehow
negligent or might assert a third-party claim against PBW&amp;T for
contribution is also unavailing. First of all, the Wolff Defendants have of=
fered
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>no</i> facts to support their alleg=
ation
that PBW&amp;T's actions may have somehow contributed to FIAC's losses in t=
his
case. Moreover, except for one of the state law claims, all of FIAC's claims
allege intentional conduct on the part of the defendants, and contribution =
is
generally not available to &quot;any tortfeasor who has intentionally caused
the&nbsp;[**26]&nbsp; harm.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>S=
ee</i>
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-GG40-0054-5010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Anderson v. L=
ocal
Union No. 3, Int'l Bhd. of Elec. Workers</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-GG40-0054-5010-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 582 F. Supp.
627, 632</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (S.D.N.Y.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>aff=
'd</i>,
</span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FCRPPX0K1MNJ0F70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-THW0-003B-G035-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>751 F.2d 546,=
 549
(2d Cir. 1984)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Thus, the Wolff Defendants have failed to demonstrate =
that
the &quot;drastic remedy&quot; of disqualifying FIAC's chosen counsel is
warranted and their motion to do so is therefore denied.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>CONCLUSION</span></i></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
the foregoing reasons, the motion to dismiss is granted as to Slovie Wolff,
Rachell Skydell, Manhattan Management, Inc., Chadwick Funding Company and
Burham Corp., N.V. and denied as to Emanuel Wolff, CIC, Mark Karasick and
Thomas Tyndall. In addition, the motion to disqualify Patterson, Belknap, W=
ebb
&amp; Tyler LLP as counsel for FIAC is denied.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal23 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
