893 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We conclude that the Bankruptcy Orders and the Agricul=
ture
Order were not admissible to establish the existence of a statutory trust. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The Bankruptcy Orders. </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ25S0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In evaluating Bankers Trust's cl=
aim
that no statutory trust had been created, the district court took &quot;not=
ice
of the Bankruptcy Court's factual finding that the Sellers listed in Schedu=
le A
attached to its order provided&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; the notices required to
preserve their trust rights and were cash sellers,&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ25R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>758 F. Supp. =
at
893 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, citing </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2991-FG36-11WV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Evid.
201(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_22"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2991-FG36-11WV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 201(b)</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
judicially noticed fact must be one not subject to reasonable dispute in th=
at
it is either (1) generally known within the territorial jurisdiction of the
trial court or (2) capable of accurate and ready determination by resort to
sources whose accuracy cannot reasonably be questioned. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ25V0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Apparently, the district court
believed that the finding of the bankruptcy court fell into the latter
category. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ26W0000400"></a>We disagree. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_21"></a>A court may take judicial notice of a
document filed in another court &quot;not for the truth of the matters asse=
rted
in the other litigation, but rather to establish the fact of such litigation
and related filings.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ25T0000=
400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BSX0-008H-V3DD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kramer v. Time
Warner Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BSX0-008H-V3DD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 937 F.2d 767,
774 (2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citing </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ26S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-48H0-0039-M3GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 ex
rel. Geisler v. Walters,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-48H0-0039-M3GT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 510 F.2d 887,
890 n.4 (3d Cir. 1975))</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ26W0000400_2"></a>Here, however, the district
court relied upon the Bankruptcy Orders precisely to establish facts assert=
ed
therein, i.e., &quot;that the Sellers listed in Schedule A attached to [the
bankruptcy court's] order provided the&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; notices required to
preserve their trust rights and were cash sellers.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ26V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>758 F. Supp. =
at
893 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. We note further, as the district court recognized, </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ26X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 893</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
that the doctrine of collateral estoppel cannot be invoked in support of the
district court's ruling because Bankers &nbsp;[*1389]&nbsp; Trust was not a
party to the bankruptcy proceeding. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ27R0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2CXRY1YJ5N000YJP87001MX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ27T0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I2CXRY2079C000YJP87001GF"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ28S0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The cases cited by the district
court <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>( </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ27P0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 893 n.3</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>)
in support of its judicial notice ruling are inapposite. In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ27S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5XJ0-0039-P2W5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>E.I. du Pont =
de
Nemours &amp; Co. v. Cullen,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5XJ0-0039-P2W5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 791 F.2d 5 (=
1st
Cir. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, the court took judicial notice of a complaint that had
been filed in a related state court action to ascertain the legal nature of=
 the
claim stated in that complaint, not to support any factual determination in=
 the
subsequent litigation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ28R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5XJ0-0039-P2W5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5XJ0-0039-P2W5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 7-8</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Similarly, in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ28T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-63X0-0039-W2VM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Ives Lab., In=
c.
v. Darby Drug Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-63X0-0039-W2VM-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 638 F.2d 538,
544 n.8 (2d Cir. 1981)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>rev'd on other grounds sub nom.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nb=
sp;
</span></i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ28W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5H70-003B-S53R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Inwood Lab., =
Inc.
v. Ives Lab., Inc.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5H70-003B-S53R-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 456 U.S. 844,
102 S. Ct. 2182, 72 L. Ed. 2d 606 (1982)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-=
family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, we took judicial
notice of several indictments simply to establish that such indictments had=
 in
fact been returned, but&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; &quot;of course expressed no opin=
ion
as to the guilt or innocence of those indicted.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>The Agriculture Order. </i><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ29W0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court deemed the
Agriculture Order admissible pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Fed. R. Evid.
803(8)(C)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ29V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>758 F. Supp. =
at
893 n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_21"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8MW9-SG52-8T6X-70MT-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 803(8)(C=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
establishes a hearsay exception for &quot;reports . . . of public offices or
agencies . . . setting forth . . . factual findings resulting from an
investigation made pursuant to authority granted by law, unless the sources=
 of
information or other circumstances indicate lack of trustworthiness.&quot; =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Assuming
that the rule applies by its terms to the Agriculture Order, however, that
order lends no support to the existence of a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
statutory trust. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn480' href=3D"#_ftn48=
0"
name=3D"_ftnref480" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>4</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Rather, the Agriculture Order
involved an inquiry whether Rotches had violated another provision of the A=
ct, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>7 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
202(a) (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, and expressly stated: &quot;a statutory trust as requi=
red
by Section 206(b) of the Act, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>7 U.S.C. &sec=
t;
196(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-f=
ont-family:
Times;color:black'>, is not involved here.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ29Y0000400"></a>Bankers Trust contends on
appeal that Liberty Mutual introduced no evidence other than the Bankruptcy
Orders and the Agriculture Order in support of the establishment of a statu=
tory
trust, so summary judgment should be awarded to Bankers Trust. There are
substantial indications in the record, however, that a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
statutory trust was in fact established. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
also In re Arbogast &amp; Bastian, Inc. (Gibson v. Arbogast &amp; Bastian,
Inc.),</i> </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ29X0000400"></a><a
