style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
ruling of the district court in the instant case, however, goes well beyond=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotham Provision Co.</i> Bankers Trust=
 was
not a secured lender to Arbogast, but rather to Rotches. The receivables in=
 the
possession of Bankers Trust and at issue in this litigation are not receiva=
bles
owed to Arbogast by Rotches, but rather receivables owed to Rotches by its =
customers.
These receivables were pledged as security for Rotches' open account with
Arbogast, and that security interest was then subordinated to the security
interest of Bankers Trust in a Subordination Agreement effected approximate=
ly a
year prior to the sales of livestock that generated the statutory trust at
issue in this litigation. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
trust in this case clearly covers receivables due to Arbogast from Rotches =
for
sales of meat products derived from livestock sold to Arbogast by cash sell=
ers.
We see nothing in the statutory language, however, that extends the trust to
property pledged to Arbogast by Rotches to secure payment of such receivabl=
es. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nor
can the language of the statute be sensibly construed to encompass
receivables&nbsp;[**22]&nbsp; due to Rotches from third parties. The statute
requires the packer to hold &quot;in trust&quot; certain livestock and
derivative products, inventories, proceeds, and receivables. In the ordinary
course, the packer would never have title to the products, inventories,
proceeds, and receivables of third parties like Rotches, and could hardly be
commanded to hold &quot;in trust&quot; property to which it never had title=
 in
the first place. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2F20000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Thus, we disagree with the distr=
ict
court's ruling that Arbogast &quot;did not have power to enter into the
[Subordination] Agreement which purported to subordinate trust assets, i.e.
[Arbogast's] prior security interest in the assets of [Rotches].&quot; </sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2F10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-FR30-001T-7257-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>758 F. Supp. =
at
895</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (footnote omitted). That security interest is not
encompassed by the language of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The statute did not command Arbogast to obtain such a security interest, an=
d it
did not constrain Arbogast from subordinating it. It only required that
Arbogast hold the receivables due to Arbogast from Rotches, and any proceeds
resulting from the payment of those receivables, in trust for cash sellers =
of
livestock to Arbogast. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
see no undermining of the policy of the Act in this ruling. It cannot
be&nbsp;[**23]&nbsp; plausibly contended that the Act barred Arbogast from
selling to Rotches on credit, or required Arbogast to arrange any particular
form of security (or for that matter any security at all) for the line of
credit that it extended to Rotches. The statute only required that Arbogast=
 pay
over to cash sellers of livestock any payments received from Rotches, and n=
ot
give any third party a preferred position as to the receivables directly due
from Rotches to Arbogast. In sum, no </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim could be made against Bankers Trust if Arbogast had never obtained any
security interest from Rotches. We do not see how such a claim can result
because Arbogast obtained such an interest and then, in the exercise of its
business judgment, subordinated that interest to one held by Bankers Trust.=
 </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
add one caveat to the foregoing. There is in the record an internal Bankers
Trust memorandum dated May 10, 1984 that states in part: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A
few days ago Dave Rotches, President, called to inform the bank that he dec=
ided
to cease operations due to problems with [Arbogast]. [Arbogast] over the ye=
ars
has been the subject's major supplier of dressed hogs. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*1392]&nbsp;
By way of background, a key to the subject's success over&nbsp;[**24]&nbsp;=
 the
years has been the way in which they bought hogs. Dave Rotches negotiated t=
he
prices with farmers who shipped and sold their hogs to [Arbogast] at the pr=
ice
negotiated by Dave Rotches. [Arbogast] paid the farmer and slaughtered the =
hogs
for Rotches. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Under this method, Rot=
ches
was able to avoid the Packers and Stockyard Act (title passed to [Arbogast]=
).</i>
[Emphasis added.] It also allowed him to buy hogs a couple dollars per hund=
red
weight, on average, below the market price. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2F40000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2FJ0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Our opinion should not be read as
addressing any issue regarding a fraudulent or otherwise wrongful circumven=
tion
of the Act that could result in Arbogast being regarded as an &quot;alter
ego&quot; of Rotches for purposes of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8S6M-SWF2-D6RV-H37D-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 196(b)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cf.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&n=
bsp;
</span></i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2F30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C040-003B-644N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Central Trust=
 Co.
v. B &amp; L Leasing,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-C040-003B-644N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 669 F. Supp.
828, 829 (S.D. Ohio 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",=
serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (secured lender challenged stat=
us
of asserted cash seller because of overlapping ownership with
purchaser/statutory trustee). In any such event, of course, some additional
showing would be required as to any possible liability of <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>Bankers Trust. Cf. </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHC0NG0K1MNJ2F50000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5W10-008H-V2PH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>C.H. Robinson
Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5W10-008H-V2PH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 952 F.2d at =
1316</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(secured lender that receives funds from statutory trustee for value and
without notice&nbsp;[**25]&nbsp; of breach of trust entitled to retain
payments) (dictum); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotham Provisio=
n Co.,</i>
669 F.2d at 1011 (secured lender &quot;had constructive notice of the trust
because a federal statute created the trust&quot;). In any event, no such c=
ase
is presented on this appeal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Conclusion
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal24 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
reverse on the appeal, affirm on the cross-appeal, and remand for further
proceedings not inconsistent with this opinion.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

