<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
is an appeal of RICO convictions arising from a law firm's fraudulent condu=
ct
&nbsp;[*251]&nbsp; of civil litigation as plaintiff's counsel in personal
injury cases. The appeal is brought by Morris J. Eisen, Joseph P. Napoli,
Harold M. Fishman, Dennis Rella, Marty Gabe, Geraldine G. Morganti, and Alan
Weinstein from judgments of the United States District Court for the Eastern
District of New York (Charles P. Sifton, Judge), following a four-month jury
trial. We affirm.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Background</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Morris
J. Eisen, P.C. (&quot;the Eisen firm&quot;) was a large Manhattan law firm =
that
specialized in bringing personal injury suits on behalf of plaintiffs. The
defendants, seven of the Eisen firm's attorneys, investigators, and office
personnel, were tried jointly on two counts of conducting and conspiring to
conduct the affairs of the Eisen firm through a pattern of racketeering
activity, in violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1962(c)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>(d) (1988)</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The indictment alleged, as the underlying acts of racketeering, that each of
the defendants committed, among other crimes, numerous acts of mail fraud, =
in
violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1341</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>, and bribery of &nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; witnesses, in violati=
on
of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>New York Penal
Law &sect; 215.00</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (McKinney 1988). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Eisen
was the founder, sole shareholder, and principal attorney of the Eisen firm.
Napoli was associated with the Eisen firm in an &quot;of counsel&quot;
capacity, and he was the main trial attorney for the firm. Fishman, a trial
attorney, was also &quot;of counsel&quot; to the firm. The Eisen firm regul=
arly
used investigators to assist attorneys in the trial preparation of personal
injury cases, and defendants Weinstein, Gabe, and Rella were private
investigators affiliated with the firm. Morganti was the office administrat=
or
of the Eisen firm with responsibility for managing the daily affairs of the
firm, including assigning attorneys and investigators to particular cases,
monitoring the firm's daily calendar, and managing the financial and person=
nel
operations of the firm. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
evidence at trial established that the defendants conducted the affairs of =
the
Eisen law firm through a pattern of mail fraud and witness bribery by pursu=
ing
counterfeit claims and using false witnesses in personal injury trials, and
that the Eisen firm earned millions in contingency fees from personal injury
suits involving fraud or bribery. The methods&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; by which the
frauds were accomplished included pressuring accident witnesses to testify
falsely, paying individuals to testify falsely that they had witnessed
accidents, paying unfavorable witnesses not to testify, and creating false
photographs, documents, and physical evidence of accidents for use before a=
nd
during trial. The Government's proof included the testimony of numerous Eis=
en
firm attorneys and employees as well as Eisen firm clients, defense attorne=
ys,
and witnesses involved in the fraudulent personal injury suits. Transcripts,
correspondence, and trial exhibits from the fraudulent personal injury suits
were also introduced. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
racketeering acts considered by the jury related to the defendants' conduct
with regard to 18 fraudulent personal injury lawsuits in which the plaintiff
was represented by the Eisen firm. The defendants were found guilty of
racketeering acts involving the following personal injury cases: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Eisen:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Mulnick, Schwartz, Stanton;</i> </s=
pan></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Napoli:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Ferri, Mulnick, Robbins, Rehberger;=
</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fishman:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Aboud, Schwartz, Tuning, Nieves;</i=
> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Rella:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Miceli, Rehberger, Schwartz;</i> </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Gabe:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Robbins, Stanton, Nieves;</i> </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Morganti:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Miceli, Schwartz, Stanton,</i>
&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span>Pietrafesa;</i> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Weinstein:
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Aboud, Schwartz, Tuning, Nieves, Me=
trano.</i>
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
jury convicted all seven defendants of RICO substantive and conspiracy offe=
nses
after three weeks of deliberations. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn4=
82'
href=3D"#_ftn482" name=3D"_ftnref482" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-f=
amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Discussion
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>I.
Legal Sufficiency of Charges</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A.
Mail Fraud </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1V80000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VB0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VM0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Weinstein argues that a scheme to
deprive an adversary of money by means &nbsp;[*252]&nbsp; of a civil lawsuit
conducted fraudulently does not constitute mail fraud because there is no
deprivation of property as defined by the Supreme Court in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1V70000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McNally v. Un=
ited
States,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 483 U.S. 350=
, 97
L. Ed. 2d 292, 107 S. Ct. 2875 (1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. In that case, the
Supreme Court held that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc1_23"></a>the mail =
fraud
statute does not reach schemes to defraud citizens of their right to honest=
 and
impartial government but is instead &quot;limited in scope to the protectio=
n of
property rights.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1V90000400"=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id. at 360</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VP0000400"></a>Looking to the legislative h=
istory
of the statute, which &quot;indicates&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; that its original
impetus . . . was to protect the people from schemes to deprive them of the=
ir
money and property,&quot; the Court concluded that </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'text-align:justify;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>the
words &quot;to defraud&quot; commonly refer to &quot;wronging one in his
property rights by dishonest methods or schemes,&quot; and &quot;usually
signify the deprivation of something of value by trick, deceit, chicane or
overreaching.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VC0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 356, 358<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(footnote and citation omitted). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VP00004=
00_2"></a>In
reversing the convictions of defendants charged with scheming to deprive the
state of its right to honest government by having a state agency share proc=
eeds
with business entities in which the defendants held interests, the Supreme
Court emphasized that there was no allegation that the state or its citizens
had been deprived of any money or property.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VN0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-H4V0-003B-44MG-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 360-61</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Weinstein
contends that the right of the civil defendants and their liability insurer=
s to
have a judgment in a civil proceeding obtained free of fraud and perjury is=
 an
intangible right not cognizable under the mail fraud statute. The Government
responds that the mail fraud predicates at issue here allege a scheme to
defraud that comports squarely with the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:nor=
mal'>McNally</i>
definition&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; of mail fraud because the Eisen indictment
explicitly alleges a scheme to deprive the victims of money. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1K033T31B3000KB5YH0056H"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VS0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1W30000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Weinstein relies on </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1VR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
