Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Weinstein next contends that eve=
n if
the civil&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; defendants were deprived of property,
&nbsp;[*253]&nbsp; the only party &quot;deceived&quot; by the alleged fraud
were civil juries that awarded the money judgment. Because the juries were =
not
claimed to have been injured, Weinstein argues that the &quot;convergence
theory&quot; of mail fraud, which, he contends, requires that the party
defrauded and the party injured be identical, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>see </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1WS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1JK0-001B-K1B6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Evans,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1JK0-001B-K1B6-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 844 F.2d 36,=
 39
(2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, is not satisfied. Even if the &quot;convergence
theory&quot; is applicable, which we do not decide, </span><a style=3D'mso-=
footnote-id:
ftn483' href=3D"#_ftn483" name=3D"_ftnref483" title=3D""><sup><span style=
=3D'font-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
its requirements are met here. Weinstein's argument that &quot;the civil
defendant is contesting, not relying on, the truth of the allegedly false
testimony,&quot; Brief for Appellant Weinstein at 36, and is therefore not
deceived by a corrupt adversary takes an unrealistically narrow view of the
charges in this case and ignores settled authority that perjury and fake
evidence defraud the adverse party. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;
First, a number of the mail fraud predicates in the indictment alleged fraud
that was perpetrated directly on the civil defendants and their liability
insurers before the lawsuit reached trial. In several cases, misrepresentat=
ions
in pleadings and pretrial submissions were made in the hope of fraudulently
inducing a settlement before trial. And in cases that went to trial, fraudu=
lent
representations concerning the claims were directed at the civil defendants=
 and
their insurers in an effort to induce settlement before verdict. In fact,
several of the lawsuits listed in the indictment were settled. Even in cases
decided by a jury, defendants' misconduct was intended to defraud their
adversaries. Litigants depend on the integrity of the conduct of participan=
ts
in civil proceedings though disputing the validity of their opponents' clai=
ms
to impose or resist civil liability. It is one thing to challenge the
perception, memory, or bias of an opponent's witnesses; it is quite another=
 for
a party's lawyer and a witness to concoct testimony that they know has been
wholly fabricated. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YC0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YP0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YS0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, Weinstein's claim that
perjured testimony suborned by the defendants was directed at the
civil&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; juries rather than at the other litigants ignores
relevant case law. In <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YB0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>United States v. Rodolitz, 786 F.2d 77=
</i>
(2d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied,</i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YD0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>479 U.S. 826, 93 L. Ed. 2d 52, 107 S. Ct. 102 (1986)</i>, the defen=
dant
had brought a fraudulent civil action against his insurance company and
recovered a money judgment from the company at trial. In affirming the
defendant's conviction for mail fraud, this Court explicitly recognized tha=
t <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc2_23"></a>false evidence at a civil trial work=
s a
fraud not only on the jury but on the opposing party as well.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YN0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id. 786 F.2d at 80-81</i>. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>=
See
also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YR0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CW10-001B-K27M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Averbach v. R=
ival
Manufacturing Co.,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CW10-001B-K27M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 809 F.2d 101=
6</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(3d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied,</i> <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YT0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>482 U.S. 915, 96 L. Ed. 2d 675, 107 S. Ct. 3187 (1987)</i>. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YX0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Weinstein also argues that the
causation between the fraud and the resulting deprivation of property is too
&quot;attenuated.&quot; However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc3_23"></a>=
the
Government need establish only an intent to harm; it is not required to pro=
ve
that the victim was actually injured as a result of the scheme, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BKB0-001B-K276-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Starr,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-BKB0-001B-K276-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 816 F.2d 94,=
 98
(2d Cir. 1987)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, much less that any injury that did occur resulted sole=
ly
from the fraud, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ1YY0000400"></a>Rodolitz, supra.</i>
In&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Rodolitz,</i> =
we
found a sufficient causal link between the defendant's failure to disclose
evidence in his trial to recover losses from his insurance company and the
resulting judgment against the company, regardless of what the trial jury m=
ay
actually have relied upon in reaching its verdict. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Weinstein contends that permitting the mail fraud offenses charged in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i> indictment to serve as RICO
predicate acts conflicts with the deliberate decision made by Congress in
omitting perjury as one of the enumerated RICO predicate offenses within the
definition of &quot;racketeering activity.&quot; See </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1961(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. &nbsp;[*254]&nbsp; Contrary to the Government's abrupt
dismissal of this argument as &quot;baseless,&quot; Brief for Appellee at 3=
6,
we recognize that there is some tension between the congressional decision =
to
include federal mail fraud as a predicate offense and to exclude perjury,
whether in violation of federal or state law. That tension is illustrated by
this prosecution in which the fraudulent scheme consists primarily of arran=
ging
for state court witnesses to commit perjury. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Though
the tension exists, we do not believe it places the indictment in this case
beyond the purview of &nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; RICO. Congress did not wish to per=
mit
instances of federal or state court perjury as such to constitute a pattern=
 of
RICO racketeering acts. Apparently, there was an understandable reluctance =
to
use federal criminal law as a back-stop for all state court litigation.
Nevertheless, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4_23"></a>where, as here, a
fraudulent scheme falls within the scope of the federal mail fraud statute =
and
the other elements of RICO are established, use of the mail fraud offense a=
s a
RICO predicate act cannot be suspended simply because perjury is part of th=
e means
for perpetrating the fraud. We do not doubt that where a series of related
state court perjuries occurs, it will often be possible to allege and prove
both a scheme to defraud within the meaning of the mail fraud statute as we=
ll
as the elements of a RICO violation. But in such cases, it will not be the =
fact
of the perjuries alone that suffices to bring the matter within the scope of
RICO. In any event, we cannot carve out from the coverage of RICO an except=
ion
for mail fraud offenses that involve state court perjuries. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>B.
State Law Bribery </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>&quot;Witness&quot; as required by </i=
></span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 215.00=
 of
New York Penal Law</span></i></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><s=
pan
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></i><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
Weinstein, Rella, Morganti, and Fishman all &nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; attack vario=
us
witness bribery racketeering acts by arguing that the individual allegedly
bribed was not a &quot;witness or a person about to be called as a
witness&quot; within the meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 215.0=
0 of
the New York Penal Law</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The statute provides in pertin=
ent
part: </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_22"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>A person is guilty of bribing a
witness when he confers, or offers or agrees to confer, any benefit upon a
witness or a person about to be called as a witness in any action or procee=
ding
upon an agreement or understanding that (a) the testimony of such witness w=
ill
thereby be influenced, or (b) such witness will absent himself from, or
otherwise avoid or seek to avoid appearing or testifying at, such action or
proceeding. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Penal Law
&sect; 215.00</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (McKinney 1988).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20G0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The New York Court of Appeals has
held that <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_22"></a>a person's status as a
witness under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&quot;depends upon the evidence he can supply the court, not the immediacy =
of
the need for the evidence,&quot; nor whether a subpoena has been issued in
order to secure it.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20F0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-BFM0-003V-B19M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>People v. Bel=
l,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-BFM0-003V-B19M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 538 N.Y.S.2d
754, 760, 73 N.Y.2d 153, 164, 535 N.E.2d 1294 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The Court stated that if &quot;the evidence is sufficient to support a find=
ing
that defendant reasonably should&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; have believed that the
person would be a witness and that he intentionally attempted to influence =
the
witness's testimony . . . the crime is complete.&quot; Id.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Weinstein
challenges the witness bribery racketeering act in one of the fraudulent to=
rt
cases, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Metrano,</i> on the ground t=
hat
there was insufficient evidence that Alberto Troche was a &quot;witness or a
person about to be called as a witness&quot; within the meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Metrano</i> case arose from inj=
uries
sustained by Metrano when he was shot in the leg by a Manhattan parking lot
attendant, Alberto Troche, during a row over a parking lot fee. Metrano
subsequently retained the Eisen firm, which brought a civil suit against Tr=
oche
and Troche's employer, Simone Grossman. The action against Grossman was bas=
ed
on allegations that Grossman should have known that Troche was dangerous and
carried a gun. The Eisen firm retained Weinstein as the investigator on the
case, which settled on the eve of trial for $ 300,000. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>At
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i> trial, Troche testifi=
ed
that Weinstein had come to his home and offered him $ 5,000 to testify that=
 his
employer was aware that he had dangerous propensities and that he carried a
gun. Troche&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp; had secretly taped this meeting, and &nbsp;[*=
255]&nbsp;
the tape was introduced into evidence at the Eisen trial.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Troche,
having inflicted the injury that formed the basis of the tort action, was
clearly someone Weinstein reasonably should have believed would be a witnes=
s in
the case. On the tape, Weinstein made repeated references to what Troche mu=
st
do &quot;when he comes to court&quot; or when he &quot;takes the stand,&quo=
t;
showing that he clearly anticipated that Troche would be a key witness in t=
he
case. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
an effort to avoid Troche's obvious status as a witness, Weinstein argues t=
hat
he did not commit bribery against Troche, but that Troche committed extorti=
on
against him. The extortionate conduct, he contends, defeats a </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
violation because it negates the essential element of <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>mens rea</i> in the crime of bribery. Although the tape recording
provides some support for Weinstein's contention that Troche might have off=
ered
his testimony to the highest bidder, other aspects of the tape recording and
Troche's testimony at the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i>=
 trial
provide sufficient evidence that Weinstein had the requisite intent to bribe
Troche and that Weinstein's conduct fits easily within the proscription of =
</span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
&nbsp;[**17]&nbsp; Both the tape recording and the trial testimony reveal t=
hat
Weinstein initiated the contact with Troche, that Weinstein was the first to
broach the subject of money during their taped meeting, and that Weinstein
described the testimony he sought to buy from Troche. Clearly a reasonable =
jury
could find that Troche was a &quot;person about to be called as a witness&q=
uot;
in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Metrano</i> trial and that
Weinstein had the requisite intent to bribe Troche. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Rella
argues that Eddie Goldstein, who testified in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Rehberger</i> case was not a &quot;witness&quot; within the meaning=
 of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
and Morganti makes the same claim with respect to Arnold Lustig, who testif=
ied
in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Schwartz</i> and <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Aboud</i> cases, because neither Golds=
tein
nor Lustig had observed the accidents about which they testified but were p=
aid
to offer completely fraudulent testimony. The claim that Goldstein and Lust=
ig
do not meet the statutory definition of &quot;witness&quot; because their
testimony was entirely fraudulent and thus they had no &quot;evidence&quot;=
 to
supply the court is frivolous. In the dictionary sense, a witness is &quot;=
one
who, being present, personally sees or perceives a thing,&quot; or one
&quot;whose declaration under oath (or affirmation) &nbsp;[**18]&nbsp; is
received as evidence for any purpose.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>See
Black's Law Dictionary</i> 1438 (5th ed. 1979). </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
contains no indication that it is limited to this core definition of this t=
erm
to the exclusion of a more functional definition. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_20"></a>The section, which explicitly reaches
attempts to influence the testimony of a &quot;witness&quot; as well as tho=
se
&quot;about to be called as a witness,&quot; applies to benefits conferred =
in
order to induce someone to hold himself out <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style=
:normal'>as</i>
a witness and offer wholly fraudulent testimony to the court. The statute is
not limited to payments for testimony that is false only in part. Both
Goldstein and Lustig were paid money to give false testimony, both were
prepared to testify falsely, and both were called to testify falsely at
personal injury trials. It is of no consequence that the &quot;evidence&quo=
t;
offered and the status of the men as &quot;witnesses&quot; was entirely an
outgrowth of the defendants' criminal designs. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20J0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Falsity of influenced testimony.</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20T0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8_20"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
provides in pertinent part that a person is guilty of bribing a witness
&quot;when he confers, or offers or agrees to confer, any benefit upon a
witness or a person about to be called as a witness . . . upon
an&nbsp;[**19]&nbsp; agreement or understanding that (a) the testimony of s=
uch
witness will thereby be influenced. . . .&quot; On its face, the statute
requires just two elements of proof: the offer of a benefit to a witness and
the agreement or understanding that the witness's testimony will be influen=
ced
by such benefit. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20W0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20H0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-0DW0-003D-G0NY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>People v.
Shaffer,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-0DW0-003D-G0NY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 130 A.D.2d 4=
31,
432, 515 N.Y.S.2d 470, 471</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (1st Dep't 1987) (&quot;All tha=
t is
required for a bribery to be complete is the offer or agreement to confer a
benefit upon the defendant's agreement or understanding that the witness'
testimony will thereby be influenced.&quot;). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20W0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20T0000400_2"></a>&quot;Understanding,&quot;=
 as
used in the statute, has long been construed as tantamount to the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>defendant's</i> intent, s<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ee </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20S0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3T5F-V510-0039-417V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>People </span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3T5F-V510-0039-417V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*256]&=
nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3T5F-V510-0039-417V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> </span></i><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3T5F-V510-0039-417V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>v. Kathan,</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3T5F-V510-0039-417V-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 136 A.D. 303,
120 N.Y.S. 1096 (1910)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and it is not necessary for
conviction that the jury find that the bribe's intended recipient shared th=
at
intent. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2160000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20V0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-2S70-003D-G4J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>People v. Kra=
mer,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-2S70-003D-G4J3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 132 Misc.2d =
753,
505 N.Y.S.2d 769</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (2d Dep't), <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>modified on other grounds,</i> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ20X0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-0B70-003D-G2M2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>132 A.D.2d 70=
8,
518 N.Y.S.2d 189 (2d Dept. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2160000400_2"></a>The statute contains no
requirement that the benefit&nbsp;[**20]&nbsp; actually be conferred or that
the testimony actually be influenced. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norma=
l'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2150000400"></a><a
