:contentItem:3S3K-0DW0-003D-G0NY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 130 A.D.2d at
432, 515 N.Y.S.2d at 471</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Weinstein
argued in the District Court that </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 215.0=
0</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires two additional elements of proof: (a) that the defendant subjectiv=
ely
believe that testimony, &quot;influenced&quot; as he desires would be
untruthful and (b) that testimony so &quot;influenced&quot; would in fact be
untruthful. Although the District Judge originally incorporated both
requirements in the proposed charge, he reconsidered his view following the
Government's timely objection. Weinstein now complains about the substance =
and
the timing of that ruling. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
charge that Judge Sifton ultimately gave incorporated one of the elements
proposed by Weinstein. He instructed the jurors that with respect to the in=
tent
element on the charge of witness bribery, they must find that the defendant
paid money to a witness in order to get the witness to modify the substance=
 of
his testimony in a way that the defendant believed would be false. He did n=
ot
instruct the jury that it would be a defense to the crime if by sheer
happenstance the testimony so influenced turned out to be true. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2180000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The essence of bribery is the
intent&nbsp;[**21]&nbsp; to influence improperly the conduct of another by
bestowing a benefit, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see</i> 39 </s=
pan><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1N31-6RDJ-8489-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Penal Law
&sect; 215.00</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, at 553 (Practice Commentaries) (McKinney 1988); as the=
re
is no requirement that the intended result be accomplished, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2170000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3K-0DW0-003D-G0NY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
