<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2990000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>There is no suggestion that
Shargel's vigorous representation of Napoli would have subjected him to
sanction or risked exposure of any wrongdoing on his part. And any claim th=
at
Shargel's attention may have been &quot;diverted&quot; by the disqualificat=
ion
proceeding in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotti</i> case is
insufficient to establish an actual conflict of interest. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc19_5"></a>A theoretical or merely speculative
conflict of interest will not invoke the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>per
se</i> rule. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2980000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-64F0-003B-53D0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Aiello,</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-64F0-003B-53D0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 900 F.2d at =
532</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1K033T3WJN000KB5YH0056R"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29C0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29P0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1K033T41CT000KB5YH0056S"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29S0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc20_4"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Because
Napoli has failed to show an actual conflict of interest, he must overcome =
the
presumption that his counsel's conduct was reasonable by satisfying the
two-pronged standard of </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29B0000400=
"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FN0-003B-S3TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Strickland v.
Washington,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-3FN0-003B-S3TN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 466 U.S. 668=
, 80
L. Ed. 2d 674, 104 S. Ct. 2052 (1984)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <i style=3D'mso-=
bidi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8M20-0039-P24N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Cruz,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8M20-0039-P24N-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 785 F.2d 399,
405 (2d Cir. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Napoli must show that (1)
&quot;counsel's representation fell below an objective standard of
reasonableness&quot; under &quot;prevailing professional norms&quot; and (2)
&quot;a reasonable probability that, but for counsel's unprofessional error=
s,
the result of the&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp; proceeding would have been different.&q=
uot;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29R0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font=
-style:
normal'>Strickland, 466 U.S. at 688, 694</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B10000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Out of a trial record of almost
10,000 pages, Napoli culls five instances of alleged deficiencies in Sharge=
l's
performance. On review of the record, we are convinced that Shargel's overa=
ll
performance was vigorous, sustained, and effective. Furthermore, we find th=
at
none of the five instances complained of falls &quot;outside the wide range=
 of
professionally competent assistance.&quot; <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ29T0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>Id. at 690</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Napoli
first points to Shargel's stipulation that a disputed mailing in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Robbins</i> case had been mailed. Shar=
gel
decided to stipulate to the fact of mailing once informed that James LaRoss=
a,
co-defendant Eisen's attorney, had entered into a stipulation with the
Government to that effect. Napoli now claims that the author of the letter
would have testified that the letter had been hand-delivered and that it was
incompetent for Shargel to stipulate to the contrary. Napoli ignores eviden=
ce
that the relevant document had in fact been mailed a subsequent letter writ=
ten
by its author so stating. Moreover, even if Shargel did not personally veri=
fy
this fact, it was reasonable for him to &nbsp;[**54]&nbsp; rely on the
representation of co-defendant's counsel that the author admitted the maili=
ng.
Furthermore, had the author been called to take the stand to testify to hand
delivery, his testimony would have been impeached by his previous letter
maintaining that it had been mailed. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>See
Aiello,</i> 900 F.2d ate 532-33 (failure to call exculpatory witness not
ineffective representation because testimony could have been impeached). </=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B30000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The next four instances of claim=
ed
ineffective assistance relate to decisions that &quot;fall squarely within =
the
ambit of trial strategy, and, if reasonably made,&quot; cannot support an
ineffective assistance claim. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <=
/i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9340-001B-K2GY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Nersesian,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9340-001B-K2GY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 824 F.2d 129=
4,
1321</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (2d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert.
denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B40000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>484 U.S. 957, 108 S. Ct. 355, 98 L. Ed=
. 2d
380 (1987)</i>. As his second example, Napoli claims that Shargel should ha=
ve
advised him to testify on his own behalf, and that Shargel should have done=
 a
more thorough job of impeaching two prosecution witnesses. &quot;<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc21_4"></a>The decision whether to call any wit=
nesses
on behalf of the defendant, and if so which witnesses to call, is a tactical
decision of the sort engaged in by defense attorneys in almost every
trial.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> It was a
reasonable&nbsp;[**55]&nbsp; tactical decision to rely exclusively on attac=
king
the Government's witnesses and presenting independent testimony rather than=
 to
subject Napoli to all of the risk attendant on cross-examination.
&quot;Decisions whether to engage in cross-examination, and if so to what
extent and in what manner, are similarly strategic in nature.&quot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Shargel had subjected both of =
these
witnesses to vigorous cross-examination. As Judge Sifton found, Shargel cou=
ld
have reasonably concluded that further cross-examination on relatively
unimportant matters would have confused or fatigued the jury. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*266]&nbsp;
As the third instance, Napoli points to Shargel's decision not to lay blame=
 for
the charged crimes on the other defendants. Clearly this was a reasonable
strategic decision. An effort to blame the other defendants might have prov=
oked
retaliation in the same vein, and the Government would have been the sole
beneficiary of such a development. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fourth,
Napoli claims that, in support of Napoli's motion for sequestration of the
jury, Shargel should have introduced newspaper clippings referring to the
Government's accusations against Shargel in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Gotti</i> case. While this might have been the preferable course,
&nbsp;[**56]&nbsp; we cannot say that Shargel's omission was below an objec=
tive
level of competence since none of the other defense attorneys included such
clippings in their similar motions but instead relied on paraphrasing.
Furthermore, Shargel did submit a number of the press accounts in support of
Napoli's claim of error in failure to sequester made in his motion for a new
trial. The issue, including the nature and content of the articles, was thus
properly preserved for appeal, and no prejudice occurred from the initial
failure to include the materials. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
Napoli claims that Shargel's failure to attack the Government in his summat=
ion
is evidence of a change in his attitude toward the Government following the
disqualification motion. Shargel's summation lasted an entire day and occup=
ies
almost two hundred pages of transcript. Shargel assailed the Government's
evidence in each of the racketeering acts charged against Napoli and review=
ed
the exculpatory testimony. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B70000400"></=
a>Furthermore,
he clearly was not seeking to curry favor with the prosecution when he clai=
med
that the Government &quot;paid their witnesses with a price that we could n=
ever
afford,&quot; had intentionally overlooked its own witnesses'
inconsistent&nbsp;[**57]&nbsp; statements, and had been overly &quot;righte=
ous
and sanctimonious&quot; in discrediting exculpatory testimony. The
comprehensive and vehement nature of Shargel's summation refutes Napoli's
claim. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B60000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9340-001B-K2GY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Nersesian,</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9340-001B-K2GY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 824 F.2d at =
1321</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
none of the five instances complained of did Shargel's performance fall bel=
ow
an objective standard of reasonableness, and Napoli does not even attempt to
demonstrate a reasonable probability that the result of the proceeding would
have been different absent the allegedly unprofessional conduct. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>VII.
Prosecutorial Misconduct and Prejudicial Publicity Regarding the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotti</i> Case </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
Friday, February 22, 1991, after the jury in the Eisen trial had begun
deliberations, argument was heard on an application by the prosecution in t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotti</i> case to disqualify several o=
f the
defense lawyers in that case including Shargel, who was then representing
Napoli in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i> trial. Napo=
li
contends that the prosecutors in the Gotti case acted improperly by repeati=
ng
in open court allegations made against Shargel in the Government's sealed
papers, and that these allegations improperly affected the jury's deliberat=
ions
in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i> case.
Napoli&nbsp;[**58]&nbsp; also argues that the Court improperly denied his
motion to sequester the jury. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B90000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc22_4"></a>If a prosecutor abuses her discretio=
n by
intentionally attempting to distort the fact-finding process, then a due
process violation exists.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2B80000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6KH0-003B-508H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Angiulo,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6KH0-003B-508H-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 897 F.2d 116=
9,
1191</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (1st Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cer=
t.
denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BB0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>111 S. Ct. 130, 112 L. Ed. 2d 98 (1990=
)</i>.
But Napoli does not claim that the government prosecutors in the Gotti case
intentionally provoked press coverage with the aim of prejudicing the jury =
in
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Eisen</i> case, nor does he off=
er any
evidence that the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotti</i> prosecu=
tors
even anticipated such a result. Moreover, the District Court took suitable
precautions to ensure that the jury was not exposed to the press accounts of
the allegations. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>When
the problem was brought to Judge Sifton's attention on the Friday of the
hearing in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gotti</i> case, the =
Judge
determined that he would speak with each juror individually in chambers abo=
ut
avoiding all news media over the weekend. Defense counsel objected to
individual interviews, arguing that it would magnify the problem,
&nbsp;[*267]&nbsp; and instead requested sequestration. The Judge denied
sequestration, but conducted a general inquiry&nbsp;[**59]&nbsp; of the jur=
y,
asking the jury to avoid all news media over the weekend and providing a me=
ans
by which concerned jurors could call the court to ascertain the progress of
then pending events in the Persian Gulf without resort to the media. The ju=
rors
gave their general agreement that they could comply. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BM0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The steps taken to protect the
integrity of the jury deliberations were adequate under the circumstances. =
In </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BK0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8WR0-003B-549J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Casamento,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8WR0-003B-549J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 887 F.2d 114=
1,
1154-55 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>cert. denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BN0000400"></a=
><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>493 U.S. 1081, 107 L. Ed. 2d 1043, 110=
 S.
Ct. 1138 (1990)</i>, this Court found that the &quot;great deal&quot; of me=
dia
attention surrounding an organized crime trial did not render it unfair in
light of Judge Leval's instruction to the jury to avoid press accounts about
the case. We held that &quot;in the absence of evidence to the contrary, we
will presume the jury followed these admonitions and avoided exposure to ne=
ws
reports about the trial.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.<=
/i>
Although Judge Leval conducted an individual interview with the jurors, the
defendants here specifically requested that no such individual <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>voir dire</i> be conducted for fear of
magnifying the problem. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See also </=
i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2BX0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CV70-001B-K24G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Gaggi,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CV70-001B-K24G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 811 F.2d 47,=
 53</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(2d&nbsp;[**60]&nbsp; Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert.
denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2C00000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>482 U.S. 929, 96 L. Ed. 2d 701, 107 S.=
 Ct.
3214 (1987)</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2C80000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover, as in Gaggi, we may fi=
nd
confirmation of the jury's ability to render an impartial verdict in &quot;=
the
care which it took in its deliberations.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2C20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-CV70-001B-K24G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>811 F.2d at 5=
3</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
As the District Court noted in rejecting these claims post-trial, jury
deliberations lasted from February 14 through March 4, 1991, with several n=
otes
sent out each day requesting to review exhibits or to have extensive testim=
ony
read back. And in its determination, the jury carefully distinguished among
defendants and among predicate acts, finding 16 of 22 racketeering acts pro=
ven.
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
the absence of any suggestion that the prosecutors in the Gotti case were
acting in bad faith, in light of the fact that the allegations did not conc=
ern
the trial in this case, the defendants on trial, or any of the events at is=
sue
in the case, and giving due weight to the district court's cautionary measu=
res,
we find no due process violation and reject Napoli's request for a new tria=
l. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CB0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, Napoli's claim that=
 the
District Court erred in denying his motion to sequester the jury in respons=
e to
the Gotti proceedings is without merit. &quot;<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23_4"></a>The decision&nbsp;[**61]&nbsp; to
sequester the jury to avoid exposure to publicity is committed to the
discretion of the court, and failure to sequester the jury can rarely be
grounds for reversal.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2C9000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D930-003B-50SR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Salerno,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-D930-003B-50SR-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 868 F.2d 524,
540</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (2d Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert.
denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CC0000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>491 U.S. 907, 109 S. Ct. 3192, 105 L. =
Ed. 2d
700 (1989)</i>. Judge Sifton did not abuse his discretion and his precautio=
ns
seem entirely adequate to the remote threat of prejudice from the expected
publicity concerning Napoli's attorney and his representation of another cl=
ient
in a completely unrelated matter. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>VIII.
Sentencing</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fishman,
Napoli, Gabe, and Rella contend that the District Court erred in applying t=
he
Sentencing Guidelines in sentencing them on the RICO conspiracy count. Each=
 of
these defendants asserts that, for various reasons, his liability for
participation in the conspiracy cannot extend past November 1, 1987, the
effective date of the Guidelines. We conclude that the District Court corre=
ctly
applied the Sentencing Guidelines to each of these defendants. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>This Court has determined that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc24_4"></a>persons convicted of offenses that b=
egan
before and continued after November 1, 1987, (so called &quot;straddle
crimes&quot;) would, upon sentencing, &nbsp;[**62]&nbsp; be subject to the
Sentencing Guidelines. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></sp=
an><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8160-003B-50JN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Story,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8160-003B-50JN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 891 F.2d 988,
994 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The RICO conspiracy charged in
this case ran from January 1981 to June 1990, and thus straddled the effect=
ive
date of the guidelines. Moreover, the jury found three defendants--Eisen, G=
abe,
and Morganti--guilty of a predicate act of mail fraud in connection with th=
e <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Stanton</i> case, which included a mai=
ling
on March 29, 1988. Thus, the conspiracy of which the defendants were
&nbsp;[*268]&nbsp; convicted continued after the effective date of the
guidelines. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CR0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Gabe complains that the jury was=
 not
asked to determine whether the conspiracy straddled the effective date of t=
he
Guidelines. However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc25_4"></a>for purposes=
 of
applying the Guidelines, &quot;the period during which an offense occurs is
considered a 'sentencing factor' to be determined by a judge--instead of an
element of the offense to be determined by a jury.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93N0-008H-V2M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Bloom,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-93N0-008H-V2M1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 945 F.2d 14,=
 17
(2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. The District Court did not err in finding by a
preponderance of the evidence that the conspiracy extended beyond November =
1,
1987. Gabe also argues that he should not have been sentenced under
the&nbsp;[**63]&nbsp; Guidelines because he played a &quot;minimal role&quo=
t;
in the conspiracy. The extent of Gabe's role, while relevant in determining=
 the
appropriate length of his sentence within the Guidelines, see </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6FW7-28Y3-RW74-84XN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>U.S.S.G. &sec=
t;
3B1.2</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fo=
nt-family:
Times;color:black'> (Guideline adjustments for mitigating role in the offen=
se),
has no bearing on the threshold question of whether the Guidelines should a=
pply
to his conspiracy conviction. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2FY0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Napoli argues that application of
the Guidelines to his conviction violates <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:n=
ormal'>ex
post facto</i> principles because he &quot;did nothing after November 1, 19=
87
and did not plan anything which ultimately transpired after that date.&quot;
Brief for Appellant Napoli at 140. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2G3000=
0400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc26_4"></a>The Court may find a continuation of
conspiratorial liability even though the particular defendant has ceased to
engage in overt conduct relating to the conspiracy prior to November 1, 198=
7,
if it was foreseeable that the conspiracy would continue past that date. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See, e.g., </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2CS0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C3V0-008H-V4BY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Devine,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-C3V0-008H-V4BY-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 934 F.2d 132=
5,
1332</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (5th Cir.), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cer=
t.
denied,</i> <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2G00000400"></a><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>112 S. Ct. 349, 116 L. Ed. 2d 288 (199=
1)</i>.
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GB0000400"></a>This holding derives from=
 the
basic principle that conspirators are generally held liable for the known or
reasonably foreseeable acts of all other co-conspirators&nbsp;[**64]&nbsp;
committed in furtherance of the conspiracy. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GB0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2G30000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GD0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2G20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JX20-003B-S2B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Pinkerton v.
United States,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JX20-003B-S2B0-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 328 U.S. 640,
647, 90 L. Ed. 1489, 66 S. Ct. 1180 (1946)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GD0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JH0000400"></a>Contrary to Napoli's content=
ion,
the acts of co-conspirators occurring after November 1, 1987, may be
attributable to a defendant for purposes of the application of the Sentenci=
ng
Guidelines in the absence of evidence that the defendant somehow &quot;caus=
ed&quot;
those acts. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2G90000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8180-003B-50K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Rosa,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8180-003B-50K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 891 F.2d 106=
3,
1068-69 (3d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (applying Guidelines to
co-conspirator who did nothing to further the conspiracy after 1986 in the
absence of any proof that he affirmatively renounced the conspiracy prior to
November 1, 1987). <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JH0000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GD0000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc27_4"></a>Defendants committing no acts in
furtherance of a conspiracy after the effective date of the Guidelines are =
not
subject to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ex post facto</i> punish=
ment
because they could have &quot;taken steps to prevent the final element of t=
he
crime from occurring,&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GC000=
0400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GGP0-008H-V3V9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Alkins,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-GGP0-008H-V3V9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 925 F.2d 541,
549 (2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, before the statute became
effective; they could have withdrawn from the conspiracy. <i style=3D'mso-b=
idi-font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2GF0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8180-003B-50K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rosa,</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-8180-003B-50K3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 891 F.2d at =
1069</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Napoli does not claim that he ever withdrew from the conspiracy. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**65]&nbsp;
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JK0000400"></a>Rella and Fishman contend=
 that
they affirmatively withdrew from the conspiracy before the effective date of
the Guidelines. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I655CF51WXT000VXJTM000BK"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JN0000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc28_4"></a>In
order to demonstrate withdrawal from a conspiracy, the defendant has the bu=
rden
of proving &quot;some act that affirmatively established that he disavowed =
his
criminal association with the conspiracy . . . and that he communicated his
withdrawal to the co-conspirators.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JJ0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6500-008H-V408-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Minicone,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-6500-008H-V408-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 960 F.2d at =
1108
(2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (citations omitted). &quot;Mere cessation of conspirato=
rial
activity is not enough&quot; to satisfy this standard. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JN0000400_2"></a><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Nerlinger,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 862 F.2d 967,
974 (2d Cir. 1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Rella
contends that he withdrew from the conspiracy when he left the firm as its
&quot;in-house&quot; investigator in 1984. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JX0000400"></a>However, Rella continued to =
work
for the firm on an <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ad hoc</i> basis
thereafter. The parties' stipulation that there was no showing that any of =
the
work performed by Rella after 1984 was tainted by illegality does not alter=
 the
significance of Rella's continued association &nbsp;[*269]&nbsp; with the E=
isen
firm. The District Court correctly found that the stipulation established, =
at
most, Rella's &quot;mere cessation&quot; of illegal conduct,
which&nbsp;[**66]&nbsp; was insufficient to prove his withdrawal from the
conspiracy. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JW0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 974</spa=
n></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2K00000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Fishman contends that he
affirmatively withdrew from the conspiracy before the effective date of the
Guidelines by resigning his position at the Eisen firm in order to practice
with another, independent law firm. Fishman relies on this Court's decision=
 in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Nerlinger,</i> in which the defendant
participated in a conspiracy to defraud the customers of a brokerage firm. =
The
defendant's role in the conspiracy was to open and maintain a bogus account
with the firm for the purpose of diverting profits fraudulently obtained by=
 his
co-conspirators. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> We ruled t=
hat
the defendant had withdrawn from the conspiracy before the conspiracy's
termination by resigning from the brokerage firm and closing the account,
reasoning that by doing so the defendant had foreclosed the possibility of
further participation in the conspiracy and relinquished any claim to
subsequent profits.<span style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2JY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4W-XGT0-001B-K251-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 974-75</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
However, in this case the District Court found that there was evidence that,
after leaving the law firm, Fishman &quot;continued to be entitled to a
percentage of the recovery on all cases he tried&nbsp;[**67]&nbsp; including
those giving rise to his pre-1985 racketeering acts.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Fishman
argues that he received an annualized salary from the Eisen firm and was not
entitled to a percentage of the recovery of the cases he tried. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2K70000400"></a>Stephen DiJoseph, a cooperat=
ing
co-defendant, testified that Fishman had told him that he was getting &quot=
;a
piece of the action on the cases he tried.&quot; Fishman faults the District
Court for denying his request for a hearing on this disputed sentencing fac=
tor.
However, <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc29_2"></a>the District Court has b=
road
discretion to determine the procedure by which it will resolve disputed iss=
ues
at sentencing, so long as it affords the defendant some opportunity to rebut
the Government's allegations. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <=
/i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FHBSR40K1MNJ2K10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-13P0-003B-54BV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>United States=
 v.
Prescott,</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-13P0-003B-54BV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 920 F.2d 139,
143-44 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. The District Court did not abu=
se
its discretion in denying the hearing and was not clearly erroneous in
concluding that Fishman failed to satisfy his burden of proving withdrawal =
from
the conspiracy. </span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn489' href=3D"#_ftn489"
name=3D"_ftnref489" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[**68]&nbsp;
Conclusion </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal25 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
