amily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
affirm the final judgment and supplemental final judgment of the district
court.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Background</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>From
1982 through June 1985, Stochastic provided insurance brokerage services to=
 all
of the corporations owned by Anthony (husband) and Carol (wife) that were in
the interstate bus transportation business. Stochastic also purchased two b=
uses
that it leased to Anthony and Carol's former&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp; operating
company, Eagle Bus, Inc. (&quot;Eagle&quot;). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
1984, Anthony and Carol and their corporations fell behind in their payment=
 of
insurance premiums to Stochastic. In order to pay Stochastic and other
creditors, Anthony indicated that he and Carol would sell some of their real
estate. In the fall of 1984, the DiDomenicos retained Wagner and his law fi=
rm,
Wagner McNiff, to represent them in various actions brought against them as
guarantors of obligations of Eagle. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
October 1985, allegedly unbeknownst to Wagner, Eagle filed for reorganizati=
on
under Chapter 11. At the time, Eagle owed approximately $ 2.3 million in
secured claims, and Stochastic was listed as an unsecured creditor having a=
 $
165,000 claim. Stochastic asserts that Anthony and Carol filed misleading
reports during the bankruptcy, fraudulently transferred Eagle's assets to C=
arol
Coaches and others, and paid their personal living expenses under the guise=
 of
&quot;officer's loans.&quot;. A garage in Staten Island (the
&quot;Garage&quot;) was one of the assets allegedly transferred from Eagle =
to
Carol Coaches without disclosure to the bankruptcy court and without
consideration. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>&nbsp;[*1162]&nbsp;
Ultimately, the bankruptcy petition was dismissed when Eagle &nbsp;[**6]&nb=
sp;
failed to file financial reports. No Eagle creditors were ever paid. Stocha=
stic
alleges that Anthony and Carol continued to have their living expenses paid=
 by
Carol Coaches and thereafter by Southgate, Anthony and Carol's current bus
operating company, formed by Wagner McNiff after Stochastic obtained a judg=
ment
against Carol Coaches. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Following
the bankruptcy of Eagle, Anthony and Carol arranged for Wagner and Wagner
McNiff to take control of essentially all of their assets and much of what
were, at least on their face, the assets of James (son of Anthony and Carol=
),
to whom Anthony and Carol were then transferring most of their own assets. =
The
district court found that Wagner thereafter used these assets, which were
adequate to pay all of the debts of the DiDomenicos and their companies, to
settle some of those debts, to pay personal expenses of Anthony and Carol a=
nd
business expenses of James, to pay fees to Wagner and Wagner McNiff, and to=
 pay
Wagner's personal family expenses. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
January 10, 1986, Stochastic instituted a suit in New Jersey Superior Court=
 to
recover unpaid insurance premiums against James, Carol Coaches, DCJM, and t=
wo
other bus companies operated by Anthony and&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; Carol. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Stochastic Decisions, Inc. v. James
DiDomenico</i>, No. L-009868-86 (N.J. Super. Ct. Law Div.) (the &quot;<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i>&quot;). Stochastic
alleged that James was authorized to sign checks on behalf of Eagle, and had
issued several Eagle checks to Stochastic in payment of insurance premiums =
for
the corporate defendants that were returned for insufficient funds, or on w=
hich
James stopped payment. Stochastic further alleged that James' fraudulent ac=
ts
deprived him of the protection of the corporate veil under New Jersey law. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
June 13, 1988, judgment was entered in favor of Stochastic. The court held
that: (1) the corporate defendants were liable for $ 175,564 in compensatory
damages and $ 46,584.92 in prejudgment interest; and (2) James was personal=
ly
liable for $ 41,265 of the compensatory damages plus $ 10,949.42 of prejudg=
ment
interest, and $ 100,000 in punitive damages. The court indicated that it wo=
uld
consider rescinding the punitive damages award if Stochastic received payme=
nt
of the balance of the judgment. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
October 31, 1988, Stochastic moved in New York Supreme Court, Richmond Coun=
ty
for the appointment of a receiver for James, Carol Coaches, and DCJM,
contending that&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; James and the corporations had been determ=
ined
by the New Jersey court to have fraudulently transferred assets. On Decembe=
r 6,
1988, a receiver was appointed, and ascertained that $ 150,000 would be
available by the end of that month for payment of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>Insurance Case</i> judgment as a result of Stochastic's levy upon a
contract between the New York City Board of Education and one of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i> defendants. </span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i> judgment was
ultimately affirmed by the Appellate Division of the Superior Court, and
certification was denied by the New Jersey Supreme Court. No. A-5827-87T1 (=
N.J.
Super. Ct. App. Div. Oct. 30, 1989), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal=
'>certification
denied</i>, No. C-502 (N.J. Jan. 25, 1990). Stochastic eventually recovered=
 the
full amount of the judgment subsequent to the filing of the instant action.=
 </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic
also filed suit on January 10, 1986 in New Jersey Superior Court against
Anthony and Carol as guarantors of Eagle's obligations under the bus lease =
with
Stochastic. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Stochastic Decisions, I=
nc. v.
Anthony DiDomenico</i>, No. L-010054-86 (N.J. Super. Ct. Law Div.) (the &qu=
ot;<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i>&quot;). On April 10,
1989, Stochastic obtained a judgment in the amount of $ 474,150.38.
&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; On September 7, 1989, Stochastic was awarded an additional
judgment of $ 19,338.38 for legal fees and expenses incurred in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i>. No appeal has been=
 taken
from these judgments. Stochastic apparently has not filed these judgments in
New York, and they remain unpaid. In 1986, Wagner proposed to Anthony and C=
arol
that the Garage be sold, and allegedly developed a plan to conceal the proc=
eeds
of the sale from Eagle's creditors. In December 1984, the Garage had been u=
sed
by Anthony and Carol to secure a $ 499,000 loan to Eagle from Gateway State
Bank (&quot;Gateway&quot;). In addition, Anthony and Carol personally
&nbsp;[*1163]&nbsp; guaranteed the loan, and pledged fifteen acres of land =
in
Staten Island (the &quot;Vacant Land&quot;) and their Bayonne residence (the
&quot;Residence&quot;) as collateral. The loan closing was handled by DiMai=
o,
an employee of, and alleged partner in, Wagner McNiff. <i style=3D'mso-bidi=
-font-style:
normal'>See supra</i> note 2.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Garage was sold for $ 2.1 million in May 1987. The Gateway mortgage was
satisfied from the proceeds of this sale, but Wagner arranged for the mortg=
age
to be assigned by Gateway to Wagner's son-in-law, Berke, as a continuing
encumbrance upon the Vacant Land and the Residence. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Wagner,
on behalf of Berke, &nbsp;[**10]&nbsp; then proceeded with a foreclosure ac=
tion
against the Residence that had initially been instituted by Gateway prior to
the full satisfaction of its mortgage. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Berke
v. Anthony DiDomenico</i>, No. F-5186-88 (N.J. Super. Ct. Ch. Div.) (the &q=
uot;<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Foreclosure Case</i>)&quot;; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also Gateway State Bank v. DCJM Re=
alty
Corp.</i>, No. F-6628-86 (N.J. Super. Ct. Ch. Div.) (original foreclosure
action by Gateway). The complaint in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>Foreclosure
Case</i> was amended on December 28, 1988 to name Stochastic as a defendant,
but the amended complaint was not served upon Stochastic until February 14,
1989. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
a question arose in the foreclosure proceedings whether Berke had paid
consideration for the assignment of the mortgage on the Residence, Berke's
counsel wrote a letter to the court dated December 11, 1987 that enclosed a
check from Berke to Wagner McNiff dated December 10, 1987 in the amount of $
249,139.48 as evidence of such payment. Wagner subsequently admitted in
testimony at the trial of the instant case, however, that &quot;the 247 or =
249
was never actually put up by [Berke]. It was put up by money that we advanc=
ed
to him out of the Carol Coaches mortgage for the purpose of attempting to
insulate&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; the elder DiDomenico's family home in Bayonne, N=
ew
Jersey.&quot; This testimony is confirmed by documentary evidence of a $
250,000 wire transfer to Berke on December 7, 1987. The New Jersey chancery
court ultimately determined that there was &quot;a very neat, contrived,
controlled plan of both Wagner and [Berke]&quot; designed &quot;for the pur=
pose
of causing a fraudulent conveyance [to] insulate a judgment [debtor] from .=
 . .
creditors,&quot; and granted summary judgment against Berke in the foreclos=
ure
proceeding on October 31, 1990. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
June 6, 1988, just prior to the entry of judgment in the <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Insurance Case</i>, James, upon Wagner's instructions, executed a d=
eed
on behalf of DCJM that transferred the Vacant Land to Kingsbury for nominal
cash and an assumption of existing obligations. Kingsbury was a shell
corporation recently formed for this purpose whose nominal shareholder was a
Wagner McNiff client. Wagner testified in the instant action that the trans=
fer
was intended &quot;to safeguard the property&quot; against seizure for
execution of the forthcoming judgment in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>Insurance Case</i>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
transferring the Vacant Land, Wagner solicited $ 350,000 from clients, the
principals of Gluck, for an unspecified&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; investment, which
took the form of a loan to Wagner in that amount. The $ 350,000 check was m=
ade
out to Paradise Properties, an entity controlled by Wagner. After the loan =
was
made, Wagner had Kingsbury execute a $ 425,000 mortgage on the Vacant Land =
to
Gluck. Wagner testified at this action that the purpose of the mortgage was
&quot;to put another step between the property and Stochastic.&quot; Gluck
apparently never requested a mortgage, and remained unaware of the mortgage
until shortly before a principal of Gluck was deposed in the instant case. =
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>On
January 31, 1989, Stochastic filed the instant action in the United States
District Court for the Eastern District of New York, alleging that the
defendants engaged in a civil RICO conspiracy involving bankruptcy fraud, w=
ire
fraud, and mail fraud to prevent Stochastic from collecting the amounts it =
was
owed. The complaint also alleged that the property transfers effected in the
course of the conspiracy were fraudulent conveyances under applicable state
law, and that the overall scheme constituted common law fraud. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>After
a twelve-day bench trial, the district court awarded judgment to Stochastic=
 on
all its claims against most of the defendants. &nbsp;[**13]&nbsp; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See supra</i> text accompanying note 1=
. The
court found that Wagner and Wagner McNiff &nbsp;[*1164]&nbsp; developed a p=
lan
to block Stochastic's attempts to collect on its judgments, and in the
execution of that plan &quot;used dummy corporations, dummy sales and mortg=
ages,
trust and special accounts of [Wagner McNiff], and nominees who had no
understanding of how they were being used, including Wagner's son-in-law, s=
ome
of his country club friends, and clients.&quot; The court further found that
the resulting conspiracy continued over a period of five years, and
&quot;included the commission of scores of wire frauds and scores of mail
frauds.&quot;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court stated that &quot;particular attention was devoted by the coconspirat=
ors
to protecting the Vacant Land so that Wagner and [Wagner McNiff] could
ultimately profit from its sale,&quot; and that Wagner's intention in that
regard was to &quot;cheat[] the elder DiDomenicos out of their last substan=
tial
asset&quot; for his own personal benefit. The court also found that DiMaio
individually was not responsible for any of the RICO predicate acts, and th=
at
Wagner McNiff &quot;was not a bona fide partnership although it held itself=
 out
to the public as a general partnership.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-sty=
le:
normal'>See supra</i> note 2.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp;
court awarded Stochastic: (i) $ 467,477.24 on its civil RICO claim; (ii) $
689,930.70 on its claim for violations of New York State fraudulent conveya=
nce
law; and (iii) $ 589,930.70 on its claim of common law fraud. The court fur=
ther
ruled that the first and second claims, totalling $ 1,157,407.94, could be
independently recovered; that the second and third claims, on the other han=
d,
could not be independently recovered, but were mutually exclusive; but that=
 if
judgment on the second claim was reversed, Stochastic could recover on the
third. Ancillary equitable relief was also granted to effectuate collection=
 of
the monetary awards. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>With
respect to the civil RICO award, the court designated as RICO damages $ 50,=
000
in legal fees expended by the plaintiff in attempting to collect its New Je=
rsey
judgments. $ 71,976.26 was originally billed &quot;for this phase of legal
services,&quot; of which the court determined $ 5,100 was not properly rela=
ted
RICO damages and $ 16,876.26 was excessive. In accordance with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1964(c) (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, the resulting $ 50,000 amount was trebled because it w=
as
deemed &quot;damages attributable and foreseeable [by] the conspirators,
&nbsp;[**15]&nbsp; resulting directly from the RICO violations.&quot; </spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
court also awarded the plaintiff $ 317,477.24 in RICO attorney fees and
expenses pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
calculated as follows. Stochastic's counsel billed $ 497,477.24 for pursuing
the instant case. The court subtracted $ 80,000 which was deemed
&quot;unreasonably excessive litigation,&quot; and the resulting amount was
determined to be &quot;an entirely reasonable sum given the difficulty of t=
his
kind of litigation.&quot; Of the remaining sum, $ 100,000 was allocated to =
fees
and expenses involved in pursuing the fraudulent conveyance claim. </span><=
/p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
$ 689,930.70 award on the fraudulent conveyance claim represented the amount
due on the outstanding <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Ca=
se</i>
judgment with applicable interest, plus $ 100,000 in legal fees. Finally, in
the event that the fraudulent conveyance award was reversed, the district c=
ourt
awarded Stochastic $ 589,930.70 on its claim for common law fraud, but
specified that attorney fees were unavailable on this claim. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>This
appeal, and the related cross-appeals, followed. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Discussion</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic
contends on appeal that the district court erred: (1) in not trebling
Stochastic's entire damage claim (i.e., the amount owed to&nbsp;[**16]&nbsp;
Stochastic by the defendants on January 31, 1989 when Stochastic commenced =
this
lawsuit, assertedly $ 924,428.66, the legal fee incurred in obtaining the N=
ew
Jersey judgment, and the unreduced legal fees of $ 71,976.26 billed in
connection with collection of the New Jersey judgments); (2) by wrongly
disallowing $ 180,000 of Stochastic's claimed legal fees in ascertaining the
RICO attorney fee recoverable pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>;
and (3) in finding Lucille Wagner, DiMaio, Gluck, and Berke not liable to
Stochastic as RICO conspirators. Cross-Appellants counter all these argumen=
ts,
and on cross-appeal maintain that: (1) the district court erred (a) in
determining that any of the legal fees expended by Stochastic in collecting
&nbsp;[*1165]&nbsp; its New Jersey judgments constituted RICO damages, and =
(b)
by awarding an excessive attorney fee to Stochastic on its RICO claim; (2) =
New
Jersey's &quot;entire controversy&quot; doctrine bars Stochastic from asser=
ting
any of its claims in the instant action; and (3) Wagner was improperly held
liable under the fraudulent conveyance and fraud counts for the amount of t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i> judgment. We procee=
d to
the consideration of all these issues.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>A. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>RICO Damages.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
