<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
district court ruled that Lucille Wagner, DiMaio, Gluck, and Berke were not
liable to Stochastic. On appeal, Stochastic contends that all these parties
should have been held to be liable to Stochastic as RICO conspirators.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1.
Lucille Wagner.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic's
amended complaint alleges no facts that implicate Lucille Wagner as a
participant in the RICO violations. The complaint only identifies her as Ar=
thur
Wagner's wife, and then encompasses her in a general charge that &quot;all
&nbsp;[**31]&nbsp; of the defendants, directed by Wagner,&quot; engaged in =
RICO
violations. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0TS0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>&quot;<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6_23"></a>The bare minimum of a RICO charge is=
 that
a defendant personally committed or aided and abetted the commission of two
predicate acts.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0TR0000400">=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-47K0-008H-V4DN-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>McLaughlin v.
Anderson, 962 F.2d 187, 192 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(citing </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0TT0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>H.J. Inc. v.
Northwestern Bell Tel. Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9VH0-003B-411T-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 492 U.S. 22=
9,
237, 106 L. Ed. 2d 195, 109 S. Ct. 2893 (1989)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0V20000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. at=
 496
n.14</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'>). The evidence adduced at trial concerning Lucille Wagn=
er
was inconclusive at best. There was proof that she was paid a $ 15,000 annu=
al
wage by Wagner McNiff during 1986 and 1987, but she denied ever working for=
 the
firm. Additionally, during 1987 and 1988, a number of checks from Paradise
Properties, an entity that was a vehicle for the receipt of fraudulent
transfers in pursuance of the RICO &nbsp;[*1169]&nbsp; scheme, were made
payable to Lucille Wagner, but she denied ever receiving any money from Par=
adise
Properties. The district court was certainly free to consider this proof to=
 be
inadequate to establish liability of Lucille Wagner to Stochastic.</span></=
