k'>2. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>DiMaio.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic
also argues that the district court erred in not holding DiMaio liable
&nbsp;[**32]&nbsp; as a RICO conspirator. This argument is irrelevant, beca=
use
DiMaio was not named as a defendant. See supra note 2. The alleged partners=
hip,
Wagner McNiff, was named as a defendant, however, and judgment was entered
against the partnership. Accordingly, all of the assets of the partnership,=
 but
none of DiMaio's nonpartnership assets, are subject to the judgment. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6707-C8X3-GXF6-81X9-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Civ. Pra=
c.
L. &amp; R. 5201(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> cmt. 5201:17 (McKinney 1978).</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>If
DiMaio advanced personal funds to the partnership, some issue as to the
amenability of those funds to the judgment in this action might conceivably
turn on whether they constituted a capital account of a partner or some oth=
er
form of advance. In any event, no such issue is presented on this appeal.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
add that in our view, contrary to the conclusion of the district court, DiM=
aio
was a partner in the law firm. DiMaio and Wagner worked under the firm name
Wagner, McNiff &amp; DiMaio for over thirty years. They shared office space=
, a
secretary, a letterhead indicating that the firm was a partnership, and thr=
ee
bank accounts under the name Wagner, McNiff &amp; DiMaio. Both lawyers sign=
ed a
partnership certificate for Marine Midland Bank when they opened one of
&nbsp;[**33]&nbsp; the accounts which stated that they were general partner=
s of
the firm, and that &quot;each general partner has full authority to represe=
nt
and sign for the firm in all respects.&quot; Both Wagner and DiMaio were li=
sted
as members of the firm in Martindale-Hubbell. Further, they assisted each o=
ther
in performing work for clients brought in by the other when needed, as in t=
he
case of the DiDomenicos. When DiMaio did work for Wagner's clients, they wo=
uld
frequently work out some type of revenue-sharing arrangement.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic
also asserts that DiMaio is liable to Stochastic as a partner by estoppel in
accordance with </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1PK1-6RDJ-84PD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>section 27</s=
pan></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
the Uniform Partnership Act as enacted in New York. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>See</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1PK1-6RDJ-84PD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>N.Y. Partners=
hip
Law &sect; 27</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fa=
reast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (McKinney 1988). This argument is specious. The statute
provides that a person who is deemed a partner by estoppel &quot;is liable =
to
any such person to whom [a representation of partner status] has been made,=
 <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>who has, on the faith of such
representation, given credit</i> to the actual or apparent partnership . . .
.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id</i>. </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5CT3-1PK1-6RDJ-84PD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 27(1)<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(emphasis added). Stochastic only claims that DiMaio made such representati=
ons
throughout the existence of the alleged conspiracy. Whether or not this is =
so,
&nbsp;[**34]&nbsp; Stochastic does not even assert the further statutory
predicate for relief, to wit, that Stochastic gave &quot;credit&quot; to Wa=
gner
McNiff on the basis of such representations.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Gluck</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic
also claims that the district court should not have dismissed the RICO
complaint against Gluck. Only the most tenuous claims are advanced concerni=
ng
Gluck's involvement in the transactions at issue in this case, however, and
there is no challenge to the assertion that Gluck was not even aware of the
mortgage on the Vacant Land arranged by Wagner, with Gluck as mortgagee, un=
til
shortly before a Gluck representative was deposed in this case. The district
court was certainly justified in concluding that Gluck was not a knowing
participant in the RICO conspiracy.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>4. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Berke.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0V50000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Stochastic contends that the New
Jersey judgment in the Berke foreclosure action should have been binding on=
 the
district court as to Berke's liability, in accordance with the doctrine of
collateral estoppel. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7_21"></a>This doctrine
applies if &quot;an issue tendered for resolution in a later litigation has
been finally determined in a prior adjudication after a full and fair
opportunity for litigation in which the issue was actually&nbsp;[**35]&nbsp;
litigated and necessary to the prior decision.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0V40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1N20-008H-V089-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Laaman v. Uni=
ted
States, 973 F.2d 107, 112 (2d Cir. </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1N20-008H-V089-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&nbsp;[*1170]=
&nbsp;</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1N20-008H-V089-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> 1992)</span>=
</i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(collecting cases), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>cert. denied</i=
>, <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VJ0000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>113 S. Ct. 1368, 122 L. Ed. 2d 746, 1992 WL 347108</i> (Feb. 22, 19=
93)
(No. 92-6592). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Stochastic's
submissions to this court contradict its issue preclusion argument. In its
reply brief (addressing the issue of claim preclusion), Stochastic states t=
hat
&quot;the factual predicate underlying this [RICO] action, and the evidence
required to prove the claims in this action, are entirely different from the
discrete contract and fraud issues involved in . . . the Berke Foreclosure
action.&quot; </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In any event, all of the elements
required to impose RICO liability upon Berke were not necessarily determine=
d in
the New Jersey foreclosure action. The district court expressly found that =
the
RICO enterprise was operated with Berke &quot;having no understanding of how
[he was] being used.&quot; Further, the New Jersey court's determination th=
at
Berke collaborated in an effort, violative of state law, to effectuate a
fraudulent conveyance does not necessarily establish knowing participation =
in
the commission of RICO predicate acts that establish a pattern of racketeer=
ing
activity &nbsp;[**36]&nbsp; within the meaning of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PV-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1962(c) (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-f=
areast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, thus providing a basis for liability under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1964(c=
)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0940-008H-V3G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tel-Phonic
Servs., Inc. v. TBS Int'l, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-0940-008H-V3G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 975 F.2d 11=
34,
1139 (5th Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (collecting cases).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
issue might be closer with respect to the district court's determination th=
at
Berke was not liable on Stochastic's state law claims, but Stochastic conte=
nds
on appeal only that Berke should have been held liable as a RICO conspirato=
r.
We perceive no reversible error in the district court's contrary determinat=
ion.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>C.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The New Jersey &quot;Entire
Controversy&quot; Doctrine.</span></i></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VX0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Counter-Appellants assert that
Stochastic's RICO claim is barred by New Jersey's &quot;entire
controversy&quot; doctrine. We are required by </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0NS-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1738 (1988)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> to accord the pertinent New Jersey proceedings (i.e., t=
he <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i>, the <i style=3D'ms=
o-bidi-font-style:
normal'>Guarantee Case</i>, and the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'=
>Foreclosure
Case</i>) &quot;the same full faith and credit . . . as they have by law or
usage in the courts of&quot; New Jersey. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5J70-003B-S04S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kremer v.
Chemical Constr. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5J70-003B-S04S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 456 U.S. 46=
1,
466, 72 L. Ed. 2d 262, 102 S. Ct. 1883 (1982)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(federal courts must give state&nbsp;[**37]&nbsp; court judgments same
preclusive effect they would have in issuing state); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>see also</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8T9R-R3R2-8T6X-72XJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>U.S. Const., =
art.
IV, &sect; 1</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-far=
east-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (imposing similar obligation upon state courts). </span=
></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1KHVH2BSMC000PRNK600575"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W00000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc8_21"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
New Jersey entire controversy doctrine</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W00000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>is a preclusionary principle
intended to prevent the fractionalization of litigation by requiring all cl=
aims
between the same parties arising out of or relating to the same transaction=
al
circumstances to be joined in a single action. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W40000400"></a>The effect of the doctrine i=
s to
preclude a party from withholding from the action for separate and later
litigation a constituent component of the controversy even where that compo=
nent
is a separate and independently cognizable cause of action.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W40000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W00000400_3"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'><span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp;</span></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0VY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Brown v. Brow=
n</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 208 N.J. Su=
per.
372, 506 A.2d 29, 32 (N.J. Super. Ct. App. Div. 1986)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(collecting cases); <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see also </i></=
span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W10000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cogdell v.
Hospital Ctr.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 116 N.J. 7,=
 560
A.2d 1169, 1172-74 (N.J. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Ti=
mes",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0WS0000400"></a>The Third Circuit has noted =
that
the New Jersey doctrine &quot;reaches more broadly than the 'same cause of
action' requirement of traditional res judicata doctrine.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W30000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5HW0-0039-P25C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Melikian v.
Corradetti</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-5HW0-0039-P25C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 791 F.2d 27=
4,
279 (3d Cir. 1986)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>see also </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0W50000400"><=
/a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1HJ0-008H-V4TF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Kozyra v. All=
en</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1HJ0-008H-V4TF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 973 F.2d 11=
10,
