Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The New Jersey Supreme Court has
stated that:</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;margin-right:0in;margin-bott=
om:0in;
margin-left:20.0pt;margin-bottom:.0001pt;text-align:justify;line-height:15.=
0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0X30000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0X10000400_2"></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The purposes of the [entire
controversy] doctrine include the needs of economy and the avoidance of was=
te,
efficiency and the reduction of delay, fairness to parties, and the need for
complete and final disposition through the avoidance of &quot;piecemeal
decisions.&quot; 2 State of New Jersey Constitutional Convention of 1947,
Committee on &nbsp;[*1171]&nbsp; the Judiciary Report &sect; II(J), at 1187
(1947). The entire controversy doctrine has evolved &quot;to eliminate dela=
y,
prevent harassment of a party and unnecessary clogging of the judicial syst=
em,
avoid wasting the time and effort of the parties, and promote fundamental
fairness.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0X00000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3RRM-X6N0-003C-N2MJ-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Barres v. Hol=
t,
Rinehart and Winston, Inc.</span></i></a><a
