style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0Y40000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DWP0-003B-61G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DWP0-003B-61G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 1449-50<=
/span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1NKYW822XW00079X8P001M1"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0YN0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In any event, as will appear, we
find other grounds for nonapplication of the entire controversy doctrine in
this case, and it is axiomatic that &quot;we may affirm on any basis for wh=
ich
there is a record sufficient to permit conclusions of law, including grounds
upon which the district court did not rely.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0YM0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9170-008H-V2CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Citrus Mktg. =
Bd.
of Israel v. J. Lauritzen A/S</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9170-008H-V2CF-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 943 F.2d 22=
0,
223 (2d Cir. 1991)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (collecting cases). </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0YR0000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1000000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>As indicated <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>supra</i>, the entire controversy doctrine requires &quot;all claims
between the same parties arising out of or relating&nbsp;[**41]&nbsp; to the
same transactional circumstances to be joined in a single action.&quot; </s=
pan><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0YP0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Brown</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 506 A.2d at=
 32</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
The &quot;transactional circumstances&quot; of the instant case involve a
fraudulent scheme to prevent the collection of the judgments in favor of
Stochastic in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i=
> and
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i>. Most of this
activity occurred after the entry of the judgment in the <i style=3D'mso-bi=
di-font-style:
normal'>Insurance Case</i>, so it cannot plausibly be claimed that the clai=
ms
presented in the instant litigation should have been brought in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i>. The &quot;transact=
ional
circumstances&quot; of the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarante=
e Case</i>
involved a simple claim that Anthony and Carol were liable, as guarantors, =
on
amounts owing under a defaulted lease of school buses by Eagle from Stochas=
tic.
Even though the entire controversy doctrine requires the presentation of
related claims arising after the inception of litigation to the court for i=
ts
determination as to their inclusion in the pending litigation, <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ0YY0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Brown</span><=
/i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-WSW0-003C-P456-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 506 A.2d at=
 34</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
it takes a considerable leap to conclude that the widely ramified scheme to
thwart Stochastic's collection of any and all amounts owed to it by the
DiDomenico&nbsp;[**42]&nbsp; interests should be regarded as having a close
transactional relationship to the much simpler matter of the guarantees owe=
d on
the defaulted bus lease.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1020000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Furthermore, a contrary ruling w=
ould
probably inure only to the benefit of Anthony and Carol, the only named
defendants in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i=
>, who
may be practically judgment proof at this juncture as a result of the
machinations that are the subject of the instant litigation. Mandatory join=
der
of parties was relatively limited in New Jersey practice prior to the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Cogdell</i> decision in 1989. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Cogdell</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 560 A.2d at
1171-74</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>. It was only established in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-s=
tyle:
normal'>Cogdell</i> that &quot;the entire controversy doctrine appropriately
encompasses the mandatory joinder of parties,&quot; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 1178</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
as well as claims, and this ruling was made applicable only prospectively a=
s of
July &nbsp;[*1172]&nbsp; 24, 1989, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>id</span></i>=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S3J-VT20-003C-P226-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>. at 1179</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
at which time final judgments in favor of Stochastic had been entered in bo=
th
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Insurance Case</i> and the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We recall at this juncture our
ruling <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>supra</i> that Stochastic may
ultimately have a claim for lost debt against one or more of
the&nbsp;[**43]&nbsp; defendants in this action, but any such claim has not=
 yet
accrued. Obviously, Stochastic cannot be faulted for its failure to assert a
claim that still has not accrued in the prior New Jersey litigation. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DWP0-003B-61G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Printing
Mart-Morristown</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4N-DWP0-003B-61G5-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 650 F. Supp=
. at
1449</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-fon=
t-family:
Times;color:black'> (<a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc9_19"></a>a prior judg=
ment
&quot;'cannot be given the effect of extinguishing claims which did not . .=
 .
exist and which could not possibly have been sued upon in the previous
case'&quot;) (quoting </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1050000400">=
</a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JBC0-003B-S1T4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lawlor v.
National Screen Serv. Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-JBC0-003B-S1T4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 349 U.S. 32=
2,
328, 99 L. Ed. 1122, 75 S. Ct. 865 (1955))</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
as to the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Foreclosure Case</i>,
Stochastic asserts without contradiction that it was not served as a defend=
ant
with the amended complaint in that case until after the initiation of the
instant litigation. Thus, there can be no serious claim that the opportunit=
y to
assert counterclaims in the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Foreclo=
sure
Case</i> precluded the assertion of any claims by Stochastic herein.</span>=
</p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
all the foregoing reasons, we affirm the ruling of the district court that =
the
assertion of Stochastic's claims in this case was not barred by the New Jer=
sey
entire controversy doctrine.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>D. <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Wagner's Liability for the Fraud Count=
s.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Wagner
contends&nbsp;[**44]&nbsp; that the district court incorrectly found him li=
able
on Stochastic's fraudulent conveyance and common law fraud claims. Wagner
concedes in his main appeal brief, however, that transfers were made &quot;=
in
reaction to a New Jersey judgment about to be entered, or [that] had been
entered against James, DCJM and Carol Coaches; James was the legal owner of=
 the
assets in question and the intent was to disguise his continued
ownership.&quot; Further, the district court explicitly found that Wagner m=
asterminded
these fraudulent transfers, used some of the transferred assets to pay his
legal fees and personal expenses, and intended ultimately to cheat the
DiDomenicos out of the Vacant Land for his personal benefit. </span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I1NKYW826S100079X8P005X2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1080000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The New York Court of Appeals has
made it clear that the pertinent provisions of the New York Debtor and Cred=
itor
Law provide a creditor's remedy for money damages against parties who
participate in the fraudulent transfer of a debtor's property and are trans=
ferees
of the assets and beneficiaries of the conveyance. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fon=
t-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ1070000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-9MV0-003V-B0F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>FDIC v. Porco=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S2R-9MV0-003V-B0F1-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 75 N.Y.2d 8=
40,
842, 552 N.E.2d 158, 159-60, 552 N.Y.S.2d 910, 911-12 (1990)</span></i></a>=
<span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(per curiam). &nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; It follows that Wagner was properly held
liable on the fraudulent conveyance claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ10P0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Wagner's liability for common law
fraud is also amply sustained by this record. We note that the district cou=
rt
allowed this claim only as an alternative to the recovery on the fraudulent
conveyance claim. Further, if Stochastic recovers the judgment in the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Guarantee Case</i> independently in th=
e New
Jersey proceedings, the &quot;one satisfaction&quot; rule will preclude any
recovery thereon as a component of the fraudulent conveyance claim, or the
totality of the common law fraud claim, in this case. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>See </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ10N0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1JK0-008H-V4Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Phelan v. Loc=
al
305 of the United Ass'n of Journeymen &amp; Apprentices of the Plumbing &am=
p;
Pipefitting Indus.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1JK0-008H-V4Y7-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 973 F.2d 10=
50,
1063 (2d Cir. 1992)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (&quot;A plaintiff may not reco=
ver
twice for the same injury.&quot;) (collecting cases), <i style=3D'mso-bidi-=
font-style:
normal'>petition for cert. filed</i>, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4FH5Y1T0K1MNJ10R0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S65-K8C0-003B-R23G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>113 S. Ct. 14=
15,
122 L. Ed. 2d 785, 61 U.S.L.W. 3511</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (U.S. Jan. 4, 199=
3)
(No. 92-1158).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Conclusion</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal26 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heig=
ht:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
final judgment and supplemental final judgment of the district court are
affirmed. Stochastic's motion to strike the second supplemental appendix is
denied. Cross-Appellants' application to suppress Stochastic's reply brief =
is
denied. The&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp; parties shall bear their own costs.<span
style=3D'mso-spacerun:yes'>&nbsp; </span></span></p>

