<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Plaintiff-appellant
Roy Den Hollander appeals from a September 28, 2004 order dismissing pursua=
nt
to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Federal Rule =
of
Civil Procedure 12(b)(6)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",s=
erif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> &nbsp;[*17]&nbsp; Hollander's
claims for civil remedies under the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt
Organizations Act (RICO), </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:65TD-2HT3-GXF6-804J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>18 U.S.C.
&sect;&sect; 1961-68</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, and denying leave to amend the
complaint. We assume the parties' familiarity with the facts of the case, t=
he
relevant procedural background, and the issues on appeal.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1340000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1360000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review a district court's grant of a motion to dismiss pursuant to </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i>, construing the complaint =
in the
light most favorable to the plaintiff and accepting its allegations as true.
&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1330000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48DP-W9V0-0038-X15Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Desiano v.
Warner-Lambert Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:48DP-W9V0-0038-X15Y-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 326 F.3d 33=
9,
347 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFcls=
cc2"></a>&quot;A
complaint should not be dismissed for failure to state a claim unless it
appears beyond doubt that the plaintiff can prove no set of facts in suppor=
t of
his claim which would entitle him to relief.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1350000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:44R3-71P0-0038-X4HD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Todd v. Exxon
Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:44R3-71P0-0038-X4HD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 275 F.3d 19=
1,
197-98 (2d Cir. 2001)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citations and quotation marks
omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0><o:p>&nbsp;</o:p></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><b
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",se=
rif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. Injury to Business or Property
and Proximate Causation</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1400000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1420000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc3"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0PX-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Section 1964(=
c)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
Title 18 authorizes suit by &quot;any person injured in his business or
property by reason of a violation of </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5042-D6RV-H0R2-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 1962</=
span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.&quot;
Thus, a RICO plaintiff &quot;only has standing if, and can only recover to =
the
extent that, he has been injured in his business or property by the conduct
constituting the violation.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1370000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Sedima, S.P.R=
.L.
v. Imrex Co., Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-9Y60-0039-N48C-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 473 U.S. 47=
9,
496, 105 S. Ct. 3275, 87 L. Ed. 2d 346 (1985)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
This injury must be proximately caused by the predicate acts of the RICO
violation. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1410000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lerner v. Fle=
et
Bank, N.A.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:47RT-5S10-0038-X036-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 318 F.3d 11=
3,
120 (2d Cir. 2003)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1440000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ14X0000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We have held that <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc4"></a>&quot;the RICO pattern or acts proximat=
ely
cause a plaintiff's injury if they are a substantial factor in the sequence=
 of
responsible causation, &nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; and if the injury is reasonably
foreseeable or anticipated as a natural consequence.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1430000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Hecht v. Comm=
erce
Clearing House, Inc</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-74M0-003B-533J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 897 F.2d 2=
1,
23-24 (2d Cir. 1990)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. Thus, we have affirmed the
dismissal under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:6CHH-WBV3-SCV7-1000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 12(b)(6)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> of
a RICO complaint where the alleged injuries were proximately caused not by =
the
alleged racketeering violations, but by the public exposure of those activi=
ties
or their discovery by the victim and the consequences of that exposure or
discovery. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ14W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Lewis ex rel.
American Express Co. v. Robinson (In re American Express Co. Shareholder
Litig.)</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 39 F.3d 39=
5,
400 (2d Cir. 1994)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that injury to American
Express shareholders following disclosure of a scheme to discredit a compet=
itor
was not a foreseeable result of that scheme, although the disclosure result=
ed
in business injury to American Express); </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ14Y0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DFN0-003B-51WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Burdick v. Am.
Express Co.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DFN0-003B-51WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 865 F.2d 52=
7,
529 (2d Cir. 1989)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (holding that a plaintiff fired=
 by
the defendant company after he complained about racketeering activities and
refused to participate in them could not show proximate causation, even tho=
ugh
their actions allegedly interfered with his &quot;ability . . . to earn a
living for himself&quot; and deprived him of his job).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Hollander's&nbsp;[**5]&nbsp;
complaint deals primarily with damages stemming from the investigation he
initiated, including &quot;loss of profits from the plaintiff's law and
consulting business from cessation of normal work as the result of . . . th=
e plaintiff's
ongoing investigation of the Enterprise's Scheme&quot;; &quot;business
interruption expenses of $ 50,000&quot;; &quot;loss of business opportuniti=
es
of $ 150,000&quot; and expenses of the investigation. These are the costs of
Hollander's acts, not the defendants' RICO violations. Indeed, as the distr=
ict
court noted, the conspirators allegedly tried to <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-=
style:
normal'>prevent </i>Hollander from investigating them, and thus from incurr=
ing
the expenses he claims as damages.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>One
of the lost profit allegations appears to involve more than just the
investigation: &quot;Loss of profits from the plaintiff's law and consulting
business . . . from cessation of &nbsp;[*18]&nbsp; normal work <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>as a result of the initial success of =
the
Enterprise's Scheme in defrauding the plaintiff into arranging for Member A=
lina
Shipilina to enter the U.S.</i> and, subsequently, the plaintiff's ongoing
investigation of the Enterprise's Scheme.&quot; (emphasis added). We take it
that Hollander alleges that he ceased business while helping to arrange
Shipilina's&nbsp;[**6]&nbsp; immigration, and that he lost money because he
could have been gainfully employed instead of making those arrangements. Ev=
en
if it was the criminal syndicate's intent to trick Hollander into
&quot;arranging for Member Alina Shipilina to enter the U.S.,&quot; it was =
not
the object of the conspiracy to cause him to cease work and thereby lose
profits in order to make those arrangements.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Other
allegations involve efforts to &quot;thwart&quot; Hollander's efforts, via =
his
investigation and this litigation, to recover unspecified injuries to his
business. &quot;Funds from racketeering activities have been used to build a
litigation war chest to fund fraudulent proceedings against the plaintiff a=
nd
fraudulently defend against legitimate proceedings . .<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ15S0000400"></a> .&quot;; &quot;Funds from
racketeering activities have been used to bribe public employees into
subverting the proper administration of the law . . . so as to thwart the
plaintiff's efforts to rectify the Enterprise's harm done to his business
reputation and goodwill.&quot; These allegations involve <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5"></a>injury to the administration of law, wh=
ich of
course is not compensable under RICO, and the hindrance of the plaintiff's
efforts to repair other damages. But <a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc6"></a=
>injuries
caused not by&nbsp;[**7]&nbsp; the alleged racketeering violations, but by =
the
discovery of those actions and the consequences of that discovery, are not
cognizable damages under RICO. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See =
</i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ1510000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Am. Exp=
ress
Co. S'holders Litig</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 39 F.3d at=
 400</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ15T0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DFN0-003B-51WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Burdick</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-DFN0-003B-51WH-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 865 F.2d at=
 529</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Finally,
the complaint also contains conclusory allegations that claim damages to
financial interests, but do not specify how racketeering activities caused
those damages. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ15X0000400"></a>&quot;The
plaintiff has suffered damages to his business and financial interests by t=
he
use of funds from racketeering activities to finance the Enterprise's
Scheme.&quot; &quot;The predicate acts committed in furtherance of the
Enterprise's Scheme against the plaintiff have caused damages to his busine=
ss
and financial assets as well as imperiled his safety, life, liberty and rig=
ht
not to live in fear.&quot; These paragraphs allege that damages to financial
interests have been caused, but make no factual allegation from which an
inference of proximate cause could reasonably be drawn. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc7"></a>Merely restating the requirements of RI=
CO in
the form of an allegation does not suffice to state a claim without some
factual allegation from which proximately caused financial
injury&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; may be inferred. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>See
</i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I4JC3VM00K1MNJ15W0000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Am. Exp=
ress
Co. S'holders Litig</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:3S4X-1DD0-003B-P2XD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>., 39 F.3d at=
 400
n.3</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font=
-family:
Times;color:black'> (&quot;While the complaint does cursorily assert that
American Express was a victim of the RICO defendants' acts and that these a=
cts
were the proximate cause of American Express's alleged injuries, these
conclusory allegations of the legal status of the defendants' acts need not=
 be
accepted as true for the purposes of ruling on a motion to dismiss.&quot;).=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Nor
does the Supplemental Complaint indicate any damages to business or property
proximately caused by racketeering violations. It alleges that the
&quot;additional illegal activities recounted in this supplemental complaint
have increased the harm to the plaintiff's business and property by causing
loss of profits, business interruption expenses, loss of business opportuni=
ties
and damage to the plaintiff's reputation and good will in the amount of $
100,000.&quot; It fails to indicate how the alleged activities, principally
threats &nbsp;[*19]&nbsp; against him and abuses of the justice system in
response to his lawsuit, damaged his business in any direct way. Thus,
Hollander has failed to state a claim under RICO.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal0 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>II. Leave to Amend</span></b></p>

