<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <u>Background</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kopchik
was employed as the Supervisor of Buildings and Grounds by the
Defendant-Appellee Town of East Fishkill (&quot;the Town&quot;). Kopchik
alleges that he was involved in an on-the-job motor vehicle accident in Oct=
ober
2014. The accident resulted in concussions, orbital fracture, and
post-concussion syndrome. Kopchik was hospitalized for a week, and he was
unable to work for several months. After returning to work, Kopchik was abl=
e to
continue performing his supervisory role adequately but, as the Town knew, =
he suffered
&quot;residua&quot; from his head injuries.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
addition, on January 7, 2015,</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn3'
href=3D"#_ftn3" name=3D"_ftnref3" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>1</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Kopchik filed a charge of age
discrimination &nbsp;[*34]&nbsp; with the EEOC against the Town and its Town
Supervisor, John Hickman, alleging that they improperly attempted to force =
him
to retire on September 22, 2014 (prior to Kopchik's on-the-job accident). T=
he
EEOC subsequently issued him a right-to-sue letter. The 90-day period for
Kopchik to bring an </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-7066-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>ADEA</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
claim after the EEOC issued the letter expired on November 30, 2015. Less t=
han
two months later, on&nbsp;[**3]&nbsp; January 28, 2016, the Town Board (thr=
ough
a resolution) eliminated all building-and ground-maintenance
positions&#8212;including Kopchik's position as supervisor&#8212;and
consolidated those positions into its Highway Department. The resolution
authorized the Town's Highway Superintendent to rehire all these
employees&#8212;except Kopchik&#8212;as laborers and motor equipment operat=
ors,
and at the same salary they earned in their previous positions.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
to Kopchik, the Town Board merely authorized the Town Supervisor to recomme=
nd
to the board that it pass another resolution that would permit rehiring Kop=
chik
as a motor equipment operator, a lower level position than supervisor.</spa=
n></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kopchik
alleges that the key decision makers</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn4'
href=3D"#_ftn4" name=3D"_ftnref4" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>2</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> for the Town were aware that he
lacked a license that was required for his becoming a motor equipment opera=
tor.
They were also aware that the ongoing impact of his head injuries would mak=
e it
difficult for Kopchik to pass the licensing examination required to become a
motor equipment operator.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
Town gave Kopchik until February 26, 2016, to obtain another job with the T=
own
or face termination. The Town knew that, due to Kopchik's &quot;health
status,&quot; the &quot;lifetime medical benefits&quot; he stood
to&nbsp;[**4]&nbsp; receive by retiring from his current position were of
&quot;vital importance.&quot; App'x 9. He alleges that if he did not retire=
 by
February 26, 2016, he would have been &quot;terminated&quot; and stood to l=
ose
those medical benefits. App'x 9-10. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2SF8NF00100=
00400"></a>The
Town purported to negotiate with Kopchik during this period but, as of Febr=
uary
25, 2016, the Town, by design, had still not offered Kopchik a job he was
physically able to perform. And so, he did retire, on February 25, 2016, the
day before he would have been terminated. Kopchik filed a charge of disabil=
ity
discrimination with the EEOC on April 25, 2016,</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn5' href=3D"#_ftn5" name=3D"_ftnref5" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>3</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and he timely filed suit after receiving a right-to-sue letter in May 2017.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
