on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5TK2-8T6X-7066-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>ADEA</span></=
i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
retaliation claim.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3. <u>ADA
and NYSHRL Discrimination Claims</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4SPNT7Y842000063K60012J"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N80020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N80040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>We next consider whether the
district court properly dismissed Kopchik's claims &nbsp;[*37]&nbsp; of
disability discrimination under the ADA and NYSHRL.<a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc5_0"></a> &quot;The elements&nbsp;[**10]&nbsp;=
 of a
[discrimination] claim under the ADA are that: (1) the employer is subject =
to
the ADA; (2) the plaintiff is disabled within the meaning of the ADA or
perceived to be so by her employer; (3) she was otherwise qualified to perf=
orm
the essential functions of the job with or without reasonable accommodation;
(4) she suffered an adverse employment action; and (5) the adverse action w=
as
imposed because of her disability.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N80010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H60-CKW1-F04K-J02W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Davis v. N.Y.=
C.
Dep't of Educ.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5H60-CKW1-F04K-J02W-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 804 F.3d 23=
1,
235 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. &quot;New York State disability
discrimination claims are governed by the same legal standards as federal A=
DA
claims.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N80030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CFW-1530-0038-X15K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rodal v.
Anesthesia Grp. of Onondaga, P.C.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4CFW-1530-0038-X15K-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 369 F.3d 11=
3,
117 n.1 (2d Cir. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
an initial matter, we find Kopchik's allegations sufficient to satisfy the
disability element of his discrimination claims. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4SPNT7Y9KM000063K6001WH"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2SF8NH0010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc6_0"></a>To
be considered disabled, a plaintiff must have &quot;a physical or mental
impairment that substantially limits one or more major life activities; a
record of such impairment; or be[] regarded as having such impairment.&quot=
; </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0732-D6RV-H0VP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>42 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
12102(1)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast=
-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. Major life activities include, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-fo=
nt-style:
normal'>inter alia</i>, &quot;learning, . . . concentrating, [and]
thinking.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SHT-0732-D6RV-H0VP-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect; 12102(=
2)</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
As we recounted in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N80050000400"></a=
><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BTT-3D51-F04K-J000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Parada v. Ban=
co
Indus. De Venezuela, C.A.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BTT-3D51-F04K-J000-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 753 F.3d 62=
, 68
n.3 (2d Cir. 2014)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, a regulation enacted pursuant =
to
the <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>ADA Amendments Act of 2008</i>
provides that &quot;[t]he term 'substantially limits' shall be construed
broadly in favor of expansive coverage, . . . [and] is not meant to be a
demanding standard.&quot;&nbsp;[**11]&nbsp; (quoting </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:608H-2J01-DYB7-W1V3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>29 C.F.R. &se=
ct;
1630.2(j)(1)(i)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>). Thus, &quot;[a]n impairment n=
eed
not prevent, or significantly or severely restrict, the individual from
performing a major life activity in order to be considered substantially
limiting.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> (quoting </=
span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dadministrative-cod=
es&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:608H-2J01-DYB7-W1V3-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>&sect;
1630.2(j)(1)(ii)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Here,
Kopchik plausibly alleges both that he was substantially limited in his abi=
lity
to learn, concentrate, and think from 2014 onward and that the Town regarded
him as such. As to the first point, Kopchik's allegations of severe head
injuries and specific diagnoses from the 2014 on-the-job motor vehicle acci=
dent
plausibly support that his ability to learn, think and/or concentrate were
substantially limited.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn8' href=3D"#_ft=
n8"
name=3D"_ftnref8" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>6</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-family=
:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Moreover, Kopchik provides
additional support for these effects, and that they were long-lasting, as he
had particular difficulty in 2016 &quot;plow[ing] significant amounts of sn=
ow
with <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>changing routes</i> in areas o=
f the
Town that were <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>unfamiliar</i> to
him.&quot; App'x 9 (emphasis added).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
for the Town regarding Kopchik as disabled, we think it is plausible that t=
he
Town was familiar with the type, extent, and &quot;residua&quot; of its
employee's injuries from 2014 onward. The injuries, after all, resulted fro=
m an
on-the-job accident, and Kopchik alleges he was out of work for months.</sp=
an></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2SF8NH0030000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Moreover,&nbsp;[**12]&nbsp; Kopc=
hik
plausibly alleges that the Town eventually and detrimentally acted upon that
information in 2016 when it terminated his position as a supervisor. A
plaintiff need only &quot;give plausible support to a minimal inference of
discriminatory motivation&quot; at the pleading stage. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2SF8NH0020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GKJ-MJF1-F04K-J09J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Littlejohn</s=
pan></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GKJ-MJF1-F04K-J09J-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 795 F.3d at=
 311</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>see </i></span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2SF8NH0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5HMS-JX41-F04K-J061-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Dooley v. Jet=
Blue
Airways Corp.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5HMS-JX41-F04K-J061-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 636 Fed. Ap=
px.
16, 21 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (applying this standard to
&nbsp;[*38]&nbsp; ADA discrimination claim). The district court held that
whether the Town Board's resolution eliminating the maintenance workers' ro=
les
&quot;had a unique effect on plaintiff because of his disability is irrelev=
ant
to [the] causal connection&quot; element. App'x 135. But this did not
accurately capture the extent of the facts Kopchik alleges. As discussed ab=
ove,
these allegations include that the resolution appeared designed to force on=
ly
Kopchik to find new employment with the Town, that the Town then offered him
only employment that it believed he was substantially unable to perform, and
that, as a result, his employment with the Town ended. Kopchik's allegations
thus satisfy the minimal pleading threshold as to the causation element of
Kopchik's ADA and NYSHRL discrimination claims.</span><a style=3D'mso-footn=
ote-id:
ftn9' href=3D"#_ftn9" name=3D"_ftnref9" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font=
-family:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>7</span></sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See id.</i></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>4. <u>Other
Elements of Kopchik's Claims</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Kopchik
and the Town also raise arguments regarding other elements&nbsp;[**13]&nbsp=
; of
Kopchik's claims that the district court did not address when it dismissed
Kopchik's claims for failure to satisfy causation pleading standards. We le=
ave
it to the district court to consider these arguments in the first instance.=
</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>5. <u>The
District Court's Denial of Leave to Replead</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I4SPNT7YC64000063K6001WJ"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2D6NJ50040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>Lastly, we call to the district
court's attention that in </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2D6NJ500300004=
00"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley Fin.
(Jersey) No. 3 Ltd. v. Wells Fargo Sec., LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d 16=
0,
189-91 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, we expressly rejected the
procedure that the district court followed here as the basis for denying
Kopchik the opportunity to replead. When the Town filed its motion to dismi=
ss
Kopchik's complaint for failure to state a claim, the district court&#8212;=
before
ruling on the motion&#8212;asked Kopchik if he would like to amend his
complaint, noting that the court would not expect Kopchik to have another
opportunity to do so. Kopchik declined. After the district court granted the
Town's motion to dismiss, the court did not grant Kopchik the opportunity to
amend his complaint.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2HM6N90010000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc7_0"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>There
is no obligation on a plaintiff to expend time and resources to amend a
complaint before knowing whether the court will find it insufficient, and if
so, in what ways. &quot;Without the benefit of a ruling, many a plaintiff w=
ill
not see the necessity of amendment or be in a position to
weigh&nbsp;[**14]&nbsp; the practicality and possible means of curing speci=
fic
deficiencies.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5V2SRRN2D6NJ50050000400"></=
a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d at=
 190</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
It is inappropriate to deny a plaintiff the opportunity to replead after a
defendant's motion to dismiss is granted, simply because the plaintiff deci=
ded
not to replead before learning whether the court would find the complaint
insufficient. The opportunity to amend the complaint is appropriately prese=
nted
<i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>after</i> the district court rules =
on a
motion to dismiss.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal1 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Accordingly,
the order of the district court is <b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'=
>VACATED
AND REMANDED</b>.</span></p>

