<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>DISCUSSION</span></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2HM6T80020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2HM6T80040000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc1_1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>We
review <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>de novo</i> the district cou=
rt's
dismissal of a derivative action for failure to satisfy </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2101-FG36-137S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 23.1, Fe=
d.
R. Civ. P.</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, accepting all facts in the complaint as true. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2HM6T80010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GNV-8V81-F04K-J0CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Espinoza ex r=
el.
JPMorgan Chase &amp; Co. v. Dimon</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GNV-8V81-F04K-J0CB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d 22=
9,
234-36, 239 (2d Cir.)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",seri=
f;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>certified question answered</i>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:no=
rmal'>124
A.3d 33 (Del. 2015)</i>. The parties agree that Delaware law applies.</span=
><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn17' href=3D"#_ftn17" name=3D"_ftnref17" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>8</span><=
/sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>See <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2HM6T80030000400"></a>Chau v. Lewis, 771 F.3d 118,=
 126
(2d Cir. 2014)</i>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>For
organizational clarity, this opinion proceeds as follows. First, we discuss
whether F5's class action dilution claim is properly considered derivative =
or
direct. We&nbsp;[**8]&nbsp; conclude that the dilution claim is properly
considered derivative, and therefore </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2101-FG36-137S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 23.1</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>'s
demand requirements apply. We next turn to the two issues of subject matter
jurisdiction that arise in connection with the derivative claims. On those =
two
issues, we hold (1) that the district court properly retained jurisdiction =
over
the case after it became clear that CAFA, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0G4-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1332(d)(2)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-farea=
st-font-family:
Times;color:black'>, could no longer anchor jurisdiction; and (2) that the
exception to supplemental jurisdiction in certain diversity cases, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:8SG9-5HW2-D6RV-H0HC-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>28 U.S.C. &se=
ct;
1367(b)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-=
font-family:
Times;color:black'>, did not preclude the district court from exercising
jurisdiction over the derivative state-law claims. Finally, we conclude tha=
t F5
failed to plead demand futility as </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2101-FG36-137S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 23.1</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
requires. We therefore affirm.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>I. The Class Action Dilution Cla=
im
is Derivative</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareas=
t-font-family:
Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5
Capital brought a putatively direct class-action claim for dilution of its =
(and
&nbsp;[*72]&nbsp; the potential class members') ownership interest in Star
Bulk. Specifically, F5 alleges that, &quot;[a]s a result of the Oceanbulk
Merger and the Excel Transaction . . . the interest of Plaintiff in Star Bu=
lk
has been diluted and the interest of the Defendants has been increased.&quo=
t;
Compl. &para; 141. Before the Oceanbulk Merger, F5 owned 1.4% of Star Bulk.
After the merger, that interest diminished&nbsp;[**9]&nbsp; to 0.5%. F5's s=
hare
of Star Bulk then decreased to 0.36% after the Excel Transaction. At the sa=
me
time, the Oaktree, Monarch, and Pappas defendants increased their collective
ownership of Star Bulk from 43.9% before the Oceanbulk Merger to 80.2% after
that transaction. In dismissing the complaint, the district court agreed wi=
th
the defendants that the dilution claim is properly considered a derivative
claim that belongs to Star Bulk, not to its shareholders.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I13MHJ0V4PT0007952T003V6"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2N1RG00010000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2N1RG00030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc2_1"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>In
order to determine whether a claim is derivative or direct, courts rely on =
the
following two inquiries: (1) &quot;[w]ho suffered the alleged harm&#8212;the
corporation or the suing stockholder individually&quot;; and (2) &quot;who
would receive the benefit of the recovery or other remedy.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2HM6T80050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4C3M-DD20-0039-4051-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Tooley v.
Donaldson, Lufkin &amp; Jenrette, Inc.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4C3M-DD20-0039-4051-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 845 A.2d 10=
31,
1035 (Del. 2004)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. &quot;If the corporation alone,
rather than the individual stockholder, suffered the alleged harm, the
corporation alone is entitled to recover, and the claim in question is
derivative.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S2N1RG00020000400"></a>=
<a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PDM-7SG0-TXFP-21WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Feldman v. Cu=
taia</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PDM-7SG0-TXFP-21WB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 956 A.2d 64=
4,
655 (Del. Ch. 2007)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>aff'd</i>, </span><a
