 29
n.62 (Del. Ch. 2002)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>. F5's attempt to characterize t=
he
payment as an undue personal financial benefit is unavailing and unsupporte=
d by
particularized facts. Thus, at least a majority of the board was disinteres=
ted
with respect to the Oceanbulk Merger.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2. <u>Excel
Transaction</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5's
allegations concerning five board members' partiality with respect to the E=
xcel
Transaction are even less compelling. As explained, in the Excel Transactio=
n,
Star Bulk purchased 34 dry bulk vessels from Excel, a bankrupt company. In
exchange for the ships, Star Bulk transferred 29.917 million shares of comm=
on
stock and $288.39 million in cash to Excel, yielding a total purchase price=
 of
$634.91 million. Defendants and their affiliated entities also lent Star Bu=
lk
approximately $213 million to purchase the ships, a fact that F5 claims
produced an obvious conflict. Star Bulk's board voted on the transaction, b=
ut
its shareholders did not.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>As
with the Oceanbulk Merger, F5 complains that Sourie, Stephens, and Kemp were
beholden to Oaktree, which benefitted from the Excel
Transaction&nbsp;[**45]&nbsp; because it was one of Excel's largest secured
lenders and therefore held a claim against the company in connection with i=
ts
bankruptcy reorganization plan. F5 also alleges that Oaktree orchestrated t=
he
Excel Transaction in a way that would increase Oaktree's share of Star Bulk.
Again, the mere fact of the three directors' appointment by Oaktree is not
sufficient to show demand futility.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn27'
href=3D"#_ftn27" name=3D"_ftnref27" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>18</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> F5 does not allege
any facts that raise a reasonable doubt concerning the impartiality of Zavv=
os
and Capralos. Therefore, the five directors that we identified were imparti=
al
with respect to the Excel Transaction.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5's
only other allegations concerning the Excel Transaction are that unidentifi=
ed
entities associated with Monarch, which Schmitz manages, lent money to Star
Bulk to pay for the Excel ships, thus conflicting Schmitz out of the pre-su=
it
demand concerning the transaction. Maybe so, but even assuming that Schmitz=
 was
not impartial as to this transaction, F5 has not pled particularized facts =
that
raise a reasonable doubt about the impartiality of a <i style=3D'mso-bidi-f=
ont-style:
normal'>majority</i> of Star Bulk's board. Accordingly, F5 has not pled dem=
and
futility with respect to the Excel Transaction based on a lack of director
independence.&nbsp;[**46]&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3. <u>Service
Maintenance Contracts</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5
alleges on information and belief that Star Bulk overpays for ship maintena=
nce
service contracts. Specifically, Star Bulk hired Pappas-related entities to
provide ship maintenance for $750 per &nbsp;[*87]&nbsp; ship per day, where=
 the
standard industry cost for such services is only $250 per ship per day. Bec=
ause
of the size of Star Bulk's fleet, those contracts apparently cost the compa=
ny
approximately $28 million per year. Although those allegations raise some
questions, F5 does not allege that any director other than Pappas was
conflicted in entering into the service contracts. Thus, as with the other
transactions, the plaintiff has failed to plead particularized facts that r=
aise
a reasonable doubt concerning the interestedness of a majority of board
members.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>B. The Business Judgment Rule</s=
pan></b></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S28T41N0030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S28T41N0050000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00020000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc21"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
other way that derivative plaintiffs can plead demand excusal is by alleging
&quot;particularized facts creating a reasonable doubt that . . . the
challenged transaction was otherwise the product of a valid exercise of
business judgment.&quot; <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S28T41N0020000400"></a>=
<i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Delaware Cty. Employees Ret. Fund, 124=
 A.3d
at 1020</i> (internal quotation marks omitted). <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00040000400"></a>The business judgment rule i=
s the
&quot;presumption that in making a business decision the directors of a
corporation&nbsp;[**47]&nbsp; acted on an informed basis, in good faith and=
 in
the honest belief that the action was taken in the best interests of the
company.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27S28T41N0040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VGG-YW50-TXFP-539M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gantler v.
Stephens</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VGG-YW50-TXFP-539M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 965 A.2d 69=
5,
705-06 (Del. 2009)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitt=
ed).
<a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM10010000400"></a>That presumption &quot;ca=
n be
rebutted if the plaintiff shows that the directors breached their fiduciary
duty of care or of loyalty or acted in bad faith.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K4Y-0FW0-TVT4-92DB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re Walt Di=
sney
Co. Derivative Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4K4Y-0FW0-TVT4-92DB-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 906 A.2d 27=
, 52
(Del. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'>. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM10010000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00040000400_2"></a>The &quot;business judgmen=
t rule
is [therefore] process oriented and informed by a deep respect for all <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>good faith</i> board decisions.&quot; =
<a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00030000400"></a><i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-st=
yle:
normal'>In re Citigroup Inc. S'holder Derivative Litig., 964 A.2d 106, 122
(Del. Ch. 2009)</i> (emphasis in original). The rule requires substantial
deference, and ultimately &quot;protects decisions unless they cannot be
attributed to any rational business purpose.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM00050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PK8-G0H0-TXFP-22JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re infoUSA=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PK8-G0H0-TXFP-22JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 953 A.2d at=
 972</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I13MHJ0Y7FC0007952T003VX"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM10030000400"></a><a name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFc=
lscc22"></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>To
excuse demand under the business judgment rule prong of the <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Aronson</i> test, the &quot;plaintiffs=
 must
plead particularized facts sufficient to raise (1) a reason to doubt that t=
he
action was taken honestly and in good faith or (2) a reason to doubt that t=
he
board was adequately informed in making the decision.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM10020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G3V-K6F0-TVSY-W386-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re J.P. Mo=
rgan
Chase &amp; Co. S'holder Litig.</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4G3V-K6F0-TVSY-W386-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 906 A.2d 80=
8,
824 (Del. Ch. 2005)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-styl=
e:
normal'>aff'd</i>, </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4JG4-VCR0-0039-439G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>906 A.2d 766
(Del. 2006)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fare=
ast-font-family:
Times;color:black'> (internal quotation marks omitted); <i style=3D'mso-bid=
i-font-style:
normal'>see </i></span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2D6NM10040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PK8-G0H0-TXFP-22JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>In re infoUSA=
</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4PK8-G0H0-TXFP-22JD-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 953 A.2d at=
 972</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5
does not give the business judgment rule prong of the demand futility test =
much
attention until its reply brief. But even the arguments presented
there&nbsp;[**48]&nbsp; fail to persuade us that there is reason to doubt t=
hat
the challenged transactions are entitled to the protections of the business
judgment rule. Thus, demand was not excused.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>1. <u>Oceanbulk
Merger</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5
principally alleges that the Oceanbulk Merger was a bad business decision
because Oceanbulk was in financial trouble at the time the transaction took
place. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TJ0020000400"></a>F5 also argues tha=
t the
transaction was a poor business decision because Oceanbulk was a troubled
company, and that Star Bulk had to take on a lot of additional debt and rai=
se
significant capital to purchase it. Although we assume Pappas's personal in=
terest
in the transaction, the allegations are insufficient to show that the other
directors acted in bad faith or failed to inform themselves about the
transaction before voting in its favor. The committee reviewing the transac=
tion
commissioned an expert report, undermining any attempt by F5 to characterize
the board as uninformed about the merger. Although F5 contends that the rep=
ort
was flawed, those flaws do not raise a reasonable doubt that &nbsp;[*88]&nb=
sp;
F5 will be able to rebut the presumptive protections of the business judgme=
nt
rule. Mere disagreement with the merits of the transaction is
insufficient&nbsp;[**49]&nbsp; for us to doubt that the &quot;decision [cou=
ld]
be attributed to any rational business purpose.&quot; </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TJ0010000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VGG-YW50-TXFP-539M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Gantler</span=
></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:4VGG-YW50-TXFP-539M-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 965 A.2d at=
 706</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
(internal quotation marks omitted).</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>2. <u>Excel
Transaction</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>Again,
F5 does not advance any specific arguments demonstrating that the Excel
Transaction was not the product of valid business judgment. There is no
indication that Star Bulk overpaid for the ships, and the mere fact that St=
ar
Bulk had to borrow money to buy the vessels is not on its own suspicious. In
its reply, F5 contends that incurring $213 million in debt to buy the ships
demonstrates that the transaction was flawed, arguing that because Excel was
bankrupt, Star Bulk should have gotten the ships at a discount. Based on F5=
's
complaint, however, we do not know whether Star Bulk did in fact receive a
discount on the ships because F5 does not provide any pricing benchmark by
which to assess the deal. More importantly for purposes of the business jud=
gment
rule, F5 does not allege any facts suggesting that the board entered into t=
he
Excel Transaction in bad faith or without sufficient information about its
terms. Thus, the complaint does not raise a reasonable doubt that the Excel
Transaction is entitled to the protection of the business&nbsp;[**50]&nbsp;
judgment rule.</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn28' href=3D"#_ftn28"
name=3D"_ftnref28" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>19</span></sup></a></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:6.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>3. <u>Service
Maintenance Contracts</u></span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>The
service contracts are somewhat suspect, but F5 has not alleged particulariz=
ed
facts raising a reasonable doubt concerning the applicability of the busine=
ss
judgment rule. Taking the complaint as true, a Pappas-controlled entity is
receiving three times the normal, market-rate payment for its ship maintena=
nce
services. That fact alone, however does not rebut the presumption of valid
business judgment. F5 has not alleged that the majority of the board acted =
in
bad faith with respect to those contracts or that the board was inadequately
informed before contracting with the service providers, nor does F5 suggest=
 in
its briefing how it could amend the complaint to allege facts that would ma=
ke
such a conclusion plausible. In the context of demand futility, F5's
allegations concerning the service contracts are insufficiently particulari=
zed
to satisfy </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2101-FG36-137S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 23.1</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;line-height:15.0pt'><br>
<b style=3D'mso-bidi-font-weight:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times"=
,serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>IV. The District Court Properly
Denied Leave to Amend</span></b><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG70030000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG70050000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>The district court denied F5's
implied request to amend the complaint because an amendment would be futile=
.</span><a
style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn29' href=3D"#_ftn29" name=3D"_ftnref29" title=
=3D""><sup><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>20</span>=
</sup></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'> </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_LNHNREFclscc23"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15(a)(2)=
</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
&nbsp;[*89]&nbsp; allows a party to amend its pleading with the court's lea=
ve,
which should be &quot;freely give[n] . . . when justice so requires.&quot;
&quot;Leave to amend&nbsp;[**51]&nbsp; may properly be denied if the amendm=
ent
would be futile,&quot; however. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TK0020000400"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG70020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BYD-KG01-F04K-J00G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Krys v. Pigot=
t</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5BYD-KG01-F04K-J00G-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 749 F.3d 11=
7,
134 (2d Cir. 2014)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> (citation omitted). &quot;A
proposed amendment to a complaint is futile when it could not withstand a
motion to dismiss.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG7004000040=
0"></a><a
