if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 164</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:12.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TK0040000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In a footnote in its opposition =
to
the motion to dismiss, F5 generally indicated its desire to amend the compl=
aint
should the district court decide to dismiss it. Although the informality of=
 the
request is not ordinarily a basis to deny leave to amend, </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TK0030000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley Fin.
(Jersey) No. 3 Ltd. v. Wells Fargo Sec., LLC</span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d 16=
0,
190 (2d Cir. 2015)</span></i></a><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>, the district court did not deny
leave to amend on that basis. Instead, it determined, albeit after only a b=
rief
analysis, that any amendment would be futile, a rationale that our decision=
 in <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Loreley</i> &quot;leaves unaltered.&qu=
ot; <i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> Even on appeal, F5 has still
&quot;give[n] no clue as to how the complaint's defects would be cured&quot;
through an amendment. <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> (inte=
rnal
quotation marks and citation omitted). In light of F5's failure to indicate=
 how
it might amend the complaint to eliminate its gaping holes, along with the
heightened pleading requirements associated with demand&nbsp;[**52]&nbsp;
futility under </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:5GYC-2101-FG36-137S-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 23.1</sp=
an></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
we see no error in the district court's denial of leave to amend based on
futility.</span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>F5's
principal argument on appeal is not persuasive. F5 contends that the distri=
ct
court's individual practices in civil cases improperly limited its ability =
to
amend the complaint, contravening </span><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dstatutes-legislati=
on&amp;id=3Durn:contentItem:69J2-YK63-RSM8-R265-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Rule 15</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>
and our decision in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Loreley</i>. At=
 the
time that the district court dismissed the complaint, its individual practi=
ces
provided in relevant part that: &quot;If a motion to dismiss is filed, the
plaintiff has a right to amend its pleading within twenty-one days . . . . =
If
the plaintiff elects not to amend its pleading, no further opportunities to
amend to address the deficiencies identified by the motion to dismiss will
ordinarily be granted.&quot;</span><a style=3D'mso-footnote-id:ftn30'
href=3D"#_ftn30" name=3D"_ftnref30" title=3D""><sup><span style=3D'font-fam=
ily:"Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times'>21</span></sup></a><span style=3D'font-famil=
y:
"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'> Appellant Br. 55.
Whatever the possible problems with the district court's individual practic=
es
might be as a general matter, in this case the district court was clearly
justified in finding that F5's implicit request for amendment was futile.</=
span></p>

<p class=3DNormal2 style=3D'margin-top:10.0pt;text-align:justify;line-heigh=
t:15.0pt'><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80010000400"></a><span style=3D'font-family:"=
Times",serif;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:black'>In its reply, F5 claims that it =
was
entitled to a judicial resolution of the complaint before it could be expec=
ted
to propose specific amendments. <a name=3D"Bookmark_I13MHJ0YC8H0007952T003V=
Y"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80030000400"></a>In F5's view, the pleading
deficiencies were &quot;latent,&quot; &quot;easily missed or
misperceived&nbsp;[**53]&nbsp; without full briefing and judicial
resolution,&quot; and even &quot;borderline, . . . subject to reasonable
dispute.&quot; </span><a name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2HM6TK0050000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley Fin.
(Jersey) No. </span></i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>3 Ltd.</span>=
</i></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>, 797 F.3d at=
 191</span></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
Thus, the plaintiff argues that, as in <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:norm=
al'>Loreley</i>,
the district court's decision to deny leave to amend was error. <a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80030000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80050000400"></a>The procedure that we reject=
ed in </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80050000400_2"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80030000400_3"></a><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80020000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Loreley</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>,
however, is distinct from what occurred in this case: there, the court requ=
ired
the &nbsp;[*90]&nbsp; parties to attend a pre-motion conference and to exch=
ange
three-page letters concerning the anticipated motion to dismiss. </span><a
name=3D"Bookmark_I5NKV27T2N1RG80040000400"></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'>Id.</span></i=
></a><a
href=3D"https://plus.lexis.com/api/document?collection=3Dcases&amp;id=3Durn=
:contentItem:5GHD-XPY1-F04K-J085-00000-00&amp;context=3D"><i
style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'><span style=3D'font-family:"Times",ser=
if;
mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:#0077CC;background:white'> at 190</span=
></i></a><span
style=3D'font-family:"Times",serif;mso-fareast-font-family:Times;color:blac=
k'>.
&quot;The impropriety occurred . . . when, in the course of the conference,
[the court] presented Plaintiffs with a Hobson's choice: agree to cure
deficiencies not yet fully briefed and decided or forfeit the opportunity to
replead.&quot; <i style=3D'mso-bidi-font-style:normal'>Id.</i> The district=
 court
did not employ that prohibited procedure here, where F5 had an opportunity =
to
amend in response to full briefing of the defendants' motion to dismiss, and
failed, in its own briefing on the motion or by motion for leave to amend a=
fter
the district court dismissed the case, to explain how it proposed to amend =
the
complaint to cure its defects. To this day, F5 has not explained how it cou=
ld
amend the complaint to plead demand futility or otherwise&nbsp;[**54]&nbsp;
survive a motion to dismiss. Thus, we affirm the district court's decision =
to
deny F5 leave to file its unspecified amendments to the complaint.</span></=
p>

